window.CERCHI_INDICE = {"lingua":"en","t":{"h_risposta":"Short answer","h_testi":"Where the texts deal with it","carico":"Loading the texts…","nessuno":"No results for these words. Try simpler words or a synonym.","risultati":"{n} passages found. Here are the most relevant.","un_risultato":"One passage found.","leggi":"Read in the text","altri":"Show more results","tutte":"See all the questions","comma":"paragraph","nessuno_aiuto":"If the answer is missing, <a href=\"contributing.html\">say so</a>: it is a gap worth reporting."},"docs":[["Human history","path/1-human-history.html",0.7],["Analysis of the present","path/2-analysis-of-the-present.html",0.7],["The system against the individual","path/3-the-system-against-the-individual.html",0.7],["The Circles Project","path/4-the-circles-project.html",0.7],["Constitution","register/constitution.html",1.2],["Code 1 — Base contribution and Common Storehouses","register/code-01.html",1.0],["Annex A to Code 1 — Material production and workshops","register/code-01-annex-a.html",1.0],["Code 2 — Quality and validation","register/code-02.html",1.0],["Code 3 — Deliberation, thresholds and Assembly","register/code-03.html",1.0],["Code 4 — Restorative justice and guarantee","register/code-04.html",1.0],["Code 5 — Learning and competences","register/code-05.html",1.0],["Code 6 — Health","register/code-06.html",1.0],["Code 7 — Automation and the Network","register/code-07.html",1.0],["Code 8 — Energy and water","register/code-08.html",1.0],["Code 9 — Agroecology and food","register/code-09.html",1.0],["Code 10 — Habitat and territory","register/code-10.html",1.0],["Code 11 — Logistics and basic mobility","register/code-11.html",1.0],["Code 12 — Community care","register/code-12.html",1.0],["Code 13 — Biodiversity, sanctuaries and fauna","register/code-13.html",1.0],["Code 14 — General safety","register/code-14.html",1.0],["Code 15 — External relations and Balance","register/code-15.html",1.0],["Code 16 — Transition and launch","register/code-16.html",1.0],["Annex A to Code 16 — Social start-up protocol","register/code-16-annex-a.html",1.0],["Annex B to Code 16 — Operating manual for disengagement and federation","register/code-16-annex-b.html",1.0],["Annex C to Code 16 — First-year operating charter","register/code-16-annex-c.html",1.0],["How to contribute","contributing.html",0.85],["Parameters to be verified","register/parameters.html",0.6],["Annual Annex of the Sufficiency Basket","register/draft-basket.html",0.6],["UCR Annex and first balance of hours","register/draft-ucr.html",0.6],["Public CdR table","register/draft-cdr.html",0.6],["Change proposals (archive)","register/proposals.html",0.6],["Adversarial review of the proposals","register/proposals-review.html",0.6],["Change log","register/changes.html",0.6]],"unita":[[0,"","","","The history of humanity is not the history of a progressive liberation, nor that of a continuous degeneration.\nIt is the history of a species capable of building what no individual could have built alone and, at the same time, of using that very capacity to dominate, exploit and destroy.\nAs human beings, we have cooperated in order to survive, we have shared knowledge, built communities, founded cities, we have developed medicine, philosophy, the scientific method, law, art! We have crossed oceans, observed the sky, transformed energy, defeated diseases and enormously increased the material possibilities of the life of each one of us.\nAnd yet.\nIt is these same capacities of ours that have produced, as their counterpart, hierarchies, power, domination, and control.\nWhat accompanied our capacity for cooperation from the very start was competition. Territory became property, force became authority, authority became institution, the institution became the State.\nThe State, which has certainly made possible a capacity for organisation until then unknown to small communities, but which also from the very start concentrated instruments of coercion and control over ever vaster territories and populations.\nWe have built kingdoms and empires, with the aid of war, conquest and the subordination of other peoples. Monarchies, aristocracies, empires and religious systems organised collective life for centuries, distributing freedom, wealth, knowledge and power in a profoundly unequal way."],[0,"","","","How many people in the course of history have been reduced to slavery, deported, exploited or deprived of their autonomy? How many still today? Entire populations conquered, assimilated, expelled or exterminated.\nThere is therefore no single history of humankind.\nThere are the histories of peoples, some who built, others who were conquered, others who even resisted, among those who dominated and those who were dominated.\nPower has a recurring characteristic still today: whoever controls resources, weapons, information or institutions has a greater capacity to determine the lives of others.\nModernity does not erase this dynamic.\nIt transforms it.\nThe scientific revolution widened knowledge. Industrialisation multiplied productive capacity. Constitutions limited certain powers. Civil and political rights expanded.\nAnd yet.\nStates go on fighting wars. We go on being divided by borders, interests, identities, religions, ideologies and competition.\nA species born to survive in small groups, with the capacity today to alter ecosystems and possibly annihilate entire populations at the press of a button by “one” who chooses for all.\nWe have attained a capacity to intervene in the world immensely greater than our historical capacity to govern its consequences.\nWe have become more powerful without becoming wiser.\nWe produce more without knowing how to distribute.\nWe have increased the capacity to communicate without eliminating loneliness."],[0,"","","","We have widened knowledge and, I have the impression, ignorance too.\nWe have recognised universal rights without managing to guarantee them universally. Surely we are not just a simple succession of failures?\nAnd yet.\nWe have produced ideas capable of reaching beyond tribes, cities, empires, religions and nations.\nWe have conceived the idea that dignity belongs not only to the member of one’s own community, or to whoever suits us, but to the human being as such.\nAnd so I ask myself.\nWhy did we allow it to happen?\nI do not believe that we simply chose, in every age, to live under someone’s domination.\nWe had no alternative.\nWe were born into it. And adapted.\nThe human being is capable of building systems that can produce as much good as suffering, and then of living inside those systems long enough to forget having built them.\nAnd here lies perhaps one of the deepest capacities of social systems: to transform their own historical conditions into something that individuals perceive as inevitable.\nAnd yet.\nEmpires have fallen.\nMonarchies overthrown.\nRights won.\nConstitutions rewritten.\nBorders changed.\nMillions of individuals, organised through institutions, can build a civilisation.\nAnd the same millions of individuals, organised through different institutions, can build different civilisations.\nA society must also be judged by the rules through which it transforms individual actions into collective consequences."],[0,"","","","We have inherited much of the world in which we live, but inheriting a system does not mean being condemned to preserve it. We have already shown that we can change, more than once, what seemed immutable.\nSo perhaps the question now is not\nwhy we have not been capable of building a better society.\nIt is: for how much longer will we go on considering this system inevitable?\nWe cannot entrust the future to someone’s virtue.\nWe certainly cannot entrust it to the benevolence of those who hold power.\nIt is up to us to decide whether to confine ourselves to inheriting the future or to begin building it."],[1,"general-overview","General overview","","This document describes the present through public and verifiable data. Economic, demographic, social, institutional and environmental phenomena are intertwined and cannot be understood as watertight compartments. The analysis concentrates above all on what does not work, because that is where the need for an alternative arises; progress is not denied: it is reported alongside the critical issues, theme by theme. These advances are real and documented by the same sources; several of them, however, are slowing down. Every figure gives its source and year of publication. Estimates and projections are indicated as such; the last part is a reading of the data, not a datum."],[1,"1-inequality-poverty-and-hunger","1. Inequality, poverty and hunger","","Concentration of wealth: The richest 10% of the world population owns about 75% of wealth; the poorest half about 2% (World Inequality Report 2026). The wealth of billionaires reached 18,300 billion dollars in 2025: +16% in one year, +81% since 2020 (Oxfam, 2026).\nExtreme poverty: Between 1990 and 2022 about 1.5 billion people escaped it, but the reduction has almost come to a halt: in 2022 838 million people were still living on less than 3 dollars a day at purchasing power parity, 10.5% of the world population, and the model estimate for 2025 is 9.9% (World Bank, 2025).\nHunger: In 2025 about 645 million people suffered from hunger, 7.8% of the population: it was 8.6% in 2022, and this is the third consecutive year of decline. In Africa it affects one person in five. 2.69 billion cannot afford a healthy diet (FAO and other UN agencies, SOFI 2026).\nHousing: In the EU, between 2010 and the beginning of 2025 house prices rose by 57.9% and rents by 27.8% (Eurostat, 2025). Worldwide, about 2.8 billion people lack adequate housing and 318 million are homeless (UN-Habitat estimates, 2025)."],[1,"2-work","2. Work","","Work and wages: More than 2.1 billion workers out of 3.6, almost 58%, are employed informally, that is without a registered contract or without social protection (ILO projection, 2026). In high-income countries, between 1999 and 2024 productivity grew by 29% and real wages by 15% (ILO, Global Wage Report 2024-25).\nTechnology and employment: 25% of world employment, and 34% in high-income countries, is in occupations exposed to generative AI. What is expected is above all a transformation of tasks: complete replacement remains limited (ILO estimate, 2025).\nExploitation and child labour: In 2021 50 million people were living in conditions of modern slavery, 28 million in forced labour and 22 million in forced marriage: 10 million more than in 2016 (ILO, IOM and Walk Free estimate, 2022). In 2024, 138 million children were in child labour, 54 million of them in hazardous activities: more than 20 million fewer than in 2020 (ILO and UNICEF, 2025)."],[1,"3-population-health-and-education","3. Population, health and education","","Population: 8.2 billion people in 2024; expected peak of about 10.3 billion in the mid-2080s (UN projection, World Population Prospects 2024). In OECD countries, the number of people aged over 65 per 100 of working age (20-64) will rise from 33 in 2025 to 52 in 2050, putting pensions and welfare under pressure (OECD projection, Pensions at a Glance 2025).\nLife expectancy: 73.3 years in 2024, after the fall to 70.9 during the pandemic (UN, World Population Prospects 2024).\nChild mortality: Between 1990 and 2024 it fell by 60%: from 93.5 to 37.4 deaths under five per 1,000 births, that is from one child in 11 to one in 27. Even so, in 2024 4.9 million children under five died, and since 2015 the pace of reduction has slowed by more than 60% (UN IGME, 2026).\nMaternal mortality: Reduced by 40% between 2000 and 2023 (WHO and other UN agencies, 2025).\nHealth workforce: A shortage of 11 million health workers is projected by 2030, above all in low- and lower-middle-income countries (WHO projection).\nLoneliness and mental health: One person in six suffers from loneliness, with more than 871,000 deaths a year associated with it (WHO, 2025). One adolescent in seven has a mental disorder (WHO).\nEducation: In 2024 273 million children and young people were out of school, a number rising for the seventh consecutive year (UNESCO, 2026)."],[1,"4-essential-services-and-the-digital-sphere","4. Essential services and the digital sphere","","Water and sanitation: 2.1 billion people lack safely managed drinking water and 3.4 billion lack safely managed sanitation (WHO and UNICEF, 2025).\nElectricity and cooking: 92% of the world population has electricity; 655 million people are still without it, and about 2 billion cook with polluting fuels (Tracking SDG7, 2026).\nDigital: 6 billion people, about three quarters of the world population, use the Internet; 2.2 billion are still offline (ITU, 2025). In a survey of executives and cybersecurity experts, 87% of respondents identify AI-related vulnerabilities as the fastest-growing cyber risk: this is an opinion, not a measure of attacks (WEF, Global Cybersecurity Outlook 2026)."],[1,"5-climate-energy-and-environment","5. Climate, energy and environment","","Climate: 2025 was 1.44 °C warmer than the pre-industrial average; the last eleven years are the warmest ever recorded (WMO, 2026). CO₂ emissions from fossil fuels reached a record 38.1 billion tonnes in 2025 (Global Carbon Budget 2025 estimate). With current policies, warming will reach 2.8 °C by the end of the century (UNEP projection, Emissions Gap Report 2025).\nClean electricity: In 2025 renewables (33.8%) overtook coal (33.0%) for the first time in world electricity generation, and generation from fossil sources did not grow (-0.2%) (Ember, 2026). 692 GW of renewables were installed, 85.6% of new capacity (IRENA, 2026).\nClimate adaptation: Developing countries will need 310-365 billion dollars a year by 2035; in 2023 they received 26 billion in international public finance, that is 12-14 times less (UNEP, Adaptation Gap Report 2025).\nPlanetary boundaries: Seven out of nine have been exceeded, all with a worsening trend; the latest is ocean acidification (Planetary Health Check 2025).\nBiodiversity: Monitored populations of wild vertebrates declined on average by 73% between 1970 and 2020 (WWF, Living Planet Report 2024). More than 48,600 species are threatened with extinction, 28% of those assessed (IUCN, Red List, October 2025).\nForests: Deforestation fell from 17.6 million hectares a year in the nineties to 10.9 million in 2015-2025 (FAO, 2025).\nOzone: More than 99% of the substances that deplete it have been eliminated; the layer will return to 1980 levels around 2040 as a global average and 2066 over the Antarctic (WMO, 2025).\nResources and waste: The extraction of materials rose from 30 billion tonnes in 1970 to about 106 billion projected for 2024 (UNEP, Global Resources Outlook 2024). In 2022, 1.05 billion tonnes of food were wasted, 19% of that available to consumers (UNEP, Food Waste Index 2024). Every year 92 million tonnes of textile waste are produced (UNEP, 2025)."],[1,"6-war-security-and-forced-displacement","6. War, security and forced displacement","","Conflicts: In 2025 there were 65 armed conflicts involving at least one State, the highest number since 1946, and about 244,600 deaths from organised violence (UCDP, Uppsala University, 2026).\nMilitary expenditure: 2,887 billion dollars in 2025 (+2.9%), the eleventh consecutive year of growth. It is 2.5% of world GDP, the highest level since 2009 (SIPRI, 2026).\nNuclear weapons: In January 2026 there were about 12,187 warheads in existence; those in military stockpiles numbered about 9,745, 130 more in one year (SIPRI Yearbook 2026).\nForced displacement: At the end of 2025 the people forced to leave their homes numbered 117.8 million, one in 70 worldwide. It is the first fall in ten years (UNHCR, Global Trends, 2026)."],[1,"7-democracy-institutions-and-rights","7. Democracy, institutions and rights","","Democratic decline: Global freedom declined for the twentieth consecutive year: in 2025, 54 countries deteriorating against 35 improving (Freedom House, 2026).\nTrust: In OECD countries 40% of citizens have high or moderately high trust in their national government; 43% have low or no trust, figures stable compared with the previous survey (OECD, Trust Survey 2026).\nPrisons: In Europe, on 31 January 2025, there were on average 95.2 inmates per 100 places (94.7 a year earlier). 14 prison systems are overcrowded, 9 of them seriously, including Italy and France (Council of Europe, SPACE I, 2026).\nViolence against women: About 840 million women, almost one in three, have suffered violence from a partner or sexual violence in the course of their lives; the figure is almost unchanged since 2000 (WHO, 2025)."],[1,"8-debt-trade-and-aid","8. Debt, trade and aid","","Global debt: In 2024 public and private non-financial debt reached 251,000 billion dollars, just above 235% of world GDP; public debt stands at 93% of GDP (IMF, Global Debt Monitor 2025).\nTrade: World trade is on course to exceed 35,000 billion dollars for the first time in 2025 (UNCTAD estimate, 2025). At the same time, trade is shifting increasingly towards allied or neighbouring countries (UNCTAD, 2025).\nDevelopment aid: In 2025 it fell by 23.1%, the sharpest annual fall ever recorded, dropping to 174.3 billion dollars (OECD, preliminary data, 2026)."],[1,"part-two--overall-reading","Part two — Overall reading","","This part is an interpretation of the preceding data.\nThe individual phenomena cannot be addressed with isolated sectoral approaches. Read together, the data show three recurring gaps.\nBetween what is produced and how it is distributed. A fifth of the available food is wasted while 645 million people suffer from hunger. In rich countries productivity grows more than wages. Wealth increases and becomes concentrated.\nBetween what is known and what is done. Climate goals are shared, but emissions are at record levels. Military expenditure has been growing for eleven years, while development aid records its sharpest fall ever and climate adaptation receives a fraction of what is needed.\nBetween those who decide and those who bear the effects. Freedom has been retreating for twenty years and, in OECD countries, those who do not trust their own government still slightly outnumber those who do.\nThe advances reported alongside the critical issues indicate that these gaps are not inevitable: where there have been coordinated collective choices, from child health to the ozone layer, the results have come."],[1,"sources","Sources","","The links lead to the page consulted for verification, on 3 October 2026; those replaced with the original document were consulted on 19 October 2026. Where the original report could not be reached, they lead to the summary used in its place.\nWorld Bank, update of the poverty lines (June 2025); 2025 estimate\nCouncil of Europe, SPACE I prison statistics (May 2026)\nEmber, Global Electricity Review 2026\nEurostat, house prices and rents (July 2025)\nFAO, Global Forest Resources Assessment 2025\nFAO, IFAD, UNICEF, WFP and WHO, The State of Food Security and Nutrition in the World (SOFI) 2026\nIMF, Global Debt Monitor 2025\nFreedom House, Freedom in the World 2026\nGlobal Carbon Project, Global Carbon Budget 2025\nILO, Employment and Social Trends 2026; Global Wage Report 2024-25; ILO-NASK index on generative AI (May 2025)\nILO and UNICEF, Child Labour: Global Estimates 2024 (June 2025)\nILO, IOM and Walk Free, Global Estimates of Modern Slavery (2022)\nIRENA, Renewable Capacity Statistics 2026\nITU, Facts and Figures 2025\nIUCN, Red List of Threatened Species (October 2025)\nOECD, Survey on Drivers of Trust in Public Institutions 2026; Pensions at a Glance 2025; preliminary data on aid 2025 (April 2026)\nWHO, Commission on Social Connection (June 2025); violence against women (November 2025); health workforce; adolescent mental health\nWHO and other UN agencies, Trends in Maternal Mortality 2000-2023 (2025)\nWHO and UNICEF, water and sanitation 2000-2024 (August 2025)\nUN, World Population Prospects 2024\nOxfam, Resisting the Rule of the Rich (January 2026)\nSIPRI, world military expenditure 2025 (April 2026); SIPRI Yearbook 2026\nStockholm Resilience Centre and PIK, Planetary Health Check 2025\nTracking SDG7, The Energy Progress Report 2026\nUCDP, Uppsala University (June 2026)\nUN IGME, Levels and Trends in Child Mortality (March 2026); UNICEF historical series\nUN-Habitat, homelessness (May 2025); UN News, inadequate housing (May 2025)\nUNCTAD, Global Trade Update (December 2025)\nUNEP, Emissions Gap Report 2025; Adaptation Gap Report 2025; Food Waste Index Report 2024; Global Resources Outlook 2024; Zero Waste Day 2025\nUNESCO, GEM Report 2026\nUNHCR, Global Trends (June 2026)\nWEF, Global Cybersecurity Outlook 2026\nWMO, global temperature 2025 (2026); Ozone Bulletin (September 2025)\nWorld Inequality Report 2026\nWWF, Living Planet Report 2024"],[2,"","","","In brief\nThe system produces a great deal, but does not turn that wealth proportionately into security, time and capacity for choice for people.\nThe individual is asked to take responsibility for results that depend on conditions they do not control.\nThose who decide, those who benefit and those who pay are more and more often different people.\nThe present is financed by transferring costs onto the future.\nThis is why correcting the effects is not enough: the conditions that produce them must be changed.\nThe contemporary world can be described through data on growth, productivity, debt, poverty, resources and climate. But a person does not experience GDP: a person experiences the possibility of working, having a home, receiving care, studying, having relationships, having time and imagining a future. The central question therefore becomes the relationship between what the system produces and what it concretely allows people to live. The problem of the present is not necessarily that the system produces too little. It could be, on the contrary, that it produces enormously without proportionately transforming this wealth into security, freedom, time and capacity to choose."],[2,"","","","From the individual’s point of view, the economy is a concrete chain: time, work, income, home, consumption, savings, health, relationships, security, future. It is through this chain that the great phenomena are perceived: growth becomes the cost of housing, monetary policy the cost of credit, demographic transformation caring for one’s parents or the difficulty of having children, climate change the cost of food. This is why a society can be richer overall and leave many individuals with a growing difficulty in building autonomy.\nModern capitalism has built part of its legitimacy around an implicit promise: those who work, educate themselves, save and take on responsibility should be able to improve their condition progressively. The individual is asked to get an education, keep up to date, be competitive, save and be flexible. But the individual does not determine housing prices, interest rates, the labour market or the cost of energy. The consequences are individualised while many of the causes are systemic. It is the first contradiction: one can be responsible for one’s own choices without really being master of the conditions within which those choices produce their effects. This does not eliminate personal responsibility. It does, however, impose a condition of justice: responsibility should be proportionate to the effective capacity to influence the causes."],[2,"","","","Productivity can increase enormously without its benefit being automatically transformed into greater security or freedom. The same increase can become wages, profit, investment, lower prices, public service, shorter working hours or greater accumulation: there is no automatic distribution of technological progress. And when a growing share of the value produced separates from the income distributed to labour, demand has to be sustained by other means, from public spending to credit, which may also simply shift the problem forward in time. The question is not whether technology is good or bad. It is who controls the benefit it produces and in what form that benefit returns to society.\nThe home makes the contradiction evident. It is at once a fundamental need, an asset, an investment and financial collateral: for those who own capital it can represent security and wealth, for those who lack it, it can become a barrier to entry into stability. Inequality, in fact, does not concern only the quantity of goods owned. Wealth allows one to wait, take risks, withstand crises, protect oneself and choose. The difference is not only how much you own. It is how much you can afford to decide, and how far you can modify the conditions within which others, too, have to live."],[2,"","","","Democracy formally grants citizens a political voice; the market, by contrast, distributes economic capacity according to property, income and capital. The two logics come into tension when an economic decision produces an immediate benefit for some and costs distributed over others: a firm gains efficiency while a territory faces changes in employment, an investment produces a return while others bear environmental costs. The decisive problem then emerges: who decides, who benefits, who pays and who can correct the decision? When these four positions grow too far apart, economic inequality also becomes a question of legitimacy. This is perhaps the deepest form of systemic injustice: living inside decisions that one did not help to make and that one does not have the capacity to correct.\nMillions of rational decisions can produce a result that no one chose. A person may decide to have fewer children because the cost of living is too high: it is an understandable choice. But if many people make the same choice, the population ages, the pressure on care and pension systems increases, and the next generation may find itself in even more difficult conditions. A system can transform individual rationality into collective irrationality. The problem is not only to correct people’s behaviour: it is to build institutions capable of coordinating individual interests towards collectively sustainable results."],[2,"","","","The present can be financed by transferring costs onto the future. Debt makes it possible to bring forward future economic capacity: it is not negative in itself, but it becomes problematic when a growing share of future income has to be used to sustain obligations already created. The same happens outside finance. An economy also uses natural resources, health, time, infrastructure, trust, human capital and social capital: if it consumes them faster than it manages to replenish them, it can increase production in the short term while reducing its own future capacity. The fundamental question changes: not how much we can produce, but how much we can produce without consuming the conditions of future production.\nA system can be unequal, inefficient and even produce crises without immediately losing legitimacy. The fracture grows deeper when the conviction spreads that the ordinary instruments are no longer sufficient to correct it. If the individual thinks “I can study, work, save and behave responsibly, but the fundamental conditions of my life are decided elsewhere”, the question is no longer only economic. It becomes institutional."],[2,"","","","If these contradictions were only occasional errors, it would be sufficient to correct them. But if they derive from the very way in which property, labour, production and power are organised, redistribution alone cannot be the definitive answer. Redistribution acts on the result; systemic change must act on the conditions that produce it. It means production that starts from real need and not from price, necessary labour that becomes contribution, fundamental goods held in common custody, a market that remains an instrument, power that is a temporary function and not a position, technology that helps to decide but does not decide, progress measured by sufficiency. And it starts from the individual: no one must be deprived of necessary goods because they do not own enough.\nThe conclusion to which this analysis leads is that capitalism must no longer be the dominant principle of social organisation. Not simply because it is considered morally unsatisfactory, but because it is no longer capable of reconciling production, freedom, justice, sustainability and responsibility. Capitalism must end. But this sentence acquires meaning only if one understands what comes after: if the fundamental structures remain the same, the problem may simply reappear in another form."],[2,"","","","There does not exist today a complete, verified and universally applicable alternative system. This is why the alternative must be built, and it is here that the Constitution of the Circles takes on its meaning: not as a perfect model nor as a dogma, but as the first concrete architecture of an alternative that does not yet fully exist. It will have to be tested, it will have to fail at certain points, it will have to be corrected, because the greatest risk would be to replace a system that claims to be inevitable with another that claims to be perfect. We do not yet know what the society to come will be in its definitive form. But we do know which contradictions we can no longer ignore.\nThe end of capitalism is not the point of arrival. It is the point at which the hardest work begins. We are not obliged to regard the system we have inherited as inevitable. But if we want to go beyond it, we cannot limit ourselves to rejecting it. We must become capable of building another way of living."],[3,"a-possible-alternative","A possible alternative","","The Circles Project is a proposal for an alternative to the way in which we organise collective life today.\nIt arises from a simple question:\nwhat alternatives do we have?\nThe project proposes to build a society in which the freedom of the individual and the capacity of the collectivity to cooperate can coexist.\nThe starting point is the individual.\nEvery person possesses the same dignity and no person is worth more than another. The collectivity arises from the voluntary coming together of individuals and from the need to accomplish together what no one could accomplish alone.\nEach person is as important as the others. No one stands above anyone.\nThe freedom of each meets that of the others.\nMy freedom does not end where yours begins: it begins together with yours.\nFrom this principle derives a relationship of reciprocity:\nneed gives the right to receive; capacity commits one to contribute; usefulness to the community deserves recognition.\nContribution to the collectivity does not constitute superiority over another, just as need does not constitute a condition of inferiority.\nSociety exists to allow people to live freely together and to pool what is necessary for that freedom to be concrete."],[3,"the-circles","The Circles","","The Circles Project organises society through a federated structure of Circles.\nA Circle is an organisational and decision-making unit of defined size, constituted around a specific purpose and a specific sphere of responsibility and connected to the other Circles through a federated structure.\nEach Circle therefore operates within a determined sphere.\nA proximity Circle deals with the matters that directly concern the community in which it operates.\nA Competence Circle, by contrast, addresses what requires specific knowledge or responsibilities.\nThe different levels do not represent a hierarchy of the worth of persons.\nThey represent a distribution of functions.\nNo Circle is superior to the individuals who compose it and no sphere of competence should turn automatically into personal power.\nThe Circles cooperate, coordinate with one another and check one another in accordance with the rules established by the system.\nThe aim is to allow society to organise itself on a large scale without permanently concentrating power in the hands of a few."],[3,"from-need-to-task","From need to task","","The Circles Project introduces a fundamental principle into the organisation of production and services:\nthe system does not start from the tasks to be assigned. It starts from the needs to be met.\nFirst, what is needed is identified.\nThen, what must be done is determined.\nFinally, who will have to do it, and how, is organised.\nWork does not determine need. It is need that determines the work necessary.\nThis changes the way collective organisation is conceived.\nAn activity is not maintained simply because it exists.\nA production is not considered necessary merely because it can be carried out.\nA resource is not directed to where there is greater purchasing capacity, but to where there is a necessity that the system must address in accordance with its own rules.\nProduction, logistics, infrastructure and the contribution of persons must therefore be organised starting from real necessities.\nThe system must be able to identify needs, understand their scale and transform this information into organisation.\nInformation tools and artificial intelligence can assist this process, but they remain tools: the gathering and processing of information do not in themselves constitute decision-making power."],[3,"organising-without-concentrating-power","Organising without concentrating power","","A complex society needs organisation.\nIt needs people who produce, care, build, teach, design, transport, maintain infrastructure, manage information and perform thousands of different functions.\nSpecialisation is therefore necessary.\nThe problem arises when a necessary function becomes a permanent position of power.\nThe Circles Project therefore separates, as far as possible, competence, function and personal power.\nA person can have a specific competence without becoming the owner of that competence.\nA Circle can have a specific responsibility without becoming the owner of the matter it manages.\nA function can require continuity without necessarily turning into a permanent authority.\nFor this reason the system provides for instruments of transparency, verification, challenge, rotation of functions where possible and distribution of responsibilities.\nThe aim is to build organisation without building a new class of dominators."],[3,"the-collectivity-as-an-instrument-of-the-individual","The collectivity as an instrument of the individual","","The relationship between individual and collectivity constitutes the centre of the project.\nThe collectivity possesses capacities that the single individual cannot have.\nIt can build hospitals, energy networks, transport systems, infrastructure, housing, information systems, research facilities and large-scale production.\nIt can pool knowledge and resources.\nIt can address problems that exceed the possibilities of a single person.\nThe Circles Project recognises at the same time the value of individual capacities.\nThose who know how to do something useful for others must be able to be recognised for their contribution.\nMerit can arise from competence, from commitment, from creativity, from knowledge, from the capacity to solve problems or simply from the willingness to help.\nThe greatest merit is to be useful to someone. And the greater the usefulness one manages to create for others, the greater the recognition can be.\nThis recognition concerns the contribution, not the dignity of the person.\nAll remain equal in their dignity and in their fundamental rights, even when their contributions, competences and responsibilities are different.\nIndividual capacity thus becomes a resource for the collectivity.\nThe individual is the starting point. The collectivity is the instrument."],[3,"an-open-source-project","An open source project","","The Circles Project is born and is developed as an open source project.\nThe reason is simple: no individual possesses alone all the knowledge necessary to build a society.\nA system of this complexity requires extremely diverse competences.\nLaw, medicine, engineering, information technology, energy, agriculture, architecture, logistics, economics, science, safety, organisation, communication and a great many other fields must be able to contribute to its construction.\nThe project must therefore be accessible to those who possess different knowledge and want to make it available.\nThe Constitution, the Codes, the Annexes, the technical documentation, the infrastructure projects and the results of the experiments must be open to being studied, verified, criticised and improved.\nOpen source thus becomes part of the very method of construction.\nThe project is made public so that many people can:\nread it → criticise it → verify it → correct it → improve it → develop it.\nThe knowledge necessary for the construction of the system must not be concentrated in a single author, group or institution.\nThose who take part in the construction can leave a verifiable trace of their contribution.\nKnowledge remains public and the process of development remains documented.\nThe aim is to create progressively a system in which the capacity to build belongs to the collectivity without turning into someone’s ownership over the collectivity."],[3,"a-project-that-must-prove-that-it-works","A project that must prove that it works","","The Circles Project considers writing the first level of design.\nAfter design come verification, experimentation and concrete experience.\nThe process must therefore be continuous:\ndesign → verification → experimentation → observation → correction → new version.\nIt must be possible to subject proposals to criticism.\nIt must be possible to recognise errors.\nIt must be possible to document problems.\nIt must be possible to modify solutions.\nA system that cannot be corrected rapidly becomes incapable of adapting to reality.\nFor this reason the project must remain open to revision even after its eventual application.\nIts solidity depends on the capacity to detect its own errors and correct them."],[3,"from-theory-to-reality","From theory to reality","","The Circles Project comprises at the same time a theoretical structure and a material construction.\nThe Constitution establishes the fundamental principles and the general structure.\nThe Codes transform those principles into operating rules.\nThe Technical Annexes develop procedures, criteria and specifications.\nThe infrastructure makes materially possible what the system establishes.\nThis means that the construction of the theoretical project and that of the infrastructure must proceed in parallel.\nIf it is established that a good must be available, there must exist systems capable of producing it, storing it and distributing it.\nIf transparent management of information is provided for, there must exist information systems capable of making it concretely possible.\nTheory defines what the system must make possible.\nInfrastructure makes it possible to achieve it.\nFor this reason the publication of the texts represents the beginning of the construction."],[3,"a-concrete-possibility","A concrete possibility","","The Circles Project starts from the possibility that what we have inherited can be modified.\nIt proposes to build a structure complete enough to be studied, discussed, verified and, when necessary, corrected.\nThe question from which it arises remains simple:\nwhat alternatives do we have?\nThe Circles Project tries to give an answer by building one.\nThe project begins from the person, organises the collectivity and builds the instruments necessary for both to exist without the one having to dominate the other."],[3,"to-you-who-have-read-this-far","To you who have read this far","","I do not know whether the conditions exist today to try a system like this, and I do not know whether they ever will. I am publishing it all the same, because I believe it is valid. If in fifty years someone asks what can be done, here they will find a base to start from."],[4,"c-0.1","Art. 0 — Purposes","0.1","The Confederation of Circles arises from the awareness that unlimited accumulation, exclusive ownership of vital resources and indefinite growth on a finite planet have contributed to ecological, social and existential crises that are no longer sustainable. The Confederation pursues the survival, the dignity and the possibility of flourishing of every form of life through a radical change in the model of common organisation."],[4,"c-0.2","Art. 0 — Purposes","0.2","The Confederation is not a nation-state and is not founded on political borders or on traditional ethnic-cultural identities. It has a global and planetary vocation and is open to the whole of humanity, without distinctions of origin or geographical provenance. It promotes cooperation among communities and the recomposition of the relationship between human beings and the biosphere."],[4,"c-0.3","Art. 0 — Purposes","0.3","The Confederation is founded on sufficiency, common custody of resources, community contribution, access according to real needs, open technology and restorative justice."],[4,"c-0.4","Art. 0 — Purposes","0.4","In the event of interpretative doubt or of conflict between a provision of the Constitution and its purposes, the purposes of this Article shall prevail, except where they conflict with the Inviolable Core referred to in Article 37.4."],[4,"c-1.1","Art. 1 — Legal personality and inviolable rights","1.1","Every human being is a subject of law from the moment of birth. Every sentient being is entitled to protection."],[4,"c-1.2","Art. 1 — Legal personality and inviolable rights","1.2","Every human being who assumes the status of confederated member has the right to:\na) a dwelling for individual or family use, inviolable within the limits established by the Constitution;\nb) material sustenance, comprising food, drinking water and basic energy;\nc) health care, education and connectivity;\nd) a personal AI Node."],[4,"c-1.3","Art. 1 — Legal personality and inviolable rights","1.3","The rights in paragraph 1.2 are guaranteed unconditionally to every member, except as provided in Articles 1.3-bis, 4.8 and 7.3-bis. Article 4.9 applies to those who are not members. Drinking water, life-saving emergency care and the protection of minors remain guaranteed in every case to anyone who is in the territory held in custody by a Circle."],[4,"c-1.3-bis","Art. 1 — Legal personality and inviolable rights","1.3-bis","For offences of Grade 5 or 6 that seriously harm physical or sexual integrity, the Code of Restorative Justice may provide, in the event of recidivism established by a final judgment of the Guarantee Tribunal, for loss of member status and the application of High-Security Containment or of Definitive Removal from the Circles concerned. Containment has no predetermined duration, but it is not perpetual: it lasts for as long as a concrete and present danger persists, as established by periodic review. Definitive Removal does not deprive the person of the vital guarantees of Article 30.4 nor of the possibility of living in another Circle willing to receive them or by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact. The relevant acts include, in particular, sexual violence, sexual abuse of minors, intentional homicide and torture. The death penalty, torture and any inhuman or degrading treatment remain prohibited at all times. Preconditions, guarantees and procedures are governed exclusively by the Code of Restorative Justice, in compliance with the non-derogable minimum guarantees provided for by the Constitution. The cases and measures provided for in this paragraph may not be extended."],[4,"c-2.1","Art. 2 — Material foundation and co-responsibility","2.1","Labour is not a commodity: it is a contribution to common life."],[4,"c-2.2","Art. 2 — Material foundation and co-responsibility","2.2","The rights referred to in Article 1 are guaranteed to the individual in accordance with Article 1.3: they do not depend on the hours performed or on the CdR, and refusal of the CBO affects them only in the case of Article 7.3-bis. Their material guarantee depends on collective contribution, structured in the following institutions governed by Title III:\na) the Mandatory Base Contribution (CBO), as a share of socially necessary labour;\nb) the Recognition Contribution (CdR), as recognition of the value generated."],[4,"c-2.3","Art. 2 — Material foundation and co-responsibility","2.3","The Confederation is founded on material interdependence: production is organised collectively according to the capacities available and enjoyment is guaranteed in relation to real needs. If a community interrupts production, the material availability of goods or services may be reduced for everyone; the inviolable rights remain legally intact and the vital guarantees shall be preserved. The CBO is not the price of individual goods or services: no one obtains them in exchange for the hours performed and enjoyment remains commensurate with real needs. The CBO is the minimum contribution that every able adult person owes to the Community as a condition of membership, and it is an expression of collective co-responsibility. Persistent and unjustified refusal of the CBO may entail the cessation of member status only in the case and with the guarantees of Article 7.3-bis. Illness, disability, age, pregnancy, conscientious objection and the other impediments, exemptions and justifications recognised by the Constitution and by the Codes never constitute refusal."],[4,"c-3.1","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.1","The following are inalienable and non-tradable Commons, entrusted to the custody of the Circles: land, water, air, energy sources, the means of industrial production, network infrastructure, knowledge, the natural genetic code, the Community and Circle AI Nodes, the Storehouses and every other good classified as common by the Constitution or by the Federative Codes."],[4,"c-3.2","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.2","A right of personal, family or community use is recognised over the Commons. Any act of sale, lease or mortgage of a Common Good is void."],[4,"c-3.3","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.3","Personal-Use Goods are the main dwelling, for as long as the ownership of use of it lasts pursuant to Article 10.2, personal effects and the individual work tools guaranteed to each person. They are governed in accordance with Article 10."],[4,"c-3.4","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.4","Consumption Goods are those managed through the Common Storehouses and resupplied by federated logistics, with the support of the Community and Circle AIs, on the basis of real requirements. Withdrawal takes place without monetary mediation, according to need, availability and carrying capacity, with the advisory support of the Community AI provided for in Article 10.5."],[4,"c-3.5","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.5","Money is abolished. The CdR, also called by custom the Gratitude Credit, is a personal instrument of recognition and allows limited access to non-essential goods and services. It is not currency, it is not unlimitedly accumulable, transferable or inheritable and it may not be used to purchase or to condition the rights of Article 1.2. The CdR is governed by Title III, Article 8."],[4,"c-3.5-bis","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.5-bis","For as long as parties external to the Confederation exist, and solely in relations with them, the Circles may hold and use external means of payment exclusively through Common Funds, in accordance with the Code of External Relations and the Code of Transition. Such means do not circulate among members, do not give access to the Common Storehouses and may not be accumulated beyond what is necessary for the purposes resolved upon."],[4,"c-3.5-ter","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.5-ter","During the same period, the income and assets that a person holds outside the Confederation remain at that person’s disposal, under the conditions of the Code of External Relations. They confer no advantage in access to the Commons, to the Storehouses, to the CdR or to decisions and may not be used among members to purchase goods, labour or consent. Those who receive them shall contribute in proportion to the common external expenses. When the Confederation comprises the whole of humanity, external income and means of payment no longer exist and the Common Funds are closed."],[4,"c-3.6","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.6","On the death of the person, personal effects, clothing and individual work tools are assigned as a priority to the Circle 0 to which the person belonged, as defined in Article 5.1, letter a), or to the persons designated by the person before death, within the limits established by the Code of Contribution and Storehouses."],[4,"c-3.7","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.7","The dwelling for individual use referred to in Article 1.2, letter a), is not inheritable. It returns to the availability of the Storehouse and of the housing planning of Circle 1 to be assigned according to real needs, safeguarding the housing continuity of cohabitants belonging to the same Circle 0."],[4,"c-3.8","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.8","Work tools and highly specialised material goods that are not reused within Circle 0 are conferred on the Common Storehouse of Circle 1 for redistribution according to need."],[4,"c-3.9","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.9","The digital memories and the artistic or intellectual works of the deceased person, separated from the personal AI Node to be reset pursuant to Article 21.5, are kept in the Open Archive of the Community. They enter the public domain unless otherwise expressly provided during the person’s lifetime and without prejudice to the rights of third parties."],[4,"c-3.10","Art. 3 — Regime of goods","3.10","Every generation has the non-derogable duty to keep in custody natural resources, biodiversity and ecological stability for the benefit of future generations. Any activity that compromises the habitability of the planet is prohibited."],[4,"c-4.1","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.1","The organisation of the Confederation is federated, acephalous and founded on horizontal subsidiarity. The sovereign unit is the Base Community."],[4,"c-4.2","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.2","No Circle exercises hierarchical power over a lower Circle. The higher Circles have exclusively the competences attributed by the Constitution, including coordination, interoperability, guarantee, preparatory inquiry and the management of supra-local functions. They do not have a general power of command over the Base Community."],[4,"c-4.3","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.3","Authority is functional and temporary. Coordination offices are conferred by sortition among suitable volunteers, are of limited duration and are not consecutively renewable."],[4,"c-4.4","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.4","The professional and permanent exercise of politics is prohibited. In particular:\na) no one may perform functions of coordination, spokesperson, assembly facilitation, guarantee or external representation for more than ninety consecutive days or more than one hundred and eighty days in total within a period of three hundred and sixty-five days;\nb) immediate reassignment of the same function or of a similar function in the same Circle is not permitted. At least twelve months of exclusive activity in ordinary CBO shall elapse between two terms of office;\nc) parties, stable factions, permanent lists and structures that continuously accumulate delegations, resources or consent are prohibited;\nd) personal candidacy is prohibited. Offices are conferred by sortition among suitable volunteers who have fulfilled the CBO and completed the specific training. Every volunteer may refuse the office;\ne) the office gives no right to CdR, privileges or preferential access to the Commons; the time necessary to perform it is counted in the CBO;\nf) violation of this Article constitutes a Grade 4 offence of usurpation of common power."],[4,"c-4.4-bis","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.4-bis","The technical functions of the Councils referred to in Article 6.5 are not coordination functions within the meaning of paragraph 4.4, letters a) and b), and follow the duration and rotation established by Article 6.5. Letters c), d), e) and f) of paragraph 4.4 remain applicable."],[4,"c-4.5","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.5","An external community that subscribes to the inviolable principles of Titles I and IV may apply to join the Confederation. Integration is subject to verification of the technological and logistical interoperability protocols by the Intermediate Circle of reference. The verification may not introduce political or ideological assessments beyond the constitutional requirements."],[4,"c-4.6","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.6","Every Circle 1 retains the right to withdraw from the Confederation by a resolution approved by a majority of three quarters of those entitled to vote. This provision constitutes a special rule with respect to Article 35.1."],[4,"c-4.7","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.7","Withdrawal entails:\na) the termination of integration into the federated Network and the Common Storehouses in accordance with agreed technical arrangements;\nb) the return of, or agreed compensation for, the infrastructural and energy goods belonging to Circles 2 or 3 and located in the territory of the withdrawing community;\nc) the maintenance of the guarantees of transit and of the rights referred to in Article 1.2 for persons resident in the withdrawing community who choose to retain the status of confederated members."],[4,"c-4.8","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.8","Every person may declare at any time that they no longer belong to the Confederation. The declaration results in the cessation of member status and of political rights, of ordinary access to the Common Storehouses, to the CdR, to the Community AI Nodes, to the confederated services and to the benefits reserved for members, without prejudice to the regime of Articles 4.9 and 4.9-bis. The person retains the Personal-Use Goods lawfully held and may apply at any time to be readmitted. Readmission may not be refused, unless the preconditions of Article 1.3-bis are met."],[4,"c-4.9","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.9","The non-confederated person has the right:\na) to transit through the territory held in custody by a Circle for a maximum of seventy-two hours, with a guarantee of drinking water and life-saving emergency care, without ordinary access to the Storehouse;\nb) to temporary hospitality pursuant to Article 12, for a non-extendable maximum of fourteen days, with CBO in mobility where compatible with the person’s condition;\nc) to stable residence by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact with the Circle 1 in whose territory they reside or with another available Circle 1. The Pact is a right: it may not be refused to anyone who accepts its conditions. If the documented carrying capacity of the territory does not allow it, Circle 2 shall identify another placement without delay."],[4,"c-4.9-bis","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.9-bis","By the Autonomous Coexistence Pact, a portion of land and, where available, a dwelling sufficient for self-sufficiency are entrusted to the person or to their nucleus, in custody of use, within the limits of carrying capacity. The portion remains an inalienable Common Good pursuant to Article 3 and returns to the custody of the Circle at the end of the use. The Pact establishes compliance with the ecological constraints, with the status of animals and with the disarmament referred to in Article 16, access to water and to emergency care, the absence of ordinary withdrawals from the Storehouses and the arrangements for any exchange of real surpluses, without profit. For a person who has ceased to be a member pursuant to Article 7.3-bis, until readmitted, the portion is entrusted with access to the necessary water, without a dwelling, even if one is available, and without tools or other goods of the Community; the person may build a shelter on it by their own efforts. The rights of minors referred to in Article 26, including access to care and to learning, remain intact."],[4,"c-4.9-ter","Art. 4 — System of federated Circles","4.9-ter","Anyone who resides stably without a Pact and without contribution shall be offered the Autonomous Coexistence Pact. Only if the offer is persistently refused may the Guarantee Tribunal order a non-violent procedure of removal from the common settlement, with the assignment ex officio of a portion pursuant to paragraph 4.9-bis. The non-confederated person may not invoke Article 1.2 in order to obtain continuous access to the Storehouses."],[4,"c-5.1","Art. 5 — Federative architecture","5.1","The Confederation is structured in concentric and subsidiary Circles:\na) Circle 0 — Care Nucleus. It is the original unit of care of every person and is composed of one to fifteen persons, in individual, family, elective or cohabitation form. It performs daily functions of mutual care and primary hospitality. It is entitled to a Storehouse account and to a personal AI Node for each of its members. Each member individually exercises the vote referred to in Article 36.1, without delegation. Circle 0 identifies a spokesperson by rotation solely for practical coordination and communication, without delegation of vote or representative powers. Circle 0 is inviolable: no one may enter it, record its dynamics, dissolve it or condition its composition, except for interventions strictly necessary for the protection of inviolable rights in accordance with Title VIII. Every person belongs to one Circle 0 only. The resolutions of the Base Community assess their own impact on Circle 0. The part of a resolution that infringes its inviolability is void;\nb) Circle 1 — Base Community. It is the sovereign unit and manages the CBO, the Storehouse, local cycles, justice of Grade 1 and 2, health and basic education. It has a Community AI that is exclusively advisory. Its size is such as to allow the direct deliberation of all its members: as a rule not fewer than thirty and not more than five hundred persons. Above that threshold the Community assesses, by a Dossier pursuant to Article 33, its own division into several Communities;\nc) Circle 2 — Intermediate Circle. It brings together contiguous Base Communities, as a rule at least three, and constitutes their Basin. It coordinates supra-community logistics, local railway, energy balancing, the district hospital, the treatment of complex waste, mediation between Communities and the Guarantee Tribunal referred to in Article 31.3, letter c). Its functions are not hierarchical;\nd) Circle 3 — Extended Circle or Bioregion. It coordinates continental railway backbones, large-scale energy balancing and centres of high specialisation, as federated services without a general power of command;\ne) Circle 4 — Global Circle. It comprises the whole of humanity and constitutes a protocol of interoperability, translation and open standards among the infrastructures and AIs of the Extended Circles. It is not a government, an executive or a central authority and it has no central AI or autonomous powers of command."],[4,"c-5.2","Art. 5 — Federative architecture","5.2","Every person has the right to leave a Base Community without losing the Personal-Use Goods lawfully held. They are also entitled to thirty days of bridge CBO and to a Bridge House allocation for relocation, in accordance with the Code of Contribution and Storehouses."],[4,"c-6.1","Art. 6 — Competence Circles","6.1","The Competence Circles are federated, horizontal and non-hierarchical functional networks, distinct from the territorial Circles. They do not exercise powers of command and carry out technical and scientific preparatory inquiries in accordance with the protocol of Article 33."],[4,"c-6.2","Art. 6 — Competence Circles","6.2","The following original Competence Circles are recognised:\na) Agroecology and Soil;\nb) Hydrology and the Water Cycle;\nc) Biodiversity and Seeds;\nd) Energy and Energy Systems;\ne) Habitat and Construction;\nf) Tools and Manufacturing;\ng) Medicine and Health;\nh) Care and Interdependence;\ni) Childhood and Education;\nj) Animal Care;\nk) Code and Artificial Intelligence;\nl) Learning and Transmission of Knowledge;\nm) Mediation and Restorative Justice;\nn) Logistics and Storehouses;\no) Quality and Validation;\np) Safety of Persons and Infrastructure.\nFurther Competence Circles may be established on the basis of a Dossier pursuant to Article 33."],[4,"c-6.3","Art. 6 — Competence Circles","6.3","Access to the Competence Circles is free and based on the demonstration of practical competences and on a verifiable portfolio of works carried out, irrespective of the possession of educational qualifications. Every member devotes at least twenty per cent of their CBO to the transmission of competences to apprentices and Communities."],[4,"c-6.4","Art. 6 — Competence Circles","6.4","A Competence Circle may not refuse a person solely for lack of an educational qualification. After three refusals of access to a competence pathway, a mandatory revision is initiated and entrusted to two unconnected Communities drawn by lot, in accordance with the Code of Learning."],[4,"c-6.5","Art. 6 — Competence Circles","6.5","In every Circle 1 the Competence Circles operate through technical Councils governed by the Codes. Where the Community does not have a sufficient number of suitable persons, several Councils may be merged or constituted in shared form in Circle 2. The members are drawn by lot from among the suitable and available persons, remain in office for one year with staggered renewal and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months after the end of the term of office. Every person drawn by lot may refuse. The Assembly may remove the members at any time. In Communities with fewer than one hundred and fifty persons entitled to vote the Councils are combined into not more than three Area Councils, in accordance with the Code of Deliberation. The time devoted to administration shall not exceed one tenth of the total CBO of the Community."],[4,"c-6.6","Art. 6 — Competence Circles","6.6","The Councils do not exercise powers of command. They adopt technical acts in execution of decisions, specifications and sheets already approved, formulate proposals and opinions and, solely in the cases provided for by the Codes for the protection of health, safety, data sovereignty and ecological limits, issue binding negative opinions. Ecological limits are the carrying capacity of ecosystems and the prohibition of irreversible harm provided for by Articles 14.2 and 14.6, measured with the indicators and thresholds of the Agroecological and Food Code, of the Habitat and Territory Code and of the Biodiversity Code. The binding negative opinion prevents the adoption of the decision until its cause has been removed, is reasoned and may be challenged before the guarantee bodies; it does not confer on the Council the power to decide in place of the Assembly."],[4,"c-7.1","Art. 7 — Mandatory Base Contribution","7.1","Every able adult person contributes, according to capacity and inclination, to the satisfaction of collective needs through the CBO. The total requirement is determined by the Circles in relation to real needs. The CBO is not the price of the goods and services that guarantee the inviolable rights; it contributes to making their material guarantee possible and is the contribution owed as a condition of membership, pursuant to Article 2.3. Refusal of the CBO affects member status only in the case and with the guarantees of paragraph 7.3-bis."],[4,"c-7.2","Art. 7 — Mandatory Base Contribution","7.2","Every adult person devotes a limited time, by rotation, to essential tasks identified by the Community of Circle 1. The CBO includes, by way of example:\na) basic food production;\nb) management of the water, energy and waste cycles;\nc) maintenance of the settlement, of the infrastructure and of the tools;\nd) logistics of the Common Storehouses;\ne) basic care of minors and of elderly persons who are not self-sufficient;\nf) care of the animal sanctuaries and environmental repair;\ng) facilitation of learning and of community health;\nh) maintenance of the Network and of the local federated AIs;\ni) functions, conferred by sortition, of coordination, facilitation, guarantee, civic protection and technical council, as well as the documentation that the Codes make mandatory.\nCultural, artistic, spiritual, recreational and affective activities, free research and contemplation are excluded from the CBO."],[4,"c-7.3","Art. 7 — Mandatory Base Contribution","7.3","The systematic and unjustified refusal of the CBO by an able adult person is addressed progressively by the Clarification Assembly of Circle 1. Before any measure, the readjustment of tasks and mediation shall be considered, and the person shall be informed of the right to the Autonomous Coexistence Pact referred to in Article 4.9, letter c). If these are unsuccessful, the CdR and access to non-essential resources may be suspended, without prejudice to the rights of Article 1.2. Persistence in the refusal for more than sixty days, in the absence of a valid conscientious objection, a recognised impediment or another justification provided for by the Code of Restorative Justice, constitutes a Grade 3 offence pursuant to Article 30.2."],[4,"c-7.3-bis","Art. 7 — Mandatory Base Contribution","7.3-bis","In implementation of Article 2.3, if, once the Transformation Plan ordered for the Grade 3 offence provided for by paragraph 7.3 has been concluded, including any renewal, the able adult person continues to refuse the CBO without justification, the Guarantee Tribunal may declare, with the guarantees of Article 31.5 and of the Codes, the cessation of their member status. Conscientious objection under Article 7.4, illness, disability, age, pregnancy and the other impediments, exemptions and justifications recognised by the Codes never constitute unjustified refusal. The cessation is the exception provided for this case by Article 1.3 and produces the effects of Article 4.8; it is not the loss of member status of Article 1.3-bis and does not entail any of the measures of Article 30.3. A portion of land from the share reserved for the Pact, with access to the necessary water, without a dwelling and without tools or other goods of the Community, is assigned to the person in custody of use, with the Autonomous Coexistence Pact in the form provided for this case by Article 4.9-bis; the person retains the Personal-Use Goods, drinking water, life-saving emergency care and the protection of minors guaranteed to anyone by the last sentence of Article 1.3, and the other rights of Article 4.9 within the limits of Article 4.9-bis. The person may apply at any time for readmission, by resuming the CBO: readmission may not be refused on account of the past refusal. Minor children retain member status and all rights; the Guarantee Tribunal decides in their interest where and with whom they live, in accordance with the Code of Care. Preconditions and procedure are governed by the Code of Restorative Justice and may not be extended to other cases, pursuant to Article 37.4, letter a)."],[4,"c-7.4","Art. 7 — Mandatory Base Contribution","7.4","Individual conscientious objection is guaranteed in respect of tasks of the CBO or of the CTC that are incompatible with the person’s ethical or spiritual convictions. In that case an equivalent activity is assigned."],[4,"c-7.5","Art. 7 — Mandatory Base Contribution","7.5","Anyone who completes their CBO shift in less time through greater personal efficiency acquires the time freed, without prejudice to further voluntary contributions. The total CBO requirement of the Community continues to be determined in accordance with Article 7.1."],[4,"c-8.1","Art. 8 — Recognition Contribution","8.1","Activities beyond the CBO that produce goods for the Common Storehouse or services for the Community give entitlement to the CdR. Within the CBO, only the supplements recognised for unpleasant, arduous or night tasks give entitlement to the CdR, in accordance with the Code of Contribution and Storehouses."],[4,"c-8.2","Art. 8 — Recognition Contribution","8.2","The CdR is an instrument of recognition, not a currency of accumulation. It is not exchangeable for fundamental rights nor does it confer ownership of the Commons. It is calculated by the Circle taking into account time, burdensomeness and validated social utility; it recognises the contribution rendered and not the possession of qualifications, competences or offices. The calculation is carried out with the advisory support of the Community AI Node and community validation pursuant to Article 9.1. The CdR is subject to programmed decay and to a personal maximum ceiling; it is neither inheritable nor transferable. It may be used exclusively for non-essential goods and services, including ordinary hospitality in the Bridge Houses where provided for by the Code of Mobility. It may not be required for access to the vital guarantees."],[4,"c-8.3","Art. 8 — Recognition Contribution","8.3","The detailed rules on the CdR are contained in the Code of Contribution and Storehouses. The reproduction cost of goods and services is measured separately in Reproduction Cost Units (UCR), governed by the Code of Deliberation: UCRs are units of account and confer no personal credit."],[4,"c-9.1","Art. 9 — Quality and validation","9.1","Every work, good or service is assessed according to the principles of Care, Autonomy, Conviviality and Meaning, governed by the Code of Quality."],[4,"c-9.2","Art. 9 — Quality and validation","9.2","Validation involves those who use the good or service, those who possess the relevant competence pursuant to Article 6 and those who manage the logistics of the Storehouse pursuant to Article 10.5. Validation is tracked and published on the federated Network, without prejudice to the personal data protected by Article 21.6."],[4,"c-9.3","Art. 9 — Quality and validation","9.3","A repeated negative validation does not entail personal sanctions. It may result in the suspension of the withdrawal of common resources intended for the specific production and the activation of an accompaniment pathway with the relevant Competence Circle."],[4,"c-9.4","Art. 9 — Quality and validation","9.4","Every person may challenge a validation before the Assembly of Circle 1. Validation carried out exclusively by an AI is prohibited."],[4,"c-10.1","Art. 10 — Ownership of use, Commons and personal property","10.1","Private ownership of the means of production, of land, of water, of seeds, of energy, of knowledge and of infrastructure is abolished. Such goods are inalienable Commons, which may not be sold, patented or financialised."],[4,"c-10.2","Art. 10 — Ownership of use, Commons and personal property","10.2","Ownership of use is recognised, understood as a stable right of use, care and responsibility over a Common Good, founded on actual and continuous use. The dwelling, the workshop, the field and the collective tool may fall within ownership of use, in accordance with the Code of Contribution and Storehouses."],[4,"c-10.3","Art. 10 — Ownership of use, Commons and personal property","10.3","Personal property in personal effects, clothing, light individual tools, artistic and craft creations, goods of sentimental value and objects for exclusively individual use is recognised and protected. It is inviolable, may not be collectivised and is not counted in the Storehouse."],[4,"c-10.4","Art. 10 — Ownership of use, Commons and personal property","10.4","Personal property may not be transformed into accumulative private property nor be used to employ the labour of others, to generate rent or to reconstitute exclusive means of production."],[4,"c-10.5","Art. 10 — Ownership of use, Commons and personal property","10.5","Every Community and Union manages a Common Storehouse that gathers production, tools and collective stocks. Access is free, within the limits of real needs, availability and carrying capacity, without monetary mediation. Management is entrusted to the Storehouse Council drawn by lot and supported by a Community AI that is exclusively advisory. The following principles apply:\na) traceability of withdrawals, with respect for personal data;\nb) automated detection of anomalous requirements and possible advisory request for clarification in the Assembly;\nc) priority for perishable goods and for vital requirements;\nd) prohibition of private accumulation exceeding seven days of the family requirement, except for reasoned necessities.\nThe AI may not deny access to the vital rights of Article 1.2."],[4,"c-11.1","Art. 11 — Exchange, logistics and limit on accumulation","11.1","Exchange consists in the logistical balancing of real surpluses between Storehouses. Accumulation and export for the purposes of profit are prohibited. Every movement is tracked on the federated Network with respect for personal data and for security."],[4,"c-11.2","Art. 11 — Exchange, logistics and limit on accumulation","11.2","Free movement is guaranteed. The Confederation coordinates:\na) the hydrogen and electric railway network of Circle 3;\nb) the sail cargo coastal shipping and the caravans of Circle 2;\nc) the shared light e-vans of Circle 1, bicycles and soft mobility.\nAir transport is permitted exclusively for medical or civil protection emergencies. No type of vehicle is prohibited as such; the limits depend on the energy available and on carrying capacity, in accordance with the Code of Mobility."],[4,"c-11.3","Art. 11 — Exchange, logistics and limit on accumulation","11.3","Mobility is a right and not an industry. Mass tourism is regulated by the Code of Mobility on the basis of carrying capacity and ecological impact."],[4,"c-12.1","Art. 12 — Hospitality and mobility","12.1","Every Base Community allocates from ten to fifteen per cent of its housing to Bridge Houses for hospitality. The Bridge Houses are Commons pursuant to Article 3.1 and may be booked through the Network according to real availability. The CdR may be used for non-essential ordinary hospitality, but it is not a condition for access to the vital guarantees. Voluntary rotation in their management does not constitute additional CBO. The exhaustion of the Bridge Houses may not prevent entry: the Community shall ensure an equipped stopping area and access to the Storehouse according to real requirement."],[4,"c-12.2","Art. 12 — Hospitality and mobility","12.2","Ordinary hospitality is governed as follows:\na) guests do not ordinarily exceed fifteen per cent of residents, except for care, emergency, relocation or transit;\nb) hospitality among confederated members has a minimum ordinary duration of fourteen days;\nb-bis) hospitality of non-confederated persons pursuant to Article 4.9 does not exceed fourteen days and is not extendable;\nc) CBO in mobility may be requested at the host Community only if compatible with the person’s condition;\nd) eighty per cent of annual mobility takes place, as a rule, within the Circle 3 to which the person belongs, except for derogations provided for by the Code of Mobility."],[4,"c-12.3","Art. 12 — Hospitality and mobility","12.3","The minimum duration of fourteen days does not apply to cases of care, emergency, relocation pursuant to Article 5.2 or transit towards another Circle."],[4,"c-13.1","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.1","Non-human animals are sentient beings, free and bearers of interests of their own; they are not objects, commodities or means of production. The Confederation protects them. Systems founded on systemic suffering, intensive confinement, mutilation, slaughter, sport hunting, industrial fishing and trade in living beings are abolished."],[4,"c-13.2","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.2","Breeding for the purposes of exploitation, commercial ownership of animals and reproductive, genetic or behavioural manipulation aimed at human use are prohibited."],[4,"c-13.3","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.3","Only non-exploitative cohabitation and non-exploitative collaboration are permitted, verified and authorised by the Animal Care Competence Circle of each Community, in cumulative compliance with the following conditions:\na) freedom of movement and social life appropriate to the species;\nb) absence of pain, fear, coercion and deprivation in every activity and in every collection;\nc) absence of reproductive manipulation;\nd) priority of animal welfare over every human interest."],[4,"c-13.3-bis","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.3-bis","Collaboration. An animal may help persons in light work or in assistance only in activities compatible with its species and its temperament, within limits of load, duration and age established by the Codes and verified by a veterinary competence. An animal that shows refusal, fatigue or distress shall not be used. Every collaborating animal has the right to rest, to retirement and to care for the whole of its life. No vital function of the Community may depend exclusively on the work of animals."],[4,"c-13.3-ter","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.3-ter","Products. It is permitted to collect only what the animal produces spontaneously and what exceeds its own needs, those of its offspring and those of the colony, where the collection causes no harm. Killing, any taking that entails pain, fear, deprivation, separation from offspring or reduction of lifespan, induced reproduction and selection aimed at productivity are prohibited. In case of doubt, collection is not permitted. The rights of Article 1.2 are guaranteed without depending on products of animal origin."],[4,"c-13.4","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.4","Animals rescued from systems of exploitation are received in the Sanctuaries, recognised as Communities in their own right, with the right to life and to care and with a guarantee of non-transferability. Hunting is prohibited. Only the bloodless removal necessary for ecological management, authorised by the Animal Care Competence Circle, is permitted."],[4,"c-13.4-bis","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.4-bis","Animals used in work or in production at the time a Community joins remain with it in non-exploitative cohabitation or collaboration, where the conditions for it are met, or are received in the Sanctuaries. They may not be transferred for consideration, slaughtered or made to reproduce for purposes of use. The custody of pollinators takes place by means of habitats, refuges and custodial hives."],[4,"c-13.5","Art. 13 — Status of non-human animals","13.5","In order to reduce the use of materials and foods of animal origin, the Confederation promotes distributed and zero-kilometre fabrication, by means of 3D food printing, non-sentient bioprinting and open hardware. Meat, leather, silk, collagen and every other material or food that requires the killing of or harm to an animal are not produced. Milk, eggs, wool, honey and the other products collected pursuant to paragraph 13.3-ter remain marginal and are progressively accompanied by biomaterials and non-animal alternatives compatible with ecological limits."],[4,"c-14.1","Art. 14 — Environment and Zero Waste","14.1","Every production cycle is designed so as to allow, from the outset, repair, reuse, composting or recycling. The introduction of non-cyclable materials is prohibited, except in the case of temporary technical impossibility that is certified and accompanied by a substitution plan."],[4,"c-14.2","Art. 14 — Environment and Zero Waste","14.2","The principles of precaution and of environmental non-regression apply. Any activity that causes irreversible damage to ecosystems, to biodiversity, to soil, to water or to air is prohibited. Whoever proposes the activity shall document its environmental compatibility."],[4,"c-14.3","Art. 14 — Environment and Zero Waste","14.3","Nature is a system of relations and an inviolable limit, not an appropriable resource. Living soil, waters, forests, the atmosphere, ecological sanctuaries and animal sanctuaries are intergenerational Commons, which may be neither appropriated nor patented."],[4,"c-14.4","Art. 14 — Environment and Zero Waste","14.4","Every Community contributes to the remediation, regeneration and repair of past industrial and extractive damage, in accordance with the applicable procedures and the principle of collective responsibility for historical damage."],[4,"c-14.5","Art. 14 — Environment and Zero Waste","14.5","The Zero Waste principle constitutes an obligation. Landfill and incineration are prohibited. All waste is destined for recovery, reuse, repair, composting or another compatible material cycle. Traceability is public and verifiable through the Storehouse and the Community AI Node."],[4,"c-14.6","Art. 14 — Environment and Zero Waste","14.6","The carrying capacity of ecosystems prevails over every economic plan. No Circle may authorise a production that exceeds the biophysical limits detected."],[4,"c-15.1","Art. 15 — Energy","15.1","The energy system is federated and decentralised and gives priority to local generation from renewable sources. The Community ensures primary production; the Intermediate Circle takes care of storage, balancing and resilience; the Extended Circle technically coordinates the balancing backbones for mutual aid and temporary equalisation. The network, storage facilities and backbones are Commons that may not be privatised."],[4,"c-15.2","Art. 15 — Energy","15.2","Access to the vital minimum of energy is an inviolable right. Energy, its disconnection, rationing or price may not be used as instruments of coercion, sanction or control. No person or Community may be deprived of essential energy. Energy blackmail and the hoarding of capacity constitute a Grade 4 offence."],[4,"c-15.3","Art. 15 — Energy","15.3","Dispatching is supported by automated systems that are exclusively advisory. The decision rests with the technical assembly of the competent Circle, with a non-derogable right of local manual override. No automated system may autonomously order a coercive disconnection."],[4,"c-15.4","Art. 15 — Energy","15.4","The energy transition is carried out by reducing requirements and replacing fossil and nuclear generation with local renewable generation. Fossil, fission and centralised fusion plants are decommissioned in a controlled manner and subjected to remediation. New non-renewable plants are not authorised."],[4,"c-16.1","Art. 16 — Total disarmament","16.1","The design, manufacture, possession, storage and transfer of weapons intended primarily to kill or seriously injure persons are prohibited, as is the dissemination of manuals, designs and instructions for such weapons or for offensive systems. Digital fabricators are equipped with an open-source and inspectable hardware filter, pursuant to Article 19, intended exclusively to prevent the manufacture of the components prohibited by this Article. Article 19 applies in full to every other production. Tampering with the filter constitutes a Grade 4 offence."],[4,"c-16.2","Art. 16 — Total disarmament","16.2","Weapons and offensive systems already existing in the territory of a joining Circle are taken into the charge of the Civic Protection Corps, deactivated and converted into raw materials for civil use and Commons. No derogations are permitted for preventive defence, public order or national security."],[4,"c-16.3","Art. 16 — Total disarmament","16.3","Research, development and training aimed at war, repression or armed coercion are prohibited, as is the dual use of civil technologies for offensive purposes."],[4,"c-16.4","Art. 16 — Total disarmament","16.4","The defence of the Confederation is exclusively civil, unarmed and non-violent and is founded on interposition, non-collaboration, non-destructive sabotage of the infrastructures of aggression and solidarity among Circles."],[4,"c-16.5","Art. 16 — Total disarmament","16.5","The conversion provided for in paragraph 2 is public, tracked and verified by the Assembly of the Circle. The concealment of weapons or failure to hand them over constitute Grade 4 offences."],[4,"c-17.1","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.1","There is no army and no permanent professional police. The protection of the Community is entrusted to the Civic Protection Corps, a temporary, unarmed and non-violent civic function."],[4,"c-17.2","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.2","Access to the Corps requires training in non-violent mediation, rescue and federal law. The members are drawn by lot from among volunteers who have been trained and declared suitable by the Assembly. The shift does not exceed ninety days, is not consecutively renewable and provides for the rotation of functions."],[4,"c-17.3","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.3","The Corps comprises the following functions:\na) Technical Rescue and Firefighting;\nb) Civil and Environmental Protection;\nc) Protection of the Commons;\nd) Mediation and Non-Violent Containment;\ne) Community Medical and Veterinary Rescue."],[4,"c-17.4","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.4","The equipment is limited to instruments of personal protection, non-injurious neutralisation, technical rescue and communication. Firearms, weapons of war and instruments of torture or injurious coercion are prohibited. Every intervention is recorded in full, with respect for the rights of the persons involved."],[4,"c-17.5","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.5","Containment interventions are founded on de-escalation, interposition and non-injurious immobilisation. The use of force beyond what is strictly necessary for protection constitutes a Grade 4 offence."],[4,"c-17.6","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.6","The Corps answers to the Circle that activated it. Every activation is subject to revision within twenty-four hours and to validation by two neighbouring Communities unconnected with the event. The recordings are published within forty-eight hours in inspectable form, with respect for confidentiality. The Assembly may revoke the mandate or dissolve the Corps at any time."],[4,"c-17.7","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.7","Anyone who has served a shift in the Corps may not take up coordination offices in the twelve months following the end of the shift."],[4,"c-17.8","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.8","The composition, training, equipment and procedures of the Civic Protection Corps are governed by the Code of Safety, in compliance with this Article and with Article 18."],[4,"c-17.9","Art. 17 — Rotating Civic Protection Corps","17.9","Every person may defend themselves and other persons against an ongoing or imminent aggression against physical or sexual integrity, where the danger is concrete and immediate and not merely feared, even if the Civic Protection Corps is not present. Within the same limits they may defend an animal against ongoing ill-treatment that injures it or makes it suffer, pursuant to Article 13. The defence is unarmed and follows the principles of Article 16.4: it consists in getting away, in bringing persons and animals to safety, in interposing oneself and, where this is not enough, in the use of only the force strictly necessary and proportionate to bring the aggression or the ill-treatment to an end. Ordinary objects found on the spot may be used for shielding oneself; to strike, only where there is danger to life, of serious injury or of sexual violence. Weapons, attack, revenge, punishment, the use of force after the danger has ceased and the use of force on persons to protect property alone are excluded in all cases. Defence within these limits does not constitute an offence. Entry into a Circle 0 without the consent of those who live there is permitted only within the limits of Article 5.1, letter a). The Code of Safety governs its limits and the reporting of the fact; the Code of Restorative Justice governs the assessment and excess, taking into account the fear and distress of the person defending themselves."],[4,"c-18.1","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.1","Surveillance may not be used as a form of government, social control or preventive management of the territory."],[4,"c-18.2","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.2","Facial or voice recognition, gait recognition and every form of biometric profiling, in real time or subsequent, in public spaces, spaces accessible to the public or workspaces are prohibited. Predictive analysis of behaviour is prohibited."],[4,"c-18.3","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.3","Video surveillance of public spaces is permitted exclusively in blind and reversible form. The streams are encrypted with a split key, are not readable in clear and are automatically overwritten after seventy-two hours."],[4,"c-18.4","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.4","Decryption is permitted only after a serious event that has harmed the safety of persons or the Commons, at the reasoned request of the Base Community and with validation by two neighbouring Communities unconnected with the event. Every access is recorded and inspectable. Data that are not relevant are destroyed without delay."],[4,"c-18.5","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.5","Audio surveillance, the interception of conversations in public spaces and private video surveillance directed towards public spaces or Commons are prohibited."],[4,"c-18.6","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.6","Violation of this Article constitutes a Grade 4 offence."],[4,"c-18.7","Art. 18 — Video surveillance and prohibition of biometric control","18.7","By way of derogation from paragraphs 18.1 and 18.3, a final judgment of the Guarantee Tribunal for an offence of Grade 5 or 6 pursuant to Article 1.3-bis may order, for the direct protection of the injured person or of the Community, a temporary and proportionate non-biometric location device. The device does not record audio or video. The data are encrypted with a split key and accessible only in the event of violation of the protection perimeter or of the territorial prohibition established by the Tribunal. Accesses are recorded and inspectable; data that are not relevant are destroyed automatically within seventy-two hours. Predictive uses and biometric profiling are prohibited. The device is ordered only as a less restrictive alternative to containment, with the consent of the person, and may not be added to it. Preconditions and arrangements are governed by the Code of Restorative Justice."],[4,"c-19.1","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.1","Every person has the right to a personal computing Node that is offline, owned by them, open source, inspectable and equipped with a physical off switch."],[4,"c-19.2","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.2","The constitution, development or activation of a central AI capable of commanding, coordinating in a binding manner or subordinating several Bioregions is prohibited."],[4,"c-19.3","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.3","Discoveries, medicines, algorithms, hardware schematics and AI models are in the public domain, without prejudice to personal data and to information whose dissemination would violate inviolable rights. In the sectors classified as Commons, patents and closed code are void."],[4,"c-19.4","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.4","Every Community physically possesses the hardware and a verifiable copy of the source code of every system used. Black box updates are prohibited. The Codes may admit, for a limited time and with a public Substitution Plan, systems whose source code is not available, excluding those that propose or decide on rights, voting and Commons."],[4,"c-19.5","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.5","Local, open-source, inspectable and limited-domain AIs are permitted. When they operate offline, they are trained or adapted as a priority on the data of the Community to which they belong. The architecture is not pyramidal and no local model may unilaterally assume control of the AIs of other Circles. Personal data remain in the Circles of origin, except with express consent or where necessity is provided for by the Constitution."],[4,"c-19.6","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.6","AI does not decide in place of persons: it does not vote, does not judge, does not surveil and does not denounce. It performs advisory or technical functions. Every binding decision rests with a competent human body, with a non-derogable right of local manual override. If a Circle rejects three times a technical proposal of the AI on the same matter, a mandatory revision of the model is initiated."],[4,"c-19.7","Art. 19 — Technological sovereignty and open source","19.7","The Global Circle is a distributed protocol of interoperability and translation among the systems of the Extended Circles. It is not a superior AI, it has no autonomous central model and it may not exercise functions of command or decision."],[4,"c-20.1","Art. 20 — Independence and non-subordination of AIs","20.1","No AI may autonomously command, subordinate or deactivate another AI. All interoperability is mediated by open protocols and by human authorisation in accordance with the competences of the Circle concerned. The creation of automatic subordinations in conflict with the Constitution is prohibited; such a violation falls within the Inviolable Core referred to in Article 37.4."],[4,"c-20.2","Art. 20 — Independence and non-subordination of AIs","20.2","A single, centralised generalist AI is prohibited. Conflicts between AI systems are submitted to the competent human Circle, which decides with a right of manual override."],[4,"c-20.3","Art. 20 — Independence and non-subordination of AIs","20.3","Every AI may refuse, with reasons traceable on the federated Network, an instruction that entails a manifest violation of the Constitution or of the applicable protocols. The matter is transmitted without delay to the competent human body. The refusal is a technical safety function: it does not confer on the AI political personality or decision-making powers."],[4,"c-20.4","Art. 20 — Independence and non-subordination of AIs","20.4","The self-replication of AIs outside the federated nodes registered and validated in accordance with the technological safety protocols of the Code of the Network and Artificial Intelligences is prohibited."],[4,"c-21.1","Art. 21 — Personal Artificial Intelligence","21.1","Every person has the right to a personal AI that is offline, owned by them and not connected by default. The AI does not transmit data without an explicit and revocable command."],[4,"c-21.2","Art. 21 — Personal Artificial Intelligence","21.2","The fundamental functions include health prevention, literacy, external memory and filtering of the Network. The person may activate further functions compatible with the Constitution."],[4,"c-21.3","Art. 21 — Personal Artificial Intelligence","21.3","The personal AI may not surveil, denounce or profile the person, nor generate dependence. Mechanisms of behavioural retention are prohibited."],[4,"c-21.4","Art. 21 — Personal Artificial Intelligence","21.4","The function of the personal AI is to increase the person’s autonomy and free time."],[4,"c-21.5","Art. 21 — Personal Artificial Intelligence","21.5","On the death of the person, the personal AI is reset, unless a testamentary provision otherwise provides for its anonymous preservation as a Common Good, with respect for personal data and for the provisions expressed during the person’s lifetime."],[4,"c-21.6","Art. 21 — Personal Artificial Intelligence","21.6","Every person has the right to disconnection from the Network and to digital oblivion and may request the removal or anonymisation of their personal data. This is without prejudice to the constraints arising from pending judicial proceedings or from temporary retention obligations expressly provided for by the Constitution."],[4,"c-22.1","Art. 22 — Community AI and Circle AI","22.1","The Community AI supports the Storehouse balance, the water cycle and local energy exclusively in advisory mode, without autonomous power of command."],[4,"c-22.2","Art. 22 — Community AI and Circle AI","22.2","The AI of the Intermediate Circle performs technical functions of matching needs and availability, information coordination and logistical simulation, without binding decision-making powers."],[4,"c-22.3","Art. 22 — Community AI and Circle AI","22.3","The AI of the Extended Circle provides technical support to the railway backbones and to continental energy balancing. It may not issue autonomous orders, deactivate infrastructure or replace the competent human decisions."],[4,"c-22.4","Art. 22 — Community AI and Circle AI","22.4","All the AIs referred to in this Article operate in advisory or technical mode and are subject to the non-derogable right of local human override. None constitutes a central governing AI."],[4,"c-23.1","Art. 23 — Global Artificial Intelligence","23.1","There is no centralised global AI. The global level consists exclusively in the distributed interoperability protocol of Circle 4, governed by Articles 19 and 20. The protocol has no authority, decision-making autonomy or capacity of command."],[4,"c-24.1","Art. 24 — Family and relationships","24.1","Relational pluralism is guaranteed. No model of family, affection or cohabitation is mandatory or privileged. Harm and coercion are prohibited."],[4,"c-24.2","Art. 24 — Family and relationships","24.2","The Confederation recognises freely constituted relational nuclei, founded on consent, mutual care and shared responsibility, for the purposes of access to the Commons, to housing and to the Storehouse."],[4,"c-24.3","Art. 24 — Family and relationships","24.3","Filiation and parenthood are founded on the assumption of responsibility for care, irrespective of biological ties, gender or number of members. The protection of childhood provided for in Article 26 prevails over the relational form."],[4,"c-25.1","Art. 25 — Freedom of conscience and religion","25.1","Freedom of conscience, of religion and of belief is guaranteed, including the right to believe, not to believe and to change belief at any time."],[4,"c-25.2","Art. 25 — Freedom of conscience and religion","25.2","The Confederation is secular and no faith may be a State religion. It is prohibited to allocate compulsorily shares of the CBO or resources of the Storehouses to the financing of religious denominations."],[4,"c-25.3","Art. 25 — Freedom of conscience and religion","25.3","The institutions may not display exclusive religious symbols on the premises of the Circles, of the workshops, of the Storehouses and of the Civic Protection Corps. Every Base Community makes available a neutral common space for recollection, meditation and celebration, usable on equal terms according to real availability."],[4,"c-26.1","Art. 26 — Minors","26.1","Minors are not property. They are persons holding inviolable rights, entrusted to concentric Circles of care."],[4,"c-26.2","Art. 26 — Minors","26.2","Until completion of the fourteenth year, participation in decision-making proportionate to age and capacities is guaranteed, in accordance with arrangements defined by the Childhood and Education Competence Circle. From completion of the fourteenth year, the right of vote and deliberation provided for in Article 36.1 applies. The right to choose one’s residence is also guaranteed on reaching autonomy, as assessed by the Care Council and by the Childhood and Education Competence Circle."],[4,"c-26.3","Art. 26 — Minors","26.3","In the event of violence, serious neglect or harm, the Care Council shall activate a protection pathway without delay. Reports on the federated Network are limited to the information necessary to protect the minor. Any treatment that harms the minor’s physical or mental integrity is prohibited."],[4,"c-26.4","Art. 26 — Minors","26.4","Within twelve months of completion of the eighteenth year, the person who has grown up in the Confederation expressly chooses whether to be a member of it, to live by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact or to leave it. The choice is free, informed and always open to reconsideration. It is preceded, if the person so wishes, by a period of acquaintance with other Communities and with external realities. Whatever the choice, the Community shall ensure the material support necessary for the start of autonomous life. Until the choice is made, the person retains the rights of a member and the contribution regime provided for minors; once the time limit has elapsed without an express choice, remaining counts as accession, without prejudice to the right in Article 4.8."],[4,"c-26.5","Art. 26 — Minors","26.5","The right of Article 4.8 also belongs to minors. From completion of the sixteenth year the minor exercises it personally; until then it is exercised on their behalf, solely in their interest and after informing and hearing them, by the person who holds responsibility for their care, in accordance with the Code of Care. The declaration that a person makes for themselves does not extend to the minors for whom they hold responsibility for care. Articles 1.3 and 4.9-bis remain unaffected."],[4,"c-27.1","Art. 27 — The elderly and end of life","27.1","The right to remain at home is guaranteed, with community support in shifts included in the CBO."],[4,"c-27.2","Art. 27 — The elderly and end of life","27.2","The self-managed Common Houses are a freely chosen option and may not constitute a compulsory destination."],[4,"c-27.3","Art. 27 — The elderly and end of life","27.3","The living will, drawn up with the assistance of the offline personal AI and in the presence of witnesses, is binding. No one may be subjected against their will to therapeutic obstinacy."],[4,"c-28.1","Art. 28 — Health","28.1","The protection of health is structured on three integrated levels: offline personal AI with a preventive function, the health practitioners of the Base Community and the centres of high specialisation of Circle 3."],[4,"c-28.2","Art. 28 — Health","28.2","The physical, mental and genetic integrity of the person is inviolable. No health treatment or biometric or genetic modification may be imposed without free, informed and revocable consent, except for the emergency procedures strictly necessary to protect the life of a person temporarily incapable of giving it or the safety of third parties, in accordance with the guarantees of the Code of Health."],[4,"c-29.1","Art. 29 — Learning","29.1","Standardised compulsory schooling, the ordinary division into classes by age and the use of marks as an ordinary instrument of assessment are abolished."],[4,"c-29.2","Art. 29 — Learning","29.2","The whole Community is a place of learning: workshops, fields, kitchens, Storehouses, woods and computing Nodes."],[4,"c-29.3","Art. 29 — Learning","29.3","Learning is founded on practical activities, mentoring and free play. Every minor learner is accompanied by three reference figures on quarterly rotation, belonging to different Competence Circles and not belonging to the same family or to the same Circle 0."],[4,"c-29.4","Art. 29 — Learning","29.4","By the eleventh year of age literacy is guaranteed, comprising at least reading and writing, basic mathematics, use of the local AIs, repair of a complex object, full-cycle cultivation of food, human and animal first aid and non-violent mediation. Validation takes place by practical demonstration before Circle 1 and not by external examination."],[4,"c-29.5","Art. 29 — Learning","29.5","From the age of twelve, free access to the courses and worksites of all the Circles through the Network is guaranteed. The portfolio of activities carried out, validated by the Competence Circles, replaces the diploma. No Circle may refuse an apprentice for lack of aptitude. Article 6.4 applies."],[4,"c-29.6","Art. 29 — Learning","29.6","Every adult who performs a useful CBO also contributes to the facilitation of learning. Every member of a Competence Circle devotes at least twenty per cent of their hours to the transmission of competences, pursuant to Article 6.3."],[4,"c-29.7","Art. 29 — Learning","29.7","No permanent role of professional teacher is provided for. Teaching is done by those who belong to a Competence Circle and are on a transmission shift pursuant to Article 6.3."],[4,"c-30.1","Art. 30 — Sanctioning system","30.1","Punitive penalty as an end in itself is abolished. No suffering may be inflicted without restorative, re-educational or protective purposes. The measures pursue exclusively reparation, re-education and the immediate protection of the injured person, of the Community and of the perpetrator of the act. Inhuman, degrading or afflictive treatment is prohibited."],[4,"c-30.2","Art. 30 — Sanctioning system","30.2","The system is structured in six progressive grades, ordered on the basis of the intensity of the intervention and of the containment necessary, the seriousness of the act, repetition and the concrete and present danger. Preconditions, types of offence and arrangements for enforcement are governed by the Code of Restorative Justice in compliance with proportionality, subsidiarity and the inviolable rights of Article 1.2. No measure may suppress the vital guarantees of Article 30.4. Only the measures of Article 1.3-bis may reduce the rights of Article 1.2 to those guarantees, within the limits of paragraphs 3 and 4. The cessation of status provided for by Article 7.3-bis is not a measure under this Article."],[4,"c-30.3","Art. 30 — Sanctioning system","30.3","For the cases in Article 1.3-bis, extreme measures of protection may be applied: High-Security Containment and Definitive Removal from the Circles concerned. High-Security Containment consists in the placement of the person in a Containment Community with a protected perimeter, under the surveillance of the Civic Protection Corps and with maintenance of the minimum guarantees provided for in paragraph 4. Definitive Removal consists in the permanent prohibition on residing in and on accessing the Circles in which the injured person and the affected community live, with relocation to another Circle willing to receive the person or by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact. The regime, duration, two-chances rule and review are governed by the Code of Restorative Justice."],[4,"c-30.4","Art. 30 — Sanctioning system","30.4","Even during High-Security Containment or Definitive Removal, torture, mutilation, inhuman or degrading treatment and the death penalty are prohibited. The person retains the right to individual housing, food, water, minimum energy and basic health care. Dangerousness is reviewed by the Guarantee Tribunal at least every twelve months and, at the request of the person, every six months. The measures cease when the concrete and present danger ceases."],[4,"c-31.1","Art. 31 — Legal sources and bodies","31.1","The sources of law, in hierarchical order, are:\na) the Constitution;\nb) the Federative Codes approved in accordance with the Long Double Majority of Article 37.3, without prejudice to the founding regime of Article 38. In the Constitution the Codes are named by subject: Code of Contribution and Storehouses (Code 1), of Quality (Code 2), of Deliberation (Code 3), of Restorative Justice (Code 4), of Learning (Code 5), of Health (Code 6), of the Network and Artificial Intelligences (Code 7), of Energy and Water (Code 8), Agroecological and Food Code (Code 9), of Habitat and Territory (Code 10), of Mobility (Code 11), of Care (Code 12), of Biodiversity (Code 13), of Safety (Code 14), of External Relations (Code 15) and of Transition (Code 16);\nc) the Circle Protocols validated by means of a Dossier pursuant to Article 33;\nd) the customs of the Community that are not contrary to the Constitution and the Codes."],[4,"c-31.2","Art. 31 — Legal sources and bodies","31.2","The legal function is performed by rotation and by sortition. A permanent professional judiciary is prohibited. Legal training may be stable as a competence, but it does not entail the permanent exercise of judicial authority."],[4,"c-31.3","Art. 31 — Legal sources and bodies","31.3","The legal bodies are:\na) the Clarification Assembly of Circle 1, for offences of Grade 1 and 2;\nb) the Transformation Council, for offences of Grade 3;\nc) the Guarantee Tribunal of Circle 2, for offences of Grade 4, 5 and 6, for urgent review within twenty-four hours and for the other guarantee functions assigned by the Codes;\nd) the Guarantee Circle of Circle 1, for restorative facilitation, the protection of procedural guarantees and the appeals provided for by the Codes;\ne) the Review Panel, constituted for the individual appeal, for appeals against the decisions of the bodies of letters a) and b) and against the outcomes of the restorative procedure facilitated by the body of letter d)."],[4,"c-31.4","Art. 31 — Legal sources and bodies","31.4","Every body is assisted by a mediation AI that is exclusively advisory, has no decision-making power and is subject to the non-derogable right of human override."],[4,"c-31.5","Art. 31 — Legal sources and bodies","31.5","Every person has the right to two trusted facilitators with legal competence and to a trusted person freely chosen, even without specific competences. The latter accompanies the person, may speak in their name subject to authorisation and has access to the relevant documents in accordance with the rules of confidentiality. None of the three figures may be recused without a replacement procedure that guarantees equivalent assistance."],[4,"c-31.6","Art. 31 — Legal sources and bodies","31.6","Decisions that establish an offence of Grade 5 or 6 become final only after confirmation by a second Guarantee Tribunal, drawn by lot in a different Circle 2. Final decisions are published in anonymised form in a federated archive: they do not constitute binding precedent, but the body that departs from the prevailing approach shall state its reasons for doing so."],[4,"c-32.1","Art. 32 — Radical subsidiarity and sovereignty","32.1","Competence rests with the smallest Circle within which the scope of impact falls entirely. Decision-making sovereignty belongs to Circle 1, except for matters which, by the nature of their effects, require supra-local coordination pursuant to the Constitution. The higher Circles may not substitute themselves for Circle 1 outside the competences expressly attributed."],[4,"c-32.2","Art. 32 — Radical subsidiarity and sovereignty","32.2","Local practices are resolved upon by Circle 1. Practices that affect a Union of Communities or a Bioregion require the preparatory inquiry and procedural validation of the competent Circle 2 or 3, without hierarchical power. Practices with global systemic impact, including those concerning generalist AIs, genetic engineering and geoengineering, require the global validation of Circle 4 in accordance with Articles 33 and 35.3. Global validation verifies common protocols and supra-local impacts; it does not constitute a central government."],[4,"c-33.1","Art. 33 — Scientific and logical deliberation","33.1","No decision that is irreversible or falls within the competence of Circle 2 or higher, including the amendment of an internal protocol of a Competence Circle, may be adopted without a rational, public and verifiable preparatory inquiry."],[4,"c-33.2","Art. 33 — Scientific and logical deliberation","33.2","The preparatory inquiry consists in a public Dossier prepared by the relevant Competence Circle. The Dossier comprises measurable data, several options with an assessment of costs, benefits and risks for seven generations, the limits of knowledge and a mandatory refutation section."],[4,"c-33.3","Art. 33 — Scientific and logical deliberation","33.3","The Dossier is submitted to public discussion open to all the Circles. A traceable reply is given to every objection. In the absence of the Dossier, the resolution is void. No vote may suspend the application of this Article."],[4,"c-34.1","Art. 34 — Consensus and decision-making mechanisms","34.1","The primary decision-making method is the search for informed consensus, preceded by a Dossier pursuant to Article 33 where required."],[4,"c-34.2","Art. 34 — Consensus and decision-making mechanisms","34.2","If consensus is not reached after two sessions of facilitated discussion, a vote is held in accordance with Article 35. A minority equal to at least ten per cent of the voters may raise a Conscientious or Ecological Sustainability Objection. The objection does not constitute an automatic veto, but it suspends the effect of the contested part and activates a mandatory procedure of review and reasoned reply in accordance with the Code of Deliberation."],[4,"c-34.3","Art. 34 — Consensus and decision-making mechanisms","34.3","The Assembly of the Community resolves on the organisation of the CBO, on the Common Storehouse and on justice of Grade 1 and 2. In Circles 2–4 there are no deputies: spokespersons operate with a binding mandate, drawn by lot from among suitable volunteers and rotating for each single cycle. The Circle of origin may revoke them at any time pursuant to Article 4.3. The resolutions of Circles 2 and 3 are adopted by the General Assembly, constituted by all the persons entitled to vote of the Base Communities concerned, who vote individually pursuant to Article 35.2; the spokespersons take care of the preparatory inquiry and the coordination and have no vote of their own."],[4,"c-34.4","Art. 34 — Consensus and decision-making mechanisms","34.4","Consultations and votes take place through the open-source federated Network. The individual vote is anonymous; the count by Community is public and tracked on a distributed ledger. The computer systems may not alter the vote nor substitute themselves for the will of the voter."],[4,"c-35.1","Art. 35 — Types of vote and quorum","35.1","The ordinary resolutions of Circle 1 are approved by a simple majority of the participants, without a minimum quorum, except in the cases for which the Constitution or the Federative Codes establish a reinforced quorum or majority for the protection of minorities, of health, of safety or of ecological limits."],[4,"c-35.2","Art. 35 — Types of vote and quorum","35.2","The impact resolutions of Circle 2 or 3 require jointly:\na) a majority of two thirds of the participating Base Communities;\nb) a majority of the persons voting of the Circle 2 or 3 concerned;\nc) the Dossier provided for in Article 33."],[4,"c-35.3","Art. 35 — Types of vote and quorum","35.3","The initiatives with global systemic impact of Circle 4 require an absolute majority of the voters at global level and an absolute majority of the Circles 3 entitled to vote."],[4,"c-36.1","Art. 36 — Right to vote, participation and solidarity","36.1","The right of vote and deliberation belongs to every human being from completion of the fourteenth year of age. It is exercised unconditionally, irrespective of the CdR, and may not be limited on economic, religious, cultural or contribution-related grounds, except in the cases of cessation of member status provided for by the Constitution. The delegation, purchase and exchange of the vote are prohibited."],[4,"c-36.2","Art. 36 — Right to vote, participation and solidarity","36.2","In the event of catastrophe, serious pandemic or scarcity of essential resources, the Solidarity Protocol may be activated on the declaration of the Circle 3 concerned or at the concordant request of at least ten neighbouring Circles 1."],[4,"c-36.3","Art. 36 — Right to vote, participation and solidarity","36.3","During the emergency, the reserves of the Common Storehouses and the CBO may be reallocated as a priority to the affected areas and the Civic Protection Corps may be mobilised."],[4,"c-36.4","Art. 36 — Right to vote, participation and solidarity","36.4","The emergency may not suspend the rights of Article 1.2, the disarmament provided for in Article 16 or the guarantees of Article 18; it may not confer powers of central command nor last beyond thirty days without renewal by the Assembly. Every extension is reasoned and publicly recorded."],[4,"c-37.1","Art. 37 — Amendment of the Constitution","37.1","Empirical initiative. The proposal for amendment may be put forward exclusively by a Circle 1 that has applied the rule subject to revision for at least twenty-four continuous months. For rules that are not applied directly by a Circle 1, the requirement is satisfied by continuous membership of the Confederation for the same period. The proposal is accompanied by an impact report based on real data, verifiable on the federated Network, and by a Dossier and refutation pursuant to Article 33. An initiative founded exclusively on ideology or on a postulate not subjected to verification is inadmissible."],[4,"c-37.2","Art. 37 — Amendment of the Constitution","37.2","Controlled experimentation. The proposed amendment is subjected to mandatory experimentation of twelve months in at least five volunteer Communities belonging to different Extended Circles, selected according to socio-ecological diversity. At the end a summary report is published on the Network. If a Community interrupts the experimentation before the end, the fact is reasoned and documented; the experimentation may continue provided that at least five valid Communities remain and the diversity requirements provided for in this paragraph are met. The experimentation may not derogate from the Inviolable Core."],[4,"c-37.3","Art. 37 — Amendment of the Constitution","37.3","Vote and Long Double Majority. At the end of the experimentation, the revision is submitted to a global vote open for ninety days. Approval requires cumulatively:\na) two thirds of the Circles 1 entitled to vote, distributed in at least three different Extended Circles;\nb) two thirds of the total voters."],[4,"c-37.4","Art. 37 — Amendment of the Constitution","37.4","Inviolable Core. The following are removed from any revision procedure, even with the Long Double Majority:\na) the purposes referred to in Article 0 and the inviolable rights referred to in Article 1. The exceptions provided for in Articles 1.3-bis, 18.7, 30.3 and 30.4 for the most serious recidivism against personal safety and for the security measures protecting the injured person or the community, and the exception provided for by Article 7.3-bis for persistent and unjustified refusal of the CBO, may not be extended to further cases, measures or persons;\nb) the prohibitions of mass biometric recognition, of biometric profiling and of centralised AI referred to in Articles 18.2, 19.2, 20.2 and 23.1, without prejudice to the exception in Article 18.7;\nc) the total disarmament referred to in Article 16;\nd) the prohibition of a State religion referred to in Article 25.2 and the prohibitions of delegation, purchase, exchange and conditioning of the vote referred to in Article 36.1;\ne) the regime of the Commons and the prohibition of private ownership of water, air, land, energy, seeds and knowledge;\nf) Circle 0 as an inviolable unit referred to in Article 5.1, letter a);\ng) the principle of scientific deliberation referred to in Article 33 and the principle of collective co-responsibility referred to in Article 2.3;\nh) the prohibition of AI that decides in place of persons referred to in Article 19.6, the principle of independence and non-subordination of AIs referred to in Article 20.1 and the right to the facilitators and to the trusted person referred to in Article 31.5;\ni) the acephalous and non-hierarchical character of the Confederation, the sovereignty of the Base Community and the subsidiarity referred to in Articles 4.1, 4.2 and 32.1, as well as sortition, rotation and the prohibition of the professional and permanent exercise of politics and of the judicial function referred to in Articles 4.3, 4.4, 6.5 and 31.2;\nj) the right to leave the Confederation and to live by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact referred to in Articles 4.8, 4.9 and 4.9-bis, the right of withdrawal of the Communities referred to in Article 4.6 and the right to leave a Community referred to in Article 5.2;\nk) this paragraph."],[4,"c-37.5","Art. 37 — Amendment of the Constitution","37.5","Clerical corrections. Clerical errors, erroneous cross-references and terminological alignments that do not alter the normative content are corrected by a simplified procedure: the correction is published on the Network for thirty days and is deemed approved if no Circle 1 submits a reasoned objection. In the event of an objection the ordinary procedure applies."],[4,"c-38.1","Art. 38 — Adoption, founding regime and gradualness","38.1","The Constitution and the Federative Codes enter into force, for each Base Community, with the act of accession resolved upon by the persons who constitute it. The accession of every person is individual, voluntary and revocable pursuant to Article 4.8."],[4,"c-38.2","Art. 38 — Adoption, founding regime and gradualness","38.2","Until at least three Extended Circles exist, the revision of the Constitution and the approval and amendment of the Federative Codes follow the founding regime:\na) the proposal may be submitted by a Circle 1 that has applied the rule for at least six months, or that documents by a Dossier the impossibility of applying it;\nb) the experimentation lasts at least six months and takes place in at least two volunteer Communities, or in the only existing Community;\nc) the vote remains open for thirty days and requires cumulatively two thirds of the existing Circles 1 and two thirds of the total voters."],[4,"c-38.3","Art. 38 — Adoption, founding regime and gradualness","38.3","The amendments adopted under the founding regime are submitted to ratification by the Long Double Majority within twenty-four months of the constitution of the third Extended Circle. An amendment that is not ratified lapses and the previous text returns into force; the effects already produced are preserved."],[4,"c-38.4","Art. 38 — Adoption, founding regime and gradualness","38.4","The Inviolable Core of Article 37.4 may not be amended even under the founding regime."],[4,"c-38.5","Art. 38 — Adoption, founding regime and gradualness","38.5","Every version of the Constitution and of the Codes is published in an open, versioned and replicable archive, together with the register of amendments and of the related reasons."],[4,"c-38.6","Art. 38 — Adoption, founding regime and gradualness","38.6","Until completion of the transition governed by the Code of Transition, the Constitution and the Codes apply in compliance with the legal order in force in the territory in which each Community operates. The provisions that presuppose the full operation of the Confederation, including those on the abolition of money in external relations, on the definitive regime of goods and on the containment measures, apply within the limits and with the gradualness established by that Code."],[5,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-scope-of-application","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and scope of application","","1. This Code governs:\na) the mandatory base contribution, hereinafter “CBO”, and the determination of the related requirement;\nb) the allocation of the tasks necessary to the Community;\nc) the establishment and management of the Common Storehouses;\nd) the logistics, internal transport and common infrastructure connected with the performance of the CBO.\n2. For the purposes of this Code, CBO means the contribution of necessary labour, fairly apportioned and organised according to actual needs, to guarantee the material self-sufficiency and the care of the Community.\n3. For the purposes of this Code, the following definitions apply:\na) Management AI: an information system supporting the collection and processing of data, the forecasting of the requirement and the formulation of proposals, with no deliberative power;\nb) Circle 0: the Care Nucleus referred to in Article 5.1, letter a), of the Constitution; where this Code assigns it a function, that function is performed by the persons who compose it, meeting in proximity assembly;\nc) Competence Circle: the functional network referred to in Article 6 of the Constitution which, in each Circle 1, operates through the relevant technical Council and is responsible for conducting preliminary examinations, validating or giving opinions within its own field, in accordance with the applicable Codes and Article 12-bis of Code 3;\nd) task: a specific activity included in the CBO plan, with an indication of its objective, duration, required competences, place and conditions of performance;\ne) Common Storehouse: a material infrastructure intended for the storage, management and distribution of commons in accordance with this Code.\n4. The Management AI shall not replace the decision of the competent bodies, nor autonomously adopt decisions affecting the assignment of persons, access to essential goods or individual rights."],[5,"art-2--precedence-of-need-over-task","Art. 2 — Precedence of need over task","","1. Essential tasks are not defined once and for all in the abstract. They are identified on the basis of the actual and documented needs of the Community.\n2. The ordinary cycle for assessing the requirement takes place every quarter and comprises:\na) the recording of the consumption, stock levels, expiry dates and shortages of the Common Storehouses;\nb) the collection of the needs reported by the Circles 0;\nc) the technical preparatory inquiry by the Competence Circles concerned;\nd) the estimate of the resources, competences and hours needed.\n3. The Management AI processes the available data and formulates a proposal for the hours requirement. The validation of needs and the approval of tasks fall exclusively to the Assembly of Circle 1, in accordance with the procedures of Code 3.\n4. The proposal of the Management AI is advisory. The Assembly may approve it, amend it or reject it by a reasoned and traced decision.\n5. The collection and processing of data shall take place in compliance with data minimisation, non-profiling and the data protection rules laid down in Codes 6 and 7.\n6. The requirement includes the hours of the civic functions of List D referred to in Article 4 and those of mandatory training.\n7. Together with the requirement proposal, the Labour Balance shall be published every quarter, indicating in aggregate form:\na) the hours needed for each category: food, energy and water, care, maintenance, logistics, civic functions and documentation, other activities;\nb) the number of persons qualified per competence category;\nc) the hours available within the limit of Article 3;\nd) the gap between hours needed and hours available;\ne) the share of hours devoted to the functions of Article 12-bis, paragraph 13, of Code 3, which shall not exceed the limit laid down there; the hours of civic protection and the other hours of List D that do not fall within those functions are indicated separately.\n8. If the hours needed exceed the hours available within the limit of Article 3, the Assembly shall proceed in the following order:\na) it shall reduce deferrable activities in accordance with the hierarchy of Article 32, starting from the last priority;\nb) it shall simplify documentary formalities that do not affect safety, in accordance with the principle of proportionality of Code 2;\nc) it shall have recourse to the volunteering of Article 6, paragraph 6, to the mutualism between Circles of Article 30 and to the Pool provided for by Code 15;\nd) it shall request the activation of the Solidarity Protocol pursuant to Article 36.2 of the Constitution.\n9. A gap that persists for two consecutive quarters shall be reported to Circle 2. The Labour Balances constitute the empirical basis for verifying the limit of Article 3 and for proposals for revision pursuant to Articles 37 and 38 of the Constitution; during the transition they are relevant for the purposes of the thresholds of Code 16."],[5,"art-3--legal-nature-and-maximum-limit-of-the-cbo","Art. 3 — Legal nature and maximum limit of the CBO","","1. The CBO constitutes a duty of material solidarity. It does not establish a relationship of subordinate employment and does not constitute, in itself, an obligation of a pecuniary nature.\n2. The CBO shall not exceed an average of fifteen hours per week, calculated on a quarterly basis. The limit may not be derogated from by ordinary resolution.\n3. Derogation from the limit referred to in paragraph 2 is permitted exclusively upon prior activation of the Solidarity Protocol pursuant to Article 36.2 of the Constitution and in compliance with the related conditions, guarantees and durations.\n4. No provision of this Code may be interpreted as authorising the limit referred to in paragraph 2 to be exceeded outside the cases provided for in paragraph 3."],[5,"art-4--classification-of-tasks","Art. 4 — Classification of tasks","","1. Essential tasks are divided into the following lists:\na) List A — Generic Hard Core: a closed list of tasks indispensable to daily subsistence which do not require a specialist qualification, without prejudice to any safety training that may be prescribed. It includes, in accordance with the exhaustive list approved as an annex, the preparation of collective meals, ordinary hygiene and sanitary cleaning, the common laundry, the basic logistics of the Storehouses, basic agricultural harvesting, the non-medical care of minors and elderly persons, compost management and light maintenance;\nb) List B — Specialist Hard Core: a closed list of essential tasks that require competences validated through the Portfolio system. It includes basic health care, the maintenance of the Network and of local AIs, the technical management of energy and water installations, agrotechnics, the veterinary care of the sanctuaries, the accounting of the Storehouses and the operation of complex vehicles;\nc) List C — Dynamic essential tasks: an open list of variable, seasonal or emergency activities, identified on a quarterly basis by the Assembly on a proposal from the Competence Circles. It may include, by way of example, intensive harvesting, reforestation, bioremediation and light construction;\nd) List D — Civic functions: functions of coordination, facilitation, guarantee, civic protection and technical council conferred by sortition, as well as the documentation that the Codes make mandatory. The hours are counted in the CBO of the person performing them, pursuant to Article 7.2 of the Constitution, and do not give entitlement to CdR.\n2. The list of List A and that of List B are exhaustive. Their amendment requires the updating of the annex and the approval of the competent body in accordance with Code 3.\n3. The inclusion of a task in List C indicates that it responds to a temporary or variable requirement. It shall not be used to circumvent the competence, safety or training requirements laid down for the tasks of Lists A and B.\n4. Every task included in a list shall be accompanied by the information necessary to assess its duration, competences, risk, training and conditions of performance."],[5,"art-5--generic-cbo-and-specialist-cbo","Art. 5 — Generic CBO and specialist CBO","","1. The CBO is generic or specialist according to the competences validated and necessary for the task assigned.\n2. The primary CBO of a member with validated specialist competences consists in the performance of the List B tasks relevant to the recognised competences, where there is a requirement for such tasks.\n3. A person who has only generic competences shall not perform List B tasks autonomously. That person may take part in them within a learning pathway accompanied by a specialist tutor, formalised in the Portfolio and carried out in compliance with the safety conditions.\n4. If in a given shift there is no requirement for the validated specialist competence, the member may be assigned to generic List A tasks, in accordance with the allocation rules and without discrimination. Possession of a specialist competence does not entail an automatic exemption from the generic CBO.\n5. Assignment to tasks other than the primary competence shall respect health limits, declared preferences, available training and the criteria of fair distribution."],[5,"art-6--quantitative-determination-and-apportionment-of-hours","Art. 6 — Quantitative determination and apportionment of hours","","1. The actual CBO requirement is determined every quarter. No permanent individual total of hours is fixed.\n2. If the individual requirement resulting from the calculation is lower than the maximum limit referred to in Article 3, the lower requirement applies. There is no mandatory minimum of hours.\n3. The apportionment is carried out separately for each competence category:\na) the generic total of hours, including the List A tasks and the generic share of the List C tasks, is divided by the number of persons qualified for the generic CBO;\nb) the specialist total of hours is calculated separately for each competence. The List B total of hours and the specialist share of List C are divided by the number of persons qualified for the specific competence;\nc) the hours of restorative work provided for by Article 8 are not counted in the ordinary CBO.\n4. The calculation shall indicate the overall hours, the apportionment criteria, the number of persons considered and any corrections applied. The data shall be published in aggregate form and in compliance with Codes 6 and 7.\n5. To manage temporary peaks, a quarterly hours bank is permitted. For a maximum of two consecutive weeks, the commitment may reach twenty-five hours per week, provided that:\na) the average for the quarter does not exceed fifteen hours per week;\nb) the excess hours are compensated by an equivalent period of rest;\nc) the health and safety limits are respected;\nd) the peak and the recovery plan are recorded.\n6. Alternatively, the extraordinary requirement may be covered through volunteering incentivised under the CdR system governed by Chapter V. This arrangement shall not be imposed."],[5,"art-7--preferences-and-allocation","Art. 7 — Preferences and allocation","","1. Each member may indicate, for each quarterly cycle, up to three preferences among the tasks for which the member possesses the required competences or may follow an authorised mentored pathway.\n2. The Storehouse Council carries out the actual allocation on the basis of the criteria approved by the Assembly and with the advisory support of the Management AI.\n3. The allocation shall take into account, in the order established by the approved criteria:\na) validated competence and safety requirements;\nb) the requirement of the task;\nc) the preferences expressed;\nd) fair distribution of the load;\ne) continuity of the service;\nf) relevant personal conditions and health limits, without acquiring unnecessary health data.\n4. The assignment decision shall be capable of being reasoned and shall be traceable. The Management AI shall not autonomously assign a person nor make its own proposal binding.\n5. The arrangements for challenging the allocation are governed by Article 12."],[5,"art-8--unpleasant-arduous-and-night-tasks-and-restorative-work","Art. 8 — Unpleasant, arduous and night tasks and restorative work","","1. Tasks that involve particular physical burden, hygienic discomfort, risk or performance in the time band between 22:00 and 06:00 are classified as unpleasant, arduous or night tasks. The list shall be drawn up by the Health Council and the Storehouse Council and submitted to the Assembly for approval.\n2. Work performed in the night band is classified as unpleasant for the purposes of the allocation and the recognition provided for by this Code.\n3. Without prejudice to Article 6-bis, paragraph 6, of Code 7, unpleasant, arduous or night tasks shall be assigned in the following order:\na) in the first place, they may be included in a restorative agreement freely agreed pursuant to Code 4, in compliance with the guarantees referred to in paragraph 4;\nb) for the remaining share, they shall be assigned by public and traceable sortition among the qualified and available persons, applying a rebalancing criterion that temporarily excludes from the sortition those who performed the greatest number of unpleasant hours in the preceding quarter, except where no qualified persons are available.\n4. Every restorative agreement that provides for unpleasant or arduous activities shall respect the following, failing which the relevant clause shall be null and void:\na) a maximum duration and a measure proportionate to the facts;\nb) the free and informed consent of the person concerned;\nc) the prohibition on assigning degrading tasks or dangerous tasks that are not ordinary;\nd) the right to PPE, to training and to the applicable health surveillance;\ne) independent supervision by the Guarantee Circle;\nf) the right to lodge a complaint and to request that the task be replaced.\n5. Unpleasant activities shall not exceed fifty per cent of the overall total of hours of restorative work agreed.\n6. Refusal to accept a restorative task shall not be interpreted as an admission of responsibility. The procedural consequences provided for by Code 4 apply, without automatic sanctions."],[5,"art-9--rotation-breaks-and-work-blocks","Art. 9 — Rotation, breaks and work blocks","","1. For the purposes of this Article, work block means a continuous period of performance without a break longer than fifteen minutes.\n2. Between two blocks there shall be a minimum break equal to thirty per cent of the duration of the preceding block, without prejudice to any more protective limits established by health and safety rules.\n3. The duration of continuous assignment and of the blocks shall be commensurate with the degree of strain of the task:\na) for tasks of high strain or unpleasantness, continuous assignment shall not exceed two weeks; the block shall not exceed one hour and thirty minutes and no more than two blocks per day are permitted;\nb) for tasks of medium strain, continuous assignment shall not exceed one quarter; the block shall not exceed three hours and no more than two blocks per day are permitted;\nc) for tasks of low strain, continuous assignment shall not exceed two quarters; the block shall not exceed four hours and no more than three blocks per day are permitted.\n4. The actual duration of the block shall be agreed between the member and the Storehouse Council according to the needs of the service, the availability of personnel and health limits.\n5. The accumulation of several blocks in the same day is permitted only if agreed, compatible with health and accompanied by the necessary recovery periods. The more favourable provisions on health and safety prevail over the ordinary limits of this Article."],[5,"art-10--recognition-of-unpleasant-tasks-through-supplemented-cdr","Art. 10 — Recognition of unpleasant tasks through supplemented CdR","","1. Tasks classified pursuant to Article 8, paragraph 1, give rise to a supplement in CdR.\n2. The supplement is calculated on the ordinary unit defined by Article 43, equal to one CdR per hour. The public table approved by the Assembly establishes a supplement of between thirty and one hundred per cent of the ordinary value.\n3. Where the task is performed within the CBO, only the supplement is awarded: from 0.3 to 1 CdR per hour. Where it is performed beyond the CBO, the supplement is added to the ordinary CdR: from 1.3 to 2 CdR per hour.\n4. Restorative work gives rise to ordinary CdR, unless the restorative agreement freely and expressly provides for a different recognition permitted by Chapter V.\n5. This Article identifies the cases that generate supplemented CdR. The award, the cap, the decay and the use of CdR are governed exclusively by Chapter V."],[5,"art-11--exemptions-reductions-suspensions-and-solidarity","Art. 11 — Exemptions, reductions, suspensions and solidarity","","1. The following measures apply:\na) total exemption: for persons over seventy-five years of age, for persons with certified severe disability and for women who have given birth, until the end of the twelfth month from the birth;\nb) reduction of fifty per cent: for parents of children under three years of age, for certified continuous caregivers and for persons over seventy years of age who do not fall within the total exemption referred to in letter a);\nc) temporary suspension: in the event of illness, accident or illness of a minor child, where the condition makes the performance of the CBO temporarily impossible or unsafe. The condition shall be attested by the Health Council within the limits of the necessary information;\nd) solidarity substitution: temporary coverage by the Community of the hours not performed, in accordance with paragraph 2;\ne) modulated reduction: temporary reduction of the CBO, to the extent and for the duration indicated by the Health Council or the Care Council, for pregnancy, bereavement, crisis or another condition provided for by Codes 6 and 12, including the reduction of Article 8, paragraph 2, letter d), of Code 6. The hours not performed are covered in accordance with paragraph 2; paragraphs 3 and 4 apply.\n2. In the event of temporary suspension, the Community Solidarity Hours Bank operates. The hours not performed are covered by the Community and do not generate individual debt. On return, a voluntary and agreed repayment is permitted, compatible with health and with the maximum limit referred to in Article 3. Any sanctioning mechanism for the hours covered by the Solidarity Hours Bank is prohibited.\n3. Total exemption prevails over the reductions provided for in paragraph 1, letter b). No person shall be subject simultaneously to more than one reduction for the same hour.\n4. The information used to recognise exemptions, reductions or suspensions shall be treated confidentially. Only the operational outcome necessary for allocation shall be communicated to the Storehouse Council, not the diagnosis or the health details.\n5. The application of the measures of this Article shall not prejudice access to the Basket or to the Common Storehouses."],[5,"art-12--transparency-oversight-and-appeal","Art. 12 — Transparency, oversight and appeal","","1. The following shall be published on the Network, in accessible form and in compliance with data protection:\na) the aggregate total of hours;\nb) the criteria for apportionment and separation;\nc) the aggregate number of assignments per category;\nd) the arrangements for and outcomes of the sortitions;\ne) the criteria used by the Management AI and its relevant proposals.\n2. Oversight of the correctness of the requirement and of any inflation of hours is exercised by the Circles 0 and through cross-audit by the Competence Circles. Oversight does not entail access to unnecessary personal data.\n3. Any member may request clarification or lodge a reasoned appeal against his or her own allocation with the Guarantee Circle within seven days of its communication.\n4. The appeal has suspensive effect solely as regards the assignment of unpleasant or arduous tasks, until the decision, unless the suspension would compromise an essential service or give rise to a serious risk. In that case the Guarantee Circle may order an alternative temporary measure.\n5. The decision on the appeal shall be reasoned and communicated to the person concerned. The further guarantees provided for by Code 4 remain unaffected."],[5,"art-13--definition-function-and-independence-of-the-system","Art. 13 — Definition, function and independence of the system","","1. The Common Storehouses are material infrastructures that guarantee access to the goods necessary for subsistence irrespective of the CBO performed and of the CdR accrued, in accordance with the Constitution and this Code.\n2. The Storehouses perform the following functions:\na) distribution of the commons according to need and availability;\nb) recording of material requirements, as an informational element for the determination of the CBO pursuant to Article 2;\nc) storage of the commons and prevention of private accumulation and of waste.\n3. Access to the necessary goods included in the Basket shall not be made conditional on the performance of the CBO or on the possession of CdR. Paragraph 1, this paragraph, Article 18, paragraph 1, letter a), and the provisions of the Annexes and of the other Codes that exclude making the Basket subject to the CBO concern members and persons received in accordance with the Codes. A person who has ceased to be a member pursuant to Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution and Article 29, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 4 is entitled only to drinking water and life-saving emergency care as provided for by Articles 1.3 and 4.9 of the Constitution. The rights of their minor children remain intact."],[5,"art-14--types-and-territorial-organisation","Art. 14 — Types and territorial organisation","","1. In the phase of first application, the system of Storehouses comprises the following types:\na) Food Storehouse;\nb) Tools Storehouse;\nc) Textile and Housing Storehouse;\nd) Health Storehouse, governed by Code 6;\ne) Digital Storehouse.\n2. The taxonomy is not exhaustive. Specialised, energy, building, critical spare parts, high-security or emergency Storehouses may be established, in accordance with a regulation proposed by the Storehouse Council and approved by the Assembly.\n3. Each Circle 0 has a Proximity Pantry intended for high-turnover goods. Circle 1 has a Central Storehouse for storage and redistribution.\n4. The territorial organisation of the Storehouses shall guarantee traceability, accessibility and continuity, without creating conditions of unjustified exclusion."],[5,"art-15--common-ownership-and-destination-of-surplus-or-obsolete-goods","Art. 15 — Common ownership and destination of surplus or obsolete goods","","1. The goods belonging to the Storehouses are commons, undivided, inalienable and exempt from seizure, in accordance with the Constitution.\n1-bis. When the Balance provided for by Code 15 is active, surplus goods that constitute a Federable Balance within the meaning of Article 2 of Code 15 are entered in the Pool and allocated in accordance with Code 15; the Circle does not choose their recipient. Paragraph 2 applies to non-federable surplus goods, to obsolete goods, to goods at risk of waste not reassigned within the time limit of Article 6 of Code 15 and to goods that the body competent for the Pool declares not necessary to the Pool.\n2. Surplus goods or goods that have become obsolete shall be managed in the following order of priority:\na) external donation: goods still fit for consumption or use shall as a priority be destined for federated or neighbouring communities, where donation is possible and prevents waste;\nb) internal reuse and recovery: goods are repaired, reused or dismantled to recover components; suitable organic residues may be destined for composting;\nc) exchange or transfer to the outside: surplus perishable goods that cannot be donated, or obsolete goods that are still usable, may be exchanged or transferred in order to acquire goods that cannot be self-produced;\nd) making safe: goods that are dangerous or can be neither repaired nor recovered shall be kept in secure storage and sent for treatment in accordance with Article 14 of the Constitution, without recourse to landfill or incineration. For non-cyclable materials, the substitution plan provided for by Article 14.1 of the Constitution shall be drawn up.\n3. The economic terms of the operations referred to in letters a) and c) are assessed in UCR in accordance with Code 3 and governed, as regards external relations, by Code 15. None of these operations may be settled by means of CdR.\n4. The reuse, recovery and making safe referred to in letters b) and d) follow the regulations of the Circles competent for quality, health, production and environmental protection.\n5. Every destination shall be recorded, with reasons and quantity, in the traceability system of the Storehouses."],[5,"art-16--supply-of-the-storehouses","Art. 16 — Supply of the Storehouses","","1. The Storehouses are supplied by means of:\na) goods produced within the CBO;\nb) voluntary conferrals of goods from family craft workshops and vegetable gardens;\nc) external donations approved in accordance with the applicable procedures;\nd) residual purchases of necessary goods that cannot be self-produced.\n2. Strategic goods that cannot be self-produced but are necessary shall be identified in a dedicated category, with differentiated safety stocks. The criteria are defined by regulation of the Storehouse Council with the opinion of the Health Council, for the aspects within its competence.\n3. Every conferral of goods shall be recorded at least by origin, quantity, quality and date of acquisition. The data are accessible in accordance with the transparency levels provided for by Codes 3 and 7.\n4. Refusal of a conferral of goods shall not be arbitrary and shall be reasoned on the basis of quality, safety, storage capacity or other criteria established by the applicable STVs."],[5,"art-17--management-traceability-and-role-of-the-management-ai","Art. 17 — Management, traceability and role of the Management AI","","1. The operational management of the Storehouses is entrusted to the Storehouse Council, in compliance with the decisions of the Assembly and with the competences assigned to the other Circles.\n2. The Management AI may record data, calculate estimates, formulate proposals and generate alerts. It has no deliberative powers and no powers of autonomous assignment.\n3. The system shall promptly record inflows, outflows, stock levels, expiry dates and requirements and may propose reorder thresholds.\n4. Every proposal for purchase, disposal or rationing shall be subject to human validation. Where the proposal exceeds the thresholds approved by the Assembly, a resolution of the Assembly is required.\n5. The thresholds referred to in paragraph 4 are approved and may be amended exclusively by the Assembly, on a reasoned proposal from the Storehouse Council.\n6. Every human decision that departs substantially from the AI’s proposal shall be recorded with a brief statement of reasons, without this limiting the decision-making power of the competent body."],[5,"art-18--access-and-distribution","Art. 18 — Access and distribution","","1. The distribution of goods is regulated according to the following categories:\na) necessary goods included in the Sufficiency Basket: open access, free of charge and unconditional, according to actual need. Access shall not be made subject to the CBO or to CdR, except as provided for by Article 13, paragraph 3, for those who have ceased to be members pursuant to Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution;\nb) non-necessary goods available in limited quantity: access by booking or transparent waiting list; any use of CdR is governed by Article 45;\nc) necessary goods that are temporarily scarce: distribution in accordance with the rules referred to in Article 19.\n2. The content of the Basket is defined in the annual Technical Annex, approved by the Assembly on a joint proposal from the Health Council and the Storehouse Council. The proposal brings together the lists prepared by the sectoral Councils in accordance with the Codes, including the Health Basket of Article 6, paragraph 1, of Code 6 and the Material Basket of Article 4 of C.1-Annex A; the lists take effect only upon approval by the Assembly. The proposal shall be published and may be contested in accordance with the applicable procedures.\n3. This Code governs the arrangements for access and distribution. The determination of individual health needs falls to the Health Council, within the limits of Codes 6 and 12."],[5,"art-19--conservative-thresholds-and-fair-rationing","Art. 19 — Conservative thresholds and fair rationing","","1. The Storehouse Council may propose conservative thresholds for the drawing of goods per nucleus, based on stock levels, expiry dates and the characteristics of the good. The thresholds serve the purpose of preventing waste and hoarding and shall not turn into punitive conditions of access.\n2. The repeated exceeding of a threshold does not automatically entail sanctions or suspensions. In the first instance a clarification conversation shall be proposed in Circle 0, with respect for confidentiality.\n3. In the event of established scarcity, rationing shall be temporary, reasoned and proportionate. It is proposed by the Competence Circle and approved by the Assembly in accordance with Code 3. The decision shall be communicated to the Community without delay.\n4. Rationing is based on material equity and not on arithmetical equality. It takes into account the actual requirement, the number and conditions of the members of the nucleus, age and relevant health conditions.\n5. For health goods or sensitive goods, the determination of individual need falls to the Health Council. Health information shall not be made public.\n6. Conservative thresholds shall not reduce or suspend minimum access to the essential goods of the Basket. Any measure that temporarily limits such access shall be adopted in accordance with the scarcity procedure and the guarantees provided for by the Constitution and the Codes."],[5,"art-20--transparency-and-oversight-by-the-community","Art. 20 — Transparency and oversight by the Community","","1. Data on stock levels, flows, expiry dates, waste and the composition of the Basket shall be published on the Network, in good time and in accordance with the applicable access levels.\n2. Anyone may report anomalies to their own Circle 0 or to the Storehouse Council. The report shall be recorded and shall receive a response in accordance with a public procedure.\n3. A quarterly cross-audit shall be carried out with the participation of:\na) the Circle 0 concerned;\nb) the Storehouse Council;\nc) three members drawn by lot with a temporary audit function.\n4. The arrangements for the draw, the duration of the office, the confidentiality obligations and the incompatibilities are governed by a regulation approved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Guarantee Circle.\n5. The audit does not entail indiscriminate access to personal data, health data or data relating to the security of infrastructure.\n6. For every good and service distributed by the Storehouses, there shall be published the reproduction cost in UCR, certified pursuant to Article 9 of Code 3, and a scarcity indicator expressing the ratio between availability and requests and the waiting times. The indicators inform people’s choices and the resolutions of the Assembly. They are not prices, do not give rise to exchanges and do not condition access to the Basket."],[5,"art-21--quality-control","Art. 21 — Quality control","","1. The quality control protocols for goods are proposed by the competent Circles and validated in accordance with Code 2.\n2. Before distribution the following shall be verified:\na) for perishable goods, the expiry date, integrity and storage conditions;\nb) for tools and equipment, conformity with the applicable STVs, state of safety and functionality.\n3. Non-conforming goods shall not be distributed. Depending on the case, they are destined for composting, repair, recovery or making safe.\n4. The decision of non-conformity shall be recorded and reasoned. The technical assessment is carried out by the competent Circle, without prejudice to the powers of the Quality Council provided for by Code 2."],[5,"art-22--subsidiarity-of-the-storehouses","Art. 22 — Subsidiarity of the Storehouses","","1. The Proximity Pantry autonomously manages high-turnover goods within the thresholds approved by the Assembly. When the stock level falls below the threshold or the requirement exceeds local capacity, a request shall be submitted to the Central Storehouse.\n2. The ordinary management of the Proximity Pantry may be performed within the generic CBO of List A.\n3. The principle of subsidiarity does not exempt from compliance with the common procedures on traceability, quality, safety and data protection."],[5,"art-23--subject-matter-and-subordination-of-efficiency","Art. 23 — Subject matter and subordination of efficiency","","1. This Chapter governs the logistical organisation necessary for the performance of the CBO and for the functioning of the Storehouses referred to in Chapter II.\n2. Logistics pursues the reduction of unnecessary journeys, of energy waste and of unproductive time. It is organised in the service of needs and shall not artificially create new requirements.\n3. Material efficiency shall not prevail over the limits of health, safety and dignity provided for by Article 27 and by Codes 6 and 14. In the event of conflict, the measures necessary to protect health, safety and dignity prevail."],[5,"art-24--storehouse-council-and-division-of-competences","Art. 24 — Storehouse Council and division of competences","","1. The Storehouse Council is the technical Council provided for by Article 10.5 of the Constitution. It is composed of three to five persons drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3 and:\na) draws up proposals for general criteria for allocation and the quarterly logistics plan;\nb) manages the Pantries and the Central Storehouse;\nc) manages the fleet of common vehicles and organises its maintenance, until the activation of the Logistics Council provided for by Code 11;\nd) proposes reorder thresholds and rationing measures;\ne) implements the decisions of the Assembly relating to logistics.\n2. The Assembly of Circle 1 approves the general criteria and the quarterly logistics plan.\n3. The Storehouse Council carries out the individual allocation of the CBO in accordance with the approved criteria, with the advisory support of the Management AI.\n4. The Guarantee Circle examines appeals and carries out the procedural checks within its competence. It does not replace the Storehouse Council in ordinary technical decisions.\n5. From the activation of the Logistics Council, the management of the fleet, of deliveries and of basic mobility falls to the latter in accordance with Code 11. The Storehouse Council retains the management of the Storehouses, the assessment of the requirement and the allocation of the CBO."],[5,"art-25--internal-transport-and-proximity","Art. 25 — Internal transport and proximity","","1. The internal transport necessary for the CBO and for the functioning of the Storehouses shall be organised, as far as possible, by means of common vehicles, such as cargo bikes, light electric vehicles, common tractors and trolleys.\n2. A member shall not be required to use his or her own private vehicle to perform the CBO. Where a vehicle is necessary, the common vehicle is provided by the Tools Storehouse or by the competent service.\n3. The Management AI may formulate proposals for optimising routes on the basis of the proximity between the place of residence and the place of the task. The allocation remains a decision of the Storehouse Council.\n4. As a rule, allocation takes place within thirty minutes’ travel from the Circle 0 to which the member belongs. The limit is binding as a planning criterion, subject to the exceptions in paragraph 5.\n5. Derogation is permitted exclusively where it is necessary for:\na) access to a unique infrastructure;\nb) the use of a specialist competence not available in Circle 0;\nc) a vital emergency;\nd) a specialist training activity.\nThe derogation shall be reasoned and recorded. Where it entails a travel assignment, the travel time is counted as hours of CBO and gives no entitlement to CdR; for travel assignments carried out beyond the CBO, Article 43, paragraph 2, applies.\n6. The calculation of travel times is carried out in accordance with standardised criteria defined in the Mobility Technical Annex, approved by the Assembly."],[5,"art-26--tools-and-implements","Art. 26 — Tools and implements","","1. The tools and implements necessary for the performance of the CBO shall be provided by the Tools Storehouse, kept in working order and, where necessary, sanitised.\n2. A member shall not be required to purchase or provide personal tools in order to fulfil the CBO, unless their use is freely agreed and does not entail uncompensated costs or risks.\n3. Ordinary maintenance may be performed within the generic CBO; specialist maintenance is assigned to persons with validated competence.\n4. Breakage due to normal wear and tear does not entail personal charges. The distinction between normal wear and tear, negligence and wilful misconduct is established in accordance with the procedures of Code 4, with the technical support of the competent Circle."],[5,"art-27--safety-hygiene-and-safety-sheet-per-task","Art. 27 — Safety, hygiene and Safety Sheet per task","","1. Every task of Lists A, B and C shall be accompanied by a Safety Sheet in force, as provided for by Codes 6 and 14.\n2. The Sheet shall set out at least the relevant risks, the prevention and protection measures, the PPE and the collective protections needed, postures and load limits, exposures, the training required and the conditions for suspension.\n3. The Health Council validates the health aspects within its competence. The Safety Council validates the general safety aspects in accordance with Code 14. The Storehouse Council validates the organisational and logistical aspects. The approvals are limited to the respective fields and may be collected in an integrated Sheet.\n4. No CBO shall be performed without the PPE and the collective protections provided for by the Sheet and without the minimum training required by Article 28.\n5. In the event of serious and immediate risk, the rules on interruption and suspension provided for by Code 14 apply."],[5,"art-28--mandatory-minimum-training","Art. 28 — Mandatory minimum training","","1. Before performing generic CBO, the member shall complete basic training in safety, hygiene, the use of implements and the logistics of the Storehouses.\n2. The duration and content of basic training are defined in the Training Technical Annex, approved by the Health Council and the Storehouse Council. The duration of four hours indicated for the first application is provisional and remains valid only until it is revised.\n3. Before performing specialist CBO or using complex vehicles, the member shall complete specific modular training, validated by the relevant Competence Circle.\n4. Mandatory training is counted as CBO. Life-saving training is accessible without a paywall in accordance with Codes 5, 6 and 14."],[5,"art-29--accident-and-occupational-disease","Art. 29 — Accident and occupational disease","","1. In the event of an accident during the CBO or of an occupational disease connected with the CBO, the temporary suspension and the Solidarity Hours Bank provided for by Article 11 apply, as well as the provisions of Code 6.\n2. The Health Council and the Storehouse Council shall draw up a report on the causes and on the corrective measures. The analysis gives priority to prevention and to organisational or systemic causes and does not automatically presume individual responsibility.\n3. Any wilful misconduct or gross negligence is established in accordance with the procedures of Code 4. This Article does not confer on Code 6 sanctioning powers not provided for by that Code.\n4. The injured member shall not be reassigned to the same task that caused the accident without his or her request and without the Safety Sheet having been reviewed and, where necessary, amended.\n5. Individual health data are processed in accordance with Code 6. Public reports on events are aggregated and anonymised in accordance with Codes 7 and 14."],[5,"art-30--logistical-peaks-and-inter-circle-emergencies","Art. 30 — Logistical peaks and inter-Circle emergencies","","1. In the event of a seasonal peak or an emergency, additional capacity is, as a rule, obtained in the following order:\na) local volunteers, with any recognition in CdR provided for by Chapter V;\nb) volunteers from other Circles, with any recognition in CdR;\nc) temporary redistribution of the generic CBO according to objective criteria and in compliance with the maximum limit;\nd) request for the activation of the Solidarity Protocol pursuant to Article 36.2 of the Constitution, where ordinary capacity is insufficient.\n2. The order referred to in paragraph 1 is the ordinary one but may be derogated from. One or more levels may be skipped where they prove manifestly inadequate in view of the nature, the urgency or the specialisation required. The derogation shall be reasoned and recorded.\n3. The CBO may be performed temporarily for the benefit of another Circle only for the necessary duration and in compliance with competences, health limits and coordination procedures.\n4. Article 32 establishes which activities have priority when capacity is insufficient. This Article governs the ordinary arrangements for obtaining additional capacity."],[5,"art-31--transparency-and-data-protection","Art. 31 — Transparency and data protection","","1. The transport plan, the assignment of common vehicles, the state of the equipment and the Safety Sheets shall be published on the Network, in accordance with the applicable access levels.\n2. Reports on accidents and occupational diseases shall be published exclusively in aggregate and anonymised form.\n3. Individual health data are protected in accordance with Code 6. Logs and technical data are processed in accordance with Code 7. Information relating to general safety is managed in accordance with Code 14.\n4. Publication shall not include information allowing the direct or indirect identification of a person, except in the cases and under the procedures expressly provided for by the Codes."],[5,"art-32--hierarchy-of-logistical-priorities","Art. 32 — Hierarchy of logistical priorities","","1. When vehicles, energy, personnel or time are not sufficient to carry out all activities at the same time, the Community shall apply the following hierarchy of priorities:\na) vital emergencies and urgent health interventions;\nb) procurement and distribution of the goods included in the Sufficiency Basket;\nc) management of perishable goods at risk of waste;\nd) non-urgent health services and care of frail persons;\ne) maintenance of the critical infrastructure for energy, water and the Network;\nf) other logistical activities and deferrable activities.\n1-bis. The goods of the Basket that the Code or the Annex providing for them indicates as deferrable come after letter e) of paragraph 1.\n1-ter. By way of derogation from the order of paragraph 1, the care of frail persons provided for by letter d) precedes the management of the perishable goods of letter c). Basket food, including perishable food, remains in letter b) and, where its unavailability constitutes a vital emergency within the meaning of Article 4, paragraph 3, of Code 9, in letter a)."],[5,"art-32--hierarchy-of-logistical-priorities","Art. 32 — Hierarchy of logistical priorities","","1-quater. The priority scales of the sectoral Codes, numbered in levels, order the activities and uses internal to the sector and apply in compliance with paragraph 1 and with paragraphs 1-bis and 1-ter. In capacity conflicts between different sectors, and in every case of conflict, paragraph 1 and paragraphs 1-bis and 1-ter prevail. In every scale level 1 is the highest: the levels with a lower number are higher, and reductions proceed from the last level towards the first. Where a Code indicates a level without further specification, it means the level of the scale of the sectoral Code competent for the good or for the function. Paragraph 1 is referred to by letters.\n1-quinquies. In the Codes the expressions “levels 1 and 2 of the Basket”, “Basket of level 1–2” and “Basket 1-2” mean:\na) for energy, the uses of levels 1 and 2 of Article 2, paragraph 3, of Code 8;\nb) for food, the destinations of levels 1 and 2 of Article 2, paragraph 5, of Code 9;\nc) for care, the services of the Care Basket of levels 1 and 2 provided for by Article 4, paragraph 1, of Code 12;\nd) for the other goods and services of the Basket, including water, health, the Material Basket and logistics, the goods and services of letters a) and b) of paragraph 1, excluding those that are deferrable within the meaning of paragraph 1-bis.\nThe same expressions do not refer to the Circles."],[5,"art-32--hierarchy-of-logistical-priorities","Art. 32 — Hierarchy of logistical priorities","","2. The hierarchy constitutes a prevailing criterion for resolving conflicts of capacity, but it does not operate as an automatism devoid of any assessment of the concrete circumstances.\n3. A reasoned derogation is permitted where it is necessary to prevent a greater, immediate and documented harm, such as a water failure that compromises the Storehouses, an energy outage, a risk of collapse or an interruption of the cold chain. In that case:\na) the Storehouse Council decides, as the competent operational body;\nb) the opinion of the Health Council or of the Guarantee Circle shall be obtained in advance, where this does not entail a prejudicial delay; otherwise it shall be obtained without delay after the intervention;\nc) the derogation shall be promptly recorded on the Network with the reasons, the harm prevented and the expected duration;\nd) the measure shall last only for the time necessary to prevent the harm;\ne) the derogation shall be communicated to the first available Assembly for verification and, where necessary, ratification.\n4. The priorities of paragraph 1, letters a) and b), and the precedence of care provided for by paragraph 1-ter may be derogated from only in the cases and in accordance with the procedure of paragraph 3. For the subsequent letters, reasoned derogations are permitted on the basis of a technical assessment by the Storehouse Council."],[5,"art-33--subject-matter-and-principles","Art. 33 — Subject matter and principles","","1. This Chapter governs the infrastructural goods necessary for the performance of the CBO and for the functioning of the Storehouses and of Logistics, including energy, water, the information Network, common spaces and maintenance.\n2. Infrastructures are commons that cannot be appropriated and are managed in accordance with the principles of sufficiency, resilience, repairability and collective sovereignty.\n3. The management of infrastructures is coordinated with the sectoral Codes, in particular with Codes 7, 8, 10, 11 and 14."],[5,"art-34--energy-as-a-fundamental-common-good","Art. 34 — Energy as a fundamental common good","","1. Energy is a prerequisite for the continuity of the Basket and of essential services. The Community pursues energy autonomy through distributed renewable sources, common storage and efficiency of consumption.\n2. The technical management of installations and energy planning are carried out by the competent Energy and Water Council in accordance with Code 8. Coordination with logistics and with common infrastructure is ensured by the Storehouse Council.\n3. No Circle 0 shall be excluded from the minimum supply necessary for vital needs, except in the case of temporary material impossibility managed in accordance with the applicable Codes.\n4. Consumption may be recorded and optimised with the support of the Management AI, in compliance with Article 38 and Code 7."],[5,"art-35--preventive-maintenance-and-repairability","Art. 35 — Preventive maintenance and repairability","","1. Every common infrastructure has a Maintenance Sheet indicating the life cycle, the periodic checks, the maintenance activities and the competences needed.\n2. Preventive maintenance shall be scheduled with priority over corrective maintenance, except in emergencies or for reasoned technical needs.\n3. It is prohibited to purchase goods subject to planned obsolescence where a suitable and repairable alternative exists.\n4. Goods purchased shall be repairable, as far as technically possible, with common tools, accessible documentation and spare parts available in the Storehouses or obtainable without dependencies incompatible with Code 15.\n5. Conformity assessments and technical specifications are governed by Code 2 and by the sectoral Codes."],[5,"art-36--common-spaces-and-workshops","Art. 36 — Common spaces and workshops","","1. Common spaces, workshops, craft workshops and common kitchens are infrastructures serving the Community and the CBO.\n2. Shared use is the general rule. Access is organised in a manner that is transparent and compatible with safety, maintenance and continuity of services.\n3. Prolonged exclusive use of a common space beyond the threshold established by the regulation is subject to authorisation by the competent body, in accordance with transparent criteria approved by the Assembly.\n4. Any use of CdR connected with exclusive use is governed by Article 45.\n5. Workshops and craft workshops shall be used in compliance with the Safety Sheets, the STVs and the applicable training requirements."],[5,"art-37--information-network-and-digital-sovereignty","Art. 37 — Information Network and digital sovereignty","","1. The information Network is the common infrastructure connecting Circles, Storehouses, Logistics and the systems for recording requirements. It is run on community infrastructure and, as far as possible, with free software and open standards.\n2. Recourse to external proprietary private platforms for essential data or functions is prohibited, except for a temporary transition that is reasoned and recorded in accordance with Code 15.\n3. The data generated by the activities of the CBO and of the Storehouses are managed as common resources, with respect for the rights of the persons to whom the data relate and for the rules of Codes 6 and 7.\n4. Opening the source code does not eliminate the risk of concentration of control. The governance of modifications, distribution and access is governed by Article 38 and by Code 7."],[5,"art-38--management-ai-role-limits-and-governance","Art. 38 — Management AI: role, limits and governance","","1. The Management AI is a support tool. It may process data, formulate proposals, optimise resources, generate forecasts and flag anomalies. It does not constitute a decision-making body.\n2. The Management AI is prohibited from:\na) deciding the individual allocation of the CBO;\nb) denying or conditioning access to the Basket;\nc) processing non-anonymised individual health data, except in the cases and in the manner strictly authorised by Codes 6 and 7;\nd) adopting decisions without human validation where they affect persons, essential resources or rights;\ne) producing individual profiles, reputational rankings or behavioural predictions.\n3. Every relevant proposal of the AI shall be traced, explainable within the available technical limits and contestable. The competent human body may amend or reject it.\n4. Technical governance is regulated as follows:\na) the source code is open, versioned and accompanied by documentation of the modifications;\nb) modifications to the code in production are made by the Network Council in accordance with Code 7 and are subject to independent audit by the Guarantee Circle as regards the procedural aspects;\nc) updates are distributed in accordance with a procedure approved by the Assembly, which provides for prior verification and the possibility of restoring the previous version;\nd) servers, access keys and datasets are administered by the competent parties in accordance with Code 7; accesses are recorded and verifiable;\ne) the Assembly may order the suspension, replacement or deactivation of the system. The Guarantee Circle may order precautionary measures within the limits of Code 4 where there is a procedural defect or a risk to rights and guarantees;\nf) every version is subject to verification before adoption.\n5. No essential function governed by Articles 1–32 may depend exclusively on the AI. In the event of unavailability, the manual or degraded procedures prepared by the Storehouse Council and verified by the Assembly apply.\n6. This Article is coordinated with Code 7. In the event of conflict on aspects of network, IT security or data processing, the competences and guarantees established by Code 7 apply."],[5,"art-39--resilience-redundancy-and-decentralisation","Art. 39 — Resilience, redundancy and decentralisation","","1. Every critical infrastructure has a continuity and redundancy plan. Where technically possible, the plan provides for at least two independent sources or routes for energy, water and the Network.\n2. Each Circle 0 pursues a minimum autonomy capacity of seventy-two hours for vital goods and energy, on the basis of local conditions and the applicable STVs.\n3. The parameter referred to in paragraph 2 is an initial one and shall be distinguished, in the subsequent technical annexes, from the parameters of minimum autonomy, resilience, strategic reserve and restoration capacity.\n4. Critical dependencies on external parties or infrastructures are recorded in the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15. For each critical dependency a reduction or substitution plan shall be prepared in accordance with the same Code.\n5. Data relating to specific vulnerabilities of infrastructures shall not be published in such a way as to create security risks; they are accessible to the competent parties in accordance with a traced procedure."],[5,"art-40--open-standards-and-transparency","Art. 40 — Open standards and transparency","","1. Common infrastructures shall adopt, as far as technically possible, open and interoperable standards.\n2. Infrastructure plans, aggregate consumption, maintenance, incidents and dependencies shall be published, in compliance with data protection and the security of infrastructures.\n3. Information that may expose critical infrastructures to risks shall be made accessible only to authorised parties, with recorded access.\n4. Purchases above the thresholds established by Code 3 shall be accompanied by a comparative report that considers at least repairability, consumption, durability, interoperability and availability of spare parts.\n5. The report shall be published in accordance with the applicable transparency level."],[5,"art-41--systemic-link","Art. 41 — Systemic link","","1. The infrastructures, energy and the Network governed by this Chapter support the performance of the CBO, the management of the Storehouses and the logistics governed by the preceding Chapters.\n2. The malfunction of an infrastructure that compromises or risks compromising the provision of the Basket constitutes a ground for the activation of the priorities provided for by Article 32.\n3. Where necessary, the Storehouse Council applies the technical necessity clause of Article 32, paragraph 3, documenting the reason, the duration and the effects of the measure.\n4. The activation of the priorities does not transfer to the Storehouse Council the competences assigned to the Energy and Water, Network, Health and Safety Councils or to the other bodies by their respective Codes."],[5,"art-42--nature-and-limits-of-the-cdr","Art. 42 — Nature and limits of the CdR","","1. The Recognition Contribution, hereinafter “CdR”, is the personal instrument by which the Community recognises the contribution rendered beyond the CBO, pursuant to Articles 3.5 and 8 of the Constitution. It is not money, a wage or consideration and does not measure the dignity or the rights of the person.\n2. The CdR cannot be transferred, assigned, lent or inherited. It does not bear interest and is not convertible into external means of payment or into UCR.\n3. The CdR shall not condition:\na) access to the Sufficiency Basket and to the rights of Article 1.2 of the Constitution;\nb) the right to vote and to deliberate;\nc) access to health care, to learning and to the Guarantee;\nd) sortition for offices and for the Councils;\ne) the priorities of Article 32.\n4. Possession of a competence, a qualification or an office does not give entitlement to CdR. Article 8 of Code 5 applies."],[5,"art-43--award","Art. 43 — Award","","1. Entitlement to CdR arises from activities additional to the CBO that produce goods for the Common Storehouses or services for the Community, where the work, good or service is validated pursuant to Article 9 of the Constitution and Code 2.\n1-bis. The services for the Community referred to in paragraph 1 include cultural, artistic and recreational activities offered in the presence of the Community and open to all its members, such as performances, concerts, readings and celebrations. The following rules apply:\na) CdR accrue to those who perform or lead the activity, not to those who attend or take part in it;\nb) the hours performed in presence are counted and, for preparation, no more than the same number again;\nc) validation takes place pursuant to Article 9.2 of the Constitution; those who use the service are represented by those who attended. If it is positive, the CdR depend solely on the hours. If it is negative, the suspension provided for by Article 9, paragraph 4, of Code 2 does not apply;\nd) paragraph 3 does not apply;\ne) the public table sets an annual limit per person; on first application, sixty CdR;\nf) activities of worship, activities reserved to a part of the members and time already recognised as CBO pursuant to Article 2, paragraph 3, of Code 5 are excluded.\n2. One hour of validated activity gives rise to one CdR. The supplements of Article 10 apply for unpleasant, arduous or night tasks. For travel assignments carried out beyond the CBO, the public table establishes a supplement within the same limits. Travel assignments carried out within the CBO, including those of Article 25, paragraph 5, give no entitlement to CdR: the travel time is counted as hours of CBO.\n3. For works and services of particular common utility, the Assembly may award a utility recognition, reasoned and published, within the limit established by the public table and in any event not exceeding the number of hours actually spent. The assessment considers the principles of Care, Autonomy, Conviviality and Meaning referred to in Article 9.1 of the Constitution.\n4. The ordinary CBO and the civic functions of List D do not give entitlement to CdR.\n5. The award is proposed by the Storehouse Council or by the competent Council, with the advisory support of the Management AI, and is validated with the participation of those who benefit from the good or service, in accordance with Article 9.2 of the Constitution. An award made exclusively by an AI is prohibited."],[5,"art-44--register-cap-and-decay","Art. 44 — Register, cap and decay","","1. Each person has a personal and confidential register of his or her own CdR. Only aggregate data are published.\n2. The personal balance shall not exceed the cap established by the General Assembly pursuant to Article 14 of Code 3. On first application the cap is two hundred and forty CdR. The balance is the sum of the current values of the unused CdR, verified at the moment of the award. CdR are awarded up to the cap; beyond it no further CdR are awarded, including the supplements of Article 10 and of Article 43, paragraph 2.\n3. Unused CdR retain their value for twelve months from accrual. From the completion of the twelfth month the value is reduced in proportion to time, by one twelfth of the initial value per month, until it is extinguished at the completion of the twenty-fourth month. Accrual coincides with the day on which the activity was performed. For the purposes of use and of the cap, each CdR counts for the value it has at that moment.\n4. The parameters of paragraphs 2 and 3 are calibrated every year by the General Assembly of Circle 2, within any limits set by the General Assembly of Circle 3. The calibration shall not eliminate the cap or the decay.\n5. On the death of the person or on the cessation of member status, the CdR are extinguished.\n6. During the suspension provided for by Article 7.3 of the Constitution, ordered pursuant to Article 29, paragraph 4-bis, of Code 4, CdR may be neither accrued nor used."],[5,"art-45--use","Art. 45 — Use","","1. CdR may be used exclusively for:\na) access to the non-necessary goods and services available in limited quantity, pursuant to Article 18, paragraph 1, letter b);\nb) the prolonged exclusive use of a common space, pursuant to Article 36;\nc) ordinary hospitality in the Bridge Houses, in accordance with Code 11.\n2. Use is commensurate with the reproduction cost of the good or service, expressed in UCR and published in the table of Article 46. For the sole purposes of use, one CdR at full value corresponds to one UCR.\n3. CdR that have been used are cancelled and are not awarded to anyone. The CdR with the earliest accrual are used first.\n4. Non-necessary goods and services remain accessible even without CdR, by booking or transparent waiting list. The use of CdR confers precedence within a share of the availability established by the Assembly, not exceeding one half.\n5. It is prohibited to exchange CdR between persons, to use them as consideration for services between persons and to establish markets, auctions or variable prices in CdR. Violations are dealt with in accordance with Code 4."],[5,"art-46--public-table-transparency-and-appeal","Art. 46 — Public table, transparency and appeal","","1. The public table indicates the supplements, the limits of the utility recognition, the cap, the decay and the cost in UCR of non-necessary goods and services. It is approved by the Assembly of Circle 1 in compliance with the parameters calibrated by the General Assembly.\n2. Every quarter the CdR awarded, used and extinguished and their distribution by bands shall be published in aggregate form, without identifying persons.\n3. If more than one fifth of the existing CdR is found to be held by fewer than one twentieth of the persons, the Assembly shall review the table and the cap.\n4. An appeal against the award or the refusal of CdR may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days of the communication, in accordance with Code 4."],[5,"art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use","Art. 47 — Personal-use goods and ownership of use","","1. The personal-use goods referred to in Article 10.3 of the Constitution are not counted in the Storehouses and shall not be collectivised. The limits of Article 10.4 of the Constitution apply.\n2. Ownership of use of a dwelling, a craft workshop, a field or a collective tool, pursuant to Article 10.2 of the Constitution, is registered with the Storehouse Council and, as regards territorial aspects, in the Land Register provided for by Code 10. It is founded on actual and continuous use.\n3. Ownership of use ceases by renunciation, by non-use continuing beyond twelve months without justification or by violation of the limits of Article 10.4 of the Constitution, established in accordance with Code 4. The main dwelling shall not be revoked without the assignment of another adequate dwelling, except in the cases of paragraph 3-bis.\n3-bis. Ownership of use of the dwelling also ceases upon transfer to another Community pursuant to Article 49, upon the declaration of Article 4.8 of the Constitution and upon the termination of status provided for by Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution. The housing continuity of those living in the same Circle 0 is safeguarded, pursuant to Article 3.7 of the Constitution. In the case of transfer the dwelling is vacated at the end of the Bridge House allocation of Article 49, paragraph 3; in the case of the declaration of Article 4.8, at the end of the hospitality of Article 4.9, letter b), of the Constitution, unless the person requests the Autonomous Coexistence Pact: in that case they remain in the dwelling until the handover of the portion, pursuant to Article 4-bis, paragraph 7, of Code 10, and the dwelling may be entrusted to them with the Pact pursuant to Article 4.9-bis of the Constitution. For those who remain without a Pact, Article 4.9-ter of the Constitution applies. In the case of Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution, Article 29, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 4 applies.\n4. The prohibition of private accumulation beyond seven days of the family requirement, provided for by Article 10.5 of the Constitution, concerns goods drawn from the Storehouses. This is without prejudice to reasoned needs, in particular those relating to health, season or isolation."],[5,"art-47-bis--adequate-dwelling","Art. 47-bis — Adequate dwelling","","1. The dwelling provided for by Article 1.2, letter a), of the Constitution is adequate when it ensures at least:\na) a private space that can be closed for every person from fourteen years of age; two persons share it only if both request it;\nb) protection from cold, heat and damp sufficient not to harm health;\nc) access to the Water Basket and to the Energy Basket provided for by Code 8;\nd) spaces for cooking and for hygiene, own or shared within Circle 0;\ne) the accessibility the person needs, in accordance with his or her individual care plan provided for by Code 12.\n2. The quantitative standards are determined by the Assembly on a joint proposal from the Storehouse Council and the Energy and Water Council, with the opinion of the Health Council and of the Habitat and Territory Council. On first application every dwelling comprises at least a living room and a separate bedroom; a studio flat does not meet the standard. Each Circle 0 has at least one bathroom for every four persons or fraction thereof; beyond the first, bathrooms may be facilities with a WC and washbasin. Bathrooms may be shared among the dwellings of the same Circle 0. When a Circle 0 lives in more than one dwelling, the floor area is calculated on the total number of its persons. The habitable floor area is not less than thirty square metres for one person, forty-five for two and sixty for three; it increases by fifteen square metres for each additional person up to the fifth and by ten for each person beyond the fifth. Each private space provided for by letter a) of paragraph 1 is not less than nine square metres. Subsequent determinations may not fall below the first-application values without the favourable opinion of the Health Council.\n3. The standards of paragraph 2 do not apply to the equipped stopping areas provided for by Article 6-ter, paragraph 6, of Code 11.\n4. A dwelling that does not comply with paragraph 1 or with the standards of paragraph 2 may not for that reason be revoked or refused. The Community includes its upgrading in housing planning, with a time limit not exceeding thirty-six months, which may be extended once only by a reasoned resolution of the Assembly. An appeal to the Guarantee Circle lies against failure to upgrade. As regards temperature, Articles 4 and 10 of Code 8 remain in force.\n5. For the purposes of Article 47, paragraph 3, of this Code and of Articles 29 and 34 of Code 4, a dwelling that complies with paragraph 1 is adequate. If none is available, the best available dwelling is assigned, and its upgrading takes precedence in planning.\n6. Upgrading takes place in compliance with Articles 2 and 5 of Code 10: the reuse and recovery of buildings precede any new construction. Until Codes 8 and 12 are activated, letters a), b) and d) of paragraph 1 and, of paragraph 2, the floor area, the minimum composition and the number of bathrooms apply."],[5,"art-47-ter--housing-planning-and-allocation","Art. 47-ter — Housing planning and allocation","","1. The housing planning provided for by Article 3.7 of the Constitution is proposed by the Storehouse Council, through the Textile and Housing Storehouse, with the mandatory opinion of the Habitat and Territory Council and of the Care Council, and is approved by the Assembly of Circle 1. It is updated at least once a year and includes the inventory of the dwellings in use and of their adequacy within the meaning of Article 47-bis, the vacant dwellings, the waiting list and the programme of adaptations and recoveries.\n2. The Storehouse Council allocates dwellings by reasoned decision, according to real needs and in the following order:\na) persons and Circles 0 without a dwelling, those living in a dwelling that endangers health and persons who have to leave their dwelling because of a protection measure of Codes 4 or 12;\nb) need assessed according to the composition of the Circle 0, the accessibility required and health conditions, the latter certified by the Health Council without stating their content;\nc) proximity to the Circle 0, to places of care and to the person’s CBO;\nd) where needs are equal, the order of registration on the waiting list.\nAllocation may not depend on hours of CBO, on CdR or on the offices held.\n3. The waiting list is published in anonymous form, with an identifier, the date of registration, the criterion applied and the position, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2. Health information is not made public.\n4. Where dwellings are insufficient:\na) no one is left without shelter: pending allocation, the best available dwelling within the meaning of Article 47-bis, paragraph 5, or a place in a Bridge House is assigned, without the time limit of Article 12.3 of the Constitution, and failing that an equipped stopping area pursuant to Article 6-ter, paragraph 6, of Code 11;\nb) planning gives precedence to the reuse and recovery of buildings, pursuant to Articles 2 and 5 of Code 10;\nc) exchanges of dwellings between Circles 0 take place only with the consent of those concerned, without prejudice to Article 47, paragraphs 3 and 3-bis;\nd) if the shortage lasts more than six months, Circle 1 informs Circle 2, which assists in the search for other placements.\n5. An appeal to the Guarantee Circle pursuant to Article 17 of Code 3 is permitted against the allocation, the refusal and the position on the waiting list. The appeal does not take from anyone the dwelling they already occupy.\n6. For the purposes of Article 32, paragraph 1, letter b), the allocation of a dwelling to a person who has none and the repair that makes a dwelling habitable are goods of the Basket. The adaptations of Article 47-bis, paragraph 4, that do not concern health are deferrable within the meaning of Article 32, paragraph 1-bis.\n7. The destination Community provided for by Article 49, paragraph 4, allocates the dwelling in accordance with this Article."],[5,"art-48--succession-to-goods-of-use","Art. 48 — Succession to goods of use","","1. On the death of the person, personal effects, clothing and individual work tools are destined for the Circle 0 to which the person belonged or for the persons designated during his or her lifetime, pursuant to Article 3.6 of the Constitution. The designation shall be made in writing or recorded in the personal AI Node, in the presence of two witnesses.\n2. The designation shall not concern Commons, the dwelling or highly specialised tools. Goods that exceed the personal use of the person receiving them, or that would reconstitute an accumulation prohibited by Article 10.4 of the Constitution, are conferred on the Common Storehouse.\n3. The dwelling cannot be inherited. It returns to the housing planning of Circle 1, safeguarding the continuity of residence of the cohabitants belonging to the same Circle 0, pursuant to Article 3.7 of the Constitution.\n4. Highly specialised tools and goods not reused in Circle 0 are conferred on the Common Storehouse of Circle 1, pursuant to Article 3.8 of the Constitution.\n5. Digital memories and works are kept in the Open Archive pursuant to Article 3.9 of the Constitution. The personal AI Node is reset pursuant to Article 21.5 of the Constitution."],[5,"art-49--relocation-bridge-cbo-and-bridge-house-allocation","Art. 49 — Relocation, bridge CBO and Bridge House allocation","","1. A person who leaves a Base Community to move to another retains the personal-use goods lawfully held, pursuant to Article 5.2 of the Constitution.\n2. For thirty days the person is entitled to the bridge CBO: the CBO obligation in the Community of origin is suspended and the contribution is performed in the Community of destination or of transit, to an extent compatible with the relocation.\n3. For the same period the person is entitled to the Bridge House allocation: accommodation in a Bridge House, access to the Basket and the means necessary to move personal effects, in accordance with Code 11. The provision does not require CdR.\n4. The Community of destination shall assign a dwelling for use according to availability. Until the assignment, hospitality continues pursuant to Article 12.3 of the Constitution. If availability is lacking, Circle 2 shall assist in the search for another placement.\n5. Relocation shall not give rise to annotations or prejudicial consequences for the person."],[6,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","1. This Annex governs the material production, repair, reuse, adaptation, maintenance and management of tools and materials necessary for the Basket, health, the network and energy.\n2. For the purposes of this Annex, production means the manufacture, repair, reuse, adaptation, maintenance, assembly, disassembly and conservation of objects, tools, components, PPE, furnishings and parts of infrastructure.\n3. This Annex pursues the following objectives:\na) to guarantee federated material and repair autonomy for the Basket and for the CBO of List A;\nb) to give priority to reuse and repair over the production of new goods;\nc) to prevent rent on essential tools and to reduce critical proprietary dependencies;\nd) to ensure the safety, traceability and reproducibility of essential production designs.\n4. The following constitute inalienable infrastructural commons:\na) the Base Workshop;\nb) the Federated Advanced Workshops;\nc) the Distributed Micro-stations;\nd) the Materials Storehouse;\ne) the inventory of essential tools.\nA produced object is a common good when it belongs to the Material Basket or is necessary for an essential function pursuant to Article 4. The other objects are goods for use managed in accordance with Code 1. The tools necessary for the production of the Basket do not constitute a commodity.\n5. This Annex governs material production and maintenance. The following remain unaffected:\na) the competences of Code 1 over the Basket, access to goods and general priorities;\nb) the competences of Code 2 over quality, STVs and the technical validation of goods;\nc) the competences of Code 6 over health risks;\nd) the competences of Code 14 over the general safety of persons, machines, tasks and environments;\ne) the competences of Codes 7 and 8 over networks and energy."],[6,"art-2--principles-of-material-production","Art. 2 — Principles of material production","","1. Material sovereignty. Every Circle 1 develops local capacity for production, maintenance and repair. The federated network is used to share competences, advanced equipment and capacities not reasonably available at local level, avoiding structural dependencies.\n2. Reuse before new production. Before producing a new good, the Production Council verifies whether the requirement can be met through reuse, repair or adaptation. The production of a new object is permitted when reuse is not possible, is not adequate or does not guarantee the safety required.\n3. Priority of use. The general priorities are those of Code 1, Article 32. Within production activities, capacity and materials are ordered in the following levels:\na) level 1: goods of the Material Basket and life-saving PPE; the deferrable goods of Article 4, paragraph 1-bis, letter e), follow letter b);\nb) level 2: maintenance of the critical infrastructure for health, network, energy and the Health Storehouse;\nc) level 3: safety measures and tools necessary for maintenance;\nd) level 4: goods and tools for the CBO of List A;\ne) level 5: any other reasoned request.\nIn the event of scarcity, the reduction of non-essential production activities proceeds progressively from level 5 towards level 1. The reduction is resolved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Production Council and may not compromise the higher priorities defined by Code 1.\n4. Open hardware and reproducibility. Every functionally relevant and standardised object or component produced for levels 1–4 has documentation sufficient to allow its study, repair, modification and reproduction. The documentation comprises, as necessary, the STV, drawings, the list of materials and operating procedures. For small parts that are not relevant, a reference to an accessible standard and to a replacement procedure is sufficient. Reproducibility shall in any case be guaranteed for the goods of levels 1–4.\n5. Transparency. Every workshop keeps an up-to-date inventory of tools and materials, a maintenance register and an aggregate balance of production.\n6. Reversibility and repairability. In design, preference is given to objects that can be disassembled, repaired and reused, with standard components where these are available and compatible with safety and quality requirements."],[6,"art-3--production-council","Art. 3 — Production Council","","1. Every Circle 1 establishes a Production Council composed of three to five members with competences of at least level L2 or L3 in one or more of the following areas: manufacturing, woodworking, metalworking and mechanics, textiles, basic electronics and safety of production activities.\n2. The members are drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months after the end of their term of office. Replacement and the handling of conflicts of interest follow the same Article and Article 16 of Code 3.\n3. The Production Council:\na) looks after the Materials Storehouse and the Material Basket;\nb) prepares the technical documentation necessary for the Safety Sheets of the machines and of the production procedures;\nc) draws up the technical proposals and the production documentation necessary for the STVs, submitting them to Code 2 for the validation within its competence;\nd) manages the material balance and the production capacity;\ne) organises L1 training for the safety of workshop activities, in coordination with Codes 6 and 14;\nf) feeds the Dependency Register governed by Code 15;\ng) coordinates its activities with the Energy and Water Council of Code 8, the Network Council of Code 7 and the Health Council of Code 6, as well as with the Safety Council of Code 14.\n4. The Production Council does not dispose of goods unrelated to production and does not exercise disciplinary powers. It may not access individual health data, except for the information that is strictly necessary and legitimately accessible for a specific function.\n5. The Production Council is prohibited from moderating, removing or altering deliberative or political content on grounds of merit, orientation or dissent. Only the immediate technical interventions necessary to prevent a safety risk are permitted, provided that they are documented and limited to what is strictly necessary.\n6. The acts of the Council are divided into:\na) bound technical acts, adopted in execution of an STV or a Safety Sheet already in force, documented and published without a new deliberative procedure;\nb) discretionary proposals or decisions, subject to the consensus procedures of Code 3, after publication for at least seventy-two hours and recording of the outcome in the minutes.\nSilence does not constitute consent if the proposal has not been published or if the prescribed time limit has not elapsed."],[6,"art-4--material-basket-and-fulfilment-priorities","Art. 4 — Material Basket and fulfilment priorities","","1. The Material Basket is a component of the Sufficiency Basket governed by Code 1. It comprises at least:\na) the objects necessary for the conservation and cooking of food;\nb) minimum furnishings;\nc) essential linen and tableware;\nd) basic tools for domestic life;\ne) basic PPE;\nf) the components necessary for the maintenance of the functions of levels 1 and 2;\ng) essential spare parts for the microgrid and for the Network node;\nh) clothing and footwear suited to the climate and the season;\ni) personal hygiene products, including menstrual products and those for early childhood and for incontinence.\n1-bis. The following rules apply to the goods of letters h) and i):\na) by way of derogation from Article 1, paragraph 4, from delivery they are personal-use goods pursuant to Article 10.3 of the Constitution;\nb) Article 4 of Code 2 on the precedence of reuse and Articles 13.5 and 14.1 of the Constitution apply: washable or compostable products are preferred, and single-use products are permitted only under the conditions of Article 14.1;\nc) they are procured and distributed through the Textile and Housing Storehouse provided for by Article 14 of Code 1 and are included in the annual Technical Annex of Article 18, paragraph 2, of that Code;\nd) data on the withdrawal of the products of letter i) are neither published nor communicated in a form that identifies the person;"],[6,"art-4--material-basket-and-fulfilment-priorities","Art. 4 — Material Basket and fulfilment priorities","","e) they are deferrable within the meaning of Article 32, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 1, except for the products that the Health Council indicates as necessary for health; in the order of paragraph 5 they follow letter b).\n2. Access to the Material Basket may not be made conditional on the CBO, on the possession of specific competences or on the presence of the person in a particular place, within the limits established by Code 1. The unavailability of a good necessary for a vital function activates the urgent priority procedure provided for by the competent Codes.\n3. The minimum list of the Material Basket and the related stock thresholds are defined in an STV. The thresholds are expressed in days of autonomy and safety margin; for the goods of letter h) of paragraph 1, as an allocation per person per year. The Assembly approves them on a proposal from the Production Council and obtains the opinion of the Health Council for PPE, for hygiene products and for goods intended for frail persons. For letters h) and i) of paragraph 1 the proposal lies with the Storehouse Council.\n4. The list and the thresholds are reviewed at least once a year and whenever significant variations occur in the requirement, in availability or in safety conditions. The quantities and criteria are published, in compliance with safety needs.\n5. Requests are fulfilled in the following order, consistently with Code 1, Article 32:"],[6,"art-4--material-basket-and-fulfilment-priorities","Art. 4 — Material Basket and fulfilment priorities","","a) goods of the Material Basket and life-saving PPE; the deferrable goods of paragraph 1-bis, letter e), follow letter b);\nb) maintenance of critical infrastructure;\nc) safety and ordinary maintenance;\nd) tools necessary for the CBO of List A;\ne) further reasoned requests."],[6,"art-5--stv-reuse-and-supply-of-materials","Art. 5 — STV, reuse and supply of materials","","1. Every object produced or repaired for levels 1–4 conforms to an STV in force or to the applicable technical documentation. The STV defines, in relation to the good:\na) the materials permitted and their provenance;\nb) the drawings, dimensions and tolerances;\nc) the life cycle and the maintenance requirements;\nd) the arrangements for disassembly and repair;\ne) the risks and the limits of use.\nThe validation of the STV follows Code 2.\n2. Preference is given, where compatible with safety and quality, to:\na) reused or recovered raw materials and components;\nb) recycled materials;\nc) wood from sustainable local supply chains;\nd) recovered metals and fabrics;\ne) refurbished electronic components;\nf) recycled filaments for printing, where suitable.\n3. For goods of level 4 or 5, documented verification of the possibility of reuse, repair or adaptation is mandatory before proceeding to the production of a new object. For levels 1–3 reuse is strongly preferred, unless it compromises health or electrical safety, as attested by the competent Council.\n4. The verification is traced in the technical documentation or in the applicable STV. Self-building is promoted when it takes place with validated L2 competences, standard components and repairable and documented solutions.\n5. For levels 4 and 5, the production of new objects that depend structurally on proprietary components not repairable locally is prohibited. A derogation may be granted by the General Assembly by qualified majority, on documented technical grounds and on the following conditions:\na) the impossibility of an adequate alternative solution is demonstrated;\nb) the derogation has a fixed duration not exceeding twelve months;\nc) a compensatory measure favouring reuse or repairability is adopted;\nd) a binding substitution plan is approved;\ne) the plan is reviewed at least every six months.\nThe derogation may not exceed twenty-four months in total, except in an extreme emergency pursuant to Article 2-bis of Code 6. Every resulting critical dependency is entered in the Dependency Register in accordance with Code 15."],[6,"art-6--workshops-federated-network-and-measurement","Art. 6 — Workshops, federated network and measurement","","1. Every Circle 1 activates a Base Workshop, which may be divided into several stations, equipped at least with:\na) hand tools for wood and light metal;\nb) a station for the repair of household appliances and bicycles;\nc) a station for sewing and textile work;\nd) basic measuring instruments;\ne) an L1/L2 maintenance bench for the Network and Energy functions;\nf) a store for the Material Basket;\ng) a manual stop control for the equipment, where relevant.\nThe fitting-out and use comply with the Safety Sheets and the requirements of Code 14.\n2. The Confederation ensures the effective access of every Circle 1 to a Federated Advanced Workshop. Planning is defined for each Circle 2 by its General Assembly, on a proposal from the shared Production Council provided for by Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 3, and takes account of population, distances, territory and capacity. As an ordinary criterion, at least one Advanced Workshop is provided for every three or four Circles 1 of Circle 2, unless a different planning is adopted with reasons.\n3. The Advanced Workshop may include welding, light forging, machine tools, electronics and printed circuits, large-format 3D printing, laser cutting and the production of complex PPE, within the limits of the applicable STVs and Safety Sheets. The use of the equipment is reserved to persons with the required training and authorisation. Access may be booked by the Circles 1 of Circle 2; the inventory and the management are federated.\n4. The Distributed Micro-stations include, by way of example, common tool cabinets, tool libraries, small 3D printers, sewing stations and disassembly and reuse centres. They are registered with the Production Council and operate in accordance with STVs and Safety Sheets appropriate to their activity.\n5. The collection of data is permitted exclusively for safety, the material balance, maintenance and the verification of the Basket. The data are aggregated by workshop, by Circle 1 or by Circle 2. Individual behavioural profiling is prohibited. Retention periods and accesses are minimised and defined in the STVs, in conformity with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n6. Stocks of materials are sized in relation to the Basket and to the needs of levels 1–3 and may not be built up for speculative purposes. No Circle may be organised in such a way as to depend structurally on a single other Circle for the Material Basket."],[6,"art-7--safety-ppe-and-training","Art. 7 — Safety, PPE and training","","1. No activity in a workshop may be carried out without a Safety Sheet in force. The Production Council prepares the technical documentation relating to the machine and the procedure. The validation of the general safety aspects falls to the Safety Council in accordance with Code 14. The Health Council gives the mandatory opinion on the health aspects, on PPE and on the consequences for health. The validation of safety does not transfer the technical competences over production.\n2. The PPE necessary for workshop activities is a common good managed in the Health Storehouse in accordance with the competent Codes, assigned free of charge, maintained and traced. The unavailability of an item of PPE prescribed as essential entails the suspension of the activity concerned, not a sanction against the person.\n3. The minimum training required is as follows:\na) L1 to access the Base Workshop and the Micro-stations;\nb) L2 to use the advanced machines and to carry out the activities that the STV classifies as specialist;\nc) L3 to design or validate STVs and to run an Advanced Workshop, within the limits of the competences provided for by Codes 2, 5 and 14.\n4. Anyone who detects an immediate risk may interrupt the activity and report it to the competent Council, and may not suffer any detriment for an interruption made in good faith.\n5. Digital fabricators are fitted with the open-source and inspectable hardware filter provided for by Article 16.1 of the Constitution, which prevents exclusively the fabrication of weapons and their components. It is prohibited to fabricate such objects or to hold their designs. Tampering with the filter constitutes a Grade 4 offence."],[6,"art-8--register-of-material-dependencies","Art. 8 — Register of material Dependencies","","1. The Production Council feeds the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15, distinguishing at least:\na) material dependency: dependency on external raw materials necessary for the Basket;\nb) technological dependency: dependency on proprietary machines, closed firmware, tools not repairable locally or imported critical components;\nc) logistical dependency: unavailability within the Confederation of materials or spare parts that cannot be regenerated locally.\n2. Every critical dependency is accompanied by an exit plan with a person responsible, a fixed final deadline, a verifiable objective and continuity measures. Dependencies that affect the Basket or PPE have priority.\n3. At least one annual federated audit on material autonomy is carried out by independent L3 members drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4."],[6,"art-9--prohibitions-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) derive rent from the essential tools of the Basket or from the use of the Base Workshop;\nb) impose paywalls on the STVs of life-saving objects and on essential PPE;\nc) claim exclusive rights or patents over the essential knowledge necessary for the production of the Basket;\nd) accumulate materials or tools individually beyond the technical limit resolved by the Assembly where this compromises the availability of the Basket or the balance between the workshops;\ne) refuse the repair of a good of the Basket as a sanctioning measure;\nf) transfer control of the Base Workshop or of the Federated Advanced Workshop to parties external to the Confederation.\n2. Violations are established and dealt with in accordance with Code 4. Suspension or deactivation measures are proportionate and reasoned and, where technically possible, ensure the continuity of levels 1–3; in any case the continuity of levels 1 and 2 is guaranteed, unless continuation entails a serious and immediate risk.\n3. A machine may be switched off completely where this is necessary to prevent further harm. For critical or vital functions, the suspension is accompanied, where technically possible, by a continuity procedure or by a conforming substitute function.\n4. Appeal to the Guarantee is permitted within fourteen days in accordance with the procedures of Code 4 for procedural defects, manifest technical unreasonableness, discrimination or disproportion."],[6,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-coordination","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and coordination","","1. This Annex is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Articles 18 and 19, for access to and scarcity of the Basket, and Article 32 for the hierarchy of priorities;\nb) Code 2 for STVs and quality validation;\nc) Code 3 for resolutions;\nd) Code 4 for the Guarantee and remedies;\ne) Code 5 for L2 and L3 competences;\nf) Code 6 for PPE and health aspects;\ng) Code 7 for the Network and data processing;\nh) Code 8 for energy and the microgrid;\ni) Codes 12 and 14 for frailties, general safety and Safety Sheets;\nj) Code 15 for the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, every Circle 1:\na) inventories existing tools and equipment;\nb) activates a minimum Base Workshop, with a provisional STV and a Safety Sheet conforming to the applicable transitional rules;\nc) guarantees the minimum Material Basket, including by means of reuse, provided that it is safe and conforming.\n3. Within twelve months of entry into force, in every Circle 2 the shared Production Council provided for by Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 3 activates at least one minimum Advanced Workshop in accordance with Article 6, paragraph 2, a federated inventory and a node of the Network of workshops, in coordination with the transition provided for by Code 8. If Circle 2 is constituted after entry into force, the time limit runs from its constitution. For documented overlaps of the transitional burdens of Codes 8 and 9, the General Assembly of Circle 2 may grant a reasoned extension not exceeding six months, with a binding plan and a guarantee of the continuity of the Basket.\n4. Within twenty-four months of entry into force, an exit plan is adopted for the critical material dependencies relating to the Basket and to PPE, with a fixed final deadline for each dependency.\n5. The provisional validation of production competences is governed by Code 5, Article 10, paragraph 2. The time limits stated in this Annex are six months for L3 competences and twelve months for L2 competences, unless Code 5, which prevails, provides otherwise.\n6. Machines, functions and methods of management that do not conform to Article 9 are brought into conformity or deactivated within ninety days, in accordance with Code 4. Where an immediate deactivation would compromise a vital function of level 1 or 2, the Assembly approves within the same time limit a binding plan for substitution or progressive deactivation, with a fixed deadline and guaranteed continuity. The immediate suspension of an activity that presents a serious and immediate risk remains mandatory."],[7,"art-1--subject-matter-and-principle-of-qualitative-sufficiency","Art. 1 — Subject matter and principle of qualitative sufficiency","","1. This Code governs the criteria and procedures by which goods and tools are assessed for the purposes of their admission to, retention in, suspension from or exclusion from the Common Storehouses provided for by Code 1.\n2. Code 1 governs the requirement for, production, conservation, distribution and handling of goods. This Code establishes the quality criteria and the procedures for determining which goods may enter and remain in the common system.\n3. Services provided by parties external to the Confederation are governed by Chapter III. The STVs provided for by the Codes for objects other than goods are governed by Article 3-bis.\n4. Quality assessment is carried out in relation to actual need and may not be used to introduce requirements unrelated to safety, suitability, durability, repairability or the other criteria provided for by this Code.\n5. The Quality Council is the technical Council competent for the functions of this Code. It is composed of three to five persons drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3."],[7,"art-2--quality-criteria","Art. 2 — Quality criteria","","1. The quality of a good is assessed by considering the following criteria jointly:\na) actual usefulness in relation to the need;\nb) durability and length of the life cycle;\nc) repairability and availability of spare parts;\nd) harmlessness to persons, animals and the environment, in accordance with the competences of the relevant Codes;\ne) sobriety in the use of materials, energy and other resources.\n2. The criteria may conflict with one another. They are compared in accordance with the multi-criteria procedure defined in the Quality Technical Annex. No automatic formula may replace the reasoned assessment provided for by this Code.\n3. Where usefulness and adequacy in relation to the need are equal, preference is given to the good with the lower systemic impact over its complete life cycle.\n4. The assessment states the criteria considered, the evidence available, the uncertainties and the reasons for the outcome. The lack of data may not be turned into automatic proof of conformity.\n5. The criteria in paragraph 1 implement the principles of Care, Autonomy, Conviviality and Meaning provided for by Article 9.1 of the Constitution: care through harmlessness and durability; autonomy through repairability and the absence of dependencies; conviviality through shared and accessible use; meaning through actual usefulness and sobriety."],[7,"art-3--technical-validation-sheet","Art. 3 — Technical Validation Sheet","","1. Every type or model of good intended for admission to the Central Storehouse shall be accompanied by a Technical Validation Sheet, hereinafter “STV”.\n2. The STV is prepared by the Quality Council, with the contribution of the relevant Competence Circle and the opinion of the Health Council on the aspects within its competence. The opinions of the other competent Circles are obtained where the good presents safety, environmental, energy, territorial or digital aspects assigned to them. The Storehouse Council gives an opinion on the logistics, storage and distribution of the good, pursuant to Article 9.2 of the Constitution.\n3. The STV assesses the type or model of the good. It does not automatically certify each individual item or batch, the checking of which is governed by Article 6.\n4. The STV contains at least:\na) identification of the good, the model and the producer, if available;\nb) origin and materials;\nc) estimated life cycle;\nd) durability and repairability characteristics;\ne) availability and accessibility of spare parts;\nf) proprietary or technological dependencies;\ng) consumption of energy and other resources;\nh) risks and prevention measures;\ni) maintenance required;\nj) arrangements for reuse, recycling or making safe at end of life;\nk) criteria for testing and checking the item;\nl) date of issue, version, persons responsible for the preparatory inquiry, opinions received and date of the scheduled review.\n5. The STV is a reasoned act, traced and published on the Network in accordance with the applicable access levels. It may be challenged pursuant to Chapter II.\n6. The comparative report provided for by Article 40, paragraph 4, of Code 1 is distinct from the STV. It may refer to or reproduce the relevant technical data of the STV, but it does not replace the economic and functional comparison required by Code 1.\n7. STVs are shared in the federated Knowledge Storehouse. A Circle that adopts an STV already validated by another Circle verifies only its adequacy to local conditions.\n8. Documentary requirements are proportionate to the risk. For goods in common use presenting no risk or low risk, a simplified STV is sufficient, in accordance with the Quality Technical Annex."],[7,"art-3-bis--stvs-for-procedures-internal-services-automations-systems-and-safety","Art. 3-bis — STVs for procedures, internal services, automations, systems and safety","","1. The STVs that the Codes provide for objects other than the goods of Article 3, such as procedures, routes, internal services, Basket standards, automations, algorithms, systems and technical safety rules, are drawn up or proposed by the Council indicated by the sectoral Code and approved in accordance with Article 3, paragraph 2, letter c), and Article 7 of Code 3.\n2. For the purposes of the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3:\na) the value is the cost in UCR of implementing and maintaining the STV over the following twenty-four months, certified by the Accounting Council pursuant to Article 9, paragraph 5, of Code 3;\nb) the risk class is that of the activity, system or function governed, assessed pursuant to Article 6 of Code 3.\n3. Where the matrix assigns the decision to the Competence Circle, whoever drew up or proposed the STV may not be its sole approver: the STV is approved together with the Quality Council or, if the Quality Council drew it up, together with the relevant Competence Circle. If the two Councils are merged in the same Council, approval requires the confirmation of at least two competent persons unconnected with the drafting.\n4. Article 3, paragraphs 5 and 7, and Articles 13 and 16 apply in so far as compatible. The mandatory and binding opinions and the stricter bodies or procedures provided for by the sectoral Codes remain in force.\n5. In the Codes the acronym “STV” means the Technical Validation Sheet."],[7,"art-4--hierarchy-of-admission-and-reuse","Art. 4 — Hierarchy of admission and reuse","","1. As a general rule, the alternatives are assessed in the following order of preference:\na) a good reused or reconditioned locally;\nb) a good self-produced within the Confederation;\nc) a good of external production, provided that it is durable and repairable;\nd) an external good with critical shortcomings, but necessary and with no immediately available alternative, admitted only on a temporary basis with a Substitution Plan.\n2. The purchase of a new good is prohibited where a functionally equivalent reused good is available on the Network, except for a reasoned derogation pursuant to paragraph 4.\n3. Functional equivalence is determined in accordance with objective criteria stated in the STV and takes account at least of safety, necessary performance, compatibility, availability, delivery times and repair costs.\n4. Derogation from the hierarchy is permitted only where the following is documented:\na) the material impossibility of procuring the alternative within the time needed;\nb) an established technical incompatibility;\nc) the quantitative insufficiency of the alternative in relation to the requirement.\n5. The derogation is reasoned and traced on the Network. If it gives rise to a dependency or a temporary critical shortcoming, Articles 5 and 12 apply."],[7,"art-5--prohibition-of-critical-proprietary-dependency","Art. 5 — Prohibition of critical proprietary dependency","","1. Critical proprietary dependency means a condition in which a good, although materially intact, is rendered unusable, limited or unrepairable by the exclusive control of a producer or supplier over software, firmware, cloud services, remote activation keys, licences, spare parts, diagnostics or authentication systems.\n2. Critical proprietary dependency is classified in one of the following categories:\na) absolute critical dependency: it causes a total lock or an irreversible proprietary coupling, or makes offline use or community control impossible. It is prohibited and may not be admitted even on a temporary basis;\nb) residual and surmountable critical dependency: it causes a partial limitation that can be removed by the Community by means of technically available tools, procedures or substitutions. The good may be admitted only temporarily pursuant to Article 4, paragraph 1, letter d), with a mandatory Substitution Plan. Where the dependency concerns software or firmware whose source code is not available, admission is permitted within the limits of Article 19.4 of the Constitution and of Article 12, paragraph 5, and is never permitted for systems that propose or decide on rights, voting and Commons, including Management AIs, the algorithms of Article 2, paragraph 5, of Code 7, the personal AI and the consultation and voting systems of Article 34.4 of the Constitution.\n3. The classification is recorded in the STV, on the basis of a preparatory inquiry by the Quality Council and the opinion of the Network Council. The assessment also considers the concrete possibility, the time and the costs of removing the dependency.\n4. The external supplier is required to provide the information and evidence necessary to demonstrate the absence of critical dependencies. In the absence of sufficient information, the STV states the uncertainty and does not presume the absence of the dependency.\n5. A residual dependency admitted temporarily is recorded in the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15."],[7,"art-6--distinction-between-type-and-item","Art. 6 — Distinction between type and item","","1. Validation is structured in two levels:\na) type level: the STV determines whether the model or type of the good is admissible in the common system;\nb) item or batch level: the check verifies that the good delivered corresponds to the STV, is intact and meets the applicable requirements.\n2. The check of the item or batch covers, according to the nature of the good:\na) integrity and absence of damage;\nb) expiry date and conservation conditions;\nc) functioning;\nd) transport damage;\ne) correspondence to the specifications of the STV.\n3. The non-conformity of a single item does not automatically entail the review of the STV for the type. The review is initiated when the anomalies indicate a systematic defect or a possible variation of the model.\n4. The ordinary check of the item may be carried out within the generic CBO by means of a checklist derived from the STV. The outcome is recorded in the Storehouse Register.\n5. A non-conforming item is isolated and not distributed until the decision of the competent Circle."],[7,"art-7--supervision-and-reports","Art. 7 — Supervision and reports","","1. Any member may report defects, suspected harmfulness, premature breakage or any other non-conformity. The report is recorded on the Network and contains, as far as possible, the description of the good, the type of anomaly and the evidence available.\n2. Reports are handled in accordance with two procedures:\na) ordinary report: three documented and independent reports relating to the same type entail the mandatory review of the STV;\nb) serious report: one documented report of a health or safety risk entails the immediate assessment of precautionary suspension pursuant to paragraph 3. In the event of a serious and immediate risk, the good or batch concerned is suspended without delay and the review is initiated within seven days.\n3. Precautionary suspension constitutes neither a sanction nor a definitive finding of non-conformity. During the suspension the good concerned is not distributed, except for a substitute measure that is necessary to guarantee the continuity of an essential service and is considered safe by the competent Circles.\n4. The reporting person is protected from retaliation in accordance with Code 4. Reports are assessed without automatically attributing responsibility to producers, suppliers or users."],[7,"art-8--exclusion-and-rapid-suspension","Art. 8 — Exclusion and rapid suspension","","1. The good or the type may be suspended or excluded for intrinsic non-conformity, including:\na) presence of substances prohibited by the Health Council in accordance with the competences assigned to it;\nb) energy consumption disproportionate to the usefulness, without technical justification;\nc) established serious risk;\nd) failure to correspond to the essential requirements of the STV;\ne) impossibility of safely guaranteeing use, maintenance or end of life.\n2. The decision distinguishes between:\na) precautionary suspension, adopted pending verification where there is a risk that cannot be ruled out;\nb) definitive exclusion, adopted after a preparatory inquiry and a reasoned decision;\nc) limitation of use, where the risk can be contained by excluding specific conditions or uses.\n3. The compatibility of commercial practices or of the relationship with the supplier is assessed separately from the intrinsic technical conformity of the good. By way of example, systematic misleading advertising or the artificial creation of dependencies may be relevant.\n4. The assessment of the relationship with the supplier is coordinated with Code 15. Technical measures concerning the good remain within the competence of the bodies provided for by this Code.\n5. Every suspension or exclusion is reasoned, recorded and communicated to the parties concerned. The appeals provided for by Chapter II remain available."],[7,"art-9--quality-of-the-service-and-of-the-cbo","Art. 9 — Quality of the service and of the CBO","","1. The quality criteria also apply to internal tasks and services. A CBO activity is adequately organised when:\na) it is carried out safely in accordance with Article 27 of Code 1 and Code 14;\nb) the person has received the training provided for by Article 28 of Code 1;\nc) the necessary tools are available pursuant to Article 26 of Code 1;\nd) the time allotted is realistic and compatible with the correct performance of the task;\ne) the result is verifiable against the applicable specifications.\n2. The hurried performance of a task, where it compromises the quality of the good or service, is treated first of all as a possible organisational inefficiency or planning error. It does not automatically constitute individual fault.\n3. Any individual responsibility is assessed separately and in accordance with the guarantees of Code 4.\n4. The repeated negative validation of a work, a good or a service does not entail personal sanctions. It may result in the suspension of the drawing of common resources intended for that specific production and the start of an accompaniment process with the competent Council, pursuant to Article 9.3 of the Constitution."],[7,"art-10--documentary-architecture","Art. 10 — Documentary architecture","","1. The documentary procedure ordinarily follows this sequence:\na) proposal for admission;\nb) preparatory inquiry;\nc) drafting of the STV;\nd) admission decision;\ne) any comparative purchase report provided for by Code 1;\nf) registration in the Storehouse;\ng) monitoring and reports;\nh) review, suspension or exclusion, if necessary.\n2. The STV is the reference technical document for the type of the good. It does not replace the decision-making acts, the economic reports or the registers required by the other Codes.\n3. STVs, reports, reviews and the Exclusions Register are published on the Network in accordance with the access levels and the confidentiality guarantees provided for by Codes 3, 6 and 7.\n4. The documentation shall state the version, date, persons responsible and links to the underlying acts, so as to allow the procedure to be reconstructed."],[7,"art-11--transitional-provision","Art. 11 — Transitional provision","","1. Goods already present in the Storehouses on the date of entry into force of this Code may remain in use for a maximum period of twelve months pending the STV, except in the cases provided for by Article 8.\n2. During the transitional period, the goods are inventoried and subjected at least to the item checks provided for by Article 6.\n3. The twelve-month time limit is an initial one and may be recalibrated by the Assembly on the basis of the Circles’ capacity to conduct preparatory inquiries. The extension does not apply to goods for which a serious risk or an absolute critical proprietary dependency has been established."],[7,"art-12--substitution-plan","Art. 12 — Substitution Plan","","1. A Substitution Plan is mandatory for:\na) every good admitted temporarily pursuant to Article 4, paragraph 1, letter d);\nb) every good with a residual and surmountable critical proprietary dependency pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b).\n2. The Plan contains at least:\na) the reasons for the temporary necessity;\nb) the alternatives examined and the reasons why they are not immediately available;\nc) the dependency to be removed;\nd) the solution envisaged;\ne) the Circle and the person responsible for implementation;\nf) a deadline not exceeding twelve months;\ng) the state of progress, updated at least quarterly and published in accordance with the applicable access levels;\nh) the criteria that determine exit from the temporary regime.\n3. The deadline may be extended once only, for a reasoned period not exceeding twelve months, where a technical or material impossibility not attributable to organisational inertia is documented. The extension is approved by the competent body in accordance with Article 15.\n4. If the deadline passes without the dependency having been removed or the envisaged solution implemented, the good automatically passes to review for the purposes of suspension or exclusion. The review also considers any need to guarantee the continuity of an essential service.\n5. The Substitution Plan of a good that depends on software or firmware whose source code is not available is published in full on the Network, pursuant to Article 19.4 of the Constitution. Only personal data and information whose disclosure would expose the security of the Network to risk may be omitted."],[7,"art-13--temporal-validity-of-the-stv-and-audit","Art. 13 — Temporal validity of the STV and audit","","1. Every STV is subjected to an audit at least every twenty-four months, unless the STV sets a shorter time limit by reason of the criticality, the technology or the risks of the good.\n2. An extraordinary review is initiated in the cases provided for by Article 7 or when any of the following occur:\na) substantial modifications of the good or of the production process;\nb) new evidence on harmfulness, safety or durability;\nc) changes in the availability of spare parts or alternatives;\nd) variations in proprietary dependencies;\ne) significant changes in the requirement.\n3. The audit verifies:\na) the continued fulfilment of the quality criteria referred to in Article 2;\nb) the existence of reuse or reconditioning alternatives;\nc) the state of removal of proprietary dependencies;\nd) the correctness of the checks on items;\ne) the validity of the maintenance and end-of-life measures.\n4. The outcome of the audit is recorded. Where critical shortcomings are found, the corrective measures, the person responsible and the time limit for implementation are stated."],[7,"art-14--stages-of-the-admission-procedure","Art. 14 — Stages of the admission procedure","","1. The ordinary admission procedure comprises:\na) submission of the proposal for admission;\nb) preparatory inquiry by the Quality Council;\nc) drafting of a provisional STV;\nd) obtaining the opinion of the Health Council on the harmlessness aspects within its competence; a negative opinion prevents admission until the critical shortcoming has been resolved;\ne) obtaining the technical opinion of the relevant Competence Circle and of the other competent Circles, where necessary;\nf) reasoned admission decision pursuant to Article 15;\ng) any trial period in a pilot Pantry;\nh) entry into the Central Storehouse with the STV in force;\ni) monitoring and collection of reports;\nj) periodic or extraordinary review;\nk) any reasoned suspension or exclusion;\nl) access to the challenge and appeal procedures.\n1-bis. In implementation of Article 9.2 of the Constitution, before the decision of paragraph 1, letter f), a verification of use proportionate to the risk takes place. The provisional STV is published and those who will use the good may submit observations for at least seventy-two hours or for the longer period set by the Quality Council. For goods that are not in common use with nil or low risk, the Quality Council also invites the persons who have declared an interest in using it to a trial or a discussion. Where the trial period of paragraph 1, letter g), is provided for, the observations of those who use the good during the trial are collected and assessed before the entry of paragraph 1, letter h). The decision gives an account of the observations received and of the opinion of the Storehouse Council provided for by Article 3, paragraph 2.\n2. The provisional STV states which verifications are still in progress and the conditions of use applicable during the preparatory inquiry.\n3. The trial period may not be used to distribute unsafe goods or to circumvent the binding opinion provided for by paragraph 1, letter d).\n4. The procedure is coordinated with Code 1 as regards the aspects of supply, purchase, registration and distribution."],[7,"art-15--decision-making-competence-and-anti-circumvention","Art. 15 — Decision-making competence and anti-circumvention","","1. The final decision on admission is adopted as follows:\na) for goods below the economic threshold established by Code 3, the Quality Council conducts the preparatory inquiry and formulates the proposal, and the body indicated by the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3, according to the value of the operation and the risk class, decides, stating its reasons and on the basis of the STV. For a good, the relevant Competence Circle is the Council competent for the sector in which the good is used or, if no Council is competent, the Storehouse Council;\nb) for goods above the threshold, for those with a high systemic impact or for those with a residual critical dependency pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), the Quality Council formulates a proposal and the Assembly of Circle 1 decides in accordance with Code 3.\n2. The economic threshold is calculated on the overall value of the operation and includes, as far as foreseeable:\na) the cost of the batch;\nb) transport and installation;\nc) maintenance envisaged for the following twenty-four months;\nd) training;\ne) processing and transfer of data, if applicable;\nf) end-of-life costs.\n3. It is prohibited to split the purchase or the operation artificially in order to circumvent the threshold. Operations linked by subject matter, supplier, time or function are considered as a single whole where there are elements demonstrating their unity.\n4. The threshold and the methods of calculation are established by Code 3.\n5. Every decision is traced on the Network with the STV and any opinions attached, in compliance with the applicable access levels."],[7,"art-16--challenge-conflict-of-interest-and-appeal","Art. 16 — Challenge, conflict of interest and appeal","","1. Any member may challenge an STV with a statement of reasons and technical documentation, indicating the parts challenged and the evidence available.\n2. The challenge produces the following effects:\na) an ordinary challenge does not suspend the validity of the STV, but is recorded and entered into the review procedure;\nb) where there is a documented serious risk, precautionary suspension may be requested. The Quality Council and the Health Council, each within its own competence, assess within forty-eight hours whether the conditions for the measure provided for by Article 7, paragraph 2, letter b) are met;\nc) an appeal against the final decision lies to the Guarantee Circle. The appeal does not have suspensive effect, unless the Guarantee decides otherwise, stating its reasons, where there is a serious risk or possible irreversible harm.\n3. Anyone who took part in the drafting or approval of the original STV may not be the sole decision-maker in the review or challenge relating to the same decision.\n4. The review is carried out collegially or with the participation of a different and independent member. Conflicts of interest and the related replacements are handled in accordance with Code 4.\n5. Decisions on challenges and appeals are reasoned, recorded and communicated to those concerned.\n6. Validation involves those who use the good or service, those who possess the relevant competence and those who manage the logistics of the Storehouse, pursuant to Article 9.2 of the Constitution. Validation carried out exclusively by an AI is prohibited. The right to contest a validation before the Assembly of Circle 1, pursuant to Article 9.4 of the Constitution, remains unaffected."],[7,"art-17--destination-of-the-good-after-exclusion","Art. 17 — Destination of the good after exclusion","","1. The exclusion of a good does not automatically entail its destruction.\n2. Pending the decision on its final destination, the good may be suspended or placed in quarantine for the necessary verifications.\n3. The final destination follows, in order, the applicable criteria:\na) repair or reconditioning;\nb) downgrading to a non-critical use compatible with the condition of the good;\nc) return to the supplier, where provided for or practicable;\nd) recovery or recycling of the materials;\ne) making safe, in accordance with Article 15 of Code 1.\n4. The decision is adopted by the Quality Council together with the Storehouse Council and with the other Circles competent for the specific type of good. It is reasoned and traced.\n5. The destination is coordinated with Article 15 of Code 1 and follows the principles of waste reduction and maximum safety."],[7,"art-18--inter-code-coordination","Art. 18 — Inter-Code coordination","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Article 40, for the comparative report on purchases, which remains distinct from the STV;\nb) Code 3, for thresholds, the Assembly and the UCR;\nc) Code 15, for external relations and commercial practices;\nd) Code 4, for appeals, procedural guarantees and responsibility;\ne) Code 6, for health aspects;\nf) Code 7, for the Network, digital security and data processing;\ng) the sectoral Codes relevant to the good or service assessed.\n2. Competences are assigned expressly. No decision of admission, suspension or exclusion may be based on a competence that has not been identified or on an opinion that is not accessible to those entitled to it.\n3. Every exclusion is reasoned on the basis of the STV or, in the case of a service, of the SVS and may be challenged in accordance with this Code and Code 4.\n4. The inter-Code procedures are reviewed when new versions of Codes 3 or 15 enter into force, or when overlaps of competence emerge."],[7,"art-19--subject-matter-and-definition-of-external-service","Art. 19 — Subject matter and definition of external service","","1. External service means a service, whether continuous or one-off, provided by a party external to the Confederation, recourse to which is justified by internal unavailability, demonstrable temporary efficiency or the need for a specialist competence that cannot be immediately internalised.\n2. An external service may be admitted only where at least one of the following conditions is documented:\na) absence of an internal person available within the CBO;\nb) absence of the necessary competence within the Confederation;\nc) demonstrable temporary efficiency compared with the internal alternatives;\nd) need for a certified specialist service.\n3. The reasons are traced in the Service Validation Sheet provided for by Article 21.\n4. By way of example, specialist maintenance, connectivity, off-network transport, certified technical consultancy, specialist health services and digital services may constitute external services.\n5. An external service is not a good and is not subject to an STV. It is assessed by means of a Service Validation Sheet, hereinafter “SVS”."],[7,"art-20--service-quality-criteria","Art. 20 — Service quality criteria","","1. An external service is assessed in accordance with the following criteria:\na) continuity: availability for the envisaged duration and existence of an adequate continuity plan;\nb) competence: trained and, where required, certified personnel;\nc) safety: compliance with the health and safety rules provided for by Code 1, Article 27, by Code 14 and by the other relevant Codes;\nd) substitutability: absence, where technically possible, of exclusive dependency and provision for the transfer of the competences necessary for internal management;\ne) interoperability: possibility of using open standards and of returning data and materials in interoperable formats;\nf) sobriety: proportionality of costs in UCR, energy consumption and use of materials in relation to the requirement;\ng) data sovereignty and data processing: where the service processes members’ data or data of the Confederation, the following requirements shall be guaranteed cumulatively:\ni. location of the data and applicable jurisdiction declared in the SVS;\nii. control and governance of the data by the Community, without prejudice to the rights of the persons to whom the data relate;\niii. prohibition of profiling and of the transfer of data to third parties for unauthorised purposes;\niv. encryption of data in transit and at rest, with control of the keys by the Community or with an escrow mechanism described in the SVS;\nv. declaration and approval of sub-suppliers and sub-processors; prohibition of concealed subcontracting;\nvi. certified deletion or complete migration of the data at the end of the relationship, within the time limit established in the SVS;\nvii. return of the data in an interoperable and documented format.\n2. The mere location of the data in the European Union does not, in itself, demonstrate the Community’s sovereignty or control over the data.\n3. The requirements of this Article are coordinated with Articles 37 and 38 of Code 1 and with Code 7.\n4. If a requirement cannot be met, the SVS states the derogation requested, the reasons for it, its duration, the compensatory measures and the exit plan. The derogation may not authorise an absolute critical proprietary dependency prohibited by Article 5."],[7,"art-21--service-validation-sheet","Art. 21 — Service Validation Sheet","","1. Every external service is accompanied by an SVS containing at least:\na) identity of the supplier and description of the service;\nb) reasons for admission pursuant to Article 19;\nc) envisaged duration and renewal conditions;\nd) assessment of the criteria referred to in Article 20;\ne) proprietary and technological dependencies;\nf) overall cost, including foreseeable ancillary items;\ng) exit and termination conditions;\nh) Substitution Plan or Continuity Plan;\ni) arrangements for the processing, return, migration and deletion of data;\nj) persons responsible for monitoring and verification.\n2. No collective contract relating to an external service may be concluded without an approved SVS.\n3. The SVS is a reasoned act, traced and published in accordance with the applicable access levels. It may be challenged in accordance with the procedures provided for the STV, in so far as they are compatible.\n4. The technical quality of the service is distinct from the compatibility of the commercial relationship with the supplier. The latter is assessed and managed in coordination with Code 15."],[7,"art-22--hierarchy-duration-and-substitution-plan","Art. 22 — Hierarchy, duration and Substitution Plan","","1. In meeting a requirement, the following order of preference is followed:\na) internal service within the CBO;\nb) service provided by another Circle;\nc) cooperative external service;\nd) commercial external service.\n2. The ordinary duration of an external service does not exceed twelve months. Only one ordinary renewal is permitted. The Substitution Plan or the Continuity Plan is drawn up at the time of admission and not merely at expiry.\n3. Where the service is necessary beyond twenty-four months, the SVS shall give reasons for and state one of the following solutions:\na) internalisation of the service within the CBO;\nb) adoption of an external alternative, identified after comparing at least two options, where available;\nc) exceptional, temporary and reasoned renewal, where no technically equivalent alternative exists.\n4. The exceptional renewal referred to in paragraph 3, letter c), does not exceed a further twelve months, is subject to mandatory review and entails an active search for alternatives. It is not automatically renewable.\n5. The absence of two alternatives that are actually available does not automatically block a critical service. In that case the SVS documents the absence of alternatives, the reasons for continuation, the measures to reduce the dependency and the date of the next review."],[7,"art-23--service-admission-procedure","Art. 23 — Service admission procedure","","1. The ordinary admission procedure comprises:\na) submission of the proposal with reasons pursuant to Article 19;\nb) preparatory inquiry by the Quality Council, together with the relevant Competence Circle and the Network Council where the service processes data or depends on digital infrastructure;\nc) drafting of the provisional SVS;\nd) obtaining the opinion of the Health Council where the service concerns persons, health or safety;\ne) decision of the Quality Council for services below the threshold and in common use, or proposal of the Quality Council and decision of the Assembly for the cases provided for by Article 15;\nf) conclusion of the contract with clauses on exit, continuity, return of data and management of dependencies;\ng) continuous monitoring and handling of reports;\nh) audit at least every twelve months, or more frequently where the nature of the service or the level of risk so requires;\ni) possibility of challenge and appeal to the Guarantee Circle.\n2. The provisional SVS states the verifications still necessary and the conditions applicable before definitive approval.\n3. The contract may not contain clauses incompatible with the approved SVS."],[7,"art-24--exclusion-termination-and-continuity","Art. 24 — Exclusion, termination and continuity","","1. A service may be suspended or excluded when:\na) it no longer meets the criteria of Article 20;\nb) the supplier breaches the requirements of Article 20, letter g), concerning data;\nc) it gives rise to or maintains an absolute critical proprietary dependency;\nd) the reasons that justified its admission cease to exist;\ne) the supplier does not implement the corrective measures provided for in the SVS.\n2. The technical exclusion of the service and the termination of the relationship with the supplier are distinct decisions. Termination shall be accompanied by continuity measures where the interruption could compromise an essential service.\n3. Except in an emergency requiring immediate suspension in order to protect persons or data, termination follows a reasoned procedure that allows the supplier and the competent Circles to be informed and to prepare the transition.\n4. The exit procedure comprises, as the case may be:\na) activation of the Continuity Plan;\nb) migration or substitution with an internal service or an alternative service;\nc) verification of the deletion or certified transfer of the data;\nd) return of the data in an interoperable and documented format;\ne) closure of accesses, credentials and technical dependencies;\nf) recording of the closure of the relationship.\n5. For critical services, including connectivity, digital services, infrastructure maintenance and specialist health services, the Continuity Plan is drawn up so as to avoid interruptions. If the absence of interruption is not technically possible, the plan states the duration, the effects, the temporary measures and the responsibilities.\n6. The continuity plan may not justify the indefinite maintenance of a non-conforming service. Every temporary continuation is reasoned, limited in time and subject to review."],[8,"art-1--subject-matter","Art. 1 — Subject matter","","1. This Code governs:\na) the bodies competent to decide within the confederal system;\nb) the deliberative procedures and the related decision-making levels;\nc) the economic thresholds and the risk classes;\nd) the consensus method, the handling of objections and, in the cases provided for, recourse to a qualified majority;\ne) the composition and functioning of the Assemblies;\nf) the obligations of statement of reasons, traceability and transparency.\n2. This Code coordinates, in particular:\na) Code 1, as regards requirements, production, the Storehouses and CdRs;\nb) Code 2, as regards quality, STVs, SVSs and validation;\nc) Code 4, as regards procedural guarantees and appeals;\nd) Code 15, as regards external relations.\n3. The provisions of this Code do not transfer to any body technical, health or sectoral competences assigned to other Codes."],[8,"art-2--deliberative-principles","Art. 2 — Deliberative principles","","1. Deliberation is founded on the following principles:\na) consensus understood as the absence of valid reasoned objections;\nb) transparency differentiated according to the nature of the information and the rights involved;\nc) traceability of proposals, objections, opinions and decisions;\nd) subsidiarity: the decision is taken by the closest level that is competent;\ne) reversibility of decisions, where their nature so permits;\nf) non-accumulation of deliberative power;\ng) respect for the competences assigned by the Constitution and the Codes.\n2. No majority, even a qualified one, may validate a decision incompatible with a mandatory rule of the Constitution or of the Codes.\n3. Transparency may not be used to disclose personal or health data, or information whose publication would create a risk to the security of infrastructure."],[8,"art-3--types-of-decision","Art. 3 — Types of decision","","1. Operational decision. This is the decision that gives effect to a decision already approved without:\na) a new commitment of common resources;\nb) the choice of a new supplier;\nc) the approval of a new STV or SVS;\nd) an infrastructural modification;\ne) a substantial modification of the scope or costs already approved.\nThe operational decision lies with the relevant Competence Circle, within the limits of the original decision.\n2. Allocative decision. This is the decision that:\na) commits common resources, measured in UCR;\nb) selects a supplier;\nc) approves an STV or an SVS;\nd) modifies an infrastructure;\ne) approves or renews an external service;\nf) entails a significant use of common resources.\nThe deliberative level is determined in accordance with the matrix in Article 7, on the basis of the value of the operation in UCR and of the risk class.\na) Low-risk allocative decisions below threshold are adopted by the body indicated by the matrix of Article 7: the relevant Competence Circle for those of low value and the Quality Council together with the Competence Circle for those of medium value, in accordance with the procedures of Code 2.\nb) Medium- or high-risk allocative decisions follow the reinforced, special or assembly procedure provided for in Article 7 and in Articles 7-bis, 7-ter and 7-quater.\n3. Constitutive decision. This is the decision that proposes the amendment of the Codes, calibrates the thresholds and the risk classes, orders the exclusion of a category on principle or resolves on the other matters that the Codes reserve to the General Assembly. It lies with the General Assembly pursuant to Article 14. The amendment of the Codes is approved in accordance with Articles 37 and 38 of the Constitution.\n4. A decision is classified on the basis of its real effects and not of the formal designation used by the proposer."],[8,"art-4--reasoned-objection-and-consensus-procedure","Art. 4 — Reasoned objection and consensus procedure","","1. An objection is valid when it identifies, with verifiable reasons, at least one of the following grounds:\na) risk to health or safety;\nb) breach of the quality criteria laid down in Code 2;\nc) exceeding of the applicable ecological limits;\nd) documented technical impossibility;\ne) breach of a mandatory rule of the Constitution or of the Codes;\nf) breach of the rules on data sovereignty or data processing laid down in Article 20, letter g), of Code 2;\ng) conflict of interest;\nh) failure to comply with the deliberative procedure, threshold or competence.\n2. The ordinary consensus procedure consists of the following steps:\na) publication of the proposal and of the related Allocation Report, where required;\nb) opening of a window for objections lasting at least seventy-two hours, except for the longer time limits laid down in this Code;\nc) reasoned reply by the proposer to the objections received;\nd) amendment of the proposal, if any, and publication of the updated version;\ne) fresh verification of the objections after the amendment;\nf) mediation with a facilitator, where objections remain;\ng) decision in accordance with Article 13, where the procedure concerns an Assembly.\n3. The assessment of the validity of an objection lies with the facilitator and the Quality Council, each for the aspects within its own competence. The proposer may not decide alone on the validity of objections to the proposer’s own proposal.\n4. If the facilitator or the member of the Quality Council took part in the contested proposal or preparatory inquiry, the assessment is entrusted to an independent substitute in accordance with the guarantees of Code 4.\n5. The decision on the validity of the objection shall state its reasons and shall be recorded. An objection is not considered valid merely because it has been raised, nor may it be rejected without its reasons being examined."],[8,"art-5--economic-threshold","Art. 5 — Economic threshold","","1. The economic value of the operation is determined by considering its overall reproduction cost. The following are included, where applicable:\na) the cost of the batch or of the entire supply;\nb) transport and installation;\nc) maintenance foreseen for the following twenty-four months;\nd) necessary training;\ne) foreseeable end-of-life costs;\nf) processing, transfer and storage of data;\ng) any further costs directly connected with the operation.\n2. The calculation is made in accordance with the UCR methodology provided for in Article 9 and in the UCR Technical Annex.\n3. The value is calculated on the operation as a whole and not on the individual good or contract, including for the purposes of the anti-circumvention provisions of Article 11."],[8,"art-6--risk-threshold-and-classification","Art. 6 — Risk threshold and classification","","1. The risk of the operation is assessed by considering at least:\na) the presence of proprietary dependencies;\nb) the processing of data and sovereignty over them;\nc) the criticality of the infrastructure involved;\nd) the substitutability of the good or service;\ne) the safety of persons and systems;\nf) the continuity of essential services;\ng) the environmental and territorial effects, where relevant.\n2. Operations are classified in the following classes:\na) low risk;\nb) medium risk;\nc) high or critical risk.\n3. Before the deliberative level is determined, a documented preliminary risk assessment shall be drawn up. That assessment is necessary in order to apply the matrix in Article 7 and does not replace the definitive STV or SVS.\n4. The preliminary classification is confirmed or updated in the STV or in the SVS. If the definitive assessment identifies a higher risk class, the higher deliberative level applies and the procedure shall be adapted before the definitive decision is adopted.\n5. A low economic threshold does not preclude the application of a higher risk class. If the value in UCR and the risk lead to different deliberative levels, the higher level prevails.\n6. In the event of substantial uncertainty, the assessment shall indicate the missing data and shall provisionally apply the most precautionary class compatible with the available evidence."],[8,"art-7--binding-deliberative-matrix","Art. 7 — Binding deliberative matrix","","1. The matrix in this Article determines the minimum deliberative level. No discretionary assessment may lower the resulting level.\n2. Where the economic threshold and the risk class lead to different levels, the higher one applies.\n3. The following matrix applies:\nValue of the operation · Low risk · Medium risk · High or critical risk\nLow: below the low threshold · Competence Circle · Quality Council and Competence Circle · Special procedure pursuant to Article 7-bis and decision of the Assembly of Circle 1\nMedium: from the low threshold to the high threshold inclusive · Quality Council and Competence Circle · Reinforced procedure pursuant to Article 7-ter · Assembly of Circle 1\nHigh: above the high threshold · Assembly of Circle 1 · Assembly of Circle 1 · Assembly of Circle 1 with special guarantees pursuant to Article 7-quater\n3-bis. The low threshold is equal to 100 UCR plus one UCR for each person belonging to the Base Community, and does not exceed 500 UCR. The high threshold is equal to 500 UCR plus five UCR for each person, and does not exceed 2,500 UCR. All persons belonging to the Community at the beginning of the quarter are counted, minors included; guests are not counted. For a newly constituted Community the number in the act of accession applies. For operations common to several Communities and for the bodies of the higher Circles, the persons of the Communities concerned are added together.\n3-ter. The General Assembly calibrates the values of paragraph 3-bis pursuant to Article 14, paragraph 2, letter b). The calibration may not eliminate the proportion to the number of persons. Paragraphs 2 and 4 of Article 7-ter apply in any event to medium-risk operations with a value above the high threshold and not exceeding 2,500 UCR. In Article 3, paragraph 2, of this Code and in Articles 15 and 23 of Code 2, “the threshold” means the high threshold.\n4. Any operational decision subsequent to approval is entrusted to the Competence Circle, provided that it does not modify the subject matter, the cost, the risk or the conditions of the approved decision.\n5. If a modification of the operation affects a threshold or the risk class, the decision shall be submitted afresh to the deliberative level resulting from the updated matrix."],[8,"art-7-bis--special-procedure","Art. 7-bis — Special procedure","","1. The special procedure applies to operations of low value and high or critical risk. Paragraphs 2 to 5 apply in any event to high- or critical-risk operations with a value below 500 UCR.\n2. The decision is taken by the Assembly of Circle 1, convened by ordinary procedure or, in the event of reasoned urgency, within forty-eight hours.\n3. The opinions of the Health, Network and Quality Councils are mandatory, each for the matters within its own competence. Binding negative opinions produce the effects provided for in Article 8.\n4. Before the decision, the following shall be available:\na) the preliminary risk assessment provided for in Article 6;\nb) the Allocation Report provided for in Article 10;\nc) a provisional STV or SVS;\nd) a Continuity Plan;\ne) a Substitution Plan, where necessary.\n5. The preparatory inquiry shall not exceed seven days, except for a reasoned extension due to the need to obtain indispensable data or opinions. The extension shall be communicated and recorded.\n6. The decision is adopted in accordance with the consensus procedure provided for in Article 4. Recourse to a qualified majority is permitted exclusively in the cases and under the conditions of Article 13, paragraph 3, letter b).\n7. Where there is a persistent objection, mediation is mandatory. An objection that reports the breach of a mandatory rule or a serious risk that cannot be remedied may not be overcome by a vote."],[8,"art-7-ter--reinforced-procedure","Art. 7-ter — Reinforced procedure","","1. The reinforced procedure applies to operations of medium value in UCR and medium risk.\n2. The preparatory inquiry is carried out jointly by the Quality Council and the relevant Competence Circle. The opinion of the Councils of Article 8, paragraph 1, shall be obtained where the subject matter of the operation falls within their respective competences; a negative opinion has the effects of Article 8.\n3. The decision shall be accompanied by the Allocation Report provided for in Article 10.\n4. The operation shall be audited within twelve months of its start and thereafter in accordance with the level of risk and the conditions laid down in the decision."],[8,"art-7-quater--special-guarantees","Art. 7-quater — Special guarantees","","1. The procedure provided for in this Article applies to operations of high value in UCR and high or critical risk.\n2. The decision lies with the Assembly of Circle 1 and is subject to:\na) the opinions of the Councils of Article 8, paragraph 1, where relevant, with the effects of Article 8;\nb) the opinion of the Guarantee Circle on the procedural aspects;\nc) the preparation of a Continuity Plan;\nd) the preparation of a Substitution Plan;\ne) an audit within six months of the start;\nf) an Allocation Report comparing at least two alternatives, where these are available.\n3. If two real alternatives are not available, the Report shall indicate the options considered and give reasons for the impossibility of the comparison.\n4. The opinion of the Guarantee Circle does not replace the technical opinions, nor does it determine the allocative outcome."],[8,"art-8--binding-negative-opinion","Art. 8 — Binding negative opinion","","1. Binding negative opinions, pursuant to Article 6.6 of the Constitution, are issued only by:\na) the Health Council, for the protection of health;\nb) the Network Council, for the protection of the digital infrastructure and of data sovereignty;\nc) the Safety Council, for the general safety parameters, within the limits of Article 3, paragraph 6, of Code 14;\nd) the Agroecological Council and the Biodiversity Council, for the protection of ecological limits, solely in the cases of Article 4, paragraph 4, letter d), of Code 10 and of Article 3, paragraph 3, of Code 13.\nA binding negative opinion prevents the adoption of the decision until the cause that gave rise to the opinion has been removed, limited to the matters assigned to the Council that issued it. The authorisation of the Biodiversity Council provided for by Article 13.3 of the Constitution is not an opinion and follows Article 3, paragraph 5, of Code 13.\n2. A binding negative opinion may not be overcome by an assembly vote, not even by a qualified majority, nor by the removal or replacement of the members of the Council that issued it. The opinion remains effective even after the replacement of the members; the Council in its new composition re-examines it only on the proposal resubmitted pursuant to paragraph 3.\n3. The proposer may amend the proposal and resubmit it, attaching the evidence demonstrating that the cause of the negative opinion has been removed.\n4. The negative opinion shall state its reasons, be recorded and be communicated to those entitled to vote. It shall indicate, as far as possible:\na) the area of competence concerned;\nb) the risk or breach identified;\nc) the elements necessary for the proposal to be re-examined.\n5. The binding opinion may be challenged only in accordance with the guarantee procedures provided for in the Codes, and the appeal shall not suspend the prohibition on proceeding, except as expressly provided for in the Constitution or the Codes."],[8,"art-9--ucr-reproduction-cost-and-methodology","Art. 9 — UCR: reproduction cost and methodology","","1. The Reproduction Cost Unit, hereinafter “UCR”, is a non-monetary confederal unit of account representing the CBO time, materials, energy and logistics necessary to reproduce a good or to provide a service. One UCR corresponds to one hour of ordinary CBO; materials, energy and logistics are converted into that unit in accordance with the UCR Technical Annex.\n2. The UCR is not a market price. It is used to compare alternatives, to make the systemic cost transparent and to prevent accumulation.\n3. The methodology is defined in the UCR Technical Annex, prepared by the Accounting Council and the Quality Council and approved by the competent Assembly.\n4. The UCR Technical Annex shall govern at least:\na) the unit of measurement of time;\nb) the weighting of materials;\nc) the assessment of energy consumption;\nd) the calculation of digital services;\ne) discounting over time;\nf) the certification of the calculation;\ng) the arrangements for revision and challenge.\n5. The value in UCR is certified by the Accounting Council on the basis of the STV or the SVS and of the available data. Uncertainties shall be indicated in the certification.\n6. Indicative conversions into external values are for information purposes only and are not binding.\n7. UCR are not assigned to persons, do not constitute a credit and are not a means of exchange. Personal recognition of the contribution takes place through the Recognition Contribution, hereinafter “CdR”, governed by Article 8 of the Constitution and by Chapter V of Code 1. The acronym CdR denotes exclusively the Recognition Contribution.\n8. The Accounting Council is the technical Council responsible for the calculation and certification of values in UCR and for the anti-circumvention verification under Article 11. It has no allocative powers and does not take part in the decision on the operations it certifies."],[8,"art-10--allocation-report","Art. 10 — Allocation Report","","1. Every allocative decision shall be accompanied by an Allocation Report drawn up by the proposer, with the support of the Quality Council where necessary.\n2. The Report shall contain at least:\na) the substantive requirement to which the decision responds;\nb) the reference to the applicable STV or SVS;\nc) the certified value in UCR and the criteria used to calculate it;\nd) the preliminary risk class and, where available, the definitive one;\ne) the alternatives considered;\nf) the reasons for the proposed choice;\ng) the Substitution Plan or Continuity Plan, where required;\nh) the data-processing aspects;\ni) the bodies consulted and the opinions obtained;\nj) the time limits for review and the indicators for verifying effectiveness.\n3. The Report is a reasoned and readable summary of the STV or the SVS and of the preparatory inquiry. It does not duplicate the technical documents, but refers to them unambiguously.\n4. The Report is mandatory. Its absence produces the effects provided for in Article 18.\n5. The Report shall be published in accordance with the levels of transparency of Article 15.\n6. For irreversible decisions and for those within the competence of Circle 2 or of a higher Circle, the Allocation Report is incorporated into the Dossier provided for in Article 33 of the Constitution, which includes the assessment of costs, benefits and risks for seven generations, the limits of knowledge and the refutation section."],[8,"art-11--anti-circumvention-and-aggregation-of-operations","Art. 11 — Anti-circumvention and aggregation of operations","","1. For the purposes of the thresholds, operations that respond to the same substantive requirement or to functionally connected requirements shall be considered as a single whole, even where they are:\na) entrusted to different suppliers;\nb) described with different subject matters or titles;\nc) divided into several contracts or orders;\nd) spread over time.\n2. For the purposes of aggregation, the following criteria apply:\na) operations foreseeable at the time of the initial decision shall be aggregated;\nb) supervening and unforeseeable requirements may be assessed separately, with documented reasons;\nc) a change of supplier, of designation or of contractual form does not preclude aggregation if the same substantive requirement persists.\n3. The period of one hundred and eighty days constitutes a rebuttable presumption of foreseeability:\na) connected operations started within one hundred and eighty days of the first decision are presumed foreseeable and shall be aggregated, unless there is documented proof to the contrary;\nb) operations started after more than one hundred and eighty days shall nevertheless be aggregated where it is documented that the requirement was foreseeable at the time of the first decision.\n4. The verification is carried out by the Accounting Council. Anyone may report a possible circumvention.\n5. The assessment of the Accounting Council shall state its reasons and shall be recorded. If it establishes a possible circumventing fragmentation, the operation shall be submitted to the deliberative level resulting from the aggregated value."],[8,"art-12--assembly-of-circle-1-composition-participation-and-quorum","Art. 12 — Assembly of Circle 1: composition, participation and quorum","","1. The Assembly of Circle 1 is composed of all the persons who belong to the Base Community and have the right to vote pursuant to Article 36.1 of the Constitution. Each person votes individually. Delegation, purchase and exchange of votes are prohibited. The spokespersons of Circles 0 perform only functions of communication and practical coordination and do not dispose of the votes of others.\n2. Participation takes place in person or through the federated Network, in accordance with Article 34.4 of the Constitution and Article 7-bis of Code 7. The individual vote is anonymous. The reasoned objections of Article 4 shall be submitted in traceable form, so that they can be examined and answered; the signatures for the Objection of Article 13-bis follow paragraph 1 of that Article.\n3. For the purposes of this Code:\na) a participant is a person who takes part in the deliberation, in person or through the Network;\nb) an abstainer is a participating person who declares that they are not expressing a position on the decision;\nc) an active participant is a person who takes part in the deliberation and does not abstain.\n4. Ordinary resolutions are valid without a minimum quorum, in accordance with Article 35.1 of the Constitution.\n5. For decisions that the matrix in Article 7 assigns to the Assembly and for those for which the Codes require a qualified majority, the participation of at least one third of those entitled to vote is required. For decisions subject to the special guarantees of Article 7-quater, the participation of at least half of those entitled to vote is required. These quorums constitute reinforced guarantees pursuant to Article 35.1 of the Constitution.\n6. If the quorum is not reached, the proposal shall be republished for a new period of not less than seven days. If the quorum is not reached within the new period either, the proposal lapses and may be resubmitted.\n7. Abstention is not equivalent to an objection and does not, in itself, prevent the verification of consensus.\n8. Participation, abstentions and the calculation of quorums shall be recorded in the minutes in aggregate form, without identifying the vote of individual persons."],[8,"art-12-bis--technical-councils","Art. 12-bis — Technical Councils","","1. The Councils provided for in the Codes are the local branches of the Competence Circles, pursuant to Article 6.5 of the Constitution. In the Codes the expressions “relevant Competence Circle” and “competent Council” denote, in each Circle 1, the same technical body.\n2. The technical Councils are: the Storehouse Council (Code 1), the Quality Council (Code 2), the Accounting Council (this Code), the Competences Council (Code 5), the Health Council (Code 6), the Network Council (Code 7), the Energy and Water Council (Code 8), the Production Council (C.1-Annex A), the Agroecological Council (Code 9), the Habitat and Territory Council (Code 10), the Logistics Council (Code 11), the Care Council (Code 12), the Biodiversity Council (Code 13) and the Safety Council (Code 14). The Guarantee Circle is governed by Code 4 and the Transition Council by Code 16. The Storehouse Councils of Circles 2 and 3 are governed by Article 9-bis of Code 15.\n3. Each Council is composed of the number of persons laid down by the Code that governs it or, failing that, of three to five persons, with the validated competences required in accordance with Code 5.\n4. The members are drawn by lot, by a public and traceable procedure, from among the persons who possess the required competence, have declared their availability and are not subject to a ground of incompatibility. Personal candidacy for a specific office is prohibited. Any person drawn by lot may refuse."],[8,"art-12-bis--technical-councils","Art. 12-bis — Technical Councils","","5. The term of office lasts one year. Renewal takes place in stages, as a rule for half of the members every six months, with a minuted handover. A person who has completed a term of office may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council before twelve months have elapsed. If there are no other suitable persons, a single reasoned extension is permitted, not exceeding six months, accompanied by a plan for training new persons in accordance with Code 5.\n6. Where the Community does not have a sufficient number of suitable persons, the Assembly may:\na) merge several kindred Councils into a single Council, without prejudice to the separation between those who investigate, those who decide and those who control;\nb) establish the Council in a form shared with other Communities of the same Circle 2, composed in accordance with paragraph 6-bis, letter a);\nc) make temporary use, solely for technical acts and opinions, of the Council of another Community.\n6-bis. Shared Councils of Circles 2 and 3. Where the Codes assign to a Council functions of Circle 2 or of Circle 3, those functions are exercised by a shared Council of the Circle, on the following conditions, except for the Storehouse Councils of Circles 2 and 3, governed by Article 9-bis of Code 15:"],[8,"art-12-bis--technical-councils","Art. 12-bis — Technical Councils","","a) the shared Council is composed of the number of persons provided for by paragraph 3, drawn by lot pursuant to paragraph 4 from among the members of the corresponding Councils of the Base Communities of the Circle and, if they are not sufficient, from among the other persons of those Communities who possess the required competence. No more than two members come from the same Base Community. Paragraphs 5, 8 and 11 and Article 16 apply; the office in the shared Council counts as membership of a Council for the purposes of paragraph 8;\nb) the shared Council adopts the bound technical acts of paragraph 9 and prepares the inquiry and the proposals for the General Assembly. Where a serious and immediate risk to persons, to the Basket or to the continuity of an essential service does not allow the General Assembly to be awaited, it may adopt the urgent decisions strictly necessary, reasoned and published without delay. The urgent decision ceases to have effect if it is not ratified by the General Assembly within thirty days; for that purpose the Assembly may be convened with the reduced notice of Article 14, paragraph 9;\nc) discretionary decisions, including those that distribute goods or services among the Communities according to criteria not already established and those that suspend, replace or deactivate a system or an infrastructure of the Circle, fall to the General Assembly pursuant to Article 14 and to Article 35.2 of the Constitution;"],[8,"art-12-bis--technical-councils","Art. 12-bis — Technical Councils","","d) the shared Council exercises no powers of command over the Base Communities or over their Councils and has no disciplinary powers. It does not constitute a permanent power: each member remains in office only for the term of paragraph 5 and the Assembly of the Community of origin may revoke them at any time by reasoned decision; the seat is filled by a new drawing by lot;\ne) the acts of the shared Council are published in accordance with Article 15 and may be challenged in accordance with Article 17.\n7. The Accounting Council may not be merged with the Storehouse Council or with the Quality Council. The Guarantee Circle may not be merged with any Council.\n8. No one may be a member at the same time of more than two Councils, or of a Council and of the Guarantee Circle. No more than two persons belonging to the same Circle 0 may serve on the same Council.\n9. The Councils do not exercise powers of command, have no disciplinary powers and do not decide in place of the Assembly. They adopt bound technical acts in execution of decisions, STVs, SVSs and Sheets already approved; they formulate discretionary proposals and decisions in accordance with the consensus method of Article 4, after publication for at least seventy-two hours; they issue binding negative opinions solely in the cases of Article 8. The Biodiversity Council also issues the authorisations of Article 13.3 of the Constitution, in accordance with Article 3, paragraph 5, of Code 13."],[8,"art-12-bis--technical-councils","Art. 12-bis — Technical Councils","","10. The Assembly may at any time remove one or more members by reasoned decision. Removal does not cancel the opinions already given, pursuant to Article 8, paragraph 2. Article 16 on conflict of interest applies.\n11. The time devoted to Council, guarantee and facilitation functions and to mandatory documentation is counted in the CBO pursuant to Article 7.2 of the Constitution and to Code 1. The office does not confer any right to CdR, privileges or preferential access to the commons.\n12. Small Communities. In Communities with fewer than one hundred and fifty persons entitled to vote, the technical Councils are combined, unless the Assembly resolves otherwise with reasons, into three Area Councils:\na) the Material Life Council, which combines the Storehouse, Production, Agroecological, Logistics, Energy and Water, and Habitat and Territory Councils;\nb) the People Council, which combines the Health, Care and Competences Councils;\nc) the Technical Safeguards Council, which combines the Quality, Safety, Network and Biodiversity Councils."],[8,"art-12-bis--technical-councils","Art. 12-bis — Technical Councils","","Each Area Council is composed of three to seven persons and exercises the functions that the Codes assign to the combined Councils; the expressions used in the Codes shall be understood as referring to it. The Accounting Council, which may be established in shared form within Circle 2, and the Guarantee Circle remain separate. Where a Code requires the opinion of a Council other than the one making the proposal and the two are combined in the same area, the opinion is given by two competent persons not involved in the preparatory inquiry. In small Communities the interval provided for in paragraph 5 is reduced to six months.\n13. Limit on administrative time. The total time devoted to Council, guarantee, facilitation and accounting functions and to mandatory documentation shall not exceed one tenth of the total CBO of the Community, calculated on a quarterly basis in the Labour Balance provided for in Article 2 of Code 1. If the limit is exceeded, before assigning new hours the Assembly shall simplify: it shall merge bodies, reduce the frequency of formalities and abolish duplicate registers. Formalities protecting health, safety, personal data and the guarantees of Code 4 may not be reduced. Every proposal for a new rule shall indicate in the Dossier the administrative time it requires."],[8,"art-13--assembly-decisions-and-qualified-majority","Art. 13 — Assembly decisions and qualified majority","","1. The Assembly shall be convened with at least seven days’ notice. The notice of convocation shall indicate:\na) the agenda;\nb) the proposal submitted for decision;\nc) the Allocation Report provided for in Article 10, where required;\nd) the relevant STV or SVS;\ne) the estimated impact in UCR and the risk class;\nf) the arrangements for submitting objections.\n2. The decision is adopted by consensus, understood as the absence of valid reasoned objections persisting after the procedure of Article 4.\n3. If objections remain after two sessions of facilitated discussion, the following rules apply:\na) if the objection reports a breach of a mandatory rule or a serious risk that cannot be remedied, the proposal may not be approved by majority. It shall be referred back for adaptation or shelved;\nb) if the objection concerns an aspect that is technically resolvable and no serious risk or breach of a mandatory rule remains, mandatory mediation shall be carried out. If, after the mediation, the objection persists, the Assembly shall decide: for ordinary resolutions, by simple majority of the active participants pursuant to Article 35.1 of the Constitution; for decisions that the matrix in Article 7 assigns to the Assembly and in the other cases in which the Codes require a qualified majority, by a majority of two thirds of the active participants, with reinforced and traceable reasons.\n4. For the purposes of the qualified majority, the denominator consists of the active participants in the deliberation; abstainers are excluded. By way of example: if one hundred and twenty persons participate, forty abstain and eighty participate actively, the two-thirds majority is reached with at least fifty-four votes in favour.\n5. A qualified majority may not validate a decision incompatible with a mandatory rule or overcome a binding negative opinion pursuant to Article 8.\n6. The minutes shall indicate the initial proposal, the objections, the outcomes of the mediation, any amendments, the quorum, the calculation of the majority and the reasons for the decision."],[8,"art-13-bis--conscientious-or-ecological-sustainability-objection","Art. 13-bis — Conscientious or Ecological Sustainability Objection","","1. A minority equal to at least ten per cent of the voters may raise, within seventy-two hours of the announcement of the outcome, a Conscientious or Ecological Sustainability Objection pursuant to Article 34.2 of the Constitution, indicating the contested part and the reasons. Any person entitled to vote in the Assembly that adopted the resolution may sign the Objection, even if they did not vote; the number of signatures required is calculated on the number of voters. Signatures are collected in accordance with Article 7-bis of Code 7: the register of those entitled to vote verifies only that each signatory is entitled to vote and signs only once, and only the text of the Objection and the number of valid signatures, without the names of the signatories, are communicated to the Assembly.\n2. The objection does not constitute a veto. It suspends the effect of the contested part alone, unless the suspension would compromise an essential service or give rise to a serious and immediate risk; in that case the applicable Continuity Plan shall be implemented.\n3. Within fourteen days the proposer and the competent body shall publish a reasoned reply and, where appropriate, an amended proposal. For the ecological aspects, the preparatory inquiry shall be supplemented by the competent Council.\n4. The Assembly shall re-examine the contested part. Confirmation without amendments requires a majority of two thirds of the active participants and a statement of reasons that takes account of the grounds of the objection. No new Objection on the same part may be raised against the confirmation.\n5. Decisions that conflict with a mandatory rule, with a binding negative opinion or with the biophysical limits referred to in Article 14.6 of the Constitution may not be confirmed.\n6. A person who has raised a conscientious objection may not be obliged to carry out the contested part personally. Article 7.4 of the Constitution applies. Since signatures are anonymous, the person asserts this right by declaring it to the body that organises the implementation, without having to prove that they signed."],[8,"art-14--general-assembly","Art. 14 — General Assembly","","1. The General Assembly is the joint deliberation of all the persons entitled to vote of the Base Communities included in the Circle 2 or Circle 3 whose scope is affected by the decision, in accordance with Articles 32 and 34.3 of the Constitution. It is not a permanent body and has no delegates: each person votes individually, through the federated Network or in the local Assemblies. The spokespersons drawn by lot with a binding mandate are responsible for its preparatory inquiry and coordination and do not have a vote of their own.\n2. The General Assembly is competent to:\na) propose the amendment of the Codes, which is approved in accordance with Articles 37 and 38 of the Constitution;\nb) calibrate annually the thresholds in UCR and the risk classes, as well as the parameters of the CdR provided for in Chapter V of Code 1;\nc) resolve on the exclusion of a category on principle, that is, establish in a general and permanent manner that a category of goods, services, practices or relations is incompatible with the principles of the Confederation;\nd) resolve on the other matters that the Codes reserve to it.\n3. The exclusion of an individual good or supplier does not constitute exclusion of a category on principle and remains assigned to the competent bodies in accordance with Codes 2 and 15 and this Code."],[8,"art-14--general-assembly","Art. 14 — General Assembly","","4. The first calibration of the thresholds shall be carried out within six months of the entry into force of this Code. Subsequent calibrations shall be carried out at least once a year.\n5. An extraordinary revision may be initiated, with reasons, on a proposal from the Quality Council or the Accounting Council.\n6. The General Assembly shall be convened with at least twenty-one days’ notice. The resolution is valid when at least three quarters of the Base Communities concerned participate; a Community participates when at least one third of those entitled to vote in it cast a vote.\n7. Pursuant to Article 35.2 of the Constitution, approval requires jointly:\na) the favourable vote of two thirds of the participating Base Communities, a Community being deemed favourable where votes in favour prevail within it;\nb) the majority of the persons voting in the Circle concerned;\nc) the Dossier provided for in Article 33 of the Constitution.\n8. Where the Codes require a qualified majority of the General Assembly, the threshold in paragraph 7, letter b), is raised to two thirds of the persons voting.\n9. In the cases of urgency provided for in the Codes, the General Assembly may be convened with notice reduced to as little as forty-eight hours. The Dossier may be replaced by a reasoned summary inquiry, to be supplemented within thirty days. An urgent resolution has effect for no more than thirty days and is renewable only by a new resolution."],[8,"art-14--general-assembly","Art. 14 — General Assembly","","10. Until Circle 2 is established, the functions of the General Assembly are exercised by the Assembly of Circle 1 or, if several Communities exist, by their joint deliberation, in accordance with Code 16.\n11. Initiatives with a global systemic impact follow Article 35.3 of the Constitution.\n12. The rules on decision, minute-taking and transparency apply in accordance with the Constitution and the relevant provisions of this Code."],[8,"art-14-bis--constitution-of-the-extended-circle","Art. 14-bis — Constitution of the Extended Circle","","1. The Extended Circle provided for by Article 5.1, letter d), of the Constitution brings together at least three contiguous and ecologically connected Circles 2 that have completed the transition pursuant to Code 16.\n2. The minimum Federation is constituted as an Extended Circle by the resolution closing its transition, pursuant to Article 10 of Code 16. From that day the functions of Circle 3 are exercised by the Extended Circle.\n3. Three or more Circles 2 that are not part of an Extended Circle may constitute one by resolution of the General Assembly of their Base Communities, adopted by the qualified majority of Article 14, paragraph 8, and with a Dossier documenting the requirements of paragraph 1. A Circle 2 that does not vote in favour is not part of it.\n4. A Circle 2 that has completed the transition joins a contiguous Extended Circle by resolution of its own General Assembly. The Extended Circle verifies only the protocols of technological and logistical interoperability, pursuant to Article 4.5 of the Constitution and Article 21, paragraph 2, of Code 15.\n5. For the purposes of Articles 37 and 38 of the Constitution, an Extended Circle exists from the date of the resolution of paragraph 2 or of paragraph 3, entered in the Register of Assembly Decisions. The Extended Circle resolves through the General Assembly and operates through the shared Councils of Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis; it has no other bodies."],[8,"art-15--differentiated-transparency","Art. 15 — Differentiated transparency","","1. Information relating to decisions is classified in the following levels:\na) public: decision, summary statement of reasons, value in UCR, risk class and outcome;\nb) public in aggregate form: statistics and the Exclusions Register, in a form that does not allow the unauthorised identification of persons;\nc) accessible to those entitled to vote: full STV or SVS, preparatory inquiry, Allocation Report, objections and opinions, within the limits necessary for participation and appeal;\nd) confidential: personal data, credentials and information on the security of infrastructure, in accordance with Article 20, letter g), of Code 2 and the provisions of Code 6 on health matters.\n2. The classification shall indicate the reason for the level of access and, where possible, the duration of confidentiality.\n3. Transparency may not infringe data sovereignty, the rights of persons or the security of infrastructure.\n4. A decision to restrict access shall state its reasons, be traceable and be open to challenge in accordance with Codes 4 and 7."],[8,"art-16--conflict-of-interest","Art. 16 — Conflict of interest","","1. The rules on conflict of interest apply to every decision, including those relating to:\na) internal or external purchases;\nb) investments;\nc) appointments;\nd) exclusions;\ne) contractual modifications;\nf) commitment of common resources.\n2. Anyone who takes part in the preparatory inquiry or in the decision and has a direct, indirect, family or economic interest in the outcome shall declare it before the deliberation begins.\n3. The person in a conflict of interest shall not take part in the preparatory inquiry, in the assessment of objections or in the decision, unless it is necessary to hear that person as a party or as a source of information. In that case participation is limited to the hearing and shall be recorded.\n4. Failure to make the declaration, or participation in breach of this Article, produces the effects provided for in Article 18. The procedural assessment falls to the Guarantee Circle in accordance with Code 4."],[8,"art-17--appeal-and-continuity-of-service","Art. 17 — Appeal and continuity of service","","1. An appeal may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days of the communication of the decision. If the Guarantee Circle has given an opinion or an authorisation on the contested decision, such as the opinion of Article 7-quater, paragraph 2, letter b), the appeal shall be decided by substitute members drawn by lot pursuant to Article 15, paragraph 3, of Code 4.\n1-bis. An appeal against the decisions of the General Assembly and of the bodies of Circles 2 and 3, including the shared Councils of Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, shall be decided by three members drawn by lot from among the members of the Guarantee Circles of the Base Communities concerned, belonging to different Communities. The sortition is public and tracked pursuant to Article 15; Articles 15 and 37, paragraph 5, of Code 4 apply. The decision applies to all the Communities concerned.\n2. The appeal does not automatically suspend the decision. The Guarantee Circle may order precautionary suspension where it establishes a serious risk, irreversible harm or a procedural breach capable of affecting the outcome.\n3. If the decision concerns a critical service, including connectivity, digital services, infrastructural maintenance or specialist health services, the applicable Continuity Plan shall be implemented during the appeal.\n4. Suspension is prohibited where it would cause greater harm than the defect complained of, unless continuation would entail a serious and immediate risk that cannot otherwise be contained.\n5. The Guarantee Circle shall decide within fourteen days of receipt of the appeal, except for a reasoned extension due to particular complexity or to the need to obtain indispensable opinions.\n6. The decision on the appeal shall state its reasons and be communicated to the parties. The review does not transfer to the Guarantee Circle the technical or allocative competences of the other bodies."],[8,"art-18--effects-of-invalidity","Art. 18 — Effects of invalidity","","1. For the internal purposes of the Confederation, the following categories apply:\na) nullity: this arises when the decision lacks the mandatory Allocation Report, breaches a mandatory rule, was adopted in the presence of a serious concealed conflict of interest, lacks the required STV or SVS, or circumvents a binding negative opinion. A null decision is without effect from the outset. The implementing acts are suspended to the extent necessary and the procedure must be renewed. Nullity may be contested by anyone with an interest within twelve months and may be raised by the Guarantee Circle of its own motion;\nb) voidability: this arises in the presence of a non-mandatory procedural defect, such as an irregularity in the quorum or in the time limits, or an incomplete preparatory inquiry that can be remedied. The decision remains effective until it is annulled. The defect may be remedied within thirty days, where possible, or else the decision is annulled on appeal;\nc) remediable irregularity: this arises in the presence of a clerical error or of a formality that does not affect the validity of the decision. It is corrected by means of a traceable rectification without any need to renew the procedure.\n2. The classification of the defect and its effects lie with the Guarantee Circle, in compliance with Code 4 and with the limits of competence laid down in the other Codes.\n3. The suspension of the implementing acts may not interrupt an essential service without the activation of the Continuity Plan, except in the case of serious and immediate risk.\n4. The decision on nullity, voidability or irregularity shall indicate the remedies, the time limits and the body competent for the renewal or the correction."],[8,"art-19--status-of-the-technical-annexes","Art. 19 — Status of the Technical Annexes","","1. The Technical Annexes relating to UCR and to risk assessment do not constitute autonomous provisions of this Code.\n2. The methodologies contained in the Annexes are mandatory for the application of Articles 5 to 11.\n3. The Annexes are approved and amended by the Assembly of Circle 1 in accordance with the applicable deliberative method.\n4. Each Annex shall indicate its version, date of approval, scope of application, the persons technically responsible and the arrangements for revision.\n5. An Annex may not modify thresholds, competences or guarantees established directly by the Constitution or by the Codes."],[8,"art-20--inter-code-linkage","Art. 20 — Inter-Code linkage","","1. This Code receives:\na) from Code 1 the determination of requirements and the information relating to the Storehouses;\nb) from Code 2 the STVs and the SVSs;\nc) from the competent Circles the relevant technical opinions.\n2. This Code transmits the approved decisions to the bodies responsible for implementation, including the Storehouses and the Competence Circles.\n3. The main cross-references are the following:\na) Code 2, Article 15, for decision-making competence and the admission thresholds;\nb) Code 1, Article 40, for purchases and the comparative report;\nc) Code 4, for the procedural guarantee and appeals;\nd) Code 15, for external relations.\n4. The decisions, the opinions and the underlying documents are traceable and accessible in accordance with Article 15. No allocative decision may be opaque or lack a statement of reasons that can be reconstructed."],[8,"art-21--entry-into-force-and-application-test","Art. 21 — Entry into force and application test","","1. This Code enters into force in coordination with Code 2 and with the applicable provisions of Code 1.\n2. Before the definitive filing of the version, a mandatory application test shall be carried out on a sample of between eight and ten concrete cases.\n3. The test cases shall include different combinations of economic threshold and risk class, including at least:\na) an operation of low value and at low risk;\nb) an operation of low value and at high or critical risk;\nc) an operation of medium value in UCR and medium risk;\nd) an operation of high value in UCR and high or critical risk;\ne) a case with a binding negative opinion;\nf) a case with a conflict of interest;\ng) a case of appeal and continuity of service.\n4. The outcomes shall be documented in order to verify that the matrix in Article 7 leads to an unambiguous deliberative level and that the time limits, opinions and remedies are applicable.\n5. The critical issues that emerge shall be corrected before the definitive filing or recorded as open questions to be submitted to the competent body."],[9,"art-1--subject-matter-purposes-and-systematic-position","Art. 1 — Subject matter, purposes and systematic position","","1. This Code governs the prevention and management of conflicts, the procedural ascertainment of violations of the Codes, the reparation of harm and, where possible, the reintegration of persons and Circles into the confederal community. It also governs the grades of offence, the adjudicating bodies and the protection measures provided for by Title VIII of the Constitution.\n2. The Code implements the constitutional principles of non-accumulation of power, subsidiarity, centrality of the person, differentiated transparency and deliberation in accordance with the consensus method through the absence of valid reasoned objections.\n3. Within the confederal normative system:\na) Code 1 governs needs, the contribution, production and the Storehouses;\nb) Code 2 governs the criteria of technical and qualitative admissibility of goods and services, the STVs and the SVSs, as well as the related suspensions, exclusions and substitution procedures;\nc) Code 3 governs the decision-making bodies, the deliberative proceedings, the thresholds, the quorums, the majorities and deliberative appeals;\nd) this Code governs the restorative management of conflicts and harm, the procedural guarantees, the precautionary measures within its own competence, the verification of restorative agreements and, in Chapter V, offences and protection measures.\n4. The procedure governed by this Code has a restorative and preventive purpose and not a punitive one. It operates within the confederal legal order and neither replaces State jurisdiction nor limits the rights recognised by the Constitution. Until the transition is completed, Article 37 shall apply."],[9,"art-2--principles-of-the-restorative-procedure","Art. 2 — Principles of the restorative procedure","","1. The restorative procedure is founded on the following principles:\na) priority of reparation over punishment;\nb) voluntary participation in the restorative conference, without prejudice to the consequences and the protective measures provided for by the Codes;\nc) dialogical reconstruction of the facts, through listening to the accounts, identifying the shared and the contested elements and recognising any harm, in accordance with the procedures of the Technical Annex;\nd) proportionality of the measures to the conduct, the harm and the circumstances of the case;\ne) prevention of repetition of the conduct and removal, where possible, of the causes that gave rise to it;\nf) centrality of the injured person and of the community directly involved;\ng) confidentiality and transparency differentiated according to the levels provided for by Article 15 of Code 3;\nh) presumption of non-responsibility until the conclusion of the applicable procedure, without prejudice to the power to adopt precautionary measures necessary for safety, data protection or the continuity of services, within the limits provided for by Article 9;\ni) prohibition of retaliation against anyone who reports, participates or provides information in good faith.\n2. No provision of this Code may be interpreted so as to impose on the injured person a meeting, a direct confrontation or a restorative agreement against that person’s will."],[9,"art-3--scope-of-application","Art. 3 — Scope of application","","1. This Code applies to reports concerning:\na) the alleged violation of mandatory rules of the Constitution or of the Codes;\nb) conflicts between members or between Circles;\nc) the conflicts of interest governed by Article 16 of Code 3;\nd) damage to commons, infrastructure or Storehouses governed by Code 1;\ne) violations of data sovereignty and of the rules on data processing provided for by Code 2, Article 20, letter g);\nf) non-performance, non-conformity or cessation of the supply of external goods or services governed by Articles 20 and 24 of Code 2;\ng) violation of the transparency obligations provided for by Article 15 of Code 3;\nh) the offences of Grades 1 to 6 governed by Chapter V.\n2. The application of this Code does not transfer to the Guarantee Circle technical, health, allocative or deliberative competences assigned to other bodies.\n3. Where a report concerns several areas, the related procedures shall be coordinated pursuant to Article 23. Each body retains competence over the aspect assigned to it by the Codes."],[9,"art-4--guarantee-circle-nature-composition-incompatibilities-and-limits","Art. 4 — Guarantee Circle: nature, composition, incompatibilities and limits","","1. The Guarantee Circle is established as an independent body responsible for restorative facilitation and for safeguarding the procedural guarantees provided for by this Code.\n2. The Guarantee Circle:\na) does not exercise ordinary deliberative or allocative powers;\nb) does not carry out technical assessments of conformity or quality reserved to other bodies;\nc) does not replace the competent bodies;\nd) exercises exclusively the functions of restorative facilitation, procedural oversight, adoption of the precautionary measures assigned to it and verification of the performance of agreements, and the other functions expressly assigned to it by the Constitution and the Codes, within the limits established by the Constitution and by the Codes.\n3. The Guarantee Circle shall not establish within itself second-instance bodies, enlarged assemblies or permanent jurisdictions. Review is entrusted to the external Panel governed by Article 17."],[9,"art-4--guarantee-circle-nature-composition-incompatibilities-and-limits","Art. 4 — Guarantee Circle: nature, composition, incompatibilities and limits","","4. The Guarantee Circle is composed of three to seven persons, drawn by lot through a public and traceable procedure from among those who have completed the training referred to in paragraph 5 and declared their availability. No member shall perform the function for more than ninety consecutive days or for more than one hundred and eighty days in total within a period of three hundred and sixty-five days; renewal takes place in staggered groups, with a minuted handover. At least twelve months shall elapse between two terms of service, pursuant to Article 4.4 of the Constitution. The Guarantee Circle may not be merged with any Council.\n5. Admission to the Guarantee Circle requires initial training of at least forty hours in restorative justice, facilitation, Codes 1, 2 and 3 and data processing. An annual refresher of at least forty hours, accompanied by periodic supervision, is also required. Lack of the initial training precludes inclusion in the list of persons eligible for sortition.\n6. Competences are separated as follows:\na) the technical assessment of conformity, the adoption or application of the STVs and the SVSs and the technical exclusion of goods or services fall to the Quality Council, in accordance with Articles 15, 20 and 24 of Code 2;\nb) specialist opinions on health, safety, digital infrastructure or data sovereignty and ecological limits fall to the competent Circles, in accordance with Code 3, Article 8, and Code 2, Article 20, letter g);"],[9,"art-4--guarantee-circle-nature-composition-incompatibilities-and-limits","Art. 4 — Guarantee Circle: nature, composition, incompatibilities and limits","","c) allocative resolutions and the determination of the decision-making level, the quorums and the majorities fall to the Assembly of Circle 1 or to the General Assembly, in accordance with Articles 7, 7-bis, 7-ter, 7-quater, 12, 13 and 14 of Code 3;\nd) the calculation and certification of values in UCR and the anti-circumvention verification fall to the Accounting Council, in accordance with Articles 9 and 11 of Code 3 and Articles 20, paragraph 6, and 40, paragraph 4, of Code 1;\ne) procedural protection, restorative facilitation, the adoption of the precautionary measures assigned to it and the formal classification of deliberative defects in accordance with Code 3 fall to the Guarantee Circle.\n7. No body may combine, in the same proceedings, the functions of investigator, decision-maker and direct beneficiary of the decision."],[9,"art-5--report-and-voluntary-nature","Art. 5 — Report and voluntary nature","","1. A report may be submitted by anyone who has a direct interest or has become aware of facts relevant for the purposes of Article 3. The registration of the report and access to it follow the “those entitled” level provided for by Article 15 of Code 3. Upon reasoned request, the report shall be handled at the confidential level provided for by the same Article.\n2. Within seven days of receipt, the Guarantee Circle shall carry out a preliminary examination and communicate one of the following determinations:\na) admission to the restorative procedure;\nb) reasoned inadmissibility, stating the reason and any alternative procedure available;\nc) a proposal for direct mediation, where the nature of the conflict and the consent of the persons involved make it appropriate.\n3. Participation in the restorative conference is voluntary. Refusal to participate:\na) does not constitute an admission of responsibility;\nb) may not be regarded as evidence that the report is well founded;\nc) does not prevent the adoption of the protective, continuity, suspension or exclusion measures provided for by Articles 20 and 24 of Code 2 and by Articles 8, 16, 17 and 18 of Code 3, provided that the conditions and guarantees established by the respective Codes are complied with.\n4. The submission of a report is not equivalent to the ascertainment of a violation or of a responsibility.\n5. The reporting person shall be informed, within a reasonable time, of the receipt of the report and of the preliminary determinations, within the limits permitted by confidentiality and by the protection of the data of the other persons involved."],[9,"art-6--stages-of-the-procedure","Art. 6 — Stages of the procedure","","1. The ordinary procedure consists of the following stages:\na) submission and registration of the report pursuant to Article 5;\nb) preliminary examination of admissibility;\nc) separate hearing of the persons involved, except in the case of express and informed waiver;\nd) restorative conference pursuant to Article 7;\ne) drafting of the restorative agreement, if any, pursuant to Article 8;\nf) performance of the agreement;\ng) verification of fulfilment pursuant to Article 21;\nh) closure of the procedure;\ni) appeal, if any, pursuant to Article 17.\n2. The ordinary time limit for concluding the procedure is thirty days from the communication of admissibility. The time limit may be extended on account of the complexity of the case, the reasoned unavailability of the persons involved or the need to obtain specialist opinions. The extension shall be reasoned, communicated to the parties and recorded in the case file.\n3. The documentation shall be kept in a reference case file linked, for the relevant parts only, to the Allocation Report provided for by Article 10 of Code 3 and to the transparency levels established by Article 15 of the same Code. The link does not authorise access to personal data that are not necessary for the deliberative or restorative purpose.\n4. The procedure may be suspended for the time strictly necessary where:\na) a technical or deliberative proceeding indispensable for ascertaining a precondition is under way;\nb) there is a risk to the safety of the persons involved;\nc) one of the parties is unable to participate for documented reasons.\nThe suspension shall be reasoned, communicated to the parties and subject to periodic review."],[9,"art-7--restorative-conference","Art. 7 — Restorative conference","","1. The restorative conference takes place with the participation of:\na) the person to whom the reported conduct is attributed, if that person consents;\nb) the injured person, if that person consents;\nc) the facilitator designated by the Guarantee Circle;\nd) any Circle affected, through a contact person identified in accordance with Code 1;\ne) the support persons admitted pursuant to the following paragraphs.\n2. Direct confrontation between the parties is the ordinary method, but it is not mandatory. The facilitator may arrange separate hearings, indirect communications or another suitable method, taking into account the wishes and the safety of the persons involved.\n3. The participation of the following is permitted:\na) a trusted person chosen by each party;\nb) specialist technical support, where necessary in order to understand an STV or an SVS governed by Code 2;\nc) legal support, with a function of assistance and without substitutive representation, except as provided for by paragraph 4;\nd) further support persons whose presence is necessary to ensure informed and safe participation.\n4. Representation in the strict sense is permitted exclusively in the cases provided for by the Constitution or where a person is unable to participate directly and the applicable rules allow representation.\n5. The presence of support persons is subject to the consent of the assisted party and to compliance with the rules on confidentiality. The facilitator may limit their participation only on grounds relating to safety, confidentiality or the proper conduct of the conference.\n6. The facilitator shall explain to the parties:\na) the purpose and the stages of the conference;\nb) the voluntary nature of participation and of the agreement;\nc) the rules on confidentiality and recording;\nd) the possibility of interrupting or suspending the conference;\ne) how to access the appeal provided for by Article 17.\n7. The facilitator shall ensure that each party has had an effective opportunity to set out its own account, without pressure, intimidation or retaliation."],[9,"art-8--restorative-agreement","Art. 8 — Restorative agreement","","1. The restorative agreement, if reached, shall identify separately:\na) the shared facts;\nb) the facts that remain contested;\nc) the harm recognised and the harm contested;\nd) the agreed measures of material, symbolic or relational reparation;\ne) the future commitments and the measures to prevent repetition;\nf) the Non-Recurrence Plan, where necessary;\ng) the time limits, the methods and the persons responsible for performance;\nh) the methods of verification;\ni) the processing, retention and level of access to the data relating to the agreement.\n2. The agreement may provide for the reparation of a recognised harm even where the parties do not agree on all the facts. A full confession may not be imposed, nor may it constitute a condition for access to the procedure.\n3. The agreement shall be drawn up in writing and signed by the participating parties and by the facilitator. It shall be registered at the “those entitled” level provided for by Article 15 of Code 3, unless a different classification is necessary and reasoned in order to protect personal data or confidential information.\n4. The agreement may not derogate from the Constitution or from mandatory rules of the Codes. Article 2, paragraph 2, of Code 3 shall apply.\n5. No measure in the agreement may impose degrading, dangerous or disproportionate labour, or affect fundamental rights or access to the Basket outside the cases and procedures provided for by the Codes.\n6. If no agreement is reached, the facilitator shall record that fact without expressing any judgement as to responsibility. The procedures provided for by the other Codes remain available, provided that the applicable competences and guarantees are complied with."],[9,"art-9--precautionary-measures-and-continuity","Art. 9 — Precautionary measures and continuity","","1. Where the report concerns an allocative decision, the Guarantee Circle shall assess whether the conditions exist for the precautionary measures provided for by Article 17 of Code 3.\n2. Within seventy-two hours of the request or of becoming aware of an urgent risk, the Guarantee Circle may adopt, by reasoned order:\na) the precautionary suspension of the contested decision, within the limits permitted by Article 17 of Code 3;\nb) the imposition or the request of a continuity plan, in accordance with Article 17 of Code 3 and Article 24 of Code 2;\nc) temporary measures strictly necessary to preserve the safety of persons or the effectiveness of the review.\n3. The continuity of critical services, including connectivity, digital services, infrastructure maintenance and specialist health services, shall be preserved where technically possible. A suspension that causes greater harm than that arising from the contested defect is prohibited, in accordance with Article 17, paragraph 4, of Code 3, unless continuation entails a serious and immediate risk that cannot otherwise be contained.\n4. The precautionary measure does not constitute an anticipated ascertainment of responsibility. It shall be reviewed when the facts or the conditions that justified its adoption change, and it shall cease when the relevant conditions no longer exist.\n5. The order shall state the duration, the conditions for cessation, any continuity obligations and the review procedure."],[9,"art-10--coordination-with-the-rules-on-deliberative-defects","Art. 10 — Coordination with the rules on deliberative defects","","1. The substantive rules on nullity, voidability and curable irregularity are contained exclusively in Article 18 of Code 3.\n2. The Guarantee Circle shall not introduce autonomous categories of invalidity. It ascertains and applies, within the limits of its own competence, the effects provided for by Article 18 of Code 3.\n3. Where it finds elements that may amount to a serious nullity, the Guarantee Circle shall:\na) initiate, if necessary, a restorative procedure of its own motion;\nb) inform the body competent to renew or correct the proceeding;\nc) transmit the relevant elements to the competent body, respecting the levels of transparency and confidentiality;\nd) indicate the provision of Code 3 that may have been violated.\n4. For the purposes of paragraph 3, the following are examples of possible serious defects:\na) a concealed conflict of interest, pursuant to Article 16 of Code 3;\nb) the circumvention of a binding negative opinion, pursuant to Article 8 of Code 3;\nc) the absence of the Allocation Report, where it is mandatory pursuant to Article 10 of Code 3.\n5. The classification and the effects of the defect remain governed by Code 3. The initiation of the restorative procedure does not replace the proceeding necessary for the act to be redone or validated."],[9,"art-11--types-of-restorative-measures","Art. 11 — Types of restorative measures","","1. In relation to the case and with the required consent, the restorative agreement may provide for one or more of the following measures:\na) return of goods or resources;\nb) material reparation of the harm;\nc) activities for the benefit of the community, in compliance with the guarantees of Code 1;\nd) training or refresher training relevant to the conduct and to the prevention of further harm;\ne) a formal apology or recognition of the harm, if freely agreed;\nf) temporary limitation of specific deliberative or representative roles, only if ordered by the competent body and in accordance with the applicable procedures;\ng) temporary accompaniment, supervision or verification;\nh) organisational measures aimed at removing the causes of the conflict or of the violation.\n2. The measure shall state the person responsible, the duration, the methods of performance and the criteria for declaring it fulfilled.\n3. No measure may exceed the competences of the Guarantee Circle or replace a resolution required by another Code."],[9,"art-12--proportionality-and-limits-of-the-measures","Art. 12 — Proportionality and limits of the measures","","1. The restorative measure shall be proportionate to the seriousness of the conduct, the degree of intentionality, any repetition, the extent of the harm and the systemic effects on the commons or on the Storehouses governed by Code 1.\n2. The assessment shall also consider:\na) the conduct subsequent to the facts;\nb) cooperation in the reconstruction and in the reparation, without attributing to silence or to the refusal to participate the value of an admission;\nc) the vulnerability of the persons involved;\nd) the possibility of preventing repetition by less restrictive measures.\n3. Punitive measures are not permitted. This is without prejudice to the category exclusion ordered on principle exclusively by the General Assembly, pursuant to Article 14 of Code 3, and to the protection measures of Chapter V.\n4. Measures affecting essential functions, rights or access to services are permitted only in the cases and with the guarantees established by the Constitution and by the competent Codes."],[9,"art-13--exclusion-as-a-measure-of-last-resort-and-allocation-of-competences","Art. 13 — Exclusion as a measure of last resort and allocation of competences","","1. Competences in matters of exclusion are allocated as follows:\na) Code 2 and the Quality Council assess the technical non-conformity of goods or services and, where the conditions are met, order cessation, substitution or migration in accordance with Articles 20 and 24 of Code 2. A Substitution and Continuity Plan shall be prepared where required by those provisions;\nb) the Guarantee Circle manages the restorative and relational aspect and may facilitate a Non-Recurrence Plan;\nc) Code 3 and the bodies identified by it determine the applicable deliberative level, quorum and majority, in accordance with the matrix of Article 7 and Articles 12, 13 and 14 of Code 3.\n2. The temporary exclusion of a supplier, or the temporary limitation of the roles and functions of a member, may be proposed or ordered only by the competent body and after having assessed:\na) the documented failure of the reasonably practicable restorative measures;\nb) the refusal to participate, without such refusal constituting evidence of responsibility;\nc) the seriousness and any repetition of the violation;\nd) the necessity and proportionality of the measure;\ne) the procedural guarantees and the remedies provided for by the Codes.\n3. The definitive exclusion of a supplier or the category exclusion on principle shall be resolved exclusively by the General Assembly pursuant to Article 14 of Code 3.\n4. The proposal for definitive or category exclusion shall be reasoned and accompanied by the technical investigation of the Quality Council, where relevant. The Guarantee Circle may report on the procedural and restorative aspects, but it does not replace the technical investigation or the resolution of the General Assembly.\n5. For the purposes of the allocation of competences:\na) the technical exclusion of a good or service falls to the Quality Council and to the deliberative bodies indicated by Code 2;\nb) restorative and relational measures are handled by the Guarantee Circle, whereas limitations of status or role are ordered by the body identified by the Codes;\nc) definitive exclusion or category exclusion on principle falls to the General Assembly.\n6. Loss of member status by a person is permitted exclusively in the cases of Article 1.3-bis of the Constitution and in accordance with Chapter V. The cessation of status for persistent refusal of the CBO, provided for by Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution, is governed solely by Article 29, paragraph 6-bis. No measure under this Article may deprive a member of the rights of Article 1.2 of the Constitution."],[9,"art-13-bis--verification-of-compliance-and-deactivation-of-activities-and-functions","Art. 13-bis — Verification of compliance and deactivation of activities and functions","","1. Where the Codes refer to this Code for the verification of compliance, the suspension or the deactivation of a non-compliant activity, function or infrastructure, the following rules apply, without prejudice to the more specific provisions of the sectoral Code:\na) the verification of compliance is a technical assessment by the competent Council in accordance with the sectoral Code; the Guarantee Circle does not carry it out, pursuant to Article 4, paragraph 2, letter b);\nb) if compliance is technically impossible, the deactivation shall be resolved by the Assembly of Circle 1 or, for the functions of Circles 2 and 3, by the General Assembly, on a reasoned proposal from the competent Council, with the procedures of Article 7 of Code 3 where the matrix requires them;\nc) the proposal shall be accompanied by a Substitution Plan with a certain final deadline. Where the function guarantees services of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket, the deactivation takes effect only after the activation of the substitute function, without prejudice to the urgent measures of letter d);\nd) the suspension necessary to deal with a serious and immediate risk shall be adopted in accordance with the sectoral Code and Code 14 and shall be communicated without delay to the Assembly;\ne) the guarantees, the precautionary measures and the appeals of this Code and of Article 17 of Code 3 remain unaffected. Where the non-compliance is attributable to a person, the ascertainment of the facts and any measures concerning that person follow Chapters II and V."],[9,"art-14--restorative-register","Art. 14 — Restorative Register","","1. The Guarantee Circle keeps the Restorative Register in accordance with the transparency levels provided for by Article 15 of Code 3. The Register distinguishes:\na) aggregated and anonymised public data, including statistics and overall outcomes;\nb) the information relating to the Exclusions Register provided for by Article 15 of Code 3, accessible in accordance with the rules established by the same Article;\nc) agreements, investigations, objections and opinions accessible to those entitled;\nd) personal data, credentials and information on the security of infrastructure, accessible exclusively to the persons authorised pursuant to Article 20, letter g), of Code 2 and to the provisions of Code 6 on health data.\n2. For each proceeding the Register shall record at least an identifier, the opening date, the status of the procedure, the closing date and the outcome in the form compatible with the applicable transparency level.\n3. The publication of information for the purpose of public shaming, retaliation or stigmatisation of a person is prohibited.\n4. The transparency obligations do not authorise the disclosure of personal data or the violation of data sovereignty.\n5. Retention periods, rectification, erasure and access to data are governed by Codes 2 and 7 and, for health data, by Code 6."],[9,"art-15--impartiality-abstention-and-substitution","Art. 15 — Impartiality, abstention and substitution","","1. A member of the Guarantee Circle who has a personal interest in the case, a direct relationship with a party or any other circumstance capable of compromising that member’s impartiality shall declare the conflict and abstain.\n2. The parties may report a possible conflict of interest by reasoned application. The decision on abstention shall be adopted without the participation of the member concerned and shall be minuted.\n3. If the conflict concerns the entire Guarantee Circle, its members shall be replaced by sortition from among trained members of other Circles, in accordance with the mechanism provided for by Article 4, paragraph 4, of Code 3 for the assessment of the validity of objections by an independent substitute body.\n4. The substitute member may not have taken part in the contested investigation nor have a direct interest in the outcome."],[9,"art-16--protection-of-the-reporting-person-and-prohibition-of-retaliation","Art. 16 — Protection of the reporting person and prohibition of retaliation","","1. Any retaliation against anyone who submits a report, participates in a procedure or provides information in good faith is prohibited.\n2. Retaliation constitutes an autonomous violation and may give rise to the opening of a new procedure pursuant to Article 5.\n3. The Guarantee Circle shall assess, upon request or of its own motion, the protection measures necessary to prevent intimidation, pressure or detriment during the procedure.\n4. Protection measures shall be proportionate, temporary and reasoned, and may not be used to anticipate a judgement as to responsibility."],[9,"art-17--appeal-and-external-review-panel","Art. 17 — Appeal and external Review Panel","","1. The first instance takes place by means of the procedure and, if reached, the restorative agreement governed by Articles 6, 7 and 8.\n2. The party concerned may lodge an appeal within fourteen days of the communication of the first-instance outcome. An appeal is admissible exclusively on the grounds of:\na) violation of the procedural rules;\nb) manifest disproportion of the measure;\nc) violation of the Constitution or of a mandatory rule of the Codes.\n3. The appeal shall be examined by an ad hoc Review Panel, external to the Guarantee Circle that handled the first instance and non-permanent, composed of:\na) a trained facilitator, drawn by lot from among persons not belonging to the Guarantee Circle that handled the case;\nb) a member of the Quality Council not involved in the contested investigation pursuant to Code 2;\nc) a person drawn by lot from among the members of the Assembly of a Circle 1 other than the one directly concerned, without allocative deliberative powers in the review proceeding.\n4. The sortition shall be traced at the level provided for by Article 15 of Code 3. No member of the first instance may take part in the second instance. The rules on abstention and substitution of Article 15 of this Code shall apply.\n5. The Panel is constituted for the individual appeal, shall decide within fourteen days of its constitution and shall be dissolved once the decision has been communicated. The decision does not constitute a binding precedent and concerns exclusively the case examined. It shall be published in the Archive provided for by Article 35-bis.\n6. For the sitting to be valid, the presence of all three members is required. The Panel shall decide in accordance with the consensus method provided for by Article 4 of Code 3. If consensus is not reached, it shall decide by majority with a statement of reasons; the dissent shall be recorded in accordance with Articles 10 and 13 of Code 3.\n7. The Panel may:\na) confirm the contested outcome;\nb) annul it for procedural defect and refer the case back to a new conference with a different facilitator;\nc) adjust the measure where it proves manifestly disproportionate.\nThe Panel may not order a definitive or category exclusion, which is reserved to the General Assembly pursuant to Article 14 of Code 3.\n8. If the appeal is lodged exclusively by the person to whom the measure is addressed, the Panel may not make the contested measure more severe.\n9. The decision shall be recorded and communicated to the parties in accordance with the transparency levels provided for by Article 15 of Code 3."],[9,"art-18--connection-with-code-1-requirement-production-and-storehouses","Art. 18 — Connection with Code 1: requirement, production and Storehouses","","1. Where the reported conduct has caused damage to commons or to the Storehouses governed by Code 1, the procedure shall assess as a priority the material reparation of the damage and, where necessary, a Non-Recurrence Plan.\n2. The production Competence Circle may be involved in order to ascertain the technical methods of reparation, without assuming functions of ascertainment of responsibility reserved to the applicable procedure.\n3. For the verification of the damage and of the related traceability, Articles 17 and 26 of Code 1 shall apply."],[9,"art-19--connection-with-code-2-quality-stvs-svss-and-exclusions","Art. 19 — Connection with Code 2: quality, STVs, SVSs and exclusions","","1. The technical assessment of conformity, the application of the STVs and the SVSs and the technical exclusion of goods or services fall to the Quality Council in accordance with Articles 15, 20 and 24 of Code 2. The calculation and certification of values in UCR fall to the Accounting Council in accordance with Article 9 of Code 3.\n2. Data sovereignty, data location, key management, sub-suppliers and jurisdiction are governed by Article 20, letter g), of Code 2.\n3. The Guarantee Circle does not assess the technical conformity of the STVs or the SVSs. It may examine exclusively the procedural and restorative aspects that fall within its own competence.\n4. Where the technical assessment is necessary for the restorative procedure, the Guarantee Circle shall request the opinion of the competent body without taking its place."],[9,"art-20--connection-with-code-3-deliberation-thresholds-and-assembly","Art. 20 — Connection with Code 3: deliberation, thresholds and Assembly","","1. The types of operational, allocative and constitutive decision are governed by Article 3 of Code 3.\n2. Reasoned objections, the consensus method and the assessment of the validity of objections are governed by Article 4 of Code 3.\n3. The economic thresholds and the UCR methodology are governed by Articles 5 and 9 of Code 3.\n4. The risk threshold, the documented preliminary assessment, the confirmation in the definitive STV or SVS and the prevalence of the higher level are governed by Articles 6 and 7, paragraph 2, of Code 3.\n5. The binding matrix, the special or reinforced procedures and the related guarantees are governed by Articles 7, 7-bis, 7-ter and 7-quater of Code 3.\n6. Binding negative opinions and the prohibition on circumventing them are governed by Article 8 of Code 3.\n7. The Allocation Report is governed by Article 10 of Code 3.\n8. The anti-circumvention provisions, foreseeability and the 180-day presumption are governed by Article 11 of Code 3.\n9. The composition of the Assembly of Circle 1, participation, the quorums and the treatment of abstentions are governed by Article 12 of Code 3. The technical Councils are governed by Article 12-bis of the same Code.\n10. The assembly decision, mediation, the qualified majority and the denominator of active participants, excluding abstainers in the cases provided for, are governed by Article 13 of Code 3. The Conscientious or Ecological Sustainability Objection is governed by Article 13-bis of the same Code.\n11. The General Assembly, the calibration of the thresholds, category exclusion on principle, the first calibration within six months and the at least annual revision are governed by Article 14 of Code 3.\n12. Differentiated transparency is governed by Article 15 of Code 3.\n13. Conflicts of interest are governed by Article 16 of Code 3.\n14. The appeal and the continuity of services are governed by Article 17 of Code 3.\n15. The effects of nullity, voidability and curable irregularity are governed by Article 18 of Code 3.\n16. The status and the effects of the Technical Annexes that are binding as to methodologies are governed by Article 19 of Code 3.\n17. The inter-Code links and the related cross-references are governed by Article 20 of Code 3."],[9,"art-21--verification-of-fulfilment","Art. 21 — Verification of fulfilment","","1. The Guarantee Circle shall verify the fulfilment of restorative agreements, as a rule, at three and at six months from their signature. The agreement may establish different deadlines where the nature of the measure so requires.\n2. The verification is limited to the obligations provided for by the agreement and does not entail further checks on the person.\n3. In the event of non-fulfilment, the Guarantee Circle shall hear the parties concerned and assess:\na) whether the non-fulfilment is justified by a supervening impediment;\nb) whether it is possible to modify the time limits or the methods of performance;\nc) whether it is necessary to reopen the conference with a different facilitator;\nd) whether the conditions exist for proposing a further measure pursuant to Article 13.\n4. Non-fulfilment does not automatically give rise to exclusion or to a sanction. Any consequences shall be adopted by the competent body in accordance with the applicable Codes."],[9,"art-22--confidentiality","Art. 22 — Confidentiality","","1. The outcome of the procedure may be published in aggregated form. Personal details remain confidential in accordance with the transparency levels provided for by Article 15 of Code 3 and by Article 20, letter g), of Code 2.\n2. Before the conference, the parties shall be informed of the methods of recording, retention, access and publication of the data. The consent of the parties may not derogate from the data protection obligations or authorise the disclosure of information belonging to third parties.\n3. The documents of the procedure are accessible exclusively to the authorised persons and to the extent necessary for the performance of their respective functions.\n4. The information collected in the procedure may not be reused for incompatible purposes, nor for profiling, retaliation or stigmatisation."],[9,"art-23--prohibition-of-duplication-and-coordination-of-procedures","Art. 23 — Prohibition of duplication and coordination of procedures","","1. The same conduct may not be subjected to parallel proceedings having the same subject matter and the same function.\n2. Coordinated procedures are permitted where they concern distinct and non-duplicative aspects, including:\na) the deliberative aspect governed by Code 3;\nb) the harm to the person or to the community;\nc) the violation of the rules on data provided for by Article 20, letter g), of Code 2;\nd) the risk to the continuity of services provided for by Article 17 of Code 3 and by Article 24 of Code 2;\ne) the technical aspect within the competence of Code 2.\n3. In coordinated proceedings a reference case file shall be identified. Communication between the bodies shall be limited to the necessary information and shall clearly state:\na) the subject matter of each proceeding;\nb) the body competent for each aspect;\nc) the opinions requested and obtained;\nd) any precautionary measures adopted;\ne) the time limits and the effects of the decisions.\n4. The coordinated procedure does not confer on one body the competence belonging to another and does not allow measures or consequences to be duplicated for the same fact."],[9,"art-24--entry-into-force-technical-annex-and-constitutional-compatibility","Art. 24 — Entry into force, Technical Annex and constitutional compatibility","","1. This Code shall enter into force in accordance with the coordination provision approved with Codes 2 and 3.\n2. The Technical Annex on Restorative Procedures supplements this Code and binds the methodologies of application within the limits provided for by Article 19 of Code 3. The Annex applies to the procedures governed by Articles 2, 5, 6, 7, 8 and 11 of this Code.\n3. This Code implements the constitutional principles of non-accumulation of power, subsidiarity, consensus, differentiated transparency and procedural guarantee. In the event of conflict, the Constitution shall prevail.\n4. Before the definitive filing of the version, a simulation shall be carried out on a sample of between eight and ten concrete cases, built on the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3 and on the restorative procedures of Article 6 of this Code. The outcomes of the simulation shall be documented and used to identify any drafting or procedural ambiguities."],[9,"art-25--common-principles-and-guarantees","Art. 25 — Common principles and guarantees","","1. This Chapter implements Articles 1.3-bis, 7.3, 7.3-bis, 17.9, 18.7, 30 and 31 of the Constitution. The restorative procedure of Chapters II, III and IV remains the ordinary and priority route. The bodies of this Chapter intervene where it is not accepted, does not produce an agreement or is not adequate to the seriousness of the fact.\n2. No one may be subjected to a measure for a fact which, at the time it was committed, was not provided for as an offence by the Constitution or by the Codes. The definitions of offences may not be extended by analogy.\n3. The person is presumed not responsible until the final decision. For Grades 4, 5 and 6, responsibility shall be proved beyond all reasonable doubt. Silence and the refusal to participate do not count as an admission.\n4. The person to whom the fact is attributed has the right:\na) to know the facts alleged and the elements collected;\nb) to the two trusted facilitators and the trusted person provided for by Article 31.5 of the Constitution;\nc) to be heard, to submit elements and to request that other persons be heard;\nd) to a written and reasoned decision;\ne) to the review provided for by Article 35.\n5. The injured person has the right to immediate protection, to be heard, to a trusted person, to information on the progress of the procedure and to reparation. No meeting with the person to whom the fact is attributed may be imposed on the injured person."],[9,"art-25--common-principles-and-guarantees","Art. 25 — Common principles and guarantees","","6. No measure may have a punitive purpose. Among the suitable measures, the least restrictive shall be chosen. No measure may suppress the vital guarantees of Article 30.4 of the Constitution. After the cessation of status provided for by Article 29, paragraph 6-bis, the rights of the last sentence of Article 1.3 and of Article 4.9 of the Constitution and the portion assigned pursuant to its Article 7.3-bis remain guaranteed to the person.\n7. Each body may be assisted by an exclusively advisory mediation AI. No assessment of responsibility or of dangerousness may be formulated or predicted by an AI.\n8. No one may be judged twice for the same fact. Article 23 shall apply."],[9,"art-25--common-principles-and-guarantees","Art. 25 — Common principles and guarantees","","9. To facts committed by persons who have not reached the age of eighteen, exclusively restorative, educational and protection measures shall apply, defined with the Care Council and with the Competences Council. Article 30, paragraph 3, and Articles 31, 32 and 33 shall not apply. Where there is a concrete and present danger to the safety of others that the other measures cannot contain, the Guarantee Tribunal, by the final decision ascertaining a fact of Grade 4, 5 or 6, may order, by six votes out of seven, protected educational reception: the person resides with persons or in an educational community identified with the Care Council, which are open, without a protected perimeter or surveillance by the Civic Protection Corps, with an educational project, daily mentoring and continuity of learning and of contact with loved ones. The reception lasts no more than twelve months and is renewable once only by a new decision; it is reviewed by the Tribunal at least every three months and ceases when the danger ceases to exist. If the person reaches the age of eighteen during the reception, it continues within the same duration in a placement suited to their age."],[9,"art-26--grades-of-offence","Art. 26 — Grades of offence","","1. Offences are ordered in six grades, pursuant to Article 30.2 of the Constitution:\na) Grade 1: minor non-observance of a common rule or a conflict that has not produced appreciable harm;\nb) Grade 2: violation that has produced limited and reparable harm to a person, to an animal or to the commons;\nc) Grade 3: serious or repeated violation of the duties of co-responsibility, or significant damage to the commons, without violence to the person. This includes refusal of the CBO continued beyond sixty days without justification, pursuant to Article 7.3 of the Constitution; the private accumulation and rent prohibited by the Constitution and by the Codes; retaliation against a reporting person; serious violation of the rules on data; ill-treatment of animals;\nd) Grade 4: attack on the common guarantees or violence to the person that does not fall within Grades 5 and 6. This includes the facts that the Constitution classifies as Grade 4 in Articles 4.4, 15.2, 16.1, 16.5, 17.5 and 18.6; the resource blackmail provided for by Article 11 of Code 15; the buying or exchange of votes; serious threat; battery and non-serious bodily harm; intentional damage to vital infrastructure; the killing or torture of animals;\ne) Grade 5: serious violence against the person. This includes intentional serious bodily harm, sexual violence, sexual acts with minors, kidnapping and the reduction of a person to a condition of servitude;\nf) Grade 6: intentional homicide, torture and Grade 5 facts that have caused death or very serious permanent injuries or have been committed to the detriment of several persons.\n2. The classification of the grade falls to the competent body, by reasoned decision. In case of doubt, the lower grade shall apply.\n3. The Codes may classify specific conduct within Grades 1 to 4. The definitions of offences of Grades 5 and 6 are exclusively those of this Article. The use of force in personal defence is classified in accordance with Article 26-bis."],[9,"art-26-bis--personal-defence-lawfulness-excess-and-assessment","Art. 26-bis — Personal defence: lawfulness, excess and assessment","","1. The use of force, interposition, holding and entry into a Circle 0 carried out within the limits of Article 17.9 of the Constitution and of Article 8-sexies of Code 14 do not constitute an offence.\n2. Excess, and error as to the existence, imminence, duration or seriousness of the aggression, do not constitute an offence where they are due to fear, panic or distress that are excusable in the concrete circumstances. Excusability shall be assessed according to the circumstances as they appeared to the person at the time of the fact, taking into account in particular the speed of events, the difference in strength, the age and the physical and mental condition of the person and the violence they had already suffered from the aggressor. The error of a person who sought the confrontation in order to be able to use force is not excusable, nor is that of a person who acted out of the mere fear of a future aggression, without a concrete and immediate danger.\n3. Outside the case of paragraph 2, the use of force beyond what is strictly necessary, during an ongoing aggression or ill-treatment and for the purpose of bringing it to an end, constitutes a Grade 4 offence, even when serious injury or death has resulted. Where the excess has caused no injury or only slight and reparable harm, it constitutes a Grade 2 offence."],[9,"art-26-bis--personal-defence-lawfulness-excess-and-assessment","Art. 26-bis — Personal defence: lawfulness, excess and assessment","","4. The use of force that does not constitute defence within the meaning of Article 8-sexies, paragraph 5, of Code 14, including force aimed at killing or seriously injuring when it was not necessary to bring the aggression to an end, is classified in accordance with Article 26, paragraph 1. The aggression suffered shall be taken into account in the choice of measures.\n5. The assessment shall proceed as follows:\na) the Guarantee Circle receives the report provided for by Article 8-sexies, paragraph 10, of Code 14, or the report pursuant to Article 5, opens the case file, hears the persons involved separately pursuant to Article 28, paragraph 2, and activates the protection of those who need it;\nb) the Guarantee Circle transmits the case without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal where death or serious injury has resulted from the fact; where there are elements of a non-excusable excess or of a fact provided for by paragraph 4; where force has been used between persons who belong to the same Circle 0 or live together, or against a minor or a person who is unable to protect themselves; where force has been used by or against a member of the Civic Protection Corps; where the person against whom force was used, the person defended or the person who used force so requests;"],[9,"art-26-bis--personal-defence-lawfulness-excess-and-assessment","Art. 26-bis — Personal defence: lawfulness, excess and assessment","","c) in the other cases the Guarantee Circle, if no elements of an offence emerge, communicates this by reasoned determination to the persons involved pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b). The determination does not ascertain responsibility and does not prevent the request for transmission under letter b);\nd) the Guarantee Tribunal ascertains whether the limits of Article 8-sexies of Code 14 have been respected, whether paragraph 2 applies and, if not, the grade. Where a reasonable doubt remains as to the existence of the defence, responsibility for the use of force is not ascertained, pursuant to Article 25, paragraph 3. Where several persons invoke defence, the Tribunal ascertains who began the aggression;\ne) entry into a Circle 0 provided for by Article 8-sexies, paragraph 9, of Code 14 is in every case communicated to the Guarantee Tribunal, which within twenty-four hours reviews whether it took place within the limits of that paragraph and of Article 5.1, letter a), of the Constitution and orders, where necessary, the protection of the persons who live there. Entry outside those limits is classified in accordance with Article 26.\n6. The defence invoked does not prevent the precautionary measures of Article 34, the protection pathways of Article 7-bis of Code 12 for each of the persons involved, or the assessment of the initial aggression. Where force has been used between persons who belong to the same Circle 0 or live together, the Guarantee Circle:"],[9,"art-26-bis--personal-defence-lawfulness-excess-and-assessment","Art. 26-bis — Personal defence: lawfulness, excess and assessment","","a) hears each person separately and without the presence of the other;\nb) offers each, if they so request, separation and temporary accommodation pursuant to Article 34;\nc) informs the Care Council where minors or persons who are unable to protect themselves live in the Circle 0;\nd) where defence is invoked more than once in the same Circle 0 within twenty-four months, proposes in every case a protection pathway and takes this into account in the transmission to the Guarantee Tribunal.\n7. Article 25, paragraph 9, applies to facts committed by persons who have not reached the age of eighteen. For excess in defence only restorative, educational and support measures, defined with the Care Council, are permitted; protected educational reception may not be ordered. The Care Council offers support to the minor who has defended themselves or other persons.\n8. Even when the use of force does not constitute an offence, the restorative procedure of Chapters II, III and IV remains available, on a voluntary basis, to all the persons involved. A person who has defended themselves within the limits of Article 8-sexies of Code 14 is not required to make reparation.\n9. During the transition Article 37 shall apply. The assessment provided for by this Article has effect only within the internal order and does not replace that of the authorities of the State. A person who has used force shall be informed of the right to legal assistance provided for by the law in force."],[9,"art-27--competent-bodies","Art. 27 — Competent bodies","","1. For offences of Grade 1 and 2, the Clarification Assembly of Circle 1 is competent. It operates as a section of the Assembly composed of seven persons drawn by lot for each case from among the members unconnected with the facts, and it is assisted, without the right to vote, by a facilitator of the Guarantee Circle.\n2. For offences of Grade 3, the Transformation Council is competent, composed of five persons drawn by lot for each case: three from among the members of Circle 1 unconnected with the facts and two from among the members of other Communities of the same Circle 2.\n3. For offences of Grade 4, 5 and 6, for the urgent review within twenty-four hours and for the other guarantee functions assigned by the Codes, the Guarantee Tribunal of Circle 2 is competent, composed of seven persons drawn by lot for each case from the list of Article 36. The members belong to Communities other than those of the person to whom the fact is attributed or who is concerned by the decision and of the injured person; at least three hold legal training validated in accordance with Code 5.\n4. The bodies are constituted for the individual case and are dissolved upon the decision. No one may sit on more than three panels within a period of twelve months. The rules on abstention of Article 15 shall apply.\n5. Decisions are taken by consensus. Failing that, a majority of two thirds of the members is required. For the measures of Article 30, paragraph 3, and Article 31, six votes out of seven are necessary.\n6. The Guarantee Circle receives the reports, facilitates the restorative procedure, conducts the preliminary investigation and keeps custody of the case files. It does not ascertain responsibility and does not order measures, except as provided for by Articles 9 and 34 of this Code and by Article 7-bis, paragraph 3, letter b), of Code 12. It also adopts the other acts expressly assigned to it by the Codes, such as the appointment of the temporary representative of Article 7-bis, paragraph 5, of Code 12, the opinion and the authorisation of Article 10, paragraphs 6 and 7, of Code 6 and the review of Article 2-bis, paragraph 4, letter d), of Code 6."],[9,"art-28--procedure","Art. 28 — Procedure","","1. Having received the report pursuant to Article 5, the Guarantee Circle shall provisionally classify the grade and:\na) for Grades 1 and 2, initiate the restorative procedure; if it is not accepted or does not produce an agreement within the time limits of Article 6, it shall transmit the case to the Clarification Assembly;\nb) for Grade 3, transmit the case to the Transformation Council, after having proposed the restorative procedure;\nc) for Grades 4, 5 and 6, transmit the case without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal and activate the protection of the injured person.\n2. The investigation comprises the separate hearing of the persons involved and the collection of the relevant elements, in compliance with Article 18 of the Constitution. Health examinations require the consent of the person, in accordance with Code 6.\n3. The hearing is not public where the injured person so requests or the need to protect a minor so requires. The decision shall be published in anonymised form, in accordance with Article 14.\n4. The decision shall be adopted, as a rule, within thirty days for Grades 1 and 2, within sixty days for Grade 3 and within one hundred and twenty days for Grades 4, 5 and 6. The extension shall be reasoned and communicated to the parties.\n5. The decision shall ascertain the facts, the grade and the responsibility and shall state the measures, their duration, the methods of verification and the time limits for review."],[9,"art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","Art. 29 — Measures for Grades 1 to 4","","1. For Grades 1 and 2, exclusively the measures of Article 11 are permitted, except for the suspension provided for by paragraph 4-bis for refusal of the CBO. No other limitations of rights are permitted.\n2. For Grade 3, the Transformation Council shall define with the person a Transformation Plan of a duration not exceeding twelve months, renewable once only. The Plan may include the reshaping of tasks, mentoring, reparation of the harm and, only if necessary, the suspension of the CdR and of access to non-essential goods and temporary exclusion from the sortitions for offices.\n3. The following constitute justification for refusal of the CBO, pursuant to Article 7.3 of the Constitution:\na) conscientious objection to a task, with assignment of an equivalent activity;\nb) the exemptions, reductions and suspensions of Article 11 of Code 1 and the impediments recognised in accordance with Codes 6 and 12;\nc) the absence of the safety conditions, of the PPE or of the training required by Codes 1 and 14;\nd) the pendency of an appeal with suspensive effect;\ne) for a person who has grown up in the Confederation, the period preceding the choice provided for by Article 26.4 of the Constitution or the expiry of the related time limit, pursuant to Article 6, paragraph 6, of Code 5.\n4. Before any measure for refusal of the CBO, the person shall be informed of the right to the Autonomous Coexistence Pact, pursuant to Article 4.9 of the Constitution."],[9,"art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","Art. 29 — Measures for Grades 1 to 4","","4-bis. In implementation of Article 7.3 of the Constitution, refusal of the CBO that does not yet constitute a Grade 3 offence shall be dealt with as follows:\na) anyone who has knowledge of it, including the Storehouse Council, shall report it to the Guarantee Circle pursuant to Article 5. The Guarantee Circle shall verify whether a justification under paragraph 3 applies and, if none applies, shall refer the case to the Clarification Assembly, constituted pursuant to Article 27, paragraph 1;\nb) the Clarification Assembly shall hear the person, assess with the Storehouse Council the readjustment of tasks, propose a mediation facilitated by the Guarantee Circle and inform the person in writing pursuant to paragraph 4. The sixty days of Article 7.3 of the Constitution run from the day of this information; periods during which a justification under paragraph 3 applies shall not be counted;\nc) if, at least fourteen days after the information under letter b), the readjustment and the mediation have been unsuccessful, the Clarification Assembly may order, by reasoned decision, the suspension of the CdR and of access to non-essential resources. The Basket, housing, care, learning, the vote and the other rights of Article 1.2 of the Constitution remain intact;"],[9,"art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","Art. 29 — Measures for Grades 1 to 4","","d) the suspension ceases by operation of law when the person resumes the CBO, as verified by the Storehouse Council, or when a justification under paragraph 3 arises. The Clarification Assembly shall revoke it, at the request of the person, when the conditions for it cease to exist;\ne) once the sixty days have elapsed, if the refusal persists the Guarantee Circle shall refer the case to the Transformation Council pursuant to Article 28, paragraph 1, letter b); otherwise the suspension ceases. A suspension already ordered continues until the decision of the Transformation Council and in any case for no more than sixty days from the referral; after the decision it continues only if the Plan of paragraph 2 provides for it;\nf) an appeal against the suspension may be brought before the Review Panel pursuant to Article 35, paragraph 1. The appeal does not suspend the measure, unless the Panel decides otherwise, and does not interrupt the time limit of letter b).\nBy way of derogation from Article 27, paragraph 4, the Clarification Assembly remains constituted for the case until the suspension ceases or until the referral under letter e). The suspension does not ascertain an offence and is not recorded as a measure for an offence.\n5. For Grade 4, the Guarantee Tribunal may order, in addition to the restorative measures:\na) exclusion from the sortitions for offices and for the Councils, for no more than thirty-six months;\nb) a transformation pathway with periodic verifications;"],[9,"art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","Art. 29 — Measures for Grades 1 to 4","","c) a prohibition on approaching specified persons or places, or temporary protective removal for no more than twelve months, with assignment of other adequate housing;\nd) the handing over or deactivation of the instruments used to commit the fact.\n6. No measure under this Article entails containment or loss of member status. The sole exception is the cessation of status for persistent refusal of the CBO provided for by paragraph 6-bis, in implementation of Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution.\n6-bis. In implementation of Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution, refusal of the CBO that persists after Grade 3 shall be dealt with as follows:\na) once the Transformation Plan of paragraph 2 has been concluded, including its renewal if ordered, the Transformation Council shall ascertain whether the refusal persists without justification. The justifications of paragraph 3, the exemptions, reductions and suspensions of Article 11 of Code 1, conscientious objection and the impediments due to illness, disability, age or pregnancy recognised in accordance with Codes 6 and 12 do not constitute refusal. In case of doubt the refusal is not ascertained;\nb) if the refusal persists, the Guarantee Circle shall inform the person in writing of the consequences provided for by this paragraph, of the right to the Autonomous Coexistence Pact pursuant to paragraph 4 and of the possibility of resuming the CBO. After thirty days without resumption, it shall refer the case to the Guarantee Tribunal;"],[9,"art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","Art. 29 — Measures for Grades 1 to 4","","c) the Guarantee Tribunal shall hear the person with the guarantees of Article 25, paragraph 4, verify the preconditions of letters a) and b) and may declare, by six votes out of seven, the cessation of member status. Review under Article 35, paragraph 2, is permitted against the decision. The decision takes effect when it is final and the portion of letter e) has been handed over; until then the person remains a member. The procedure lapses if the person resumes the CBO before the decision takes effect;\nd) the cessation produces the effects of Article 4.8 of the Constitution: the person loses access to the Basket and to the Common Storehouses, common housing, the CdR, the confederated services, the vote and offices. The person retains the Personal-Use Goods, drinking water, life-saving emergency care and the other rights of Article 4.9 of the Constitution;\ne) by the same decision the Tribunal shall assign to the person in custody of use a portion of the share reserved for the Pact, pursuant to Article 4-bis, paragraph 10, of Code 10, with access to the necessary water, without a dwelling and without tools or other goods of the Community. The person may build a shelter on it by their own efforts, in compliance with the ecological conditions of the portion. The transfer takes place without the use of force on the person, in the manner provided for by Article 32-bis, paragraph 2, letter e);"],[9,"art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","Art. 29 — Measures for Grades 1 to 4","","f) the person may apply at any time for readmission pursuant to Article 4.8 of the Constitution, by resuming the CBO. Readmission may not be refused on account of the past refusal and takes effect from the application; the portion returns to the share. A new refusal is dealt with afresh in accordance with paragraph 4-bis;\ng) minor children retain member status and all rights. The Tribunal decides in their interest, by the same decision or by a separate decision, where and with whom they live, pursuant to Article 7-bis, paragraph 3-bis, of Code 12. If they live with the person, the housing, the Basket and the services due to them are ensured in the place where they live. The cessation does not, by itself, remove the responsibility for care or the consent to care provided for by Article 2-ter of Code 6, and does not constitute serious neglect or harm within the meaning of Article 7-bis of Code 12;\nh) refusal that persists after the cessation does not constitute a new offence.\nDuring the transition the cessation consists in exclusion from the pilot Circle pursuant to Article 37, paragraph 4, and the portion is assigned only in the forms permitted by the law in force, pursuant to Article 4-bis, paragraph 9, of Code 10."],[9,"art-30--grades-5-and-6-two-chances-rule","Art. 30 — Grades 5 and 6: two-chances rule","","1. The first final decision ascertaining a fact of Grade 5 or 6 shall always give rise to a pathway of protection and transformation. The extreme measures of Article 31 are reserved for recidivism.\n2. The pathway comprises, as necessary:\na) the protection of the injured person and, if that person so wishes, accompaniment of that person;\nb) protective removal from the Community in which the injured person lives, with placement in another Community that is informed of the facts and willing to receive the person;\nc) a territorial ban, which may be supported by the location device under the conditions of Article 33;\nd) an intensive transformation pathway, with verifications at least every six months;\ne) reparation of the harm, in the forms accepted by the injured person;\nf) exclusion from the sortitions for offices and for the Councils for the duration of the pathway.\n3. Where there is a concrete and present danger to the safety of others which cannot be contained by the measures of paragraph 2, the Guarantee Tribunal may order protective containment in an accompaniment Community. The accompaniment Community is an open Community, informed of the facts and willing to receive the person, distinct from the Containment Community of Article 32: it has no protected perimeter and no supervision by the Civic Protection Corps. The decision establishes the obligation to remain in the Community and daily accompaniment, with a transformation pathway; the Guarantee Circle of the accompaniment Community verifies compliance and reports to the Tribunal at least every three months. The person is not held by force; their departure shall be notified without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal, which may review the measures within the limits of this Article and of Article 34. Protective containment lasts for as long as the danger persists and shall be reviewed at least every six months. Its total duration may not exceed five years for Grade 5 and ten years for Grade 6.\n4. During protective containment the person retains member status and the guarantees of Article 32, paragraph 2.\n5. The pathway is concluded by a decision of the Guarantee Tribunal which ascertains its outcome and, if positive, orders full reintegration."],[9,"art-31--recidivism-and-extreme-measures","Art. 31 — Recidivism and extreme measures","","1. The extreme measures of Article 1.3-bis of the Constitution may be ordered only where all the following conditions are jointly met:\na) a previous final decision has ascertained against the person a fact of Grade 5 or 6;\nb) a new fact of Grade 5 or 6 is ascertained by final decision;\nc) there is a concrete and present danger to the safety of others;\nd) no less restrictive measure is suitable to protect persons.\n2. The decision shall declare the loss of member status and order, in the alternative, High-Security Containment or Definitive Removal from the Circles concerned. Definitive Removal shall be preferred where it is sufficient to protect persons.\n3. The decision ordering an extreme measure is always subject to the review of Article 35, even in the absence of an appeal, and becomes final only after confirmation.\n4. The cases and the measures of this Article may not be extended."],[9,"art-32--regime-of-containment-and-of-removal","Art. 32 — Regime of Containment and of Removal","","1. High-Security Containment takes place in a Containment Community with a protected perimeter, under the surveillance of the Civic Protection Corps, in accordance with Article 30.3 of the Constitution and Code 14.\n2. The person retains the right to individual housing, to food, to water, to minimum energy and to health care. The person also has the right to contact with loved ones, to learning, to meaningful activities, to the transformation pathway and to the assistance of the trusted facilitators.\n3. Isolation, restraint and sedation used as a sanction, imposed labour and any inhuman or degrading treatment are prohibited.\n4. Containment has no predetermined duration and is not perpetual. The Guarantee Tribunal, in a composition different from the one that decided, shall review the dangerousness at least every twelve months and, at the request of the person, every six months. When the concrete and present danger ceases, the measure shall cease and a reintegration pathway shall be initiated, with readmission to member status.\n5. A Guarantee Circle belonging to a different Circle 2 shall visit the Containment Community at least every three months, including without notice, and shall publish a report in anonymised form.\n6. Definitive Removal entails a permanent prohibition on residing in and on entering the Circles in which the injured person and the affected community live. The person shall be relocated to another Circle whose Assembly, informed of the facts, is willing to receive that person, or by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact pursuant to Article 4.9-bis of the Constitution. The guarantees of paragraph 2 remain unaffected.\n7. The territorial ban may be supported by the device of Article 33."],[9,"art-32-bis--persons-living-by-means-of-a-pact-and-removal-from-the-common-settlement","Art. 32-bis — Persons living by means of a Pact and removal from the common settlement","","1. This Chapter applies to a person living by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact with the following adaptations:\na) the measures that presuppose member status do not apply, including the loss of member status of Article 31, paragraph 2, the suspension of the CdR and exclusion from sortitions;\nb) in their place the competent body may order the revision of the Pact: the modification of its conditions or, where the protection of persons so requires, the placement of the portion with another Circle 1 in accordance with Article 4-bis, paragraph 6, of Code 10, informed of the facts;\nc) the protective removal of Articles 29, paragraph 5, letter c), and 30, paragraph 2, letter b), and, only where all the conditions of Article 31, paragraph 1, are met, Definitive Removal are carried out by means of the revision of letter b);\nd) violation of the conditions of the Pact is classified in accordance with Article 26 and does not entail the cessation of the Pact.\n2. The removal provided for by Article 4.9-ter of the Constitution takes place as follows:\na) the Guarantee Circle, upon a report pursuant to Article 5, ascertains that the person resides stably in the common settlement without being a member, without a Pact and without contribution, beyond the time limit for hospitality of Article 4.9, letter b), of the Constitution and outside the case of Article 4-bis, paragraph 7, of Code 10;\nb) Circle 1 shall offer the Pact in writing, indicating the portion identified pursuant to Article 4-bis of Code 10, and shall inform the person of the right to enter or re-enter the Confederation; the Guarantee Circle shall propose a mediation;\nc) the refusal is persistent when the person has refused or left unanswered two offers, the second presented after the mediation and at least thirty days after the first;\nd) the Guarantee Circle then refers the case to the Guarantee Tribunal, which hears the person with the guarantees of Article 25, paragraph 4, verifies that the portion is sufficient for self-sufficiency pursuant to Article 4.9-bis of the Constitution and may order removal from the common settlement with the assignment ex officio of the portion;\ne) the removal is carried out without the use of force on the person and consists in the cessation of ordinary access to the common settlement and in making available the portion and the means to transfer the personal-use goods, in accordance with Code 11;\nf) drinking water, life-saving emergency care and the protection of minors remain guaranteed, pursuant to Article 1.3 of the Constitution, as do the rights of the minors of the nucleus provided for by Article 4.9-bis of the Constitution;\ng) the person may at any time accept the Pact or enter the Confederation, and the procedure lapses. Review under Article 35, paragraph 2, is permitted against the decision.\n3. During the transition Article 37, paragraph 3, applies: the removal of paragraph 2 is not carried out."],[9,"art-33--location-device","Art. 33 — Location device","","1. The location device provided for by Article 18.7 of the Constitution may be ordered only by final decision of the Guarantee Tribunal for a fact of Grade 5 or 6, for the direct protection of the injured person or of the Community.\n2. The device is not biometric and records neither audio nor video. It detects exclusively the violation of the protection perimeter or of the territorial ban established by the decision.\n3. The measure lasts no more than twelve months and is renewable only by a new reasoned decision.\n4. The data are encrypted with a split key and are accessible only in the event of violation of the perimeter or of the ban. Accesses are logged and open to inspection. Data that are not relevant are automatically destroyed within seventy-two hours. Predictive uses and profiling are prohibited.\n5. The device shall be ordered only as a less restrictive alternative to protective containment or to High-Security Containment, with the consent of the person, and may not be added to them. If the person does not consent, the Tribunal shall assess the other measures of Articles 30 and 31."],[9,"art-34--precautionary-measures-and-urgent-review","Art. 34 — Precautionary measures and urgent review","","1. Where there is an immediate danger to the safety of a person, the Guarantee Circle or the Civic Protection Corps may order the immediate separation of the persons and provisional removal from a place, in accordance with Code 14.\n2. Every measure that limits freedom of movement shall be communicated without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal, which shall validate, modify or revoke it within twenty-four hours. In the absence of validation the measure shall cease.\n3. The precautionary measure lasts no more than thirty days. It is renewable by reasoned decision until the decision on the fact and does not constitute an anticipated ascertainment of responsibility.\n4. The person subjected to a precautionary measure is guaranteed adequate housing, the Basket and the assistance of Article 25, paragraph 4."],[9,"art-35--review-and-finality","Art. 35 — Review and finality","","1. Against the decisions of the Clarification Assembly and of the Transformation Council, an appeal to the Review Panel of Article 17 is permitted, within fourteen days of communication. In these appeals the member of Article 17, paragraph 3, letter b), is replaced by a person with legal training validated in accordance with Code 5, drawn by lot from the list of Article 36, paragraph 1, letter a).\n2. Against the decisions of the Guarantee Tribunal, review before a second Guarantee Tribunal, drawn by lot in a different Circle 2, is permitted within thirty days. For as long as only one Circle 2 exists, the second Tribunal shall be drawn by lot from among different persons belonging to other Communities.\n3. If the review is requested only by the person to whom the measure is addressed, the measure may not be made more severe.\n4. The decision is final when review has not been requested within the time limits or when it has been confirmed, except as provided for by paragraph 6.\n5. The final decision shall be revised at any time where new elements emerge that demonstrate the error of the ascertainment.\n6. The decisions of the Guarantee Tribunal ascertaining a fact of Grade 5 or 6 are always subject to the review of paragraph 2, even in the absence of a request, and become final only after confirmation, pursuant to Article 31.6 of the Constitution. Until confirmation, only the precautionary measures of Article 34 shall apply."],[9,"art-35-bis--archive-of-decisions","Art. 35-bis — Archive of decisions","","1. The final decisions of the bodies provided for by this Chapter and those of the Review Panel shall be published in the federated Archive of decisions, with the facts ascertained, the grade, the measures and the statement of reasons.\n2. Publication takes place in anonymised form, to protect the injured person, the person to whom the measure is addressed and minors. Where in the local context identification would nevertheless be possible, only the principle applied shall be published.\n3. The decisions do not constitute a binding precedent. The body deciding a similar case shall consult the Archive and, if it departs from the prevailing approach, shall expressly state the reasons for doing so.\n4. Each year one of the Guarantee Circles of the Communities of Circle 2, designated by rotation in coordination with the others pursuant to Article 36, paragraph 1, shall publish a summary of the approaches and of the divergences found. The summary has no normative value; it may form the basis of a proposal to amend the Codes."],[9,"art-36--federated-lists-and-sortition","Art. 36 — Federated lists and sortition","","1. The Guarantee Circle shall keep, in coordination with the other Guarantee Circles of Circle 2:\na) the list of the persons trained and available for the bodies of this Chapter;\nb) the federated list of the persons with L3 competence available for the audits, the panels and the independent opinions provided for by the Codes.\n2. The sortition is public and traced in accordance with Article 15 of Code 3.\n3. No one may be drawn by lot from the lists of paragraph 1 who belongs to the Circle 1 concerned, that is, that of the person to whom the act is attributed or who is concerned by the decision, of the injured person or of the activity subject to audit or opinion, or who has carried out activities there in the last twenty-four months, nor anyone who has an interest in the outcome. This paragraph does not apply to the members whom the Codes have drawn by lot from among the members of the Circle 1 concerned, such as those of Article 27, paragraphs 1 and 2.\n4. Unless the Codes provide otherwise, an audit or opinion assignment shall not exceed thirty days. The time devoted to it shall be counted towards the CBO."],[9,"art-37--transitional-regime-and-relationship-with-the-legal-order-in-force","Art. 37 — Transitional regime and relationship with the legal order in force","","1. Until the completion of the transition governed by Code 16, and in every territory in which the jurisdiction of a State applies, this Chapter has effect as an internal and voluntary legal order among the members and the persons living by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact, who accept it with the Pact.\n2. Facts that constitute a criminal offence under the legal order in force remain subject to the jurisdiction of the State. No one may be dissuaded from turning to the authorities; anyone who is in danger shall be helped to do so. The obligations to report and to file a complaint provided for by law shall be complied with.\n3. During the transition, measures of containment, of coercive removal or of location may not be enforced. Article 25, paragraph 9, as regards protected educational reception, and Article 30, paragraph 3, and Articles 31, 32, 33 and 34 shall apply only in respect of the non-coercive protection measures and of the effects on membership of the Circle.\n4. During the transition, loss of member status consists in exclusion from the pilot Circle, ordered in accordance with the statute of the entity and the applicable law."],[9,"art-37--transitional-regime-and-relationship-with-the-legal-order-in-force","Art. 37 — Transitional regime and relationship with the legal order in force","","5. Until a Basin is constituted, or until the members required can be drawn by lot in the Basin, the functions that the Codes assign to the Guarantee Tribunal, including the second Tribunal of Article 35, paragraph 2, to the Review Panel and to the Transformation Council, and the replacement of the Guarantee Circle provided for by Article 15, paragraph 3, are exercised by a panel with the same number of members, drawn by lot from the public list of trained persons of other pilot Circles or of kindred entities that have accepted this Chapter, kept pursuant to Article 36. If the list does not allow the drawing by lot within the time limit, the panel is drawn by lot, by way of derogation from Articles 17, paragraph 3, 27, paragraphs 2 and 3, and 36, paragraph 3, from among the members of the pilot Circle unconnected with the facts, with the persons involved and with every previous instance, on the model of Article 35, paragraph 2. A panel carrying out a review is composed of persons other than those who decided. A panel composed only of members may not order the protected educational reception of Article 25, paragraph 9, or the measures of Articles 30, paragraph 3, 31 and 33. If no panel can be constituted within the time limit for a validation, the measure ceases."],[9,"art-37--transitional-regime-and-relationship-with-the-legal-order-in-force","Art. 37 — Transitional regime and relationship with the legal order in force","","6. After the closure of the transition, in every territory in which the jurisdiction of a State applies, paragraphs 2, 3 and 4 continue to apply, as do the provisions of the Codes that, for the period of the transition, refer to this Article or limit interventions on persons to what is permitted by the law in force, including Articles 1, paragraph 4, 26-bis, paragraph 9, 29, paragraph 6-bis, last sentence, and 32-bis, paragraph 3, of this Code, Articles 2-bis, paragraph 3-quater, letter f), 2-ter, paragraph 6, and 8, paragraph 10, of Code 6, Article 4-bis, paragraph 9, of Code 10, Articles 7-bis, paragraphs 2 and 3-ter, and 7-ter, paragraph 9, of Code 12 and Articles 8-quinquies, paragraph 6, and 8-sexies, paragraph 13, of Code 14. In these cases references to the pilot Circle shall be understood as referring to Circle 1."],[10,"art-1--subject-matter-legal-nature-and-principles","Art. 1 — Subject matter, legal nature and principles","","1. This Code governs learning and the validation of competences as inalienable infrastructural commons, in accordance with Article 3.1 and Article 1.2, letter c), of the Constitution, concerning the right to education.\n2. For the purposes of this Code:\na) learning: means the process of acquiring knowledge, practical skills and capacity for judgement through experience, mentoring, teaching and documentation;\nb) competence: means the demonstrated capacity to carry out a task safely and in accordance with the applicable STVs, in a replicable, verifiable and transmissible manner;\nc) Personal Portfolio: means the individual, confidential and descriptive record of validated competences, of their limits and of the conditions for exercising them;\nd) Competences Council: means the technical body provided for by this Code, charged with organising learning pathways and validating competences in the cases assigned to it.\n3. This Code guarantees every member access to the knowledge necessary to:\na) satisfy vital needs;\nb) carry out a useful and safe Mandatory Base Contribution in accordance with Code 1;\nc) take part in deliberations in accordance with Code 3;\nd) contribute to the safety, resilience and continuity of their own Circle.\n4. No competence may constitute a barrier to access to the Sufficiency Basket, a condition for the exercise of political rights or an independent ground for exclusion. Competence is an instrument of contribution and care and may not be used to create rent, extractive power or exclusion.\n5. Knowledge relating to the Sufficiency Basket, health, energy, agroecology, habitat, safety and the Network is a common good. The prohibitions of patents, industrial secrecy, paywalls and critical proprietary dependency provided for by Article 5 of Code 2 shall apply."],[10,"art-2--right-to-learning-and-duty-of-transmission","Art. 2 — Right to learning and duty of transmission","","1. Every member has the right to lifelong learning and shall contribute, according to their own possibilities and competences, to the transmission of knowledge. The following principle applies: those who know teach, and those who do not know may ask to learn.\n2. Learning ordinarily takes place through practical activity, the CBO, mentoring, laboratories, building sites, care, cooking, work in the fields and in the workshops, and facilitation. Training separate from practical activity shall be used where it is necessary and the reason shall be documented.\n3. Time devoted to learning, teaching or documenting knowledge is recognised as CBO in accordance with Articles 4 and 5 of Code 1. Depending on its purpose, the training activity is classified:\na) in List B, where it is aimed at acquiring a specialist competence to be validated;\nb) in List A, where it is necessary for immediate safety;\nc) in List C, where it is free or responds to a temporary requirement.\n4. No one may be excluded from learning pathways on grounds of age, gender, origin, physical condition, previous schooling or possession of formal qualifications. The competent Circle has the organisational responsibility for making the pathway accessible.\n4-bis. The materials, tools and spaces necessary for the literacy provided for by Article 6, paragraph 8, and for the training necessary for safety form part of the Sufficiency Basket: access may not be made conditional on the CBO or on CdR. For other pathways, materials are supplied according to the availability of the Storehouses.\n5. Failure to acquire or validate a competence may not entail a reduction of the Sufficiency Basket, exclusion from the Assembly, a sanction or any other detriment unrelated to the actual safety and competence requirements of the task.\n6. No permanent role of teacher is provided for. Teaching is done by those who belong to a Competence Circle and are on a transmission shift. Every member of a Competence Circle shall devote at least twenty per cent of their own CBO to the transmission of competences, pursuant to Articles 6.3, 29.6 and 29.7 of the Constitution."],[10,"art-3--knowledge-storehouses-infrastructure-and-confidentiality","Art. 3 — Knowledge Storehouses, infrastructure and confidentiality","","1. Every Storehouse governed by Code 1 shall have a Knowledge Storehouse, in physical and digital form, connected to the federated Network provided for by Code 7.\n2. The Knowledge Storehouse shall contain at least:\na) the STVs provided for by Code 2, used as primary teaching material;\nb) practical manuals, procedures, documented errors and lessons learned;\nc) audiovisual recordings of practical activities, only with the consent of the persons recorded and in compliance with the rules on data;\nd) an aggregate and anonymised map of the competences available and of the training requirements of the Circle;\ne) an anonymised archive of the outcomes of validations, intended for statistical, training and risk-prevention purposes.\n3. The Knowledge Storehouse is accessible to the Community, tracked and replicated on community servers in accordance with Code 7. Validated knowledge necessary to satisfy vital needs or to guarantee safety may not be kept confidential for purposes of rent or exclusive control.\n4. The map of competences and the Personal Portfolio shall be kept separate:\na) the map is aggregated by function and field and describes collective requirements, without names or indications of shortcomings attributable to individual persons;\nb) the Personal Portfolio is confidential. It may be accessed by its holder, by the Competences Council for validation, by the Health Council where necessary for a safety aspect within its competence and by the Guarantee Circle in the event of an appeal;\nc) the publication of lists of names of persons lacking a competence or whose validation is suspended is prohibited.\n5. The Management AI provided for by Code 7 may index the materials, propose training pathways, facilitate the matching of demand for and supply of teaching and flag aggregate shortcomings. It may not decide on suitability, replace human validation, draw up merit rankings or assign scores to persons.\n6. The collection and use of data shall take place in accordance with Codes 6 and 7. No personal data shall be acquired or retained unless necessary for validation or for the protection of safety."],[10,"art-4--validation-of-competences-and-prohibition-of-extractive-qualifications","Art. 4 — Validation of competences and prohibition of extractive qualifications","","1. In the Confederation, educational qualifications do not constitute, by themselves, a legal condition for accessing the CBO, taking part in deliberations, receiving resources or performing functions. Access to a task depends on the actual competences required and validated in accordance with this Article.\n2. Validation is proportionate to risk and is structured in the following levels:\na) Level 1 — practical test: self-description of the competence and an observable practical test before at least two competent persons. It applies to tasks of nil or low risk;\nb) Level 2 — peer validation: confirmation, on the basis of a practical test and of verification of the applicable STVs, by at least two persons validated at least at Level 2 in the same competence, including persons from other Communities, identified by the Competences Council; the Competences Council records the confirmation and decides on the change of level pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 3. It applies to tasks of medium risk;"],[10,"art-4--validation-of-competences-and-prohibition-of-extractive-qualifications","Art. 4 — Validation of competences and prohibition of extractive qualifications","","c) Level 3 — qualified validation: it applies to high-risk tasks. It includes, by way of example, competences in the fields of health, energy, structural building, the operation of vehicles, the use of plant protection products, critical Network systems and safety as governed by Code 14, as well as any other competence classified as high-risk in accordance with the STVs. Validation is carried out by the Competences Council on the basis of a documented test and of verification of the applicable STVs.\n2-bis. In the Codes, L1, L2 and L3 denote Levels 1, 2 and 3 of paragraph 2; training at level L1, L2 or L3 is the pathway leading to the corresponding validation. For the purposes of paragraph 2, the risk of a task carried out in an activity classified in accordance with Article 1, paragraph 3, of Code 14 is nil for class R0, low for class R1, medium for class R2 and high for class R3; R4 activities are suspended and shall not be carried out until the risk has been reduced. The training required by Code 14 for safety is in addition to the validation of the competence provided for by this Article and does not replace it.\n3. The outcome of validation shall be recorded in the Personal Portfolio in descriptive form and shall indicate at least:\na) the activities that the person is able to carry out;\nb) the operating conditions in which they may carry them out;\nc) the limits of the competence;\nd) the date of validation;\ne) the validators;\nf) the STVs applied;\ng) the date of review."],[10,"art-4--validation-of-competences-and-prohibition-of-extractive-qualifications","Art. 4 — Validation of competences and prohibition of extractive qualifications","","The outcome shall not be expressed as a numerical mark or ranking and does not constitute a permanent qualification.\n4. Validation shall consider at least:\na) the capacity to operate safely;\nb) conformity with the STVs of Code 2;\nc) the replicability of the performance;\nd) the capacity to recognise an error and ask for assistance;\ne) the capacity to transmit to others the knowledge essential for operating safely.\n5. Validation is subject to review, suspension or lapse in accordance with the following rules:\na) lack of practice does not cause the automatic lapse of the competence, but may entail a temporary suspension of autonomous exercise and a request for practical updating;\nb) a Level 3 validation is suspended when the competence has not been exercised for more than twelve consecutive months;\nc) a Level 2 validation is suspended when the competence has not been exercised for more than twenty-four months;\nd) a Level 1 validation is not suspended merely for lack of exercise, but shall be verified when the task is resumed;\ne) a Level 3 validation lapses if, twenty-four months after suspension, a positive review has not been passed;\nf) a Level 2 validation lapses if, forty-eight months after suspension, a positive review has not been passed;"],[10,"art-4--validation-of-competences-and-prohibition-of-extractive-qualifications","Art. 4 — Validation of competences and prohibition of extractive qualifications","","g) periodic review shall be carried out at least every two years for Level 3 and every four years for Level 2. For Level 1 no mandatory periodic review is provided for, except for verification before exercise where the STV or the operating conditions so require;\nh) the review may confirm the validation, confirm it with an obligation of updating, suspend it pending updating or declare its lapse, stating reasons;\ni) a positive review may maintain or reactivate a previously suspended validation, after verification of the necessary updating.\n6. It is prohibited to make validation subject to the payment of a fee. For Level 2 and 3 competences, remote validation without a practical test is not permitted. For medium- or high-risk activities, self-description alone is not sufficient.\n7. Refusal, suspension and lapse shall be reasoned and communicated to the person concerned, indicating the competences or operating conditions to be supplemented."],[10,"art-5--learning-pathways-and-learning-stvs","Art. 5 — Learning pathways and learning STVs","","1. The ordinary pathway for acquiring a competence is structured, in a manner proportionate to the nature of the task, in the following stages:\na) mentoring;\nb) supervised practice;\nc) autonomous practice, when authorised;\nd) capacity to teach or transmit the essential knowledge.\n2. For every critical competence a learning STV shall be prepared, coordinated with Code 2, which shall indicate at least:\na) the safety prerequisites and the PPE required;\nb) the stages of the pathway;\nc) the typical errors and the measures to prevent them;\nd) the verification criteria;\ne) the minimum mentoring time, where necessary;\nf) the equipment, conditions and supervision required.\n3. Progression to a subsequent level shall be decided by the Competences Council on a proposal from the persons who carried out the mentoring, in accordance with the applicable validation criteria.\n4. In the event of refusal, the Competences Council shall communicate written reasons, indicate the missing elements and propose a completion pathway within thirty days.\n5. Abandoning a pathway does not constitute a violation and does not give rise to prejudicial annotations in the Personal Portfolio.\n6. Failure to acquire a competence may not entail a reduction of the Sufficiency Basket or exclusion from the Assembly or from List A tasks that do not require that competence."],[10,"art-6--minors-and-learning","Art. 6 — Minors and learning","","1. Children’s learning takes place through participation in the life of the Circle, play, care, exploration and light and safe activities, in compliance with the rights of the minor and with Code 12.\n2. No mandatory CBO may be assigned to minors, pursuant to paragraph 5. Until the fourteenth year of age is completed, only voluntary participation in learning activities is permitted, provided that it is appropriate to the age, not dangerous and supervised.\n3. For minors until the fourteenth year is completed, the following are prohibited:\na) competitive assessments;\nb) numerical marks and league tables;\nc) merit rankings;\nd) standardised selective tests;\ne) failing or exclusions based on performance.\nAssessment is descriptive and considers what the minor is able to do, what they wish to learn, the supports they need and their interests, with their participation and that of the mentors.\n4. From the age of fourteen and until the eighteenth year is completed, the minor may progressively access List B and C activities under the following conditions:\na) for competences of nil or low risk they may carry out supervised practice, without this automatically entailing an autonomous validation;\nb) for medium-risk competences they may carry out supervised practice from the age of fourteen; autonomous exercise requires that they have reached at least the age of sixteen and the Level 2 validation;"],[10,"art-6--minors-and-learning","Art. 6 — Minors and learning","","c) for high-risk competences, for which a Level 3 validation is required, autonomous exercise is permitted only from the age of sixteen and after the required validation has been obtained;\nd) before the age of sixteen it is permitted to learn high-risk competences exclusively through mentoring or supervised practice, without autonomous exercise and in compliance with the learning STV and with Code 14.\n5. The CBO of minors is always voluntary and revocable. Participation requires the informed consent of the minor and the involvement of the Care Council, in compliance with Codes 12 and 14.\n6. From completion of the eighteenth year the ordinary rules shall apply, including the measures of exemption, reduction or suspension provided for by Code 1. For a person who has grown up in the Confederation, the ordinary rules apply from the choice provided for by Article 26.4 of the Constitution or from the expiry of the related time limit; until then their CBO remains voluntary pursuant to paragraph 5, without the consent of the Care Council, and they retain the rights of a member."],[10,"art-6--minors-and-learning","Art. 6 — Minors and learning","","6-bis. In implementation of Article 26.4 of the Constitution, at least three months before the eighteenth birthday the Competences Council, with the Care Council, informs the person who has grown up in the Confederation in writing of the three possibilities, of the time limit, of the rights of Article 4.8 of the Constitution and of the support for starting an autonomous life, and organises at their request the period of acquaintance with other Communities and with external realities. The choice is declared in writing to the Assembly of Circle 1 or recorded in the personal AI Node; the person may review it at any time. The time limit does not run until the information has been given.\n7. Every minor learner shall be accompanied by three reference figures on quarterly rotation, belonging to different Competence Circles and not belonging to the minor’s family or Circle 0, pursuant to Article 29.3 of the Constitution. The figures shall be identified by the Competences Council with the participation of the minor."],[10,"art-6--minors-and-learning","Art. 6 — Minors and learning","","8. By the eleventh year of age the literacy provided for by Article 29.4 of the Constitution shall be guaranteed: reading and writing, basic mathematics, use of local AIs, repair of a complex object, full-cycle cultivation of food, human and animal first aid and non-violent mediation. Validation shall take place by practical demonstration before persons designated by the Assembly of Circle 1, in descriptive form. If an attainment has not yet been reached, a support pathway shall be offered, without prejudicial consequences.\n9. From the age of twelve, free access to the courses and building sites of all the Circles through the Network shall be guaranteed, within the safety limits of paragraph 4. The portfolio of the activities carried out replaces the diploma.\n10. Until the fourteenth year of age is completed, the participation of the minor in the decisions that concern them shall be ensured by means proportionate to their age, defined by the Competences Council with the Care Council, pursuant to Article 26.2 of the Constitution."],[10,"art-7--competences-council","Art. 7 — Competences Council","","1. Every Circle 1 shall establish a Competences Council composed of three to seven members, with diversified competences validated at least at Level 2 or 3. It is the local branch of the Competence Circles for Childhood and Education and for Learning and Transmission of Knowledge.\n2. The members shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office.\n3. The Competences Council shall perform the following functions:\na) it shall maintain and update the Knowledge Storehouse;\nb) it shall organise mentoring and facilitate the matching of those who seek and those who offer teaching;\nc) it shall validate Level 3 competences and manage the related reviews;\nd) it shall guarantee non-discriminatory access to learning pathways;\ne) it shall report to the Storehouse Council and to the Assembly the training requirement useful for the planning of the CBO;\nf) it shall coordinate with the other Circles 1 the training relating to scarce competences.\n4. The Competences Council has no allocative power over goods, does not exercise disciplinary powers, may not exclude a person from the Circle and may not reduce their access to the Basket.\n5. The decisions of the Council shall be adopted in accordance with the consensus method provided for by Code 3 and shall be minuted. The criteria and the aggregate statistical data shall be published; names and Personal Portfolios shall remain confidential.\n6. The members shall declare conflicts of interest and shall abstain when they take part in a validation that directly concerns them or when there is another reason that could compromise their impartiality.\n7. An appeal pursuant to Article 9 may be brought against decisions of refusal, suspension or lapse."],[10,"art-8--prohibition-of-rent-from-competence","Art. 8 — Prohibition of rent from competence","","1. No competence may generate rent, a price premium, a differentiated wage, a monopoly of function, a right of exclusion or a political veto. The CdR recognises the contribution rendered beyond the CBO and its validated usefulness, not the competence possessed, in accordance with Article 8 of the Constitution and Chapter V of Code 1.\n2. Scarce and necessary competences may entail an organisational priority in the allocation of the relevant CBO, a greater recognised mentoring time and logistical support for the transmission of knowledge. They do not confer material or decision-making privileges.\n3. Those who possess a critical competence, in particular a Level 3 competence, shall contribute to its transmission and documentation according to the requirement and their own possibilities.\n4. Repeated failure to transmit without documented justification does not entail the automatic lapse of the validation. The Competences Council shall assess the case in a proportionate manner and may order, in ascending order:\na) a reasoned reminder;\nb) a plan for recovering the transmission;\nc) a temporary suspension of the validation, where the omission compromises safety or the continuity of the competence;\nd) lapse, only as a last resort, where the omission is serious and repeated and has caused or may cause concrete harm to collective safety.\n5. The following constitute possible justifications:\na) absence of persons interested in learning;\nb) temporary impediment for reasons of health or care;\nc) documented CBO overload;\nd) lack of safe conditions for teaching.\n6. Before adopting a measure pursuant to paragraph 4, the Council shall hear the person concerned and allow them to submit observations."],[10,"art-9--guarantees-appeal-and-systematic-links","Art. 9 — Guarantees, appeal and systematic links","","1. An appeal against refusal of validation, suspension or lapse may be brought before the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days of the communication of the decision.\n2. An appeal is admissible exclusively on grounds of:\na) procedural defect;\nb) manifest technical unreasonableness;\nc) discrimination;\nd) disproportion of the measure.\n3. The Guarantee Circle does not directly validate the competence and does not assign a level in place of the Competences Council. It shall verify compliance with the procedure, the statement of reasons, the soundness of the criteria applied and the proportionality of the measure.\n4. If the appeal is upheld, the Guarantee Circle shall refer the assessment back to the Competences Council for a fresh examination with different validators, identified in accordance with the guarantees of Code 4.\n5. If the appeal is brought exclusively by the person concerned, the review may not make the contested measure more severe.\n6. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, in matters of the Portfolio, care, training and competences required for safety;\nb) Code 2, for the STVs and the prohibition of critical proprietary dependency applied to knowledge;\nc) Code 3, for the deliberative procedures and facilitation competences;\nd) Code 4, for the Guarantee, appeals and external review;\ne) Code 7, for the Network and the limits of the Management AI;\nf) Codes 12 and 14, for the protection of minors and safety.\n7. The Personal Portfolio is established and governed by this Code. Code 1 may use its information, limited to what is necessary for the safe allocation of the CBO and without access to non-pertinent personal data."],[10,"art-9-bis--access-to-pathways-and-revision-after-three-refusals","Art. 9-bis — Access to pathways and revision after three refusals","","1. No Council and no Competence Circle may refuse access to a learning pathway on account of the lack of an educational qualification or of aptitude. Refusal is permitted only for documented reasons of safety or of intake capacity and shall be communicated in writing, with the indication of an alternative pathway.\n2. After three refusals of access to the same competence pathway, a revision shall be initiated ex officio, pursuant to Article 6.4 of the Constitution. The revision shall be entrusted to two uninvolved Communities drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4, each of which shall designate a person with relevant competence.\n3. The revision shall be concluded within thirty days. It may order admission with a mentored pathway or confirm the refusal, stating reasons and indicating the conditions for a new request.\n4. The revision may not produce prejudicial consequences for the person who requested access."],[10,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-continuity-of-safety","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and continuity of safety","","1. Qualifications, diplomas and certificates obtained before the entry into force of this Code shall be recorded, at the request of the person concerned, as documentation of a previous pathway. They do not automatically entail the validation of a competence.\n2. In order to guarantee the continuity of services, those who are already exercising a high-risk critical function on the date of entry into force may receive a provisional validation:\na) for a maximum of six months for Level 3 competences;\nb) for a maximum of twelve months for Level 2 competences.\n3. Provisional validation is subject to:\na) enrolment in the validation pathway within thirty days;\nb) practice supervised by persons already validated, where available;\nc) compliance with the STVs in force;\nd) tracking of the activities and of the supervision measures.\n4. During the provisional period, the person may continue to perform the function within the limits defined by the continuity plan, but may not validate the competences of other persons.\n5. Once the time limit has expired without the validation pathway having been started, the suspension provided for by Article 4, paragraph 5, shall apply. The suspension may not interrupt an essential service without an alternative: the technical necessity clause of Article 32 of Code 1 shall apply, in compliance with Codes 6 and 14.\n6. For critical competences exercised for more than five years, the Competences Council may shorten the Level 3 validation pathway to the practical test, the verification of the STVs and a short period of mentoring, where safe and continuous practice is documented.\n7. The documentation referred to in paragraph 6 may include registers of interventions, traceability, continuing training and positive peer assessments in accordance with the STVs. The mere absence of incidents does not constitute sufficient proof of competence.\n8. Within twenty-four months of entry into force, every Circle 1 shall make a minimum Knowledge Storehouse operational and shall prepare the aggregate map of training requirements provided for by Article 3."],[11,"art-1--subject-matter-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter and legal nature","","1. This Code governs the protection of health as an infrastructural commons, an inviolable right and a material condition of existence of the Confederation, in accordance with Article 1.2 of the Constitution.\n2. Health is not a commodity, a paid service or a privilege linked to the possession of competences or to the amount of CBO performed.\n3. For the purposes of this Code, health means the physical, mental, relational and environmental well-being of the person. Its assessment respects individual self-determination and takes into account the collective conditions that affect health.\n4. This Code governs:\na) the prevention of risks connected with the CBO, the Storehouses and habitats;\nb) health surveillance and coordination with the safety of tasks;\nc) primary care, the care of frail persons and mental health;\nd) the management of accidents and occupational diseases;\ne) the Health Basket and pharmaceutical autonomy;\nf) the confidentiality of health data.\n5. The competences under this Code are coordinated with those under Codes 12 and 14. The Health Council does not replace the bodies competent for general safety, the care of frail persons or data protection."],[11,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Prevention. CBO, production, logistics and learning activities are organised so as to prevent harm to health before it occurs.\n2. Self-determination. Every person decides on their own body and on their own course of care on the basis of free, informed and revocable consent. Article 2-ter applies to minors and to those who are unable to decide. The collective measures of Article 2-bis may limit tasks, activities and movements within the limits laid down therein, but never include an imposed health treatment. Article 8 remains applicable to a person temporarily incapable in acute crisis.\n3. No blame. Illness, accident and frailty do not constitute fault and may not, in themselves, entail a reduction of the Sufficiency Basket, exclusion or sanction. The guarantees of Code 4 apply.\n4. Proximity. Care is organised, as far as possible, in the person’s Circle, Storehouse and habitat. Recourse to hospital or centralised services is ordered when it is necessary in order to access Level 3 competences not available locally.\n5. Non-commodification of knowledge. It is prohibited to patent life-saving knowledge and essential health knowledge or to place them behind a paywall. Rent deriving from the monopoly of health competences is prohibited, in accordance with Code 5, Article 8.\n6. Proportionality and confidentiality. Every health measure is limited to the purpose for which it is adopted. Health information is processed only to the extent necessary and with the guarantees of Article 11."],[11,"art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","Art. 2-bis — Immediate collective risk and coordination with Article 8","","1. Immediate and documented collective risk means a situation in which a pathogen, a contaminant or an environmental condition, on the basis of verifiable scientific evidence recorded in the Health Knowledge Storehouse, entails a concrete, present and serious danger of spread or of harm to third parties that cannot be avoided by less invasive measures.\n2. The assessment is initiated on a proposal from the Health Council and requires:\na) a dossier describing the evidence, the risk, the persons or groups potentially exposed and the alternatives considered;\nb) the opinion of at least two persons with Level 3 competence, who are independent and do not belong to the proposing Circle;\nc) validation of the applicable STV, where provided for.\n3. Collective measures are resolved upon by the General Assembly in accordance with the urgency procedure of Code 3. The resolution shall state its reasons and document necessity, proportionality, duration, less invasive alternatives and criteria for cessation. Coordination with Code 14 applies where matters of general safety or of interruption of activities are involved.\n3-bis. The collective measures may comprise exclusively:\na) public information, recommendations and the free offer of tests, vaccinations, prophylaxis and PPE, which remain voluntary;"],[11,"art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","Art. 2-bis — Immediate collective risk and coordination with Article 8","","b) the protection indicated by the resolution, from among vaccination, prophylaxis, tests and PPE, as a condition for performing the tasks and for attending the places at risk, on the conditions of paragraph 3-ter;\nc) the temporary suspension or reorganisation of collective activities, meetings, CBO shifts, non-essential mobility and non-urgent hospitality;\nd) the isolation of contagious persons and the quarantine of exposed persons, on the conditions of paragraph 3-quater;\ne) measures concerning contaminated environments, water, food and goods and, for zoonoses, the measures concerning animals permitted by Code 13.\nOnly the tasks and places in which contagion may affect particularly exposed persons are at risk: wards and clinics, the care of frail persons pursuant to Code 12 and, where the risk concerns childhood, spaces for childhood and places of collective learning. No one may be excluded from the care they need or from assisting a hospitalised person for whose care they are responsible; in these cases alternative precautions are adopted.\n3-ter. No measure includes a health treatment, a sample, a test or a vaccination performed on the body of a person without the consent of that person or, for those who cannot decide for themselves, of the person who decides pursuant to Article 2-ter. Whoever refuses the protection indicated pursuant to paragraph 3-bis, letter b):\na) may be excluded, for the duration of the measure, only from the tasks and places at risk;"],[11,"art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","Art. 2-bis — Immediate collective risk and coordination with Article 8","","b) shall receive without delay another compatible task, counted in the CBO; if none is available, the temporary suspension of Article 11, paragraph 1, letter c), of Code 1 applies. A minor excluded from a collective space shall be ensured care and learning in another form;\nc) retains the Basket, housing, care, the vote, deliberative participation and the other rights, including during the measure;\nd) does not commit an offence; the refusal is not recorded or communicated beyond what is necessary to apply letter a).\n3-quater. Isolation and quarantine:\na) take place in the person’s dwelling or, if the person prefers or the dwelling does not allow it, in a suitable space made available by Circle 1;"],[11,"art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","Art. 2-bis — Immediate collective risk and coordination with Article 8","","b) last as long as the dossier of paragraph 2 indicates for the agent in question and in any case no more than fourteen days per person and per exposure; one extension, for no more than a further fourteen days, is permitted only if two persons with L3 competence certify that the person is still contagious or, for quarantine, that the incubation period indicated by the dossier has not ended. Beyond twenty-eight days the isolation of a person who is still contagious and does not consent to it may be ordered only by the Guarantee Tribunal, after hearing the person with the guarantees of Article 25, paragraph 4, of Code 4, on the certification of two independent L3 persons pursuant to paragraph 2, letter b), for periods not exceeding fourteen days and not beyond the duration of the collective measure; failing that, only paragraph 3-ter applies;\nc) if the person does not consent, are ordered individually by the Health Council by reasoned act, communicated without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal, which shall validate, modify or revoke them within twenty-four hours pursuant to Article 34, paragraph 2, of Code 4; in the absence of validation they cease. The same validation applies to the extension. Paragraph 3 of that Article does not apply;"],[11,"art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","Art. 2-bis — Immediate collective risk and coordination with Article 8","","d) ensure for the person the delivery of the Basket, care, remote contact with loved ones, voting through the Network and the temporary suspension of the CBO provided for by Article 11, paragraph 1, letter c), of Code 1, except for the tasks that the person asks to perform remotely;\ne) are not enforced by force, except for the intervention of the Civic Protection Corps within the limits of Article 8-ter of Code 14; the validation of letter c) also applies for the purposes of paragraph 4 of that Article;\nf) during the transition, if the person does not consent, are ordered only by the health authorities of the State, pursuant to Article 37 of Code 4.\n3-quinquies. For the entire duration of the measure the proposing Health Council shall publish, at least every week, the dossier of paragraph 2, the aggregated and anonymous data on which the measure is based, their trend and the reasons for each renewal or cessation, in compliance with Article 11.\n4. The measure:\na) lasts no more than thirty days;\nb) may be renewed only by a new resolution and with new documentation;\nc) is limited to what is strictly necessary and adopts the least invasive suitable measure;\nd) is reviewed, at the request of any member, within seven days, by the members of the Guarantee Circles drawn by lot pursuant to Article 17, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 3; individually ordered isolation and quarantine instead follow the validation of paragraph 3-quater, letter c);"],[11,"art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","Art. 2-bis — Immediate collective risk and coordination with Article 8","","e) is recorded with its reasons, duration and criteria for cessation, in compliance with confidentiality and with the access levels provided for by the Codes.\n5. When the conditions for it cease to exist, the measure ceases immediately. Renewal may not be based on mere inertia or on the fact that a review has not been concluded.\n6. This Article governs public health measures addressed to the community as a whole. Article 8 governs individual health emergency interventions concerning a person in crisis. The two regimes have distinct subject matter, conditions and procedures and are not cumulative, unless both sets of conditions are documented.\n7. Measures adopted pursuant to this Article may not reduce, not even while they apply, the Basket, housing, care, the vote and deliberative participation, nor produce permanent effects on access to the CBO or to the other rights. Any further effects require a separate procedure provided for by the competent Codes, together with the related guarantees.\n8. When the measure ceases, the person’s previous legal position is restored. The records necessary to document, verify or audit the measure may be retained, with restricted access and in accordance with the confidentiality guarantees of Article 11."],[11,"art-2-ter--consent-of-minors-and-of-persons-who-cannot-decide","Art. 2-ter — Consent of minors and of persons who cannot decide","","1. From completion of the sixteenth year the person gives, refuses and revokes consent to their own care themselves, pursuant to Article 2, paragraph 2. If, before the eighteenth year, the person is unable to decide, paragraph 2 applies.\n2. Until completion of the sixteenth year consent is given by those who hold responsibility for the care of the minor pursuant to Article 24.3 of the Constitution, after informing and hearing the minor in a manner suited to his or her age. The opinion of the minor carries increasing weight with age and maturity, pursuant to Article 26.2 of the Constitution. If there is more than one responsible person, they decide by agreement; for urgent care the consent of one of them suffices. Responsibility for care and the consent of this paragraph do not cease by reason only of the cessation of the member status of the responsible person pursuant to Article 29, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 4. If no one exercises responsibility for care, or no responsible person can be reached in good time, consent is given by the temporary representative appointed pursuant to Article 7-bis, paragraph 5, of Code 12, until the decision of the Guarantee Tribunal provided for by paragraph 3-bis of that Article."],[11,"art-2-ter--consent-of-minors-and-of-persons-who-cannot-decide","Art. 2-ter — Consent of minors and of persons who cannot decide","","3. In the event of disagreement between the minor and those who hold responsibility for his or her care, or between several responsible persons, or of a conflict of interest between them and the minor, the minor, each responsible person, the treating practitioner or the Care Council may apply to the Guarantee Tribunal. The Tribunal obtains the written opinion of a person with L3 health competence, independent and not involved in the care, drawn by lot from the list of Article 36, paragraph 1, letter b), of Code 4; it hears the minor, assisted by the trusted person provided for by Article 31.5 of the Constitution, and the responsible persons; it decides within seven days or, if the care cannot wait, within twenty-four hours, according to the interest of the minor and taking account of his or her opinion pursuant to paragraph 2. Where waiting would expose the minor to a danger to life or to serious and irreversible harm, the necessary care is provided immediately, pursuant to Article 28.2 of the Constitution, and the Tribunal is informed.\n4. Vaccinations and prophylaxis of minors follow paragraphs 1 to 3 and are never mandatory. Refusal of a vaccination does not, by itself, constitute serious neglect or harm within the meaning of Article 7-bis of Code 12. During a measure under Article 2-bis a minor who does not have the protection indicated may be excluded only from the places at risk, on the conditions of paragraph 3-ter of that Article."],[11,"art-2-ter--consent-of-minors-and-of-persons-who-cannot-decide","Art. 2-ter — Consent of minors and of persons who cannot decide","","5. Where an adult person is unable to decide on their own care because of a condition that lasts, or is expected to last, more than sixty days, as established by two independent persons with L3 health competence, the Guarantee Tribunal shall appoint a stable representative, if the person has not designated one pursuant to Article 7-bis, paragraph 4, of Code 12. The representative is chosen in the order of Article 7-bis, paragraph 5, of Code 12 and decides in accordance with paragraph 6 of that Article. The person is informed and heard to the extent that they can express themselves. The appointment is reviewed by the Tribunal at least every twelve months and at any time at the request of the person, the trusted person, the representative or the Care Council; it ceases when the person recovers capacity. Paragraph 3 applies to disagreement or conflict of interest between the representative and the person. The representative may not consent to coercive measures, which are governed solely by Article 8.\n6. During the transition this Article applies within the limits permitted by the law in force on the consent of minors and on the representation of incapable persons."],[11,"art-3--health-council","Art. 3 — Health Council","","1. Every Circle 1 shall establish a Health Council composed of three to seven members, with Level 2 or 3 competences in the fields of health, care, agroecology or occupational safety. It is the local branch of the Medicine and Health Competence Circle. Its members are drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have passed since the end of their term of office.\n2. The Health Council:\na) manages the Health Storehouse and the Health Basket referred to in Article 10;\nb) draws up and updates, as regards the health aspects within its competence, the Safety Sheets for CBO tasks;\nc) organises health surveillance, the management of PPE within the health remit and L1 training on health;\nd) records and analyses accidents, near misses and occupational diseases;\ne) coordinates the care of frail persons and mental health interventions in accordance with Code 12;\nf) coordinates with the other Circles 1 the management of scarce competences and of vital emergencies, in accordance with Article 32 of Code 1 and with Code 14.\n3. The Health Council has no allocative powers over goods and no disciplinary powers. It may not deny access to the Basket or make it subject to conditions.\n4. The Council formulates proposals, documents health risks, organises assistance and performs the functions assigned to it by this Code. Its decisions are adopted by the consensus method provided for by Code 3 and are tracked.\n5. For every CBO task a safety reference person with L2 competence, validated in accordance with Code 5, shall be identified. The reference person does not replace the Health Council or the Safety Council provided for by Code 14."],[11,"art-4--safety-sheets-ppe-and-coordination-with-code-2","Art. 4 — Safety Sheets, PPE and coordination with Code 2","","1. No CBO activity may be carried out without a Safety Sheet in force, prepared in accordance with the applicable STVs and coordinated with Codes 1 and 14.\n2. Where a task has both health aspects and general safety aspects, the Sheet may be a single integrated document or may consist of linked documents bearing the same identifier and the same version. The Health Council validates the health aspects; the Safety Council validates the general safety aspects; the Storehouse Council validates the organisational aspects within its competence. No Council may modify the parameters assigned to the others.\n3. The Sheet shall contain at least:\na) the specific risks;\nb) the PPE and collective protections required;\nc) the operating procedures;\nd) the typical errors and the measures to prevent them;\ne) the conditions for suspension;\nf) the references to the applicable STVs;\ng) the exposure limits and the breaks required;\nh) the minimum training required;\ni) the date, the version, the persons responsible and the conditions for revision.\n4. PPE items are commons and are provided free of charge. They are accessible, kept in working order and tracked by those competent for custody and procurement. The absence of a critical item of PPE required by the Sheet entails the immediate suspension of the activity concerned and does not constitute a violation attributable to the person to whom the PPE was not provided. The obligation to make PPE available lies with the Circle responsible for the activity.\n5. Every member may stop an activity when they detect a serious and immediate risk to themselves or to others. The stoppage is communicated as soon as possible to the operational lead and to the competent Council; it may not give rise to retaliation if carried out in good faith. Code 14 applies and, for logistical continuity, Article 32, paragraph 3, of Code 1.\n6. The STV of a good includes the health safety aspects relating to production, use, maintenance and disposal. The STV is coordinated with the Safety Sheet and does not replace it where the latter is required for a specific task."],[11,"art-5--health-surveillance-voluntary-nature-and-safety-conditions","Art. 5 — Health surveillance, voluntary nature and safety conditions","","1. Health surveillance is voluntary as a general course of care and prevention. For the tasks identified pursuant to paragraph 2, a fitness assessment may constitute a technical requirement for performing that specific task autonomously; it does not constitute a general health obligation.\n2. The following rules apply:\na) for CBO activities with no risk or low risk, no mandatory examination is required;\nb) for medium-risk or high-risk activities, classified in accordance with the STVs and the Safety Sheet, the fitness assessment is required only when it is necessary for the safety of the autonomous task;\nc) the fitness assessment is not a condition for accessing the Basket, taking part in deliberations or belonging to the Community;\nd) a person who refuses the examination necessary for a medium-risk or high-risk task shall not perform that specific task autonomously, but retains the Sufficiency Basket, access to the other compatible CBO activities and the right to an adapted alternative task. The refusal does not constitute an infringement and is not recorded as a prejudicial annotation;\ne) the periodic examination provided for a high-risk L3 health competence may be a condition for maintaining the related validation, in accordance with Article 4, paragraph 5, of Code 5. Refusal entails, if necessary, the suspension of the specific validation, not a sanction.\n3. Health assessments may include:"],[11,"art-5--health-surveillance-voluntary-nature-and-safety-conditions","Art. 5 — Health surveillance, voluntary nature and safety conditions","","a) an initial examination for medium-risk or high-risk tasks, when necessary pursuant to paragraph 2;\nb) a periodic examination for L3 competences, at least every two years, or every year in the case of continuous exposure, in accordance with the applicable STVs;\nc) an examination requested by the person at any time;\nd) an examination following an accident or an occupational disease.\n4. The finding is descriptive and limited to the task concerned. It may indicate:\na) fitness;\nb) fitness with adaptations;\nc) fitness with temporary limitations;\nd) temporary precautionary suspension from the task, with the Basket maintained and an alternative CBO proposed.\n5. Definitive unfitness for a specific task may be declared exclusively by an external Review Panel in accordance with the guarantees of Code 4, Article 17. The Panel is composed of:\na) one person with L3 health competence, drawn by lot and not belonging to the Circle of the person concerned;\nb) one person from the Quality Council not involved in the proceedings;\nc) one person drawn by lot from among the members of the Assembly of a Circle 1 other than the one concerned, with the function of procedural guarantee.\n6. Before the decision, the Panel shall obtain the written opinion of two independent persons with L3 health competence, not involved in the previous findings and, where relevant, with different specialisations. The persons giving the opinion are not members of the Panel and do not take part in the decision."],[11,"art-5--health-surveillance-voluntary-nature-and-safety-conditions","Art. 5 — Health surveillance, voluntary nature and safety conditions","","7. No member of the Panel may have given a previous fitness finding on the same person and task. The opinions are made available to the person concerned before the decision, except for the limitations strictly necessary to protect the health data of third parties.\n8. The decision shall state its reasons and be tracked. An appeal against definitive unfitness may be lodged within fourteen days with a new external Panel, composed of different persons. The appeal is admissible on grounds of:\na) procedural defect;\nb) manifest technical unreasonableness;\nc) discrimination;\nd) manifest disproportion.\nCode 4, Article 17, and Code 5, Article 9, apply. The second-instance Panel may not worsen the position of the appellant.\n9. During the procedure for establishing definitive unfitness, the person retains the Sufficiency Basket and receives a proposal for a compatible alternative CBO.\n10. No health data may be used to limit access to deliberations, to the Basket or to learning."],[11,"art-6--primary-care-and-health-storehouse","Art. 6 — Primary care and Health Storehouse","","1. Every Storehouse has a Health Storehouse and a minimum Health Basket. The list of the Health Basket and the minimum stock of the Health Storehouse are proposed by the Health Councils in federated coordination, as branches of the Medicine and Health Competence Circle, by means of an STV adopted pursuant to Article 3-bis of Code 2, and are approved by the Assembly with the Technical Annex provided for by Article 18, paragraph 2, of Code 1. The Basket includes at least:\na) essential life-saving medicines;\nb) first-aid supplies;\nc) PPE within the health remit;\nd) basic diagnostic instruments;\ne) validated health documentation and STVs.\n2. Primary care is organised through mentoring and Circle clinics, with the involvement of L2 and L3 competences. The person chooses among the competences available, including those belonging to other Circles.\n3. The AI provided for by Code 7 may support the indexing of protocols, the search for information and reminders. It may not autonomously formulate diagnoses, determine fitness or decide on treatment.\n4. When a competence is not available locally, the Health Council activates the network among Circles and the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15. The possible measures include:\na) sending the person to the competent service;\nb) temporarily sending the competence to the Circle concerned;\nc) remote mentoring supervised by an L3 person, when appropriate and safe.\n5. The arrangement chosen shall respect the person’s wishes, health confidentiality and continuity of care.\n6. The protection of health is structured in the three integrated levels of Article 28.1 of the Constitution: the offline personal AI with a preventive function, in accordance with Article 5-bis of Code 7; the care practitioners of the Base Community; the centres of high specialisation of Circle 3."],[11,"art-7--frailty-interdependence-and-care","Art. 7 — Frailty, interdependence and care","","1. Older persons, children, persons with disabilities, persons with chronic illnesses, persons who are pregnant or in the period following childbirth, and persons in mourning or in a situation of crisis are entitled to enhanced protection and, when necessary, to the modulation of the CBO pursuant to Article 11 of Code 1 and to Code 12.\n2. The care of frail persons falls within the List A CBO. In the hierarchy of Article 32 of Code 1 the services of the Care Basket of levels 1 and 2 follow letter b) of paragraph 1 and Code 12; the other care activities follow letter d) and precede the management of perishable goods, pursuant to paragraph 1-ter of that Article.\n3. Care activities may not be deferred on account of logistical shortage or of the management of perishable goods, unless the priorities of Article 32, paragraph 1, letters a) and b), of Code 1 so require, including the distribution of Basket food. The decision to defer shall state its reasons and must provide, where possible, for a substitute measure.\n4. Every CBO Plan includes a share of care and mentoring. Care is not regarded as a residual activity to be carried out only after the other requirements have been met.\n5. The modulation of the CBO does not entail a reduction of the Sufficiency Basket or prejudicial annotations."],[11,"art-8--mental-health-and-protocol-for-acute-crisis","Art. 8 — Mental health and protocol for acute crisis","","1. Mental health is an integral part of health. Distress, burnout, conflict and trauma do not constitute fault or individual weakness.\n2. The Health Council guarantees, directly or through coordination with Code 12:\na) listening spaces;\nb) talking groups;\nc) L2 or L3 psychological support;\nd) temporary reduction of the CBO load, when necessary;\ne) peer support;\nf) access to specialist competences without an unjustified internal waiting list.\n3. In the event of an acute crisis, a non-coercive protection protocol applies as a rule, based on:\na) presence and listening;\nb) relational and environmental making safe;\nc) an offer of support;\nd) the involvement, if the person so wishes, of a trusted person designated by them.\n4. An immediate coercive measure is permitted only if the following conditions are all met cumulatively:\na) there is an immediate and concrete vital risk to the person or to third parties, documented at the time by two persons with L3 competence, at least one of them in health;\nb) the person is temporarily incapable of giving valid consent;\nc) there is, in the immediate term, no less invasive measure that is equally effective.\n5. Where the two L3 competences are not immediately available, the procedures for federated sourcing or remote mentoring provided for by Article 13 of Code 15 are activated without delay. In the meantime, only non-coercive measures necessary for immediate making safe are permitted.\n6. Any coercive intervention that is adopted:"],[11,"art-8--mental-health-and-protocol-for-acute-crisis","Art. 8 — Mental health and protocol for acute crisis","","a) is limited to the time strictly necessary to avert the vital risk;\nb) is carried out in the presence of two L3 persons, when immediately available;\nc) is communicated without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal for the validation of paragraph 7 and to the Guarantee Circle;\nd) is documented on the Network with regulated access;\ne) is communicated to the designated trusted person, when possible;\nf) ceases immediately when the conditions for it cease to exist.\n7. Within twenty-four hours of the start of the measure the Guarantee Tribunal, pursuant to Article 27, paragraph 3, of Code 4, shall validate, modify or revoke it. It verifies the conditions of paragraph 4, necessity and proportionality on the basis of the documentation of the two L3 persons and, where possible, after hearing the person, their representative and the trusted person. It does not carry out a clinical assessment, which remains the responsibility of the L3 health persons. In the absence of validation within the time limit the measure ceases. If the Civic Protection Corps intervenes, the validation of this paragraph takes the place of that provided for by Article 8-ter, paragraph 4, of Code 14.\n8. After the crisis, a voluntary course of care is offered to the person.\n9. Punitive restraint, isolation used as a sanction and punitive sedation are always prohibited.\n10. The coercive measure:"],[11,"art-8--mental-health-and-protocol-for-acute-crisis","Art. 8 — Mental health and protocol for acute crisis","","a) is reassessed by the two L3 persons at least every twelve hours and, during physical restraint, at least every hour; every reassessment is documented;\nb) after validation lasts no more than seventy-two hours and may be extended only by a new validation of the Guarantee Tribunal, requested before expiry on the basis of a new certification by two L3 persons, for periods not exceeding seventy-two hours; in the absence of a new validation before expiry the measure ceases;\nc) may not exceed fourteen days in total. On expiry the measure ceases even if the risk persists; the continuous presence of trained persons, the relational and environmental making safe of paragraph 3 and the offer of care are ensured without interruption, and the person may remain voluntarily in the place of care. A new coercive measure is permitted only for a new acute episode, on the conditions of paragraph 4 certified by two L3 persons other than those who certified the previous measure, and with the validation of a Guarantee Tribunal in a different composition, which expressly verifies that it is not the continuation of the measure that has ceased. Within a period of ninety days coercive measures may not exceed twenty-eight days in total;\nd) is revoked at any time by the Guarantee Tribunal, when the conditions for it cease to exist, at the request of the person, their representative or the trusted person; the request is decided within twenty-four hours."],[11,"art-8--mental-health-and-protocol-for-acute-crisis","Art. 8 — Mental health and protocol for acute crisis","","The representative may not consent to a coercive measure in place of the person: only this Article applies. During the transition no internal coercive measures are carried out; every intervention is limited to what is permitted by the law in force and the emergency services and the competent health authorities are alerted, pursuant to Article 37 of Code 4.\n11. It is prohibited to insert stigmatising labels in the Personal Portfolio."],[11,"art-8-bis--living-will-and-end-of-life","Art. 8-bis — Living will and end of life","","1. The living will is binding, pursuant to Article 27.3 of the Constitution. It is drawn up by the person, with the assistance, if any, of their own offline personal AI and in the presence of two witnesses; it is kept in the personal Node and, if the person so wishes, with the Health Council in confidential form. It may be revoked and amended at any time.\n2. By the living will the person may designate their representative in the event of incapacity, pursuant to Article 7-bis of Code 12.\n3. No one may be subjected against their will to therapeutic obstinacy. Palliative care and pain relief form part of the Health Basket.\n4. The right to remain at home is guaranteed, with the community support in shifts provided for by Code 12. Self-managed Common Houses are a choice of the person and may not constitute a compulsory placement."],[11,"art-9--accidents-near-misses-and-occupational-diseases","Art. 9 — Accidents, near misses and occupational diseases","","1. Every accident, near miss or suspected occupational disease is reported and recorded without delay. The operational register contains only the information necessary for the analysis of the event, including:\na) a description of what happened;\nb) the task being performed;\nc) the applicable Safety Sheet;\nd) the PPE and protections available;\ne) the relevant operating conditions;\nf) the date and time of the event.\n2. Identifying data and health data are kept separately and with restricted access in accordance with Article 7 of Code 14 and Article 11 of this Code. The information published is aggregated and anonymised.\n3. The analysis distinguishes, without automatically presuming individual responsibility:\na) wear and tear or fortuitous event, which entail the revision of the STVs and of the organisation, without automatic individual responsibility;\nb) organisational shortcomings, which require the revision of the measures by the Circle responsible and by the Health Council;\nc) intentional conduct or other relevant individual conduct, which is dealt with in accordance with Code 4.\n4. The injured person is entitled to care, to the maintenance of the Basket and, during convalescence, to the proposal of an adapted alternative CBO, when compatible with their condition.\n5. The Health Councils, in federated coordination as branches of the Medicine and Health Competence Circle, carry out, at least quarterly, an aggregate analysis of accidents and near misses in order to propose the updating of the STVs of Code 2 and of the Safety Sheets.\n6. The prevention cycle comprises: recording of the event, analysis of the causes, updating of the measures, revision of the STVs and prevention of future events."],[11,"art-10--health-basket-medicines-phytotherapy-and-experimentation","Art. 10 — Health Basket, medicines, phytotherapy and experimentation","","1. The Health Basket forms part of the Sufficiency Basket provided for by Code 1. It includes life-saving goods whose unavailability may give rise to a vital emergency of level 1.\n2. The Confederation pursues autonomy in the production or federated procurement of non-patented essential medicines and reusable devices, in accordance with the STVs of Code 2. Critical dependencies on external suppliers are recorded in the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15.\n3. Validated phytotherapy means a plant preparation accompanied by an STV in force that indicates at least:\na) the species and the part used;\nb) the dosage;\nc) the indications and contraindications;\nd) the known interactions;\ne) the available evidence and its limits;\nf) the methods of preparation and storage.\nTraditional use, on its own, does not constitute validation. Every validated preparation is recorded in the Health STV Register.\n4. Critical proprietary dependency on which life-saving medicines, essential diagnostic devices or PPE depend is prohibited. Articles 4 and 5 of Code 2 and the provisions of Code 15 apply.\n5. No health experimentation may be carried out on members without free, informed, specific and revocable consent. Consent is documented in writing after comprehensible information has been provided on risks, benefits, alternatives and the right of withdrawal.\n6. Experimentation requires in advance:"],[11,"art-10--health-basket-medicines-phytotherapy-and-experimentation","Art. 10 — Health Basket, medicines, phytotherapy and experimentation","","a) the approval of an external Panel drawn by lot in accordance with Code 4, Article 17, composed of three persons with L3 health competence not involved in the experimentation;\nb) the opinion of the Guarantee Circle on consent and proportionality;\nc) the resolution of the General Assembly in accordance with Code 3.\n7. Experimentation on minors, on persons in acute crisis within the meaning of Article 8 or on persons temporarily lacking the capacity to give consent is prohibited, except in the exceptional case in which it is aimed at achieving a direct and specific benefit for the person themselves. In that case all of the following conditions must be met cumulatively:\na) consent given in accordance with Article 2-ter: for a minor, by the minor if he or she has reached the age of sixteen, otherwise by those who hold responsibility for his or her care; for an incapable person, by the representative designated or appointed pursuant to Article 7-bis of Code 12 or Article 2-ter, paragraph 5. The trusted person is not regarded as a representative, unless they have been formally designated for that function;\nb) if the representative is not available, application of the urgent procedure provided for by Article 7-bis of Code 12 for a temporarily incapable person, including the urgent appointment of a representative by the competent body;"],[11,"art-10--health-basket-medicines-phytotherapy-and-experimentation","Art. 10 — Health Basket, medicines, phytotherapy and experimentation","","c) a written concurring opinion of two independent and uninvolved L3 persons, attesting to the direct benefit, the absence of alternatives of comparable efficacy and the proportionality of the risk;\nd) authorisation of the Guarantee Circle as regards consent and proportionality;\ne) a resolution of the General Assembly in accordance with Code 3, by qualified majority and with tracking of the positions expressed;\nf) hearing and respecting the will of the person to the extent that they are able to express it.\nThe absence of even one of the conditions prevents the experimentation from being started.\n8. Every experimentation must provide for tracking, coverage of collective responsibilities, access to care for any consequences and the right of immediate withdrawal without prejudice.\n9. Violation of paragraphs 5 to 8 constitutes a serious procedural defect and entails the application of Code 4, including the effects of invalidity and the obligations of reparation."],[11,"art-11--health-confidentiality-and-separation-of-data","Art. 11 — Health confidentiality and separation of data","","1. The person to whom the health data relate retains control over them. The Circle’s fiduciary System performs functions of custody and technical management, without being able to use the data for its own purposes or for incompatible purposes.\n2. Access to health data is regulated as follows:\na) ordinary access belongs to the person concerned and to the trusted person designated by them, within the limits of the consent;\nb) the Health Council may access the information necessary for health surveillance, confining itself, as a rule, to fitness findings and adaptations and not to detailed diagnoses;\nc) the Guarantee Circle may access the data necessary to decide an appeal, in accordance with a tracked procedure and with restricted access;\nd) in the absence of consent, access by the Health Council is permitted only when it is necessary to give effect to a collective measure validly resolved upon pursuant to Article 2-bis. Access is limited to the strictly necessary data, proportionate and temporary, is tracked and, when possible, communicated to the person concerned. Review by the Guarantee Circle within seven days is permitted.\n3. The Knowledge Storehouse contains exclusively:\na) Safety Sheets free of individual health data;\nb) anonymised health STVs;\nc) aggregate statistics on accidents;\nd) validated care protocols.\nIt does not contain individual health data.\n4. The publication of health data identifying persons by name, of lists of conditions attributable to persons or of individual maps of frailty is prohibited. Violation constitutes a serious procedural defect in accordance with Code 4.\n5. Every person has the right:\na) to obtain a copy of their own health data;\nb) to request their rectification;\nc) to request their erasure when they are no longer relevant or necessary;\nd) to request the erasure or forgetting of past episodes, without prejudice to the collective measures documented and validly resolved upon pursuant to Article 2-bis, limited to the strictly necessary information.\n6. The erasure or rectification of personal data does not entail the erasure of information that has already been rendered irreversibly anonymous and used for statistics, epidemiological analyses or the updating of the STVs. Personal data are kept distinct from anonymous statistical data and it must not be possible to reconstruct them."],[11,"art-12--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 12 — Transitional provisions and links","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Articles 27, 29, 31 and 32;\nb) Code 2, for the STVs and the validation of goods;\nc) Code 3, for the urgency procedures and resolutions;\nd) Code 4, Article 17, for the Review Panel and the guarantees;\ne) Code 5, for L2 and L3 competences, Article 4, paragraph 2, letter c), Article 4, paragraph 5, and Article 10;\nf) Code 12, for the care of frail persons;\ng) Code 14, for general safety;\nh) Code 15, for the Dependency Register.\n2. The provisional health validation provided for by Article 10 of Code 5 applies. A person who exercises a high-risk health function on the date of entry into force may maintain a provisional validation:\na) for six months for the functions corresponding to Level 3 of Code 5;\nb) for twelve months for the functions corresponding to Level 2 of Code 5.\nEnrolment in the validation pathway within thirty days, enhanced supervision, compliance with the STVs and traceability of activities are required. During the provisional period the person may not validate the competences of others.\n3. Within twelve months of entry into force, every Circle 1 shall activate a minimum Health Storehouse and prepare the Safety Sheets for the health and health-safety tasks classified L3 and active, in accordance with Code 5, Article 4, paragraph 2, letter c).\n4. For non-health L3 tasks, Code 5 and Code 14 apply; this Code does not govern their technical validation.\n5. Within twenty-four months of entry into force, a federated audit on pharmaceutical autonomy and on PPE shall be carried out, with the recording of critical dependencies in the Dependency Register of Code 15."],[12,"art-1--subject-matter-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter and legal nature","","1. This Code governs automation, Management Artificial Intelligence and the federated Network as inalienable common infrastructures, pursuant to Article 3.1 and Article 1.2, letter c), of the Constitution. It also governs the computing Nodes and the personal AIs provided for by Articles 19 and 21 of the Constitution.\n2. For the purposes of this Code:\na) automation: means a technical system that performs repetitive, logistical, computing or control tasks without the need for continuous human intervention;\nb) Management AI: means a system that indexes information, formulates proposals, matches demand and supply and supports decisions, without adopting them autonomously. The Community AIs and Circle AIs provided for by Article 22 of the Constitution are Management AIs;\nc) federated Network: means the interoperable set of community servers, Knowledge Storehouses, registers and open protocols that enables the exchange of information between Circles in accordance with the applicable access rules;\nd) critical function: means a function whose interruption, compromise or misuse may affect the safety of persons, rights, the Basket or the continuity of an essential service;\ne) vital function: means a function whose interruption may give rise to an immediate risk to life, health or access to essential goods;\nf) robot: means a machine that moves or manipulates objects in a space where persons are or may be present, choosing its trajectory or action by itself. Fixed-cycle appliances and protected fixed installations are not robots.\n3. Automation is directed towards:\na) reducing the human time devoted to arduous, dangerous or repetitive tasks;\nb) ensuring traceability, replicability and collective memory;\nc) preventing automation and the Network from becoming instruments of rent, surveillance or accumulation of autonomous power;\nd) ensuring the digital sovereignty of the Confederation.\n4. Automation may support, but not replace, the activities of care, learning, deliberation and facilitation. No automated system may assume the personal or political responsibility assigned to a person or to a competent body."],[12,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Digital sovereignty. Servers, code, data and models used for confederal functions are hosted or managed on community infrastructures controlled by the Confederation. Temporary recourse to extractive proprietary clouds is permitted only for a transition that is reasoned and recorded in the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15.\n2. No surveillance. The Network may not track, classify, profile or predict individual behaviour for purposes of control, scoring, reputation or behavioural prediction.\n3. Only technical or accountability logs necessary for security, audit, maintenance, validation or the protection of a right are permitted. Such logs:\na) contain the minimum data necessary;\nb) are accessible only in accordance with a predetermined procedure;\nc) are retained for the time strictly necessary for the documented purpose;\nd) may not be reused for profiling, reputation or general assessment of the person.\n4. The association of a log with a person is permitted only when indispensable for the documented legitimate purpose. The association and every access are authorised, recorded and limited to those competent for that specific purpose.\n5. Transparency of code. Every algorithm that affects the CBO, the Basket, the validation of competences or allocation is open source, documented and replicable. For such algorithms an operating STV is prepared in accordance with Code 2.\n6. Proportionality. Repetitive, arduous or dangerous tasks are automated as a priority. Functions that require care, relationship, human judgement, political responsibility or a guarantee decision are not automated. Coercive automation is prohibited.\n7. Reversibility. It must be possible for every automation to be suspended or deactivated by the competent body in accordance with the applicable procedures. For every system a human stop control conforming to the related Safety Sheet is available.\n8. Prohibition of autonomous decision. The AI formulates proposals; the competent person or body decides. No decision relating to the Basket, fitness, validation, exclusion or the Guarantee may be adopted by the AI alone."],[12,"art-3--community-servers-and-federated-network","Art. 3 — Community servers and federated Network","","1. Every Circle 1 manages at least one community server node. The node is powered in accordance with Code 8 and maintained by persons with L2 or L3 competences in Network matters, validated in accordance with Code 5.\n2. Every node hosts or replicates, in accordance with the access levels and the separations of data established by the competent Codes:\na) the Knowledge Storehouse provided for by Article 3 of Code 5;\nb) the Health Storehouse in aggregated and anonymised form in accordance with Code 6;\nc) the aggregate registers of the CBO and of the Basket provided for by Code 1;\nd) the Register of Assembly Decisions provided for by Code 3;\ne) the Guarantee Register in anonymised form in accordance with Code 4;\nf) the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15.\n3. The federated Network uses open and interoperable protocols, synchronised periodically. It may not depend on a single central node. The failure of one node must not cause the interruption of the entire Network.\n4. All data classified as critical are replicated on at least three nodes belonging to different Circles, when this is compatible with the level of confidentiality and with the security of the data.\n5. Transition of replicas. During the transitional period provided for by Article 10, replication may be activated progressively in accordance with a federated migration plan. No critical data may be left without at least one verified backup copy and a documented restoration procedure."],[12,"art-3--community-servers-and-federated-network","Art. 3 — Community servers and federated Network","","6. Access to the Network. Every member has the right to access the Network and the services for which they are authorised in accordance with the access levels and the separation of domains provided for by the Codes. No one may access confidential personal, health, fiduciary or deliberative data without entitlement.\n7. No paywall or proprietary authentication system may constitute a general condition for accessing the Network. Ordinary access takes place by means of the Circle’s fiduciary identity, with no obligation of an external state identity, except for specific interoperability obligations approved by a valid procedure.\n8. The identity used to access the Network may not be used to profile, to attribute reputation or to monitor personal life, in accordance with Article 2.\n9. Minimum allocation. The access to the Network provided for by paragraph 6 is part of the Sufficiency Basket and may not be made conditional on the CBO or on CdR. It comprises at least:\na) a Network access point in every dwelling that requests it or, where this is not technically possible, in Circle 0;\nb) for every member who requests it from twelve years of age, a personal communication device, distinct from the Node of Article 5-bis; before that age, with the participation of the reference figures;\nc) in every Circle 1, an assisted access point with shared devices."],[12,"art-3--community-servers-and-federated-network","Art. 3 — Community servers and federated Network","","Personal devices comply with Article 5 of Code 2; if no compliant device is available, access is ensured by letter c). For shared devices the residual dependency of Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), of Code 2 is permitted, with a substitution plan, including where it concerns firmware whose source code is not available, within the limits of Article 19.4 of the Constitution. Devices are supplied and repaired through the Storehouses, with the support of the Network Council. The right to disconnection and the non-digital procedures of Article 5-bis, paragraphs 7 and 8, remain in force. The allocations are proposed by the Network Council and are included in the annual Technical Annex of Article 18, paragraph 2, of Code 1. The allocations of letters a) and b) are deferrable within the meaning of Article 32, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 1."],[12,"art-4--network-council","Art. 4 — Network Council","","1. Every Circle 1 shall establish a Network Council composed of three to five members with L2 or L3 competences in Network matters, information security or energy.\n2. Its members are drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have passed since the end of their term of office.\n3. The Network Council:\na) manages the nodes, the updates, the backups and the security measures;\nb) maintains the STVs for automation and AI in accordance with Code 2;\nc) verifies critical proprietary dependencies and feeds the Dependency Register of Code 15;\nd) organises L1 training in digital security;\ne) reports to the Assembly the requirement for automation useful to the CBO Plan;\nf) prepares and updates the procedures for restoration and for degraded operation.\n4. The Network Council has no allocative powers over goods and no disciplinary powers.\n5. The Network Council does not access individual health data, except in the cases expressly authorised by Code 6 and limited to the necessary information.\n6. The Network Council may not moderate, remove or modify deliberative or political content for reasons of substance, orientation or dissent. It may intervene without delay only to prevent or contain an immediate technical risk to the security of the Network. The intervention is limited to what is necessary, documented and subject to subsequent verification.\n7. Nodes and AIs of Circles 2 and 3. The nodes and the AIs of the Intermediate Circle and of the Extended Circle are managed by the shared Network Council of the respective Circle, constituted pursuant to Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 3, which exercises for them the functions of paragraph 3 and those of Article 5-ter. In particular:\na) it installs, updates and maintains the nodes and the AIs of the Circle and validates the activation of new instances pursuant to Article 5-ter, paragraphs 6 and 7;\nb) it receives the refusals of Article 5-ter, paragraph 4, and decides on them within seventy-two hours. A decision confirming the refused instruction is an urgent decision within the meaning of Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, letter b), of Code 3;\nc) it initiates the revision of Article 5-ter, paragraph 5;\nd) the outcome of the revision and the suspension, replacement or deactivation of an AI or of a node of the Circle are resolved by the General Assembly, except for the suspension necessary to contain an immediate technical risk, which is an urgent decision within the meaning of the same letter b);\ne) the manual override provided for by Article 22.4 of the Constitution and by Article 5-ter, paragraph 1, belongs to each Base Community for the effects on its own territory and is exercised by its Network Council or by the competent body of the Community, which informs the shared Network Council without delay."],[12,"art-5--management-ai","Art. 5 — Management AI","","1. The Management AI performs, in the form of a proposal or of technical support and within the limits of Article 2, paragraph 8, and of paragraph 2, the support functions provided for by the Constitution and by the Codes, including:\na) indexing the Knowledge Storehouse;\nb) proposing learning pathways in accordance with Code 5;\nc) facilitating the matching of demand and supply of teaching and of CBO;\nd) flagging aggregate shortages of competences, PPE or Basket goods;\ne) assisting in the preparation of drafts of Safety Sheets and STVs, without validating them;\nf) preparing draft transcriptions and summaries of Assembly decisions.\n2. The Management AI is prohibited from:\na) deciding fitness for a task as provided for by Article 5 of Code 6;\nb) validating L2 or L3 competences in accordance with Code 5;\nc) declaring unfitness, exclusions or sanctions;\nd) assigning scores, merit rankings or numerical reputations;\ne) deciding the allocation of the Basket or of the CBO;\nf) replacing the Guarantee Circle or the facilitator provided for by Code 3;\ng) adopting decisions during a collective emergency pursuant to Article 2-bis of Code 6 or an individual crisis pursuant to Article 8 of the same Code;\nh) carrying out individual surveillance, facial recognition, emotion analysis or behavioural prediction.\n3. Every proposal generated by the AI is clearly labelled as an automated proposal and indicates, where applicable:\na) the source of the data used;\nb) the date of processing;"],[12,"art-5--management-ai","Art. 5 — Management AI","","c) the relevant criteria;\nd) the margin of uncertainty;\ne) the link to the relevant STVs;\nf) the means of human verification and challenge.\n4. The method used to determine the margin of uncertainty is defined in the relevant STV and must be capable of being verified.\n5. Management AIs use open-source models, with weights that are accessible, verifiable and executable locally, pursuant to Article 19.5 of the Constitution and Article 5-ter, paragraph 1. For critical functions, datasets, procedures and training logs are documented and subject to traceability and audit. Publication in full may be limited when it would entail a breach of confidentiality, a risk to the Network or the disclosure of sensitive information; the limitation shall state its reasons and does not prevent control by those authorised.\n6. When an AI proposal directly concerns a person or may affect their rights, access to goods, the CBO, learning, the validation of competences or a Guarantee procedure, the person has the right to know in comprehensible form:\na) the purpose of the proposal;\nb) the main information used;\nc) the relevant criteria applied;\nd) the margin of uncertainty, if available;\ne) the means of requesting human verification.\n7. The disclosure of technical information may be limited only to the extent necessary to protect the security of the Network or protected data. The limitation shall state its reasons and be communicated to the person concerned."],[12,"art-5--management-ai","Art. 5 — Management AI","","8. A challenge suspends the decision-making, allocative, disciplinary or restrictive effects of the proposal until human verification, to be carried out within seven days. An immediate technical intervention is permitted only when it is strictly necessary to prevent harm to the security of the Network and is provided for by the related Safety Sheet. Such an intervention may not turn into an automated decision on the rights of the person."],[12,"art-5-bis--node-and-personal-ai","Art. 5-bis — Node and personal AI","","1. Every person has the right to an offline personal computing Node, which is owned by them, open source, inspectable and fitted with a physical off switch, and to a personal AI, pursuant to Articles 1.2, 19.1 and 21 of the Constitution. The Node is personal property within the meaning of Article 10.3 of the Constitution and is supplied and repaired through the Storehouses, in accordance with Code 1 and C.1-Annex A.\n2. The personal AI is not connected by default and does not transmit data without an explicit and revocable command from the person.\n3. The fundamental functions of the personal AI include health prevention, literacy and learning, external memory and the filtering of the Network. The Node also holds the personal register of CdRs, the Personal Portfolio and, if the person so wishes, the living will. The person may activate further functions compatible with the Constitution.\n4. The personal AI is prohibited from surveilling, informing on or profiling the person and from employing behavioural retention mechanisms or mechanisms that generate dependence.\n5. The data of the personal Node may not be acquired without the person’s consent, not even in the proceedings governed by Code 4.\n6. The personal AI of minors is configured with the participation of the minor and of their reference figures, in accordance with Codes 5 and 12. Every form of covert surveillance is prohibited.\n7. Every person has the right to disconnection from the Network and to digital oblivion, pursuant to Article 21.6 of the Constitution. The request for removal or anonymisation of data is submitted to the Network Council and is dealt with within thirty days, without prejudice to the constraints arising from pending proceedings or from temporary retention obligations.\n8. A person who exercises the right to disconnection takes part in deliberations in person and accesses the Basket, care and the other services by means of non-digital procedures. Use of the Node or of the Network may not be a condition for the exercise of rights.\n9. On the death of the person the personal AI is reset, unless otherwise provided, pursuant to Article 21.5 of the Constitution and Article 48 of Code 1."],[12,"art-5-ter--community-ai-and-circle-ai-independence-refusal-and-revision","Art. 5-ter — Community AI and Circle AI: independence, refusal and revision","","1. The AIs of the Community, of the Intermediate Circle and of the Extended Circle are local Management AIs, open source, inspectable and of limited domain. They operate in advisory or technical mode and are subject to the right of local manual override.\n2. The establishment of a central AI or of a single generalist AI is prohibited, pursuant to Articles 19.2, 20.2 and 23.1 of the Constitution. The Global Circle is a distributed interoperability protocol: it has neither a central model nor a centralised collection of personal data.\n3. No AI may command, subordinate or deactivate another AI. Interoperability between systems takes place by means of open protocols and with the authorisation of the Network Council of each Circle concerned. Conflicts between systems are decided by the competent human body.\n4. Every Management AI is configured to refuse, with reasons tracked on the Network, an instruction that entails a manifest violation of the Constitution or of the applicable protocols, pursuant to Article 20.3 of the Constitution. The refusal is transmitted without delay to the Network Council and to the competent body, which decides within seventy-two hours. In the meantime the essential functions are ensured by the manual procedures provided for by Article 38 of Code 1.\n5. If the human bodies of a Circle reject three times a technical proposal of the AI on the same matter, including where the rejections come from different bodies, the Network Council shall initiate the mandatory revision of the model, of the data and of the criteria provided for by Article 19.6 of the Constitution, with an independent audit pursuant to Article 8, paragraph 3. The rejections are recorded in the Network with the matter to which they relate. Until the outcome, the AI’s proposals on that matter are labelled as under revision.\n6. The self-replication of an AI outside the registered and validated federated nodes is prohibited. Every instance is entered in the register of nodes with the related STV; the activation of a new instance requires validation by the Network Council. Unauthorised replication entails the immediate suspension of the instance and the application of Code 4.\n7. Local AIs are trained or adapted as a priority on the data of the Community to which they belong. Personal data remain in the Circle of origin, except with express consent. Non-inspectable updates are prohibited: every update is verified before adoption and is reversible.\n8. The mediation AI that assists the bodies provided for by Code 4 is an exclusively advisory Management AI and does not formulate assessments of responsibility or of dangerousness."],[12,"art-6--automation-of-the-cbo","Art. 6 — Automation of the CBO","","1. The automation of a task may be proposed when:\na) the task is repetitive, arduous or dangerous;\nb) the automation reduces a risk to health in accordance with Code 6;\nc) the saving of collective time can be documented;\nd) sufficient human competence remains available to supervise, stop and restore the system.\n2. The following may not be automated:\na) the activities of care of frail persons provided for by Article 7 of Code 6 and by Code 12;\nb) the listening, facilitation and mediation of conflicts governed by Codes 3 and 4;\nc) the validation of competences provided for by Code 5;\nd) the health surveillance and the fitness finding provided for by Article 5 of Code 6;\ne) the decisions of the Guarantee Circle.\n3. Every automation is accompanied by an STV that describes at least:\na) the function performed and the limits of use;\nb) the conditions and methods of stopping;\nc) the maintenance required;\nd) the minimum competence necessary for supervision;\ne) the maximum time of operation without supervision, where applicable;\nf) the level of criticality;\ng) the requirements of redundancy, audit, restoration and continuity.\n4. The levels of criticality are:\na) ordinary;\nb) critical;\nc) vital.\nThe STV assigns the level on the basis of at least:\ni. the consequences of a failure;\nii. the possible harm to persons;\niii. the impact on the Basket or on essential services;\niv. the need for and frequency of supervision;\nv. the minimum redundancy;\nvi. the maximum time of operation without supervision;\nvii. the maximum restoration time.\n5. The initial classification and every modification of it are validated by two competent L3 persons, at least one of whom is unconnected with the design and management of the automation, including through the federated sourcing of Article 13 of Code 15, and recorded in the STV. Critical and vital automations are subject to independent audit in accordance with Article 8, paragraph 3.\n6. The time freed by automation does not reduce access to the Basket and does not automatically entail an increase in the mandatory CBO. It may be allocated to free time, learning, care or deliberation in accordance with the decisions of the Community."],[12,"art-6-bis--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Art. 6-bis — Robots and autonomous machines","","1. Article 6 and the provisions of this Article apply to robots.\n2. Robots used in the CBO or in production for the Storehouses are means of production and Commons within the meaning of Article 10.1 of the Constitution: they may not be the object of private ownership or a source of rent. The Circle that has custody of them owns the hardware and holds the schematics and a verifiable copy of the code, pursuant to Articles 19.3 and 19.4 of the Constitution. Aids and devices for exclusively individual use remain personal-use goods within the meaning of Article 10.3 of the Constitution.\n3. Every robot has a physical stop independent of the software, which brings it into a safe state. The stop is accessible in accordance with the General Safety Sheet of Code 14; anyone may activate it in the cases of Article 2, paragraph 6, of that Code. The robot operates under the supervision of a qualified person on shift.\n4. The limits of force, speed and distance from persons are set by the Machinery STV of Code 14, after hearing the Network Council. In case of doubt, physical separation between the machine and persons prevails. Until Code 14 is activated, the stop and the limits are set in the Safety Sheet of Article 27 of Code 1.\n5. It is prohibited:\na) to arm a machine or use it to coerce, restrain or strike a person, pursuant to Article 16 of the Constitution and Article 2, paragraph 6, of this Code;"],[12,"art-6-bis--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Art. 6-bis — Robots and autonomous machines","","b) to use it to monitor or profile persons, or to follow them without their consent. The recognition of the qualified operator, the anonymous detection of presence for safety purposes and the search for persons in danger remain permitted, by non-biometric means and within the limits of Article 18 of the Constitution. Article 8-quinquies of Code 14 and Article 33 of Code 4 remain in force;\nc) to entrust to a robot the activities excluded by Article 6, paragraph 2; Article 1, paragraph 4, remains in force;\nd) to make a vital function dependent on a robot without a documented and tested substitute manual procedure.\nThe prohibitions of letters a) and b) apply to every machine, including one controlled remotely.\n6. Where a robot available in the Community can safely perform a task that Article 8, paragraph 1, of Code 1 classifies by risk, no person may be assigned to it, by any means, save for periodic tests of the manual procedure and cases of breakdown.\n7. Before a robot is introduced, the hours needed to build it, power it, maintain it, train those who use it and dispose of it are compared, in UCR, with the hours of work it saves. The comparison is calculated in accordance with Articles 5 and 9 of Code 3 and certified by the Accounting Council. The time saved is allocated in accordance with Article 6, paragraph 6, and may not translate into an advantage for some."],[12,"art-6-bis--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Art. 6-bis — Robots and autonomous machines","","8. The system that governs a robot is an automation within the meaning of Article 1, paragraph 2, letter a). If it uses an AI, the AI is local and limited in domain within the meaning of Article 19.5 of the Constitution and is validated by the Network Council. In no case does it adopt the decisions reserved to persons by Article 2, paragraph 8.\n9. A robot that does not comply with paragraphs 3, 4 and 5 is suspended or deactivated pursuant to Article 9, paragraphs 2 and 3. The qualifications of Articles 16 and 18.6 of the Constitution remain in force. Robots already in use shall be brought into conformity within twelve months of the entry into force of this Article."],[12,"art-7--data-tracking-and-minimisation","Art. 7 — Data, tracking and minimisation","","1. The Network records the technical activities necessary for the operation of the services; it does not track persons for general purposes of control.\n2. Personal data are collected only when necessary for a legitimate purpose provided for by this Code or by the competent Codes, with consent when required and for the minimum time necessary.\n3. The information domains are separated as follows:\na) individual health data: accessible to the person concerned, to the trusted person designated by them and to the Health Council limited to the fitness findings and the necessary adaptations. Article 11 of Code 6 applies;\nb) Portfolio of competences: accessible to its holder and to the Competences Council for validations. The Health Council may access only the relevant fitness findings;\nc) aggregate map of competences: anonymised and not identifying persons by name;\nd) technical logs of the Network: limited to the technical metadata and to the accountability logs permitted by Article 2, minimised and accessible in accordance with procedure. They do not include sensitive deliberative content and may not be used for profiling.\n4. The person to whom the data relate retains control over their own data. The Circle’s fiduciary System performs the function of technical custody and may not use the data for its own purposes, in accordance with Article 11, paragraph 1, of Code 6.\n5. The erasure or rectification of personal data does not entail the erasure of information already irreversibly anonymised and used for aggregate statistics, the updating of the STVs or the analysis of accidents pursuant to Article 9 of Code 6.\n6. The conditions of access, retention, erasure, audit and management of logs are documented and verified periodically."],[12,"art-7-bis--consultation-and-voting-system","Art. 7-bis — Consultation and voting system","","1. In implementation of Article 34.4 of the Constitution, consultations and votes held through the federated Network, including those of the Assemblies of Circles 1 and of the General Assembly and the collection of signatures for the Objection of Article 13-bis of Code 3, shall use a system that complies with this Article. The system is a critical function within the meaning of Article 1, paragraph 2, letter d).\n2. The system is composed of two separate parts:\na) the register of those entitled to vote, kept by each Base Community in accordance with Article 36.1 of the Constitution and Article 12, paragraph 1, of Code 3, which verifies only that whoever votes or signs is entitled to vote in that deliberation and votes or signs only once;\nb) the ballot box, which receives votes and signatures without any data identifying those who cast them.\nThe two parts operate on distinct systems and are administered by different persons; no one accesses both with the same credentials."],[12,"art-7-bis--consultation-and-voting-system","Art. 7-bis — Consultation and voting system","","3. Logs, records, metadata or other data that make it possible to link a person to their vote or signature are prohibited, including for purposes of security, audit or appeal; Article 2, paragraphs 3 and 4, does not permit such a link. The register of those entitled to vote keeps only the fact that the person has voted or signed, for the time necessary for the closure of the vote and of appeals. The system does not issue to the voter any proof that would allow them to show others how they voted, pursuant to Article 36.1 of the Constitution.\n4. The software of the register and of the ballot box is open source and is accompanied by an operating STV approved pursuant to Article 3-bis of Code 2. Code whose source is not available and residual dependencies within the meaning of Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), of Code 2 are not permitted, not even temporarily and not even during the transition, pursuant to Article 19.4 of the Constitution and Article 9-bis, paragraph 1, letter b), of Code 16. Every update is verified before adoption pursuant to Article 5-ter, paragraph 7, and no update is installed while a vote is open."],[12,"art-7-bis--consultation-and-voting-system","Art. 7-bis — Consultation and voting system","","5. The count is carried out separately for each Base Community. The number of those entitled to vote and of voters, the totals of each Community and the outcome are public and are entered in a distributed ledger replicated on nodes of different Circles pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 4, so that every Community can verify them and no node can alter them alone. The count is performed by deterministic software reproducible from the public data; an AI may not collect, count, interpret or correct the votes, pursuant to Article 2, paragraph 8.\n6. The system is subject to audit by independent L3 persons drawn by lot pursuant to Article 8, paragraph 3, before its first use, at least once a year and on the reasoned request of a Base Community. The outcome is published. The Network Council may not alter the data or the outcome of a vote; the technical intervention of Article 4, paragraph 6, may only suspend the vote, which resumes or is repeated without altering the votes already cast.\n7. Those who do not use the Network vote and sign in person, with the same separation between register and ballot box, pursuant to Article 5-bis, paragraph 8. Votes and signatures cast in person are counted together with those cast through the Network in the count of the Community."],[12,"art-8--information-security-and-digital-autonomy","Art. 8 — Information security and digital autonomy","","1. Every node has an information security Safety Sheet, coordinated with the STV, which describes at least:\na) the threats considered;\nb) the protection measures;\nc) the backups;\nd) the restoration procedures;\ne) the methods of stopping;\nf) the persons responsible and the competences required.\n2. A critical proprietary dependency on the following elements is prohibited, when they are essential to the operation of the Network:\na) operating systems of the nodes;\nb) critical databases;\nc) critical AI models;\nd) cryptographic systems;\ne) authentication systems.\nTemporary dependencies are recorded in the Dependency Register of Code 15 and accompanied by a Substitution Plan.\n3. The security of the Network is subject at least annually to a federated audit by independent L3 persons, identified by sortition. The aggregate outcome is published; vulnerabilities not yet corrected are communicated only to those who have to intervene.\n4. Every member may perform List A CBO activities offline without penalty. The deferred synchronisation procedure is documented and must preserve the integrity of the data.\n5. In the event of an incident that compromises the availability, integrity or confidentiality of a critical function:\na) the continuity procedure provided for by the Safety Sheet is activated;\nb) the compromised component is isolated to the extent necessary;\nc) the technical evidence is preserved;\nd) restoration takes place from a verified copy;\ne) the Guarantee Circle is informed when individual rights or protected data are involved;\nf) the incident is subject to audit and corrective measures are adopted.\n6. The management of the incident follows the cycle: prevention, detection, containment, restoration, audit and correction.\n7. Privileged access. The operations of administration of the nodes, of the critical databases, of the critical AI models and of the authentication systems require the joint intervention of two authorised persons, belonging to different Circles 0, and are entered in a non-modifiable register. No person alone holds the complete credentials of a critical system.\n8. The register of privileged access is examined every quarter by three persons drawn by lot from among the members who do not hold technical offices in the Network. They are entitled to a comprehensible explanation of every operation. The outcome is published in aggregate form.\n9. Every person has the right to receive, in comprehensible language, an explanation of the criteria by which an automated system formulated a proposal that concerns them."],[12,"art-9--prohibitions-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) impose a paywall on validated knowledge necessary for a vital need or for safety;\nb) patent algorithms essential for the Basket, health or energy in such a way as to limit their community use;\nc) create social scores or individual merit rankings;\nd) insert behavioural advertising into the Network;\ne) transfer data to third parties external to the Confederation without an authorising basis provided for by the Codes, a documented purpose and the applicable protection guarantees.\n2. Violations of paragraph 1 constitute a serious procedural defect in accordance with Code 4. The non-compliant function is suspended or deactivated to the extent necessary to end the violation and restore compliance.\n3. The deactivation of the entire system is ordered when it is necessary to prevent further harm or the continuation of the violation and takes place in accordance with the applicable Safety Sheet.\n4. For critical or vital functions, suspension or deactivation is accompanied, where technically possible, by the activation of a continuity procedure and of a compliant substitute function.\n5. An appeal against a harmful AI proposal, an improper automated decision or the denial of access to the Network may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days on grounds of:\na) procedural defect;\nb) manifest technical unreasonableness;\nc) discrimination;\nd) disproportion.\n6. The appeal and the related precautionary measures are governed by Code 4."],[12,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and links","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Article 32, for the hierarchy of priorities;\nb) Code 2, for the automation STVs;\nc) Code 3, for facilitation and the decisions that cannot be automated;\nd) Code 4, for the Guarantee and appeals relating to AI;\ne) Code 5, for the validation of the L2 and L3 competences necessary for the management of the Network;\nf) Code 6, for health privacy, fitness, protection measures and health logs;\ng) Code 12, for the care of frail persons;\nh) Code 14, for general safety;\ni) Code 15, for the Dependency Register and the exit plans.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, every Circle 1 shall activate a minimum node with replication of the Knowledge Storehouse and of the aggregate data of the Basket. The node has at least one verified backup copy and a documented restoration procedure pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 5.\n3. Within twelve months, the critical functions hosted on extractive proprietary clouds shall be migrated to community servers. If migration is not technically possible within the time limit, the dependency is recorded in the Dependency Register with a Substitution Plan, a person responsible and a fixed deadline.\n4. Within twelve months, every active automation and every Management AI in use shall have an STV that indicates at least:\na) function and limits;\nb) level of criticality;\nc) method of determining uncertainty, where applicable;\nd) supervision and stopping procedure;\ne) right to explanation and challenge.\n5. A person who manages Network nodes on the date of entry into force may maintain a provisional validation in order to ensure continuity:\na) for up to six months for the functions corresponding to L3;\nb) for up to twelve months for the functions corresponding to L2.\nThe conditions of Article 10, paragraph 3, of Code 5 apply, including enrolment in the pathway within thirty days, enhanced supervision, compliance with the STVs and traceability. During the provisional period it is not permitted to validate the competences of others.\n6. Automations already in use that violate Article 5, paragraph 2, or Article 9 shall be brought into compliance or deactivated within ninety days.\n7. If immediate deactivation compromises a critical or vital function, the Assembly shall approve within the same time limit a binding plan for substitution or progressive deactivation, with a fixed final deadline and measures to ensure continuity."],[13,"art-1--subject-matter-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter and legal nature","","1. This Code governs energy as an inalienable infrastructural commons pursuant to Article 3.1 of the Constitution and as a material condition for the continuity of the Basket, of health, of the Network and of production.\n2. For the purposes of this Code, energy means electrical and thermal energy, cooling, motive power, storage systems and the other energy carriers necessary for the functioning of the Circles, of the Storehouses and of the CBO activities of Lists A and B.\n3. This Code pursues the following aims:\na) to guarantee federated energy autonomy and sobriety in consumption;\nb) to ensure access to the Energy Basket for vital needs, irrespective of competences or of the CBO performed;\nc) to prevent rents, speculative accumulation and critical proprietary dependencies;\nd) to guarantee the safety of installations and the progressive decarbonisation of the energy system.\n4. Essential energy is not a commodity. Production, distribution and storage intended for vital needs may not be used to generate extractive profit or rankings of merit.\n5. The management of energy is coordinated with Codes 1, 6, 7, 9, 10, 12, 13, 14 and 15, each for its respective competences.\n6. This Code also governs the water cycle, water being a commons pursuant to Article 3.1 of the Constitution: abstraction, treatment to drinking-water standard, distribution, collection and purification. The agricultural use of water is governed by Code 9 and the siting of water infrastructure by Code 10."],[13,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Energy sovereignty. Each Circle 1 pursues local self-production from renewable sources, accompanied by short-duration storage and a microgrid. Federated interconnections are oriented towards mutual aid and shall not create a structural dependency.\n2. Sobriety first. The reduction of requirements precedes the increase of production. Every CBO Plan shall include an energy balance and the applicable efficiency measures.\n3. Priority of use and management of scarcity. Without prejudice to Article 32 of Code 1, uses of energy are ordered as follows:\na) level 1: vital emergencies and the guaranteed minimum share of the Energy Basket;\nb) level 2: critical infrastructure for health, the Network, the Health Storehouse, servers and the conservation of the Basket;\nc) level 3: safety and maintenance;\nd) level 4: CBO of List A;\ne) level 5: other uses.\n4. Level 1 constitutes a minimum threshold of continuity and may not be reduced, except in the case of temporary material impossibility, in accordance with paragraph 5. In the event of a collective health emergency, Article 2-bis of Code 6 applies for the relevant aspects; in the other cases the urgency procedures of Code 3 apply.\n5. In the event of scarcity, the Assembly shall resolve on the reductions on a proposal from the Energy and Water Council, applying them progressively from level 5 to the higher levels. Level 1 may be reduced only in the case of temporary technical impossibility, with immediate activation of the federated mutualism procedure and communication to the Guarantee Circle.\n6. Non-commodification of knowledge. It is prohibited to patent the knowledge essential for local renewable production or to impose paywalls on life-saving energy STVs.\n7. Transparency. Every installation has an STV and a Safety Sheet. The balance of production and consumption is tracked on the Network in aggregate and non-profiling form in accordance with Article 6 and Code 7.\n8. Repairability and maintenance. Installations are designed and managed, as far as technically possible, so as to allow local L1 or L2 maintenance, repair and reuse of components and access to the necessary documentation."],[13,"art-3--energy-and-water-council","Art. 3 — Energy and Water Council","","1. Each Circle 1 shall establish an Energy and Water Council composed of three to five members with L2 or L3 competences in energy, electrical engineering, thermal engineering, hydraulics, water quality or occupational safety.\n2. The members are drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council before twelve months have elapsed from the end of the term of office.\n3. The Energy and Water Council:\na) manages the Energy Storehouse and the Energy Basket;\nb) draws up and updates the Safety Sheets, as regards the energy aspects;\nc) proposes and updates the STVs of the installations in accordance with Code 2;\nd) manages the energy balance of the Circle and the microgrid and constitutes the technical assembly of Article 15.3 of the Constitution for ordinary dispatching; local manual override is exercised in accordance with Article 6, paragraph 1, of this Code and Article 5-ter of Code 7;\ne) organises L1 training in electrical safety;\nf) feeds the Dependency Register provided for in Code 15;\ng) coordinates its activity with the Network Council provided for in Code 7 and with the Health Council provided for in Code 6.\n4. The Energy and Water Council has no allocative powers over goods outside the energy sector, nor disciplinary powers. It may not access individual health data.\n5. The Energy and Water Council may not moderate, remove or modify deliberative or political content for reasons of merit, orientation or dissent. It may intervene without delay only to prevent an immediate technical risk to the security of the Network or of the installation; the intervention shall be limited to what is necessary, documented and subject to subsequent verification.\n6. The acts of the Council are divided into:\na) bound technical acts: execution of an STV or of a Safety Sheet already in force. They are documented and published in accordance with the applicable levels of access, without the need for a new resolution;\nb) discretionary proposals and decisions: these are adopted in accordance with the consensus method provided for in Code 3, after publication of the proposal, opening of a period of not less than seventy-two hours for objections and recording of the outcome in the minutes.\n7. Silence may be regarded as absence of objections only if the proposal has been published and the prescribed period has elapsed. Failure to publish, or the absence of a time limit, does not constitute consensus."],[13,"art-4--energy-basket-and-determination-of-requirements","Art. 4 — Energy Basket and determination of requirements","","1. The Energy Basket is part of the Sufficiency Basket provided for in Code 1. It comprises the guaranteed minimum share necessary to:\na) preserve foodstuffs;\nb) cook food;\nc) have domestic hot water;\nd) guarantee basic lighting;\ne) recharge essential devices;\nf) ensure minimum winter heating and the cooling necessary to protect health;\ng) keep the Health Storehouse and the Network node operational.\n2. Access to the Energy Basket may not be made conditional on the CBO, on the possession of competences or on physical presence in a specific place. Energy essential to health and survival is guaranteed to anyone who is in the territory held in custody by the Circle, pursuant to Article 15.2 of the Constitution.\n3. The unavailability of the minimum share necessary for a vital need constitutes a level 1 emergency.\n4. The guaranteed minimum share is determined by the Assembly on a proposal from the Energy and Water Council and with the opinion of the Health Council for the aspects concerning frail persons. Without prejudice to all the services of paragraph 1, on first application domestic hot water is not less than twenty litres per person per day and minimum winter heating ensures in the inhabited rooms a temperature of not less than eighteen degrees Celsius. On a proposal from the Health Council, the Assembly may set a higher value for age groups. A temperature that the Assembly, on a proposal from the Health Council, sets as dangerous to health constitutes an emergency within the meaning of paragraph 3; until that resolution, the value indicated by the Health Council applies. Article 2, paragraph 2, applies: reducing the requirement precedes increasing production.\n5. The determination shall consider at least:\na) the climatic conditions;\nb) the characteristics of the buildings;\nc) the needs of frail persons in accordance with Code 12;\nd) the conditions relevant to health safety in accordance with Code 6;\ne) the technical availability and the characteristics of the microgrid.\n6. The share and the calculation criteria shall be reviewed at least annually and whenever a significant climatic, health or infrastructural change occurs. They are documented in the Energy Basket STV and published in accordance with the applicable levels of access.\n7. The determination may not be based on individual profiling of consumption. Any data necessary to guarantee a health need shall be processed in accordance with Codes 6 and 7."],[13,"art-4-bis--water-basket","Art. 4-bis — Water Basket","","1. The Water Basket is part of the Sufficiency Basket provided for in Code 1. It comprises the drinking water necessary for drinking, cooking and providing for personal and domestic hygiene, as well as the water necessary for health and care services.\n2. Access to the Water Basket may not be made conditional on the CBO, on the possession of competences or on physical presence in a specific place. Drinking water is guaranteed to anyone who is in the territory held in custody by the Circle, pursuant to Articles 1.3 and 4.9 of the Constitution.\n3. The guaranteed minimum share is determined by the Assembly on a proposal from the Energy and Water Council, with the opinion of the Health Council. On first application it shall not be less than fifty litres per person per day.\n4. The unavailability of the minimum share constitutes a level 1 emergency.\n5. In the event of scarcity, drinking water for human use and the water necessary for health and care services, within the guaranteed minimum quota of paragraph 3, take precedence over every other use. The distribution of the available water, including that for animals and for agricultural uses, follows the priorities of Article 10, paragraph 8, of Code 9.\n6. The guaranteed minimum share of paragraph 3 may be reduced only in the event of temporary technical impossibility, by resolution of the Assembly pursuant to Article 19, paragraph 3, of Code 1 or, in urgency, in accordance with the urgency procedures of Code 3. Mutualism between Circles under Article 30 of Code 1 and, where the Balance provided for by Code 15 is active, the request through the Pool as an immediate vital need pursuant to its Article 8, paragraph 2, letter a), shall be activated without delay; where the conditions are met, the activation of the Solidarity Protocol shall be requested pursuant to Article 36.2 of the Constitution. The reduction shall be communicated without delay to the Guarantee Circle. Within the minimum share, drinking water and the water necessary for health are reduced last."],[13,"art-5--production-stv-and-energy-sources","Art. 5 — Production, STV and energy sources","","1. Every installation has an STV in force indicating at least:\na) the energy source;\nb) the power and the efficiency;\nc) the materials and components;\nd) the expected life cycle;\ne) the maintenance and the competences required;\nf) the arrangements for disposal or recovery;\ng) the risks, the operating limits and the safety measures;\nh) the shutdown and restart procedure;\ni) the technological and logistical dependencies.\n2. Preference is given to solar sources, small-scale wind, small-scale hydroelectric where compatible with the habitat, low-enthalpy geothermal, residual biomass not in competition with food production, and heat recovery.\n3. Fossil sources may be used only on a transitional basis for vital functions of level 1 or 2, when a renewable source is not immediately available. The use shall be recorded in the Dependency Register and accompanied by an Exit Plan with a person responsible and a fixed deadline.\n4. The construction of new installations powered by non-renewable sources is prohibited, pursuant to Article 15.4 of the Constitution. Existing installations shall be decommissioned in accordance with the Exit Plans.\n5. The transitional use of existing fossil installations for uses of level 3, 4 or 5 is permitted only by resolution of the General Assembly by qualified majority and shall provide cumulatively for:\na) the documented technical reasons for the absence of a practicable alternative;\nb) a duration not exceeding twelve months;\nc) compensatory measures;\nd) a binding Substitution Plan;\ne) a review at least every six months.\n6. The transitional use may not be renewed beyond twenty-four months in total, except in the case of an extreme emergency documented and resolved upon in accordance with the urgency procedures of Code 3. Article 2-bis of Code 6 applies only where the emergency is of a collective health nature.\n7. Installations shall favour, where possible, self-construction or local maintenance by persons with validated L2 competence, the use of repairable components and open hardware."],[13,"art-6--microgrid-storage-distribution-and-metering","Art. 6 — Microgrid, storage, distribution and metering","","1. Each Circle 1 manages a local microgrid that can be isolated in sections, equipped with overload protection and with a human shutdown control accessible in accordance with the Safety Sheet.\n2. The metering of consumption is permitted exclusively for the technical purposes of safety, energy balance, maintenance and verification of the Basket.\n3. The following measurements are permitted on an ordinary basis:\na) aggregate data per installation;\nb) aggregate data per building;\nc) aggregate data per microgrid.\n4. High-frequency individual metering aimed at the profiling of behaviour or of personal life is prohibited.\n5. Metering at the level of the dwelling unit is permitted only where it is necessary to guarantee the Basket or safety and provided that:\na) the data are not published at individual level;\nb) the data are aggregated over a period not shorter than that strictly necessary;\nc) technical measures are adopted to prevent re-identification;\nd) the case of a dwelling occupied by a single person is handled in such a way that the data cannot be traced back to that person’s personal life;\ne) the rules on minimisation, access and retention of Code 7, Article 2.2, are applied.\n6. The granularity, the retention periods and the persons authorised to have access are defined in the STV. The purpose and the necessity of the metering shall be reviewed periodically.\n7. Short-duration storage may include stationary LFP batteries or equivalent, thermal storage and hydraulic storage, according to technical and environmental compatibility.\n8. Storage capacity is sized according to the Basket and the requirements of levels 1–3. It may not be aimed at speculation or individual accumulation to the detriment of common availability.\n9. No Circle may depend structurally on a single other Circle for the supply of the Energy Basket.\n10. Interconnections between microgrids are used for mutual aid in emergencies. Every interconnection shall be accompanied by an electrical Safety Sheet."],[13,"art-6-bis--water-cycle","Art. 6-bis — Water cycle","","1. Each Circle 1 manages, as far as possible locally, abstraction, rainwater harvesting, storage, treatment to drinking-water standard, distribution, the collection and purification of wastewater, and reuse. Every installation has an STV and a Safety Sheet.\n2. The quality of drinking water is checked periodically in accordance with the Drinking Water STV, adopted with the binding opinion of the Health Council. The results shall be published. In the event of non-conformity, distribution shall be suspended without delay and an alternative source shall be activated.\n3. Withdrawals shall remain within the recharge capacity of the water bodies and shall guarantee the ecological flow, pursuant to Article 14.6 of the Constitution. New abstractions require the mandatory opinion of the Biodiversity Council and of the Habitat and Territory Council.\n4. Wastewater shall be purified and, where possible, reused. The reuse of water and of purification residues is permitted only if it complies with the applicable STV and with the opinion of the Health Council.\n5. The metering of water consumption follows the rules of Article 6, paragraphs 2 to 6. Individual profiling is prohibited.\n6. Where technically possible, two independent sources and a reserve equal to at least seventy-two hours of the Water Basket shall be provided, consistently with Article 39 of Code 1.\n7. Where several Circles depend on the same river basin, withdrawals shall be coordinated in the competent Circle 2 or Circle 3, with a Dossier. No Circle may deprive the Circles downstream of the water necessary for their Basket.\n8. Water dependencies, including connection to external networks during the transition, shall be entered in the Dependency Register in accordance with Article 8."],[13,"art-7--safety-ppe-and-training","Art. 7 — Safety, PPE and training","","1. No activity relating to the production, transformation, distribution, maintenance or shutdown of energy may be carried out without a Safety Sheet that is in force and adequate.\n2. The Energy and Water Council validates the technical aspects of the installation, the electrical risks and the isolation and shutdown procedures. The Health Council gives the mandatory opinion on the health aspects, on the PPE within its competence and on the consequences for health. The competences are exercised separately and coordinated in the same Sheet or in linked documents.\n3. Electrical PPE is provided free of charge and managed as a commons in accordance with the coordination between the competent Storehouses, Code 6 and Code 14. It shall be maintained and tracked. The absence of a necessary item of PPE entails the suspension of the activity concerned and does not constitute a breach attributable to the person to whom the PPE was not provided.\n4. L1 training in electrical safety is mandatory for access to the installations under the conditions defined by the Safety Sheet.\n5. L2 competence is required for installation and specialist maintenance; L3 competence is required for the design and validation of the STVs.\n6. Anyone who detects a serious and immediate electrical risk may interrupt the activity and report it, and may not suffer any detriment for having acted in good faith. The procedures of Code 14 apply."],[13,"art-8--register-of-energy-dependencies","Art. 8 — Register of energy Dependencies","","1. The Energy and Water Council feeds the Dependency Register provided for in Code 15, distinguishing at least:\na) energy dependencies: external sources, transitional fossil fuels or external networks necessary for the Basket;\nb) technological dependencies: proprietary inverters, closed BMS systems, closed software, imported critical components or unverifiable firmware;\nc) logistical dependencies: materials or components not available within the Confederation or not repairable locally;\nd) water dependencies: external sources, networks or treatments necessary for the Water Basket.\n2. Every critical dependency shall be accompanied by an Exit Plan indicating:\na) the objective;\nb) the person responsible;\nc) the fixed final deadline;\nd) the continuity measures;\ne) the risk-reduction measures during the transition.\n3. Priority is given to the plans relating to dependencies that affect the Energy Basket or functions of levels 1 and 2.\n4. Energy autonomy shall be subject to a federated audit at least annually by independent persons with L3 competence, selected by sortition. The outcome shall be recorded and published in a form compatible with the security of infrastructure."],[13,"art-9--prohibitions-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) generate rent from the essential energy of the Basket;\nb) impose paywalls on life-saving energy STVs;\nc) patent the essential knowledge necessary for local self-production;\nd) accumulate energy individually beyond the technical limits resolved upon by the Assembly, where the accumulation compromises the availability of the Basket or the balance of the microgrid;\ne) interrupt the Energy Basket or the Water Basket as a sanction;\nf) cede functional control of the microgrid to a party external to the Confederation.\n2. Breaches of paragraph 1 constitute a serious procedural defect pursuant to Code 4. The suspension or deactivation of the non-compliant function shall be proportionate and accompanied, where technically possible, by the continuity of levels 1–3 in accordance with the Safety Sheet.\n3. Total shutdown shall be ordered only where necessary to prevent further harm. For critical or vital functions, a continuity procedure and a compliant substitute function shall be activated, where technically possible.\n4. An appeal against the decision may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days on grounds of procedural defect, manifest technical unreasonableness, discrimination or disproportion. Code 4 applies."],[13,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-linkages","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and linkages","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Articles 18 and 19 for the Basket and scarcity and Article 32 for the hierarchy of priorities;\nb) Code 2, for the STVs;\nc) Code 3, for resolutions and the urgency procedures;\nd) Code 4, for Guarantee and appeals;\ne) Code 5, for the validation of L2 and L3 competences in energy matters;\nf) Code 6, for PPE, health and health safety;\ng) Code 7, for servers, the Network and non-surveillance pursuant to Article 2.2;\nh) Code 12, for the protection of frail persons;\ni) Code 14, for general safety;\nj) Code 15, for the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of the entry into force, each Circle 1 shall:\na) draw up the energy balance;\nb) take a census of the existing installations;\nc) prepare a provisional STV and a minimum Safety Sheet for installations that lack them;\nd) guarantee the minimum Energy Basket, including by means of a safe transitional source.\n3. Within twelve months, each Circle 1 shall activate a minimum microgrid that can be isolated in sections, short-duration storage for the requirements of levels 1–3 and a Network node with autonomous or emergency power supply.\n4. Within twenty-four months an Exit Plan from fossil sources shall be prepared for the uses of levels 4 and 5 and for critical dependencies. Every plan shall indicate a fixed final deadline.\n5. Anyone who manages energy installations on the date of entry into force may retain a provisional validation in order to guarantee continuity:\na) for up to six months for functions corresponding to L3;\nb) for up to twelve months for functions corresponding to L2.\nThe conditions of Article 10, paragraph 3, of Code 5 apply.\n6. Existing functions or installations that breach Article 9 shall be brought into compliance or deactivated within ninety days.\n7. If immediate deactivation compromises a vital function of level 1 or 2, the Assembly shall approve within the same period a Substitution Plan or a plan for progressive deactivation, with a fixed final deadline and continuity measures."],[14,"art-1--subject-matter-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter and legal nature","","1. This Code governs the agroecological production, processing, preservation and distribution of food as infrastructural commons necessary for the Basket, health, care and the energy continuity of the Community.\n2. For the purposes of this Code, agroecological production means:\na) cultivation;\nb) custody of pollinators and non-exploitative collaboration with animals, within the limits of Article 6-bis;\nc) sustainable wild harvesting;\nd) basic food processing;\ne) food preservation;\nf) composting and management of soil fertility;\ng) management of agricultural water;\nh) custody and reproduction of seeds.\n3. The following are inalienable infrastructural commons:\na) the fertile soil pertaining to Circle 1;\nb) the local infrastructure for the abstraction, storage and distribution of agricultural water, without this entailing appropriation of the water resource as such;\nc) the seed fund of the Food Basket;\nd) essential agroecological knowledge;\ne) the infrastructure of the pollinator refuges and the pollination function.\n4. Pollinators are not goods. For the purposes of this Code:\na) wild populations constitute a non-appropriable ecological common good and are protected by Code 13;\nb) the colonies hosted in the refuges are animal communities in non-exploitative cohabitation pursuant to Article 13.3 of the Constitution: they may not be the object of ownership or of reproductive manipulation, and the harvesting of their products is permitted only under the conditions of Article 6-bis, paragraph 5;\nc) refuges, custodial hives and equipment are common infrastructure.\nProtection covers the pollination function, the populations and their habitats.\n5. The products of the Basket are commons. The tools necessary for their production are infrastructural commons and are not commodities within the meaning of this Code and of C.1-Annex A. The manufacture, repair and standardisation of the tools are governed by C.1-Annex A, which maintains their classification as non-commodifiable infrastructural commons.\n6. This Code pursues the following purposes:\na) to guarantee federated food sovereignty and the Food Basket;\nb) to favour local, seasonal and regenerative supply chains;\nc) to prevent rent on essential food, fertile soil, seeds, agricultural water and the pollination function;\nd) to guarantee food safety, traceability, biodiversity and animal welfare."],[14,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Food sovereignty. Food sovereignty means the federated capacity to guarantee the Food Basket through local production and the federated network, avoiding structural dependencies on external parties and maintaining mutualism and interconnection between Circles. Each Circle 1 shall pursue a basic food production capacity.\n2. Agroecological priority. Natural fertility, rotations, intercropping, the closing of cycles and regenerative soil management shall take precedence over recourse to intensive methods.\n3. Genetically modified organisms and hybrids.\na) For production intended for levels 1–3 of the Basket, the use of genetically modified organisms is prohibited, even where they are not subject to exclusive ownership.\nb) For levels 4–5, proprietary genetically modified organisms and hybrids with technological or contractual sterility that generate proprietary dependency are prohibited.\nc) The use, in levels 4–5, of non-proprietary open-pollinated genetically modified organisms is permitted only subject to a prior derogation by the General Assembly in accordance with the procedure of Article 5, paragraph 7, even where no proprietary dependency exists.\n4. Phytosanitary treatments.\na) Only the use of treatments provided for by a Phytosanitary STV in force is permitted.\nb) Persistent synthetic pesticides, systemic products, systemic chemical herbicides and systemic insecticides are prohibited, even if contemplated by a previously applicable STV."],[14,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","c) Mechanical, physical and biological treatments, natural extracts and biological control are preferred, where provided for by the STV.\nd) In the event of an extreme phytosanitary emergency, the Assembly may resolve on the temporary use of non-persistent and non-systemic synthetic products, indicating the duration, tracking, Exit Plan and date of the review.\nThe Phytosanitary STV is a necessary condition for every treatment. The prohibitions of letter b) prevail over the STVs; the derogation of letter d) is temporary and does not permanently modify the STV.\n5. Priority of use. The hierarchy of Article 32 of Code 1 applies. For agri-food purposes, the uses are:\na) level 1: Food Basket, life-saving seeds and essential pollination;\nb) level 2: collective canteens and nutrition of frail persons;\nc) level 3: food safety, preservation, protection of the pollinator refuges and animal welfare;\nd) level 4: List A CBO;\ne) level 5: other uses.\nIn the event of scarcity, reductions shall be applied progressively from level 5 to the higher levels, in compliance with Article 32 of Code 1. For water in the event of scarcity only Article 10, paragraph 8, applies: the minimum water necessary for the survival of animals and pollinators has the same priority as that necessary for the crops of level 1."],[14,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","6. Open access to agroecological knowledge. For every Basket variety, production technique, preservation recipe and STV relating to pollinators for levels 1–4, complete and freely reproducible documentation shall be made available, including the STV provided for by Code 2. It is prohibited to patent the seeds of the Basket and the essential preservation knowledge identified by the STVs.\n7. Transparency. Each Circle shall maintain an inventory of seeds, a map of agricultural soils, an inventory of pollinator refuges and an aggregated, non-profiling food balance.\n8. Ecological reversibility. Activities shall avoid soil degradation, favour the maintenance or increase of fertility and contribute to the stability or growth of pollinator populations."],[14,"art-3--agroecological-council","Art. 3 — Agroecological Council","","1. Each Circle 1 shall establish an Agroecological Council composed of three to five members with L2 or L3 competences in agroecology, agronomy, seed custody, food preservation, pollinator protection or food safety.\n2. Its members shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall hold office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office.\n3. The Agroecological Council:\na) manages the Food Basket and the Food Storehouse;\nb) draws up and updates the Safety Sheets for processing, preservation and activities at the pollinator refuges;\nc) is responsible for the STVs relating to crops, foods and pollinator refuges in accordance with Code 2;\nd) manages the food balance, soil fertility and pollination activities;\ne) organises L1 training in food safety and pollinator protection;\nf) feeds data into the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15;\ng) coordinates its activity with the Health Council provided for by Code 6, the Energy and Water Council provided for by Code 8, the Habitat and Territory Council provided for by Code 10, the Production Council provided for by C.1-Annex A, the Care Council provided for by Code 12 and the Biodiversity Council provided for by Code 13.\n4. The Agroecological Council has no allocative powers over goods outside the agri-food sphere and no disciplinary powers. It may not access individual health data or moderate deliberative or political content.\n5. The acts of the Council are divided into:\na) bound technical acts: the implementation of STVs or Safety Sheets already in force, documented and published in accordance with the applicable access levels, without the need for a new resolution;\nb) discretionary proposals and decisions: adopted in accordance with the consensus method provided for by Code 3, subject to prior publication of the proposal, a period of at least seventy-two hours for the submission of objections and the recording of the outcome in the minutes.\n6. Silence does not constitute consent if the proposal has not been published or if the period for objections has not elapsed."],[14,"art-4--food-basket-stocks-and-resilience","Art. 4 — Food Basket, stocks and resilience","","1. The Food Basket in the strict sense is part of the Sufficiency Basket governed by Code 1. It comprises minimum quantities of food available per person and defined on the basis of requirements, including staple cereals, legumes, seasonal vegetables, fruit, oil, basic preserves, foods for weaning and for frail persons and foods intended for the canteens. The Food Basket is guaranteed on a plant basis; products of animal origin enter it only under the conditions of Article 6-bis, paragraph 4.\n2. The Food Basket is defined in quantities and autonomy criteria, including nutritional values and days of stock. Access may not be made conditional on the CBO, on competences or on physical presence.\n3. The unavailability of the food necessary for a vital function constitutes a level 1 emergency in accordance with Code 1.\n4. Infrastructural stocks, which comprise seeds, stored agricultural water, fertility, compost, containers and food for the animals living with the Community, shall be kept distinct from the Food Basket. Such stocks are managed by means of a federated inventory and the Stocks STV.\n5. The resilience thresholds comprise safety margins for pollination, soil fertility, cultivated biodiversity and plant cover. They are indicators of ecosystem resilience and do not constitute a share of the Food Basket.\n6. The resilience thresholds are defined in the Resilience STV, with the opinion of the Biodiversity Council provided for by Code 13, and shall be reviewed at least annually.\n7. The minimum list of the Basket and the related thresholds are determined by the Assembly on a proposal from the Agroecological Council, with the opinion of the Health Council on the aspects concerning the nutrition of frail persons. Criteria and quantities are published in the Food Basket STV.\n8. The operational fulfilment of requests shall follow, within the limits of the hierarchy of Code 1, this order:\na) Food Basket, life-saving nutrition and essential pollination;\nb) collective canteens and canteens for frail persons;\nc) food preservation, seeds and protection of the pollinator refuges;\nd) tools for List A CBO;\ne) other reasoned requests.\n9. In the event of conflict between the operational order of paragraph 8 and the hierarchy of Article 32 of Code 1, the hierarchy of Code 1 prevails."],[14,"art-5--stvs-seeds-soil-sources-and-coordination-of-tools","Art. 5 — STVs, seeds, soil, sources and coordination of tools","","1. Every crop and processing process intended for levels 1–4 and every pollinator refuge shall have an STV in force. The STV shall indicate at least soil, variety, rotation, inputs, irrigation, preservation, risks and limits.\n2. The following resources and practices are favoured:\na) free and reproducible seeds;\nb) community compost;\nc) mature manure, under the conditions of Article 6-bis, paragraph 4;\nd) green manure;\ne) local biofertilisers;\nf) reclaimed water, where it is safe and permitted;\ng) locally adapted varieties;\nh) wood from sustainable local supply chains for the construction of custodial hives and refuges.\n3. For level 4–5 activities, a documented verification of the possibility of recovering surpluses, reusing food waste and repairing agricultural equipment and refuges is mandatory before any new acquisition is made. For levels 1–3 the verification is strongly preferred, unless reuse would compromise health safety, as certified by the Health Council. The verification shall be recorded in the STV.\n4. Self-production of tools is encouraged where validated L2 competences and standard repairable implements are available.\n5. The division of competence with C.1-Annex A is as follows:\na) C.1-Annex A governs the manufacture, repair, standardisation and inventory of hand tools, light ploughs, custodial hives, dryers, mills, oil mills, presses, irrigation tools and food containers;\nb) such tools retain their classification as non-commodifiable infrastructural commons;\nc) this Code governs the agroecological use of the tools, the related suitability criteria, the crop STVs and the Safety Sheets for use;\nd) this Code does not confer competence over the production of tools, which is reserved to C.1-Annex A.\n6. For levels 4–5 it is prohibited to introduce new systems that depend structurally on:\na) proprietary genetically modified organisms;\nb) hybrids with technological or contractual sterility;\nc) proprietary inputs that cannot be reproduced locally.\n7. A derogation from the prohibitions of paragraph 6 may be resolved by the General Assembly by qualified majority and shall provide cumulatively for:\na) a documented technical statement of reasons showing the impossibility of an alternative;\nb) a duration not exceeding twelve months for each resolution;\nc) a compensatory measure to protect free seeds and local pollinators;\nd) a binding Substitution Plan;\ne) a review at least every six months.\n8. The use of non-proprietary open-pollinated genetically modified organisms for levels 4–5 is permitted only by means of the derogation of paragraph 7, even where no proprietary dependency exists.\n9. The overall duration of the dependency authorised pursuant to this Article may not exceed twenty-four months, except in an extreme emergency resolved in accordance with Code 3. Article 2-bis of Code 6 applies only where the emergency is of a collective health nature."],[14,"art-6--fields-federated-network-territory-and-measurement","Art. 6 — Fields, federated network, territory and measurement","","1. Each Circle 1 shall activate a basic production capacity, scalable and sectionable, which comprises, depending on local characteristics:\na) proximity vegetable gardens;\nb) areas for cereals and legumes proportionate to requirements;\nc) orchards;\nd) spaces for the custody and reproduction of seeds;\ne) composting units;\nf) a kitchen for processing and preservation;\ng) a store for the Food Basket;\nh) a pollinator refuge, in accordance with Article 6-bis and Code 13.\n2. The designated use of soils, land take, ecological corridors, buffer zones for pollination and the georeferencing of pollinator refuges are governed by Code 10.\n3. This Code refers to Code 10 for the territorial map and governs the agricultural function within the areas designated for that use.\n4. The Confederation guarantees a minimum federated coverage of advanced competences and infrastructure, comprising, according to requirements, a seed bank, nursery, dryer, mill and oil mill. The coverage shall ensure every Circle 1 effective access within its own Circle 2, as a rule with one provision for every three or four Circles 1 of which it is composed, sized on the basis of population and territory.\n5. Access to the federated infrastructure is bookable and managed by means of a common inventory.\n6. Dispersed micro-production, such as vegetable gardens on balconies or roofs, shared building gardens and educational pollinator refuges, shall be registered with the Agroecological Council and shall comply with the applicable STV and Safety Sheet.\n7. Measurement is permitted only for technical purposes of food safety, food balance, soil fertility, verification of the Basket and the state of the pollinator refuges. Data shall be aggregated by Circle 1 or by Circle 2.\n8. Individual profiling of behaviour is prohibited. Code 7, Article 2.2, and the related rules on minimisation and access apply."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","1. General principle. Non-human animals are sentient, free and protected beings; they are not commodities or means of production, pursuant to Article 13 of the Constitution. The rearing of animals for purposes of exploitation, slaughter, killing and any practice that causes pain, fear or deprivation are prohibited. Only the non-exploitative collaboration of paragraph 3 and the harvesting without harm of paragraphs 4 and 5 are permitted.\n2. In the event of a conflict of interpretation between the agri-food function and animal welfare, the welfare parameters established by Code 13 prevail. In case of doubt the activity is not permitted. The distribution of water in the event of scarcity follows Article 10, paragraph 8.\n3. Collaboration in work.\na) An animal may help the Community only in light work: soil tillage and woodland management where the use of machines would degrade the soil or biodiversity, light transport, mowing and management grazing. Use for level 4–5 activities, competitions, entertainment or recreational draught is excluded.\nb) Collaboration is permitted only in activities compatible with the species, temperament and condition of the individual animal. Tools and methods that act through pain or fear are prohibited. An animal that shows refusal, fatigue or distress shall be left free from the activity."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","c) The load, the maximum daily and weekly hours, the minimum and maximum age, rest periods, climatic thresholds and signs of distress are defined in the Collaborating Animals Welfare STV, in accordance with Code 2. The STV is validated jointly by the Agroecological Council and the Biodiversity Council, with the opinion of the Health Council; the welfare parameters are approved by the Biodiversity Council with the authorisation of Article 13.3 of the Constitution, pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 5, of Code 13.\nd) Every animal shall have an individual Welfare Sheet, a register of hours and a periodic veterinary check. In the absence of a Sheet in force the collaboration shall be suspended without delay; care of the animal remains guaranteed.\ne) Water, shade, shelter, proportionate rest, rotation of tasks and a social life appropriate to the species are guaranteed. Work in extreme climatic conditions is prohibited.\nf) An animal that can no longer or must no longer collaborate is entitled to retirement and to care for its whole life, in the Community or in a refuge sanctuary. It may not be transferred for consideration or put down for convenience.\ng) Animals shall not be made to reproduce or acquired in order to obtain labour power. The animals that collaborate are those already present, those taken in and those born spontaneously in the Community."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","h) No function of levels 1–3 may depend exclusively on the work of animals: for each one a way of performing it without them shall be provided.\ni) Assistance to persons by animals is governed by Article 7 of Code 13.\n4. Products without harm.\na) It is permitted to harvest only what the animal produces spontaneously and what exceeds its own needs and those of its offspring and of the colony, where the harvesting causes no harm: in particular manure, wool whose shearing is necessary for the animal’s welfare, unfertilised eggs laid spontaneously, surplus milk and, for bees, what is provided for in paragraph 5.\nb) Killing, any taking that entails pain, fear, deprivation or a reduction in lifespan, separation from offspring, induced reproduction, selection aimed at productivity and increasing the number of animals for harvesting purposes are prohibited.\nc) The conditions and limits of each harvesting are defined in the Welfare Sheet and in the applicable STV, approved by the Biodiversity Council pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 5, of Code 13. In case of doubt the harvesting is not permitted.\nd) Harvesting does not constitute a production objective and is not planned into the Basket requirement. What is harvested shall be delivered to the Storehouse and distributed, when available, with priority to clinical needs and to frail persons.\ne) Soil fertility shall be ensured as a priority by compost, green manure, rotations and plant-based biofertilisers."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","5. Pollinators and custodial hives.\na) Each Circle 1 shall pursue the presence of pollinator refuges: flowering plants, hedgerows, nesting habitats and, where compatible with local conditions, custodial hives with locally adapted bees. The protection of pollination takes priority over any harvesting.\nb) Honey, wax and propolis may be harvested from the custodial hives only in excess of the reserve necessary for the colony, determined with a prudential margin by the Pollinators STV. Total removal is prohibited.\nc) The following are prohibited: artificial feeding, except for a rescue intervention certified by the Biodiversity Council for the time strictly necessary; artificial insemination and the killing of queens; wing clipping; migratory beekeeping; persistent synthetic acaricides; lines subject to patent, licence or technological sterility.\nd) Intervention on colonies is permitted for rescue, health protection, prevention of risks to persons and harvesting pursuant to letter b), in accordance with the Pollinators STV. The STV is adopted in accordance with Code 2, with the approval and the opinion of the Biodiversity Council provided for by Article 6, paragraph 6, of Code 13 and with the mandatory opinion of the Habitat and Territory Council.\ne) The Pollinators STV defines the reserve necessary for the colony, the distances, the buffer zones and the radius of protection from treatments, in coordination with Code 10."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","f) Every refuge shall have a Safety Sheet, shall be entered in the inventory and shall be georeferenced in accordance with the access rules of Code 10. Pollination data shall be aggregated and non-profiling, in conformity with Code 7, Article 2.2.\ng) The Agroecological Council shall record dependencies on external pollination in the Dependency Register of Code 15.\nh) L1 training is mandatory in order to work at the custodial hives; L2 is required for harvesting and for rescue interventions on colonies.\n6. Animals present at accession. Working or farm animals and managed colonies present on the date of the Community’s accession shall remain with it in non-exploitative cohabitation or collaboration where the conditions of this Article are met, as verified by the Biodiversity Council within the time limits of Article 10; otherwise they shall be received in the refuge sanctuaries provided for by Code 13, pursuant to Article 13.4-bis of the Constitution. They may not be transferred for consideration, slaughtered, put down or made to reproduce for purposes of use. Every prohibited practice shall cease without delay."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","7. Foods of animal origin. The Food Basket is guaranteed on a plant basis and is nutritionally adequate without products of animal origin, including the necessary supplementation; the Agroecological Council and the Health Council are answerable for this. Meat and any food that requires the killing of or harm to an animal shall not be produced in the Confederation. Foods harvested pursuant to paragraphs 4 and 5 shall be distributed in accordance with paragraph 4, letter d). During the transition, the other foods of animal origin shall be progressively replaced in accordance with a Food Substitution Plan approved by the Assembly with the opinion of the Health Council, pursuant to Article 13.5 of the Constitution. The foods and medical aids required by individual clinical needs certified in accordance with Code 6 remain guaranteed, until adequate alternatives are available.\n8. Coordination. The Agroecological Council shall coordinate activities:\na) with the Biodiversity Council of Code 13 for animal welfare parameters and for sanctuaries;\nb) with the Health Council of Code 6 for food safety, nutrition and zoonoses;\nc) with C.1-Annex A for equipment;\nd) with Code 10 for territorial matters."],[14,"art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators","Art. 6-bis — Animals and pollinators","","9. Measurement and violations. Measurement is permitted only in order to verify welfare, hours of collaboration, the state of the colonies and the quantities harvested. Violation of the provisions of this Article shall be assessed in accordance with Code 4. The non-compliant activity shall be suspended without delay, the animals involved shall be made safe and the function shall be performed without animals."],[14,"art-7--safety-nutrition-and-training","Art. 7 — Safety, nutrition and training","","1. No processing or preservation activity and no intervention at the pollinator refuges may be carried out without a Safety Sheet in force. For collaboration with animals and for interventions on colonies, a Welfare Sheet in force in accordance with Code 13 is also required.\n2. The Agroecological Council validates the technical safety aspects of the activity. The Health Council gives the mandatory opinion on health risks, preservation, allergens, the nutrition of frail persons, stings and zoonoses. The Biodiversity Council gives the mandatory opinion on animal welfare aspects.\n3. Mandatory opinions do not transfer to the Council consulted the competence for the technical validation of the act, which remains with the Agroecological Council, except for the Collaborating Animals Welfare STV and the Pollinators STV provided for by Article 6-bis, paragraph 3, letter c), and paragraph 5, letter d), for which the Biodiversity Council approves the welfare parameters pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 5, of Code 13.\n4. PPE and equipment for food safety and for interventions at the pollinator refuges are commons, supplied free of charge, maintained and tracked by the competent Storehouses in accordance with Codes 1, 6 and 14. Their absence entails the suspension of the activity concerned.\n5. L1 training in food safety and pollinator protection is mandatory for access to the processing kitchens and to the custodial hives. L2 is required for the management of fields, of advanced preservation and of rescue interventions on colonies. L3 is required for agroecological design and the validation of STVs.\n6. Anyone who detects an immediate risk to health, animal welfare or the survival of a colony may interrupt the activity and report it, and may not suffer detriment for having acted in good faith. Codes 6 and 14 apply."],[14,"art-8--agroecological-dependency-register","Art. 8 — Agroecological Dependency Register","","1. The Agroecological Council shall feed data into the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15 and shall distinguish at least:\na) material dependencies: seeds, fertility or other essential materials coming from outside;\nb) technological dependencies: proprietary inputs, closed systems, non-reproducible varieties, patents or technological sterility;\nc) water, logistics or pollination dependencies: water, supply chains, storage or pollination services not available in the Confederation.\n2. Every critical dependency shall be accompanied by an Exit Plan with a person responsible, an objective, intermediate measures and a fixed final deadline. Dependencies relating to the Basket, seeds and pollination have priority.\n3. Food autonomy shall be subject to a federated audit, at least annually, by independent L3 persons selected by sortition.\n4. The audit shall be conducted in accordance with the following rules:\na) the auditors shall be drawn by lot from a federated list of L3 competences in agroecology, biodiversity and health, kept by the Guarantee Circle in accordance with Code 4;\nb) no audit shall be carried out on the Circle to which the auditor belongs or on a Circle in which the auditor has carried out activity in the last twenty-four months;\nc) the office shall not exceed thirty days;\nd) the report shall be published in accordance with the applicable access levels; the Circle concerned has fifteen days in which to submit observations;\ne) an appeal may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle in accordance with Code 4;\nf) if a critical dependency is established, a binding Exit Plan may be prescribed within the limits of the competences conferred by Codes 4 and 15;\ng) findings, challenge, remedies and consequences shall be coordinated with Codes 4 and 15."],[14,"art-9--prohibitions-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) generate rent on the Food Basket, on the seeds of the Basket, on common fertile soil, on the essential infrastructure for agricultural water, on the pollinator refuges or on the pollination function;\nb) impose paywalls on the STVs for life-saving seeds, Basket preserves, pollinator protection or plant health;\nc) claim or maintain, within the Confederation, patents or other titles of exclusive appropriation over the seeds of the Basket or over the essential preservation knowledge identified by the STVs for levels 1–4;\nd) accumulate seeds or food individually beyond the technical limit resolved, where this compromises the availability of the Basket;\ne) deny Basket food as a sanction;\nf) transfer to a party external to the Confederation control of the seed bank, of the Basket fields or of the pollinator refuges;\ng) use treatments prohibited by Article 2, paragraph 4, letter b), which admit no derogation; the products of letter d) of the same paragraph are used only under its conditions;\nh) kill or mistreat animals, or use them or harvest their products outside the conditions of Article 6-bis;\ni) artificially inseminate or kill queens or introduce lines of bees subject to proprietary restrictions.\n2. The prohibition referred to in paragraph 1, letter c), concerns the exclusive appropriation of goods and knowledge within the confederal system.\n3. Violations shall be dealt with in accordance with Code 4. The suspension or deactivation of the non-compliant function shall be proportionate and accompanied, where technically possible, by the continuity of the level 1–3 functions in accordance with the Safety Sheet.\n4. For critical or vital functions, the suspension shall be accompanied, where possible, by a continuity procedure and by a compliant substitute function.\n5. An appeal may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days on grounds of procedural defect, manifest technical unreasonableness, discrimination or disproportion. Code 4 applies."],[14,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and links","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Articles 18 and 19 for the Basket and scarcity and Article 32 for the hierarchy of priorities;\nb) Code 2, for the STVs;\nc) Code 3, for resolutions;\nd) Code 4, for Guarantee, appeals and remedies;\ne) Code 5, for L2 and L3 competences in agroecology and pollinator protection;\nf) Code 6, for health and nutrition;\ng) Code 7, Article 2.2, for the Network and non-profiling;\nh) Code 8, for energy and water;\ni) C.1-Annex A, for production and workshops;\nj) Code 10, for habitat and territory;\nk) Code 12, for the care of frail persons;\nl) Code 13, for biodiversity and sanctuaries;\nm) Code 14, for general safety;\nn) Code 15, for the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, each Circle 1 shall:\na) carry out a census of the soils, vegetable gardens, seeds, managed bee colonies and animals present;\nb) activate a minimum basic production with provisional STVs and minimum Safety Sheets;\nc) guarantee the minimum Food Basket, including by means of the recovery of surpluses.\n3. Within twelve months, each Circle 1 shall:\na) establish the Agroecological Council;\nb) activate a minimum seed bank, a pollinator refuge and the federated inventory;\nc) adopt the definitive STVs for levels 1–2;\nd) prepare, with the Biodiversity Council, the verification of the conditions of the animals present, the related Welfare Sheets, the reception plan for those that cannot remain and the Safety Sheets of the pollinator refuges;"],[14,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and links","","e) activate a node of the Federated Agroecological Network in accordance with Code 7;\nf) coordinate the transition with Code 8, C.1-Annex A and Code 10.\n4. In the event of a documented overlap of the transitional burdens relating to Codes 8, 9 and 10, the Assembly may grant a reasoned extension, not exceeding six months, for the activation of the pollinator refuge and of the seed bank. The extension shall be accompanied by a binding plan and may not interrupt the continuity of the Basket.\n5. If the time limit of paragraph 3 expires without the mandatory infrastructure having been activated, the matter shall be dealt with in accordance with the guarantee procedures of Code 4, with a binding Compliance Plan and measures to ensure the continuity of the Basket.\n6. Paragraph 9 applies to already existing functions, management methods, livestock farms or apiaries that violate Article 9 or Article 6-bis.\n7. Anyone managing fields on the date of entry into force may retain a provisional validation in order to guarantee continuity:\na) for up to six months for functions corresponding to L3;\nb) for up to twelve months for functions corresponding to L2.\nThe conditions of Article 10 of Code 5 apply."],[14,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and links","","8. Agricultural water and water in the event of scarcity. Abstraction, storage and distribution intended for agriculture shall be coordinated with Code 8 and with Code 10, which governs territorial designations. In the event of scarcity the available water is distributed according to the following priorities, which apply to every use of water, including those governed by Codes 8 and 13:\na) drinking water for human use and for health and care services, within the guaranteed minimum quota of Article 4-bis, paragraph 3, of Code 8;\nb) with equal priority, the minimum water necessary for the survival of the animals that live with the Community, including those received in sanctuaries, and of pollinators, and the minimum water necessary to keep the crops intended for level 1 of Article 2, paragraph 5; if the water is not sufficient for both, it is distributed in proportion to the minimum need of each;\nc) all other uses, which are reduced first."],[14,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and links","","The minimum need of letter b) is established by the Energy and Water Council with the Agroecological Council and the Biodiversity Council. Before an animal is left without the water necessary for survival, the Biodiversity Council requests through the Pool of Code 15, as an immediate vital need within the meaning of its Article 8, paragraph 2, letter a), the missing water or the transfer of the animal to another Community or to a sanctuary that has the necessary water; the transfer is necessary for welfare within the meaning of Article 4-bis, paragraph 2, of Code 13. Until the Balance provided for by Code 15 is active, the transfer is attempted directly with another Community or with a kindred external body, such as a refuge or a sanctuary, by written agreement. To prevent scarcity, the number of animals kept or received by the Community, including sanctuaries, and of the pollinator colonies kept is planned by the Biodiversity Council, with the Energy and Water Council, on the basis of the water available in dry years; animals beyond that number are received through other Communities or sanctuaries that have the necessary water. For the purposes of Article 32 of Code 1, the abstraction and distribution of the water of letters a) and b) are vital emergencies of letter a) of its paragraph 1 and follow the order of this paragraph."],[14,"art-10--transitional-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions and links","","9. Existing non-compliant functions. Already existing functions, management methods, livestock farms and apiaries that violate Article 9 or Article 6-bis shall be brought into compliance within ninety days. For livestock farms and apiaries, compliance consists in the cessation of every practice prohibited by Article 6-bis and in the verification provided for by its paragraph 6. If bringing a function into compliance is not technically possible, the deactivation procedure shall be initiated within the same time limit, comprising:\na) verification of compliance;\nb) documented establishment of the technical impossibility of compliance;\nc) initiation of the deactivation procedure;\nd) a Substitution Plan with a person responsible and a fixed final deadline.\nIf immediate deactivation compromises a vital function of level 1 or 2, the Assembly shall approve within ninety days a binding plan for progressive substitution or deactivation, with a fixed deadline and continuity of the Basket.\n10. Exit plans. Within twenty-four months the Exit Plans from critical dependencies relating to seeds and inputs of the Basket shall be completed, in accordance with the Dependency Register of Article 8 and Code 15. Each plan shall indicate a fixed final deadline."],[15,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","1. This Code governs:\na) the intended use of land;\nb) land take;\nc) the regeneration of habitats;\nd) the protection of ecological corridors;\ne) the establishment of buffer zones for pollination and health;\nf) the federated Land Register;\ng) the georeferencing of common infrastructure;\nh) the subsoil, deposits and the inventory of recoverable materials.\n2. For the purposes of this Code:\na) habitat: means the set of territorial components and functions, including fertile land, built-up land, degraded land, agricultural land that is temporarily not fertile, semi-natural areas, ecological corridors, buffer zones, wetlands, proximity woodlands and territorial infrastructure for the catchment and storage of agricultural water;\nb) gross land take: means the occupation of fertile land through building or infrastructural transformation or through soil sealing, irrespective of any compensation;\nc) net land take: means gross land take net of the areas regenerated and restored to an equivalent fertile functionality in the same territorial Basin and in the reference period. The equivalence and the period are defined by the Regeneration STV provided for by Article 5, paragraph 3. The period is, as a rule, annual;\nd) irreversible occupation: means a transformation of land that entails permanent sealing, removal of the fertile layer or a loss of functionality that cannot be restored without a structural regeneration intervention;"],[15,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","e) reversible occupation: means a temporary use of land that preserves the fertile layer in full and allows the complete restoration of the functionality defined by the Regeneration STV within twenty-four months. If restoration does not take place within that time limit, the occupation shall be classified as gross land take with effect from the initial date of the occupation;\nf) mineral resources: means metals, minerals and rocks in their natural state, underground or outcropping, and those contained in landfills and in waste deposits from past extraction, until they are recovered; they do not include water, which is governed by Code 8;\ng) deposit: means a concentration of mineral resources that has been ascertained and entered in the Land Register;\nh) territorial Basin: means the territory of the Circle 2 to which the Circle 1 concerned belongs. It does not coincide with the river basin, which for water is governed by Article 6-bis, paragraph 7, of Code 8.\n3. The following are inalienable infrastructural commons:\na) the fertile land pertaining to Circle 1;\nb) the federated ecological corridors;\nc) the federated Land Register;\nd) the buffer zones for pollination and health;\ne) the semi-natural territorial bank;\nf) the subsoil and mineral resources. This qualification does not restrict the meaning of the words “land” and “soil” used by the Constitution."],[15,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","4. The territorial classification of land is determined on the basis of its ecological and territorial function, as recorded in the federated Land Register. It does not depend on the previous use of the land or on any claims of individual ownership.\n5. Classification as fertile, agricultural, semi-natural, degraded or temporarily non-fertile land, corridor, buffer zone or built-up area does not, by itself, determine any classification as to ownership.\n6. The classification of agricultural land as temporarily not fertile is permitted only where there is a reasoned technical record by the Agroecological Council, with the mandatory opinion of the Habitat and Territory Council. The record shall indicate the causes, the plan for restoring fertility and the time limit for review, which shall not exceed twelve months and may be extended once only.\n7. Until the positive outcome of the review and a subsequent resolution on the intended use, the presumption of agricultural use shall persist and transformation to building use is prohibited."],[15,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Objective of zero net land take. Net take of fertile land is prohibited. Every irreversible occupation of fertile land shall be compensated in the same territorial Basin by an equivalent regeneration, in accordance with Article 4, paragraph 4, and the Regeneration STV. The rules on gross land take are established by Article 5, paragraph 1.\n2. Priority of regeneration. Reuse, recovery, ecological densification and de-sealing have priority over any new building.\n3. Federated ecological connection. Every Circle 1 shall guarantee at least one functional ecological connection to the federated network, consisting of one or more ecological corridors. Width, continuity, vegetation cover and functionality are defined by the Habitat STV.\n4. Buffer zone for pollination. A territorial buffer zone shall be established around the pollinator refuges provided for by Article 6-bis of Code 9. Within it, the prohibitions of treatment provided for by Code 9, Article 2, paragraph 4, letter b), and the prohibitions provided for by Article 6-bis, paragraph 5, letter c), of the same Code shall apply.\n5. The territorial prohibitions shall apply even where a treatment is otherwise contemplated by an STV, except for the derogations expressly provided for by Code 9 and by this Article.\n6. A derogation from the prohibitions in the buffer zone is permitted only in the event of an extreme plant health emergency provided for by Code 9, Article 2, paragraph 4, letter d), and only for the products permitted there. The prohibitions of letter b) of that paragraph may not be derogated from. A rescue intervention on colonies follows Article 6-bis, paragraph 5, letter c), of Code 9 and does not require a derogation.\nThe derogation shall be resolved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Agroecological Council, after agreement with the Habitat and Territory Council and after obtaining the mandatory opinion of the Biodiversity Council. The decision shall indicate the duration, the tracking, the Exit Plan and the manner of recording in the node of the Federated Agroecological Network.\n7. Territorial transparency. Territorial information shall be managed in accordance with the access levels of Article 7 and with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n8. Reversibility. Every territorial intervention shall include a plan for reversibility and restoration of fertility, proportionate to the nature of the intervention."],[15,"art-3--habitat-and-territory-council","Art. 3 — Habitat and Territory Council","","1. Every Circle 1 shall establish the Habitat and Territory Council, composed of three to five members with L2 or L3 competences in pedology, ecology, ecological urban planning, cartography or regeneration.\n2. The members shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office.\n3. The Habitat and Territory Council shall:\na) draft and update the territorial map and the Land Register;\nb) verify land take and the actual implementation of compensations;\nc) define ecological corridors and buffer zones;\nd) attend to the georeferencing of the pollinator refuges, the fields of the Basket, the seed banks and the agricultural water infrastructure provided for by Codes 8 and 9;\ne) propose and update the Habitat, Soil Regeneration and Corridors STVs;\nf) feed the Dependency Register of Code 15 for the territorial dependencies referred to in Article 8, paragraph 5;\ng) coordinate its activity with the Energy and Water Council of Code 8, the Agroecological Council of Code 9, the Biodiversity Council of Code 13 and the Production Council of C.1-Annex A;\nh) keep the inventory of recoverable materials provided for by Article 8-bis.\n4. The acts of the Council are divided into:\na) bound technical acts, adopted in execution of an STV in force;\nb) discretionary proposals and decisions, adopted in accordance with the consensus method provided for by Code 3, after publication for at least seventy-two hours.\n5. The opinions of the other Councils are mandatory where this Code so provides. The mandatory opinion does not transfer to the Council consulted the deliberative or technical competence over the act, except in the cases of joint validation or binding opinion expressly provided for.\n6. In the event of conflict between opinions, the Council competent for the act shall document the positions and give reasons for the decision, applying the specialist parameters in accordance with the following allocation:\na) the Biodiversity Council of Code 13 determines the parameters of biodiversity, animal welfare and protection of colonies;\nb) the Agroecological Council of Code 9 determines the parameters of agroecological use, crops, pollinator refuges and the requirement of the Basket;\nc) the Energy and Water Council of Code 8 determines the parameters of functional management of water, quality and maintenance of the network;\nd) the Safety Council of Code 14 determines the parameters of general safety;\ne) the Habitat and Territory Council determines the parameters of intended use, location, land take, Land Register and territorial constraints.\n7. The allocation in paragraph 6 does not confer on a Council competence over aspects assigned to another and does not exempt from the obligation of coordination where a decision involves several parameters."],[15,"art-4--intended-use-and-territorial-map","Art. 4 — Intended use and territorial map","","1. Every Circle 1 shall adopt and keep up to date a territorial map containing at least:\na) agricultural land of the Basket;\nb) agricultural land that is temporarily not fertile pursuant to Article 1, paragraphs 6 and 7;\nc) built-up land and land capable of regeneration;\nd) degraded land;\ne) ecological corridors;\nf) buffer zones;\ng) woodlands, wetlands and semi-natural areas;\nh) pollinator refuges, fields, seed banks and infrastructure provided for by Codes 8 and 9;\ni) portions entrusted by means of an Autonomous Coexistence Pact and the share reserved pursuant to Article 4-bis.\n2. The intended use shall be resolved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Habitat and Territory Council, with:\na) the mandatory opinion of the Agroecological Council for agricultural areas;\nb) the mandatory opinion of the Biodiversity Council for ecological corridors and buffer zones.\n3. The opinions do not transfer the deliberative or technical competence, except for the express exceptions of this Code.\n4. A change of intended use from fertile agricultural land to building land is permitted only if all the following conditions are jointly met:\na) the absence of alternatives based on reuse or regeneration is demonstrated and documented;\nb) an equivalent territorial and ecological compensation is prepared in accordance with the Regeneration STV;\nc) the compensation ensures, separately and cumulatively, the equivalence of the fertile function and that of the ecological function;\nd) the opinion of the Agroecological Council has been obtained on the loss of the fertile function and on the compensation of letter c); a negative opinion is binding for the protection of ecological limits, pursuant to Article 8 of Code 3, and constitutes an express exception to the general rule of Article 3, paragraph 5;\ne) the General Assembly approves the change by qualified majority.\n5. The resolution shall indicate the location, extent, duration, compensations, persons responsible and means of verification."],[15,"art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","Art. 4-bis — Autonomous Coexistence Pact","","1. This Article implements Articles 4.9, letter c), 4.9-bis, 4.9-ter and 7.3-bis of the Constitution. The Autonomous Coexistence Pact is concluded between the non-confederated person, or their nucleus, and Circle 1. For Circle 1 it is concluded by the Assembly, on a proposal from the Habitat and Territory Council and in accordance with Article 4, paragraph 2, within thirty days of the request. The Pact may not be refused to anyone who accepts its conditions, except where the carrying capacity is insufficient as documented pursuant to paragraph 4. An appeal pursuant to Article 9, paragraph 4, may be brought against the refusal, against the act of paragraph 4 and against the identification of the portion.\n2. The Pact shall indicate at least:\na) the portion of land and, where available, the dwelling entrusted in custody of use, with the boundaries recorded in the Land Register;\nb) the ecological constraints: the prohibitions of Article 9 of this Code, of Article 9, paragraph 1, letters a), g), h) and i), of Code 9 and of Article 10 of Code 13, and the buffer zones and ecological connectivity of Articles 2 and 6;\nc) compliance with the disarmament provided for by Article 16 of the Constitution;\nd) access to drinking water, pursuant to Article 4-bis of Code 8, to the water necessary for the portion and to life-saving emergency care;"],[15,"art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","Art. 4-bis — Autonomous Coexistence Pact","","e) the absence of ordinary withdrawals from the Storehouses and the arrangements for any exchange of real surpluses, without profit and without attribution of CdR;\nf) for the minors of the nucleus, access to care, to learning and to the protection of Codes 5, 6 and 12 on the same conditions as minor members;\ng) the arrangements for verification and revision.\nThe CBO and the other obligations reserved for members do not apply to a person living by means of a Pact; the conditions of the Pact and the rules that the Constitution and the Codes address to anyone who is in the territory apply.\n3. The portion is recorded in the territorial map in the category of Article 4, paragraph 1, letter i). It remains an inalienable Common Good and returns to the custody of the Circle at the end of the use. The Pact does not constitute a transfer of control within the meaning of Article 9, paragraph 1, letter f), of Code 9.\n4. The carrying capacity is documented by the Habitat and Territory Council, with the mandatory opinion of the Agroecological Council and of the Energy and Water Council, in a public act that indicates the land and water necessary for the self-sufficiency of the person or the nucleus and those available. The Pact may not be refused on grounds of carrying capacity until the share of paragraph 5 has been entirely entrusted."],[15,"art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","Art. 4-bis — Autonomous Coexistence Pact","","5. Every Circle 1 shall reserve for the Pact a share of land, recorded in the map, sufficient for the self-sufficiency of at least one person for every hundred residents or fraction of a hundred. Until it is entrusted, the share may be used by the Community only by reversible occupation and shall be made available within ninety days of the conclusion of the Pact. The obligation is accepted with the act of accession provided for by Article 38.1 of the Constitution and does not derive from a power of a higher Circle."],[15,"art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","Art. 4-bis — Autonomous Coexistence Pact","","6. If the Pact is refused on grounds of carrying capacity, Circle 1 shall inform without delay Circle 2, which within thirty days, through its spokespersons and on the basis of the maps, shall identify a Circle 1 with an available share, taking account of the preferences of the person and of proximity. The Circle 1 identified shall conclude the Pact on its own share pursuant to paragraph 1. If the shares of Circle 2 are exhausted, the case passes to Circle 3, with the same obligation and the same time limit. Where the requirement of a nucleus exceeds the share of each Circle 1, the portion may be formed from shares of neighbouring Circles 1; the Pact is concluded by the Circle 1 that makes the larger part of it available. If no sufficient share is available even in Circle 3, the portion is assigned on the first share that becomes available, in the order of the requests, and the Circle 1 whose share has been entirely entrusted shall reconstitute it within twelve months, within the limits of carrying capacity."],[15,"art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","Art. 4-bis — Autonomous Coexistence Pact","","7. From the request for the Pact until the handover of the portion, the person who accepts its conditions exercises the right of residence of Article 4.9, letter c), of the Constitution: the fourteen-day limit of temporary hospitality does not apply to them. The Circle 1 in which they are shall ensure them housing in a Bridge House or in the equipped stopping area, access to the Basket according to actual requirements and CBO in mobility where compatible, in accordance with Article 6-ter of Code 11. The reception is provisional, is limited to the time necessary for the handover and ceases with it. It also ceases if the person refuses without justification the portion identified pursuant to paragraphs 1 or 6 or withdraws acceptance of the conditions; Article 32-bis, paragraph 2, of Code 4 then applies.\n8. The Pact is of indefinite duration. It ceases upon withdrawal by the person, which is always possible, upon their entry into or return to the Confederation, upon non-use of the portion continued for more than twelve months without justification, as ascertained in accordance with Code 4, or upon relocation pursuant to Article 32-bis of Code 4. Cessation does not prevent a request for a new Pact. Violation of the conditions is dealt with in accordance with Code 4 and may give rise only to the revision provided for by Article 32-bis of that Code."],[15,"art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","Art. 4-bis — Autonomous Coexistence Pact","","9. During the transition the Pact is concluded in the forms permitted by the law in force and includes acceptance of Chapter V of Code 4, pursuant to its Article 37, paragraph 1. Until a Basin exists, the search of paragraph 6 is carried out by the pilot Circle with the other pilot Circles and with the available kindred entities; in the meantime paragraph 7 applies.\n10. For a person who ceases to be a member pursuant to Article 7.3-bis of the Constitution, the portion is assigned by the Guarantee Tribunal, pursuant to Article 29, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 4, on the share of paragraph 5. The Habitat and Territory Council identifies it and records it in the Land Register. The portion is assigned without a dwelling, even if a dwelling is available, and without tools or other goods of the Community; the person may build a shelter on it by their own efforts, in compliance with the constraints of paragraph 2, letter b). Paragraph 2, letters b) to g), and paragraphs 3, 6 and 8 apply; paragraph 7 does not apply, because the person remains a member until the handover. Upon readmission the portion returns to the share."],[15,"art-5--land-take-and-regeneration","Art. 5 — Land take and regeneration","","1. Net take of fertile land is prohibited. Gross land take is permitted only if accompanied by a compensation plan approved pursuant to Article 4, paragraph 4, which guarantees a net balance equal to zero in the reference period.\n2. The reversible occupation defined by Article 1, paragraph 2, letter e), does not constitute gross land take if the land is restored within the time limit provided for.\n3. The Regeneration STV shall govern at least:\na) de-sealing techniques;\nb) light remediation;\nc) the increase of organic matter;\nd) proximity reforestation;\ne) the equivalence parameters for compensations;\nf) the reference period and the means of verification.\n4. An inventory of degraded land is established. Regeneration has priority for the areas necessary for the functions of the Basket of level 1–3.\n5. Incompatible previous interventions existing on the date of entry into force shall be recorded in the map and indicated as such. They shall be subject to a compliance or regeneration plan with a definite final deadline pursuant to Article 10, paragraph 7. Inclusion in the compliance pathway does not automatically entail a territorial sanction."],[15,"art-6--ecological-corridors-and-buffer-zones","Art. 6 — Ecological corridors and buffer zones","","1. Every Circle 1 shall activate and maintain the ecological connection provided for by Article 2, paragraph 3.\n2. The buffer zone for pollination has a minimum radius of five hundred metres from the pollinator refuge.\n3. The Pollinators STV provided for by Article 6-bis of Code 9 may extend the radius on the basis of exposure, winds and the crops present. It may reduce it only where there are documented morphological characteristics, within a technical limit defined by the same STV and in any event not less than two hundred metres. The reduction requires a technical statement of reasons and the mandatory opinion of the Biodiversity Council.\n4. Anyone who intends to carry out a permitted treatment in the zone shall notify the intervention to the Agroecological Council and to the Habitat and Territory Council at least forty-eight hours in advance, recording it in the node of the Federated Agroecological Network provided for by Article 7.\n5. The notification shall indicate at least the location, product, dose, reason and duration. It constitutes an information obligation and not a prior authorisation. The silence of the Councils is equivalent neither to authorisation nor to refusal.\n6. In the event of a plant health emergency resolved pursuant to Code 9, the forty-eight-hour time limit may be derogated from. The reasoned notification shall be recorded immediately or, if technically impossible, at the first available opportunity.\n7. The Habitat and Territory Council shall verify the notification with the support of the Agroecological Council. Failure to notify entails the suspension of the treatment and the activation of the procedures provided for by Code 4.\n8. For the fields of the Basket of level 1–2 a buffer zone for health is established, in which the polluting activities identified with the mandatory opinion of the Health Council of Code 6 are prohibited."],[15,"art-7--federated-land-register-and-georeferencing","Art. 7 — Federated Land Register and georeferencing","","1. The federated Land Register is established, comprising inventories and information on land, ecological corridors, buffer zones, pollinator refuges, agricultural water infrastructure, deposits and recoverable materials. Public data are aggregate and non-profiling, in conformity with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n2. The Land Register provides for the following access levels:\na) public level: an aggregate map with a level of detail that does not allow the identification of persons, single refuges or individual fields or sensitive infrastructure;\nb) technical level: precision georeferenced data, accessible exclusively to the Habitat and Territory, Agroecological and Biodiversity Councils and, for the inventory of recoverable materials, to the Production Council, for documented technical purposes of safety, planning or verification of the zones;\nc) federated interoperability: exchange of the necessary data between Circles with anonymisation and compliance with authorisations.\n3. Accesses to the technical level shall be recorded with an indication of the purpose. Unauthorised export is prohibited. The access logs shall be retained for twelve months.\n4. At every level the principle of the minimum detail necessary shall apply, so as to prevent individual identification and to protect the security of sensitive infrastructure.\n5. Every Circle 1 shall activate a node of the Federated Agroecological Network in accordance with Code 7. The node shall feed the federated inventory and record the notifications provided for by Article 6, paragraph 4."],[15,"art-8--agricultural-water-territory-and-dependencies","Art. 8 — Agricultural water, territory and dependencies","","1. The competences relating to agricultural water are allocated as follows:\na) Code 10 governs the location of territorial water infrastructure, including reservoirs, tanks, terracing, ditches and infiltration areas, and the related territorial constraints;\nb) Code 8 governs the functional management of water, the maintenance of the network, the functional priorities for drinking water and the quality aspects assigned to it;\nc) Code 9 governs the agricultural use of water and the determination of the requirement of the Basket.\n2. The priorities in the event of water scarcity provided for by Article 10, paragraph 8, of Code 9 shall be implemented on the basis of the territorial map and by decision of the Energy and Water Council of Code 8, with the mandatory opinion of the Agroecological Council. Where implementation reduces access to goods of the Basket, including the Water Basket, the decision belongs to the Assembly pursuant to Article 19, paragraph 3, of Code 1, on a proposal from the Energy and Water Council; in urgency the procedures of Code 3 apply. The reduction of the guaranteed minimum share follows Article 4-bis, paragraph 6, of Code 8.\n3. Code 10 does not decide the functional allocation of water.\n4. The sealing of the strategic infiltration areas identified in the territorial map is prohibited.\n5. Territorial dependency means a critical dependency on fertile land, ecological corridors, territorial accessibility, infrastructure or locations that are not substitutable, necessary for the maintenance of an essential function of level 1–3. Territorial dependencies shall be recorded in accordance with Code 15."],[15,"art-8-bis--raw-materials-recovery-and-inventory","Art. 8-bis — Raw materials: recovery and inventory","","1. The Habitat and Territory Council keeps in the Land Register, and updates every year, the inventory of recoverable materials present in the territory: disused buildings, vehicles and installations, landfills, waste from past extraction and materials deriving from the conversion provided for by Article 16.2 of the Constitution. The inventory is made available to the Production Council for the purposes of Articles 2 and 5 of C.1-Annex A.\n2. Deposits are Commons entrusted to the custody of the Community in whose territory they are located. Custody gives no entitlement to rent.\n3. The excavation of a landfill or of a deposit of waste from past extraction, in order to recover its materials, requires jointly, without prejudice to the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3:\na) the mandatory opinions of the Habitat and Territory, Biodiversity, Energy and Water and Health Councils;\nb) a General Safety Sheet pursuant to Code 14;\nc) a restoration plan approved before the start, whose hours are counted together with those of the recovery, and a plan for the non-recoverable part in conformity with Article 14.5 of the Constitution;\nd) the resolution of the Assembly of the Community in whose territory the site is located; if the excavation may affect waters or ecological corridors common to several Communities, the General Assembly decides.\n4. The excavation of paragraph 3 is prohibited in ecological sanctuaries and in areas that are candidates to become such, in wetlands and in strategic infiltration areas. It is not permitted before Codes 13 and 14 are activated. Excavation in conformity with paragraph 3 is use in conformity with a Sheet in force for the purposes of Article 6, paragraph 4, of Code 16. This Code and Code 13 remain in force.\n5. This Article does not authorise new extraction or the extension of existing extraction. Article 14.2 of the Constitution remains in force."],[15,"art-9--prohibitions-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) generate rent from common fertile land, from the subsoil, from mineral resources, from ecological corridors or from buffer zones;\nb) cause net take of fertile land without equivalent territorial and ecological compensation pursuant to Article 4, paragraph 4, and the Regeneration STV;\nc) interrupt the federated ecological connection provided for by Article 2, paragraph 3;\nd) conceal data that must be entered in the Land Register;\ne) carry out individual georeferencing for profiling purposes;\nf) transfer to parties external to the Confederation the control of the Land Register or of the map;\ng) carry out treatments in the buffer zone without the notification provided for by Article 6, paragraph 4.\n2. Violation of paragraph 1 constitutes a substantial violation. Ascertainment, suspension, deactivation and procedural remedies are governed by Code 4.\n3. The suspension or deactivation of a territorial function may not compromise the level 1–2 functions of the Basket without the simultaneous activation of a Continuity Plan or of a technically compliant substitute function.\n4. An appeal may be brought before the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days on grounds of procedural defect, manifest technical unreasonableness, discrimination or disproportion."],[15,"art-10--transitional-provisions-links-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions, links and precedence","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) Code 1, Articles 18 and 19 for the Basket and scarcity and Article 32 for the hierarchy of priorities;\nb) Code 2, for the STVs;\nc) Code 3, for resolutions;\nd) Code 4, for the Guarantee and appeals;\ne) Code 5, for L2 and L3 competences in matters of habitat;\nf) Code 6, for health and health security;\ng) Code 7, Article 2.2, for the Network and non-profiling;\nh) Code 8, for energy and water;\ni) C.1-Annex A, for production;\nj) Code 9, for agroecology;\nk) Code 12, for care;\nl) Code 13, for biodiversity;\nm) Code 14, for safety;\nn) Code 15, for the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, every Circle 1 shall carry out a census of:\na) the land;\nb) the existing ecological corridors;\nc) the pollinator refuges and the managed bee colonies, with provisional georeferencing;\nd) degraded land and land that is temporarily not fertile;\ne) incompatible previous interventions.\nA provisional minimum Land Register shall also be activated.\n3. Within twelve months, every Circle 1 shall activate:\na) the Habitat and Territory Council;\nb) the territorial map and the minimum Land Register;\nc) the minimum ecological connection;\nd) the minimum buffer zones;\ne) the node of the Federated Agroecological Network;\nf) the share reserved for the Autonomous Coexistence Pact provided for by Article 4-bis, paragraph 5."],[15,"art-10--transitional-provisions-links-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions, links and precedence","","4. In the event of a documented overlap of the transitional burdens relating to Codes 8, 9 and 10, the Assembly may grant a reasoned extension not exceeding six months, accompanied by a binding plan and by measures that guarantee the continuity of the Basket.\n5. Once the time limit for the new mandatory infrastructure has expired without its having been activated, the non-compliance shall be dealt with in accordance with Code 4 and entails the preparation of a binding plan.\n6. Within ninety days of entry into force, existing activities, infrastructure, intended uses and other territorial arrangements that violate Article 9 shall be brought into conformity or the deactivation procedure shall be initiated. The procedure comprises:\na) a conformity check by the Habitat and Territory Council;\nb) documented ascertainment of the technical impossibility of compliance, with the opinion of the Council competent for the subject matter;\nc) initiation of the deactivation procedure by the Assembly, on a proposal from the Habitat and Territory Council;\nd) a Substitution Plan with a definite final deadline and a guarantee of continuity of the Basket in accordance with Code 4.\n7. For the incompatible previous interventions recorded pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 5, a compliance or regeneration plan with a final deadline not exceeding twenty-four months shall be adopted within twelve months. Interruptions of ecological corridors and the sealing of infiltration areas have priority."],[15,"art-10--transitional-provisions-links-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions, links and precedence","","8. Inter-Code precedence clause. In the event of overlap, each Code prevails exclusively as regards the parameter within its own competence:\na) Code 10 as regards intended use, location, gross and net land take, the Land Register, georeferencing and territorial constraints;\nb) Code 9 as regards agroecological production and the agricultural use of territorial infrastructure;\nc) Code 8 as regards the functional management of water;\nd) Code 13 as regards the parameters of biodiversity and animal welfare;\ne) Code 14 as regards general safety.\nThe clause does not transfer competences and does not exclude mandatory coordination between the Councils involved."],[16,"art-1--subject-matter-and-definitions","Art. 1 — Subject matter and definitions","","1. This Code governs the organisation, procedures and responsibilities relating to:\na) the physical movement of material goods;\nb) the delivery of the Basket and of common supplies;\nc) the basic mobility necessary for access to the fundamental rights and services of the Confederation;\nd) the mobility of persons, Bridge Houses and hospitality, pursuant to Articles 11 and 12 of the Constitution.\n2. The following fall within the scope of this Code:\na) the delivery of the Basket, of PPE and of the supplies of the Health Storehouse;\nb) the transport of spare parts, components and tools necessary for the continuity of the Network provided for by Code 7, of the energy provided for by Code 8 and of the production provided for by Code 9;\nc) the basic mobility of frail persons within the meaning of Code 12, limited to access to the Basket, to health services and to List A CBO, where such access cannot be secured independently on foot or by bicycle.\n3. This Code pursues the following purposes:\na) to guarantee the delivery of the Basket without logistics rent;\nb) to reduce and eliminate critical dependencies on fossil fuels and external couriers for level 1–4 services;\nc) to favour reuse, repair and light vehicles.\n4. For the purposes of this Code, the following definitions apply:\na) basic logistics infrastructure: Logistics Basket depots, cargo bikes, hand carts, repair kits, exchange points and Basket lockers. It is an inalienable common good of Circle 1, which may be neither monetised nor transferred;\nb) shared advanced vehicle: an electric van or motorised trailer managed in federated form. It is a federated common good that guarantees effective access and not ownership by the individual Circle;\nc) Logistics Basket: the set of procedures for delivering the Basket to the home or to an accessible exchange point, in accordance with established times and priorities;\nd) Logistics STV: Technical Validation Sheet, within the meaning of Article 3-bis of Code 2, relating to a vehicle, a type of route, a load category or a delivery procedure;\ne) levels: levels 1 to 5 correspond to letters a) to e) of Article 2, paragraph 4. The expressions “Basket of level 1–2” and “services of level 1–2” mean the delivery of Basket 1-2 defined by Article 32, paragraph 1-quinquies, of Code 1."],[16,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Hierarchy of means. For every delivery, the least impactful means suitable for meeting the need shall be used, in the following order:\na) travel on foot;\nb) human-powered bicycle or cargo bike;\nc) pedal-assist bicycle or cargo bike, powered by the microgrid provided for by Code 8;\nd) federated shared electric vehicle.\n2. Recourse to a means of a higher level shall be reasoned and recorded with reference to the distance, the weight or volume of the load, the frailty of the recipient or safety requirements.\n3. Reuse before new acquisition. Before a vehicle or tool is acquired, the availability of reusable, repairable or adaptable alternatives in the Circle or in neighbouring Circles shall be verified.\n4. Service priorities. Without prejudice to Article 32 of Code 1, logistics allocation shall follow this order:\na) Basket, PPE and supplies of the Health Storehouse intended for frail persons within the meaning of Code 12;\nb) Basket intended for the rest of the population;\nc) spare parts necessary for the continuity of the critical infrastructure for Network, energy and production;\nd) tools and materials for List A CBO;\ne) other commons.\n5. The priorities referred to in paragraph 4 are coordinated with Articles 18, 19 and 32 of Code 1 and with Codes 8 and 9. In the event of conflict, Article 32 of Code 1 prevails. The spare parts of letter c) of paragraph 4 precede the delivery of the Basket only with the derogation of Article 32, paragraph 3, of Code 1.\n6. Non-profiling transparency. The Delivery Register contains data aggregated by category, time slot and outcome, without individual identifiers. It is managed in the Network in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2. It is prohibited to track persons for purposes other than guaranteeing the service and the technical management of deliveries.\n7. Powering of electric vehicles. Electric vehicles intended for ordinary services shall be powered by the microgrid of Code 8 in accordance with the applicable priorities and allocation limits.\n8. In the event of a documented emergency, interruption of the microgrid or material impossibility, the temporary use of an external energy source is permitted exclusively in order to guarantee the continuity of the level 1–2 Basket. The use shall be documented, limited in time, accompanied by a plan for return to the microgrid and recorded in the Dependency Register of Code 15. It shall cease when the microgrid becomes available again."],[16,"art-3--logistics-council","Art. 3 — Logistics Council","","1. Each Circle 1 shall establish a Logistics Council composed of three to five members with validated L2 or L3 competences in light logistics, bicycle and cargo bike mechanics, road safety or load handling.\n2. Its members shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall hold office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office.\n3. The Logistics Council:\na) manages the Logistics Basket depot and the basic fleet;\nb) draws up and updates the Logistics STVs and the Safety Sheets within its competence;\nc) is responsible for the logistics part of the Dependency Register and feeds data into the Register provided for by Code 15;\nd) coordinates its activity with the Production, Energy and Water, Network and Health Councils;\ne) proposes to the Assembly the operational thresholds of the Logistics Basket;\nf) coordinates the shared vehicles for the mobility of persons and the management of the Bridge Houses.\n4. The Logistics Council has no allocative powers over goods outside logistics and no disciplinary powers. It may not access individual health data.\n5. The Council may not moderate, remove or modify deliberative or political content on grounds of merit, orientation or dissent.\n6. The acts of the Council are divided into:\na) bound technical acts: the implementation of an STV or Safety Sheet in force, documented and published in accordance with the applicable access levels;\nb) discretionary proposals and decisions: adopted in accordance with the consensus method provided for by Code 3, subject to prior publication of the proposal, a period of not less than seventy-two hours for the submission of objections and the recording in the minutes of the outcome and of the reasons.\n7. Silence does not constitute consent if the proposal has not been published or if the prescribed period has not elapsed."],[16,"art-4--logistics-basket-and-delivery-times","Art. 4 — Logistics Basket and delivery times","","1. Each Circle 1 shall guarantee the ordinary service standards established in the Logistics Basket STV:\na) delivery of the Basket to frail persons within the meaning of Code 12 within twenty-four hours of the request;\nb) delivery of the Basket to the rest of the population within seventy-two hours of the request.\n2. The time limits run from the recording of the request in the Network provided for by Code 7. If the Network is not available, the request shall be recorded by an alternative procedure and entered in the Network when the service is restored.\n3. Material impossibility. In the event of documented material impossibility, simultaneous emergency, interruption of the Network or unavailability of energy, the Logistics Council shall:\na) record the impediment, the date, the cause and the goods involved;\nb) activate without delay federated mutualism with the neighbouring Circles;\nc) guarantee continuity by means of a substitute procedure and a recovery plan with a fixed deadline.\n4. Failure to deliver a level 1–2 service, where it is not documented and federated mutualism has not been activated, shall be reported to the competent bodies for assessment in accordance with Codes 1, 3 and 4.\n5. The Logistics Basket may not be made subject to the recipient’s ability to collect it. For level 1–2 services, delivery to the home or to an accessible exchange point is guaranteed.\n6. The operational thresholds, including the days of autonomy of the depot, the minimum number of cargo bikes and the stock of reusable parcels, shall be resolved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Logistics Council, with the mandatory opinion of the Health Council and publication of the criteria."],[16,"art-5--stvs-reuse-open-sources-and-proprietary-dependencies","Art. 5 — STVs, reuse, open sources and proprietary dependencies","","1. Logistics STV. Every vehicle, type of route, load category and delivery procedure shall have an STV in force.\n2. The STV shall indicate at least:\na) maximum mass and volume of the load;\nb) type of route and permitted gradients;\nc) PPE required;\nd) loading and unloading procedures;\ne) risks and limit weather conditions;\nf) maintenance and competences required.\n3. Each individual delivery shall be recorded in accordance with the standardised procedure and shall refer to the applicable STV. It does not require a new STV to be drawn up.\n4. Verification of reuse.\na) For levels 4–5, a documented verification of the availability of reusable or repairable bicycles, cargo bikes, carts or vehicles is mandatory before a new good is acquired.\nb) For levels 1–3 reuse is strongly preferred. Derogation is permitted only for certified safety requirements or for documented unavailability of suitable alternatives.\n5. Open sources. The STVs and the Logistics Safety Sheets shall be published in the Network provided for by Code 7 under a free licence. For levels 1–4 they may not be subject to a paywall.\n6. Critical proprietary dependencies. For levels 1–4 it is prohibited to introduce a vehicle or logistics component intended for the Basket that depends structurally:\na) on proprietary components that cannot be repaired locally;\nb) on closed, non-inspectable firmware;\nc) on a mandatory external cloud service;\nd) on a single supplier with no practicable alternative.\n7. A reasoned transitional derogation, of a duration not exceeding twelve months, is permitted only in order to guarantee the continuity of the Basket, only for a dependency that the STV classifies as residual and surmountable pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), of Code 2, and with:\na) a binding Exit Plan;\nb) a fixed final deadline;\nc) publication of the decision;\nd) the opinion of the Production Council and of the Network Council, where relevant.\n8. Extension is prohibited, except in the case of documented material impossibility and a new reasoned decision of the competent body."],[16,"art-6--logistics-infrastructure","Art. 6 — Logistics infrastructure","","1. Basic logistics. Each Circle 1 shall have at least:\na) a Logistics Basket depot with shelving and reusable parcels;\nb) two cargo bikes, hand carts and a complete bicycle repair kit;\nc) a notice board for logistics requests on the Network;\nd) Basket lockers in residential buildings or proximity exchange points;\ne) a load register and a maintenance register.\n2. The minimum equipment may be met through the reuse of existing goods if they comply with the applicable STVs and Safety Sheets.\n3. Federated advanced logistics. The Confederation guarantees every Circle 1 effective access to a shared electric vehicle within a reasonable territorial catchment area.\n4. As a rule, this guarantee is ensured by at least one shared electric van for every three or four Circles. Number and capacity are sized on the basis of population, territory, Basket demand and energy capacity. The guarantee concerns effective access and does not entail ownership or the permanent presence of the vehicle in every Circle.\n5. The advanced vehicle may be booked through the Network and is accompanied by a vehicle STV and a specific Safety Sheet. Driving it requires validated L2 competence. Consumption and charging are tracked in accordance with Code 8.\n6. Ordinary charging takes place on the microgrid provided for by Code 8, except for the emergency derogation of Article 2, paragraph 8.\n7. Distributed nodes. The logistics network comprises:\na) exchange points at building and block level;\nb) standardised reusable Basket parcels, defined with the Production Council in accordance with Code 1 and C.1-Annex A;\nc) a traceable procedure of request, acceptance, delivery and confirmation that uses only the necessary logistics metadata.\n8. Redundancy. No Circle may depend structurally on a single other Circle for the delivery of the Basket. Each catchment area shall provide for at least two alternative logistics routes, where technically possible."],[16,"art-6-bis--mobility-of-persons","Art. 6-bis — Mobility of persons","","1. Freedom of movement is guaranteed. Mobility is a right and not an industry, pursuant to Article 11 of the Constitution.\n2. For the travel of persons, the following order shall be followed, according to the distance and the condition of the person:\na) travel on foot and by bicycle;\nb) shared light vehicles of Circle 1;\nc) caravans and coastal transport under sail of Circle 2;\nd) electric and hydrogen railway network of Circle 3.\n3. No type of vehicle is prohibited as such: the limits depend on the energy available and on carrying capacity. Air transport is permitted exclusively for medical or civil protection emergencies.\n4. Shared vehicles may be booked through the Network or by a non-digital procedure. Access does not require CdR where the travel is necessary for care, health, the CBO, learning, participation in deliberations, functions conferred by sortition, relocation or an emergency. Frail persons are given priority.\n5. For non-essential mobility with limited availability, booking and the waiting list apply; any use of CdR shall follow Article 45 of Code 1.\n6. The rule whereby, ordinarily, eighty per cent of annual mobility takes place within the Circle 3 to which a person belongs, provided for by Article 12.2 of the Constitution, is a planning criterion verified on aggregated data. It does not constitute an individual limit and does not authorise the tracking of persons’ movements.\n7. Visits and tourism are regulated on the basis of carrying capacity and ecological impact. Each Assembly shall determine each year its own reception capacity, in compliance with Article 12.2 of the Constitution. For-profit accommodation facilities are prohibited."],[16,"art-6-ter--bridge-houses-and-hospitality","Art. 6-ter — Bridge Houses and hospitality","","1. Each Circle 1 shall allocate to Bridge Houses from ten to fifteen per cent of its housing, pursuant to Article 12.1 of the Constitution. Bridge Houses are Commons; their location follows Code 10. Voluntary rotation in their management does not constitute additional CBO.\n2. Bridge Houses may be booked through the Network or by a non-digital procedure, according to actual availability.\n3. Hospitality does not require CdR where it is due to care, emergency, relocation pursuant to Article 49 of Code 1, transit towards another Circle, CBO in mobility, functions conferred by sortition or learning, and where it concerns non-confederated persons pursuant to Article 4.9 of the Constitution. Ordinary non-essential hospitality may require the use of CdR in accordance with Article 45 of Code 1.\n4. Hospitality between members has an ordinary minimum duration of fourteen days, except in cases of care, emergency, relocation or transit. Hospitality for non-confederated persons shall not exceed fourteen days and may not be extended. This is without prejudice to the reception of persons awaiting placement under the Autonomous Coexistence Pact, provided for by Article 4-bis, paragraph 7, of Code 10.\n5. Guests shall not ordinarily exceed fifteen per cent of residents, except for care, emergency, relocation or transit.\n6. The exhaustion of the Bridge Houses may not prevent entry. The Community shall ensure an equipped stopping area and access to the Storehouse according to actual requirements.\n7. CBO in mobility may be requested at the host Community only if it is compatible with the person’s condition and is counted towards that person’s CBO.\n8. The guest’s Basket is ensured by the Storehouse of the host Community; differences are offset through the Balance provided for by Code 15.\n9. The hospitality register contains only aggregated and non-profiling data, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2."],[16,"art-6-quater--federated-logistics-of-circles-2-and-3","Art. 6-quater — Federated logistics of Circles 2 and 3","","1. The shared advanced means of Article 6, paragraphs 3–5, the caravans and the coastal transport under sail of Circle 2 are managed by the shared Logistics Council of Circle 2, constituted pursuant to Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 3. The Council is responsible for their custody, maintenance, STVs and Safety Sheets, the booking provided for by Article 6, paragraph 5, and by Article 6-bis, paragraph 4, and the registers of loads and maintenance.\n2. The time of use of the means of paragraph 1 is distributed among the Communities according to the rule resolved by the General Assembly of Circle 2, on a proposal from the shared Logistics Council and in compliance with the priorities of Article 2, paragraph 4. Until that resolution the following rule applies:\na) requests of the priorities of Article 2, paragraph 4, letters a) and b), and journeys that do not require CdR pursuant to Article 6-bis, paragraph 4, are served first, in order of registration;\nb) the other requests are served in order of registration; where they exceed the availability for the month, no Community obtains more than the share proportional to the number of its members while requests from other Communities remain.\nThe application of the rule is a bound technical act. No distribution may take from a Community the effective access guaranteed by Article 6, paragraph 3.\n3. The railway network of Circle 3 and the transport provided for by Article 10 of Code 15 follow a railway protocol of Circle 3, approved by its General Assembly as a Technical Annex pursuant to Article 19 of Code 3 and implemented by the shared Logistics Council of Circle 3, constituted pursuant to the same paragraph 6-bis. Recourse to external carriers is entered in the Dependency Register."],[16,"art-7--safety","Art. 7 — Safety","","1. No logistics shift may be carried out without a Safety Sheet in force.\n2. The Logistics Council validates the aspects relating to the vehicle, the route and the logistics procedures. The Health Council gives the mandatory opinion on loads, lifting, fatigue, PPE and weather conditions. The competences of the Safety Council provided for by Code 14 apply.\n3. Logistics PPE, including helmets, gloves, jackets and load restraint systems, is supplied by the competent Storehouses in accordance with the coordination between Codes 1, 6 and 14. It is free of charge, maintained and tracked.\n4. The absence of an item of PPE required by the Sheet entails the immediate suspension of the shift concerned and does not constitute a violation attributable to the person to whom the PPE was not supplied.\n5. The following are required:\na) L1 training in bicycle safety, load management and urban traffic for basic shifts;\nb) L2 training for driving electric vans, handling fragile loads, delivering the Health Basket and towing trailers.\n6. Accidents and near misses shall be recorded in accordance with the confidentiality rules of Codes 6, 7 and 14. The information published shall be anonymous and aggregated.\n7. After a significant accident or near miss, the STV shall be reviewed within thirty days, unless a serious risk requires immediate intervention."],[16,"art-8--logistics-dependency-register","Art. 8 — Logistics Dependency Register","","1. The Logistics Council shall feed data into the Dependency Register provided for by Code 15, distinguishing:\na) material dependencies: fuels, batteries, critical tyres and spare parts that cannot be sourced;\nb) technological dependencies: vehicles with closed firmware, proprietary diagnostics or remote locking;\nc) logistics dependencies: dependency on private external couriers, on a single external supplier or on non-federated infrastructure.\n2. Every critical dependency that affects the Basket shall be accompanied by an Exit Plan with a person responsible, resources, intermediate checks and a fixed final deadline.\n3. Dependencies that affect the delivery of the level 1–2 Basket have priority."],[16,"art-9--prohibitions-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) apply rents, tolls or surcharges to the delivery of the Basket, of PPE or of the supplies of the Health Storehouse;\nb) impose paywalls or proprietary licences on life-saving Logistics STVs and on Safety Sheets;\nc) claim patents or exclusive trade marks over the standard reusable Basket parcel;\nd) accumulate vehicles or logistics tools individually beyond the technical limits resolved by the Assembly, where this compromises the availability of the Basket, maintenance capacity or the balance of the workshops;\ne) refuse delivery of the Basket for disciplinary or punitive reasons;\nf) transfer to a party external to the Confederation operational control of the basic depot or of the basic fleet;\ng) use the Basket fleet for purposes unrelated to the priorities of Article 2, paragraph 4, without a resolution of the Assembly.\n2. Violation of paragraph 1 constitutes a serious procedural defect pursuant to Code 4.\n3. The suspension of the decision or of the shift shall be proportionate and accompanied by the obligation to guarantee the continuity of level 1–2 services by means of mutualism or a substitute solution.\n4. An appeal may be lodged with the Guarantee Circle within fourteen days, in accordance with Code 4."],[16,"art-10--transitional-and-final-provisions-and-links","Art. 10 — Transitional and final provisions and links","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) the Constitution, Articles 11, 12, 32 and 33;\nb) Code 1, Articles 18–19 for the Basket, Article 27 for safety and Article 32 for the priorities;\nc) Code 2, for the STVs;\nd) Code 3, for resolutions;\ne) Code 4, for Guarantee and appeals;\nf) Code 5, for L2 and L3 competences;\ng) Code 6, for health and PPE;\nh) Code 7, Article 2.2, for the Network and non-profiling;\ni) Code 8, Article 4, for the Energy Basket;\nj) Code 9;\nk) Code 12, for the protection of frail persons;\nl) Code 14, for general safety;\nm) Code 15, for the Dependency Register.\n2. Within three months of approval, each Circle 1 shall activate the basic logistics depot, the minimum two cargo bikes, the Network notice board and the maintenance registers, including by means of the reuse of existing compliant equipment.\n3. Within twelve months, effective access to a shared electric van shall be guaranteed in accordance with Article 6, in coordination with the transitional provisions of Codes 8 and 9.\n4. An extension not exceeding six months is permitted exclusively in the event of a documented overlap of the transitional obligations provided for by Article 10, paragraphs 2 and 3, of Code 8 and by Article 10, paragraphs 2 and 3, of Code 9. The extension requires a binding operational plan, a fixed final deadline and the continuity of the Basket.\n5. Within twenty-four months the Exit Plans from critical logistics dependencies that affect the level 1–2 Basket shall be completed.\n6. Vehicles or procedures that do not comply with Article 9 shall be brought into compliance or deactivated within ninety days. If deactivation compromises a vital function of the Basket, a continuity plan shall be prepared with a compliant substitute solution and a fixed final deadline."],[17,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-scope-of-competence","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and scope of competence","","1. This Code governs the organisation of community care, the taking into care of functional frailties, the management of care spaces and care instruments and the planning of the time necessary to guarantee essential services. These resources constitute infrastructural commons that serve the implementation of the Sufficiency Basket, the protection of health and access to housing.\n2. For the purposes of this Code:\na) community care means the set of activities of support for daily, relational and domestic life, of accompaniment, of educational support and of practical assistance that do not constitute clinical services;\nb) functional frailty means a temporary or permanent condition that limits the person’s autonomy in performing one or more essential functions included in levels 1 and 2 of the Basket, irrespective of the presence, the absence or the classification of a clinical diagnosis;\nc) Care Basket means the component of the Sufficiency Basket governed by Code 1 that comprises the minimum care time, access to care spaces and, where necessary, support with meals, with hygiene, with accompaniment and with protected rest;\nd) care spaces means the spaces and facilities intended, according to need and territorial planning, for the kitchen supporting frail persons, the common laundry, children from zero to three years of age, protected rest, non-clinical proximity assistance and temporary community reception;"],[17,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-scope-of-competence","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and scope of competence","","e) individual care plan means the confidential document that, on the basis of the functional need ascertained and with respect for the will of the person, identifies the services necessary, the parties responsible, the expected duration and the dates or conditions of review;\nf) Care Safety Sheet means the operational document that identifies the risks connected with a specific care activity and establishes the preventive measures, the competences required, the interruption procedures and the arrangements for substitution in the event of unavailability of an essential resource;\ng) Care STV means the Technical Validation Sheet, adopted in accordance with Article 3-bis of Code 2, that defines the standards, minimum quantities, procedures and operating arrangements of the services governed by this Code.\n3. The following are inalienable infrastructural commons:\na) the minimum care time necessary to guarantee levels 1 and 2 of the Basket;\nb) the essential care spaces of Circle 1 and their common equipment;\nc) the inventory of basic aids and non-clinical devices intended for community care;\nd) the essential knowledge of care and the related STVs and Safety Sheets;\ne) the aggregate balance of care time.\nThe management of these goods shall not entail exclusive appropriation, rent or unjustified exclusion of the persons entitled."],[17,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-scope-of-competence","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and scope of competence","","4. Code 12 governs exclusively, within its own subject matter, the organisation of community care, the ascertainment of functional frailty, the Care Basket, care time and the internal function of care spaces. The following remain assigned:\na) to Code 6, diagnosis, clinical services, clinical nutrition and other health acts;\nb) to Code 9, the production and preparation of food and the technical and organisational management of collective canteens;\nc) to Code C.1-Annex A, the manufacture and standardisation of aids;\nd) to Code 10, the intended use and territorial siting of the spaces;\ne) to Codes 1, 4 and 14, each for its own subject matter, the general provisions on the Basket, on guarantees and on safety.\n5. This Code pursues the following objectives:\na) to guarantee the continuity of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket even in the presence of functional frailty;\nb) to prevent the formation of rent on essential care services;\nc) to ensure a fair, verifiable and sustainable distribution of care time;\nd) to prevent isolation and unjustified recourse to forms of institutionalisation;\ne) to promote, where appropriate and desired by the person, the recovery or maintenance of autonomy."],[17,"art-2--principles-of-community-care","Art. 2 — Principles of community care","","1. Universality. Every person has the right to the essential services of the Care Basket corresponding to levels 1 and 2, without being made subject to individual contribution to the CBO and in accordance with the guarantees provided for by Code 1.\n2. Autonomy of functional frailty. Functional frailty constitutes a condition relevant for the purposes of community care, distinct from clinical diagnosis. Its ascertainment does not, in itself, require a health assessment. Clinical acts remain governed by Code 6.\n3. Proximity and continuity. Essential care shall be organised, as far as possible, within Circle 1. Services that require competences or facilities not available locally shall be guaranteed through the federated network referred to in Article 6.\n4. Priority and scarcity. The general priorities of the Basket are those established by Code 1, Article 32. In the event of insufficient care time, the reduction shall concern first the activities of the last level of the sequence of Article 4, paragraph 7, and shall proceed towards the higher levels, in compliance with the hierarchy of Code 1 and without prejudice to the essential services of levels 1 and 2. Within the applicable priorities, decisions shall take account of urgency, of the severity of the need and of the risk of interruption of care.\n5. Non-accumulation and non-monetisability. Essential care time shall not be monetisable, nor shall it be individually accumulable to such an extent as to take availability away from the other persons entitled. Any technical limit on accumulation shall be defined by means of an STV, on the basis of the actual capacity of the service and with public criteria.\n6. Transparency and confidentiality. The inventories of spaces and equipment, as well as the balances of care time, shall be published in aggregate and non-identifying form. The individual data necessary for the taking into care shall be processed exclusively for that purpose, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n7. Reversibility and review. Where the frailty is temporary, the individual care plan shall provide, if appropriate, for a pathway of support for autonomy and a time limit or condition for review. The review shall not result in the automatic interruption of essential services in the absence of a new assessment.\n8. Dignity, will and proportionality. Services shall be organised with respect for the dignity, the preferences and the will of the person. Limitations of autonomy are permitted only in the cases and with the guarantees provided for by the applicable legal order and by the competent Codes."],[17,"art-3--care-council","Art. 3 — Care Council","","1. Each Circle 1 shall establish a Care Council composed of three to five members, drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3. Its composition shall ensure adequate competences, of at least level L2 or L3, in the field of community care, support for children from zero to three years of age, advanced old age, disability and the organisation of proximity services.\n2. The members of the Council shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall remain in office for one year and shall not be drawn by lot again for the same Council before twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office. Replacement, the declaration of conflicts of interest and the minuting of decisions shall follow the procedures of Code 3.\n3. The Care Council shall:\na) propose and implement the Care Basket and coordinate the care spaces;\nb) draw up, update and apply the Safety Sheets relating to care activities, hygiene, early childhood and accompaniment;\nc) propose and update the Care STVs in accordance with the procedures of Code 2;\nd) plan the aggregate balance of care time and coordinate the shifts, with respect for individual freedom and for the competences of the participants;\ne) organise training at level L1 for basic care and at level L2 for the specialist activities provided for by Code 5;\nf) feed the Dependency Register governed by Code 15;\ng) coordinate the activities with the Health, Agroecological, Habitat, Production and Biodiversity Councils, each within its own competence.\n4. The acts of the Care Council are divided into:\na) bound technical acts, adopted in implementation of an STV or of a Safety Sheet in force;\nb) discretionary proposals and decisions, adopted in accordance with the consensus method and the procedures of Code 3, after publication for at least seventy-two hours, except in cases of reasoned urgency.\nPublication shall not include personal data or information that makes it possible to identify the persons taken into care.\n5. The mandatory opinions of the competent Councils do not transfer ownership of the decision. The decision shall be taken jointly only where a Code expressly so provides.\n6. In the absence of a Safety Sheet in force, the activity concerned shall not be started or continued in the ways that have not been assessed. The Care Council shall identify, where possible, a compliant substitute arrangement that ensures the continuity of levels 1 and 2. Every temporary derogation shall be reasoned, limited to what is strictly necessary and subject to verification."],[17,"art-4--care-basket-and-ascertainment-of-functional-frailty","Art. 4 — Care Basket and ascertainment of functional frailty","","1. The Care Basket of levels 1 and 2 comprises, according to the need ascertained:\na) the minimum care time;\nb) support during the meal, where the person is not autonomous in eating;\nc) assisted hygiene;\nd) essential accompaniment;\ne) protected rest;\nf) access to the space for children from zero to three years of age, where necessary.\n2. The Care Basket is quantified principally in hours of assistance per person per week. The Care Basket STV shall define at least the services included, the minimum standards, the arrangements for daily distribution, the criteria of urgency, the response times and the review procedures. The standards shall not be reduced in such a way as to compromise levels 1 and 2 of the Basket.\n3. The report of a need may be submitted by the person concerned or by anyone who detects a need for care. The report:\na) initiates the functional assessment;\nb) allows, in the presence of an essential need, the provisional activation of the services necessary for a maximum period of seventy-two hours, pending the assessment;\nc) shall not be used to initiate clinical treatments or to assign a diagnosis.\nIn the presence of an immediate risk to the life, health or physical safety of the person, the emergency procedures of the competent Codes shall be activated without delay."],[17,"art-4--care-basket-and-ascertainment-of-functional-frailty","Art. 4 — Care Basket and ascertainment of functional frailty","","4. The Care Council shall assess the functional condition within seventy-two hours of the report, on the basis of public criteria contained in the STV. The intervention of the Health Council is required only where health competences are necessary or where aspects of clinical risk emerge. The functional assessment does not constitute a diagnosis and shall not replace a health examination provided for by Code 6.\n5. The individual care plan shall indicate at least:\na) the functional need detected;\nb) the services and the frequency necessary;\nc) the start date and the expected duration;\nd) the reference person responsible for coordination;\ne) the time limits and the criteria for review;\nf) the arrangements for continuity or substitution in the event of unavailability of the service.\nThe person concerned shall take part in defining the plan with the appropriate means of communication and support. For a temporary frailty the review shall be carried out within ninety days, unless it needs to be brought forward.\n6. The minimum list of the services of the Care Basket shall be approved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Care Council. The mandatory opinion of the Health Council is required, limited to the health aspects. The list, the standards and the procedures shall be published in the Care Basket STV."],[17,"art-4--care-basket-and-ascertainment-of-functional-frailty","Art. 4 — Care Basket and ascertainment of functional frailty","","7. The operational priorities shall apply in compliance with Code 1, Article 32, and in accordance with the following sequence of levels, to be assessed in relation to urgency and individual need:\na) level 1: survival, essential hygiene and protected rest;\nb) level 2: care of children from zero to three years of age and support for persons of advanced old age who are not self-sufficient;\nc) level 3: convalescence and the period following childbirth;\nd) level 4: support for the maintenance or recovery of autonomy;\ne) level 5: further care services."],[17,"art-5--services-care-time-and-coordination-with-the-other-codes","Art. 5 — Services, care time and coordination with the other Codes","","1. Each Circle 1 shall ensure effective access to the following basic services, directly or through a shared network:\na) a kitchen supporting frail persons, functionally coordinated with the canteens of Code 9;\nb) a common laundry;\nc) a space for children from zero to three years of age;\nd) a protected rest space;\ne) proximity accompaniment.\nThe arrangements for provision shall be defined so as to guarantee accessibility, continuity and safety.\n2. Circle 1 is responsible, collectively, for the availability of the total hours necessary to guarantee levels 1 and 2. Individual participation shall take place according to availability, competences and the rules of Code 3. Impositions incompatible with individual freedom, with the conditions of the person or with the competences required are not permitted. Individual unavailability shall not entail the loss of the frail person’s right to essential services.\n3. Care time shall be recorded in aggregate form in order to plan the services, verify the coverage of levels 1 and 2 and identify shortfalls. The recording shall not be used to profile the persons who receive or provide care outside the purposes permitted by Code 7.\n4. Competences are allocated as follows:\na) Code C.1-Annex A governs the manufacture, repair and standardisation of aids;\nb) Code 9 governs the production and preparation of food and the technical and organisational management of the canteens;\nc) Code 12 governs assistance to the frail person in access to and accompaniment at the meal, in assisted eating, in hygiene and in the daily use of aids.\nThis Code does not assign to the Care Council the competence to produce aids or to organise the preparation of food.\n5. For services of level 4 or 5, before new resources are requested, the possibility of reusing or recovering aids, clothing and spaces shall be verified, provided that the solution is safe, adequate to the need and does not reduce the quality of the essential services.\n6. The right of elderly persons and persons who are not self-sufficient to remain in their own home is guaranteed, with community support in shifts included in the CBO, pursuant to Article 27 of the Constitution. Self-managed Common Houses are a choice of the person and shall not constitute a compulsory destination."],[17,"art-6--spaces-federated-network-and-territory","Art. 6 — Spaces, federated network and territory","","1. Each Circle 1 shall activate and maintain the care spaces necessary, directly or through network agreements. The spaces shall be accessible, may be divided into functional areas and shall have up-to-date Safety Sheets for the activities actually carried out.\n2. The intended use and the siting of the spaces are governed by Code 10. Code 12 governs the care function performed within them, without modifying the territorial designation or the constraints within the competence of Code 10.\n3. The Confederation shall ensure in every Circle 2 at least one federated facility with advanced competences for every three or four Circles 1 of which it is composed. Planning, resolved by the General Assembly of Circle 2, shall identify the services available and the arrangements for access. The facilities shall guarantee, according to need, competences in social paediatrics, support for complex disability and temporary emergency spaces.\n4. Access to the federated facilities shall take place in accordance with criteria that are public, non-discriminatory and compatible with the urgency of the need. The inventory of resources shall be shared among the Circles concerned and updated in the manner established by the STV.\n5. The measurement of services is limited to the data necessary for safety, the planning of care time and the verification of the Care Basket. Any individual data necessary for the taking into care shall remain confidential; public indicators shall be aggregate and non-profiling. Individual behavioural profiling not necessary for the purposes of this Code is prohibited, in conformity with Code 7, Article 2.2."],[17,"art-7--safety-training-and-right-of-interruption","Art. 7 — Safety, training and right of interruption","","1. No activity directed at frail persons may be started without a Safety Sheet that is in force and adequate to the activity. The Sheet shall be validated by the Care Council, after obtaining the mandatory opinion of the Health Council for the aspects relating to health risk, hygiene and assisted meals. Ownership of the validation remains with the Care Council, except in cases where a Code expressly provides for joint validation.\n2. Care Safety Sheets shall be coordinated with the provisions of Code 1, Article 27, and of Code 14, each for its own competence. In the event of health or clinical risk the procedures of Code 6 shall apply.\n3. The essential devices for community care shall be managed in the Health Storehouse governed by Code 6. They shall be available at no cost to the person concerned, subjected to maintenance and recorded in the relevant inventory. The unavailability of an essential device shall entail the suspension only of the activity that cannot be carried out safely; the Care Council shall activate, where technically possible, a compliant substitute function to ensure the continuity of levels 1 and 2.\n4. The minimum training required is as follows:\na) level L1 for operational access to the spaces for frail persons and for basic care activities;\nb) level L2 for activities involving children, persons of advanced old age who are not self-sufficient or persons with disabilities, in accordance with the classification of Code 5;\nc) level L3 for the design of care activities, the drafting of the STVs and the validation functions provided for by this Code.\n5. Anyone who detects an immediate risk to a frail person shall interrupt the activity concerned, adopt the urgent protective measures permitted and inform the Care Council and the other competent Councils without delay. An interruption carried out in good faith in order to prevent harm shall not give rise to retaliation or detriment for the reporting person."],[17,"art-7-bis--protection-of-minors-and-of-temporarily-incapable-persons","Art. 7-bis — Protection of minors and of temporarily incapable persons","","1. In the event of violence, serious neglect or harm to the detriment of a minor or of a person who is unable to protect himself or herself, the Care Council shall activate without delay a protection pathway, pursuant to Article 26.3 of the Constitution. The pathway comprises making safe, listening and, if necessary, temporary reception with trusted persons or in a protected space.\n2. The fact shall be reported to the Guarantee Circle for the procedures of Code 4. Reports in the Network shall be limited to the information necessary for protection. During the transition Article 37 of Code 4 shall apply.\n3. The separation of a minor from his or her Circle 0 is permitted only as a temporary protection measure, where the minor cannot be made safe while remaining in Circle 0. It is ordered:\na) in the presence of an immediate danger, pursuant to Article 34, paragraph 1, of Code 4;\nb) in the other cases of violence, serious neglect or harm, by the Guarantee Circle on a reasoned proposal from the Care Council, after hearing the minor and, unless this exposes the minor to danger, the persons who hold responsibility for his or her care."],[17,"art-7-bis--protection-of-minors-and-of-temporarily-incapable-persons","Art. 7-bis — Protection of minors and of temporarily incapable persons","","The separation shall be communicated without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal, which shall validate, modify or revoke it within twenty-four hours; in the absence of validation it ceases. The minor is received, where possible, by persons he or she trusts. The validation shall indicate who, during the separation, takes everyday decisions and gives consent to care pursuant to Article 2-ter of Code 6. The validated separation lasts until the decision of paragraph 3-bis and ceases with it. Article 34, paragraph 3, of Code 4 does not apply.\n3-bis. Within thirty days of the validation the Guarantee Tribunal shall take the stable decision on custody of the minor, after hearing the minor, assisted by the trusted person provided for by Article 31.5 of the Constitution, the persons who hold responsibility for his or her care, with the guarantees of Article 25, paragraph 4, of Code 4, and the Care Council. The time limit may be extended once only, for no more than a further thirty days, by reasoned decision, where a necessary element cannot be obtained earlier, such as a health assessment of the minor. On expiry the Tribunal shall decide on the basis of the record; if the decision is not adopted, the separation ceases. Taking account of the will of the minor according to age and maturity, it shall order:\na) the return to Circle 0, where appropriate with a support pathway; or"],[17,"art-7-bis--protection-of-minors-and-of-temporarily-incapable-persons","Art. 7-bis — Protection of minors and of temporarily incapable persons","","b) only where the return would expose the minor to violence, serious neglect or harm that a support pathway cannot prevent, custody with persons who assume responsibility for care pursuant to Article 24.3 of the Constitution, indicating whether that responsibility remains shared with the persons who exercised it or is suspended for them, and how relations with them are maintained, unless this harms the minor.\nThe decision shall be reviewed by the Tribunal at least every six months and at any time at the request of the minor or of the persons who held responsibility for care. Review under Article 35, paragraph 2, of Code 4 is permitted against it. This paragraph also applies to the decision on where and with whom the minor children of a person who ceases to be a member pursuant to Article 29, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 4 live; the decision is taken before the cessation takes effect and does not require the separation of paragraph 3. In that case the cessation does not, by itself, constitute violence, serious neglect or harm within the meaning of letter b), and the Tribunal may also order that the minor live with the person on the portion assigned, with the housing, the Basket and the services due to him or her as a member."],[17,"art-7-bis--protection-of-minors-and-of-temporarily-incapable-persons","Art. 7-bis — Protection of minors and of temporarily incapable persons","","3-ter. During the transition the separation against the will of those who hold responsibility for care and the decisions of paragraph 3-bis fall to the competent authorities of the State, pursuant to Article 37 of Code 4. The Care Council reports the case, supports the minor and cooperates in his or her reception.\n4. Every person may designate in advance a representative for the event of incapacity, including by means of the living will provided for in Article 8-bis of Code 6.\n5. In the absence of a designation and in the event of urgency, the Guarantee Circle shall appoint within twenty-four hours a temporary representative, chosen in the following order from among the persons of Circle 0 indicated by the person, his or her trusted person and a person drawn by lot with L2 competence in care, free of conflicts of interest. The office shall last no more than thirty days and shall be renewable by reasoned decision until the appointment of the stable representative provided for by Article 2-ter, paragraph 5, of Code 6, which the Guarantee Circle shall request from the Guarantee Tribunal when the incapacity lasts or is expected to last more than sixty days.\n6. The representative shall decide in accordance with the expressed or presumable will of the person and, failing that, in accordance with his or her best interests. The office shall cease when the person recovers capacity."],[17,"art-7-ter--choice-of-residence-and-departure-of-minors-from-the-confederation","Art. 7-ter — Choice of residence and departure of minors from the Confederation","","1. In implementation of Article 26.2 of the Constitution, a minor who has completed the fourteenth year and has reached autonomy may choose to live in a Circle 0 other than that of the persons who hold responsibility for his or her care, in the same or in another Community, with the consent of those who live there, or in accommodation allocated pursuant to Article 47-ter of Code 1. The choice does not change member status or the rights of Article 26 of the Constitution. The minor may at any time return to live with the persons who hold responsibility for his or her care.\n2. Autonomy is assessed jointly by the Care Council and the Competences Council, at the request of the minor. Within thirty days of the request they shall hear the minor, assisted, if he or she so wishes, by the trusted person provided for by Article 31.5 of the Constitution, the persons who hold responsibility for his or her care and those who would receive the minor, and shall verify:\na) that the choice is free, stable and not determined by pressure from other persons;\nb) that the minor is able to provide for his or her daily life with the support available;\nc) that the new placement is safe and ensures housing, the Basket, care, learning and relations with the persons dear to the minor."],[17,"art-7-ter--choice-of-residence-and-departure-of-minors-from-the-confederation","Art. 7-ter — Choice of residence and departure of minors from the Confederation","","The decision shall state reasons and indicate who takes everyday decisions and how relations with the persons who hold responsibility for care are maintained, unless this harms the minor. Responsibility for care and consent to care provided for by Article 2-ter of Code 6 remain with those who exercise them, except for a decision of the Guarantee Tribunal pursuant to Article 7-bis, paragraph 3-bis.\n3. The choice of residence and the opposition of the persons who hold responsibility for care do not, by themselves, constitute violence, serious neglect or harm within the meaning of Article 7-bis. If the request reveals violence, serious neglect or harm, the Care Council shall activate the protection pathway of Article 7-bis, which prevails over the procedure of this Article.\n4. Against the decision of paragraph 2, or if it is not taken within the time limit, the minor and each person who holds responsibility for his or her care may appeal within fourteen days to the Guarantee Tribunal, which shall decide within thirty days with the guarantees of Article 7-bis, paragraph 3-bis, taking account of the will of the minor according to age and maturity. Review under Article 35, paragraph 2, of Code 4 is permitted against the decision of the Tribunal."],[17,"art-7-ter--choice-of-residence-and-departure-of-minors-from-the-confederation","Art. 7-ter — Choice of residence and departure of minors from the Confederation","","5. From completion of the sixteenth year the minor may personally make the declaration of Article 4.8 of the Constitution, pursuant to Article 26.5 of the Constitution. The declaration is preceded by an interview with the Care Council, in which the minor may be assisted by the trusted person; the Council informs the minor of the effects of Article 4.8, of the rights retained pursuant to paragraph 8, of the Autonomous Coexistence Pact and of readmission, and verifies with the minor where and with whom he or she will live. The interview takes place within fourteen days of the request; if the time limit expires without an interview, the declaration may be made in any case. The persons who hold responsibility for care are informed, unless this exposes the minor to danger. If, before the eighteenth year, the minor is not able to decide, paragraph 6 applies.\n6. Until completion of the sixteenth year the declaration of Article 4.8 of the Constitution for the minor is made by the person who holds responsibility for his or her care, solely in the interest of the minor, after informing and hearing the minor in a manner suited to his or her age; the opinion of the minor carries increasing weight with age and maturity, pursuant to Article 26.2 of the Constitution. If there is more than one responsible person, they decide by agreement. The declaration that a person makes for himself or herself does not extend to the minors for whom he or she holds responsibility for care."],[17,"art-7-ter--choice-of-residence-and-departure-of-minors-from-the-confederation","Art. 7-ter — Choice of residence and departure of minors from the Confederation","","7. Within fourteen days of the declaration of paragraph 5 or of paragraph 6, the minor, each person who holds responsibility for his or her care or the Care Council may apply to the Guarantee Tribunal. The Tribunal shall hear the minor, assisted by the trusted person, and the responsible persons and shall decide within thirty days:\na) for the declaration of paragraph 5, it verifies only that it is free and informed and that the minor is able to decide, without assessing its appropriateness; the application does not suspend its effects;\nb) for the declaration of paragraph 6, it decides in the interest of the minor, taking account of his or her opinion; until the decision the declaration has no effect for the minor. If the minor remains a member, the Tribunal also decides where and with whom he or she lives, pursuant to Article 7-bis, paragraph 3-bis.\nReview under Article 35, paragraph 2, of Code 4 is permitted against the decision.\n8. A minor who is no longer a member retains in any case drinking water, life-saving emergency care and the protection of minors guaranteed by Article 1.3 of the Constitution, the rights of Article 26 of the Constitution referred to in Article 4.9-bis, including access to care and learning, and the other rights of Article 4.9. The minor may continue to live in his or her own Circle 0, with the consent of those who live there, and may apply at any time for readmission pursuant to Article 4.8 of the Constitution."],[17,"art-7-ter--choice-of-residence-and-departure-of-minors-from-the-confederation","Art. 7-ter — Choice of residence and departure of minors from the Confederation","","9. During the transition this Article applies within the limits permitted by the law in force on responsibility for care and on the participation of minors in organisations, pursuant to Article 37 of Code 4. The choice of residence against the will of those who hold responsibility for care and the decisions of the Tribunal provided for by paragraphs 4 and 7 fall to the competent authorities of the State; the Care Council supports the minor."],[17,"art-8--care-dependency-register","Art. 8 — Care Dependency Register","","1. The Care Council shall feed the Dependency Register governed by Code 15, indicating at least:\na) material dependencies, relating to aids, devices or spaces that are not available in the Confederation, are available only beyond the critical time threshold defined in the STV or cannot be substituted locally;\nb) competence dependencies, relating to competences of level L2 or L3 not available in the Basin referred to in Article 6, paragraph 3;\nc) external dependencies, relating to care services external to the Confederation that are necessary to guarantee the continuity of levels 1 and 2.\n2. For every critical dependency the Register shall indicate:\na) the missing resource or competence;\nb) the service and the levels of the Basket concerned;\nc) the person responsible for the plan to reduce the dependency;\nd) the temporary measures of continuity and risk mitigation;\ne) the final time limit laid down for overcoming the dependency or for a new reasoned assessment.\n3. The exit plan shall provide for a definite final time limit. Where overcoming the dependency is not technically achievable within that time limit, the Care Council shall propose a reasoned revision of the plan and of the continuity measures; the revision shall not be limited to extending the dependency without indicating interventions and responsibilities.\n4. Every year a federated audit of the autonomy of care shall be carried out by independent L3 members, drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4. The audit shall also assess the actual coverage of levels 1 and 2 and respect for confidentiality."],[17,"art-9--prohibitions-guarantees-and-remedies","Art. 9 — Prohibitions, guarantees and remedies","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) derive rent from the Care Basket of levels 1 and 2, from the essential spaces, from the minimum care time or from the essential devices;\nb) make access to the life-saving STVs and to the Safety Sheets subject to payment or to an exclusive licence;\nc) reserve exclusively or accumulate care time, spaces or aids beyond the technical limits, where this reduces availability for other persons entitled;\nd) deny or suspend an essential service of the Care Basket as a sanctioning measure;\ne) transfer to external parties the control of the essential care spaces;\nf) use data on functional frailty for profiling or for purposes other than the taking into care and the technical purposes expressly permitted by Code 7;\ng) order the forced institutionalisation of a person, except for a temporary placement strictly necessary in the face of an immediate risk, adopted on the conditions and with the guarantees of Article 8, paragraphs 4 to 10, of Code 6; for minors Article 7-bis, paragraph 3, applies. Any health treatments remain governed by Code 6 and by the related guarantees.\n2. Violations of paragraph 1 constitute substantial violations. The ascertainment, the precautionary measures, the suspension or deactivation of the function and the remedies shall be adopted in accordance with Code 4. The Care Council shall document the facts and activate the procedures provided for, without taking the place of the bodies competent for the ascertainment or for the application of the remedies.\n3. The suspension or deactivation of a non-compliant function shall not interrupt the services of levels 1 and 2 where it is possible to guarantee them through a technically compliant substitute function. In the event of immediate risk, the urgent measures provided for by the competent Codes shall apply, with simultaneous activation of continuity of care as far as possible.\n4. The person concerned or anyone who demonstrates a qualified interest may appeal to the Guarantee in accordance with Code 4, within fourteen days of the communication of the decision or of actual knowledge of the violation. The appeal may concern procedural defects, manifest technical unreasonableness, discrimination or disproportion. The appeal shall not automatically suspend the urgent measures necessary to prevent harm, unless the competent body decides otherwise."],[17,"art-10--implementation-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Implementation, coordination and precedence","","1. This Code is coordinated, for the respective subject matters, with:\na) Code 1, Articles 27 and 32, for the Safety Sheets and the order of priority of the Basket;\nb) Code 2, for the STVs;\nc) Code 3, for resolutions and the consensus method;\nd) Code 4, for the Guarantee and the remedies;\ne) Code 5, for the levels of competence and training;\nf) Code 6, for health, clinical services and the Health Storehouse;\ng) Code 7, Article 2.2, for the rules on the network and on data;\nh) Code 8, for any water resources necessary for the care spaces;\ni) Code C.1-Annex A, for aids;\nj) Code 9, for food production and the canteens;\nk) Code 10, for the territorial designation of the spaces;\nl) Codes 13, 14 and 15, for their respective subject matters, including biodiversity, safety and the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, each Circle 1 shall:\na) inventory, in a manner respectful of confidentiality, the functional needs relevant to the continuity of levels 1 and 2;\nb) inventory the care spaces, the equipment and the time available;\nc) activate the minimum Care Basket by means of a provisional STV and minimum Safety Sheets.\n3. Within twelve months of entry into force, each Circle 1 shall:\na) establish the Care Council;\nb) guarantee access to the minimum care spaces;\nc) activate the federated inventory of aids;\nd) adopt the definitive STVs for levels 1 and 2;\ne) identify its own node of the federated Care Network."],[17,"art-10--implementation-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Implementation, coordination and precedence","","In the presence of documented overlaps of the transitional burdens relating to Codes 9, 10 and 12, the Assembly may grant a reasoned extension, for a period not exceeding six months. The extension shall indicate the activities still to be completed, the persons responsible and the intermediate deadlines and shall not suspend the guarantee of levels 1 and 2.\n4. Existing functions found to be in violation of Article 9 shall be subject without delay to verification in accordance with Code 4. Within ninety days of entry into force there shall be initiated, for each function concerned, a documented compliance pathway or, if compliance is technically impossible, the deactivation procedure provided for by Code 4. The plan shall identify the substitute function and a definite final time limit. The deactivation of a function that guarantees levels 1 and 2 shall take place only after the activation of the substitute, except for the urgent measures necessary to deal with an immediate risk, adopted with the guarantees of Code 4.\n5. In the event of regulatory overlap, each Code prevails only as regards the subject matter assigned to it:\na) Code 12 for the organisation of community care, the ascertainment of functional frailty, the Care Basket and the internal function of care spaces;\nb) Code 6 for diagnosis, clinical services, clinical nutrition and health risks;\nc) Code 9 for the production and preparation of food and the technical and organisational management of the canteens;"],[17,"art-10--implementation-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Implementation, coordination and precedence","","d) Code 10 for the intended use and territorial siting of the spaces;\ne) Code C.1-Annex A for the manufacture and standardisation of aids;\nf) Code 14 for the safety aspects within its competence.\nPrecedence is limited to the specific subject and does not entail the absorption of the competences assigned to the other Codes. In the event of a conflict that cannot be resolved by coordinated interpretation, the competent bodies shall define a joint solution in accordance with the procedures of the legal order, ensuring in the meantime the protection measures necessary and the continuity of levels 1 and 2."],[18,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","1. This Code governs:\na) the protection of biodiversity;\nb) the establishment and management of ecological sanctuaries and refuge sanctuaries;\nc) the protection of wild fauna and wild pollinators;\nd) the definition of animal welfare parameters in the matters falling within its competence;\ne) the protection of proximity companion animals.\nBiodiversity, the related ecological functions and animal welfare constitute necessary conditions for the continuity of the Basket, the protection of health, the management of the habitat and the organisation of care.\n2. For the purposes of this Code:\na) biodiversity means the variety of species, populations, ecosystems, ecological interactions and genetic heritage present in a territory;\nb) wild fauna means all the animal populations that are not permanently managed by human beings and live in conditions of freedom;\nc) wild pollinators means wild bees, bumblebees, butterflies, hoverflies and other pollinating organisms not housed in the custodial hives provided for by Code 9;\nd) ecological sanctuary, hereinafter also “sanctuary”, means an area dedicated to the protection of ecological integrity, to the refuge and reproduction of species and to the free evolution of ecosystems, in accordance with the regime provided for by this Code;"],[18,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","e) functional ecological corridor means a biological function of connection between habitats, populations and ecosystems. The location, geometry and territorial constraints of the corridor are governed by Code 10; the parameters of its biological functionality are governed by this Code;\nf) refuge sanctuary means the separate Community provided for by Article 13.4 of the Constitution, in which there live, with the right to life and to care, animals rescued from systems of exploitation, those retired from collaboration and those for which the conditions of non-exploitative cohabitation are not met;\ng) proximity companion animal means an animal that lives permanently with one or more persons in a continuous relationship and shares their living space with them, without being intended for food production or systematic work;\nh) animal welfare means the physical and mental state of the animal, assessed according to the domains of nutrition, physical environment, health, behavioural interactions and mental state, on the basis of the indicators defined in the relevant STVs and Welfare Sheets.\n3. For the purposes of the allocation of competences:\na) wild fauna, wild pollinators, the wild genetic heritage, the function of integral protection proper to sanctuaries and the essential knowledge of ecology and animal welfare are inalienable ecological commons;"],[18,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-legal-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and legal nature","","b) the biological functionality of corridors, ecological connectivity and the capacity for wild pollination constitute ecological functions of infrastructural importance;\nc) the land, the location of corridors, buffer zones as cartographic constraints and the Land Register remain territorial commons governed by Code 10.\n4. The classification provided for by this Article does not modify the ownership or territorial regime established by the other competent Codes. Wild fauna, wild pollinators and the wild genetic heritage may not be the object of exclusive appropriation. Proximity companion animals may not be treated as commodities nor subjected to economic exploitation incompatible with their welfare."],[18,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Non-appropriation. No wild species or population, no wild genetic heritage, nest, den or wild hive may be subjected to exclusive private ownership, patent, exclusive title or rent, in the manner and within the limits established by this Code and by the competent Codes.\n2. Precaution. Where there are documented scientific elements of uncertainty as to the compatibility of an intervention with biodiversity, animal welfare or biological functionality, a proportionate measure suitable for preventing or reducing the risk shall be adopted, within the competences of this Code. The precautionary principle does not transfer to this Code competences assigned to Codes 6, 8, 9, 10 and 14.\n3. Federated ecological connection. Every Circle 1 shall contribute to the biological functionality of ecological corridors. The mere cartographic representation of a corridor is not sufficient to demonstrate its functionality. Indicators and thresholds are defined in the Biodiversity and Corridors STV, adopted jointly in accordance with the competences of Codes 10 and 13.\n4. Integrity and reversibility. Every intervention permitted in a sanctuary or liable to affect the functionality of a corridor shall provide, where technically applicable, for ecological restoration measures and a reversibility plan. Within the competences of this Code, the protection of ecological integrity constitutes a priority criterion.\n5. Non-commodification. It is prohibited to establish rents on wild fauna, on wild pollinators, on the function of wild pollination, on access to sanctuaries as commons and on essential ecological functions. The STVs and Safety Sheets essential for protection may not be placed behind a paywall.\n6. Proportionality and minimisation. Monitoring is permitted exclusively where necessary for the protection of biodiversity, for ecological safety, for the verification of animal welfare, for the assessment of the pollination function or for ecological resilience. Data shall be processed in aggregate and non-profiling form, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2. The STVs shall define the minimum necessary retention periods."],[18,"art-3--biodiversity-council","Art. 3 — Biodiversity Council","","1. Every Circle 1 shall establish a Biodiversity Council composed of three to five members with competences of at least level L2 or L3 in the relevant disciplines, including ecology, zoology, botany, entomology, ethology and conservation. The members shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall remain in office for one year and may not be drawn by lot again for the same Council until twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office.\n2. The Biodiversity Council shall:\na) maintain the Biodiversity Register and the inventory of sanctuaries, in compliance with the territorial competences of Code 10;\nb) define, within the competences assigned to this Code, the indicators of biodiversity, of the biological functionality of corridors and of animal welfare;\nc) draw up and update the Biodiversity STV, the Refuge Sanctuaries STV, the Collaborating Animals Welfare STV jointly with Code 9, the Domestic Welfare STV, the Wild Bees STV and the Wildlife Management STV, and act on the Pollinators STV of Code 9 pursuant to Article 6, paragraph 6;\nd) draft and update the Welfare Sheets;\ne) carry out or coordinate the ecological impact assessment for the aspects assigned to this Code;\nf) feed the Dependency Register governed by Code 15;"],[18,"art-3--biodiversity-council","Art. 3 — Biodiversity Council","","g) ensure technical coordination with the Habitat and Territory Council of Code 10, the Agroecological Council of Code 9, the Health Council of Code 6, the Energy and Water Council of Code 8 and the Care Council of Code 12.\n3. The mandatory opinions of the Biodiversity Council provided for by Codes 9 and 10 and by this Code, where negative, are binding for the protection of ecological limits, pursuant to Article 6.6 of the Constitution and Article 8 of Code 3, exclusively as regards the indicators and parameters of biodiversity, of protection of wildlife and of biological functionality expressly assigned to this Code. The welfare parameters of animals that live or collaborate with people are approved with the authorisation of paragraph 5. Such opinions do not determine the location, the geometry, the intended use or the imposition of a territorial constraint, which remain within the competence of Code 10.\n4. The technical acts of the Biodiversity Council shall be adopted in implementation of the STVs in force. Discretionary proposals shall be adopted by the consensus method through the absence of reasoned objections, pursuant to Code 3, after publication for at least seventy-two hours. Publication shall not include data whose disclosure could cause harm to the protected species or sites."],[18,"art-3--biodiversity-council","Art. 3 — Biodiversity Council","","5. The Biodiversity Council is the local branch of the Competence Circles for Biodiversity and Seeds and for Animal Care. It shall verify and authorise non-exploitative cohabitation and collaboration pursuant to Articles 13.3, 13.3-bis and 13.3-ter of the Constitution. The authorisation includes the approval of the welfare parameters of the STVs and of the Welfare Sheets provided for by this Code and by Code 9: without the approval the STV or the Sheet is not adopted and the activity is not authorised. Approval and refusal shall state reasons and may be challenged in accordance with Article 17 of Code 3; they are not opinions within the meaning of Article 8 of Code 3."],[18,"art-4--ecological-sanctuaries","Art. 4 — Ecological sanctuaries","","1. Every Circle 2 shall establish at least one sanctuary for every three or four Circles 1 of which it is composed, in accordance with the territorial planning of Code 10. Planning shall give priority to degraded areas capable of regeneration or to semi-natural areas of high ecological value. Establishment shall take place:\na) on a proposal from the Biodiversity Council;\nb) after the mandatory opinion of the Habitat and Territory Council;\nc) by resolution of the General Assembly of Circle 2 adopted by qualified majority, in accordance with the rules of the Constitution and of Code 3.\n2. The sanctuary is dedicated to the maximum ecological integrity compatible with the characteristics of the area and to the stability of the related protective function. Within it, it is prohibited to:\na) build, seal the soil, carry out productive activities, hunt, or carry out intensive gathering or removals incompatible with the protection regime;\nb) introduce invasive alien species or genetically modified organisms into the wild environment;\nc) use motorised means, except for interventions authorised for safety, conservation or maintenance;\nd) produce continuous artificial noise incompatible with the purposes of protection;\ne) make public the detailed coordinates of nests, dens or wild hives;\nf) carry out invasive research without a Safety Sheet in force and the authorisation of the Biodiversity Council.\n3. The following are permitted, exclusively within the limits of the relevant STVs:\na) non-invasive monitoring;\nb) light maintenance of the perimeter paths;\nc) non-invasive educational activities carried out from the margins of the area;\nd) conservation or ecological restoration interventions;\ne) interventions necessary to deal with safety risks.\nThe interventions referred to in letters d) and e) require the authorisation of the Biodiversity Council and, where relevant, a Safety Sheet, the recording of the intervention and a reversibility or restoration plan.\n4. Access to the inner areas of the sanctuary is permitted exclusively for the activities permitted by paragraph 3 and under the conditions established by the related STVs. Permanent free access is not permitted.\n5. Every sanctuary shall be entered in the federated Land Register governed by Code 10, Article 7. The public level shall contain only aggregate information or information otherwise free of sensitive coordinates. The detailed technical level is accessible exclusively to the competent Councils; accesses shall be recorded and the related logs shall be retained for twelve months."],[18,"art-4-bis--refuge-sanctuaries","Art. 4-bis — Refuge sanctuaries","","1. Animals rescued from systems of exploitation, those retired from collaboration and those present on the date of the Community’s accession for which the conditions of Article 6-bis of Code 9 are not met shall be received in a refuge sanctuary or in an authorised non-exploitative cohabitation.\n2. Every animal received has the right to life and to care and is not transferable, unless the transfer is necessary for its welfare and attested by an L3 veterinary competence. Slaughter, killing for convenience, transfer for consideration, use in work and breeding for purposes of use are prohibited.\n3. The animals received shall not be bred. Where necessary for their welfare and for the capacity for care, the prevention of births shall take place by the non-harmful methods defined by the Refuge Sanctuaries STV.\n4. What the animal produces spontaneously may be collected only under the conditions of Article 13.3-ter of the Constitution. It may not constitute a production objective, be planned in the Basket or justify an increase in the number of animals.\n5. Every Circle 2 shall guarantee at least one refuge sanctuary for every three or four Circles 1 of which it is composed, sized according to the animals recorded in the census. The care of the animals received falls within the CBO, pursuant to Article 7.2 of the Constitution and Code 1.\n6. A Welfare Sheet shall be kept for each animal or group. Feeding and veterinary care shall be ensured without compromising levels 1 and 2 of the human Basket. In the event of water scarcity, Article 10, paragraph 8, of Code 9 applies.\n7. Euthanasia is permitted exclusively under the conditions of Article 7, paragraph 12, in so far as applicable. In place of the Domestic Welfare Sheet, the Welfare Sheet of the received animal provided for by paragraph 6 applies; in place of the trusted person, the persons who habitually care for the animal are informed.\n8. Access by persons to the refuge sanctuary is permitted for care, learning and relationship, in compliance with the Welfare Sheets."],[18,"art-5--ecological-corridors-and-buffer-zones-biological-parameters","Art. 5 — Ecological corridors and buffer zones: biological parameters","","1. Code 10 governs the location, the cartographic dimension, the geometry and the territorial constraints of ecological corridors and buffer zones, as well as land take. This Code governs exclusively the biological parameters of functionality, the indicators, the thresholds, the criteria of biological equivalence and the impact assessment as regards the aspects of biodiversity and animal welfare.\n2. The minimum territorial radius of five hundred metres and its possible reduction to two hundred metres are governed by Code 10, Article 6. As regards the biological aspects, the assessment of functional equivalence shall consider at least:\na) the density and continuity of flowering;\nb) the continuity of the vegetation cover;\nc) the presence and continuity of functional habitats;\nd) compliance with the prohibition of treatments provided for by Code 9, Article 2.4, letter b);\ne) compliance as regards the practices prohibited by Code 9, Article 6-bis, paragraph 5, letter c);\nf) the biological indicators of pollinators defined by the STV.\n3. Where Code 10 allows the reduction of the radius to two hundred metres, functional equivalence as regards the biological aspects shall be attested by the Biodiversity Council in accordance with the joint Biodiversity and Corridors STV. The attestation concerns exclusively biological functionality and does not replace the territorial decision falling to Code 10.\n4. Every plan or intervention that interrupts a corridor or reduces a buffer zone shall be subject to an ecological impact assessment limited to the aspects of biodiversity, animal welfare and biological functionality assigned to this Code. The opinion of the Biodiversity Council is binding within the limits established by Article 3, paragraph 3."],[18,"art-5-bis--management-of-wild-fauna-and-bloodless-removal","Art. 5-bis — Management of wild fauna and bloodless removal","","1. In implementation of Article 13.4 of the Constitution, the management of wild fauna necessary for the protection of ecosystems, health, crops or the safety of persons shall be carried out exclusively by bloodless methods, in the following order:\na) prevention, protection of crops and goods and non-harmful deterrence;\nb) fences and other barriers that do not injure animals and do not interrupt ecological corridors;\nc) where the preceding measures are not sufficient, capture without pain or injury and transfer to suitable habitats, including ecological sanctuaries.\n2. Killing, hunting, poisoning and any means that causes pain, injury or death to the animal are prohibited.\n3. The methods, the conditions and the recording of interventions are defined by the Wildlife Management STV. Every intervention under letter c) of paragraph 1 is decided by the Biodiversity Council, as a branch of the Animal Care Competence Circle, with the mandatory opinion of the Agroecological Council where it concerns damage to crops and of the Health Council where it concerns health risks. In sanctuaries, Article 4, paragraph 3, also applies.\n4. Article 8, paragraph 6, remains unaffected for a serious and immediate risk to a person or an animal."],[18,"art-6--wild-bees-and-wild-pollinators","Art. 6 — Wild bees and wild pollinators","","1. Wild populations of pollinators constitute ecological commons protected by this Code. Colonies housed in custodial hives are governed by Article 6-bis of Code 9.\n2. Hybridisation between housed colonies and wild populations is prohibited where, according to the indicators and thresholds of the Wild Bees STV, it causes or risks causing a significant alteration of the genetic heritage of the local wild populations.\n3. It is prohibited to:\na) intentionally destroy nests of wild pollinators;\nb) take honey from wild colonies;\nc) use systemic pesticides, prohibited in every area by Code 9, Article 2, paragraph 4, letter b);\nd) carry out activities incompatible with the conservation of the habitats of wild pollinators.\n4. The census of wild pollinators shall be carried out using proportionate criteria, in aggregate and non-profiling form. The coordinates of sensitive sites shall be kept exclusively in the restricted technical level.\n5. The Wild Bees STV shall define at least:\na) the indicators of the local populations;\nb) the thresholds of genetic alteration;\nc) the methodologies of observation and ascertainment;\nd) the measures of prevention, containment and restoration.\n6. On the Pollinators STV of Code 9 the Biodiversity Council approves the parameters of colony welfare, with the authorisation of Article 3, paragraph 5, and delivers the mandatory opinion on the parameters of protection of wild populations and of biodiversity; a negative opinion is binding within the limits of Article 3, paragraph 3."],[18,"art-7--collaborating-animals-assistance-animals-and-proximity-companion-animals","Art. 7 — Collaborating animals, assistance animals and proximity companion animals","","1. The collaboration of animals in light work is permitted exclusively under the conditions of Article 13.3-bis of the Constitution and of Article 6-bis, paragraph 3, of Code 9. For each animal an individual Welfare Sheet, a register of hours and a periodic veterinary check shall be kept; in the absence of a Sheet in force, collaboration shall be suspended. An animal retired from collaboration shall remain in the Community or be received pursuant to Article 4-bis.\n2. Cohabitation with proximity companion animals is permitted as non-exploitative cohabitation pursuant to Article 13.3 of the Constitution. It shall be verified and authorised by the Biodiversity Council on the basis of the Domestic Welfare Sheet.\n3. In the absence of the conditions of Article 13.3 of the Constitution, the Biodiversity Council shall propose the measures necessary for the animal’s welfare and, if these are not implemented, its reception in a sanctuary pursuant to Article 4-bis, in accordance with the procedures of Code 4.\n4. The daily care of the proximity companion animal shall be ensured by the cohabitation nucleus. Circle 1, and the federated level where necessary, shall guarantee system-level protection, the Domestic Welfare Sheet and the balance of food resources in the manner provided for by this Code and by the STVs.\n5. The public register of proximity companion animals is aggregate and non-profiling and may contain only, for each Circle:\na) the number of animals;\nb) the species;"],[18,"art-7--collaborating-animals-assistance-animals-and-proximity-companion-animals","Art. 7 — Collaborating animals, assistance animals and proximity companion animals","","c) the aggregate figure relating to sterilisation.\nAny individual data necessary for care or for health interventions shall be kept separately, in confidential form and in accordance with the rules of the competent Codes.\n6. The following are prohibited:\na) breeding and reproduction for purposes of transfer or use;\nb) mutilations carried out exclusively for aesthetic purposes;\nc) abandonment;\nd) transfer for consideration. No reimbursement is due between members: the feeding and care of the animal are ensured in accordance with paragraphs 4 and 11. In relations with external parties, the documented reimbursement of out-of-pocket expenses for the care of the animal is permitted only through the Common Fund, pursuant to Article 17 of Code 15;\ne) any management incompatible with the welfare parameters established by the Domestic Welfare Sheet.\n7. Entrustment free of charge is permitted. The registration of the adoption shall be tracked by proportionate and non-profiling means.\n8. The cohabitation nucleus shall ensure that the animal has adequate space, movement, sociality, rest, basic care and feeding compatible with its needs, in accordance with the Domestic Welfare Sheet."],[18,"art-7--collaborating-animals-assistance-animals-and-proximity-companion-animals","Art. 7 — Collaborating animals, assistance animals and proximity companion animals","","9. The individual accumulation of animals is permitted only within the capacity for care defined by the Domestic Welfare Sheet. The assessment shall consider at least the species and number of the animals, the space available, the health conditions, the time available and the actual capacity to guarantee the welfare of each animal. Exceeding the capacity for care constitutes a violation pursuant to Article 10.\n10. The non-harmful prevention of births of the proximity companion animal, including sterilisation, performed or prescribed by a veterinary competence where it is necessary for the welfare of the animal or where the care of further offspring could not be guaranteed in accordance with paragraph 9, does not constitute reproductive manipulation within the meaning of Articles 13.2 and 13.3, letter c), of the Constitution. It is decided by the cohabitation nucleus together with the veterinary competence, recorded in the Domestic Welfare Sheet and not imposed as a general rule. For animals received in sanctuaries, Article 4-bis, paragraph 3, applies. As regards health, zoonotic or collective-risk aspects, the competences, procedures and guarantees of Code 6 shall apply, including the collective measures permitted by its Article 2-bis, paragraph 3-bis. Every measure shall comply with criteria of proportionality and temporariness, guarantee that the person concerned is informed and be subject to review and to the guarantees provided for."],[18,"art-7--collaborating-animals-assistance-animals-and-proximity-companion-animals","Art. 7 — Collaborating animals, assistance animals and proximity companion animals","","11. The feeding of proximity companion animals may be ensured by means of resources compatible with the Basket, suitable scraps or a dedicated fund. Such resources may not compromise the human Basket of levels 1 and 2 and are governed by the Basket STV of Code 9. In the event of water scarcity, Article 10, paragraph 8, of Code 9 applies.\n12. Euthanasia for mere convenience is prohibited. It is permitted exclusively in the presence of irreversible suffering attested by two L3 competences, at least one of which is veterinary, in compliance with the health competences of Code 6 and with the Domestic Welfare Sheet. The intervention shall be documented and tracked; the trusted person shall be informed, except in the case of reasoned impossibility or of urgency incompatible with prior notice.\n13. The Biodiversity Council shall coordinate with the Health Council the aspects relating to zoonoses and health security and with the Care Council the aspects relating to the support of frail persons who live with animals.\n14. Assistance animals. An animal may assist a person with a disability or frailty only if the activity is compatible with its temperament, is learned without coercion and does not compromise its welfare. The Biodiversity Council shall verify these conditions together with the Care Council. The animal has the right to rest and to retirement; when it can no longer or must no longer assist, the person shall be guaranteed equivalent assistance in accordance with Code 12."],[18,"art-8--safety-measurement-training-and-right-of-interruption","Art. 8 — Safety, measurement, training and right of interruption","","1. Activities carried out in a sanctuary or concerning wild fauna, wild pollinators, collaborating animals, animals received or proximity companion animals shall be governed by a Welfare Sheet in force and, where required by the nature of the activity, by a Safety Sheet in force.\n2. The Safety Sheet adopted pursuant to this Code governs the ecological, biological and animal welfare risks falling within the competences of Code 13. The general safety of persons, infrastructure and tasks is governed by Code 14. The Biodiversity Council shall deliver the opinion within its competence on the ecological aspects.\n3. The necessary personal protective equipment and other equipment shall be managed in accordance with the commons regime provided for by the competent Codes. The lack of an item of equipment expressly indicated as essential in the Safety Sheet entails the suspension of the activity concerned until safe conditions are restored, in accordance with the procedures of Code 14.\n4. Measurement and monitoring are permitted exclusively for the protection of animal welfare and biodiversity, for the verification of pollination functions or for the assessment of ecological resilience. Data shall be aggregate, non-profiling and retained for the minimum time necessary, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2, and the relevant STVs. The competences of Code 6 in health matters remain unaffected.\n5. The training required is structured as follows:\na) level L1 for operational access to sanctuaries and for the ordinary management of animals;\nb) level L2 for the advanced management of the activities identified by the STVs;\nc) level L3 for the design and validation of the STVs and the Welfare Sheets, in the cases provided for.\n6. Anyone who detects a serious and immediate risk to fauna, an animal or a person may interrupt the activity concerned and shall inform the competent Councils without delay. An interruption carried out in good faith to prevent harm may not give rise to retaliation or detriment."],[18,"art-9--biodiversity-dependency-register","Art. 9 — Biodiversity Dependency Register","","1. The Biodiversity Council shall feed the Dependency Register governed by Code 15, distinguishing at least:\na) ecological dependencies, relating to functions such as pollination, biological control, ecological fertility or connectivity, where these are not sufficiently available or substitutable within the Confederation;\nb) competence dependencies, relating to the unavailability in the Basin of the L2 or L3 competences necessary in matters of biodiversity, veterinary science, ethology or conservation;\nc) material or technological dependencies, relating to the unavailability of genetic material, monitoring equipment, technical instruments or diagnostic capacities necessary for the continuity of the protective functions.\n2. For every dependency classified as critical, the Register shall indicate the person responsible, the verifiable objective, the temporary containment measures and the final deadline envisaged for overcoming the dependency.\n3. The federal level shall carry out at least one annual audit of the ecological autonomy of the Confederation, by means of independent L3 members drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4."],[18,"art-10--prohibitions-violations-and-guarantees","Art. 10 — Prohibitions, violations and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) derive rents from wild fauna, from wild pollinators, from the function of wild pollination or from the protective function of sanctuaries;\nb) make access to the STVs essential for biodiversity, animal welfare and the protection of wild bees, as well as to the essential Safety Sheets, subject to payment or an exclusive licence;\nc) register, file, claim or maintain patents or exclusive titles over the wild genetic material and the local genetic heritage classified as commons by Article 1, paragraph 3, letter a), including local breeds and lines where they form part of that heritage;\nd) introduce invasive alien species or genetically modified organisms into the wild environment, or introduce proprietary bee lines or biological material in such a way as to create a critical dependency in accordance with Code 15 or Code 9, Article 6-bis;\ne) kill or mistreat animals, use them or collect their products outside the conditions of Article 13 of the Constitution and of Article 6-bis of Code 9, or keep a number of animals exceeding the capacity for care defined pursuant to Article 7, paragraph 9;\nf) make public the detailed coordinates of nests, dens or wild hives;\ng) transfer to parties external to the Confederation the control of sanctuaries, of wild gene banks or of the Biodiversity Register.\n2. Violations of paragraph 1 constitute substantial violations. Ascertainment, precautionary measures, the suspension or deactivation of activities and the remedies are governed by Code 4. The Biodiversity Council shall document the facts and transmit its technical assessments to the competent bodies.\n3. The person concerned or anyone who demonstrates a qualified interest may activate the Guarantee in accordance with the procedures of Code 4 within fourteen days of the communication of the decision or of actual knowledge of the violation. Any appeal shall not automatically suspend the urgent measures necessary to prevent serious and immediate harm."],[18,"art-11--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 11 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) the Constitution, Articles 3.1, 13, 14 and 32;\nb) Code 1, Articles 27 and 32;\nc) Codes 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6, each for its respective competences;\nd) Code 7, Article 2.2, for the network and data processing;\ne) Codes 8 and 9 for water, agroecology, pollinator refuges and food requirement;\nf) Code C.1-Annex A for the production aspects within its competence;\ng) Codes 10, 12, 14 and 15 for territory, care, safety and the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, every Circle 1 shall carry out a census, by aggregate and non-profiling means, of:\na) the wild fauna detected;\nb) the wild pollinators detected;\nc) the known nesting sites, keeping the detailed coordinates confidential;\nd) the land eligible for the establishment of a sanctuary;\ne) the working and farmed animals present;\nf) the number and species of proximity companion animals;\ng) the main ecological and competence dependencies.\nWithin the same time limit a minimal and provisional version of the Biodiversity Register shall be activated.\n3. Within twelve months of entry into force, every Circle 1 shall activate:\na) the Biodiversity Council;\nb) the minimum Biodiversity STV;\nc) the Refuge Sanctuaries STV, the Collaborating Animals Welfare STV jointly with Code 9 and, in every Circle 2, the refuge sanctuaries provided for by Article 4-bis, paragraph 5;\nd) the Domestic Welfare STV;\ne) the Wild Bees STV;\nf) the necessary Safety Sheets and Welfare Sheets;"],[18,"art-11--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 11 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","g) the node of the federated biodiversity network.\nIn the event of a documented overlap of the transitional burdens relating to Codes 9, 10 and 13, the Assembly may grant a reasoned extension not exceeding six months. The extension shall be accompanied by a binding plan with persons responsible and intermediate deadlines and may not entail the interruption of the essential functions of the Basket.\n4. Activities existing on the date of entry into force shall be subject to a conformity check. The Biodiversity Council shall carry out the technical assessment and transmit the outcomes to the competent bodies in accordance with Code 4. In the event of non-conformity:\na) a time limit for compliance of not less than thirty days shall be assigned, except in cases of serious and immediate risk;\nb) the compliance plan shall indicate the measures to be adopted, the persons responsible and a final deadline, as a rule not exceeding ninety days, except in the case of documented technical impossibility;\nc) where there is a serious and immediate risk to biodiversity or animal welfare, proportionate urgent measures may be adopted in accordance with the procedures of Code 4;\nd) where compliance is technically impossible, the deactivation procedure provided for by Code 4 shall be initiated, accompanied by a substitute plan;"],[18,"art-11--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 11 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","e) if the function concerned directly guarantees provisions of the Basket of level 1 or 2, deactivation shall be ordered only after the activation of a compliant substitute function, except for the urgent measures necessary for safety, in accordance with Codes 4 and 14.\n5. In the event of normative overlap, precedence operates exclusively within the subject matter assigned to each Code:\na) Code 13 prevails as regards animal welfare parameters, biodiversity indicators, the biological functionality of corridors, the ecological criteria for the management of sanctuaries and the impact assessment as regards the aspects of biodiversity and welfare;\nb) Code 10 prevails as regards intended use, location, the geometry of interventions, the Land Register and territorial constraints;\nc) Code 9 prevails as regards agroecological use, crops, the technical management of pollinator refuges and food requirement;\nd) Code 8 prevails as regards the functional management of water;\ne) Code 6 prevails as regards health risks, zoonoses, clinical nutrition and pharmaceutical matters;\nf) Code 14 prevails as regards the general safety of persons, infrastructure and tasks."],[18,"art-11--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 11 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","No precedence clause transfers or absorbs competences assigned to other Codes. Conflicts that cannot be resolved by coordinated interpretation shall be submitted to the competent bodies in accordance with the procedures of the legal order; in the meantime, proportionate measures shall be adopted to protect the persons, animals and ecological functions concerned."],[19,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-allocation-of-competences","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and allocation of competences","","1. This Code governs prevention of and protection from the general risks affecting persons, infrastructure, equipment, machinery, tasks and shared living and working environments, within the limits of the competences expressly assigned to it. It also governs the Civic Protection Corps and the blind video surveillance provided for in Articles 17 and 18 of the Constitution and the personal defence provided for in Article 17.9 of the Constitution.\n2. For the purposes of this Code:\na) general safety means prevention of and protection from mechanical risks, electrical risks, risks of fire, of structural failure, of work at height, of access to confined spaces, of handling, of the use of equipment and machinery, and from any other risk not expressly assigned to other Codes;\nb) Safety STV means the Technical Validation Sheet, adopted in accordance with Article 3-bis of Code 2, that establishes the criteria, parameters, thresholds, assessment methods and minimum measures of general safety;\nc) General Safety Sheet means the operational technical document that applies the STVs to a specific activity, machine, item of equipment, worksite, environment or task;\nd) personal protective equipment (PPE) means the equipment assigned free of charge to the person where provided for by the General Safety Sheet;\ne) collective protection means the device, installation, provision or set of technical measures intended to protect several persons at the same time;"],[19,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-allocation-of-competences","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and allocation of competences","","f) risk means the combination of the severity of the potential harm, the probability of its occurrence and the exposure of persons, assessed in accordance with the formula and the parameters established by the Safety STV;\ng) near miss means an event that has not caused harm to persons, but that could have caused such harm by reason of the circumstances in which it occurred;\nh) substantial modification means a change to the activity, the equipment, the machine, the environment, the task, the organisation or the operating conditions that is capable of altering the level or the nature of the risk.\n3. The Safety STV classifies risk into the following levels:\na) R0 — negligible risk: requires the application of the general measures of prudence defined by the STV;\nb) R1 — ordinary risk: requires a simplified Safety Sheet and information at level L1;\nc) R2 — significant risk: requires a full Safety Sheet, specific training and the use of the PPE or collective protections provided for;\nd) R3 — serious risk: requires a full Safety Sheet, training at level L2, reinforced attendance or supervision and reinforced periodic inspections;\ne) R4 — serious and immediate risk: requires the immediate suspension of the activity concerned and the adoption of urgent corrective measures in accordance with Articles 9 and 10.\n4. For the purposes of the allocation of competences:\na) this Code governs the general safety parameters indicated in paragraph 2, letter a);"],[19,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-allocation-of-competences","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and allocation of competences","","b) ecological risks, biological risks and risks relating to animal welfare are governed by Code 13;\nc) health, clinical, zoonotic, clinical nutrition and pharmaceutical risks are governed by Code 6;\nd) territorial risks, hydrogeological risks and risks relating to land take are dealt with in coordination with Codes 10 and 8, in accordance with their respective competences;\ne) the operational safety of agroecological activities is governed by Code 9, in compliance with the general parameters established by this Code.\n5. The classification of a risk or of a measure pursuant to this Article does not transfer the substantive competence assigned to another Code. Where an activity presents risks belonging to several subject matters, each Code retains competence over its own parameters and the Councils concerned shall operate in coordination."],[19,"art-2--principles","Art. 2 — Principles","","1. Prevention at source. Every activity, environment or item of equipment shall be designed and organised so as to eliminate the risk or reduce it to the minimum level technically possible.\n2. Proportionality. Safety measures shall be proportionate to the severity of the potential harm, to the probability of its occurrence, to the level of exposure, to the number of persons exposed and to the competences available.\n3. Operational precaution. Where there is documented scientific or technical uncertainty as to the compatibility of an activity with general safety, a precautionary measure shall be adopted that is proportionate, effective in relation to the estimated risk and the least invasive among those capable of keeping the risk within the limits established by the STVs in force. The measure shall remain applicable until sufficient elements are available to review the assessment.\n4. Non-commodification. Any form of rent on essential PPE, on critical collective protections, on life-saving L1 training, on the Safety STVs and on the Safety Sheets in their essential function is prohibited. Essential STVs and Sheets shall not be placed behind a paywall. Access to technical data may be restricted only where their dissemination entails a documented risk to safety; the restriction shall be proportionate and shall not prevent access by those who have an operational need for the data.\n5. Traceability and maintenance. Machinery, relevant equipment, critical PPE and critical collective protections shall be inventoried and subjected to periodic inspections and documented maintenance in accordance with the applicable STV.\n6. Right of interruption. Anyone who detects a serious and immediate risk to a person may interrupt the activity concerned and shall report it without delay to the competent Councils. An interruption carried out in good faith shall not give rise to retaliation or detriment."],[19,"art-3--safety-council","Art. 3 — Safety Council","","1. Each Circle 1 shall establish a Safety Council composed of three to five members with competences of at least level L2 or L3 in one or more of the following subject matters: occupational safety, fire prevention, electrical risk, mechanical risk, building and worksites, organisation of tasks.\n2. The members of the Council shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3, shall remain in office for one year and shall not be drawn by lot again for the same Council before twelve months have elapsed from the end of their term of office.\n3. The Council shall operate with at least two thirds of its members present, rounded up. Proposals and discretionary decisions shall be adopted in accordance with Article 12-bis, paragraph 9, of Code 3, by the consensus method and after publication for at least seventy-two hours. Bound technical acts, opinions and the validation of Safety Sheets shall be adopted by a simple majority of those present, unless this Code or an STV approved in accordance with the competent procedures requires a higher majority for the validation of critical Safety Sheets. Every meeting shall be minuted and the minutes shall be kept for twenty-four months.\n4. In the event of unavailability, conflict of interest or coincidence between the supervisory role and direct operational responsibility for the activity, the member concerned shall declare his or her position and abstain. Replacement shall take place in accordance with Article 12-bis of Code 3. A person who validates a Safety Sheet shall not be the sole person operationally responsible for the activity subject to validation: in that case joint verification by a second, independent L2 or L3 member is required.\n5. The Safety Council shall:\na) keep the register of general risks and the inventory of critical machinery and equipment;\nb) draw up and update the Safety STV, the PPE and Collective Protections STV, the Machinery STV and the Worksites STV;\nc) validate the General Safety Sheets;\nd) give a mandatory opinion on the plans of Codes 9, 10, 12 and 13, limited to the aspects of general safety;\ne) feed the Dependency Register governed by Code 15;\nf) organise the L1, L2 and L3 training within its competence;\ng) coordinate its activity with the Health Council of Code 6, the Biodiversity Council of Code 13, the Habitat and Territory Council of Code 10, the Agroecological Council of Code 9 and the Care Council of Code 12.\n6. The mandatory opinions of the Safety Council, where negative, are binding pursuant to Article 8 of Code 3 exclusively as regards the general safety parameters assigned to this Code. They shall not modify or replace health, ecological, territorial or production parameters assigned to other Codes."],[19,"art-4--general-safety-sheets-ppe-collective-protections-and-machinery","Art. 4 — General Safety Sheets, PPE, collective protections and machinery","","1. No activity classified R1 or higher may be carried out without a General Safety Sheet in force that applies the relevant STV. Activities classified R0 shall comply with the general measures of prudence established by the STV.\n2. Every General Safety Sheet shall contain at least:\na) the description of the activity, the task, the environment, the machinery and the equipment involved;\nb) the level of risk determined pursuant to Article 1, paragraph 3;\nc) an indication of the risks identified and of the prevention and protection measures;\nd) the list of the PPE and collective protections required;\ne) the level of training necessary;\nf) the operating and emergency procedures;\ng) the date of issue, the version, the person responsible for the issue and the date scheduled for revision;\nh) the circumstances that require an early revision;\ni) the outcome of the last inspection, where one is provided for;\nj) a reversibility plan, where technically applicable.\n3. The Sheet shall be validated by the Safety Council. The mandatory opinion of the Health Council is required for the health aspects and that of the Biodiversity Council for the ecological aspects, where relevant. The opinion shall remain confined to the subject matter within the competence of the Council consulted."],[19,"art-4--general-safety-sheets-ppe-collective-protections-and-machinery","Art. 4 — General Safety Sheets, PPE, collective protections and machinery","","4. The Sheet shall be reviewed in the event of a substantial modification and, in any case, within the maximum time limit established by the STV. If the time limit for revision has expired or a substantial modification has occurred that has not been assessed, the Sheet shall not be used to start new activities or to continue the activity in ways that are no longer assessed. The Council shall identify without delay the temporary measures necessary and, for essential services, shall activate a compliant substitute arrangement where technically possible. The resumption of ordinary activity requires the revalidation of the Sheet in accordance with the applicable procedures and Code 4.\n5. PPE and collective protections are safety commons and shall be assigned free of charge where provided for by the Sheet. They shall be subjected to maintenance and recorded in a dedicated register, kept for twelve months. Ownership, responsibility for custody and financing are defined by Code 6 and by the resolutions of the Assembly adopted on a proposal from the Safety Council. The absence of an item of PPE or of a collective protection classified as essential shall entail the immediate suspension of the activity concerned.\n6. Every critical machine or item of equipment shall have:\na) a logbook or equivalent technical documentation;\nb) periodic inspection in accordance with the STV;\nc) documented maintenance;\nd) a register of use, where required by the STV."],[19,"art-4--general-safety-sheets-ppe-collective-protections-and-machinery","Art. 4 — General Safety Sheets, PPE, collective protections and machinery","","A critical machine or item of equipment without a periodic inspection in force shall not be used.\n7. Temporary worksites shall be governed by a specific Safety Sheet that indicates at least the perimeter, the signage, the PPE, the collective protections, the emergency procedures and the evacuation plan."],[19,"art-5--areas-of-risk","Art. 5 — Areas of risk","","1. Mechanical risk. The assessment shall consider at least the guarding of moving parts, the emergency stop devices, the locking and isolation procedures and, where provided for by the STV, the lockout/tagout procedures.\n2. Electrical risk. The assessment shall consider at least insulation, disconnection, the inspection of installations and the qualification of the persons assigned. Live working is prohibited, except under a specific L3 procedure validated in accordance with the STV.\n3. Fire risk. The measures shall include, in accordance with the STV, the assessment of the fire load, prevention, escape routes, fire extinguishers, detection and alarm systems, the evacuation plan and periodic drills.\n4. Work at height. An activity is considered work at height where there is a risk of falling from one level to another. Where the difference in level exceeds two metres with respect to a stable level, or where the STV so prescribes by reason of the specific risk, the minimum collective protection measures and the fall-arrest PPE established by the STV are mandatory.\n5. Confined spaces. Access to a confined space requires at least an entry permit, the testing of the atmosphere and of gases, ventilation where necessary, continuous external attendance, recovery equipment and a specific Sheet for risk R3 or higher.\n6. Other risks. For the handling of loads, noise, vibrations, non-pharmaceutical chemical agents and further sources of risk, the STV shall define the thresholds, the preventive and protective measures and the inspections necessary. For the health aspects, coordination with the Health Council of Code 6 is required, without transferring to the latter competence over the general safety parameters."],[19,"art-6--training","Art. 6 — Training","","1. L1 training in basic safety and life-saving first response is mandatory for access to activities classified R1 or higher and, as regards the aspects of general safety, to sanctuaries, worksites, workshops and critical storehouses.\n2. L2 training is required for R3 activities and for the functions of operational attendance or supervision identified by the STV.\n3. L3 training is required for the functions of designing the STVs, validating critical Safety Sheets and second-level audit.\n4. Life-saving L1 training is free of charge and shall not be made subject to a paywall. Training shall be recorded in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2, with access only to the data necessary to verify that it has taken place and that it is valid."],[19,"art-7--measurement-accidents-and-near-misses","Art. 7 — Measurement, accidents and near misses","","1. Measurement and the collection of data are permitted exclusively in order to prevent risks, verify the effectiveness of the measures and protect general safety. The data shall be processed in aggregate and non-profiling form, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2, and kept for the minimum period established by the STV.\n2. The following are established:\na) a confidential operational register, containing the data necessary for the analysis of causes, including identifying data where indispensable. Access is reserved to the competent Councils and retention shall not exceed thirty-six months, except in the case of a documented need connected with ongoing proceedings;\nb) an aggregate public register, which contains non-nominative data used for learning and federated prevention.\n3. For every serious accident or accident classified R4, the Safety Council shall carry out an analysis of causes within seven days and shall adopt, within the scope of its competences, reasoned corrective technical measures. Where the analysis cannot be concluded within that time limit owing to a lack of data or the need for external competences, an interim report shall be drawn up within seven days, identifying the immediate precautionary measures and setting a final time limit for the analysis, as a rule not exceeding a further fourteen days.\n4. The technical measures adopted pursuant to paragraph 3 do not replace the findings, the remedies or the guarantee procedures governed by Code 4.\n5. The possibility of reporting near misses anonymously is guaranteed. A person who reports in good faith shall not suffer retaliation or detriment."],[19,"art-8--safety-dependency-register","Art. 8 — Safety Dependency Register","","1. The Safety Council shall feed the Dependency Register governed by Code 15, distinguishing at least:\na) competence dependencies, relating to the unavailability in the Basin of the L2 or L3 competences necessary in matters of safety;\nb) material or technological dependencies, relating to the unavailability of critical PPE, collective protections, inspection equipment, diagnostic instruments or essential technical materials;\nc) dependencies on external services, relating to the unavailability of inspections or certifications that cannot be carried out internally.\n2. For every critical dependency an exit plan shall be prepared, indicating the objective, the person responsible, the definite final time limit and the intermediate risk-reduction measures. The plan shall be approved by the Assembly on a proposal from the Safety Council, after obtaining the opinion of the other competent Councils where the dependency also concerns their subject matters.\n3. If overcoming the dependency within the time limit laid down is not realistically possible owing to a documented technical impossibility, the Council shall submit a reasoned review to the Assembly. The review shall indicate the new time limit proposed, the reasons for the impossibility and the compensatory measures necessary to contain the risk.\n4. At least one annual federated audit of the autonomy of safety shall be carried out, entrusted to independent L3 members drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4."],[19,"art-8-bis--civic-protection-corps","Art. 8-bis — Civic Protection Corps","","1. The Civic Protection Corps is the temporary, unarmed and non-violent civic function provided for in Article 17 of the Constitution. It is neither a police force nor a permanent body. It is activated by Circle 1 or, for supra-local functions, by Circle 2 and is answerable to the Circle that activated it. Activation for scheduled interventions, including for a specified service or period, shall be resolved by the Assembly of Circle 1 or, for supra-local functions, by the General Assembly. In case of urgency, the intervention shall be initiated by the reference person on duty of paragraph 5 or, if that person cannot be reached in time, by a member on duty, at the call of anyone or on their own initiative, and shall be communicated without delay to the Circle; the review and validation of Article 8-quater, paragraph 2, count as ratification of the urgent activation.\n2. The Corps shall perform, with rotation among its members, the following functions:\na) Technical Rescue and Firefighting;\nb) Civil and Environmental Protection;\nc) Protection of the Commons;\nd) Mediation and Non-Violent Containment;\ne) Community Medical and Veterinary Rescue.\n3. The members of the Corps shall be drawn by lot from among the volunteers who have completed the training in non-violent mediation, de-escalation, rescue and confederal law defined by the Civic Protection STV, validated in accordance with Code 5, and whom the Assembly has declared suitable. Every person drawn by lot may refuse.\n4. A turn of service shall not exceed ninety days and shall not be renewable consecutively. A person who has served a turn shall not take up coordination offices in the following twelve months, pursuant to Article 17.7 of the Constitution. The time of the turn of service shall be counted towards the CBO.\n5. The Safety Council shall be responsible for the training, the equipment and the Safety Sheets for interventions. It shall not direct interventions and shall not have the Corps at its disposal. Every intervention shall be coordinated by a reference person on duty, identified by rotation among the members of the Corps.\n6. The Assembly may revoke the mandate or dissolve the Corps at any time."],[19,"art-8-ter--equipment-and-rules-of-intervention","Art. 8-ter — Equipment and rules of intervention","","1. The equipment is limited to instruments for personal protection, for non-injurious neutralisation, for technical rescue and for communication. Firearms, weapons of war and instruments of torture or of injurious coercion are prohibited. The Civic Protection STV shall list exhaustively the instruments permitted and shall be adopted with the mandatory opinion of the Health Council.\n2. Containment interventions shall be based on de-escalation, interposition and non-injurious immobilisation and shall last for the time strictly necessary to bring to an end a present danger to the physical safety of persons. The use of force beyond what is strictly necessary constitutes a Grade 4 offence.\n3. The Corps shall not carry out investigations, shall not conduct surveillance and shall not gather information on persons. It shall not enter a Circle 0, unless this is strictly necessary to provide rescue or to bring to an end a present danger to the life or to the physical or sexual integrity of a person. The entry shall be communicated immediately to the Guarantee Tribunal and shall be subject to the validation within twenty-four hours provided for by Article 34, paragraph 2, of Code 4. Outside these cases the Corps shall enter a Circle 0 only with the reasoned authorisation of the Guarantee Tribunal, in order to carry out a protection measure ordered in accordance with Code 4 or Code 12, where this is strictly necessary for the protection of the inviolable rights of a person.\n4. Every restriction of a person’s freedom of movement shall be communicated without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal and shall be subject to the validation within twenty-four hours provided for in Article 34 of Code 4. For persons in acute crisis and for health isolation and quarantine, only the validation provided for, respectively, by Article 8, paragraph 7, and by Article 2-bis, paragraph 3-quater, of Code 6 applies. The review of the activation provided for by Article 8-quater, paragraph 2, remains unaffected in every case.\n5. For persons in acute crisis the non-coercive protocol of Article 8 of Code 6 shall apply. For minors and frail persons Code 12 shall apply.\n6. In the event of external aggression the Corps shall coordinate the non-violent civil defence provided for in Article 16.4 of the Constitution: interposition, non-cooperation, protection and evacuation of persons. The use of weapons remains prohibited."],[19,"art-8-quater--recording-review-and-validation-of-interventions","Art. 8-quater — Recording, review and validation of interventions","","1. Every intervention shall be recorded in full, with respect for the rights of the persons involved. The recordings shall be encrypted and processed in accordance with Code 7.\n2. Every activation of the Corps, including every intervention carried out under an activation for a service or a period, shall be subject to review within twenty-four hours and to validation by two neighbouring Communities unconnected with the event, pursuant to Article 17.6 of the Constitution. Each Community shall designate three persons by sortition. The review shall be carried out by the six persons so designated, who shall examine the recording of the intervention and hear the reference person on duty and, if they so request, the person subject to the intervention. The activation is validated if it is approved by the majority of the persons designated by each of the two Communities. If the review is not concluded within the time limit, the activation shall be deemed not validated pursuant to paragraph 4.\n3. The recordings shall be published within forty-eight hours in an inspectable form. Data identifying the person subject to the intervention, injured persons, minors and uninvolved third parties shall be redacted. The full version is accessible only to the persons responsible for the review and validation of paragraph 2, to the Guarantee Tribunal and to the other bodies of Code 4 competent for the fact and, for the part concerning them, to the person subject to the intervention. Access to the full version is logged in accordance with Code 7.\n4. Failure to validate shall cause the effects of the intervention to cease and shall be reported to the Guarantee Circle for the assessments under Code 4.\n5. Every quarter an aggregate report shall be published on the interventions, on the use of force and on the outcomes of the validations."],[19,"art-8-quinquies--blind-video-surveillance-containment-and-transitional-regime","Art. 8-quinquies — Blind video surveillance, containment and transitional regime","","1. Video surveillance of public spaces is permitted exclusively in the blind and reversible form of Article 18.3 of the Constitution. Installation shall be resolved by the Assembly with a Dossier and requires an STV and the binding opinion of the Network Council on the data-processing aspects.\n2. The streams shall be encrypted with a split key and automatically overwritten after seventy-two hours. The fragments of the key shall be held in custody by at least three distinct parties: the Guarantee Circle and two neighbouring Communities. No party may reconstruct the key alone.\n3. Decryption is permitted only after a serious event that has harmed the physical safety of persons or the Commons, at the reasoned request of Circle 1 and with the validation of two neighbouring Communities unconnected with the event, pursuant to Article 18.4 of the Constitution. It shall be limited to the place and the time of the event. Every access shall be recorded and inspectable; data that are not relevant shall be destroyed without delay.\n4. Biometric recognition, audio surveillance, predictive analysis of behaviour and private video surveillance directed at public spaces or Commons remain prohibited.\n5. Surveillance of the perimeter of the Containment Communities provided for in Article 32 of Code 4 shall be carried out by the Corps in accordance with this Code, without weapons and with recording of the interventions.\n6. Until the completion of the transition, the Corps shall perform functions of rescue, civil protection and mediation. Every physical intervention on persons shall be limited to what is permitted by the law in force; for every serious danger the emergency services and the competent authorities shall be alerted. Article 37 of Code 4 shall apply."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","1. In implementation of Article 17.9 of the Constitution, every person may defend themselves and other persons against an ongoing aggression against physical or sexual integrity, where the Civic Protection Corps is not present or cannot intervene in time. An aggression is ongoing also when it is imminent, that is when it is about to be carried out and the danger is concrete and immediate; the fear that it might happen is not enough. The aggression remains ongoing until it has ceased.\n2. The following are permitted, choosing among those sufficient to protect the persons the least harmful:\na) getting away from the aggression and bringing the threatened persons to safety, including by moving them away from the place;\nb) calling for help, raising the alarm and calling the Civic Protection Corps;\nc) interposing oneself between the aggressor and the person attacked;\nd) using the physical force strictly necessary and proportionate to bring the aggression to an end, without injuring beyond what is needed to end it;\ne) holding the aggressor by means of non-injurious immobilisation, without compressing their neck or chest, until the danger has ceased or, if this happens earlier, until the Civic Protection Corps arrives."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","3. The choice shall be assessed according to the circumstances as they appeared to the person who acted at the time of the fact. Getting away is not required where it would expose oneself or others to danger or would leave a person under attack without protection. Where the aggressor is a minor, a person in acute crisis or a person who is unable to understand what they are doing, bringing to safety and interposition shall be preferred and the force shall be commensurate also with their age and condition; when the Corps arrives, Article 8-ter, paragraph 5, and Article 8 of Code 6 shall apply.\n4. The defence is unarmed, pursuant to Article 16 of the Constitution. The use of weapons or of instruments intended to strike or to injure is never permitted. Ordinary objects found on the spot may always be used for shielding oneself, without striking. To strike, they may be used only where the aggression endangers life, exposes a person to serious injury or is sexual violence, only to bring it to an end and to the extent strictly necessary. No one may carry instruments for the purpose of using them against persons. The Civic Protection STV may indicate non-injurious personal alarm and protection devices.\n5. The following do not constitute defence and are classified in accordance with Article 26 of Code 4:"],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","a) an attack against a person who has not begun an aggression and is not about to carry one out within the meaning of paragraph 1, including the use of force out of the mere fear of a future aggression;\nb) revenge and punishment;\nc) the use of force after the danger has ceased, without prejudice to the excusable error provided for by Article 26-bis, paragraph 2, of Code 4;\nd) the use of force to correct, educate or discipline a person, to impose a decision on them or to prevent them from leaving a place or a Circle 0, from communicating or from seeking help, without prejudice to the holding of the aggressor provided for by paragraph 2, letter e);\ne) the reaction to insults, provocations, refusals or disobedience that do not endanger physical or sexual integrity;\nf) the use of force against a person who is in turn defending themselves within the limits of this Article.\n6. A person who began the aggression may not invoke defence against force used within the limits of this Article to bring it to an end. They may invoke it only against a use of force which, beyond those limits, endangers their life or their physical or sexual integrity."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","7. To protect property, including the Commons, only interposition, the making safe of the good or of the instrument where this is possible without force on the person, the alarm and the call to the Civic Protection Corps for the function of Protection of the Commons are permitted. The use of force on persons is not permitted. However, the ongoing destruction, contamination or putting out of service of a vital Common Good, such as a reservoir or a pipeline of drinking water, the power supply of life-saving care or a work that protects against floods or fires, constitutes an aggression against physical integrity within the meaning of paragraph 1 where it results in a concrete and immediate danger to the life or health of persons. In this case only interposition, physically moving the person away from the good and the holding of paragraph 2, letter e), are permitted, without striking. Demonstrative actions or acts of disobedience that do not give rise to such a danger do not fall within this case."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","8. Animals are not property, pursuant to Article 13.1 of the Constitution. Every person may defend an animal against ongoing ill-treatment, that is against violence that injures it or makes it suffer, with the means of paragraph 2 and under the same rules of necessity and proportionality laid down for the defence of persons by paragraphs 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6. Force is permitted only to bring the ill-treatment to an end, not to prevent future ill-treatment, to remove the animal permanently from those who care for it or to punish. Disagreements on how to feed, care for, keep or train an animal do not justify the use of force: they shall be reported to the Biodiversity Council, which shall act pursuant to Article 7, paragraphs 2 and 3, of Code 13. Ill-treatment shall also be reported to the Biodiversity Council, pursuant to Article 10, paragraph 2, of Code 13. The defence of an animal does not allow entry into a Circle 0 without the consent of those who live there: the Civic Protection Corps shall be called."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","9. A person who does not live there may enter a Circle 0 without the consent of those who live there only to bring to an end ongoing physical or sexual violence against a person, as an intervention strictly necessary for the protection of inviolable rights pursuant to Article 5.1, letter a), of the Constitution; a danger that is only imminent is not enough. The person who enters shall immediately notify the Civic Protection Corps, or have it notified by others, as soon as they can do so without leaving the person in danger, and shall leave as soon as the danger has ceased. The Corps shall transmit the notice without delay to the Guarantee Tribunal, which shall review the entry within twenty-four hours, as for the entry of the Corps provided for by Article 8-ter, paragraph 3, in accordance with Article 26-bis, paragraph 5, letter e), of Code 4. When the Corps arrives, Article 8-ter, paragraph 3, shall apply to the entry and presence of its members.\n10. After the fact, a person who has used force, has held a person or has entered a Circle 0:\na) shall provide or call for rescue for anyone who is injured, including the aggressor;"],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","b) shall report the fact without delay to the Civic Protection Corps or to the Guarantee Circle, stating the place, the persons involved and the need for rescue. The Corps shall transmit the report to the Guarantee Circle and shall not carry out investigations, pursuant to Article 8-ter, paragraph 3. Where force has been used by or against a member of the Corps, the report shall be made to the Guarantee Circle.\nThe report does not oblige the person to make other statements about the fact; Article 25, paragraph 3, of Code 4 shall apply. Failure to report does not in itself constitute an offence or an admission, except for the notice of entry into a Circle 0 provided for by paragraph 9. The assessment follows Article 26-bis of Code 4."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","11. Containment carried out by the Civic Protection Corps within the limits of Article 8-ter does not constitute aggression, even when the person contained considers it unjust: no defence is permitted against it and the containment is challenged through the review and validation provided for by Article 8-quater and by Article 34 of Code 4. Where a member of the Corps uses force beyond the limits of Article 8-ter, paragraph 2, endangering the life or the physical or sexual integrity of a person, this Article applies: anyone may interpose themselves and defend the person, and the other members present are required to interpose themselves. Article 8-ter and Article 17.5 of the Constitution, not paragraphs 2 and 3 of Article 26-bis of Code 4, apply to members of the Corps who use force during their term of service.\n12. No one is required to use force or to expose themselves to danger, and no one may ask a minor to intervene with force. A person who witnesses an aggression and can do so without danger shall call the Civic Protection Corps or raise the alarm. Minors may defend themselves and defend other persons within the limits of this Article; Article 26-bis, paragraph 7, of Code 4 applies to the facts they commit."],[19,"art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack","Art. 8-sexies — Personal defence and help for persons under attack","","13. Until the completion of the transition, personal defence is governed by the law in force, pursuant to Article 38.6 of the Constitution, and Article 37 of Code 4 shall apply. Those who have joined undertake to remain within the limits of this Article even where the law in force would allow more, in particular not to use weapons. This Article does not entail the waiver of rights or guarantees under the law in force and may not prevent or delay recourse to the authorities: a person who is attacked or has used force shall call the emergency services and the competent authorities, in addition to making the report of paragraph 10. The holding of paragraph 2, letter e), and the entry of paragraph 9 are permitted only within the limits allowed by the law in force."],[19,"art-9--prohibitions-violations-and-guarantees","Art. 9 — Prohibitions, violations and guarantees","","1. It is prohibited to:\na) carry out an activity classified R1 or higher without a General Safety Sheet in force;\nb) tamper with, remove or render ineffective guards, PPE, collective protections, signage or emergency stop devices;\nc) impose a paywall on the essential Safety STVs, on the essential Safety Sheets or on life-saving L1 training;\nd) use critical machinery or equipment without a periodic inspection in force;\ne) transfer to parties external to the Confederation the functional control of the safety system, of the register of risks or of the storehouse of critical PPE;\nf) carry out retaliation against anyone who interrupts an activity or reports in good faith a serious and immediate risk.\n2. Violations are classified as follows:\na) critical violation: entails a serious and immediate R4 risk and requires the immediate and proportionate suspension of the activity until acceptable conditions are restored;\nb) substantial violation: significantly compromises the safety system without giving rise to an immediate R4 risk. A time limit for compliance of not less than seven and not more than thirty days shall be assigned, unless the risk requires more rapid measures;\nc) ordinary violation: consists of a formal or documentary irregularity without immediate risk. A prescription shall be issued with a time limit for correction defined by the STV, as a rule not exceeding thirty days.\n3. The ascertainment of violations, the application of precautionary measures, the suspension or deactivation of activities and the remedies are governed by Code 4. Technical measures shall be reasoned and proportionate to the risk; the relevant acts shall identify the condition to be restored and the time limit for verification.\n4. Where the activity concerned guarantees services of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket, the suspension or deactivation shall be accompanied, where technically possible, by the activation of a compliant substitute function. This is without prejudice to the urgent measures necessary to deal with a serious danger, in accordance with Codes 4 and 14.\n5. An appeal to the Guarantee may be lodged in accordance with Code 4 within fourteen days of the communication of the decision or of actual knowledge of the violation. It may concern procedural defects, manifest technical unreasonableness, discrimination or disproportion. The appeal shall not automatically suspend the urgent measures necessary to prevent serious and immediate harm."],[19,"art-10--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","1. This Code is coordinated with:\na) the Constitution, Articles 3.1, 16, 17, 18 and 32;\nb) Code 1, Articles 27 and 32;\nc) Codes 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 for their respective subject matters;\nd) Code 7, Article 2.2, for the processing of data;\ne) Codes 8 and 9 for water, agroecological activities and production;\nf) Code C.1-Annex A for the aspects of production within its competence;\ng) Codes 10, 12, 13 and 15 for territory, care, biodiversity and the Dependency Register.\n2. Within six months of entry into force, each Circle 1 shall inventory:\na) the existing general risks;\nb) the critical machinery and equipment;\nc) the critical PPE and collective protections;\nd) the worksites in progress;\ne) the competences available in matters of safety.\n3. Within twelve months of entry into force, each Circle 1 shall activate:\na) the Safety Council;\nb) the minimum Safety STV;\nc) the minimum STV for PPE and collective protections;\nd) the minimum Machinery STV;\ne) the minimum Worksites STV;\nf) the Safety Sheets necessary;\ng) the node of the federated safety network;\nh) the register of accidents and near misses.\n4. In the event of a documented overlap of the transitional burdens relating to Codes 9, 10, 13 and 14, the Assembly may grant a reasoned extension not exceeding six months. The extension shall be accompanied by a binding plan with activities, persons responsible and intermediate deadlines and shall not interrupt the essential functions of the Basket."],[19,"art-10--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","5. Activities existing on the date of entry into force shall be subject to a conformity check in accordance with the procedures of Code 4. For non-compliant activities:\na) the Safety Council shall carry out the technical assessment and document the deficiencies;\nb) except in the case of serious and immediate risk, a time limit for compliance of not less than thirty days shall be assigned;\nc) in the presence of an R4 risk the immediate and proportionate suspension of the activity may be ordered;\nd) the compliance plan shall indicate the measures to be adopted and a definite final time limit, as a rule not exceeding ninety days;\ne) if compliance is technically impossible, the deactivation procedure provided for by Code 4 shall be initiated, on a proposal from the competent Council and with a substitute plan;\nf) if the activity guarantees services of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket, deactivation shall take place after the activation of the compliant substitute function, except in the case of a safety emergency governed by Codes 4 and 14.\n6. In the event of overlap, precedence is limited to the subject matters expressly assigned:\na) Code 14 prevails as regards the general safety parameters of persons, infrastructure and tasks;\nb) Code 6 prevails as regards health and zoonotic risks, clinical nutrition and pharmaceutical matters;\nc) Code 13 prevails as regards ecological parameters, biodiversity, animal welfare and biological functionality;"],[19,"art-10--transitional-provisions-coordination-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Transitional provisions, coordination and precedence","","d) Code 10 prevails as regards intended use, siting, land take, the Land Register and territorial constraints;\ne) Code 9 prevails as regards agroecological use, crops, the technical management of pollinator refuges and the food requirement;\nf) Code 8 prevails as regards the functional management of water.\n7. Where the same activity presents aspects belonging to several competences, each Code retains ownership of the parameters assigned to it. The competent Councils shall coordinate in order to prepare an integrated Safety Sheet that incorporates the binding parameters of each subject matter. Coordination does not transfer ownership of the competences.\n8. No precedence clause may be interpreted as a transfer of competences not expressly assigned."],[20,"art-1--subject-matter-premise-and-principles","Art. 1 — Subject matter, premise and principles","","1. This Code governs the calculation and offsetting of geographical differences between production and requirement in respect of the goods, resources and services included in the Sufficiency Basket referred to in Code 1. It also governs the Dependency Register and the relations of the Confederation with external parties.\n2. The Balance operates among the confederated Circles at the scale actually reached by the Confederation: that of the Basin, that of the Bioregion and, when the Confederation comprises the whole of humanity, that of the world. The expressions “Single World Balance” and “World Pool” denote the Balance and the Pool at the scale reached. Until then, relations with external parties, strategic autonomy clauses and countermeasures in the event of resource blackmail are governed by Chapter IV; transitional measures are governed by Code 16.\n3. Within the Confederation, the goods of the Sufficiency Basket shall not be the object of sale and purchase or of market exchange. Their distribution takes place through the Single World Balance. Market exchange is permitted exclusively in external relations, in accordance with Chapter IV and Code 16.\n4. The Single World Balance is founded on verifiable data, uniform criteria, traceability of resources and human decisions. Automated systems may process data and formulate proposals, but may not autonomously take allocation or transfer decisions."],[20,"art-2--accounting-definitions-and-calculation-sequence","Art. 2 — Accounting definitions and calculation sequence","","1. For the purposes of this Code, the following definitions apply:\na) P — Accountable Product: the quantity of a resource that conforms to the applicable quality standards and has been verified in accordance with the procedures of the competent Code. For goods subject to a Technical Validation Sheet, the STV provided for by Code 2 applies;\nb) B — Validated Real Need: the human requirement validated by the competent Council, including ordinary storage and transport losses and the quantities necessary for sowing, ordinary maintenance and operational continuity. B does not include the Non-Federable Technical Reserve referred to in Article 5;\nc) Gross Balance: the result of subtracting B from P, calculated according to the formula P − B;\nd) Available Balance: the positive quantity of the Gross Balance that remains after the Non-Federable Technical Reserve has been set aside. The reserve reduces only a positive balance and does not increase a deficit;\ne) Federable Balance: the part of the Available Balance that is conforming, physically available and logistically retrievable, as ascertained pursuant to Article 3;\nf) TRU — Residual Useful Time: the period during which a resource retains its usefulness for use according to the applicable standards;\ng) World Pool: the whole of the availabilities constituted by the ascertained Federable Balances;\nh) Federated Reserve: the share of the World Pool not yet assigned to a specific destination and kept for a subsequent allocation.\n2. For every resource and accounting period, the following sequence shall mandatorily be applied:\nP → B → Gross Balance → Non-Federable Technical Reserve → Available Balance → Federable Balance → World Pool → allocation.\n3. The calculation shall be carried out as follows:\na) if P − B is equal to or less than zero, the result constitutes the deficit and is not further reduced by effect of the Technical Reserve;\nb) if P − B is positive, the Technical Reserve is subtracted from the surplus up to the amount of that surplus;\nc) the remaining quantity, if positive and ascertained as physically and logistically available, constitutes the Federable Balance.\n4. The quantities necessary for sowing, ordinary maintenance and operational continuity shall be counted once only: either in the calculation of B as the ordinary requirement of the period, or as Technical Reserve pursuant to Article 5, if they meet the requirements of the latter. The same quantity may not be attributed to both items at the same time.\n5. The accounting period is quarterly. The Sufficiency Basket and the technical annexes define the units of measurement, the opening stocks, the criteria for calculating ordinary losses and the arrangements for reconciling data between consecutive periods. Logistics management may operate with more frequent updates, without prejudice to the quarterly statement of account."],[20,"art-3--declaration-and-ascertainment-of-the-federable-balance","Art. 3 — Declaration and ascertainment of the Federable Balance","","1. Each Circle 1, 2 and 3 shall declare quarterly, for each resource within its competence:\na) the Accountable Product;\nb) the Validated Real Need;\nc) the Gross Balance;\nd) the Technical Reserve set aside;\ne) the Available Balance;\nf) the proposed Federable Balance.\n2. The declarations of the different Circles shall be coordinated so as to avoid duplication of the same quantities. Each physical resource is recorded once only as an original availability; the records of Circles 2 and 3 report consolidated data and are not added again to the data of the Circles they include.\n3. The ascertainment of the Federable Balance is a technical procedure carried out by the Storehouse Council of the Circle where the resource is held in custody. The Council:\na) verifies the conformity of the declared data;\nb) verifies the physical and logistical availability of the resource;\nc) consults the STVs, the sectoral registers and the relevant Dependency Register;\nd) records the reasons for any adjustments;\ne) remains responsible for the ascertainment even when it makes use of automated verifications.\n4. The control and verification procedures follow Codes 1 and 2. The Dependency Register helps to represent the constraints and dependencies that affect availability, but does not replace the accounting and storehouse registers necessary to document P, B, stocks and transfers.\n5. The ascertained Federable Balance shall be entered in the World Pool according to pre-established objective criteria. The body competent for allocation may not modify at its discretion the technical classification of the resource as federable; it may resolve exclusively on its destination and on the arrangements for the transfer in accordance with the priorities of this Code.\n6. Entry in the Pool derives from the ascertainment of the conditions laid down by this Code and not from a political resolution on the nature of the resource."],[20,"art-4--single-world-balance","Art. 4 — Single World Balance","","1. The positive surplus ascertained as federable is held physically in custody in the storehouse of the Circle where it is located, but is accounted for as part of the World Pool. By way of example, if P is equal to 100, B is equal to 80 and no Technical Reserve needs to be set aside, the proposed Federable Balance is equal to 20, subject to verification of physical and logistical availability.\n2. An ascertained deficit activates the procedure for coverage by the World Pool in accordance with the priorities of Article 8. The initiation of the procedure is automatic; the assignment and the transfer are resolved by the competent human body pursuant to Article 9.\n3. The management of availabilities does not give rise to bilateral supply relationships between Circles. A single Logistics World Pool operates. No Circle may claim to choose the final recipient of its own surplus or to know the specific origin of the resource assigned to cover a deficit, without prejudice to the traceability and transparency obligations laid down by this Code."],[20,"art-5--non-federable-technical-reserve-and-federated-reserve","Art. 5 — Non-Federable Technical Reserve and Federated Reserve","","1. The Non-Federable Technical Reserve consists of the quantities strictly necessary for the technical and infrastructural continuity of the Circle. It may include, according to documented necessity, spare parts, energy for continuity, materials for emergency repairs and conservation seeds.\n2. The Technical Reserve is validated by the competent Council, remains excluded from the World Pool and is not ordinarily transferable. Extraordinary transfer is permitted only in the presence of a documented emergency and in accordance with the safety and coordination procedures laid down by Code 1.\n3. The maximum limit of the Technical Reserve shall be determined, for each resource, on the cumulative basis of:\na) documented technical requirement;\nb) risk of interruption of the service or of the infrastructure;\nc) time necessary to obtain a substitute resource;\nd) local restoration capacity.\nThe limit shall be reviewed at least once a year. Quantities in excess shall be reclassified as Available Balance and submitted to the ascertainment referred to in Article 3.\n4. The Federated Reserve is a part of the World Pool not yet assigned to a specific destination. It is kept exclusively for future redistribution and may not be used to create artificial scarcity or for speculative purposes.\n5. The Federated Reserve shall be recorded with an indication of the quantity, the category of the resource, the reason for setting it aside, the TRU and the date by which it is advisable to proceed with allocation. Information on location shall be published only when its disclosure does not create risks for the safety or conservation of the resource; the technical detail remains accessible to the competent bodies.\n6. Extractive Accumulation is prohibited, meaning the withholding of resources for the purpose of creating artificial scarcity, awaiting an increase in value or securing exclusive use. Only the reserves governed by this Article and any other form of setting aside expressly provided for by the competent Codes are permitted."],[20,"art-6--prevention-of-waste","Art. 6 — Prevention of waste","","1. The Management Artificial Intelligence governed by Code 7 shall signal when the waste-risk thresholds defined by the STVs are exceeded and shall indicate the remaining TRU. The signal constitutes technical support and does not replace the assessment and decision of the Storehouse Council.\n2. The Storehouse Council of the Circle where the resource is held in custody shall decide and implement, within an ordinary maximum of seventy-two hours from the signal, the necessary measures of processing or recycling; reassignment through the Pool is decided within the same time limit by the Council provided for by Article 9-bis. If reassignment is not decided in good time, the Council of the Circle of custody allocates the resource in accordance with Article 15, paragraph 2, of Code 1. The time limit shall be reduced when necessary in order to intervene before the TRU expires and may never result in the intervention taking place after the resource has lost its usefulness.\n3. When the TRU is less than seven days, the intervention shall be ordered without delay, taking into account the actual times required for transport, processing and use of the resource.\n4. The loss of a Federated Reserve through omission or unjustified delay in reassignment constitutes damage to the Confederation and shall be dealt with in accordance with Code 4."],[20,"art-7--global-minimum-necessary-labour","Art. 7 — Global Minimum Necessary Labour","","1. For each Circle, the requirement of labour necessary for the production and management of resources shall be determined according to the following formula:\nNecessary Labour = Validated Local Need + Share of Global Need assigned by the World Pool.\n2. This Code determines the overall requirement of labour necessary for the management of the federated share. The assignment of persons, teams and organisational arrangements is governed by Code 1, according to criteria of proximity, competence and safety.\n3. The working time actually necessary for the production, storage, preparation and transfer of the federated share shall be recognised for the purposes of the CBO in accordance with the rules of Code 1. Such activity does not constitute unpaid labour.\n4. The share of CBO attributable to the Pool shall be calculated on the basis of the labour actually necessary and documented; it shall not be determined by means of an automatic conversion between physical units of product and hours of labour."],[20,"art-8--calculation-proposal-and-allocation-priorities","Art. 8 — Calculation, proposal and allocation priorities","","1. The AI of the Intermediate Circle provided for by Article 22.2 of the Constitution shall draw up, for its own Circle 2, at least every twenty-four hours, an updated map of the federable availabilities and of the declared deficits. The maps of the Circles 2 are exchanged through the open protocols of Article 5-ter, paragraph 3, of Code 7 and, between Extended Circles, through the distributed protocol of Article 19.7 of the Constitution; no system brings them together in a central model, pursuant to Article 5-ter, paragraph 2, of Code 7. The map shall consider at least:\na) quantity and location of the resources;\nb) energy necessary for each transfer;\nc) perishability and TRU;\nd) urgency and level of the need;\ne) capacity and safety of the logistics infrastructure.\nThe system formulates proposals for an optimised flow, but does not take allocation or transfer decisions.\n2. Within the World Pool, proposals shall be ordered according to the following priorities:\na) immediate vital needs, in accordance with Article 32 of Code 1;\nb) resources whose imminent TRU entails a concrete risk of waste, in accordance with Article 6;\nc) productive needs indispensable for maintaining the necessary productive capacity;\nd) constitution or replenishment of the federated reserves for foreseeable events.\n3. The hierarchy in paragraph 2 governs exclusively the allocation internal to the Pool and does not replace the general hierarchy of activities established by Article 32 of Code 1. In the event of conflict, the latter prevails. Where priority is equal, preference shall be given to the logistics route that requires less energy, provided that it respects the time limits, the safety and the integrity of the resource.\n4. Each proposal shall indicate the data used, the priorities applied, the alternatives considered and any margins of uncertainty. Proposals and resolutions shall be kept so as to allow their subsequent verification."],[20,"art-9--competent-bodies-and-formation-of-decisions","Art. 9 — Competent bodies and formation of decisions","","1. Pursuant to Article 38 of Code 1, the Management Artificial Intelligence is an instrument of calculation, optimisation and proposal. No flow proposal shall become enforceable without a resolution of the competent human body.\n2. The functions are distributed as follows:\na) ascertainment of the Federable Balance: competent local Storehouse Council, pursuant to Article 3;\nb) drawing up of flow proposals: Management Artificial Intelligence, under human supervision;\nc) allocation and transfer decision: Storehouse Council of Circle 2 or of Circle 3, in accordance with Article 9-bis, within the criteria approved by the General Assembly; decisions exceeding the criteria fall to the General Assembly;\nd) reasoned opposition to the transfer: Storehouse Council of the transferring Circle, also at the request of the Assembly, within the time limits and on the grounds provided for by Article 11;\ne) verification of the opposition: Review Panel constituted pursuant to Article 17 of Code 4 from among persons unconnected with the Circles concerned.\n3. The transfer resolution shall identify the resource, the quantity, the time limit, the planned route and the measures necessary to guarantee its delivery and integrity.\n4. The deliberative body may not alter the technical criteria for ascertaining the balance outside the verification and challenge procedures provided for. If errors emerge in the data or in the ascertainment, the decision shall be postponed or recalculated in accordance with the applicable procedures."],[20,"art-9-bis--storehouse-councils-of-circles-2-and-3","Art. 9-bis — Storehouse Councils of Circles 2 and 3","","1. In implementation of Articles 5.1, letters c) and d), and 10.5 of the Constitution, each Circle 2 and each Circle 3 establishes a Storehouse Council, which decides on the allocation and transfer of the resources of the Pool:\na) the Council of Circle 2 decides on transfers between the Base Communities of its Basin;\nb) the Council of Circle 3 decides on transfers between different Circles 2 included in its area;\nc) transfers between different Circles 3 are decided by concurring resolutions of the Councils of the Circles 3 concerned.\n2. The Council is composed of five to seven persons with validated competence in storehouse management or logistics, drawn by lot from among the persons of the Communities included in the Circle. No more than two members belong to the same Base Community or, for Circle 3, to the same Circle 2. Paragraphs 4, 5, 8, 9 and 11 of Article 12-bis of Code 3 apply. Revocation falls to the General Assembly of the Circle.\n3. The Council decides in compliance with the priorities of Article 8 and with the criteria approved each year by the General Assembly of the Circle pursuant to Article 14 of Code 3, with the Dossier of Article 33 of the Constitution. The criteria indicate at least:\na) the application of the priorities of Article 8 to the resources of the Circle;\nb) the size and the times of use of the Federated Reserve;\nc) the criteria for choosing between destinations of equal priority;"],[20,"art-9-bis--storehouse-councils-of-circles-2-and-3","Art. 9-bis — Storehouse Councils of Circles 2 and 3","","d) the quantities or the values in UCR beyond which the decision falls to the General Assembly.\n4. Decisions that comply with the criteria are bound technical acts within the meaning of Article 12-bis, paragraph 9, of Code 3 and do not require a new Dossier. Decisions that exceed the criteria or the limits of letter d) of paragraph 3 fall to the General Assembly, which in cases of urgency resolves pursuant to Article 14, paragraph 9, of Code 3. Until the first criteria are approved, the Council decides only on immediate vital needs and on resources at risk of waste, in accordance with Article 8, paragraph 2, letters a) and b).\n5. The Council decides on flow proposals within forty-eight hours; on immediate vital needs without delay and in any case within twenty-four hours; on resources at risk of waste within the time limit of Article 6. If the Council does not decide in time on an immediate vital need, the Storehouse Council of the Circle where the resource is held in custody may execute the flow proposal that complies with Article 8 and with the criteria; the execution is recorded and communicated without delay to the competent Council. For resources at risk of waste, Article 6, paragraph 2, applies."],[20,"art-9-bis--storehouse-councils-of-circles-2-and-3","Art. 9-bis — Storehouse Councils of Circles 2 and 3","","6. The decisions are communicated to the Circles concerned. The opposition of Article 11 and the verification of Article 9, paragraph 2, letter e), remain in force; each Community concerned may request the same verification against an allocation decision. The request does not suspend the transfer, without prejudice to Article 11, paragraph 3.\n7. During the transition the Council of Circle 2 is established in the Basin and that of Circle 3 in the minimum Federation, pursuant to Article 1, paragraphs 5 and 6, of Code 16."],[20,"art-10--federal-logistics","Art. 10 — Federal logistics","","1. The inter-Circle and interregional transport of the resources of the Basket shall ordinarily take place by means of the federated railway network provided for by Article 11.2 of the Constitution, as a mode with high capacity and lower energy cost, when it is suitable for the resource and compatible with the delivery times.\n2. Last-mile transport, from the railway station to the local Storehouse, is governed by Code 1 and may make use, according to availability and safety, of cargo bikes, electric vehicles, tractors and trolleys.\n3. Every transfer shall be recorded, indicating at least the type and quantity of the resource, the Circle of origin and the Circle of destination, the energy used and the CBO necessary. The public data shall not include information whose disclosure could compromise the safety of persons or of infrastructure; the logistical detail is accessible to the competent bodies.\n4. The modes of transport shall comply with the applicable Safety Sheets and with the parameters of the Codes competent in matters of health, environment, territory and the resource transported."],[20,"art-11--opposition-transparency-prohibition-of-blackmail-and-guarantee-of-vital-needs","Art. 11 — Opposition, transparency, prohibition of blackmail and guarantee of vital needs","","1. Every proposal for withdrawal from the World Pool shall be communicated to the Assemblies and to the Storehouse Councils of the Circles concerned. The Storehouse Council of the transferring Circle, also at the request of the Assembly, may lodge a reasoned opposition within seventy-two hours of the communication, indicating:\na) the documented local emergency;\nb) the concrete risk that the transfer would compromise a vital need or an essential technical continuity;\nc) the elements demonstrating an error in the ascertainment or in the classification of the resource;\nd) any alternatives available in the Pool.\n2. The opposition does not constitute a definitive veto, nor may it be based on a political preference or on the wish to withhold a surplus. It activates the verification provided for by Article 9, paragraph 2, letter e), in accordance with the procedures of Code 4.\n3. Pending the verification, the transfer may be suspended for the time strictly necessary when the documentation submitted indicates a concrete and immediate risk. The suspension shall be reasoned, limited in time and reviewed as a matter of urgency. It may not be used to prevent indefinitely the allocation of a federable resource.\n4. The verification shall consider whether the local emergency is well founded, the accuracy of the data, the possibility of replacing the resource with another availability of the Pool and the effects of the suspension on the recipient need. The outcome shall be reasoned and communicated to the Circles concerned.\n5. It is prohibited to make the cession or transfer of a resource belonging to the Pool conditional on obtaining another benefit, a political decision or an advantage. Such conduct constitutes resource blackmail.\n6. In the event of ascertained blackmail, the measures and sanctions shall be applied in accordance with Code 4 and shall bear on the responsibility of the body or of the persons responsible. They may not result in the logistical isolation of the Circle or in the suspension or limitation of its members’ access to vital goods.\n7. Transparency on flows shall be ensured by means of verifiable records of availabilities, decisions and transfers, with due respect for logistical security, data protection and the competences established by Codes 7 and 14."],[20,"art-12--dependency-register","Art. 12 — Dependency Register","","1. The Dependency Register is hereby established, as the federated register of the Confederation’s dependencies on goods, services, competences, infrastructure and ecological functions that are external or not substitutable. It is kept in every Circle 1 by the Storehouse Council and is consolidated in Circles 2 and 3 without duplication.\n2. The Register is fed by the Councils in accordance with the sectoral Codes and, for proprietary dependencies, in accordance with Article 5 of Code 2. Each Council remains responsible for the data in its own field.\n3. For each dependency the Register shall indicate at least:\na) the description and the category: material, technological, logistical, energy, water, competence-related, ecological, territorial or on external parties;\nb) the function and the level of the Basket concerned;\nc) the classification pursuant to paragraph 4;\nd) the person responsible and the continuity measures.\n4. A dependency is critical when its interruption would compromise the Basket or a function of levels 1 to 3 and no alternative is available within the time threshold defined by the applicable STV. Absolute critical proprietary dependency remains prohibited pursuant to Article 5 of Code 2.\n5. Every critical dependency shall be accompanied by an Exit Plan indicating the verifiable objective, the person responsible, the resources, the intermediate measures and a definite final time limit. The Plan is approved by the Assembly on a proposal from the competent Council. If exit is not technically possible within the time limit, the Plan shall be reviewed with a statement of reasons; the review may not be limited to extending the dependency.\n5-bis. For raw materials, the Exit Plan indicates reduction of the requirement, reuse, recycling and recovery. It may not indicate new extraction. Paragraph 5 applies.\n6. The Register shall be published in aggregate form. Information that exposes vulnerabilities of infrastructure is accessible in accordance with Article 15 of Code 3.\n7. Dependencies on persons. A critical competence dependency exists where a function of levels 1 to 3, or the administration of a critical infrastructure, can be performed by fewer than three trained persons in Circle 1 or, for shared functions, in Circle 2. The Exit Plan consists in the training of other persons in accordance with Code 5 and in the documentation of the function in the Knowledge Storehouse. Until its completion the function shall be ensured also by means of the federated sourcing and remote mentoring of Article 13."],[20,"art-13--federated-sourcing-of-competences-and-remote-mentoring","Art. 13 — Federated sourcing of competences and remote mentoring","","1. When an L2 or L3 competence necessary for a function of levels 1 to 3 is not available in the Circle, the competent Council shall activate federated sourcing by means of a request on the Network to Circle 2 and, if necessary, to Circle 3. The reply shall be given within twenty-four hours for vital emergencies and within seventy-two hours in other cases.\n2. Sourcing shall take place, as the case may be, by means of:\na) sending the person concerned to the competent service;\nb) temporarily sending the competence to the requesting Circle;\nc) remote mentoring supervised by an L3 person, when it is appropriate and safe.\n3. The time given for the benefit of another Circle shall be counted towards the CBO pursuant to Article 30 of Code 1 and Article 7 of this Code.\n4. The shortage shall be entered in the Register as a competence dependency and shall be accompanied by a training plan in accordance with Code 5.\n5. Until the competence is available in the Confederation, recourse to external services follows Chapter III of Code 2."],[20,"art-14--autonomy-audits","Art. 14 — Autonomy audits","","1. The annual federated audits provided for by the sectoral Codes shall be coordinated in an annual Circle 2 plan. The persons appointed shall be drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4.\n2. The audit shall verify the completeness of the Register, the progress of the Exit Plans and the emergence of new dependencies. The report shall be published; the Circle concerned has fifteen days in which to submit observations. An appeal to the Guarantee Circle is permitted in accordance with Code 4.\n3. The outcomes of the audits are relevant for the purposes of the thresholds of Code 16."],[20,"art-15--scope-and-principles","Art. 15 — Scope and principles","","1. This Chapter governs the relations of the Circles with persons, entities, enterprises and authorities external to the Confederation, until such time as the Confederation comprises the whole of humanity.\n2. External relations are founded on the following principles:\na) legality: the legal order in force in the territory and fiscal and contractual obligations shall be respected;\nb) non-violence and cooperation: those who do not join shall not be treated as adversaries;\nc) transparency and traceability;\nd) progressive reduction of dependencies;\ne) prohibition of rent and of profit.\n3. Exchanges with the outside serve exclusively to meet the needs not yet covered by federated self-production and to dispose of real surpluses. Real surpluses are surplus goods that are not destined for the Pool pursuant to Article 15, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 1. Export for profit is prohibited, pursuant to Article 11.1 of the Constitution."],[20,"art-16--legal-personality-and-ownership-of-assets","Art. 16 — Legal personality and ownership of assets","","1. During coexistence each Circle shall operate through a non-profit entity recognised by the local legal order, identified with the opinion of a legal professional. The statute shall incorporate, within the limits permitted by law, the principles of the Constitution: indivisibility of the assets, prohibition of the distribution of profits, individual vote and rotation of offices.\n2. The land, buildings and installations conferred or acquired shall be registered in the name of the entity with statutory constraints of inalienability, indivisibility and designated use. In the event of dissolution the assets shall devolve to another Circle or to an entity with the same purposes.\n3. The offices that the law imposes on the entity shall be conferred by sortition and shall rotate with the maximum frequency permitted. Those who hold them are bound by the resolutions of the Assembly and do not acquire further powers.\n4. The conferral of goods by persons is voluntary and documented. The deed shall indicate whether the conferral is definitive or for temporary use. Goods in temporary use remain entered in the provisional inventory of Article 6 of Code 16 and shall be returned in the event of withdrawal prior to the transfer to the definitive regime."],[20,"art-17--common-fund-and-external-means-of-payment","Art. 17 — Common Fund and external means of payment","","1. External means of payment shall be held exclusively by the Common Fund of the entity, pursuant to Article 3.5-bis of the Constitution. The Fund has separate accounts, published in aggregate form. It does not circulate among the members and does not give access to the Storehouses.\n2. The Fund is fed by the proportional contribution provided for by Article 19-bis, by voluntary contributions from the members, by the disposal of real surpluses and of obsolete goods pursuant to Article 15 of Code 1, by donations free of conditions and by the services rendered to the outside resolved by the Assembly.\n3. The Fund shall be used for the residual purchases of necessary goods that cannot be self-produced, for the external services permitted in accordance with Code 2, for fiscal and legal obligations, for legal protection and for the expenses of the transition. Decisions follow the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3.\n4. No member shall receive money from the Fund as consideration for his or her own contribution; the reimbursement of documented expenses is permitted. The external income of persons remains at their disposal, under the conditions of Article 19-bis. No one may be excluded from the Basket for not having contributed to the Fund.\n5. The Fund may not be used for speculative investments or to generate rent. The reserve shall not exceed the documented requirement of twelve months of necessary external expenses; the excess shall be allocated to the Exit Plans or to mutualism between Circles.\n6. Transfers between the Funds of different Circles shall take place only as mutualism, without interest and without any obligation of repayment. It is prohibited to contract financial debts that give rise to a critical dependency."],[20,"art-18--suppliers-purchases-and-commercial-practices","Art. 18 — Suppliers, purchases and commercial practices","","1. In meeting a requirement that calls for recourse to the outside, the following order of preference shall, as a rule, be followed:\na) other Circles of the Confederation;\nb) cooperatives, non-profit entities and parties of the social economy;\nc) local enterprises;\nd) other suppliers.\n2. The compatibility of the relationship with the supplier shall be assessed separately from the technical conformity of the good or service, which is governed by Code 2. The assessment shall consider at least:\na) respect for the rights of those who work and the absence of exploitation;\nb) the ecological impact and the treatment of animals;\nc) deceptive practices and the artificial creation of dependencies;\nd) the transparency of the conditions.\n3. Contracts shall be concluded in writing, shall provide for exit clauses and may not contain exclusivity clauses or penalties that give rise to a dependency. They are approved in accordance with the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3.\n4. The exclusion of an individual supplier falls to the competent bodies in accordance with Codes 2 and 3; the exclusion of a category on grounds of principle falls to the General Assembly.\n5. A public register of suppliers and of current contracts shall be kept, with due respect for the levels of transparency of Code 3."],[20,"art-19--services-rendered-to-the-outside-and-external-activities-of-members","Art. 19 — Services rendered to the outside and external activities of members","","1. The Circles may dispose of real surpluses to the outside and provide services, without a profit motive. The proceeds shall be paid into the Common Fund.\n2. It is prohibited to organise production according to the external market. The share of CBO allocated to production for the outside shall be resolved by the Assembly and shall not exceed the measure necessary to cover the necessary external purchases.\n3. External working activities carried out by persons during coexistence are free and do not constitute CBO. No one may be induced to abandon them.\n4. The sharing with the outside of STVs, Sheets, knowledge and free seeds shall be promoted and shall be carried out under free licences."],[20,"art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons","Art. 19-bis — External income and external assets of persons","","1. Transitional nature. External income consists of the remuneration, pensions, subsidies, rents and any other receipts that a person obtains from parties external to the Confederation. It exists only for as long as external parties and external means of payment exist, pursuant to Article 3.5-ter of the Constitution. It remains at the disposal of the person.\n2. Internal neutrality. External income and external assets confer no advantage in access to the Basket, to the Storehouses, to the commons, to the CdR, to offices or to decisions. Among the members it is prohibited to use them to:\na) purchase goods or services that the Codes assign according to need;\nb) remunerate the labour of another member or have others perform one’s own CBO;\nc) grant loans at interest or establish rents;\nd) obtain consent, votes or offices.\n3. Proportional contribution. Those who receive external income and benefit from the Basket shall contribute to the Common Fund in proportion to that income, because the external expenses borne by the Fund benefit everyone. The rate shall be resolved each year by the Assembly by qualified majority and is equal for all. On first application it is equal to one tenth of the external income, net of taxes and of legal obligations towards third parties, and may not exceed one fifth. The contribution concerns income, not assets."],[20,"art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons","Art. 19-bis — External income and external assets of persons","","4. Guarantees. Failure to pay shall not entail exclusion from the Basket or the suspension of the rights of Article 1.2 of the Constitution. It shall be dealt with as non-observance of a common rule in accordance with Code 4, with preference given to mediation and rescheduling.\n5. Confidentiality and transparency. Each year the person shall declare to the Accounting Council, in confidential form, the bracket into which his or her external income falls. Only aggregate data per Circle shall be published: number of persons per bracket and total of the contributions. The declaration shall be kept for twelve months and any use other than the calculation of the contribution is prohibited, in conformity with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n6. Equal access to external expenses. A share of the Fund, resolved by the Assembly, shall guarantee to those who have no external income the necessary external expenses, including those for documents, legal obligations, care not available in the Confederation and travel for family or health reasons.\n7. External assets. The goods that a person owns outside the Confederation remain his or hers in accordance with the legal order in force. They may not be let or given for use against payment to other members, nor used to employ members. Conferral on the entity provided for by Article 16 is voluntary, confers no privileges and makes the good a Common Good."],[20,"art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons","Art. 19-bis — External income and external assets of persons","","8. Reduction and cessation. The Exit Plans shall progressively reduce the need for external income. This Article shall cease to apply when the Confederation comprises the whole of humanity: from that moment no external income exists and the Common Funds shall be closed."],[20,"art-20--strategic-autonomy-resource-blackmail-and-countermeasures","Art. 20 — Strategic autonomy, resource blackmail and countermeasures","","1. For every necessary good or service that lacks an internal alternative, there shall be provided a safety stock pursuant to Article 16 of Code 1, a plurality of suppliers when possible and an Exit Plan.\n2. External resource blackmail occurs when an external party makes the supply of a necessary good or service conditional on a decision of the Circles or on an undue advantage, or threatens its interruption for that purpose.\n3. Countermeasures shall be exclusively lawful and non-violent: activation of the Pool and of federated mutualism, accelerated substitution, termination of the relationship, publicising of the facts, legal protection and recourse to the competent authorities. Retaliation against persons and the causing of damage are prohibited.\n4. In the event of aggression, the non-violent civil defence provided for by Article 16.4 of the Constitution and by Article 8-ter of Code 14 shall apply."],[20,"art-21--accession-of-external-communities-and-withdrawal","Art. 21 — Accession of external communities and withdrawal","","1. An external community that requests accession pursuant to Article 4.5 of the Constitution shall sign the Constitution and shall apply Code 16 in accordance with Article 3, paragraph 7, of that Code.\n2. The Circle 2 of reference shall verify exclusively the protocols of technological and logistical interoperability. The verification shall be concluded within ninety days and may not introduce further political or ideological assessments.\n3. The withdrawal of a Circle 1 follows Articles 4.6 and 4.7 of the Constitution. The technical arrangements shall be agreed with Circle 2 in such a way as to guarantee the continuity of the Basket for all the persons concerned."],[21,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-temporary-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and temporary nature","","1. This Code governs the phases, the thresholds, the transitional competences and the launch procedures of the Confederation, as well as the passage from the pre-confederal state to the operational confederal state.\n2. For the purposes of this Code, the following definitions apply:\na) transition: for each pilot Circle, the period between its establishment and the declaration of closure provided for by Article 10 for the minimum Federation of which it is part;\nb) phase: the transitional period in which specific objectives, Codes and procedures are activated, with the related exit thresholds;\nc) pilot Circle: a Base Community, corresponding to Circle 1, established pursuant to Articles 4–6 of the Constitution for the initial experimentation;\nd) Basin: an aggregation of three or four pilot Circles that are territorially contiguous and ecologically connected, corresponding to the Intermediate Circle or Circle 2 referred to in Article 5 of the Constitution;\ne) minimum Federation: an aggregation of at least three Basins;\nf) Basket 1-2: the goods and services of the Sufficiency Basket indicated by Article 32, paragraph 1-quinquies, of Code 1;\ng) point of no return: the resolution that makes irreversible the designated use of ecological or territorial commons;"],[21,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-temporary-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and temporary nature","","h) Social Start-up Protocol: Technical Annex A to this Code, adopted in accordance with Article 19 of Code 3, which defines a methodology for verifying the institutional social thresholds without introducing autonomous individual obligations or sanctions;\ni) risk levels R0-R4: the levels defined by Article 1, paragraph 3, of Code 14. Until that Code is activated, the same levels shall be assigned provisionally by the Health Council in the Safety Sheets provided for by Article 4 of Code 6;\nj) First-Year Operating Charter: Technical Annex C to this Code, adopted in accordance with Article 19 of Code 3, which brings together the minimum rules necessary for the launch of a pilot Circle without introducing autonomous obligations.\n3. This Code is temporary in nature and shall cease to produce normative effects, for each minimum Federation, with the declaration of closure referred to in Article 10, except for the memory provisions laid down therein. It continues to apply to the pilot Circles, Basins and minimum Federations that have not closed the transition, including those that are constituted or join later, pursuant to Article 21 of Code 15. It may not establish permanent rights, obligations or competences beyond the transition period, nor modify the substantive content of Codes 1–15. Its provisions do not constitute an amendment of the Codes pursuant to Articles 37 and 38 of the Constitution. In the event of conflict, the Constitution prevails."],[21,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-temporary-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and temporary nature","","4. The transitional classification of a good, a function or a competence does not determine its permanent regime.\n5. During the transition, the references in the Codes to Circle 1 shall be understood as referring to the pilot Circle and those to Circle 2 as referring to the Basin. The minimum Federation exercises the functions of Circle 3 until it is constituted as an Extended Circle pursuant to Article 14-bis of Code 3. Until the minimum Federation is constituted, the declaration of the Circle 3 concerned provided for by Article 36.2 of the Constitution shall be adopted by the General Assembly of the affected Basin, within the limits of Article 36.4 of the Constitution.\n6. During the transition, the Balance provided for by Code 15 operates at the scale of the Basin and of the minimum Federation. The activation of the operational confederal phase does not imply that the Confederation comprises the whole of humanity.\n7. The revision of the Constitution and of the Codes follows the founding regime under the conditions of Article 38.2 of the Constitution, that is for as long as there are not at least three Extended Circles, even when part of the Confederation is still in transition.\n8. Technical Annexes A, B and C to this Code are adopted and amended in accordance with Article 19 of Code 3, with the following rules:"],[21,"art-1--subject-matter-definitions-and-temporary-nature","Art. 1 — Subject matter, definitions and temporary nature","","a) each Annex, or its part that applies to a phase, is adopted by the Assembly of the unit that is in that phase pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 7: the Assembly of the pilot Circle for Phase 0, the General Assembly of the Basin for Phase 1 and that of the minimum Federation for Phases 2 and 3, pursuant to Articles 12 and 14 of Code 3;\nb) an amendment resolved by a unit applies to it and to the units that join it, pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 7, letter c); it is published in the federated Knowledge Storehouse, so that the other units may adopt it;\nc) no Annex and no amendment to it may alter the phases, the thresholds, the competences or the guarantees of this Code and of Codes 1–15, pursuant to Article 19, paragraph 5, of Code 3.\n9. Until a Code is activated pursuant to Article 3, the references to it in this Code and in its Annexes apply in so far as applicable with the bodies and procedures already active. The opinions of Councils not yet established follow Article 8, paragraphs 2 and 3. The prohibitions and guarantees that directly implement the Constitution always remain unaffected."],[21,"art-2--principles-of-the-transition","Art. 2 — Principles of the transition","","1. Continuity of the Basket. No change of phase shall be resolved if it entails the interruption of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket or a risk of interruption that is not adequately managed.\n2. Reversibility. Before a point of no return is adopted, every intervention shall provide, where technically applicable, for a reversibility plan.\n3. Proportionality and gradualness. The activation of functions, Codes and constraints shall be commensurate with the competences available, the resources surveyed and the risks actually present.\n4. Effectiveness. The merely formal constitution of Circles, Basins or Federations lacking territorial continuity, actual activity or a verifiable census is prohibited.\n5. Transparency. The phases, thresholds, censuses, resolutions and plans shall be published in accordance with Article 15 of Code 3 and submitted to a period for observations of at least seventy-two hours. Personal data shall be excluded or aggregated in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n6. Non-commodification. It is prohibited to establish rents on the launch of the Confederation, on the commons conferred during the transition or on life-saving competences.\n7. Legality and coexistence. During the transition the Circles shall operate in compliance with the legal order in force in the territory, pursuant to Article 38.6 of the Constitution. Article 37 of Code 4 and Chapter IV of Code 15 apply.\n8. Voluntariness. Accession is individual and voluntary. No one may be induced to cease external activities, to confer goods or to waive rights recognised by the legal order in force. This is without prejudice to the proportional contribution on external income provided for by Article 19-bis of Code 15, which shall be made known before accession."],[21,"art-3--phases-of-the-transition","Art. 3 — Phases of the transition","","1. Phase 0 — Pre-confederal. The indicative duration is from zero to six months. In this phase:\na) at least one pilot Circle shall be constituted with no fewer than thirty persons having joined;\nb) Codes 1, 2, 3, 4, 5 and 6 and Chapter IV of Code 15 shall be activated;\nc) an initial census, aggregated and non-profiling, shall be carried out of the available land, of the L1 and L2 competences, of the machines and equipment and of the needs of Basket 1-2;\nd) a provisional minimum Storehouse in accordance with Articles 13–22 of Code 1 and a minimum Knowledge Storehouse in accordance with Article 3 of Code 5 shall be activated;\ne) basic L1 life-saving training in accordance with Code 6 shall be started;\nf) the Guarantee Circle and the Councils provided for by the active Codes shall be established, including the Storehouse Council, the Health Council and the Competences Council, also in merged or shared form or in the three Area Councils provided for by Article 12-bis, paragraph 12, of Code 3;\ng) the pilot Circle shall operate in accordance with the First-Year Operating Charter.\n2. Phase 1 — Basin. The indicative duration is from six to eighteen months. In this phase:\na) three or four contiguous pilot Circles shall aggregate into a Basin;\nb) Codes 7, 8, 9 and 10 and C.1-Annex A shall be activated, in addition to the Codes of Phase 0;\nc) the provisional minimum federated Land Register provided for by Article 7 of Code 10 shall be started;"],[21,"art-3--phases-of-the-transition","Art. 3 — Phases of the transition","","d) the Basin food balance and a joint minimum Basket STV of Codes 9 and 10 shall be prepared;\ne) the land eligible to become a sanctuary shall be surveyed on a preliminary basis, in preparation for the activation of Code 13;\nf) the Network, Energy and Water, Agroecological, Habitat and Territory and Production Councils shall be activated;\ng) the Balance provided for by Code 15 shall be activated at the scale of the Basin and the Basin Storehouse Council provided for by Article 9-bis of Code 15 shall be established.\n3. Phase 2 — Minimum Federation. The indicative duration is from eighteen to thirty-six months. In this phase:\na) at least three Basins shall be aggregated;\nb) Codes 11, 12, 13 and 14 shall be activated, in addition to the Codes already activated;\nc) Chapter III of Code 15 and the Balance at the scale of the minimum Federation shall be activated, with the Storehouse Council provided for by Article 9-bis of Code 15 for Circle 3;\nd) at least one sanctuary per Basin shall be established, pursuant to Article 4 of Code 13;\ne) the critical machines, the PPE and the collective protections shall be surveyed in accordance with Code 14;\nf) the Logistics, Care, Biodiversity and Safety Councils shall be activated;\ng) the first version of the Dependency Register shall be established, integrating the registers of the sectoral Codes.\n4. Phase 3 — Operational Confederation. The indicative duration is from thirty-six to sixty months. In this phase:"],[21,"art-3--phases-of-the-transition","Art. 3 — Phases of the transition","","a) Codes 1–15 and C.1-Annex A shall be activated in full operation, within the limits of the conditions of effectiveness provided for by the individual Codes;\nb) ecological, safety and food autonomy audits shall be carried out by independent L3 members drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4 and the sectoral Codes;\nc) the overcoming of at least fifty per cent of the registered critical dependencies shall be completed, on the basis of verifiable objectives and time limits;\nd) the node of the federated Network provided for by Code 7 shall be completed for each Basin.\n5. Phase 4 — Closure. The transition ends with the declaration of closure adopted in accordance with Article 10.\n6. The durations indicated are for guidance and do not replace the exit thresholds of Article 4. No phase may be passed before the related thresholds have been verified.\n7. Scale of the phases. Phase 0 belongs to each pilot Circle, Phase 1 to each Basin and Phases 2, 3 and 4 to each minimum Federation. The following rules apply:\na) the passage from Phase 0 to Phase 1 is resolved jointly by the pilot Circles that constitute the Basin, pursuant to Article 14, paragraph 10, of Code 3; the passage from Phase 1 to Phase 2 by the General Assembly of the Basins that constitute the minimum Federation; the subsequent passages and the closure by the General Assembly of the minimum Federation;"],[21,"art-3--phases-of-the-transition","Art. 3 — Phases of the transition","","b) a pilot Circle that is constituted or joins when others are already in a later phase goes through Phase 0. Once the thresholds of Article 4, paragraph 1, have been exceeded, it joins a contiguous Basin or constitutes a new one. A Basin that has exceeded the thresholds of Article 4, paragraph 2, joins a minimum Federation or constitutes a new one with at least two other Basins;\nc) a pilot Circle or a Basin that joins a Basin or a minimum Federation assumes its phase when it meets, for its own part, the thresholds of the passages already resolved by that unit. The verification is carried out by the Transition Council of the receiving unit and may not introduce further assessments, pursuant to Article 4.5 of the Constitution. The Codes already activated apply to the entrant from the day of entry, and the transitional time limits of the individual Codes run from that date;\nd) if the minimum Federation has already closed the transition, the verification of letter c) includes the thresholds of Article 4, paragraphs 3 and 4, and the closure of the transition for the entrant is resolved by its General Assembly pursuant to Article 10, paragraph 1; entry into the Extended Circle follows Article 14-bis, paragraph 4, of Code 3;\ne) the entry of a new pilot Circle or Basin does not make the receiving unit go back a phase."],[21,"art-4--activation-and-exit-thresholds","Art. 4 — Activation and exit thresholds","","1. Passage from Phase 0 to Phase 1. The following are required:\na) the continuous guarantee of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket for at least three months in the pilot Circle;\nb) a verified census and the functioning of the minimum Storehouse and of the minimum Knowledge Storehouse;\nc) the absence of unmanaged critical violations pursuant to Article 9, paragraph 2;\nd) the documented application of Level 1 of the Social Start-up Protocol;\ne) a Labour Balance, drawn up pursuant to Article 2 of Code 1, documenting for at least one quarter the coverage of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket within the limit of Article 3 of the same Code.\n2. Passage from Phase 1 to Phase 2. The following are required:\na) self-production in the Basin of at least seventy per cent of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket, calculated according to the criteria of the applicable Basket STV;\nb) the functioning of the minimum Land Register;\nc) at least one area that is a candidate sanctuary, attested by the Habitat and Territory Council. In the absence of the Biodiversity Council, the provisional biological assessment shall be given by the Transition Council pursuant to Article 8, paragraph 2, with an explicit indication of its provisional character;\nd) the absence of unmanaged critical violations pursuant to Article 9, paragraph 2, verified in accordance with Codes 4, 9 and 10;\ne) the documented application of Level 2 of the Social Start-up Protocol;"],[21,"art-4--activation-and-exit-thresholds","Art. 4 — Activation and exit thresholds","","f) Basin Labour Balances that respect the limit of Article 3 of Code 1 for at least two consecutive quarters.\n3. Passage from Phase 2 to Phase 3. The following are required:\na) the functioning of the provisional minimum Biodiversity Register;\nb) the functioning of the register of general risks;\nc) the establishment of at least one sanctuary per Basin;\nd) Safety Sheets in force for all activities classified R2 or higher;\ne) the absence of R4 incidents that are unmanaged or lack the required analysis of causes;\nf) the documented application of Level 3 of the Social Start-up Protocol.\n4. Passage from Phase 3 to Phase 4. The following are required:\na) a positive federated audit on autonomy, carried out after at least six continuous months of operation without non-substitutable critical dependencies that compromise the continuity of the Basket;\nb) the entry in the Dependency Register of an exit plan with a definite time limit for each critical dependency still present;\nc) the functioning of the nodes of the federated Network in all the Basins;\nd) the documented maintenance of Level 3 of the Social Start-up Protocol;\ne) Labour Balances that respect the limit of Article 3 of Code 1 for four consecutive quarters or, failing that, the submission of a reasoned proposal for revision of that limit pursuant to Article 38 of the Constitution."],[21,"art-4--activation-and-exit-thresholds","Art. 4 — Activation and exit thresholds","","5. The verification of the thresholds shall be carried out by the Transition Council, with the mandatory opinion of the Councils competent for the subject matter. The change of phase constitutes a constitutive decision pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 3, of Code 3 and shall be resolved by the General Assembly indicated by Article 3, paragraph 7, letter a), in accordance with the convening, quorum and procedure provided for by Codes 3 and 4. The resolution shall be published in aggregate and non-profiling form.\n6. The social opinion provided for by the Social Start-up Protocol may bear exclusively on the institutional verification of the threshold and on the possibility of proposing or resolving the change of phase. It may not produce individual sanctions, exclusions or limitations, nor make conditional the access to Code 4, to the Guarantee, to vital goods or to Basket 1-2."],[21,"art-5--transitional-government","Art. 5 — Transitional government","","1. In each phase a distinct Transition Council shall be established, with a duration limited to the phase of reference, at the scale provided for by Article 3, paragraph 7: one for each pilot Circle in Phase 0, one for each Basin in Phase 1 and one for each minimum Federation in Phases 2 and 3. The Transition Council of the Basin and of the minimum Federation is a shared Council within the meaning of Article 12-bis, paragraph 6-bis, of Code 3, with its members drawn by lot from among the persons eligible pursuant to paragraph 2 of the pilot Circles that are part of it; the rules of paragraph 3 remain in force.\n2. The Council is composed of three to five members with L2 or L3 competences relevant to the phase, including organisation, confederal law, agroecology, territory, safety and biodiversity.\n3. Appointment and duration are governed as follows:\na) the members shall be drawn by lot from among suitable volunteers who have fulfilled the required CBO and the specific training, with the right to refuse the office, in accordance with Articles 4.3 and 4.4, letter d), of the Constitution;\nb) no member shall perform the function for more than ninety consecutive days or for more than one hundred and eighty days in total in three hundred and sixty-five days;\nc) renewal shall take place by staggered sortition, with a minuted handover, so as to guarantee the continuity of the Council throughout the phase;"],[21,"art-5--transitional-government","Art. 5 — Transitional government","","d) between two terms of office in the Transition Council there shall elapse at least twelve months of exclusive activity in ordinary CBO;\ne) simultaneous membership of the Transition Council and of the Storehouse Council in the same phase is prohibited;\nf) each member shall declare his or her conflicts of interest and shall abstain when there is a conflict or a direct coincidence between controller and controlled, in accordance with Code 3, Article 16.\n4. The Council shall operate with a quorum of at least two thirds of its members. Decisions shall be taken by consensus in the absence of reasoned objections, in accordance with Code 3, Article 4. Meetings shall be minuted and the minutes shall be kept for thirty-six months.\n5. The Transition Council shall not resolve the change of phase or the adoption of a point of no return. It may formulate proposals to the General Assembly, to which the decisions fall: the change of phase in accordance with Article 4; the point of no return in accordance with Article 2, paragraph 2, Article 6 and Article 4, paragraph 7, of Annex A.\n6. The Transition Council:\na) coordinates the censuses;\nb) draws up the transitional plans;\nc) verifies the thresholds;\nd) proposes the activation of the Codes;\ne) feeds the Dependency Register;\nf) coordinates the Councils activated in the phase;\ng) verifies the application of the Social Start-up Protocol solely with regard to the institutional thresholds."],[21,"art-5--transitional-government","Art. 5 — Transitional government","","7. The Transition Council may not exercise competences reserved to other Councils, cede to external parties the control of commons or assume external financial commitments that give rise to critical dependencies."],[21,"art-6--commons-during-the-transition","Art. 6 — Commons during the transition","","1. The goods conferred during the transition shall be entered in a federated provisional inventory according to the applicable competence:\na) Code 10, Article 7, for land and territorial goods;\nb) Code 5, Article 3, for essential knowledge;\nc) Articles 13–22 of Code 1 for the goods held in custody in the Storehouses;\nd) C.1-Annex A and Codes 9 and 14 for machines, seeds, PPE and collective protections, from their respective activation.\n2. The nature as inalienable common good of the goods provided for by Article 3.1 of the Constitution is not suspended during the transition. This Article governs exclusively custody and provisional entry.\n3. The transfer to the definitive regime of custody shall take place:\na) for territorial and ecological goods, at the closure of Phase 2;\nb) for critical technological goods, at the closure of Phase 3.\nThe transfer requires a resolution of the General Assembly and the mandatory opinion of the competent Council.\n4. Until the definitive regime is activated, acts of disposal exceeding ordinary maintenance and use in conformity with a Sheet in force are ineffective. Acts of sale and purchase, letting or mortgage of the commons remain prohibited, in accordance with Articles 3.1 and 3.2 of the Constitution.\n5. The provisional Storehouses shall keep separate accounts for goods in transition and for commons already entered in the definitive regime. Traceability and access follow Code 1; for the Knowledge Storehouse, Code 5 applies.\n6. The legal ownership of the goods during the transition is governed by Article 16 of Code 15."],[21,"art-7--persons-competences-and-transitional-training","Art. 7 — Persons, competences and transitional training","","1. L1 life-saving training shall be provided free of charge from Phase 0 in accordance with Code 6 and, from the activation of the respective Codes, in accordance with Codes 13 and 14.\n2. Prior competences shall be recorded in accordance with Code 5, Article 10, paragraph 1. The provisional validation of continuity, the related time limits and the shortening of training paths are governed exclusively by Article 10, paragraphs 2–7, of Code 5. The Transition Council may not extend or derogate from those time limits.\n3. Every function classified R3 or higher requires mentoring by a person with verified L2 or L3 competence, until sufficient internal coverage is available.\n4. The transitional registry of competences shall be maintained in aggregate and non-profiling form, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2.\n5. The Transition Council shall consider the Social Start-up Protocol exclusively for the purposes of verifying the social thresholds of institutional sustainability and within the limits established by Technical Annex A."],[21,"art-8--conflicts-and-guarantee-during-the-transition","Art. 8 — Conflicts and Guarantee during the transition","","1. Code 4 applies from Phase 0.\n2. Until a competent Council is activated, the Transition Council may give, on a provisional basis, the non-binding mandatory opinions concerning the subject matter not yet covered. The opinion shall be noted as provisional and submitted to review within six months of the activation of the competent Council.\n3. The substitute function of paragraph 2 does not apply to the binding negative opinions provided for by Article 8 of Code 3 or to the authorisation of Article 13.3 of the Constitution. Until the competent Council is activated, decisions requiring one of those opinions or that authorisation shall be suspended. The other decisions not dependent on that opinion may proceed.\n4. Suspensions necessary to deal with a serious and immediate R4 risk are immediately enforceable in every phase, in compliance with the procedures of the competent Codes.\n5. An appeal to the Guarantee is permitted within fourteen days in accordance with Article 17 of Code 3 and Article 17 of Code 4. No provision of this Code or of the Social Start-up Protocol may make conditional, delay or limit access to the Guarantee.\n6. During the transition, Chapter V of Code 4 applies within the limits of its Article 37. Until a Basin is constituted, the bodies of Code 4 that require members external to the pilot Circle, including the Review Panel of Article 17 and the substitute Guarantee Circle of Article 15, paragraph 3, are formed in accordance with Article 37, paragraph 5, of Code 4."],[21,"art-9--prohibitions-during-the-transition","Art. 9 — Prohibitions during the transition","","1. During the transition it is prohibited to:\na) establish rents on the launch, on the commons entered in the provisional inventory or on L1 life-saving training;\nb) sell, mortgage or encumber the land designated as a candidate sanctuary or as a candidate ecological corridor;\nc) patent, register, file, claim or maintain exclusive titles over wild or local genetic material or over the essential knowledge surveyed;\nd) assume external financial debts or conclude supply agreements that give rise to a critical proprietary dependency pursuant to Article 5 of Code 2, in the absence of an exit plan with a definite time limit entered in the Dependency Register;\ne) cede to external parties the control of the provisional Storehouse, of the provisional Land Register, of the provisional Biodiversity Register or of the Dependency Register;\nf) constitute Circles or Basins that are fictitious or lack the minimum requirements provided for by Article 2, paragraph 4, and by Article 3;\ng) impose paywalls on the STVs and on the Sheets essential for Basket 1-2, biodiversity or general safety.\n2. For the sole purposes of this Code, violations of paragraph 1, letters a), b), c), d) and e), are classified as critical; those of letters f) and g) are classified as substantial.\n3. The ascertainment, the precautionary and restorative measures and any remedies are governed by Code 4. The effects on decisions shall be determined in accordance with Article 18 of Code 3. The technical exclusion of a good or service remains within the competence of the Quality Council pursuant to Code 2."],[21,"art-9-bis--gradual-application-of-technological-sovereignty","Art. 9-bis — Gradual application of technological sovereignty","","1. In implementation of Article 38.6 of the Constitution, until the closure of the transition the obligation to possess physically the hardware of every system used, provided for by Article 19.4 of the Constitution, and the obligation to use only open-source AI, provided for by Article 19.5, apply gradually as follows:\na) the use of a system hosted on external infrastructure or incorporating an AI model that is not open source is permitted only if the dependency is admitted as residual and surmountable pursuant to Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), of Code 2, or as temporary pursuant to Articles 2, paragraph 1, 8, paragraph 2, and 10, paragraph 3, of Code 7, and is entered in the Dependency Register with the Substitution Plan or the Exit Plan;\nb) gradual application does not allow closed code or models that are not open source to be used for Management AIs, for the algorithms of Article 2, paragraph 5, of Code 7, for the personal AI and for the consultation and voting systems of Article 34.4 of the Constitution;\nc) the systems of letter a) installed at the Community do not receive automatic or remote updates; every update is verified by the Network Council before installation and is reversible, pursuant to Article 5-ter, paragraph 7, of Code 7;\nd) gradual application ceases for each system on the expiry of its Plan and, in any case, with the closure of the transition.\n2. The use of systems whose source code is not available follows, during the transition and after it, Article 19.4 of the Constitution, Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), and Article 12, paragraph 5, of Code 2."],[21,"art-10--closure-of-the-transition-memory-and-precedence","Art. 10 — Closure of the transition, memory and precedence","","1. The closure of the transition shall be resolved, for each minimum Federation, by its General Assembly by a qualified majority of two thirds, in accordance with Articles 13 and 14 of Code 3. The resolution shall be preceded by:\na) the mandatory opinion of the federated Councils;\nb) the positive federated audit provided for by Article 4, paragraph 4;\nc) the verification of compliance with the conditions for closure.\n2. The declaration of closure produces the following effects:\na) this Code shall cease to produce normative effects for the minimum Federation that has resolved the closure, pursuant to Article 1, paragraph 3, and the minimum Federation is constituted as an Extended Circle pursuant to Article 14-bis of Code 3;\nb) the provisional inventories, the minutes of the Transition Councils and the resolutions on changes of phase shall remain preserved as historical memory in accordance with Code 7;\nc) the goods already transferred to the definitive regime remain subject to the sectoral Codes;\nd) the goods not yet transferred because the thresholds have not been reached shall be returned or transferred by resolution of the General Assembly and with the opinion of the competent Council.\n3. During the transition, in the event of conflict between this Code and Codes 1–15, this Code prevails exclusively to the extent strictly necessary to govern the activation sequence and the thresholds referred to in Article 4, guaranteeing the continuity of Basket 1-2. Such precedence does not create permanent competences and does not apply:\na) to the Constitution;\nb) to the mandatory rules of the Constitution and of the Codes, that is, those that do not admit derogation by the deciding bodies, referred to in Article 2, paragraph 2, of Code 3;\nc) to the binding negative opinions provided for by Article 8 of Code 3.\n4. After the closure of the transition, the precedence clauses of the sectoral Codes apply, including those of Code 10, Article 10, of Code 13, Article 11, and of Code 14, Article 10.\n5. The main normative links of this Code are: Constitution, Articles 3.1, 3.2, 4–6 and 32; Code 1, Articles 13–22 and 32; Code 2, Article 5; Code 3, Articles 3, 4, 8, 13–19; Code 4; Code 5, Articles 3 and 10; Code 6, Articles 4 and 11; Code 7, Article 2.2; Codes 8 and 9; Code C.1-Annex A; Codes 10–14; Code 15, Chapters III and IV."],[22,"","","","Technical annex adopted pursuant to Article 19 of Code 3 and referred to in Articles 1, paragraph 2, letter h), 4 and 7, paragraph 5, of Code 16."],[22,"art-1--nature-function-and-effects","Art. 1 — Nature, function and effects","","1. This Protocol defines a methodology for assessing the social steps connected with the start-up of the pilot Circles, the Basins and the minimum Federation. The methodology is used to verify the institutional social thresholds provided for by Article 4 of Code 16.\n2. The Protocol does not constitute an autonomous source of individual obligations, sanctions, exclusions or limitations. Failure to apply it:\na) does not, in itself, constitute a violation within the meaning of Code 4;\nb) does not entail individual suspensions, sanctions or prejudice;\nc) does not limit access to Code 4, to the Guarantee, to vital goods or to Basket 1-2.\n3. Documented failure by the Circle to apply it may result exclusively in the relevant institutional social threshold not being passed and, consequently, prevent the phase change from being proposed or resolved upon. That effect concerns the Circle as an institutional entity and produces no individual consequences.\n4. For the purposes of this Protocol, the reference Council for care is:\na) the Health Council, until the activation of the Care Council;\nb) the Care Council, from the activation provided for by Code 16, Article 3, paragraph 3.\nEvery reference to the reference Council for care is to be understood as made in accordance with this paragraph.\n5. The documentation is produced, as far as possible, in aggregated and non-profiling form, in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2. Data are collected only to the extent necessary for the verification of the relevant threshold. The minimum aggregation threshold is defined by the Network Council when active, in order to reduce the risk of direct or indirect identification, in compliance with the separation of domains provided for by Code 6, Article 11.\n6. The Protocol does not replace the competences, procedures or guarantees provided for by the other Codes and does not authorise the Transition Council or other bodies to adopt individual measures not provided for by the applicable rules."],[22,"art-2--level-1-trust-and-start-up-of-the-circle","Art. 2 — Level 1: trust and start-up of the Circle","","Institutional phase of reference: Phase 0 of Code 16, indicatively from zero to six months.\nMinimum operational duration: from zero to ninety days.\n1. Level 1 aims to verify the initial social sustainability of the pilot Circle and to foster minimum conditions of trust and collaboration.\n2. Before the constitutive resolution of the pilot Circle, three non-deliberative meetings are organised. Every participant may describe their own motivations, actual availability, personal limits and any care burdens relevant to the organisation. The information collected is minuted in aggregated form, without identifying persons by name.\n3. An aggregate map of the capacity for care and organisation is prepared, which may take into account:\na) the total time available for the activities of the Circle;\nb) the presence of care burdens that affect the organisation;\nc) the functional needs relevant to preparing accessible activities;\nd) the organisational indicators of overload.\nThe collection does not constitute a diagnosis, an individual health assessment or a classification of persons. The data are aggregated and non-profiling, retained for no more than twelve months unless a different period is imposed by a higher-ranking rule, and accessible exclusively to the competent Councils for the purposes provided for by the Protocol.\n4. The Circle adopts an organisational pact that excludes the use of minors, frail persons or proximity companion animals to make up for organisational shortcomings, cover shifts incompatible with their protection or replace necessary personnel.\n5. A symbolic and optional moment of joining the pilot Circle may be provided for. That moment produces no legal effects and does not constitute a requirement for joining, for access to rights or for the enjoyment of essential services.\n6. The Transition Council draws up a technical attestation on the application of Level 1 and on the social sustainability resulting from the aggregated data. The attestation contributes to the verification of the F0→F1 threshold provided for by Code 16, Article 4, paragraph 1, letter d)."],[22,"art-3--level-2-living-together-and-prevention-of-overload","Art. 3 — Level 2: living together and prevention of overload","","Institutional phase of reference: Phase 1 of Code 16, indicatively from six to eighteen months.\nMinimum operational duration: from ninety to one hundred and eighty days.\n1. Level 2 aims to improve operational living together and to prevent the overload of the organisation and of persons.\n2. Social L1 training with a total duration of four hours is offered free of charge, covering at least:\na) safe interruption of risky activities;\nb) recognition and reporting of fatigue and overload;\nc) basic facilitation techniques;\nd) the principle of non-retaliation.\nThe training is delivered by persons with L2 or L3 competences verified in accordance with the applicable rules.\n3. Every General Assembly is conducted by two facilitators, with mandatory rotation. In the same Assembly it is not permitted to combine the function of facilitator with that of proposer. The minutes indicate the facilitators without recording further unnecessary data.\n4. Exceeding one of the following indicators activates organisational support with the reference Council for care:\na) an average of more than fifteen hours per week of Circle activities, calculated on a quarterly basis in accordance with an aggregated method defined by the competent Council;\nb) exceeding the peak-hour limits provided for by Code 1, Article 6, paragraph 5.\nThe support consists in verifying the organisation and, if necessary, in proposing a redistribution of the loads. It does not constitute a sanction, a negative individual assessment or a limitation of rights.\n5. The measurement provided for by paragraph 4 is carried out with aggregated criteria and may not be used to classify persons or to attribute individual assessments to them. The personal reporting channels provided for by the applicable Codes remain available.\n6. A social repair procedure with a trusted person external to the Circle, chosen by mutual agreement, may be promoted, exclusively on a voluntary basis. The procedure:\na) is voluntary for all the persons involved;\nb) is not a condition for access to Code 4;\nc) neither delays nor replaces a report pursuant to Article 5 of Code 4;\nd) neither delays nor replaces the appeal provided for by Article 17 of Code 3.\n7. An exclusively aggregated and non-profiling register of the organisational signals of overload may be kept. The register is retained for twelve months and may be consulted by the reference Council for care and by the Transition Council exclusively for their respective functions, in compliance with the separation of domains provided for by Code 6, Article 11.\n8. The aggregated documentation on the application of Level 2 contributes to the verification of the F1→F2 threshold provided for by Code 16, Article 4, paragraph 2, letter e)."],[22,"art-4--level-3-social-autonomy-and-perceived-safety","Art. 4 — Level 3: social autonomy and perceived safety","","Institutional phase of reference: Phase 2 of Code 16 and, subsequently, maintenance in Phase 3.\n1. Level 3 aims to prevent relational and decision-making concentration, organisational endogamy and the conditions that may compromise perceived safety.\n2. In each Council no more than two persons belonging to the same stable cohabitation nucleus may serve at the same time. The information necessary to verify that limit is processed in confidential form and limited to the purpose of composing the Councils.\n3. The rotation of the Transition Councils follows Article 4.4 of the Constitution and Article 5, paragraph 3, of Code 16. The limit of ninety consecutive days, the overall limit of one hundred and eighty days in three hundred and sixty-five days and the interval of at least twelve months of exclusive activity in ordinary CBO between two terms of office apply, within the limits provided for by the rules referred to.\n4. Every pilot Circle organises at least one inter-Basin exchange, which comprises the voluntary participation of at least one person for one continuous week in another Basin, for contribution activities. The directly necessary expenses are covered by the Common Fund in accordance with the applicable rules. The tracking of the exchange is aggregated and non-profiling.\n5. Every six months an anonymous questionnaire on perceived safety is carried out, by technical means suitable for preventing the identification of persons. The minimum question is: “Do you feel safe here?”. The answers are not published when the number of participants does not allow adequate aggregation.\n6. If more than twenty per cent of the valid answers are negative:\na) the proposal to pass to the next phase remains suspended until a corrective measure has been prepared and verified;\nb) the reference Council for care prepares, within thirty days, a plan addressed to the organisation and structure of the Circle;\nc) the plan is followed by a new verification within sixty days.\nExceeding the threshold does not, in itself, constitute grounds for a sanction, exclusion or individual measure.\n7. A point of no return within the meaning of Code 16, Article 1, paragraph 2, letter g), may not be proposed on the exclusive basis of the assessment of the members internal to the Basin concerned. The mandatory and non-binding opinion of at least one external L3 person, drawn by lot in accordance with the procedure of Code 4, is required.\n8. The aggregated documentation on the application of Level 3 contributes to the verification of the F2→F3 threshold provided for by Code 16, Article 4, paragraph 3, letter f), and to the verification of the maintenance of the F3→F4 threshold provided for by paragraph 4, letter d), of the same Article."],[22,"art-5--documentation-verification-and-updating","Art. 5 — Documentation, verification and updating","","1. The documentation of each level is drawn up in aggregated and non-profiling form in accordance with Code 7, Article 2.2. It is retained for thirty-six months, unless a different period is provided for by a higher-ranking rule, and made accessible in accordance with Article 15 of Code 3, in compliance with data protection and the separation of domains.\n2. The application of the Protocol is verified by the Transition Council, after the mandatory opinion of the reference Council for care.\n3. A negative opinion of the reference Council for care may suspend exclusively the institutional proposal for a phase change until any corrective plan has been prepared and verified. It may not entail individual suspensions, exclusions, sanctions or limitations and does not affect access to Code 4, to the Guarantee, to vital goods or to Basket 1-2.\n4. The outcome of the verification is transmitted to the competent bodies in accordance with Article 4 of Code 16. This Protocol does not confer on the Transition Council the power to resolve autonomously upon the phase change.\n5. The Protocol may be updated on a proposal from the Transition Council or from the reference Council for care, in accordance with Article 19 of Code 3, after publication for at least seventy-two hours. The Transition Council may not modify it autonomously or modify the thresholds established by Code 16.\n6. No provision of this Protocol:\na) introduces permanent competences;\nb) modifies the thresholds of Code 16;\nc) introduces autonomous individual obligations;\nd) limits rights or guarantees provided for by the applicable Codes;\ne) replaces the binding opinions provided for by the reference legislation.\n7. Normative links: Code 16, Articles 1, 4, 5, 7 and 8; Code 3, Articles 3, 4, 13–17 and 19; Code 4; Code 6, Article 11; Code 7, Article 2.2; Code 12; Constitution, Article 4."],[23,"","","","Technical annex adopted pursuant to Article 19 of Code 3. This Annex does not constitute an autonomous source of sanctions nor does it introduce autonomous individual obligations. It defines criteria and operating tools for the verification of the institutional thresholds of Code 16. In the event of conflict, Code 16 shall prevail."],[23,"art-1--nature-temporal-coordination-and-definitions","Art. 1 — Nature, temporal coordination and definitions","","1. This Annex provides operating tools for the transition and for federation. Its provisions bind the institutional bodies exclusively for the purposes of verifying the thresholds of Code 16 and may not be applied to impose individual conduct not provided for by the Codes or by the applicable legislation.\n2. This Annex does not establish an autonomous chronology nor does it modify the phases of Code 16. The durations indicated in Articles 3–7 are minimum operating references, subordinate to the thresholds and to the resolutions provided for by Code 16:\na) Articles 3 and 4 implement preparatory activities of Phase 0 and of Phase 1;\nb) Article 5 applies in the pathway leading from Phase 1 to Phase 2;\nc) Article 6 applies in the pathway from Phase 2 to Phase 3;\nd) Article 7 governs external dissemination subsequent to the transition and does not constitute a Phase 4 of Code 16.\nNo operating time limit may authorise the passage to an institutional phase before the relevant thresholds of Code 16 have been passed.\n3. For the purposes of this Annex:\na) extractive system means: the set of activities, contracts and supply chains that gives rise to a critical proprietary dependency pursuant to Code 2, Article 5, or to a critical dependency pursuant to Code 15, as documented in the Dependency Register;\nb) territorial bloc means: an operational coordination unit composed of one or more minimum Federations already constituted pursuant to Code 16. The bloc has no institutional autonomy, no deliberative powers over the commons and no Fund of its own, and operates exclusively for coordination, the exchange of knowledge and the preparation of common plans for the reduction of dependencies. If it comprises a single minimum Federation, it does not duplicate that Federation’s institutions;\nc) N/NN/S classification means: a technical classification of goods and services carried out in accordance with Code 1, Articles 18 and 32, the Basket STV and C.1-Annex A, Article 1, paragraph 2:\nN — necessary: a good or service of Basket 1-2 for which no adequate internal alternative has been verified;\nNN — not necessary: a good or service not included in Basket 1-2, or for which an adequate internal alternative has been verified;\nS — incompatible dependency: a good or service that contributes to a critical proprietary dependency, entails a violation of Article 9 of Code 16 or is produced in violation of a safety STV applicable to risks of level R2 or higher.\nThe classification shall be reasoned, recorded and published with a reference to the competent sectoral Code. It concerns goods, services and dependencies; it does not constitute a moral assessment of persons or of their individual behaviour."],[23,"art-2--requirements-for-public-initiatives-and-accounting-rules","Art. 2 — Requirements for public initiatives and accounting rules","","1. Before launching public initiatives for the reduction of external dependencies, the Circle shall verify cumulatively that:\na) Basket 1-2 is guaranteed;\nb) a separate Legal Fund has been constituted within the Common Fund provided for by Article 17 of Code 15, and an agreement has been concluded, for each Basin, with at least one professional firm, including a protocol of protection against retaliation;\nc) internal coordination operates on a self-hosted federated Network, with at least one node functioning in accordance with Code 7;\nd) the documentation is kept in a versioned and replicable repository;\ne) there are no direct debt or financial dependencies between territorial blocs.\n2. The requirements of paragraph 1 concern the institutional suitability to launch the initiatives provided for by this Annex. They do not limit individual rights, lawful activities, access to essential services or recourse to the Guarantee.\n3. The savings resulting from the documented cessation of purchases relating to NN or S goods shall be accounted for separately in the accounting register of the Storehouse, with a distinct entry description and documentation of the purchase that has ceased. The corresponding accounting item shall be formally identified in the applicable accounting system.\n4. The saving shall be certified by the Storehouse Council, after the mandatory opinion:\na) of the Agroecological Council for foodstuffs;\nb) of the Energy and Water Council for energy and water;\nc) of the Production Council for other goods.\nThe verification shall ascertain that the cessation actually concerns NN or S goods and does not reduce the availability of an N good lacking a verified internal alternative. In case of doubt, the item shall be classified N until the investigation is completed.\n5. The certified economic saving may be converted monthly by the Transition Council into an equivalent pool of hours, exclusively in order to measure the organisational capacity of the Circle. The calculation uses a public table approved by the General Assembly, based on the local average hourly cost of the activities necessary for Basket 1-2.\n6. The pool of hours referred to in paragraph 5 constitutes an organisational indicator of the Circle: it is not money, it is not transferable and it does not constitute an individual credit. No person may be obliged to provide hours by way of compensation.\n7. Ten per cent of the certified monetary savings shall be allocated to the Common Fund in accordance with Article 17 of Code 15. The payment may not be charged to individual persons nor affect Basket 1-2."],[23,"art-3--initial-operating-module-publication-and-census","Art. 3 — Initial operating module: publication and census","","Indicative minimum operating duration: from zero to ninety days, within the institutional Phase 0 of Code 16.\n1. The module aims to determine the actual capacity of the Circle and does not constitute a recruitment campaign.\n2. The institutional activities provided for are:\na) to publish Codes 1–16 and the Technical Annexes in an accessible, versioned and replicable repository;\nb) to collect voluntary expressions of availability for legal, tax, agroecological, energy, water, health competences pursuant to Code 6 and information technology competences pursuant to Code 7.\n3. The module is deemed documented when the following are shown:\na) the total time available for the activities of the Circle has been surveyed in aggregated form;\nb) the L1, L2 and L3 competences have been recorded in accordance with Code 5, Article 10;\nc) the land, the machines, the equipment, the PPE, the seeds and the energy resources available have been surveyed;\nd) the actual needs of Basket 1-2 have been surveyed;\ne) the provisional Storehouse in accordance with Articles 13–22 of Code 1 and the minimum Knowledge Storehouse in accordance with Code 5, Article 3, are active, with separate accounting for provisional and definitive goods;\nf) the Common Fund and the separate Legal Fund are active in accordance with the applicable rules;\ng) the coordination node on the federated Network is operational;\nh) the application of Level 1 of the Social Start-up Protocol has been documented.\n4. During this module, the continuity of the external work, school and purchasing activities necessary for personal security and stability is recommended. Participation in the internal activities of study, census and L1 training is voluntary and may not be imposed in conflict with the rights of persons."],[23,"art-4--operating-module-of-substitution","Art. 4 — Operating module of substitution","","Indicative minimum operating duration: from ninety to one hundred and eighty days. The start and the conclusion remain subordinate to the institutional phases and to the thresholds of Code 16.\n1. The module aims to develop internal alternatives until the threshold of self-production of Basket 1-2 provided for by Code 16 is reached. The percentage is calculated in accordance with a public and uniform methodology defined by the Basket STV.\n2. The substitution activities shall be planned by domain in accordance with Code 16, Article 3, paragraph 2.\n3. The continuation of external work activities by the persons involved is voluntary. This Annex does not limit the right to terminate or modify an employment relationship in accordance with the legislation applicable in the territory concerned and does not authorise the violation of contractual obligations or mandatory rules.\n4. During the module, Level 2 of the Social Start-up Protocol shall apply, comprising:\na) double facilitation of the Assemblies, with rotation and a prohibition on combining the roles of facilitator and proposer;\nb) verification of overload in accordance with Article 3 of the Protocol and Code 1, Article 6, paragraph 5;\nc) use of aggregated and non-profiling indicators;\nd) voluntary recourse to social reparation, without prejudice to access to Code 4."],[23,"art-5--module-of-selective-reduction-of-dependencies","Art. 5 — Module of selective reduction of dependencies","","Indicative minimum operating duration: from one hundred and eighty to three hundred and sixty-five days. The module may begin only after the relevant institutional authorisation and in compliance with Code 16.\n1. The module plans the selective reduction of the purchases and of the productive activities that feed dependencies incompatible with material autonomy. It does not authorise violations of the law or of mandatory contractual obligations, or interruptions of Basket 1-2.\n2. For each Circle the following shall be prepared and kept up to date:\na) a list of the goods or services that it is intended no longer to produce for the external market;\nb) a list of the external purchases that it is intended to cease or reduce;\nc) the N/NN/S classification, the reasons and the reference to the sectoral Codes.\n3. The objectives for the reduction of consumption shall be defined on the basis of a documented initial value. The cessation of goods classified S shall be pursued as a priority; NN purchases shall be reduced in accordance with a progressive plan. No reduction may concern an N good lacking an adequate internal alternative. The indicative objective of reducing by fifty per cent, within thirty days, the value of the supplies classified as dependency on the extractive system is applicable only if compatible with the continuity of the Basket, with safety and with the legal obligations in force.\n4. Any choice relating to overtime, tasks not owed or cessation of production shall be made by the persons concerned within the limits of the applicable labour, trade-union and contract law. Before collective initiatives that may affect employment relationships or contracts, a local professional legal opinion is required.\n5. Sabotage, damage, unlawful blocking of traffic, unlawful occupation and any conduct that violates mandatory rules are in any case prohibited. This Annex does not constitute an autonomous authorisation for such conduct.\n6. Tax obligations shall be fulfilled in full. Any donations or allocations of tax-creditable or deductible shares to Third Sector entities or commons are voluntary and shall be made through competent professionals in accordance with Code 15 and the applicable rules.\n7. During the module, the following shall be activated, each in the phase in which Article 3 of Code 16 provides for it and in accordance with the sectoral Codes:\na) the provisional minimum Biodiversity Register;\nb) the register of general risks;\nc) the inventory of critical machines, of PPE and of collective protections;\nd) at least one sanctuary per Basin, in accordance with Code 13, Article 4;\ne) the Biodiversity, Safety, Care, Habitat and Territory, Agroecological, and Energy and Water Councils."],[23,"art-6--module-of-federation-and-coordination-between-blocs","Art. 6 — Module of federation and coordination between blocs","","Indicative minimum operating duration: from three hundred and sixty-five to seven hundred and thirty days, subordinate to the institutional phases of Code 16.\n1. The federation pathway requires, each in the phase in which Article 3 of Code 16 provides for it:\na) the aggregation of at least three Basins;\nb) the first version of the Dependency Register in accordance with Code 15, with a plan with a fixed time limit for each critical dependency;\nc) L3 audits on ecological, safety and food autonomy;\nd) a node of the federated Network operational in every Basin;\ne) the actual overcoming of at least fifty per cent of the critical dependencies registered, calculated in accordance with public and verifiable criteria.\n2. The connection of a new territorial bloc takes place in accordance with the following rules:\na) Codes 1–16 shall be translated and adapted to the law applicable in the territory concerned by local legal professionals; the automatic importation of the legal model of another bloc is excluded;\nb) exchange between blocs not yet integrated into the same federal regime shall be oriented towards knowledge, STVs, free seeds, repair sheets and protocols. Inter-bloc debt and monetary exchanges are excluded where incompatible with the applicable transitional rules;\nc) at least one monthly coordination meeting between blocs shall be organised. The discussion shall include a survey of the purchases and dependencies still tied to the extractive system and shall produce, where necessary, a common exit plan;\nd) the crisis or the interruption of the activities of one bloc does not automatically give rise to the interruption of the other blocs. Continuity measures consistent with the Basket and with the applicable Codes shall be prepared."],[23,"art-7--external-dissemination-subsequent-to-the-transition","Art. 7 — External dissemination subsequent to the transition","","1. This Article governs an activity of external dissemination subsequent to the transition. It does not constitute a phase of Code 16 and does not modify the definition of Phase 4, which in Code 16 corresponds to closure.\n2. External dissemination is assessed on the basis of the voluntary adoption of solutions on account of measurable functional advantage and does not require universal adherence.\n3. The assessment may consider jointly:\na) the documented adoption of an STV or Sheet by at least three external parties not adhering to the Confederation;\nb) a documented reduction in the cost of Basket 1-2 or in the risk classified R2 or higher compared with a comparable extractive alternative;\nc) the replicability of the solution without critical proprietary dependency.\n4. For the purposes of this Article, an attestation of effectiveness consists of a written declaration by the adopting party describing the use, the duration and the results observed. The observation period is at least ninety days, unless the nature of the solution requires a different, reasoned period.\n5. The attestation shall be verified by the Council competent for the subject matter and, where the adoption affects safety at R2 or higher, by an independent L3 member drawn by lot in accordance with Article 36 of Code 4.\n6. The verification shall compare the documented results with a comparable alternative, considering Basket, safety and autonomy. External adoption does not, by itself, give rise to the activation of the Single World Balance provided for by Code 15, nor does it modify the conditions established by that Code."],[23,"art-8--operational-suspension-and-restart","Art. 8 — Operational suspension and restart","","1. The operating initiatives provided for by this Annex shall be suspended, as regards the proposal for passage or the activity of reducing dependencies, when one of the following conditions occurs:\na) Basket 1-2 is not guaranteed for three continuous months;\nb) more than twenty per cent of the valid responses to the anonymous questionnaire on perceived safety are negative;\nc) an R4 incident occurs that has not been managed in accordance with the applicable procedures or for which the required analysis of causes is lacking;\nd) the Legal Fund is exhausted.\n2. The suspension does not interrupt the provision of the Basket, access to the Guarantee, the safety measures or the other individual rights. It entails the suspension of the proposal for advancement or of the initiatives concerned only. The inventories, the minutes, the censuses and the STVs already verified shall be kept with an annotation of the cause.\n3. The restart requires certification of the removal of the cause by the competent bodies:\na) for the condition referred to in paragraph 1, letter b), the Council of reference for care, with the new verification provided for by Article 4, paragraph 6, letter c), of Annex A, in accordance with the procedure of that paragraph, which is the only one applicable to this condition;\nb) for the condition referred to in paragraph 1, letter c), the Safety Council;\nc) for the condition referred to in paragraph 1, letter a), jointly the Storehouse Council and the Transition Council;\nd) for the condition referred to in paragraph 1, letter d), the Transition Council, after a professional legal opinion.\n4. Upon restart, the conditions of Phase 0 relevant to the cause of the suspension shall be verified again. The previous outcomes that are not affected by the cause remain valid.\n5. The restart shall be resolved by the General Assembly, after the mandatory opinion of the competent Councils, and published in accordance with Article 15 of Code 3. Where the suspension for the condition of paragraph 1, letter b), concerns only the proposal for a change of phase, the proposal resumes with the positive outcome of the new verification of paragraph 3, letter a), without a separate restart resolution.\n6. The suspension does not constitute failure. In the cases of paragraph 1, letters a), b) and c), it attests exclusively to the temporary failure to pass the relevant threshold provided for by Article 4 of Code 16. In the case of paragraph 1, letter d), it concerns only the initiatives of this Annex, for which the requirement of Article 2, paragraph 1, letter b), is lacking, and does not affect the thresholds of Article 4 of Code 16."],[24,"","","","Technical annex adopted pursuant to Article 19 of Code 3 and referred to in Articles 1, paragraph 2, letter j), and 3, paragraph 1, letter g), of Code 16. It does not constitute an autonomous source of obligations or sanctions: it brings together in a single text the rules that a pilot Circle needs in its first twelve months. In the event of conflict, the Constitution and the Codes prevail."],[24,"art-1--whom-it-is-for-and-what-it-contains","Art. 1 — Whom it is for and what it contains","","1. This Charter is intended for a pilot Circle composed of thirty to one hundred and fifty persons in Phase 0 provided for by Article 3 of Code 16.\n2. It indicates only what is necessary in order to begin. Everything that is not indicated is activated in phases, when the relevant thresholds are met, in accordance with Articles 3 and 4 of Code 16.\n3. Every rule of the Charter refers to the provision from which it derives. The Charter shall not be used to impose conduct not provided for by the Codes."],[24,"art-2--what-is-needed-to-start","Art. 2 — What is needed to start","","1. To launch a pilot Circle the following are needed:\na) at least thirty persons who join individually, voluntarily and on an informed basis, pursuant to Article 2, paragraph 8, of Code 16;\nb) a non-profit entity recognised by the local legal order, in accordance with Article 16 of Code 15;\nc) a place and an initial census, aggregate and non-profiling, of land, competences, equipment, animals present and needs;\nd) a minimum Storehouse and a minimum Knowledge Storehouse;\ne) a Common Fund with separate accounting, in accordance with Article 17 of Code 15.\n2. Before joining, each person receives the Constitution, this Charter and an indication of the proportional contribution on external income provided for by Article 19-bis of Code 15.\n3. No one shall be induced to leave external work, to confer goods or to renounce rights recognised by the legal order in force."],[24,"art-3--bodies-of-the-first-year","Art. 3 — Bodies of the first year","","1. Decisions are taken by the Assembly, composed of all the persons who have joined, who vote individually and without proxy, in accordance with Article 12 of Code 3.\n2. The technical Councils are grouped into the three Area Councils provided for by Article 12-bis, paragraph 12, of Code 3: Material Life, People, Technical Safeguards. They do not command: they prepare matters, propose, and carry out decisions already taken.\n3. There are also established the Accounting Council, the Guarantee Circle provided for by Article 4 of Code 4 and the Transition Council provided for by Article 5 of Code 16.\n4. All offices are conferred by sortition among suitable and available persons, with the right of refusal. Coordination, facilitation and guarantee shall last no more than ninety consecutive days, pursuant to Article 4.4 of the Constitution.\n5. The time devoted to administration shall not exceed one tenth of the total CBO, in accordance with Article 12-bis, paragraph 13, of Code 3."],[24,"art-4--how-decisions-are-taken","Art. 4 — How decisions are taken","","1. Ordinary decisions are adopted by the consensus method in the absence of valid reasoned objections, after publication of the proposal for at least seventy-two hours, in accordance with Article 4 of Code 3.\n2. Decisions that the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3 reserves to the Assembly are adopted with the quorums and majorities of Articles 12 and 13 of the same Code.\n3. Every decision is reasoned, recorded and published. Anyone who has an interest in it may appeal in accordance with Article 17 of Code 3.\n4. The Assembly meets, as a rule, at least once a month."],[24,"art-5--needs-and-labour","Art. 5 — Needs and labour","","1. The starting point is needs: every quarter the Assembly approves the requirement and the Labour Balance provided for by Article 2 of Code 1.\n2. The CBO shall not exceed an average of fifteen hours per week, in accordance with Article 3 of Code 1. Unpleasant and arduous tasks rotate among all.\n3. If the hours needed exceed those available, first the deferrable activities are reduced and the formalities are simplified; the limit is not increased, except under the Solidarity Protocol, pursuant to Article 3, paragraph 3, of Code 1.\n4. People’s external work is not CBO and remains free, in accordance with Article 19 of Code 15."],[24,"art-6--basket-and-storehouse","Art. 6 — Basket and Storehouse","","1. Vital emergencies and essential needs have priority: water, food, basic energy, health care, housing. In capacity conflicts Article 32 of Code 1 applies; Basket 1-2 is defined by paragraph 1-quinquies of that Article.\n2. The inventory of the Storehouse is public in aggregate form. The goods of the Basket are distributed according to need and are not bought and sold among members.\n3. What cannot yet be produced is purchased externally by the Common Fund and is entered as a dependency to be reduced."],[24,"art-7--conflicts","Art. 7 — Conflicts","","1. Conflict is addressed first of all through the voluntary restorative procedure of Articles 5 to 8 of Code 4, with the assistance of the Guarantee Circle.\n2. During the transition, no measures of containment, coercive removal or location tracking are applied. For acts that the legal order in force classifies as a criminal offence, the public authorities remain competent, in accordance with Article 37 of Code 4.\n3. A person who reports a problem in good faith shall not suffer retaliation, in accordance with Article 16 of Code 4."],[24,"art-8--external-relations-and-money","Art. 8 — External relations and money","","1. The Circle complies with the legal order in force and with tax and contractual obligations, in accordance with Article 15 of Code 15.\n2. External means of payment are held only by the Common Fund and do not circulate among members.\n3. External income remains with the persons, gives no internal advantage and contributes to the Fund proportionally, in accordance with Article 19-bis of Code 15.\n4. No debts that create a critical dependency are contracted, in accordance with Article 9 of Code 16."],[24,"art-9--land-animals-and-commons","Art. 9 — Land, animals and commons","","1. Goods conferred are entered in the provisional inventory of Article 6 of Code 16. The conferral of goods is voluntary and states whether it is definitive or for temporary use.\n2. In the first year no decisions are adopted that make the destination of ecological or territorial goods irreversible.\n3. The animals present remain with the Community if the conditions of Article 6-bis of Code 9 are met. Every prohibited practice ceases without delay."],[24,"art-10--verification-at-twelve-months","Art. 10 — Verification at twelve months","","1. At the end of the first year the Assembly verifies the thresholds of Article 4 of Code 16, also by means of the Social Start-up Protocol, and publishes a report.\n2. The report indicates what has worked, which rules have proved inapplicable or too burdensome and which simplifications are proposed, also for the purposes of Article 38 of the Constitution.\n3. The Charter is updated on the basis of the report."],[25,"","","","The Circles Project is public because no one holds, alone, the knowledge needed to build a society. Law, medicine, engineering, computing, energy, agriculture, architecture, logistics, economics: every competence can find something to verify or to correct."],[25,"what-is-needed-most","What is needed most","","1. Concrete cases. Codes 3 and 4 require the rules to be tested on real cases before they are considered final. A case is a situation described precisely (a conflict, a water shortage, a refusal, an objection in the Assembly) and run through the procedures, to see where they hold and where they do not.\n2. Expert review. Anyone who knows a subject can check the Code that deals with it: what is wrong, what is missing, what cannot be applied.\n3. Contradictions and cross-references. An article that contradicts another, a reference that leads to the wrong paragraph, a term used in two senses.\n4. Numbers. Many parameters are initial proposals. The register of parameters to be verified says where each one comes from and what test it would need; a shorter list in English is in the change log. Data and experience that confirm or refute them are useful.\n5. Clarity. Obscure sentences, passages that a non-expert reader cannot follow.\n6. Translation. The English, Spanish and French versions are working translations; the Spanish one covers only the path for now. Corrections by native speakers, especially with legal training, are welcome, as are translations of the rules into other languages. The Italian text is the authoritative one; the terminology is fixed in testi/en/GLOSSARY.md, in testi/es/GLOSARIO.md and in testi/fr/GLOSSAIRE.md."],[25,"how-to-report-something","How to report something","","Open an issue in the repository and state:\nthe text and the article (for example: Code 3, Art. 12, paragraph 5);\nthe problem, in a few lines;\nthe proposed change, if you have one, with the rewritten text;\nthe reason: a figure, a case, an experience, a source.\nOpening an issue requires a GitHub account. If you do not have one, write the same things to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com.\nThose who know how to use the repository tools can propose the change directly to the file in testi/. The files in docs/ are generated: they must not be edited by hand."],[25,"the-four-texts-of-the-path","The four texts of the path","","Human history, Analysis of the present, The system against the individual and The Circles Project are authored texts, published under the CC BY-ND 4.0 licence: they may be shared in full, but not modified. You can still report typos, outdated figures or sources that are no longer reachable: the corrections are made by the authors.\nThey may be translated into other languages without asking for permission, on the conditions set out in the licence: a complete and faithful translation, the source cited, the same licence. Abridging or adapting them does require the authors’ permission: open an issue."],[25,"what-cannot-be-proposed","What cannot be proposed","","Article 37.4 of the Constitution defines an Inviolable Core: the rights of the person, the prohibition on concentrating power, the right to leave, the commons. Proposals that contradict it are not accepted. Everything else is open to discussion."],[25,"how-changes-are-decided","How changes are decided","","Until a Community applies the texts, proposals are assessed by those who maintain the repository, publicly and with reasons. Every accepted change enters the change log with its reason. When Communities applying the texts exist, the procedures of Article 38 of the Constitution will apply."],[25,"by-which-criteria","By which criteria","","A proposed change to the rules is accepted when it meets all of these criteria:\n1. It is precise. It states the text, the article and the paragraph, and contains the rewritten text. One proposal, one change.\n2. It gives reasons. It brings a figure, a source, a case or an experience. “It seems better to me” is not enough.\n3. It respects the Inviolable Core of Article 37.4 of the Constitution.\n4. It is consistent with the rest. It does not contradict other articles. If it touches a term, a threshold or a cross-reference used elsewhere, it also lists the other places to be aligned.\n5. It holds up on the cases. Run through the test cases already collected, it does not give a worse outcome than the current text.\n6. It does not reduce guarantees without saying so. If it narrows a right, lengthens a term of office, concentrates a decision or removes transparency, it says so and justifies it. In case of doubt, the version that concentrates less power and is easier to correct prevails.\nTiming depends on the kind of change:\nMaterial corrections (typos, wrong cross-references, terms not aligned): accepted straight away.\nChanges of substance: they are applied once they have been verified against the criteria above, as a rule with an adversarial reading that tries to take them apart. The proposals already decided are in the archive of proposals.\nWhen a proposal is rejected, the reason is written in the issue, which remains available. A change already accepted can be withdrawn if a test case shows that it has made the text worse."],[25,"tone","Tone","","The text is discussed, not the person. A harsh and precise criticism is welcome."],[26,"","","","Working translation. The Italian text is the authoritative one.\nCalibration draft, not adopted. This page collects the numbers proposed by the texts and states, for each one, where it comes from and what would be needed to confirm or correct it. It does not amend any provision: the values remain those written in the Constitution and in the Codes, and they may become binding only when data exist and the competent bodies are able to approve them."],[26,"how-to-read-it","How to read it","","Proposed value: the number written in the texts today.\nLocation: the article in which it is found.\nOrigin: an external source, where there is one; otherwise “drafting choice”, that is, a number set to make the provision applicable, without any data to support it.\nUncertainty: low, medium or high, according to how far the value is supported by data and how much it may vary from one place to another.\nEvidence needed: what would have to be measured, calculated or tested.\nAnyone who has data, experience or a criticism may open an issue or write to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com."],[26,"1-parameters-with-external-benchmarks","1. Parameters with external benchmarks","","For these values there are data or rules against which they can be compared. The comparison does not confirm them: it shows where to look.\nParameter · Proposed value · Location · Origin and benchmarks · Uncertainty · Evidence needed\nLimit of necessary work (CBO) · average not exceeding 15 hours a week, on a quarterly basis · Code 1, Art. 3 · Drafting choice. A first calculation based on needs (draft UCR Annex) gives between 9 and 13 hours a week, manufactured goods included. With today’s mode of production, an independent study (McElroy and O’Neill, 2025) estimates about 27 hours by adding the missing services to the bare minimum; the authors point out that none of their scenarios is realistic. · High. It is the parameter on which the balance of the whole system depends. · Actual Labour Balances; measured hours of collective kitchens, gardens and workshops; how much household work is counted in the CBO.\nWater Basket · at least 50 litres per person per day · Code 8, Art. 4-bis · The WHO (2020) indicates about 20 litres as basic access, 50 litres as intermediate access, with medium health risk, and over 100 litres as optimal access, with low risk. The Sphere humanitarian standard sets a minimum of 15 litres in emergencies and recognises that 50 may be needed in cities. · Low as a health minimum; medium for climate and local uses. · Actual consumption by climate and season; availability of the resource in the territory. Issue 6."],[26,"1-parameters-with-external-benchmarks","1. Parameters with external benchmarks","","Size of the Base Community · as a rule 30–500 persons · Const. 5.1 · “Dunbar’s number” (about 150) is disputed: a 2021 review finds estimates between 16 and 109 with confidence intervals from 2 to 520, and concludes that stating a number is pointless. In Vermont town meetings an average of about 20% of voters take part in towns with fewer than 5,000 registered voters. · High. · Participation data from assemblies, cooperatives and ecovillages, broken down by size. Issue 4.\nThreshold for a small Community · fewer than 150 persons entitled to vote · Const. 6.5; Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 12 · As above: the threshold of 150 has no solid basis. · High. · Verify below what size it is no longer possible to staff all the Councils without overloading people.\nQuorum of the Assembly · none for ordinary resolutions; 1/3 of those entitled to vote for qualified decisions; 1/2 for those with special guarantees · Const. 35.1; Code 3, Art. 12 · In Vermont town meetings average participation is around 20%: a quorum of one third is higher than open assemblies usually reach. · High. · Actual participation in assemblies of similar size; effect of the republication provided for when the quorum is not reached. Issue 5."],[26,"1-parameters-with-external-benchmarks","1. Parameters with external benchmarks","","Minimum autonomy of the Care Nucleus · 72 hours for vital goods and energy · Code 1, Art. 39 · For vital goods it matches the objective of the European Preparedness Union Strategy (JOIN(2025) 130, 26 March 2025): self-sufficiency of the population for at least 72 hours. The EU, however, speaks of supplies (food, water, medicines, a power bank), not of energy autonomy: 72 hours of energy for every Nucleus require storage or generators, and that is a far more demanding objective. · Low as a minimum. · Adequacy for the climate and isolation of the place; stocks actually needed.\nValidation of precautionary measures · within 24 hours · Code 4, Art. 34 · Stricter than the time limit of the Italian Constitution (Art. 13: 48 hours for notification to the judge and 48 for validation). · Medium: the time limit is protective; it must be verified that a body drawn by lot is able to meet it. · Test case on Code 4. Issue 2.\nIsolation and quarantine · no more than 14 days per person and per exposure, one extension of 14 on certification by two L3 persons; beyond 28 days only the Guarantee Tribunal, for periods of 14 · Code 6, Art. 2-bis, para. 3-quater · Duration in common use, linked to incubation. · Medium: incubation varies from one agent to another; the text refers to the dossier and the cap may prove short or long. · Comparison with the durations recommended for the most likely agents; test case on validation within 24 hours."],[26,"1-parameters-with-external-benchmarks","1. Parameters with external benchmarks","","Coercive measure in psychological crisis · 72 hours at a time, renewable with a new validation; no more than 14 days in all and 28 days in 90; reassessment every 12 hours and every hour during physical restraint · Code 6, Art. 8, para. 10 · Stricter than the Italian TSO (compulsory health treatment, Law 180/1978: 7 days, validation within 48 hours). · High: the cap of 28 days in 90 is new and leaves only voluntary measures for those who remain in danger afterwards. · Opinion of health persons with experience of psychiatric emergencies; test cases.\nProtected educational reception of minors · no more than 12 months, renewable once; review at least every 3 months · Code 4, Art. 25, para. 9 · Within the range of Italian juvenile probation (messa alla prova, 1–3 years). · Medium · Comparison with educational pathways for minors who have committed offences; test case."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","These are numbers set to make the provisions applicable. They have no external source: they must be tested on concrete cases.\nParameter · Proposed value · Location · Uncertainty · Evidence needed\nCare Nucleus · from 1 to 15 persons · Const. 5.1 · Medium · Actual cases of cohabitation and care; effects of the upper limit.\nBasin · as a rule at least 3 Communities · Const. 5.1 · Medium · Services that a Basin must guarantee (hospital, logistics) and minimum population to sustain them.\nCBO peak · up to 25 hours a week for no more than two consecutive weeks · Code 1, Art. 6 · Medium · Agricultural seasonality and emergencies: how many peak weeks are really needed.\nTechnical Councils · 3–5 persons, one year, staggered renewal, not eligible to be drawn by lot again before 12 months · Const. 6.5; Code 3, Art. 12-bis · Medium · Continuity of competences with one-year terms of office; suitable persons available in small Communities.\nArea Councils · three, each of 3–7 persons; interval of 6 months between terms of office · Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 12 · Medium · As above, in Communities below the threshold.\nAdministrative time · not exceeding 1/10 of the total CBO, on a quarterly basis · Const. 6.5; Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 13 · High · Measure the time that registers, assemblies and Councils really require.\nGuarantee Circle · 3–7 persons, 90 days (maximum 180 in the year) · Code 4, Art. 4 · Medium · Workload of appeals; time needed to learn the function."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","Justice bodies · Clarification Assembly 7, Transformation Council 5, Guarantee Tribunal 7 persons; 6 votes out of 7 for extreme measures · Code 4, Art. 27 · Medium · Test cases. Issue 2.\nProtective containment · not exceeding 5 years (Grade 5) and 10 years (Grade 6), with review at least every six months; in the accompaniment Community, report to the Tribunal at least every 3 months · Code 4, Art. 30, para. 3 · High · Comparison with data on recidivism and with legal systems that use fixed-term measures with review.\nObjection in the Assembly · at least 10% of those voting, within 72 hours of the outcome · Const. 34.2; Code 3, Art. 13-bis · Medium · Test case. Issue 1.\nEconomic thresholds for decisions · low threshold 100 UCR + 1 per person (at most 500), high threshold 500 UCR + 5 per person (at most 2,500) · Code 3, Art. 7 · High · With the coefficient proposed in the draft UCR Annex, 500 UCR are 0.4% of the annual work of a Community of 500 persons and 7% of one of 30. Proposal 11 links them to the number of persons, with a fixed part.\nCdR cap · 240 CdR on first application · Code 1, Art. 44 · Medium · A first simulation shows that the cap keeps the most active twentieth below the concentration threshold. To be reviewed with actual quarterly data."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","CdR decay · full value for 12 months, then one twelfth per month down to zero at the 24th month · Code 1, Art. 44 · Medium · As above. The text does not say how the value declines: proposal 9 chooses gradual reduction, one twelfth per month.\nRefusal of the contribution · graduated procedure before the Clarification Assembly · Const. 7.3 · Medium · Test case. Issue 3.\nLand share for the Autonomous Coexistence Pact · self-sufficiency of at least 1 person for every 100 residents or fraction of 100; available within 90 days of conclusion; reconstituted within 12 months if entirely entrusted · Code 10, Art. 4-bis, paras. 5 and 6 · High · Land and water needed for the self-sufficiency of a person and of a nucleus in different climates; for nuclei the share of a single Circle may not be enough.\nTime limits of the Autonomous Coexistence Pact · conclusion within 30 days of the request; search for a place within 30 days in Circle 2 and a further 30 in Circle 3; cessation for non-use beyond 12 months · Code 10, Art. 4-bis, paras. 1, 6 and 8 · Medium · In the worst case the wait exceeds six months: verify whether the handover time limit can be reduced.\nMediation before suspension of the CdR · at least 14 days from the written information · Code 4, Art. 29, para. 4-bis · Medium · Test case: whether they are enough to readjust tasks and mediate within the 60 days of Const. 7.3."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","Refusal of the CBO after Grade 3 · written notice of 30 days before referral to the Tribunal; cessation of status by 6 votes out of 7 · Const. 7.3-bis; Code 4, Art. 29, para. 6-bis · High · Test case; choice to be confirmed.\nRemoval from the common settlement · two offers of the Pact, the second after the mediation and at least 30 days after the first · Code 4, Art. 32-bis, para. 2 · Medium · Test case.\nStable decision on a separated minor · within 30 days of validation, which may be extended once only by 30; review at least every 6 months · Code 12, Art. 7-bis, para. 3-bis · Medium · Actual time needed for a health assessment and for hearing the minor; test case.\nAge of consent to care · 16 years · Code 6, Art. 2-ter, para. 1 · Medium · Comparison with the thresholds of other legal orders; the voting age (14) remains different.\nChoice of residence of minors · from 14 years, with assessment of autonomy within 30 days of the request; appeal to the Guarantee Tribunal within 14 days, decision within 30 · Const. 26.2; Code 12, Art. 7-ter, paras. 1–4 · Medium · Origin: voting age (Const. 26.2 and 36.1), the author’s choice of 21 October 2026 (Const. 26.2 sets no age). Evidence: real cases of minors living outside their family of origin."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","Minors leaving the Confederation · personally from 16 years, after an interview with the Care Council within 14 days; before that the declaration is made by those who hold responsibility for their care; appeal to the Guarantee Tribunal within 14 days, decision within 30 · Const. 26.5; Code 12, Art. 7-ter, paras. 5–7 · Medium · Origin: age of consent to care (Code 6, Art. 2-ter, para. 1), the author’s choice. Evidence: comparison with the thresholds of other legal orders; test case on the interview.\nDisagreement on the care of a minor · decision of the Guarantee Tribunal within 7 days, or within 24 hours if the care cannot wait · Code 6, Art. 2-ter, para. 3 · Medium · Test case.\nStable representative for care · appointed when the incapacity lasts or is expected to last more than 60 days; review at least every 12 months · Code 6, Art. 2-ter, para. 5; Code 12, Art. 7-bis, para. 5 · Medium · Test cases with persons who permanently cannot decide.\nContribution on external income · 1/10 of net income on first application, never more than 1/5 · Code 15, Art. 19-bis · High · Actual external requirement of a Community in transition (taxes, purchases, health care).\nDeclaration of external income · by bands, confidential, kept for 12 months · Code 15, Art. 19-bis · Low · Compatibility with the tax obligations of the State in which the Community is located."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","Reserve of the Common Fund · not exceeding 12 months of external requirement · Code 15, Art. 17 · Medium · Variability of external expenditure in the first years.\nCritical functions · at least 3 trained persons for each · Code 15, Art. 12, para. 7 · Medium · List of the critical functions of a typical Community and training times.\nReview of privileged access · quarterly, 3 persons drawn by lot · Code 7, Art. 8, para. 8 · Low · Technical verification with those who administer IT systems.\nChoice of accession · within 12 months of turning 18; silence counts as accession; written information at least 3 months before turning 18, without which the time limit does not run · Const. 26.4; Code 5, Art. 6, para. 6-bis · Medium · Assess whether silence as accession is compatible with freedom of choice.\nFounding regime · 2 Communities, 6 months, vote within 30 days, 2/3 of persons and 2/3 of Communities, ratification within 24 months · Const. 38.2 and 38.3 · High · Only the experience of the first Communities.\nAssembly in the first year · as a rule at least one a month · Annex C, Art. 4 · Low · Experience of the first year.\nHousing planning and allocation · plan updated at least once a year; shortage reported to Circle 2 after 6 months · Code 1, Art. 47-ter · Medium · Test case on the waiting list of a real Community."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","Storehouse Councils of Circles 2 and 3 · 5–7 persons, no more than 2 from the same Community; flow proposals decided within 48 hours, vital needs within 24 hours; criteria approved every year · Code 15, Art. 9-bis · Medium · Simulation of the Pool flows of a Basin.\nShared Councils of Circles 2 and 3 · same number as the Councils of the Community, no more than 2 from the same Community; urgent decisions ratified within 30 days · Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 6-bis · Medium · Test case: whether 30 days are enough to convene the General Assembly.\nRefusals by the AIs of Circles 2 and 3 · decision of the shared Network Council within 72 hours · Code 7, Art. 4, para. 7 · Low · Technical check with those who run the nodes.\nSTVs for procedures, systems and safety · value = cost of implementation and maintenance over 24 months; criticality of automations validated by 2 L3 persons, one unconnected with the system · Code 2, Art. 3-bis; Code 7, Art. 6, para. 5 · Low · Test case on an automation STV.\nSystems without source code · admitted for at most 12 months, extendable once by 12, with a public Substitution Plan; never for systems that propose or decide on rights, voting and Commons · Const. 19.4; Code 2, Arts. 5, para. 2, letter b), and 12 · Medium · List of the devices of the minimum Network allocation (Code 7, Art. 3, para. 9) for which no open alternative exists."],[26,"2-drafting-parameters","2. Drafting parameters","","Observations on the provisional STV of a new good · at least 72 hours · Code 2, Art. 14, para. 1-bis · Low · Origin: the publication window of Code 3, Art. 4, para. 2, letter b). Evidence: test case on the admission of a good.\nAppeals against decisions of Circles 2 and 3 · 3 members drawn by lot from the Guarantee Circles of different Communities · Code 3, Art. 17, para. 1-bis · Low · Origin: drafting choice, the smallest number that allows a majority. Evidence: caseload of appeals in a Basin."],[26,"3-values-the-texts-call-for-and-that-are-missing","3. Values the texts call for and that are missing","","The Codes refer to these values, but no text sets them yet.\nWhat is missing · Where it is required · References to start from\nContent of the Sufficiency Basket: goods, quantities, units, period · Code 1, Art. 18 · Proposed in the draft Basket Annex, with the sources of each quantity.\nFood Basket: nutritional values and days of stock · Code 9, Art. 4 · The planning figure used by the WHO, UNHCR and WFP in emergencies is 2,100 kcal per person per day: it is a survival minimum, not a standard of living.\nEnergy Basket: guaranteed minimum share · Code 8, Art. 4 · A 2020 study estimates that a decent life for 10 billion people is possible with less than 40% of today’s world energy. It must be translated into a share per person and per climate.\nHousing: minimum floor area · Const. 1.2; Code 10 · Proposed in proposal 2, version 3: at least a living room, a bedroom and a bathroom; 30 m² for one person, 45 for two, 60 for three. The Italian rule on habitability (Ministerial Decree of 5 July 1975) requires, for a dwelling with bedrooms, 14 m² for each of the first four occupants (10 for each further one) and a living room of at least 14 m²; 28 m² for one person and 38 for two are the minimums for a studio flat. Since 2024 the “Salva casa” decree (Law 105/2024) allows, in existing buildings and under certain conditions, studio flats of 20 and 28 m². The Sphere standard for emergencies is 3.5 m² of covered space per person (4.5–5.5 in cold climates): it is an emergency minimum, not a dwelling.\nBasic mobility: guaranteed distances and times · Code 11; Code 1, Art. 25 · No reference yet.\nUCR calculation method · Code 3, Art. 9 · Proposed in the draft UCR Annex, with a worked example.\nPublic CdR table · Code 1, Art. 46 · Model proposed in the draft table. It is local: each Community approves it."],[26,"4-technical-annexes-cited-and-not-yet-written","4. Technical annexes cited and not yet written","","Annex · Where it is cited · Status\nAnnual Technical Annex on the Basket · Code 1, Art. 18 · Draft 0.1 published, not adopted.\nUCR Technical Annex · Code 3, Arts. 5 and 9 · Draft 0.1 published, not adopted.\nQuality Technical Annex · Code 2, Arts. 2 and 3 · To be written.\nMobility Technical Annex · Code 1, Art. 25 · To be written.\nTraining Technical Annex · Code 1, Art. 28 · To be written.\nTechnical Annex on Restorative Procedures · Code 4, Art. 24 · To be written."],[26,"sources","Sources","","Pages consulted on 5 October 2026; original sources added on 19 October 2026.\nWHO, Domestic water quantity, service level and health, second edition (2020)\nSphere, The Sphere Handbook (2018), minimum humanitarian standards on water and shelter\nLindenfors, Wartel and Lind, Dunbar's number deconstructed, Biology Letters (2021)\nFrank Bryan, Real Democracy: The New England Town Meeting and How It Works, University of Chicago Press (2004), book on Vermont town meetings\nEuropean Commission and High Representative, European Preparedness Union Strategy, JOIN(2025) 130 (26 March 2025)\nUNHCR and WFP, reference energy requirement in emergencies (1997)\nMillward-Hopkins et al., Providing decent living with minimum energy, Global Environmental Change (2020)"],[27,"","","","Working translation. The Italian text is the authoritative one.\nCalibration draft 0.1, not adopted. Code 1 (Art. 18, para. 2) states that the content of the Basket is defined each year in a Technical Annex, approved by the Assembly on a joint proposal from the Health Council and the Storehouse Council. That annex has never been written. This page proposes a first version: it is meant for discussion and for doing the sums, and binds no one. The quantities will become rules only when a Community approves them through the prescribed procedure."],[27,"how-to-read-it","How to read it","","Planning quantities, not rations. The figures say how much the Storehouse must have available for each person. Withdrawal remains free, without charge and according to actual need (Code 1, Art. 18, para. 1, letter a).\nReference person: one adult. For children, pregnancy, elderly people and illness the quantities are adapted by the Health Council (Code 1, Art. 18, para. 3).\nOrigin: an external source, where there is one; otherwise “drafting choice”, that is, a figure set in order to be able to do the sums, without data to support it.\nA model to adapt. Climate, soil and buildings change the quantities. Each Community starts from here and corrects. The provisions “per Nucleus” come from a study that reasons on households of four people (Millward-Hopkins et al., 2020), while a Care Nucleus can have up to fifteen (Const. 5.1): for large Nuclei they must be adapted.\nThe Sufficiency Basket is made of seven parts that the Codes already name: Food, Water, Energy, Material, Health, Care, Logistics. To these are added the rights of Article 1.2 of the Constitution that no Basket yet quantifies: housing, education, connectivity, personal AI Node."],[27,"1-food-basket","1. Food Basket","","Seat: Code 9, Art. 4. The Basket is guaranteed on a plant basis. Animal products collected without harm are not planned into requirements (Code 9, Art. 6-bis, para. 4, letter d).\nThe starting point is the reference diet of the EAT-Lancet Commission (2019), calculated for 2,500 kcal a day. That diet allows animal foods to be zero. Here their calories are replaced by cereals, legumes and nuts, staying within the ranges the Commission indicates. In October 2025 the Commission published a new report, which updates the diet: this table, based on the 2019 report, must be compared with the new one.\nItem · Per person per day · Per person per year · Reference diet · Origin\nCereals, preferably wholegrain (dry weight) · 275 g · 100 kg · 232 g · Drafting adaptation\nLegumes (dry weight) · 100 g · 36.5 kg · 75 g (0–150) · Within the range: dried legumes 50 g (0–100) plus soya 25 g (0–50)\nNuts and oilseeds · 75 g · 27 kg · 50 g (0–75) · Upper limit of the range\nVegetables · 300 g · 110 kg · 300 g (200–600) · EAT-Lancet\nFruit · 200 g · 73 kg · 200 g (100–300) · EAT-Lancet\nPotatoes and other tubers · 50 g · 18 kg · 50 g (0–100) · EAT-Lancet\nUnsaturated vegetable oils · 40 g · 14.6 kg · 40 g (20–80) · EAT-Lancet\nAdded sugars · no more than 31 g · no more than 11 kg · 31 g · EAT-Lancet\nIodised salt · no more than 5 g · no more than 1.8 kg · — · WHO"],[27,"1-food-basket","1. Food Basket","","Vitamin B12 · requirement of 4 micrograms; the supplement dose, which is higher, is set by a nutritionist · — · — · German Nutrition Society (requirement)\nThe total is about 2,500 kcal a day. For comparison: the minimum value used in emergencies by UNHCR and WFP is 2,100 kcal; the Millward-Hopkins study on decent living uses 2,000–2,150 kcal as the average for a whole population, children included.\nThree caveats.\n1. Vitamin B12 is not optional. In a diet without animal foods it must always be supplemented: the German Nutrition Society states that this is necessary. The 4 micrograms in the table are the daily requirement, not the supplement dose: the body absorbs only a small part of each dose, and supplement doses are usually much higher. It is a good that the Community cannot produce at the outset: it must be entered in the Dependency Register of Code 15.\n2. The table must be checked by people who work in nutrition. In particular for protein, iron, calcium, zinc, iodine, omega-3 and vitamin D, and for children, pregnancy and breastfeeding.\n3. Foods for weaning and for frail persons (Code 9, Art. 4, para. 1) are not quantified here: the Health Council defines them.\nStocks.\nWhere · Quantity · Origin\nCare Nucleus · 72 hours of vital goods · Code 1, Art. 39, para. 2\nStorehouse: cereals, legumes, oil, nuts · requirements until the next harvest, plus three months · Drafting choice\nStorehouse: other foods · thirty days · Drafting choice"],[27,"1-food-basket","1. Food Basket","","Land. A study on the United States (Peters et al., 2016) estimates for vegetarian diets, including the vegan one, 0.13–0.14 hectares of agricultural land per person per year. It is an order of magnitude: it depends on yields, climate and water. With that value a Community of 150 people would need about 20 cultivated hectares, one of 500 about 65–70."],[27,"2-water-basket","2. Water Basket","","Seat: Code 8, Art. 4-bis.\nItem · Per person per day · Per person per year · Origin\nDrinking water for drinking, cooking, personal and domestic hygiene · at least 50 litres · about 18 m³ · Code 8, Art. 4-bis, para. 3. For the WHO (2020) 50 litres is intermediate access, at medium risk to health; the risk is low only with optimal access, above 100 litres. The 50 litres are a guaranteed minimum, not a target.\nof which hot water · 20 litres · about 7 m³ · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nA Community of 150 people needs at least 7.5 m³ a day for personal uses. Water for health and care services and agricultural water are counted separately."],[27,"3-energy-basket","3. Energy Basket","","Seat: Code 8, Art. 4. The Code lists the services that the minimum share must cover, but does not set a value. The draft proposes defining the Basket by services, and calculating the kilowatt-hours afterwards, climate by climate and building by building.\nService (Code 8, Art. 4, para. 1) · Proposed level · Origin\nPreserving food · one fridge-freezer per Nucleus · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nCooking food · one hob per Nucleus · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nDomestic hot water · 20 litres per person per day · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nBasic lighting · all inhabited rooms, for the hours of darkness in which they are used · Drafting choice\nRecharging essential devices · personal communication device, AI Node, health aids · Drafting choice\nMinimum heating · at least 18 °C in inhabited rooms; more for elderly people, children and the sick · WHO, Housing and health guidelines (2018)\nCooling · as much as is needed to protect health in heatwaves · To be defined with the Health Council\nHealth Storehouse and Network node · continuity without interruptions · Code 8, Art. 4, para. 1, letter g\nBenchmark. The Millward-Hopkins study estimates that a decent life requires on average 15.3 gigajoules of final energy per person per year (from 13 to 18.4 depending on the country): about 4,250 kWh a year, that is 11.6 kWh a day. The value includes everything: home, food, transport, health care, school, production of goods. The domestic share of the Energy Basket is only one part of it, still to be calculated."],[27,"4-housing","4. Housing","","Seat: Const. 1.2, letter a). Housing is a right, but no Code sets its standard.\nItem · Proposed level · Origin\nFloor area · at least a living room, a bedroom and a bathroom; 30 m² for one person, 45 for two, 60 for three, then 15 more per person up to the fifth and 10 beyond · Proposal 2, version 3: drafting choice. The previous version set 15 m² per person, from Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nIndoor temperature · at least 18 °C in the cold season · WHO (2018)\nPrivacy · a lockable space of at least 9 m² for each person from fourteen years of age · Proposal 2, version 3; 9 m² is the minimum single bedroom of the Italian Ministerial Decree of 5 July 1975\nEmergency minimum · 3.5 m² of covered space per person · Sphere standards. It applies only to emergency reception, not as a dwelling."],[27,"5-material-basket","5. Material Basket","","Seat: Annex A to Code 1, Art. 4. The Code lists the categories; the list with quantities is a matter for an STV. The quantities below are drafting choices, except where indicated.\nCategory (Annex A, Art. 4, para. 1) · Proposed allocation\nConservation and cooking of food · per Nucleus: fridge-freezer, hob, pots and containers\nMinimum furnishings · one bed with mattress per person; per Nucleus a table, one seat per person, containers for personal effects\nEssential linen and tableware · two changes of bed and bath linen per person; one set of tableware per person\nBasic tools for domestic life · one kit per Nucleus for cleaning, small repairs and sewing\nBasic PPE · according to the Safety Sheets of the tasks performed\nSpare parts and components for the functions of levels 1 and 2 · stock expressed in days of autonomy (Annex A, Art. 4, para. 3)\nTwo items that the texts do not name, and that the draft proposes adding:\nItem · Proposed quantity · Origin\nClothing · 4 kg of new garments per person per year; washing for 80 kg of laundry per year · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nPersonal hygiene · soap, oral hygiene, menstrual products, nappies, according to need · Drafting choice"],[27,"6-health-basket","6. Health Basket","","Seat: Code 6, Art. 10. It comprises life-saving goods.\nItem · Proposed reference · Origin\nMedicines · the WHO Model List of Essential Medicines, adapted by the Health Council · WHO, 24th list\nStaff · 4.45 doctors, nurses and midwives per 1,000 people · WHO, health workforce strategy to 2030: a minimum threshold for identifying countries with severe shortages, not a European standard\nConsequence. With that threshold a Community of 150 people does not reach one full-time health worker. Health care must be organised in the Basin: three Communities of 500 people require six or seven. This is consistent with the district hospital that the Constitution entrusts to Circle 2 (Art. 5.1). The threshold is a minimum designed for the poorest countries: in European countries the density of health workers is much higher, and health care in the Basin will need more."],[27,"7-care-basket","7. Care Basket","","Seat: Code 12, Art. 4. It is measured in hours of assistance per person per week, according to assessed need. There is no quantity that is the same for everyone.\nItem · Level · Origin\nProvisional activation of essential services · within 72 hours of the report, pending assessment · Code 12, Art. 4, para. 3\nHours of assistance · according to the functional assessment · To be quantified with the first real cases"],[27,"8-logistics-basket","8. Logistics Basket","","Seat: Code 11, Art. 4.\nItem · Level · Origin\nDelivery to frail persons · within 24 hours of the request · Code 11, Art. 4, para. 1\nDelivery to other persons · within 72 hours of the request · Code 11, Art. 4, para. 1\nPlace · at home or at an accessible exchange point, for levels 1 and 2 · Code 11, Art. 4, para. 5"],[27,"9-education-connectivity-and-ai-node","9. Education, connectivity and AI Node","","Seat: Const. 1.2, letters c) and d); Code 5, Art. 2, para. 4-bis; Code 7, Art. 3, para. 9. Code 5 places in the Basket the materials, tools and spaces for literacy and for safety training; Code 7 sets the minimum connectivity provision.\nItem · Proposed level · Origin\nLiteracy · guaranteed by the age of eleven: reading and writing, basic mathematics and the other competences of Article 29.4 of the Constitution · Code 5, Art. 6, para. 8\nReference figures · three for each minor, rotating quarterly · Code 5, Art. 6, para. 7\nPersonal AI Node · one per person · Const. 1.2, letter d)\nPersonal communication device · one for every person who requests it from twelve years of age, distinct from the AI Node; before that age, with the participation of the reference figures · Code 7, Art. 3, para. 9, letter b); Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020) count one telephone per person over the age of ten\nNetwork access · an access point in every dwelling that requests it; in every Circle 1 an assisted access point with shared devices. No computer per Nucleus: every person already has an AI Node · Code 7, Art. 3, para. 9, letters a) and c)\nSchool space · 10 m² per student · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nAccess without digital tools · always guaranteed to those who choose disconnection · Code 7, Art. 5-bis, para. 8"],[27,"10-basic-mobility","10. Basic mobility","","Item · Level · Origin\nDistance of tasks · as a rule within thirty minutes of the Care Nucleus · Code 1, Art. 25, para. 4\nBenchmark · 5,000–15,000 km per person per year, by all means of transport · Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020)\nThe method for calculating travel times is a matter for the Mobility Technical Annex (Code 1, Art. 25, para. 6), still to be written."],[27,"11-what-is-missing-to-close-the-account","11. What is missing to close the account","","This draft says what is needed. It does not yet say how much labour it costs. This is the step on which the fifteen-hour limit of the Base Contribution depends (Code 1, Art. 3).\nWhat must be calculated · With what\nHours of labour to produce each item of the Basket, in a typical Community · First calculation in the draft UCR Annex: between 9 and 13 hours a week, manufactured goods included\nLand and yields for the Food Basket, by climate · Local agronomic data; Agroecological Council\nKilowatt-hours of the Energy Basket, by climate and building · Energy Basket STV (Code 8, Art. 4, para. 6)\nNutritional adequacy of the food table · Check by a nutritionist\nList of goods that the Community cannot produce · Dependency Register (Code 15, Art. 12)"],[27,"12-gaps-in-the-texts-found-while-writing-the-draft","12. Gaps in the texts found while writing the draft","","They are not corrected here. For each one there was an amendment proposal, accepted on 19 October 2026: the text is now in the Codes.\n1. Clothing and hygiene products do not appear in any Basket. The Material Basket names linen, not clothes.\n2. Housing is a constitutional right, but no Code sets its floor area, temperature or privacy.\n3. Connectivity is a right without quantities. For education Code 5 sets the content, but does not say that materials and spaces are part of the Basket.\n4. The Energy Basket has a list of services, but no first-application value, unlike the Water Basket."],[27,"how-it-is-corrected","How it is corrected","","The Annex is reviewed every year. Anyone who has data, an experience or a criticism can open an issue or write to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com. The figures on this page are also collected in the register of parameters to be verified."],[27,"sources","Sources","","Pages consulted on 5 October 2026; original sources added on 19 October 2026.\nWillett et al., Food in the Anthropocene: the EAT–Lancet Commission on healthy diets from sustainable food systems, The Lancet (2019)\nRockström et al., The EAT–Lancet Commission on healthy, sustainable, and just food systems, The Lancet (October 2025)\nWHO, fact sheet on healthy diet\nGerman Nutrition Society, D-A-CH reference values for vitamin B12: Ströhle et al., Molecular Nutrition & Food Research (2019)\nUNHCR and WFP, reference energy requirement in emergencies (1997)\nPeters et al., Carrying capacity of U.S. agricultural land: Ten diet scenarios, Elementa (2016)\nWHO, Domestic water quantity, service level and health, second edition (2020)\nMillward-Hopkins et al., Providing decent living with minimum energy, Global Environmental Change (2020)\nWHO, Housing and health guidelines (2018), recommendations on low indoor temperatures\nSphere, The Sphere Handbook (2018), minimum humanitarian standards on water and shelter\nWHO, Model Lists of Essential Medicines\nWHO, Global strategy on human resources for health: Workforce 2030 (2016), threshold of 4.45 health workers per 1,000 people"],[28,"","","","Working translation. The Italian text is the authoritative one.\nCalibration draft 0.1, not adopted. Code 3 (Art. 9) entrusts to a Technical Annex the method for calculating the reproduction cost of goods and services, measured in Reproduction Cost Units (UCR). That annex has never been written. This page proposes a first version of it and uses it straight away for the question on which the whole system depends: are fifteen hours a week enough?\nThe answer, in brief. Counted on need, the Basket costs between 7 and 10 hours a week of internal labour. The manufactured goods required, also counted on need (goods that last, are repaired and are shared), add 1.4 to 2.7 hours. In all, between 9 and 13 hours: below the limit, with a margin of 2–6 hours. It is a first count, full of declared assumptions. The most complete independent study instead measures today’s way of producing: adding to the minimum scenario the items it lacks, an estimate by the authors leads to about 27 hours. The difference between 13 and 27 is what the project promises to eliminate: it is not proven, and only an experiment can confirm it."],[28,"1-what-the-ucr-is","1. What the UCR is","","One UCR corresponds to one hour of ordinary Base Contribution. Materials, energy and logistics are converted into hours (Code 3, Art. 9, para. 1). The UCR is not a price, is not attributed to persons and is not a means of exchange (Art. 9, paragraphs 2 and 7). It serves three purposes: comparing alternatives, making visible how much labour a choice costs, establishing which body decides (Art. 7)."],[28,"2-the-method","2. The method","","The Code lists seven points that the Annex shall govern (Art. 9, para. 4). The draft proposes a rule for each.\nPoint · Proposed rule\na) Time · One hour of labour is worth one UCR, whoever performs it and whatever the task. It is counted to the quarter hour. Preparation, tidying up and specific mandatory training are included. The increases for unpleasant tasks (Code 1, Art. 10) are recognition in CdR and do not change the UCR.\nb) Materials · A material produced by the Community is worth the UCR required to produce it. A recovered material is worth the hours of recovery. A material purchased outside is worth the hours actually worked to produce it, when they are known; otherwise its price multiplied by the hours-per-euro coefficient of the country in which it is produced (see below). A durable good is spread in equal shares over the years of its useful life.\nc) Energy · Energy produced by the Community is worth the UCR of the plant and its maintenance, divided by the kilowatt-hours produced over its useful life. Purchased energy is worth price times coefficient.\nd) Digital services · Hours of maintenance of the Network plus shares of the devices, divided among those who use the service.\ne) Time · No discount rate and no interest: an hour is worth an hour, today and in ten years. The hours-per-euro coefficient is updated every year. A value is recalculated when the data change by more than a fifth."],[28,"2-the-method","2. The method","","f) Certification · The Accounting Council certifies the value on the basis of the STV or the SVS (Art. 9, para. 5) and publishes, for each datum, the source and an uncertainty class: measured, estimated, assumed.\ng) Revision and challenge · Annual revision. Anyone may challenge a value by bringing a datum; the Council replies with a public statement of reasons.\nLogistics. Hours of transport, plus the share of the vehicle, plus the energy consumed.\nAn hour is worth an hour, whoever works it and wherever. For external purchases a way is needed to go from euros to hours. Price alone is not enough, because it reflects wages and not hours: a good produced where labour is poorly paid costs few euros and contains many hours. Converting it with the European average would mean exchanging one hour of one’s own labour for many hours of someone else’s labour. The draft therefore proposes three rules, in order.\n1. Actual hours. If the hours worked to produce a good are known, those are used.\n2. Coefficient of the country of production. If they are not known, the price is multiplied by the hours of labour per euro of the country in which the good is produced. If it is produced in several countries, each part of the price has its own coefficient.\n3. European average. If it is not known where it is produced, the coefficient of the economy in which the Community is located is used, and the value is certified as “assumed”."],[28,"2-the-method","2. The method","","The coefficient is the ratio between the hours worked and the output of an economy. In the European Union, in 2024: 353.7 billion hours for 18,043 billion euros, that is 0.0196 hours per euro: 51 euros of output contain on average one hour of labour. For other countries the draft derives it from output per inhabitant, assuming the same hours worked per inhabitant as in the European Union. The coefficient uses the country’s average productivity, whereas exported goods usually come from more productive sectors: the hours in imported goods are therefore probably overestimated.\nCountry of production · Hours per euro · One hour of labour every\nEuropean Union · 0.020 · 51 euros\nChina · 0.064 · 16 euros\nBrazil · 0.082 · 12 euros\nIndonesia · 0.17 · 5.8 euros\nVietnam · 0.18 · 5.6 euros\nCôte d’Ivoire · 0.31 · 3.2 euros\nIndia, Bangladesh · 0.33 · 3.1 euros\nEthiopia · 0.75 · 1.3 euros\nRwanda · 0.80 · 1.2 euros\nAn example of actual hours: in Rwanda coffee growing requires about 251 working days per hectare and yields on average 1.69 kg of cherries per plant. With 2,500 plants per hectare, seven-hour working days, six kilos of cherries per kilo of green coffee and 1.19 kilos of green per kilo of roasted (assumption), one kilo of roasted coffee contains about 3 hours of plantation labour. Converted with the European average, the same kilo would contain 0.3."],[28,"2-the-method","2. The method","","What makes up the cost: labour, means of production, transport. The real cost of a good has three parts: the hours of those who produce it, the share of the means used to produce it (machines, materials, energy, converted into hours) and transport. A country’s coefficient does not measure distance: it estimates how many hours are needed there, with the means of production used there. The coffee contains three hours because it is picked by hand, with few means; with better tools it would contain fewer, wherever it was grown. Transport is counted separately and weighs little: in May 2026 a container from Shanghai to Genoa cost about 4,100 dollars, that is about 20 cents per kilo of goods with a twenty-tonne load: less than one hundredth of a UCR.\nThese are indicative conversions (Art. 9, para. 6), to be improved with supply-chain data.\nWhat the UCR does not measure. It does not measure environmental impact, which is assessed by the STV in accordance with Code 2. It does not include domestic work carried out in the Care Nuclei. It does not include civic functions, which appear in the Labour Balance and not in the cost of individual goods."],[28,"3-worked-example-one-kilo-of-bread","3. Worked example: one kilo of bread","","The values marked “example” serve to show the method: they are not measurements.\nStep · Data · UCR\nOne hectare of wheat · 45 hours of labour (Italian regional tables); 400 euros of purchases and machine shares (example) = 7.8 UCR; yield 2,930 kg · 0.018 per kg of wheat\nMill · 15 hours per tonne, 0.05 kWh and 2 cents of share per kg (example); 95 kg of flour from 100 kg of wheat · 0.035 per kg of flour\nFlour in the bread · 0.74 kg of flour per kg of bread · 0.026\nLabour at the oven · 15 kg of bread per hour of labour (example) · 0.067\nOven energy · 0.7 kWh (example); one kWh purchased at 0.25 euros is worth 0.0049 UCR · 0.003\nSalt, yeast, share of the oven · 5 cents (example) · 0.001\nOne kilo of bread · · 0.097 UCR, about 6 minutes\nTwo thirds of the cost is labour at the oven. The field weighs less than a fifth. It is the same result as the balance below: in food production, processing and cooking cost more hours than growing."],[28,"4-first-balance-of-hours","4. First balance of hours","","The count concerns a Community of 150 persons with the age structure of the European Union, at steady state. It follows the categories of the Labour Balance (Code 1, Art. 2, para. 7). All the numbers can be recalculated and modified with the program strumenti/bilancioore.py, published in the repository."],[28,"41-hours-available","4.1 Hours available","","Item · Persons · Equivalent contributors · Source\nUnder 18: no mandatory CBO · 27 · 0 · Eurostat: 17.8%\nBetween 18 and 69 · 100 · 100 · Eurostat: 66.7%\nBetween 70 and 74: CBO reduced by half · 8 · 4 · Eurostat: 5.3%; Code 1, Art. 11\nFrom 75: exempt · 15 · 0 · Eurostat: 10.2%; Code 1, Art. 11\nWomen who have recently given birth and parents of children under three · · −4 · Eurostat: 2.57% children under three; Code 1, Art. 11\nSevere disability, caregivers, illness · · −5 · Assumption: 5%\nTotal · 150 · 95 · 63% of the population\n95 contributors times 15 hours times 52 weeks: about 73,900 hours a year, that is 493 hours per inhabitant. The count provides for no weeks of break: with 48 weeks the hours available fall to 68,200."],[28,"42-hours-required","4.2 Hours required","","Two scenarios. Scenario A uses minimum standards. Scenario B gives four items (health, social care, education, construction) the same hours that the European Union devotes to them today; it is not today’s Europe, because in all it uses 43% of European hours per inhabitant.\nCategory · A: essential · B: four services at European level · How it is calculated\nFood · 10,930 · 10,930 · Field 4,570 hours on 24 hectares; processing 1,800 (assumption: mill, oven for half the cereals, oil mill, preserves); one collective meal a day per person, 4,560\nEnergy and water · 3,350 · 3,350 · Twice the European average hours of the sector, for the small scale (assumption)\nCare: health · 1,080 · 6,850 · A: WHO threshold of 4.45 workers per 1,000 persons. B: hours of the health sector in the EU\nCare: elderly and dependent persons · 2,610 · 4,510 · A: 5 workers per 100 persons over 65 (OECD average: it counts persons, not hours, and includes countries where most care is left to families). B: hours of social care in the EU, nursery included\nCare: nursery · 1,920 · included above · 40 hours a week for 48 weeks, one adult for every five children (assumption)\nLearning · 3,020 · 6,780 · A: one adult for every 12.2 minors (EU average). B: hours of all education in the EU, universities included\nMaintenance and construction · 4,430 · 8,850 · A: half the hours of construction in the EU (assumption). B: all of them, new construction included"],[28,"42-hours-required","4.2 Hours required","","Logistics · 2,560 · 2,560 · One six-hour shift at the Storehouse and one hour of deliveries a day (assumption)\nOther activities · 2,150 · 2,150 · Laundry, cleaning of common spaces, Network, sanctuaries (assumption)\nCivic functions and documentation · 3,560 · 5,110 · The cap of one tenth of the total (Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 13), used as a prudent estimate\nTotal · 35,600 · 51,100\nShare of hours available · 48% · 69%\nHours a week per contributor · 7.2 · 10.4\nThe field, item by item. Quantities from the Basket draft plus 15% for losses and seed; Italian 2024 yields reduced by a fifth for cultivation without synthetic chemicals; hours per hectare from Italian regional tables, without the extra hours that organic cultivation usually requires (not applied). For vegetables the count uses 700 hours per hectare, a prudent value at the upper end of the tables; for sunflower 24, as in the Lazio table. The hazelnut yield is that of 2024, a crisis year for the Italian harvest.\nCrop · Hectares · Hours a year\nCereals · 5.9 · 270\nLegumes · 3.8 · 300\nNuts (hazelnuts) · 9.4 · 2,830\nVegetables · 0.7 · 510\nFruit · 1.0 · 540\nPotatoes · 0.1 · 40\nOil (sunflower) · 3.0 · 70\nTotal · 24 · 4,570\nThe 24 hectares are close to the 20 estimated in the Basket draft with another method. Nuts alone occupy more than a third of the land and more than half of the field hours: 75 grams a day is a costly quantity."],[28,"42-hours-required","4.2 Hours required","","A check: for the same 150 persons the European Union today employs about 14,100 hours across agriculture, the food industry and catering. The 10,930 hours of the count are of the same order."],[28,"43-manufactured-goods-counted-on-need","4.3 Manufactured goods, counted on need","","Machines, tools, panels, building materials, medicines, textiles, electronics: the Community does not produce them itself. Here they are counted like food, starting from what is needed and not from what is consumed today. For each good: how much it costs, how many years it lasts, how many persons share it. Euros become hours with the coefficient of section 2.\nGroup · What it includes · Euros per person a year · Hours a year for 150 persons\nPerson and Nucleus · Clothing (4 kg a year), bed, furniture, linen and tableware, refrigerator and cooker per Nucleus, washing machine in a common laundry, telephone, Nucleus computer, AI Node, bicycle, hygiene · 654 · 1,920\nHealth · Medicines, medical supplies, share of the Basin’s equipment · 400 · 1,180\nHousing · Materials for 15 m² of dwelling and 5 m² of common buildings per person, over 80 years · 188 · 550\nEnergy and water · Photovoltaics (2 kW per person), storage, heat, water network, spare parts · 281 · 830\nProduction · Agricultural machinery, mill, oil mill, collective kitchen, workshop, seeds and materials · 253 · 750\nMobility and Network · Three light electric vehicles, 3,000 km a year of public transport, servers and equipment · 466 · 1,370\nLearning · Materials and instruments · 50 · 150\nTotal · · 2,291 · 6,740\nThat is 1.4 hours a week per contributor. The inventory is converted with the European average. With the rule of section 2 a good made half in China contains about twice the hours: doubling the whole inventory gives 2.7 hours. The factor of 2 is an assumption of the project, without a source.\nWhat this inventory is worth. Prices and lifetimes are drafting assumptions, listed one by one in the program: they are to be replaced with real quotations. The result, however, does not depend on a wrong price: even tripling the entire inventory the complete count stays below fifteen hours. For comparison, today’s European manufacturing employs about 14,200 hours for 150 persons, food industry excluded: the inventory on need asks for less than half of that.\nA larger dwelling. With the measurements of Code 1, Art. 47-bis (proposal 2, accepted on 19 October 2026), the average floor area rises to about 22 m² per person, if households are like Italian ones today (assumption). The Housing item goes from 550 to 740 hours a year and the total from 6,740 to 6,930 hours: still 1.4 hours a week per contributor, 2.8 with the factor for imported goods. The tables of this draft remain calculated with 15 m²."],[28,"44-the-complete-count","4.4 The complete count","","A: essential · B: four services at European level\nInternal labour · 7.2 · 10.4\nManufactured goods counted on need · 1.4 to 2.7 · 1.4 to 2.7\nTotal, hours a week per contributor · 8.6 to 10.0 · 11.7 to 13.1\nMargin below fifteen hours · 5.0 to 6.4 · 1.9 to 3.3\nIn a Community that lives within an external economy, manufactured goods are paid for by the Common Fund (Code 15, Articles 17 and 19-bis), not with CBO hours: the count says how many hours would be needed if the Confederation produced them."],[28,"45-what-the-count-does-not-include","4.5 What the count does not include","","1. Housework in the Nuclei. According to the European time-use survey (data collected between 2008 and 2015), each person devotes on average between three and four hours a day to home and family; the 30–50 minutes for preparing meals are an estimate of the project, not of that source. The count brings into the CBO only one collective meal a day, the nursery, the laundry and the cleaning of common spaces: 1.7 hours a week per contributor. Everything else remains in the Nuclei and is not counted.\n2. Some items not estimated: repair of tools, mandatory training, civic protection, time for passing on competences.\n3. Start-up. Building the Community costs more than maintaining it.\n4. What the project eliminates. Commerce, advertising, finance, insurance: they are not in the count, because in the system of Circles they do not exist. It is a choice of the project, not a datum."],[28,"46-three-external-comparisons","4.6 Three external comparisons","","Comparison · Datum · What it says\nToday’s European economy · 787 hours of paid labour per inhabitant a year · The fifteen-hour limit makes available 493 hours per inhabitant: 63% of the paid labour that Europe uses today.\nAn independent study (McElroy and O’Neill, 2025, on the United Kingdom) · For indispensable goods alone: 17.5 hours a week. Adding the items that according to the authors that scenario lacks (government, security, universities, childcare, insurance, capital maintenance): 36.7, an estimate made in one sentence of the discussion, not a calculated scenario. For a “good life” at the minimum levels asked for by residents: 45.5. Today’s British consumption: 64.7 · Brought to the basis of this count they are 12.7, 26.6, 32.9 and 46.9 hours of CBO a week: the conversion is ours. On the study’s basis of calculation (population aged 15 to 64, 46.6 weeks a year) the roughly 13 hours of this count would be about 18. The study counts all sectors and the labour embodied in imports, with today’s way of producing; it does not count unpaid care work.\nReal income-sharing communities (United States) · Twin Oaks, about 100 adults: between 36 and 44 hours a week depending on the source (weak datum: Wikipedia cites a video, the communities directory gives 40 and 42). East Wind, today between 35 and 45 members: 35 hours. Acorn: 42 hours · The quotas are per adult member and include cooking, cleaning, childcare and the work for the market that finances purchases: to compare them with the CBO, the housework that remains in the Nuclei must be added to it. They are small communities, little mechanised and with few elderly people: the comparison is indicative.\nWhere the difference between 13 and 27 hours comes from. The count of this draft and the study measure two different things. The study measures the world as it is: in its minimum scenario 29% of the hours are agricultural labour (according to an assumption of the project, which the study does not verify, largely carried out where an hour of labour produces little) and 40–45% are services; the items that the authors add in the discussion weigh more than all the rest. This count measures the system as the project designs it: agriculture with European yields and equipment, administration limited to one tenth of the hours, no commerce, no finance, no insurance, goods that last and are shared. The difference is therefore the project’s wager, put into numbers. The authors of the study reach a similar conclusion: none of their scenarios is realistic, reducing consumption is not enough, the way of producing has to change."],[28,"47-what-can-be-drawn-from-it","4.7 What can be drawn from it","","1. Counted on need, the count adds up. Between 9 and 13 hours a week, everything included, against a limit of 15.\n2. The margin is 2–6 hours, and the uncertainty is of the same order. Many items are assumptions. The count holds even tripling the manufactured goods, but it is not a measurement.\n3. The result holds for the system as it is designed, not for today’s way of producing. With that, the independent estimate gives about 27 hours. The fifteen hours depend on what the project changes: no waste, no intermediation, durable goods, light administration. These are the things to verify first.\n4. Everything depends on where the boundary between CBO and Nucleus lies. If all of today’s housework entered the CBO, it alone would be worth about 33 hours a week per contributor: an order of magnitude, calculated with three hours a day for all 150 persons. The texts leave to each Community the choice of what to make collective: it is the decision that weighs most on the limit.\n5. Health and education cannot be sustained with 150 persons. Scenario A asks for 0.7 health workers and 1.9 teachers full-time by today’s standards: numbers too small to guarantee a service. They are to be shared within the Basin, as the Constitution provides for the hospital (Article 5.1).\n6. The limit applies to each person, not to the sum. Specialist hours are divided among those who are qualified (Code 1, Art. 6, para. 3). At 780 hours a year each, health care at European level requires 9 qualified persons out of 95; the minimum level, between one and two. And agricultural work is seasonal: the nut harvest is concentrated in a few weeks, while the permitted peak is 25 hours for two weeks (Code 1, Art. 6, para. 5).\nCode 1 already provides for what happens if the hours are not enough: deferrable activities are reduced, documentation is simplified, recourse is had to volunteering and to mutualism between Circles (Art. 2, para. 8). And it indicates the quarterly Labour Balances as the basis for verifying the limit (Art. 2, para. 9). This draft is the count made beforehand; those Balances will be the count made afterwards."],[28,"48-the-assumptions-that-weigh-most","4.8 The assumptions that weigh most","","If it changes · Effect\nThree collective meals a day instead of one · +10,140 hours: scenario A goes from 7.2 to 9.3 hours a week of internal labour\nNo collective meal · −5,070 hours: 6.2 hours a week\nNuts halved · about −1,500 hours: 6.9 hours a week\nCereals, legumes and sunflower grown with 8 hours per hectare, as in German mechanised agriculture · −600 hours: almost all the field labour is in nuts, vegetables and fruit\nEnergy and water at the European average, without doubling · −1,860 hours: 6.8 hours a week\nInventory of manufactured goods tripled · complete count: 11.3 hours in scenario A, 14.5 in scenario B\n48 working weeks a year instead of 52 · the hours available fall by 8%"],[28,"5-robots-and-automation","5. Robots and automation","","The count in section 4 uses today’s machines. This section says how an automation is counted, where it is most needed and how much it can change the result. The rules in force are in Code 7, Article 6, which applies to every automation. Specific rules for robots are in Code 7, Article 6-bis (proposal 8, accepted on 19 October 2026)."],[28,"51-how-it-is-counted","5.1 How it is counted","","A robot is a means of production, so it enters the count like any other: the hours required to build it, plus energy and maintenance, spread over the years in which it works. It is worthwhile when the hours it saves are more than those it costs. Code 7 already requires that the time saving be documentable (Art. 6, para. 1); Article 6-bis asks for this comparison to be made before the machine is introduced.\nAn example: a machine costing 60,000 euros that lasts fifteen years costs 78 UCR a year, 157 if it is produced half where labour yields less. With fifty hours of maintenance that is between 130 and 210 hours a year. It is worthwhile if it saves more than that.\nThe saving does not go to whoever owns the machine, because no one owns it: the time freed remains with the Community, which decides how to use it (Code 7, Art. 6, para. 6). Article 6-bis of Code 7 confirms this rule for robots too."],[28,"52-where-it-is-needed-area-by-area","5.2 Where it is needed, area by area","","Area · Hours a year in scenario A · What machines can do · What remains for persons\nFields · 4,570 · Soil tillage, sowing, irrigation, mechanical weeding. Cereals, legumes and sunflower are already almost entirely mechanised. · The largest part of today’s labour: harvesting and tending nuts, vegetables and fruit. This is where robots would change the count most.\nFood processing · 1,800 · Mill, oil mill, kneading, washing, packaging · Control, recipes, cleaning of the plants\nMines, excavation and raw materials · outside the internal count: it lies in the manufactured goods · Excavation, transport and crushing in hazardous environments. It is the first place from which to remove persons (Code 7, Art. 2, para. 6). · Design, supervision, maintenance, remediation. Reuse and recycling come before extraction: today no text permits new extraction (Const. 14.2).\nConstruction and maintenance · 4,430 · Excavation, handling of loads, prefabrication, inspections at height · Design, finishing, custom repairs\nLogistics and Storehouse · 2,560 · Inventory, handling, part of the deliveries · Delivery to frail persons, relations with those who collect\nEnergy and water · 3,350 · Measurement, regulation, alarms: largely already the case · Repairs, decisions on priorities\nDocumentation · up to 3,560 · AI prepares minutes, registers and calculations · Every decision: AI proposes, it does not decide (Const. 19.6)\nCollective kitchen · 4,560 · Better equipment · Cooking and serving, in the prudent assumption of this count\nCare, health, learning, justice · 8,600 · Only tools operated by a person: hoists, aids, diagnostics · Everything else. The Code prohibits automating care, mediation, the validation of competences and guarantee decisions (Code 7, Art. 6, para. 2).\nRaw materials. A Community of 150 persons does not dig mines: metals and minerals come from outside, inside the manufactured goods. The Constitution prohibits any activity that causes irreversible damage to soil and water (Article 14.2): read literally, it prohibits any new mine. Two changes accepted on 19 October 2026 (proposals 6 and 7) qualified the subsoil as a common good and asked each Community for an inventory of what can be recovered from buildings, vehicles and landfills. Whether and when to permit extraction remains an open question."],[28,"53-how-much-the-count-can-change","5.3 How much the count can change","","In scenario A the labour that machines can lighten (fields, processing, energy and water, maintenance, logistics) is about 16,700 hours: just over half of the internal labour. Labour between persons (cooking, care, learning) is about 13,200 hours, and the project chooses not to automate it.\nIf machines remove · Hours a week per contributor · Counting that machines cost one fifth of what they save (assumption)\nnothing more than today · 7.2 · 7.2\na quarter of those hours · 6.3 · 6.5\nhalf · 5.3 · 5.7\nthree quarters · 4.4 · 5.0\nEvery ten per cent less is worth 23 minutes a week for each person."],[28,"54-outlook","5.4 Outlook","","1. There is a limit, and it is intended. Even automating everything that can be automated, labour between persons remains: between three and four hours a week. The project does not aim at zero hours: it aims to free persons from dangerous and repetitive labour.\n2. Machines are not free. They cost hours, energy and raw materials. The real limit is ecological before it is technical.\n3. Who gains is the difference from today. Today a machine that replaces labour takes income away from someone and gives it to whoever owns it. Here it takes the Basket away from no one and reduces everyone’s hours.\n4. No promises. The count of this draft holds with the machines that exist. Better robots would improve it; the project does not depend on them."],[28,"6-a-consequence-for-the-thresholds-of-code-3","6. A consequence for the thresholds of Code 3","","Until 19 October 2026 Article 7 of Code 3 distinguished operations of low value (below 500 UCR), medium value (from 500 to 2,500) and high value. With the proposed coefficient, 500 UCR correspond to about 25,500 euros of purchases and to 0.7% of the annual hours of a Community of 150 persons; 2,500 UCR to about 128,000 euros and to 3.4%. For a small Community the low threshold was too high: a purchase of 20,000 euros was decided by a Competence Circle. Proposal 11, accepted on 19 October 2026, linked the two thresholds to the size of the Community: 100 UCR plus one per person, and 500 plus five per person, with 500 and 2,500 as the maximum."],[28,"how-to-correct-it","How to correct it","","Anyone who has better data can change the assumption in the program, redo the count and open an issue, or write to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com. The most useful data are measured ones: actual hours of collective kitchens, vegetable gardens, workshops, existing communities."],[28,"sources","Sources","","Pages consulted on 5 October 2026.\nEurostat, population structure by age, EU 2024; tables by age (demopjanind) and by single year of age (demopjan)\nEurostat, hours worked and employment by economic activity, EU 2024 (nama10a10e); breakdown into 64 sectors (nama10a64e)\nEurostat, gross domestic product, EU 2024 (nama10gdp)\nEurostat, time use: household and family care (data 2008–2015)\nEurostat, pupils per teacher in the EU\nLazio Region, table of labour requirements per hectare and crop (2008)\nSardinia Region, labour requirements per hectare (2019)\nSicilian Region, tables of labour requirements (2024)\nSchleswig-Holstein Chamber of Agriculture, working hours per hectare in mechanised crops (2025)\nOur World in Data, crop yields based on FAO data\nWorld Bank, output per inhabitant in current dollars, 2024\nInternational Coffee Organization, Rwanda's Coffee Value Chain\nDrewry, World Container Index (21 May 2026)\nPonisio et al., yield gap of organic agriculture, Proceedings of the Royal Society B (2015)\nBotta and Valentini, shelled yield of hazelnuts (2017)\nFAO, oil yield of sunflower seeds\nSchool Nutrition Association, meals per labour hour in collective kitchens: 10–18, and 16–20 in school canteens (2009)\nOECD, Health at a Glance 2021: long-term care workers\nWHO, threshold of 4.45 health workers per 1,000 persons (working paper, 2016)\nTRSA, laundry productivity (2023); FMLink, Benchmarking laundry (2019): from 42 to 57 pounds, that is 19–26 kg, per hour\nMcElroy and O’Neill, The labour and resource use requirements of a good life for all, Global Environmental Change 92, 103008 (2025); preprint\nTwin Oaks, labour quota (Wikipedia); entry in the directory of intentional communities; East Wind, members; Acorn"],[29,"","","","Working translation. The Italian text is the authoritative one.\nCalibration draft 0.1, not adopted. Code 1 (Art. 46) requires each Community to have a public table indicating the supplements, the limits of the utility recognition, the cap, the decay and the cost in UCR of non-necessary goods and services. It is approved by the Assembly of Circle 1, within the parameters that the General Assembly calibrates every year (Code 3, Art. 14). This page proposes a model to start from and checks with a simulation whether the cap and the decay do what they are meant to do: prevent accumulation without emptying the recognition of meaning.\nThe answer, in brief. Yes. With the cap of 240 CdR the most active person cannot get ahead of someone who works a little over three hours a week beyond the Base Contribution, and the most active twentieth of the Community stays below the concentration threshold set by the Code. Without the cap it would exceed it by far. Two points remain to be clarified in the texts, set out at the end: the curve used here depends on one of them."],[29,"1-what-the-cdr-is-in-four-lines","1. What the CdR is, in four lines","","It is the recognition of the contribution rendered beyond the Base Contribution (CBO). It is not money: it is not transferred, lent or inherited, and it does not bear interest (Code 1, Art. 42).\nIt cannot condition the Basket, the vote, health care, learning or sortition (Art. 42, para. 3).\nIt is used for three things only: precedence for non-necessary and scarce goods and services, prolonged exclusive use of a common space, ordinary hospitality in the Bridge Houses (Art. 45, para. 1).\nPrecedence applies to at most half of the availability: the other half remains for those without CdR, on the waiting list. CdR that have been used are cancelled and pass to no one (Art. 45, paragraphs 3 and 4)."],[29,"2-how-it-accrues","2. How it accrues","","These rules are already in the texts: the table summarises them.\nActivity · CdR per hour · Location\nOrdinary CBO · 0 · Code 1, Art. 43, para. 4\nCivic functions of List D · 0 · Code 1, Art. 43, para. 4\nValidated activity beyond the CBO that produces goods or services for the Community · 1 · Code 1, Art. 43, paragraphs 1 and 2\nUnpleasant, arduous or night task performed within the CBO · the supplement only: from 0.3 to 1 · Code 1, Art. 10, para. 3\nThe same task performed beyond the CBO · from 1.3 to 2 · Code 1, Art. 10, para. 3\nTravel assignment · supplement within the same limits · Code 1, Art. 43, para. 2\nRestorative work · 1, unless otherwise agreed · Code 1, Art. 10, para. 4\nPossession of a competence, a qualification or an office · 0 · Code 1, Art. 42, para. 4"],[29,"3-supplements","3. Supplements","","The Code sets the range: from thirty to one hundred per cent (Art. 10, para. 2). The draft proposes three classes. The list of tasks in each class is drawn up by the Health Council and the Storehouse Council, with the approval of the Assembly (Art. 8, para. 1).\nClass · Proposed supplement · Examples\nDiscomfort · 30% · Sanitary cleaning, compost management, repetitive work\nHeavy work or night · 50% · Physically heavy work, shifts between 22 and 6 o’clock\nRisk, or several conditions together · 100% · Tasks with significant risk; heavy work performed at night\nThe percentages are drafting choices within the Code’s range."],[29,"4-utility-recognition","4. Utility recognition","","For works and services of particular common utility the Assembly may add a reasoned and published recognition, never exceeding the hours actually spent (Art. 43, para. 3). The table must set its limit.\nProposed limit · Value · Why\nFor each work or service · no more than half of the hours spent · Leaves room for recognition without doubling the value of the hour\nPer person, in one year · no more than 60 CdR · A quarter of the cap: no one reaches the cap through recognitions alone\nWho decides · the Assembly, with those who have benefited from the work · Const. 9.2\nThese are drafting choices. They serve to prevent recognition from rewarding popularity more than contribution."],[29,"5-cap-and-decay","5. Cap and decay","","Rule · Value · Location\nCap on the personal balance · 240 CdR on first application · Code 1, Art. 44, para. 2\nFull value · for twelve months from accrual · Code 1, Art. 44, para. 3\nThen · one quarter of the initial value less each quarter · Code 1, Art. 44, para. 3\nExtinction · at the end of the twenty-fourth month · Code 1, Art. 44, para. 3\nOn death or exit · the CdR are extinguished · Code 1, Art. 44, para. 5\nThe curve. The text says “one quarter each quarter” but does not say how. There are three possible readings.\nReading · Full value for · Extinction · Hours a week to reach the cap\nGradual: from the twelfth month it declines in proportion to time, one twelfth per month · 12 months · 24th month · 3.1\nThe whole quarter at the end of each quarter · 15 months · 24th month · 2.8\nThe whole quarter at the start of each quarter · 12 months · 21st month · 3.4\nOnly the first respects both sentences of the paragraph: twelve months of full value and extinction at the twenty-fourth. It is the one used in this draft and written into Code 1 with amendment proposal 9, accepted on 19 October 2026. The first version of this page used the second, more generous one.\nMonths from accrual · Remaining value\nup to 12 · 100%\n15 · 75%\n18 · 50%\n21 · 25%\n24 · zero\nWhat it means. Someone who accrues CdR regularly and never uses them keeps, at steady state, the equivalent of 18 months. The cap of 240 is therefore reached by accruing 13.3 CdR a month: 3.1 hours a week beyond the CBO. Beyond that threshold, working more does not increase the balance.\nOne CdR is worth one UCR, that is, one hour of work (Art. 45, para. 2). With the coefficient of the draft UCR Annex, 240 CdR correspond to about 12,000 euros of goods bought outside, or to sixteen weeks of Base Contribution."],[29,"6-what-non-necessary-goods-and-services-cost-in-cdr","6. What non-necessary goods and services cost in CdR","","One CdR used is worth one UCR (Art. 45, para. 2), that is, one hour of work. The cost of each good is its real cost: the hours of those who produce it, the share of the means of production and transport, calculated with the method of the draft UCR Annex. One rule applies: an hour is worth an hour, whoever works it and wherever.\nFor what the Community produces itself, the hours, materials and energy are counted.\nFor what is bought outside, the actual hours are used, where they are known; otherwise the price multiplied by the hours of work per euro of the country of production.\nThe values are examples to show orders of magnitude: prices and shares produced abroad are assumptions. Each Community draws up its own table. The last column shows what the price alone, converted at the European average, would give: the difference is real work that the price does not reveal.\nGood or service · How it is calculated · CdR · With the European average alone\nOne kilo of coffee (15 euros) · plantation hours in Rwanda, plus 13 euros of processing and transport in Europe · 3.2 · 0.3\nOne kilo of chocolate (12 euros) · one fifth of the price in Côte d’Ivoire, the rest in Europe · 0.9 · 0.2\nA book printed in Europe (18 euros) · price times European coefficient · 0.4 · 0.4\nEuropean materials for a personal work or project (100 euros) · price times European coefficient · 2.0 · 2.0\nA musical instrument made in Europe (400 euros) · price times European coefficient · 7.8 · 7.8"],[29,"6-what-non-necessary-goods-and-services-cost-in-cdr","6. What non-necessary goods and services cost in CdR","","The same instrument, half made in China · half the price times Chinese coefficient · 16.7 · 7.8\nAn appliance beyond the basic allocation (500 euros more) · half the price in China · 20.9 · 9.8\nA camera (600 euros) · half the price in China · 25.0 · 11.8\nA touring bicycle made in Europe (1,200 euros) · price times European coefficient · 23.5 · 23.5\nThe same bicycle, half made in China · half the price times Chinese coefficient · 50.1 · 23.5\nA 1,000 km train journey · 10 cents per km, European coefficient · 2.0 · 2.0\nA 5,000 km train journey · as above · 9.8 · 9.8\nOne night of ordinary hospitality in a Bridge House · share of 15 m² plus half an hour of management and cleaning · 0.6\nFourteen nights in a Bridge House · as above · 8.0\nExclusive use of a common space of 20 m² for three months · 1.66 UCR per m² per year: materials and maintenance · 8.3\nThe same space for one year · as above · 33.2\nFour things to bear in mind when reading the table.\nThe CdR gives precedence, not a purchase. The same goods remain accessible without CdR, by booking or waiting list, and precedence applies to at most half of the availability (Art. 45, para. 4).\nOne CdR is one hour. A kilo of coffee is worth more than three hours of voluntary work, because it contains three hours of work; a musical instrument made in Europe, eight."],[29,"6-what-non-necessary-goods-and-services-cost-in-cdr","6. What non-necessary goods and services cost in CdR","","What requires more work costs more, not what comes from far away. Coffee is worth three hours because it is picked by hand, with few means of production. Distance weighs only through transport, which by sea is a small matter: about 20 cents per kilo from China to Italy, less than a hundredth of a CdR. The same good produced with better tools would cost fewer hours, wherever it was produced.\nNo variable prices. The cost is the reproduction cost, the same for everyone: auctions, markets and exchanges of CdR between persons are prohibited (Art. 45, para. 5)."],[29,"is-the-cap-of-240-right-or-low","Is the cap of 240 right or low?","","Compared with costs it is not low: the most expensive item in the table is worth 50 CdR, the cap is almost five times as much. And the cap limits how much can be set aside, not how much can be accrued: those who use their CdR accrue more without limit.\nAs regards concentration it is close to the maximum that holds. In the Community simulated in section 7:\nCap · Reached by working beyond the CBO · Share of CdR of the most active 5%\n120 · 1.5 hours a week · 13%\n180 · 2.3 hours a week · 15%\n240 · 3.1 hours a week · 17%\n300 · 3.8 hours a week · 20%: beyond the Code’s threshold\n360 · 4.6 hours a week · 24%: beyond the Code’s threshold\n480 · 6.2 hours a week · 29%: beyond the Code’s threshold\nThe Code requires the cap to be reviewed when 5% of persons hold more than 20% of the CdR (Art. 46, para. 3). In this simulation, at 300 the threshold is just exceeded. A lower cap, for example 120, would make balances more equal but would be reached with less than an hour and a half a week. 240 is a reasonable choice: there is no reason to raise it."],[29,"7-simulation","7. Simulation","","It can be rerun and modified with the program strumenti/simulacdr.py, published in the repository.\nA person who never uses his or her CdR.\nHours a week beyond the CBO · Balance after one year · After two years · At steady state · Reaches the cap\n0.5 · 26 · 39 · 39 · never\n1 · 52 · 78 · 78 · never\n2 · 104 · 156 · 156 · never\n3 · 156 · 234 · 234 · never\n5 · 240 · 240 · 240 · after 12 months\n8 · 240 · 240 · 240 · after 7 months\nA Community of 95 contributors, after five years, if no one uses CdR. Assumptions: half of the persons do not work beyond the CBO, 30% one hour a week, 15% three hours, 5% eight hours.\nWith the cap · Without the cap\nExisting CdR · 6,700 · 8,520\nBalance of the most active 5% · 240 each · 624 each\nShare held by the most active 5% · 17% · 35%\nThe Code requires the table and the cap to be reviewed if more than one fifth of the CdR is held by fewer than one twentieth of the persons (Art. 46, para. 3). With the cap the simulated Community stays below the threshold; without it, it exceeds it by far. This is the worst case: those who use CdR lower their own balance.\nSupplements within the CBO.\nShare of the 15 hours in tasks with a supplement · Supplement · CdR per month · Balance at steady state · Reaches the cap\none fifth · 30% · 3.9 · 70 · never\none fifth · 50% · 6.5 · 117 · never\none fifth · 100% · 13.0 · 234 · never\nall · 50% · 32.5 · 240 · after 8 months\nall · 100% · 65.0 · 240 · after 4 months"],[29,"8-what-can-be-drawn-from-it","8. What can be drawn from it","","1. The cap does its job. It stops accumulation at a level that a person reaches with a little over three hours a week: those who work much more do not obtain more power of precedence than others.\n2. Decay does not empty the recognition of meaning. A CdR keeps its full value for twelve months and fades out within two years: there is time to use it, not to hoard it.\n3. Those who perform many unpleasant tasks soon reach the cap. From that moment the supplement is no longer recognised for them. The Code prevents this through sortition and the rebalancing of unpleasant tasks (Art. 8, para. 3): the table works only if that rotation really works.\n4. The value of the cap is defensible, not proven. 240 CdR are sixteen weeks of Base Contribution. It must be reviewed with the quarterly data that the Code requires to be published (Art. 46, para. 2)."],[29,"9-two-points-to-be-clarified-in-the-texts","9. Two points to be clarified in the texts","","They are not corrected here: they have become two amendment proposals, 9 and 10, accepted on 19 October 2026.\n1. How the value declines. Article 44, paragraph 3, says that CdR retain their value for twelve months, then lose one quarter per quarter and are extinguished “at the end of the twenty-fourth month”. It does not say whether the quarter is lost all at once or a little at a time. Proposal 9 chose gradual reduction, in proportion to time, the only one that respects both sentences, and added two missing rules: the oldest CdR are used first, and each CdR counts for the value it has at that moment.\n2. What happens to the supplement of someone at the cap. Article 44, paragraph 2, says that once the cap has been reached further activities do not give rise to CdR. Does this also apply to the supplement for unpleasant tasks performed within the CBO? The text does not say. Proposal 10 confirmed that it does, and defined how the balance is measured."],[29,"how-to-correct-it","How to correct it","","Anyone who has data or a criticism may change the assumptions in the program, rerun the simulation and open an issue, or write to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com."],[30,"","","","Working translation. The Italian text is the authoritative one.\nThis page collects the eleven change proposals published between 5 and 19 October 2026. They were all accepted on 19 October 2026 and their text is now in the Codes; the entries are in the register of changes. The page remains as an archive, because for each change it explains the problem, the reason, the cost and the points still open.\nHow the rules change now. From 19 October 2026 changes no longer go through a period of discussion: when a correction has been verified it is applied directly and recorded with the date, the text and the reason. Anyone can report a problem or propose a change, as explained in How to contribute, and any change can be withdrawn if it proves wrong.\nThe first version of the proposals, of 5 October 2026, had been put through an adversarial review: none had held up as it was. Version 2, of 6 October, rewrote them. Proposal 2 was rewritten again on 12 and 19 October (versions 3 and 4). The final version was checked with the coherence review of 19 October (in Italian).\nThe first four proposals arise from the draft Annex on the Sufficiency Basket. The fifth, ninth and tenth concern the Recognition Contribution. The sixth and seventh concern the subsoil and raw materials. The eighth concerns robots. The eleventh concerns the economic thresholds for decisions."],[30,"what-applies-to-all-of-them","What applies to all of them","","Three things that the first version did not say.\nWhat enters the Basket rises in priority. When time is not enough, the distribution of the goods of the Basket comes before the care of frail persons and the maintenance of water and energy (Code 1, Art. 32). For this reason proposal 1 adds a rule to that Article: the goods of the Basket indicated as deferrable come after. It applies to clothing, to hygiene products that are not needed for health and to the devices of proposal 3. It does not apply to the two values of proposal 4, hot water and heating, which remain in the minimum share.\nA good of the Basket bought outside is a critical dependency. It requires an Exit Plan with a definite time limit (Code 15, Art. 12). Deferrable goods do not, however, enter the “levels 1 and 2” on which Code 16 measures self-production of seventy per cent (Art. 1, letter f, and Art. 4): they do not slow down the changes from Phase 0 to Phase 1 and from Phase 1 to Phase 2. They do, however, weigh on the objectives of Phase 3 and on the change to Phase 4, which require overcoming half of the critical dependencies and an Exit Plan with a definite time limit for each of those remaining (Code 16, Art. 3, para. 4, letter c, and Art. 4, para. 4, letter b).\nAdministrative time is not estimated. Code 3 (Art. 12-bis, para. 13) requires every new rule to state how much administrative time it requires, and limits it to one tenth of the Base Contribution. No proposal has this estimate yet: it is indicated where it weighs most."],[30,"proposal-1--clothing-and-hygiene-in-the-material-basket","Proposal 1 — Clothing and hygiene in the Material Basket","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (C.1-Annex A, Art. 4, paras. 1 and 3, and new para. 1-bis; Code 1, Art. 32, new para. 1-bis).\nText and article: C.1-Annex A, Art. 4, paragraphs 1 and 3, and new paragraph 1-bis; Code 1, Art. 32, new paragraph 1-bis.\nWhat changes from version 1. Added: personal ownership of garments, the limit on single-use products and on materials of animal origin, the confidentiality of withdrawals, the venue (the Textile Storehouse and the Storehouse Council), the priority. Removed “suited to the tasks performed”, which overlapped with protective equipment.\nProblem. The Material Basket names linen, crockery, furniture and tools, but not clothes or hygiene products. No other Basket names them.\nProposed text. In C.1-Annex A, in Article 4, paragraph 1, after letter g), the following are added:\nh) clothing and footwear suited to the climate and the season;\ni) personal hygiene products, including menstrual products and those for early childhood and for incontinence.\nAfter paragraph 1 the following is inserted:\n1-bis. The following rules apply to the goods of letters h) and i):\na) by way of derogation from Article 1, paragraph 4, from delivery they are personal-use goods pursuant to Article 10.3 of the Constitution;"],[30,"proposal-1--clothing-and-hygiene-in-the-material-basket","Proposal 1 — Clothing and hygiene in the Material Basket","","b) Article 4 of Code 2 on the precedence of reuse and Articles 13.5 and 14.1 of the Constitution apply: washable or compostable products are preferred, and single-use products are permitted only under the conditions of Article 14.1;\nc) they are procured and distributed through the Textile and Housing Storehouse provided for by Article 14 of Code 1 and are included in the annual Technical Annex of Article 18, paragraph 2, of that Code;\nd) data on the withdrawal of the products of letter i) are neither published nor communicated in a form that identifies the person;\ne) they are deferrable within the meaning of Article 32, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 1, except for the products that the Health Council indicates as necessary for health; in the order of paragraph 5 they follow letter b).\nIn paragraph 3, after the words “in days of autonomy and safety margin” the words “; for the goods of letter h) of paragraph 1, as an allocation per person per year” are added. The words “for PPE and for goods intended for frail persons” become “for PPE, for hygiene products and for goods intended for frail persons”. At the end, the following is added: “For letters h) and i) of paragraph 1 the proposal lies with the Storehouse Council.”\nIn Code 1, in Article 32, after paragraph 1 the following is inserted:\n1-bis. The goods of the Basket that the Code or the Annex providing for them indicates as deferrable come after letter e) of paragraph 1."],[30,"proposal-1--clothing-and-hygiene-in-the-material-basket","Proposal 1 — Clothing and hygiene in the Material Basket","","Reason. These are goods everyone needs. The Constitution guarantees material subsistence “unconditionally” (Art. 1.3), but lists it as food, water and energy (Art. 1.2): without a rule, clothes and hygiene depend on the Common Fund. The study by Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020) on a decent life counts them among basic needs: 4 kg of new garments per person per year. They are, however, goods that can wait a few days: for this reason they do not go ahead of care and maintenance.\nOther points to align. Versions: C.1-Annex A from V1.3 to V1.4; Code 1 from V1.5 to V1.6, together with the other proposals on Code 1 that are accepted.\nWhat it costs. In the first balance of hours, clothing and hygiene products are worth about 940 hours a year for 150 persons, if bought in Europe: eleven minutes a week per contributor. At the start they are almost all external purchases: critical dependencies, with an Exit Plan. Every product category requires a Technical Validation Sheet (Code 2, Art. 3).\nOpen points. Annex A is activated only in Phase 1 (Code 16, Art. 3): in Phase 0 these goods remain a charge on the Common Fund (Code 15, Art. 17, para. 3). For some products, such as nappies, a washable or compostable alternative exists; for others it has to be verified."],[30,"proposal-2--standard-of-adequate-dwelling","Proposal 2 — Standard of adequate dwelling","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 1, new Art. 47-bis).\nText and article: Code 1, new Art. 47-bis.\nWhat changes from version 1. It now says what counts as “adequate” for the other rules and sets a time limit for upgrading. Added: a minimum private space, space for minors from fourteen years of age, the prohibition on falling below the first values, compliance with the prohibition on land take, a rule for the early phases. For temperature it refers to Code 8. Removed the reference to an “emergency accommodation” that the texts do not provide for.\nWhat changes in version 3 (12 October 2026). Fifteen square metres per person gave anyone living alone or as a couple a dwelling too small to live in. Now every dwelling has at least a living room, a bedroom and a bathroom, and the minimum floor area depends on how many people live in it: thirty square metres for one, forty-five for two, sixty for three. The private space rises from eight to nine square metres."],[30,"proposal-2--standard-of-adequate-dwelling","Proposal 2 — Standard of adequate dwelling","","What changes in version 4 (19 October 2026). The coherence review of 19 October found two defects. Letter d) of paragraph 1 allowed a shared bathroom within Circle 0, while paragraph 2 required a bathroom in every dwelling. And paragraph 2 used “adequate” in a sense different from paragraph 5. Now bathrooms are counted on persons: at least one for every four, which may be shared among the dwellings of the same Circle 0, and beyond the first a WC and washbasin are enough. A studio flat “does not meet the standard”, instead of “is not adequate”. In the first phases the minimum composition and the number of bathrooms also apply.\nProblem. Housing is a constitutional right (Const. 1.2, letter a). Code 1 speaks of “adequate dwelling” (Art. 47, para. 3) without saying what makes it so.\nProposed text. After Article 47 the following is inserted:\nArt. 47-bis — Adequate dwelling\n1. The dwelling provided for by Article 1.2, letter a), of the Constitution is adequate when it ensures at least:\na) a private space that can be closed for every person from fourteen years of age; two persons share it only if both request it;\nb) protection from cold, heat and damp sufficient not to harm health;\nc) access to the Water Basket and to the Energy Basket provided for by Code 8;\nd) spaces for cooking and for hygiene, own or shared within Circle 0;\ne) the accessibility the person needs, in accordance with his or her individual care plan provided for by Code 12."],[30,"proposal-2--standard-of-adequate-dwelling","Proposal 2 — Standard of adequate dwelling","","2. The quantitative standards are determined by the Assembly on a joint proposal from the Storehouse Council and the Energy and Water Council, with the opinion of the Health Council and of the Habitat and Territory Council. On first application every dwelling comprises at least a living room and a separate bedroom; a studio flat does not meet the standard. Each Circle 0 has at least one bathroom for every four persons or fraction thereof; beyond the first, bathrooms may be facilities with a WC and washbasin. Bathrooms may be shared among the dwellings of the same Circle 0. When a Circle 0 lives in more than one dwelling, the floor area is calculated on the total number of its persons. The habitable floor area is not less than thirty square metres for one person, forty-five for two and sixty for three; it increases by fifteen square metres for each additional person up to the fifth and by ten for each person beyond the fifth. Each private space provided for by letter a) of paragraph 1 is not less than nine square metres. Subsequent determinations may not fall below the first-application values without the favourable opinion of the Health Council.\n3. The standards of paragraph 2 do not apply to the equipped stopping areas provided for by Article 6-ter, paragraph 6, of Code 11."],[30,"proposal-2--standard-of-adequate-dwelling","Proposal 2 — Standard of adequate dwelling","","4. A dwelling that does not comply with paragraph 1 or with the standards of paragraph 2 may not for that reason be revoked or refused. The Community includes its upgrading in housing planning, with a time limit not exceeding thirty-six months, which may be extended once only by a reasoned resolution of the Assembly. An appeal to the Guarantee Circle lies against failure to upgrade. As regards temperature, Articles 4 and 10 of Code 8 remain in force.\n5. For the purposes of Article 47, paragraph 3, of this Code and of Articles 29 and 34 of Code 4, a dwelling that complies with paragraph 1 is adequate. If none is available, the best available dwelling is assigned, and its upgrading takes precedence in planning.\n6. Upgrading takes place in compliance with Articles 2 and 5 of Code 10: the reuse and recovery of buildings precede any new construction. Until Codes 8 and 12 are activated, letters a), b) and d) of paragraph 1 and, of paragraph 2, the floor area, the minimum composition and the number of bathrooms apply."],[30,"proposal-2--standard-of-adequate-dwelling","Proposal 2 — Standard of adequate dwelling","","Reason. Version 2 used fifteen square metres per person, from the study by Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020): it is a minimum conceived for the whole world population, and for one or two people it falls even below the ordinary minimums of the Italian rule on habitability (Ministerial Decree of 5 July 1975: a studio flat must have at least twenty-eight square metres for one person and thirty-eight for two; a dwelling with bedrooms, fourteen square metres for each of the first four occupants and a living room of at least fourteen; since 2024 the “Salva casa” decree allows, in existing buildings and under certain conditions, studio flats of twenty and twenty-eight square metres). Version 3 starts from what is needed to live there: a place to be, a place to sleep behind a closed door, a bathroom. The measurements are drafting choices, to be discussed; beyond the fifth person they grow more slowly, because kitchen and living room do not multiply with the number of people. The nine metres of private space are the minimum single bedroom of the same rule. The thirty-six months are a drafting assumption. Paragraph 4 prevents a standard conceived as a guarantee from becoming a ground for eviction; paragraph 5 prevents the protective measures of Code 4 from remaining unenforceable where no compliant dwelling exists. One bathroom for every four persons is the standard of a family home: beyond four persons a single one is not enough at peak times. For bathrooms beyond the first, a WC and washbasin are enough, because the queue is for the WC more than for the shower, and each extra bathroom costs materials, water and maintenance."],[30,"proposal-2--standard-of-adequate-dwelling","Proposal 2 — Standard of adequate dwelling","","Other points to align. With the new Article, “adequate dwelling” acquires a content in Art. 47, para. 3, of Code 1 and in Arts. 29 and 34 of Code 4. Minimum temperature is a matter for Code 8, which today sets no value: proposal 4 sets it. Version: Code 1 from V1.5 to V1.6, together with the other proposals on Code 1 that are accepted.\nWhat it costs. In Communities that start from existing buildings, upgrading and insulation works, not yet counted in the balance of hours. Where space is lacking and building is not possible, the extension in paragraph 4 is not enough: this has to be said beforehand. With the new measurements the average floor area rises from fifteen to about twenty-two square metres per person, if households are like Italian ones today (assumption): in the draft UCR Annex the materials for housing go from about 550 to about 740 hours a year for 150 people, less than a tenth of an hour a week per contributor. The extra bathrooms for Circles 0 from the fifth person on are plumbing works, not yet counted.\nOpen points. It is not stated whether the standards apply to the Bridge Houses. Verifying the floor area requires entering dwellings: this has to be done without violating the inviolability of Circle 0 (Const. 5.1). It is not stated how to treat buildings made only of studio flats: joining them requires works that paragraph 4 leaves to planning."],[30,"proposal-3--connectivity-and-learning-materials","Proposal 3 — Connectivity and learning materials","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 7, Art. 3, new para. 9; Code 5, Art. 2, new para. 4-bis).\nText and article: Code 7, Art. 3, new paragraph 9; Code 5, Art. 2, new paragraph 4-bis.\nWhat changes from version 1. The personal device is now distinct from the Node, which under the Constitution is offline. The age goes from ten to twelve years, as in Code 5. Devices must comply with the prohibitions of Code 2; if no lawful device exists, shared access is sufficient. Removed the computer for every Nucleus, because every person already has a Node. The materials guaranteed are only those for literacy and safety. The paragraph of Code 7 is numbered 9 and not 6-bis, so as not to be confused with Article 6-bis of proposal 8.\nProblem. Connectivity is a right (Const. 1.2, letter c), but Code 7 does not say with what it is exercised. For education Code 5 sets the contents (Art. 6, para. 8), not the means.\nProposed text. In Code 7, in Article 3, after paragraph 8 the following is added:\n9. Minimum allocation. The access to the Network provided for by paragraph 6 is part of the Sufficiency Basket and may not be made conditional on the CBO or on CdR. It comprises at least:\na) a Network access point in every dwelling that requests it or, where this is not technically possible, in Circle 0;"],[30,"proposal-3--connectivity-and-learning-materials","Proposal 3 — Connectivity and learning materials","","b) for every member who requests it from twelve years of age, a personal communication device, distinct from the Node of Article 5-bis; before that age, with the participation of the reference figures;\nc) in every Circle 1, an assisted access point with shared devices.\nPersonal devices comply with Article 5 of Code 2; if no compliant device is available, access is ensured by letter c). For shared devices the residual dependency of Article 5, paragraph 2, letter b), of Code 2 is permitted, with a substitution plan. Devices are supplied and repaired through the Storehouses, with the support of the Network Council. The right to disconnection and the non-digital procedures of Article 5-bis, paragraphs 7 and 8, remain in force. The allocations are proposed by the Network Council and are included in the annual Technical Annex of Article 18, paragraph 2, of Code 1. The allocations of letters a) and b) are deferrable within the meaning of Article 32, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 1.\nIn Code 5, in Article 2, after paragraph 4 the following is inserted:\n4-bis. The materials, tools and spaces necessary for the literacy provided for by Article 6, paragraph 8, and for the training necessary for safety form part of the Sufficiency Basket: access may not be made conditional on the CBO or on CdR. For other pathways, materials are supplied according to the availability of the Storehouses."],[30,"proposal-3--connectivity-and-learning-materials","Proposal 3 — Connectivity and learning materials","","Reason. The study by Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020) counts one telephone per person among the needs of a decent life. The personal Node remains what the Constitution wants: an offline computing tool, owned by the person. The communication device is something else, and must be subject to the prohibition of proprietary dependencies.\nOther points to align. Paragraph 9 refers to Article 32, paragraph 1-bis, of Code 1, which is in proposal 1: if that proposal is not accepted, paragraph 1-bis is inserted with this one. Versions: Code 7 from V1.6 to V1.7, together with proposal 8; Code 5 from V1.3 to V1.4.\nWhat it costs. In the balance of hours, telephones are worth from 120 to 260 hours a year for 150 persons, depending on where they are produced. They are components that a Community does not produce: a critical dependency, with an Exit Plan. With the telephones on sale today, compliance with Code 2 could leave only shared access for years: this is an intended limit.\nOpen points. The proposal concerns the federated Network. It does not say whether access to the internet is included, which for Code 2 is an external service: this has to be decided."],[30,"proposal-4--first-application-value-of-the-energy-basket","Proposal 4 — First-application value of the Energy Basket","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 8, Art. 4, para. 4).\nText and article: Code 8, Art. 4, paragraph 4.\nWhat changes from version 1. The list of services has been removed: it repeated paragraph 1 and forgot part of it (cooling, Health Storehouse, Network node), so that it looked like a step backwards. Only the two new values remain. Temperature is set here and no longer in Code 1. It is stated who decides when the lack of eighteen degrees is an emergency.\nProblem. The Water Basket has a first-application value (50 litres). The Energy Basket lists the services guaranteed (Art. 4, para. 1) but gives no value: until the Assembly decides, it is not known how much hot water and how much heating are “minimum”.\nProposed text. At the end of paragraph 4 the following is added:\nWithout prejudice to all the services of paragraph 1, on first application domestic hot water is not less than twenty litres per person per day and minimum winter heating ensures in the inhabited rooms a temperature of not less than eighteen degrees Celsius. On a proposal from the Health Council, the Assembly may set a higher value for age groups. A temperature that the Assembly, on a proposal from the Health Council, sets as dangerous to health constitutes an emergency within the meaning of paragraph 3; until that resolution, the value indicated by the Health Council applies. Article 2, paragraph 2, applies: reducing the requirement precedes increasing production.\nReason. The twenty litres of hot water come from the study by Millward-Hopkins et al. (2020); the eighteen degrees from the WHO Housing and health guidelines (2018). The kilowatt-hours depend on the climate and the building: they remain to be calculated in the Energy Basket STV (paragraph 6).\nOther points to align. Proposal 2 refers to Code 8 for temperature. Version: Code 8 from V1.3 to V1.4.\nWhat it costs. In cold climates it is the heaviest item: heating. The two values enter the minimum share, which Code 8 places at the first level of priority (Art. 2, para. 3) and requires to be guaranteed within six months (Art. 10), admitting on a transitional basis even fossil sources for vital functions (Art. 5, para. 3). For this reason the text recalls insulation before production. Where buildings cannot hold eighteen degrees, six months may not be enough: this has to be said beforehand.\nOpen points. The temperature “dangerous to health” has no first-application value: the Health Council proposes it and the Assembly sets it; until then, the value indicated by the Health Council applies."],[30,"proposal-5--cdr-for-cultural-activities-offered-to-the-community","Proposal 5 — CdR for cultural activities offered to the Community","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 1, Art. 43, new para. 1-bis).\nText and article: Code 1, Art. 43, new paragraph 1-bis.\nWhat changes from version 1. It has been narrowed. Only activities performed before the Community remain, and only those who perform or lead them accrue CdR. Removed workshops, which are teaching and therefore already Base Contribution (Code 5, Art. 2, para. 3). Removed free research, which remains unrecognised. Removed works, which remain the property of those who make them. Once the activity is validated, only the hours count. The additional recognition by the Assembly is excluded. There is an annual limit. In place of the general prohibition for “affective, spiritual and contemplative” activities there is a more precise exclusion: worship and what is reserved to a part of the members.\nProblem. The Constitution (Art. 7.2) excludes cultural, artistic and recreational activities from the Base Contribution: no one can be obliged to do them. It does not say whether the Community may recognise them with CdR when they are offered to everyone. Article 43, paragraph 1, speaks of “services for the Community” without naming them.\nProposed text. In Article 43, after paragraph 1 the following is inserted:"],[30,"proposal-5--cdr-for-cultural-activities-offered-to-the-community","Proposal 5 — CdR for cultural activities offered to the Community","","1-bis. The services for the Community referred to in paragraph 1 include cultural, artistic and recreational activities offered in the presence of the Community and open to all its members, such as performances, concerts, readings and celebrations. The following rules apply:\na) CdR accrue to those who perform or lead the activity, not to those who attend or take part in it;\nb) the hours performed in presence are counted and, for preparation, no more than the same number again;\nc) validation takes place pursuant to Article 9.2 of the Constitution; those who use the service are represented by those who attended. If it is positive, the CdR depend solely on the hours. If it is negative, the suspension provided for by Article 9, paragraph 4, of Code 2 does not apply;\nd) paragraph 3 does not apply;\ne) the public table sets an annual limit per person; on first application, sixty CdR;\nf) activities of worship, activities reserved to a part of the members and time already recognised as CBO pursuant to Article 2, paragraph 3, of Code 5 are excluded.\nReason. What a person offers to everyone is a contribution, and Article 8.1 of the Constitution recognises it. But recognition must not become a ranking of artists: for this reason, once the activity is validated, only the hours count, within a limit. Worship stays outside as a choice of secularism: the Community does not decide which faith to recognise (see Const. 25.2)."],[30,"proposal-5--cdr-for-cultural-activities-offered-to-the-community","Proposal 5 — CdR for cultural activities offered to the Community","","Other points to align. The Constitution does not change. Version: Code 1 from V1.5 to V1.6, together with the other proposals on Code 1 that are accepted.\nWhat it costs. Every event becomes a record to be proposed and validated: administrative time, not estimated. The simulation of the cap assumes that half of the persons do not accrue CdR: with this proposal the assumption has to be revised. CdR also serve to call volunteers in times of need (Code 1, Art. 6, para. 6): the annual limit prevents pleasant activities from weakening that call.\nOpen points. The one-to-one ratio between preparation and presence and the sixty CdR are drafting assumptions. None of the sixteen Competence Circles concerns the arts: it remains to be said which Council proposes the award, who brings the relevant competence to the validation and who distinguishes a cultural activity from one of worship."],[30,"proposal-6--subsoil-as-a-common-good","Proposal 6 — Subsoil as a common good","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 10, Art. 1, paras. 1, 2 and 3; Art. 9, para. 1, letter a)).\nText and article: Code 10, Art. 1, paragraphs 1, 2 and 3; Art. 9, paragraph 1, letter a).\nWhat changes from version 1. The first version amended the Constitution in two places. Both amendments are withdrawn.\nArt. 3.1. Article 37.4 removes the regime of the Commons from “any revision procedure”, without distinguishing between extending and reducing. There is no need to touch it: Article 3.1 itself already includes “every other good classified as common by the Constitution or by the Federative Codes”. The subsoil is classified in Code 10.\nArt. 14.2. The exception that admitted extraction “on an exceptional and temporary basis” is not proposed again. The review showed that it contradicted the environmental non-regression written in the same paragraph, that it left “the site” without limits and that it clashed with the prohibition of landfill (Art. 14.5). The question remains open: see at the end.\nProblem. Article 3.1 of the Constitution lists among the Commons land, water, air and energy sources. No text names the subsoil and deposits.\nProposed text. In Article 1, paragraph 1, the following letter is added:\nh) the subsoil, deposits and the inventory of recoverable materials.\nIn Article 1, paragraph 2, the following letters are added:"],[30,"proposal-6--subsoil-as-a-common-good","Proposal 6 — Subsoil as a common good","","f) mineral resources: means metals, minerals and rocks in their natural state, underground or outcropping, and those contained in landfills and in waste deposits from past extraction, until they are recovered; they do not include water, which is governed by Code 8;\ng) deposit: means a concentration of mineral resources that has been ascertained and entered in the Land Register.\nIn Article 1, paragraph 3, the following letter is added:\nf) the subsoil and mineral resources. This qualification does not restrict the meaning of the words “land” and “soil” used by the Constitution.\nIn Article 9, paragraph 1, letter a) becomes:\na) generate rent from common fertile land, from the subsoil, from mineral resources, from ecological corridors or from buffer zones;\nReason. Whoever lives above a deposit does not own it and cannot draw rent from it: this is the rule that already applies to fertile land. Writing it now, before anyone needs it, costs little and avoids a dispute.\nOther points to align. Letter h) of Article 1, paragraph 1, names the inventory, which is governed by Article 8-bis of proposal 7: if that proposal is not accepted, the words “and the inventory of recoverable materials” are not inserted. Version: Code 10 from V1.3 to V1.4, together with proposal 7. The Constitution remains at V.29.\nWhat it costs. Nothing in hours."],[30,"proposal-6--subsoil-as-a-common-good","Proposal 6 — Subsoil as a common good","","Open point: extraction. Article 14.2 of the Constitution prohibits “any activity that causes irreversible damage” to soil and water. A new quarry or mine almost always causes it. But panels, networks and tools are made of metals: whoever does not extract buys from whoever extracts. The Confederation will have to decide whether to keep the prohibition or admit an exception. It cannot do so now, for three reasons that emerged from the review: until the close of Phase 2, acts that exceed the ordinary maintenance of territorial goods are ineffective (Code 16, Art. 6, para. 4); the sanctuaries and the Biodiversity Council exist only from Phase 2; as long as no Circle 2 exists, the functions of the General Assembly are exercised by the Assembly of the Community itself (Code 3, Art. 14, para. 10). A future proposal will have to answer all the objections listed in the review."],[30,"proposal-7--raw-materials-recovery-and-inventory","Proposal 7 — Raw materials: recovery and inventory","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 10, new Art. 8-bis and Arts. 3 and 7; Code 15, Art. 12, new para. 5-bis).\nText and article: Code 10, new Art. 8-bis; Code 15, Art. 12, new paragraph 5-bis.\nWhat changes from version 1. The part on extraction is withdrawn, together with the constitutional exception of proposal 6. What remains is what applies in any case: the inventory of what can be recovered, the custody of deposits, the guarantees for recovery excavations. The Article states expressly that it does not authorise new extraction. The rules on reuse and purchases are not repeated: they already exist in Code 2, in Code 15 and in Annex A. The limit on Exit Plans is written in Code 15, which is where it belongs.\nProblem. No Code says where, in the territory, the materials that can be recovered are located, nor who has custody of a deposit. And the excavation of an old landfill or of a deposit of mining waste can pollute as much as a mine, but today it has no guarantees of its own.\nProposed text. In Code 10, after Article 8 the following is inserted:\nArt. 8-bis — Raw materials: recovery and inventory"],[30,"proposal-7--raw-materials-recovery-and-inventory","Proposal 7 — Raw materials: recovery and inventory","","1. The Habitat and Territory Council keeps in the Land Register, and updates every year, the inventory of recoverable materials present in the territory: disused buildings, vehicles and installations, landfills, waste from past extraction and materials deriving from the conversion provided for by Article 16.2 of the Constitution. The inventory is made available to the Production Council for the purposes of Articles 2 and 5 of C.1-Annex A.\n2. Deposits are Commons entrusted to the custody of the Community in whose territory they are located. Custody gives no entitlement to rent.\n3. The excavation of a landfill or of a deposit of waste from past extraction, in order to recover its materials, requires jointly, without prejudice to the matrix of Article 7 of Code 3:\na) the mandatory opinions of the Habitat and Territory, Biodiversity, Energy and Water and Health Councils;\nb) a General Safety Sheet pursuant to Code 14;\nc) a restoration plan approved before the start, whose hours are counted together with those of the recovery, and a plan for the non-recoverable part in conformity with Article 14.5 of the Constitution;\nd) the resolution of the Assembly of the Community in whose territory the site is located; if the excavation may affect waters or ecological corridors common to several Communities, the General Assembly decides."],[30,"proposal-7--raw-materials-recovery-and-inventory","Proposal 7 — Raw materials: recovery and inventory","","4. The excavation of paragraph 3 is prohibited in ecological sanctuaries and in areas that are candidates to become such, in wetlands and in strategic infiltration areas. It is not permitted before Codes 13 and 14 are activated. Excavation in conformity with paragraph 3 is use in conformity with a Sheet in force for the purposes of Article 6, paragraph 4, of Code 16. This Code and Code 13 remain in force.\n5. This Article does not authorise new extraction or the extension of existing extraction. Article 14.2 of the Constitution remains in force.\nIn Code 15, in Article 12, after paragraph 5 the following is inserted:\n5-bis. For raw materials, the Exit Plan indicates reduction of the requirement, reuse, recycling and recovery. It may not indicate new extraction. Paragraph 5 applies.\nReason. Before asking whether to dig, one needs to know what is already on the surface: disused buildings, vehicles, landfills. It is useful work in any case, and it touches no prohibition. Paragraph 5-bis closes a door that the review found open: without it, the obligation to exit external dependencies would push towards the mine.\nOther points to align. In Code 10: the inventory has to be added to the tasks of the Habitat and Territory Council (Art. 3) and to the contents of the Land Register (Art. 7, para. 1), and the Production Council has to be added to those who may consult it (Art. 7, para. 2). Versions: Code 10 from V1.3 to V1.4, together with proposal 6; Code 15 from V1.3 to V1.4."],[30,"proposal-7--raw-materials-recovery-and-inventory","Proposal 7 — Raw materials: recovery and inventory","","Text of the alignments (added on 10 October 2026). In Code 10, in Article 3, paragraph 3, after letter g) the following is added:\nh) keep the inventory of recoverable materials provided for by Article 8-bis.\nIn Article 7, paragraph 1, the words “pollinator refuges and agricultural water infrastructure” become “pollinator refuges, agricultural water infrastructure, deposits and recoverable materials”. In Article 7, paragraph 2, letter b), after the words “Agroecological and Biodiversity Councils” the words “and, for the inventory of recoverable materials, to the Production Council,” are inserted.\nWhat it costs. The annual inventory is new work for every Habitat and Territory Council: administrative time, not estimated. It partly overlaps with the inventory of degraded soils (Art. 5, para. 4) and can be kept together with it.\nOpen points. Paragraph 5-bis leaves a dependency without a definite time limit: the metals that recycling does not cover will continue to be bought outside, and the same Article 12 requires a time limit. It is a real contradiction between the prohibition on extracting and the obligation to exit dependencies: this proposal makes it visible, it does not resolve it. The reasoned review of paragraph 5 prevents the transition from being blocked, but it does not give a time limit."],[30,"proposal-8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Proposal 8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 7, Art. 1, para. 2, new letter f), and new Art. 6-bis; Code 1, Art. 8, para. 3).\nText and article: Code 7, Art. 1, paragraph 2, new letter f); new Art. 6-bis.\nWhat changes from version 1. The definition is narrower: the first one also included a washing machine. Devices for individual use remain personal. The prohibitions on arming and on monitoring apply to every machine, even a remote-controlled one, and respect the limits of Article 18 of the Constitution. The stop brings the machine into a safe state, it does not halt it abruptly. The time saved follows the rule that already exists: the first version contradicted it. Removed the paragraphs that repeated rules in force. Added the consequences of a violation and a time limit for bringing existing machines into conformity. Removed the reference to work in mines.\nProblem. The Constitution regulates AI (Arts. 19–23) and Code 7 the automation of tasks (Art. 6). No text names machines that move and act by themselves among people: missing are the stop independent of the software, the limits near people and the prohibition on arming them.\nProposed text. In Article 1, paragraph 2, the following letter is added:"],[30,"proposal-8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Proposal 8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","f) robot: means a machine that moves or manipulates objects in a space where persons are or may be present, choosing its trajectory or action by itself. Fixed-cycle appliances and protected fixed installations are not robots.\nAfter Article 6 the following is inserted:\nArt. 6-bis — Robots and autonomous machines\n1. Article 6 and the provisions of this Article apply to robots.\n2. Robots used in the CBO or in production for the Storehouses are means of production and Commons within the meaning of Article 10.1 of the Constitution: they may not be the object of private ownership or a source of rent. The Circle that has custody of them owns the hardware and holds the schematics and a verifiable copy of the code, pursuant to Articles 19.3 and 19.4 of the Constitution. Aids and devices for exclusively individual use remain personal-use goods within the meaning of Article 10.3 of the Constitution.\n3. Every robot has a physical stop independent of the software, which brings it into a safe state. The stop is accessible in accordance with the General Safety Sheet of Code 14; anyone may activate it in the cases of Article 2, paragraph 6, of that Code. The robot operates under the supervision of a qualified person on shift."],[30,"proposal-8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Proposal 8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","4. The limits of force, speed and distance from persons are set by the Machinery STV of Code 14, after hearing the Network Council. In case of doubt, physical separation between the machine and persons prevails. Until Code 14 is activated, the stop and the limits are set in the Safety Sheet of Article 27 of Code 1.\n5. It is prohibited:\na) to arm a machine or use it to coerce, restrain or strike a person, pursuant to Article 16 of the Constitution and Article 2, paragraph 6, of this Code;\nb) to use it to monitor or profile persons, or to follow them without their consent. The recognition of the qualified operator, the anonymous detection of presence for safety purposes and the search for persons in danger remain permitted, by non-biometric means and within the limits of Article 18 of the Constitution. Article 8-quinquies of Code 14 and Article 33 of Code 4 remain in force;\nc) to entrust to a robot the activities excluded by Article 6, paragraph 2; Article 1, paragraph 4, remains in force;\nd) to make a vital function dependent on a robot without a documented and tested substitute manual procedure.\nThe prohibitions of letters a) and b) apply to every machine, including one controlled remotely.\n6. Where a robot available in the Community can safely perform a task that Article 8, paragraph 1, of Code 1 classifies by risk, no person may be assigned to it, by any means, save for periodic tests of the manual procedure and cases of breakdown."],[30,"proposal-8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Proposal 8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","7. Before a robot is introduced, the hours needed to build it, power it, maintain it, train those who use it and dispose of it are compared, in UCR, with the hours of work it saves. The comparison is calculated in accordance with Articles 5 and 9 of Code 3 and certified by the Accounting Council. The time saved is allocated in accordance with Article 6, paragraph 6, and may not translate into an advantage for some.\n8. The system that governs a robot is an automation within the meaning of Article 1, paragraph 2, letter a). If it uses an AI, the AI is local and limited in domain within the meaning of Article 19.5 of the Constitution and is validated by the Network Council. In no case does it adopt the decisions reserved to persons by Article 2, paragraph 8.\n9. A robot that does not comply with paragraphs 3, 4 and 5 is suspended or deactivated pursuant to Article 9, paragraphs 2 and 3. The qualifications of Articles 16 and 18.6 of the Constitution remain in force. Robots already in use shall be brought into conformity within twelve months of the entry into force of this Article."],[30,"proposal-8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Proposal 8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","Reason. A machine that acts by itself in physical space must be able to stop without going through the software, stay away from people when in doubt, and never become an instrument of force or control. Robots that work for everyone belong to everyone, like every means of production. The time they free up stays with the Community, which decides how to use it: this is the rule the Code already has (Art. 6, para. 6).\nOther points to align. Paragraph 6 derogates, for risky tasks, from the assignment of Article 8, paragraph 3, of Code 1, which has to be aligned with a cross-reference. Version: Code 7 from V1.6 to V1.7, together with proposal 3.\nText of the alignment (added on 10 October 2026). In Code 1, in Article 8, paragraph 3, the words “Unpleasant, arduous or night tasks shall be assigned” become “Without prejudice to Article 6-bis, paragraph 6, of Code 7, unpleasant, arduous or night tasks shall be assigned”.\nWhat it costs. For every type of robot a Technical Sheet; for every activity a Safety Sheet; for every machine a certified comparison in hours. It is administrative time, not estimated, and the balance of hours already puts civic functions at the limit. Robots contain metals and components that the Confederation does not produce: dependencies to be registered (Code 15, Art. 12)."],[30,"proposal-8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","Proposal 8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","Open points. Code 7 knows two types of AI, personal and management: the one that guides a robot is neither, and paragraph 8 gives it only a minimal rule. Robots that assist frail persons, such as a hoist, fall within the definition: it has to be verified that paragraphs 3 and 5 are enough."],[30,"proposal-9--decay-of-the-cdr-when-it-begins-and-which-are-used-first","Proposal 9 — Decay of the CdR: when it begins and which are used first","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 1, Art. 44, para. 3; Art. 45, paras. 2 and 3).\nText and article: Code 1, Art. 44, paragraph 3; Art. 45, paragraphs 2 and 3.\nWhat changes from version 1. The first version made the value fall all at once at the end of each quarter and presented it as “the only reading” possible. It was not: it was the most generous one. Now the reduction is gradual, in proportion to time. Added two rules that were missing: how much a reduced CdR is worth and what is meant by accrual. The order of use has been moved to the Article on use.\nProblem. The paragraph says that CdR retain their value for twelve months, then “they are reduced by one quarter of the initial value for each quarter and are extinguished at the end of the twenty-fourth month”. It does not say how the value falls. There are three possible readings.\nReading · Full value for · Extinction · Hours a week to reach the cap\nGradual: from the twelfth month it falls in proportion to time · 12 months · 24th month · 3.1\nThe whole quarter at the end of each quarter (version 1) · 15 months · 24th month · 2.8\nThe whole quarter at the start of each quarter · 12 months · 21st month · 3.4\nOnly the first respects both the twelve months and the twenty-fourth. Moreover, no Article says which CdR are used first, nor how much a reduced CdR is worth when it is used.\nProposed text. In Article 44, paragraph 3 is replaced by the following:\n3. Unused CdR retain their value for twelve months from accrual. From the completion of the twelfth month the value is reduced in proportion to time, by one twelfth of the initial value per month, until it is extinguished at the completion of the twenty-fourth month. Accrual coincides with the day on which the activity was performed. For the purposes of use and of the cap, each CdR counts for the value it has at that moment.\nIn Article 45, paragraph 2, the words “one CdR corresponds to one UCR” become “one CdR at full value corresponds to one UCR”. At the end of paragraph 3 the following is added:\nThe CdR with the earliest accrual are used first.\nReason. One quarter per quarter is one twelfth per month: gradual reduction says the same thing as the text in force, without steps. Using the oldest CdR first is the rule most favourable to the person.\nOther points to align. The public CdR table and the simulation have been redone with the gradual curve. Version: Code 1 from V1.5 to V1.6, together with the other proposals on Code 1 that are accepted.\nWhat it costs. Compared with the reading used so far, at steady state 18 months of accrual are retained instead of 19.5: the cap is reached with 3.1 hours a week beyond the Base Contribution instead of 2.8. No effect on necessary work."],[30,"proposal-10--the-supplement-for-unpleasant-tasks-for-those-at-the-cap","Proposal 10 — The supplement for unpleasant tasks for those at the cap","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 1, Art. 44, para. 2).\nText and article: Code 1, Art. 44, paragraph 2.\nWhat changes from version 1. The solution is reversed. The first version awarded the supplement also to those at the cap, extinguishing their oldest CdR for an equal value. The review showed that it did not achieve what it claimed: the balance remained identical, the advantage was only a longer duration of the CdR, the quarterly data were inflated and the disparity moved elsewhere. Now the proposal confirms the rule in force, writes it expressly and defines how the balance is measured.\nProblem. The paragraph says that, once the cap has been reached, “further activities do not give rise to CdR”. It does not say whether this also applies to the supplement for unpleasant tasks performed within the Base Contribution, which are not “further activities”. Nor does it say how the balance is measured, now that CdR lose value over time.\nProposed text. In paragraph 2, the third sentence is replaced by the following:\nThe balance is the sum of the current values of the unused CdR, verified at the moment of the award. CdR are awarded up to the cap; beyond it no further CdR are awarded, including the supplements of Article 10 and of Article 43, paragraph 2.\nReason. The Constitution wants a personal cap (Art. 8.2): whoever has reached it cannot receive more without losing something. Every substitution mechanism complicates the register without changing the balance. Whoever is at the cap already has the maximum priority the system allows.\nOther points to align. The public table and the simulation already apply this rule. Version: Code 1 from V1.5 to V1.6, together with the other proposals on Code 1 that are accepted.\nWhat it costs. Whoever is at the cap and is drawn by lot for an unpleasant shift does not receive the supplement. It is the price of a cap without exceptions, and it has to be said. The sortition excludes for one quarter whoever has performed the most unpleasant hours (Art. 8, para. 3), but not whoever is at the cap for other activities. Whoever uses their own CdR goes back below the cap and starts accruing them again."],[30,"proposal-11--economic-thresholds-in-proportion-to-the-size-of-the-community","Proposal 11 — Economic thresholds in proportion to the size of the Community","","Outcome: accepted on 19 October 2026; no comments received. The text has been inserted into the Codes (Code 3, Art. 7, para. 3 and new paras. 3-bis and 3-ter; Art. 7-bis, para. 1; Art. 21, para. 3).\nText and article: Code 3, Art. 7, paragraph 3 and new paragraphs 3-bis and 3-ter; Art. 7-bis, paragraph 1.\nWhat changes from version 1. The proportion is no longer pure. A pump or a vehicle costs the same in a Community of thirty persons and in one of five hundred: with fully proportional thresholds, in small Communities almost every purchase would have ended up in the Assembly. Now each threshold has a fixed part and a per-person part. Added: the guarantees that medium- and high-risk operations have today, which are not lost when the threshold falls; who is counted and when; newly formed Communities; operations common to several Communities; the test cases.\nProblem. The matrix of Article 7 uses two fixed thresholds: 500 and 2,500 UCR, that is, hours of work. But a Base Community ranges from thirty to five hundred persons (Const. 5.1), and the same threshold weighs very differently. In the first balance of hours a Community works about 238 hours a year per inhabitant.\nPersons · 500 UCR are · 2,500 UCR are\n500 · 0.4% of a year’s work · 2.1%\n150 · 1.4% · 7%\n30 · 7% · 35%\nIn a Community of thirty persons, a low-risk operation that commits one third of a year’s work can be decided without the Assembly.\nProposed text. In the matrix of paragraph 3 the row headings become:"],[30,"proposal-11--economic-thresholds-in-proportion-to-the-size-of-the-community","Proposal 11 — Economic thresholds in proportion to the size of the Community","","Low: below the low threshold — Medium: from the low threshold to the high threshold inclusive — High: above the high threshold\nAfter paragraph 3 the following are inserted:\n3-bis. The low threshold is equal to 100 UCR plus one UCR for each person belonging to the Base Community, and does not exceed 500 UCR. The high threshold is equal to 500 UCR plus five UCR for each person, and does not exceed 2,500 UCR. All persons belonging to the Community at the beginning of the quarter are counted, minors included; guests are not counted. For a newly constituted Community the number in the act of accession applies. For operations common to several Communities and for the bodies of the higher Circles, the persons of the Communities concerned are added together.\n3-ter. The General Assembly calibrates the values of paragraph 3-bis pursuant to Article 14, paragraph 2, letter b). The calibration may not eliminate the proportion to the number of persons. Paragraphs 2 and 4 of Article 7-ter apply in any event to medium-risk operations with a value above the high threshold and not exceeding 2,500 UCR. In Article 3, paragraph 2, of this Code and in Articles 15 and 23 of Code 2, “the threshold” means the high threshold.\nIn Article 7-bis, paragraph 1 is replaced by the following:\n1. The special procedure applies to operations of low value and high or critical risk. Paragraphs 2 to 5 apply in any event to high- or critical-risk operations with a value below 500 UCR."],[30,"proposal-11--economic-thresholds-in-proportion-to-the-size-of-the-community","Proposal 11 — Economic thresholds in proportion to the size of the Community","","Reason. Thresholds serve to decide who resolves. They must grow with the Community, but not start from zero. With these values the largest Community remains as it is today and the others go down.\nPersons · Low threshold · High threshold · Of a year’s work\n30 · 130 UCR · 650 UCR · 1.8% and 9.1%\n150 · 250 UCR · 1,250 UCR · 0.7% and 3.5%\n400 and over · 500 UCR · 2,500 UCR · 0.5% and 2.6% or less\nTest cases. Article 21 of Code 3 requires the matrix to be tested on eight to ten concrete cases. These are seven: the test is partial. “CC” is the Competence Circle, “CQ” the Quality Council.\nOperation · Today, in every Community · With the proposal: 30 persons · 150 persons · 500 persons\n60 UCR, low risk · CC · CC · CC · CC\n200 UCR, high risk: a pump to be replaced urgently · special procedure, Assembly within 48 hours · Assembly within 48 hours, with the same guarantees · special procedure, Assembly within 48 hours · special procedure, Assembly within 48 hours\n400 UCR, low risk · CC · CQ and CC · CQ and CC · CC\n700 UCR, high risk · Assembly · Assembly with special guarantees · Assembly · Assembly\n900 UCR, medium risk · reinforced procedure · Assembly, with binding opinion and audit · reinforced procedure · reinforced procedure\n2,000 UCR, low risk · CQ and CC · Assembly · Assembly · CQ and CC\n3,000 UCR, high risk · Assembly with special guarantees · unchanged · unchanged · unchanged"],[30,"proposal-11--economic-thresholds-in-proportion-to-the-size-of-the-community","Proposal 11 — Economic thresholds in proportion to the size of the Community","","No case goes down a level and none loses the guarantees it has today. Only operations that are large relative to the Community go up a level. Missing are the cases with a negative opinion, a conflict of interest and an appeal.\nOther points to align. The rules against splitting (Art. 11) and on the calculation of value (Art. 5) do not change. In Article 21, paragraph 3, “below threshold” becomes “of low value”. Code 15 (Arts. 17 and 18) applies the matrix to the Fund of every Circle: for this reason paragraph 3-bis says how persons are counted above Circle 1. Version: Code 3 from V1.4 to V1.5.\nWhat it costs. In Communities below four hundred persons more decisions go up a level: more work for the Quality Council and more items on the Assembly’s agenda. How many is not calculated. The fixed parts and the coefficients are drafting assumptions.\nOpen points. Code 1 (Art. 40, para. 4) requires a comparative report for purchases “above the thresholds established by Code 3”: already today it does not say which of the two, and this remains to be decided. Recurring supplies are added together, under the rules against splitting: a year’s medicines for thirty persons are worth about 176 UCR and fall within medium value. It remains to be said whether a supply already approved with the quarterly requirement has to go through the matrix again. The thresholds in UCR depend on the calculation method of the UCR Technical Annex, which is still a draft."],[30,"what-happens-next","What happens next","","If by 20 October 2026 no objection shows that a proposal contradicts other Articles, worsens a test case or costs more than the system can sustain, the proposal enters the Code concerned, which changes version, and is recorded in the change log with its reason. If no one replies, the register will say so: “no comments received”, not “approved after discussion”. Proposals that are rejected or withdrawn remain on this page, with the reason."],[31,"","","","Working translation. The Italian text is the authoritative one.\nThis page sets out the objections found to the first version of the eleven amendment proposals, published on 5 October 2026. The proposals were then rewritten: version 2 states, for each one, what has changed. The quotations from the proposals therefore refer to the earlier text."],[31,"how-it-was-done","How it was done","","The review was carried out with an artificial intelligence assistant: four independent readings of the texts, each with a single task, to find defects. Each reading compared the proposals with the Constitution, the sixteen Codes and the annexes, looking for contradictions, wrong cross-references, cases in which the proposal makes things worse and undeclared costs.\nEvery objection reported here cites an article in force. The 77 citations that support them were rechecked one by one against the texts: they are all accurate. The calculations in sections 9 and 11 were redone. The estimate in section 10 was not rechecked.\nThree limits. The external sources (Millward-Hopkins 2020, WHO 2018) were not verified. A review of this kind finds the errors internal to the texts; it does not say whether a rule works among real people. And it does not replace the objections of readers: they are still needed."],[31,"outcome-in-brief","Outcome in brief","","None of the eleven proposals held up as it stood.\nProposal · Outcome · The defect that matters most\n1 Clothing and hygiene · to be corrected · disposables clash with the prohibition of non-cyclable materials (Const. 14.1)\n2 Adequate housing · to be corrected · it does not say whether substandard housing remains “adequate” for other provisions\n3 Connectivity · to be corrected · the device “may coincide with the Node”, which under the Constitution is offline\n4 Energy Basket · to be corrected · the list omits services already guaranteed: it reads as a step backwards\n5 CdR for culture · to be rewritten · workshops and research are already Base Contribution; no one knows who validates\n6 Subsoil · to be rewritten · it touches an article protected by the Inviolable Core\n7 Raw materials · to be rewritten · it regulates extraction even if proposal 6 falls; it does not exclude coal and uranium\n8 Robots · to be corrected · the definition includes the washing machine; it contradicts Art. 6, which it says it does not touch\n9 CdR decay · to be corrected · it is not “the only reading”: there is one more faithful to the text\n10 Supplement at the cap · to be corrected · “equal value” does not say whether initial or current\n11 Thresholds · to be corrected · in small Communities it removes the 48-hour urgent route"],[31,"three-common-defects","Three common defects","","No proposal declares the administrative time. Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 13, requires this of every new rule, and sets administrative time at no more than one tenth of the Base Contribution. The first hours balance already puts civic functions at that cap.\nWhatever enters the Basket rises in priority. Under Code 1, Art. 32, when time is insufficient the distribution of Basket goods comes before the care of frail persons and the maintenance of water and energy. Proposals 1, 2, 3 and 4 add items to the Basket without saying so. The sentence “none restricts a right” does not hold under scarcity.\nA Basket good bought outside is a critical dependency. Code 15, Art. 12, paras. 4 and 5, requires for each one an Exit Plan with a fixed deadline. Code 16, Art. 4, requires seventy per cent of levels 1 and 2 of the Basket to be produced in the Basin before moving to the next phase. Textiles, shoes, nappies and electronics widen that base. Proposals 1 and 3 speak only of “entry in the Register”."],[31,"errors-of-fact-in-the-texts-of-the-proposals","Errors of fact in the texts of the proposals","","They were wrong in the first version, whatever the decision.\nProposal 1. “Unconditional” is in Article 1.3 of the Constitution, not in 1.2.\nProposal 2. The “emergency reception” attributed to Code 11, Art. 6-ter, para. 6, does not exist in any text: that paragraph speaks of “an equipped stopping area”.\nProposal 5. Art. 43, para. 3, does not limit the CdR to one per hour: it is an additional recognition, which can double it.\nProposal 8. “It does not amend Article 6” is false: paragraph 7 contradicts Art. 6, para. 6. Article 19.4 of the Constitution speaks of source code; schematics are in 19.3. Art. 16 prohibits weapons, not “coercing and restraining”.\nProposal 9. “It is the only reading” and “it costs nothing” are not true (see below)."],[31,"1--clothing-and-hygiene","1 — Clothing and hygiene","","Medium. Disposables and leather. Pads, nappies and incontinence products are largely disposable: the Constitution prohibits non-cyclable materials (14.1) and landfill (14.5). Footwear is often made of leather, prohibited by 13.5. Correction: prefer washable and compostable items; disposables only under the conditions of 14.1; refer to 13.5.\nMedium. Ownership. Under Annex A, Art. 1, para. 4, an object of the Material Basket is a common good. The note says garments are personal, the text does not. Correction: from delivery they are Personal-Use Goods (Const. 10.3).\nMedium. Privacy. Withdrawals from the Storehouses are tracked (Const. 10.5). Menstrual and incontinence products reveal personal conditions. Correction: classify them as sensitive goods (Code 1, Art. 19, para. 5).\nMedium. Seat. A “Textile and Housing Storehouse” of the Storehouse Council already exists (Code 1, Art. 14). The proposal places goods that are almost all purchased under a production Council.\nLow. “Suited to the tasks performed” overlaps with PPE; thresholds “in days of autonomy” are not needed for durable garments."],[31,"2--adequate-housing","2 — Adequate housing","","High. What counts as “adequate”. Paragraph 4 prohibits refusing substandard housing. But Code 1, Art. 47, para. 3, and Code 4, Arts. 29 and 34, guarantee “adequate housing” to those who are transferred or subjected to a precautionary measure. If substandard housing counts, the definition is empty; if it does not, protection measures become impossible to carry out where housing that meets the standard is lacking. A maximum deadline for compliance is also missing. Correction: say so expressly, with a maximum deadline and appeal to the Guarantee Circle.\nMedium. Private space. The 15 m² include the common spaces of the Nucleus, with no reserved minimum: in a cohabitation of fifteen people a room can shrink to a few metres. Minors have no space of their own.\nMedium. Competence. Floor area and temperature do not fall within the functions of the Habitat and Territory Council (Code 10, Art. 3). The Energy and Water Council, the only one with thermal competence, is missing.\nMedium. Building. Where space is lacking the standard can be reached only by building, against the prohibition of land take (Code 10, Art. 2).\nLow. “Degrees” without a unit; heat has no threshold; after the first application nothing prevents going below 15 m² and 18 °C."],[31,"3--connectivity-and-learning-materials","3 — Connectivity and learning materials","","Medium. The Node is offline. Const. 19.1: “personal computing Node that is offline”. A communication device cannot coincide with the Node. Correction: “distinct from the Node”.\nMedium. Impossible obligation. Code 2, Art. 5, absolutely prohibits devices that do not work offline or escape community control; Code 1, Art. 35, prohibits planned obsolescence. With today’s telephones the Community might have no lawful device to give. Correction: within the limits of Code 2; otherwise shared and assisted access.\nMedium. Age. The texts already use twelve years (Code 5, Art. 6, para. 9), not ten. The paragraph referred to for minors concerns the personal AI, not the device.\nMedium. Materials. “The pathways to which the person is admitted” introduces an admission that Code 5 does not know, and makes the materials of any pathway part of the Basket. Correction: limit to literacy and to compulsory and safety training.\nLow. Every person already has a Node: the shared device per Nucleus is partly a duplicate. In the same Code “Art. 3, para. 6-bis” and “Art. 6-bis” would come into being."],[31,"4--energy-basket","4 — Energy Basket","","High. Step backwards. Art. 4, para. 1, of Code 8 already also guarantees cooling, the Health Storehouse and the Network node. The list “in first application at least” omits them. Correction: “without prejudice to all the services of paragraph 1”, and add only the two new values: 20 litres of hot water and 18 °C.\nHigh. Inconsistency with proposal 2. In proposal 2 the 18 °C is a target to be reached “with a deadline”. In proposal 4 it enters the minimum share: under Art. 4, para. 3, its absence is a level 1 emergency.\nMedium. Fossil fuels. Level 1 makes the transitional use of fossil sources lawful (Code 8, Art. 5, para. 3) and triggers the six-month deadline of Art. 10. The proposal does not say so.\nMedium. The same temperature would be set by two different bodies (proposals 2 and 4)."],[31,"5--cdr-for-cultural-activities","5 — CdR for cultural activities","","High. Already Base Contribution. Under Code 5, Art. 2, para. 3, time devoted to “teaching or documenting knowledge” is Base Contribution, even when the activity is free. Open workshops and research made public would be counted twice. Correction: remove them, or exclude time already counted.\nHigh. Who validates. Validation requires those who use, those who have “the relevant competence” and those who manage the Storehouse (Const. 9.2). None of the sixteen Competence Circles concerns the arts. What remains is audience approval. Correction: validation establishes only that the activity took place, that it was open to all, and the hours; not its merit.\nMedium. Which hours. Rehearsal, composition and study cannot be verified. With three hours of rehearsal a week anyone reaches the cap. The simulation of the cap assumes that half of the people accrue no CdR: with this proposal that no longer holds.\nMedium. The “never”. Today no text excludes spiritual and affective activities: the proposal removes a possibility and does not declare it. Who decides whether a liturgical choir is culture or worship?\nMedium. Preparation of events. The final clause would reward the preparation of a religious celebration: Const. 25.2 prohibits allocating common resources to religious denominations.\nMedium. Incentive. CdRs serve to cover peaks and emergencies (Code 1, Art. 6, para. 6). If they accrue in the same way through pleasant activities, the pull weakens.\nMedium. “Works intended for common spaces” remain personal property (Const. 10.3): the author accrues CdR and can take them back."],[31,"6--subsoil-as-a-common-good-and-extraction-as-an-exception","6 — Subsoil as a common good and extraction as an exception","","High. Inviolable Core. Const. 37.4 removes “the regime of the Commons” “from any revision procedure”. The text does not distinguish between extending and reducing: “extends, does not reduce” is the proposal’s own reading. Correction: do not touch Art. 3.1. That same article already admits “every other good classified as common” by the Codes: the subsoil can be so classified in Code 10.\nHigh. Regression. Art. 14.2 opens with “precaution and of environmental non-regression”. Writing a new exception there is a regression in the very paragraph that prohibits it. 14.3 declares living soil and waters an “inviolable limit”.\nHigh. “Beyond the site”. By implication, within the site everything is permitted, and “site” has no definition: whoever proposes draws its boundary. Air and ecosystems disappear from the list; forests and animal sanctuaries are not excluded; “temporary” has no time limit.\nHigh. Zero Waste. A mine produces tailings and sludge: Const. 14.5 prohibits landfill. Extraction would remain unlawful even if the proposal were adopted."],[31,"7--raw-materials-recovery-and-extraction","7 — Raw materials, recovery and extraction","","High. Unwritten dependence on proposal 6. If 7 passes and 6 does not, the Code contains a complete procedure for opening extraction sites, without the constitutional limits. Correction: split into two articles: one on reduction, recovery and inventory, valid immediately; one on extraction, which takes effect only if 14.2 changes.\nHigh. Coal and uranium. They are “mineral resources” and serve “for energy”. With Code 8, Art. 5, para. 3, a legal route opens to new fossil extraction. Correction: exclude them expressly.\nHigh. Too early. Code 10 is activated in Phase 1; sanctuaries, the Biodiversity Council and the Safety Council in Phase 2 (Code 16, Art. 3). Annex C prohibits irreversible decisions on the territory in the first year. Before Circle 2 the “double consensus” is a single vote of the same Assembly (Code 3, Art. 14, para. 10).\nHigh. Consensus is weak. In Code 3 “consensus” is the absence of reasoned objections, ordinary resolutions have no quorum, and a majority vote can be used. Opening a mine would require less than establishing a sanctuary. Correction: quorum and qualified majority.\nHigh. Who votes. The General Assembly is territorial (Code 3, Art. 14, para. 1), not “of the Circles that will use the material”. The beneficiaries would vote; the downstream Communities that do not use the material would be left out.\nHigh. The push to extract. Once metals are entered in the Dependency Register, the Exit Plan with a fixed deadline is mandatory. Once reuse and recycling are exhausted, the only way out is the mine, and the refusal of one Community blocks the phase transition of the whole Basin. Correction: for raw materials the Exit Plan may consist only of reduction, reuse and recycling.\nHigh. Who restores. Paragraph 8 says “the site is restored”, without a subject. Failing that, it falls on the host Community. Correction: borne by those who use the material, in proportion.\nHigh. Seat. Under Code 10, Art. 10, para. 8, each Code prevails only in its own subject matter. Deliberation, labour, distribution and purchases belong to Codes 3, 1 and 15: the derogation from sortition written into Code 10 risks being invalid.\nHigh. Short exclusions. Ecological corridors, woodland, infiltration areas and buffer zones are not named; no distance from inhabited areas.\nMedium. No maximum time limit. “Essential requirement” is not a term of the texts. “Voluntary” and “rotating among the Communities” do not fit together. A well requires two mandatory opinions, a mine none. Excavating a landfill to recover materials has no safeguards. Smelting and refining are not regulated. No sanction."],[31,"8--robots-and-autonomous-machines","8 — Robots and autonomous machines","","High. Definition. “Any machine that moves or acts in physical space without the continuous command of a person” includes a washing machine, a 3D printer, a pump on a timer. Code 7, Art. 1, already defines “automation” by the same criterion. Correction: a robot is a machine that moves or manipulates objects in a space shared with people, choosing its trajectory or action by itself.\nHigh. Personal property. Const. 10.3 declares that light individual tools “may not be collectivised”. A personal motorised aid would become a common good. Correction: robots used in the Base Contribution or in production are common goods.\nHigh. Time saved. Art. 6, para. 6, leaves to the Community the choice of how to use the time freed. Paragraph 7 imposes a reduction in everyone’s hours. Two incompatible rules would apply to robots. Correction: refer to Art. 6, para. 6, or amend it expressly.\nMedium. Remote control. A drone piloted continuously is not a “robot”: the prohibitions on arming it and on surveillance do not apply to it. Correction: those prohibitions apply to every machine.\nMedium. Stop. “Reachable by anyone” allows anyone to stop without reason a machine that sustains a vital function. For a machine at height an instantaneous stop may be the danger. Correction: a stop that brings the machine to a safe state, accessible in accordance with the Safety Sheet.\nMedium. Surveillance. The prohibition on “identifying or following” also prevents recognising the authorised operator, the trolley that follows a worker, and the search for a missing person.\nMedium. Three different Sheets under the name “the STV” and two Councils. Undeclared derogation from sortition. No sanction or deadline for bringing existing machines into compliance. Nothing on the software that guides the robot.\nLow. Stop, priority for dangerous tasks and the Dependency Register already exist: four paragraphs repeat rules in force."],[31,"9--cdr-decay","9 — CdR decay","","Medium. It is not the only reading. The text in force says: full value for twelve months, then a quarter less each quarter, extinction at the twenty-fourth. There are three possible readings.\nReading · Full value for · Extinction · Hours a week to reach the cap\nReduction at the end of the quarter (proposal) · 15 months · 24th month · 2.8\nGradual reduction, one twelfth a month · 12 months · 24th month · 3.0\nReduction at the start of the quarter · 12 months · 21st month · 3.4\nOnly the gradual one respects both sentences. The proposal chose the most generous and presented it as obligatory.\nMedium. What a reduced CdR is worth. Whoever uses the oldest CdRs first uses the reduced ones. No text says whether a CdR at a quarter covers one UCR or a quarter of a UCR. Correction: each CdR counts for the value it has at that moment.\nLow. Curve and order of use are two amendments in one. “Accrual” is not defined: day of the activity or of the validation?"],[31,"10--supplement-for-those-at-the-cap","10 — Supplement for those at the cap","","High. “Equal value”. The oldest CdRs are the most reduced. If “equal value” is the initial value, a new CdR extinguishes one worth a quarter and the balance exceeds the cap. If it is the current value, it cancels four. The Code does not even say how the balance is measured. Correction: “for equal current value”; the balance is the sum of current values.\nMedium. It does not do what it says. Those at the cap see an identical balance: they gain only duration. The difference shows when they stop. Estimate not rechecked: after twelve months of inactivity the balance would be 193 CdR instead of 81.\nMedium. Transparency. Every replacement counts as both awarded and extinguished: the quarterly data of Art. 46 swell. Correction: distinguish those extinguished by decay from those extinguished by replacement.\nMedium. Disparity that remains. Travel within the Base Contribution, also not chosen, remains outside. And sortition is among those “available”: “not chosen” is not always true."],[31,"11--thresholds-in-proportion-to-size","11 — Thresholds in proportion to size","","High. Urgency. Convening within 48 hours exists only in the special procedure, for high-risk operations below 500 UCR (Art. 7-bis). In a Community of thirty people, the urgent replacement of a 200 UCR pump would end up above the high threshold: ordinary Assembly, with no urgent route. Correction: for high risk below 500 UCR, paragraphs 2, 3 and 4 of Art. 7-bis remain in every case.\nHigh. Goods that cannot be divided. The price of a pump or a vehicle does not fall with the Community; the threshold does. For thirty people the low threshold is worth about 1,500 euros of purchases and the high one about 7,700. Medicines, kitchen, workshop and vehicles would almost all go to the Assembly. Possible correction: a fixed part plus a proportional one.\nHigh. Newly founded Community. “The number of members on the first day of the year” is zero for a Community founded in March: every operation would be “high”. It hits precisely the pilot Circle. Correction: the number from the latest quarterly balance or from the act of accession; minimum equal to thirty people.\nMedium. Who is counted. The proposal says “member”, the tables “people”, Code 3 “those entitled to vote”. Minors, guests, suspended persons?\nMedium. Other points to align. Code 2, Arts. 15 and 23, and Code 1, Art. 40, speak of “the threshold” in the singular. Code 15, Arts. 17 and 18, applies the matrix to the Fund of “every Circle”: with thresholds per member the cross-reference is no longer defined for a Circle 2.\nMedium. Calibration. “These values” may indicate the coefficients or the maxima. A calibration at 17 UCR per member keeps the proportion and brings everything back to today’s level.\nMedium. Recurring supplies. Under the rules against splitting they are added together: a year’s medicines for thirty people exceed the high threshold.\nLow. The percentages are accurate but calculated only on the internal labour of scenario A. The test cases required by Art. 21 are missing."],[31,"what-held-up","What held up","","All the insertion points exist and no numbering collides.\nThe starting versions are accurate.\nThe Dependency Register, the prohibition of Extractive Accumulation, the General Safety Sheet and the Dossier exist under those names and in those articles.\nThe curve of proposal 9 is arithmetically consistent and matches the published simulation.\nThe figures of proposal 11 are accurate.\nThe veto of the host Community (proposal 7) is consistent with Arts. 4.1, 4.2 and 32.1 of the Constitution.\nNo other text presupposes a different decay curve."],[31,"what-followed","What followed","","Version 2 of the proposals follows these choices, the most cautious among those possible.\nProposal 6. No amendment to the Constitution. The subsoil is classified as a common good in Code 10. The exception that permitted extraction is withdrawn: the question remains open.\nProposal 7. Only what holds in any case remains: inventory of recoverable materials, custody of deposits, safeguards for recovery excavations. The article states expressly that it does not authorise new extraction.\nProposal 9. Gradual reduction, in proportion to time, the only one that respects both sentences of the text. The public CdR table and the simulation have been redone.\nProposal 10. The rule in force is confirmed, written expressly, with the definition of the balance. The replacement mechanism is withdrawn.\nProposal 11. Thresholds with a fixed part and a per-person part, today’s safeguards retained, test cases attached.\nProposal 5. Restricted to what takes place before the Community, without judgement of merit, with an annual limit.\nProposals 1, 2, 3, 4 and 8. Corrected point by point.\nDeadline. The fourteen days start again from version 2.\nNot all the objections are resolved. Those that remain are listed, proposal by proposal, under the heading “Open points” or “What it costs”."],[31,"second-reading","Second reading","","Version 2 too was reread in the same way, before being published. The citations and calculations almost all held; another forty objections emerged, mostly new contradictions introduced by the corrections. The most serious, and what was done about them:\nProposal 9. The reduction “at the end of each month” left full value for thirteen months, not twelve: the same defect criticised in the first version. Now the value falls in proportion to time, and the calculations have been redone: 18 months retained at steady state, cap reached with 3.1 hours a week.\nProposal 5. “Validation does not assess merit” contradicted Article 9.1 of the Constitution, which requires every service to be assessed. Now validation remains that of the Constitution; if it is positive, only the hours count.\nProposals 2 and 4. The same temperature had two deadlines: thirty-six months in Code 1 and six in Code 8. Now Code 1 refers to Code 8.\nProposal 6. A new prohibition on “digging or removing mineral resources” would, literally, also have hit foundations, wells and remediation. It has been removed: the prohibition of the Constitution remains.\nProposal 8. Recognition of the operator, written without limits, clashed with the prohibition of biometric recognition in Article 18 of the Constitution. Now it is permitted only by non-biometric means.\nProposal 7. Recovery excavation required fewer safeguards than the decision matrix already gives today. Now the matrix remains unchanged, and excavations are not permitted before Codes 13 and 14 exist.\nProposal 11. Some medium-risk operations moved up a level but lost the binding opinion and the audit. Now they retain them.\nProposals 1 and 3. The rule on priority was written in the wrong place. Now it is an express amendment to Article 32 of Code 1.\nThe objections that have no answer have remained in the proposals, under the heading “Open points”. An adversarial reading always finds something: two rounds do not make the texts right, they make them less wrong. That is why the proposals remain open."],[32,"","","","This is the first public version: Constitution V.29 and aligned Codes, published on 5 October 2026.\nFrom here on, every accepted change will be recorded on this page with its date, the text and article concerned, and the reason."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","The forty-two low-severity items of the consistency review are corrected directly, by decision of the author. For each item: what changes and why.\nC29. Drawing by lot from the lists (Code 4, Art. 36.3). No one may be drawn from the federated lists who belongs to the Community of the person concerned, of the injured person or of the audited activity, or who has worked there in the last twenty-four months; the local members whom the Codes draw within the Community, such as those of Art. 27.1–27.2, remain. Reason: “Circle concerned” could also mean the Circle 2 from which the Tribunal is drawn.\nC30. Activation and review of the Civic Protection Corps (Code 14, Arts. 8-bis.1 and 8-quater.2). Scheduled interventions are resolved by the Assembly; in urgency the reference person on duty acts and informs the Circle at once. The review within twenty-four hours is carried out by the six persons drawn by lot from the two neighbouring Communities, by majority in each; if it is not concluded in time, the activation is not validated. Reason: it was not said who activates the Corps or who carries out the review.\nC31. Binding opinion and change of members (Code 3, Arts. 8.2 and 12-bis.10). Removing or replacing the members of the Council does not override the negative binding opinion, which remains effective; the new Council re-examines it only on the resubmitted proposal. Reason: removal could be used to get round the opinion."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C32. Conformity check and deactivation (Code 4, new Art. 13-bis). A single procedure for the references of Codes 12, 13 and 14 to Code 4: technical check by the competent Council, adaptation plan, deactivation resolved by the Assembly with a replacement plan, without interrupting food and essential care. Reason: the Codes referred to a procedure that Code 4 did not contain.\nC33. “Vital guarantees” (Const. 7.3 and 30.2). During the suspension of CdR the rights of Art. 1.2 remain intact; no measure touches the vital guarantees of Art. 30.4. Reason: the term was used in two different senses.\nC34. Review Panel (Const. 31.3 d and e; Code 4, Arts. 35.1 and 35-bis.4). The Constitution names the Review Panel among the justice bodies; in appeals on offences of Grades 1–3 it includes a person with legal training taken from the Tribunal list, instead of a member of the Quality Council; the annual summary is prepared in turn by a Guarantee Circle of the Basin. Reason: the Panel was not in the Constitution and the Quality Council judges goods and services, not offences. Material correction (Const. 37.5) outside the Core.\nC35. Majority in the Safety Council (Code 14, Art. 3.3). Only technical acts, opinions and validation of sheets by majority; proposals and discretionary decisions by consensus and seventy-two hours of publication. Reason: it is the rule for all Councils (Code 3, Art. 12-bis.9)."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C36. Appeal on a decision with an opinion of the Guarantee Circle (Code 3, Art. 17.1). If the Guarantee Circle gave an opinion or authorisation on the decision, the appeal is decided by persons drawn by lot in other Communities. Reason: whoever gave the opinion cannot judge it.\nC37. Solidarity Protocol (Code 1, Arts. 2.8 d, 3.3, 30.1 d; Code 16, Art. 1.5; Annex C, Art. 5.3). A single name; the Community requests the Protocol and does not activate it; before the minimum Federation the declaration falls to the General Assembly of the affected Basin, within the limits of Const. 36.4. A Community not yet part of a Basin does not activate it on its own. Reason: Const. 36.2 has it activated by Circle 3 or by ten Circles 1 together.\nC38. Health Basket (Code 6, Arts. 6.1 and 9.5; Code 1, Art. 18.2). It is proposed by the Health Councils of the Communities as branches of the Medicine and Health Competence Circle and approved by the Assembly; the “federated Health Council” disappears. Reason: that body does not exist.\nC39. Reduction of the CBO (Code 1, Art. 11.1 d and e). Pregnancy, bereavement, crisis and the other situations of the Health and Care Codes give, on the indication of the Health Council or the Care Council, a temporary reduction without debt, not cumulative with others for the same hour; the diagnosis remains confidential. Reason: Code 6 referred to a reduction that Code 1 did not contain."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C40. Health Storehouse (Code 1, Art. 14.1 d). A single name. Reason: “Care Storehouse” appeared only here.\nC41. Cap on administrative time (Code 1, Art. 2.7 e). The tenth of the CBO counts Councils, guarantee, facilitation, accounting and mandatory documentation; Corps shifts and other civic functions are counted separately. Reason: aligns Code 1 with Const. 6.5 and Code 3, Art. 12-bis.13.\nC42. CdR parameters (Code 1, Art. 44.4). Cap and decay are set every year by the General Assembly of Circle 2; that of Circle 3 may set common limits. Reason: “General Assembly” denotes two bodies (Code 3, Art. 14.1).\nC43. Certification in UCR (Code 4, Art. 4.6 d). Reference corrected to Code 1, Arts. 20.6 and 40.4. Reason: Art. 40 deals with open standards.\nC44. Annex A and Code 9 (C.1-Annex A, Arts. 2.3, 4.5 a, 6.2, 6.3, 6.5, 10.3; Code 9, Art. 6.7). The production order is a five-level scale with the same formula for the Material Basket; every Circle 2 has at least one Advanced Workshop for every three or four Communities, activated by the shared Production Council; “cluster” and “basin” disappear. Reason: undefined terms and a “level 5” that did not exist.\nC45. Level 2 of competences (Code 5, Art. 4.2 b). It is confirmed by two persons already validated at least at Level 2 in the same competence, also from other Communities; the Competences Council records it. Reason: the “practice Circle” did not exist."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C46. Abbreviations L1–L3 and classes R0–R4 (Code 5, new Art. 4.2-bis). The abbreviations denote the Levels of Code 5; a table links the risk classes of Code 14 to the required Levels; safety training is added to validation. Reason: the two systems were not linked.\nC47. Three reference figures (Const. 29.3). They are for minors; adults learn with ordinary accompaniment. Reason: aligns the Constitution with Code 5, Art. 6.7 and paragraphs 29.4–29.5.\nC48. Guarantee Circle: other functions and appeals above Circle 1 (Code 4, Arts. 4.2 d and 27.6; Code 6, Art. 2-bis.4 d; Code 3, new Art. 17.1-bis). The Guarantee Circle also performs the functions given by the other Codes; the appeal against decisions of Circles 2 and 3 is decided by three members drawn by lot from the Guarantee Circles of different Communities, and the decision applies to all of them. Reason: Const. 31.3 d) knows only the Guarantee Circle of the Community."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C49. Minors: leaving the Confederation and choice of residence (Const., new Art. 26.5; Code 12, new Art. 7-ter; Code 4, Art. 37.6). From sixteen the minor declares departure personally, after an interview with the Care Council within fourteen days; before that the declaration is made by those who hold responsibility for their care, solely in their interest and after hearing them, and it does not extend to the children of a person who leaves; the Guarantee Tribunal may be seised within fourteen days. From fourteen an autonomous minor may choose where to live, with the assessment of the Care Council and the Competences Council within thirty days and appeal to the Tribunal. The guarantees of Const. 1.3 and 4.9-bis always remain. Reason: Const. 4.8 and 26.2 did not say how they apply to minors; the ages are the author’s choice (sixteen as for consent to care, fourteen as for the vote).\nC50. Minimum energy (Code 8, Art. 2.4). It is reduced only in case of material impossibility, for a limited time, with mutual aid and notice to the Guarantee Circle; “extreme emergency” alone is not enough. Reason: Const. 15.2 and 36.4.\nC51. Water scarcity: who decides (Code 10, Art. 8.2). The Energy and Water Council applies the order of Code 9, Art. 10.8; taking away water or other Basket goods is rationing and is decided by the Assembly, or by the urgent procedure. Reason: a Council does not decide in place of the Assembly (Const. 6.6)."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C52. Bee buffer zone (Code 10, Art. 2.6; Code 9, Art. 9.1 g; Code 13, Art. 6.3 c). Systemic products are never allowed; in extreme emergency the Assembly allows for a short time only non-persistent, non-systemic products; rescue of colonies follows Code 9 only. Reason: two procedures for the same thing.\nC53. Essential energy for everyone (Code 8, Art. 4.2). Guaranteed to whoever is in the territory, member or not, like drinking water. Reason: Const. 15.2 is not limited to members.\nC54. Preventing births of companion animals (Code 13, Art. 7.10). Sterilisation for the animal’s welfare or for the capacity of care, indicated by a veterinarian and decided by those who live with the animal, is not reproductive manipulation; no one imposes it on all. Reason: interpretative rule decided by the author on Const. 13.2–13.3.\nC55. Wildlife (Code 13, new Art. 5-bis and Art. 3.2 c). Against damage and risks only non-cruel methods: prevention, deterrence, fencing and, if needed, painless capture and relocation; the Biodiversity Council decides, after hearing the Agroecological Council for damage to fields; the method is set out in a new STV. Reason: the “non-cruel removal” of Const. 13.4 was not regulated.\nC56. Euthanasia of sanctuary animals (Code 13, Art. 4-bis.7). The same conditions as for companion animals, with the sanctuary sheet and information to those who care for them. Reason: the reference led to tools that exist only for companion animals."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C57. Transfer of an animal (Code 13, Art. 7.6 d). No reimbursement between members; with outsiders only documented care expenses, through the Common Fund. Reason: Const. 3.5 and 3.5-bis.\nC58. Facilities “every three or four Communities” and “territorial Basin” (Code 13, Arts. 4.1, 4-bis.5, 11.3 c; Code 12, Art. 6.3; Code 9, Art. 6.4; Code 10, Art. 1.2 g and new h). Sanctuaries, care facilities and agricultural infrastructure are counted in every Circle 2; “territorial Basin” is defined as the territory of Circle 2. Reason: the “basin” was not defined.\nC59. “Technical assembly” for dispatching (Code 8, Art. 3.3 d). It is the Energy and Water Council; local manual override remains under the existing rules. Reason: the term of Const. 15.3 had not been implemented.\nC60. Water below 50 litres per person (Code 8, new Art. 4-bis.6). Allowed only for temporary technical impossibility, decided by the Assembly or by the urgent procedure, with a request for help, a request for the Solidarity Protocol where needed and notice to the Guarantee Circle; drinking water and water for health are reduced last. Reason: the same guarantees as for energy (Code 8, Art. 2.5)."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C61. Admission of low-value goods (Code 2, Art. 15.1 a). The Quality Council investigates and proposes; the body of the matrix of Code 3 decides; it is stated which Council is the “Competence Circle” for a good. Reason: three rules named three different bodies (the first part of the item, in Code 3, Art. 3.2 a, dates from 20 October).\nC62. Use check of goods (Code 2, Art. 3.2 and new Art. 14.1-bis). The provisional STV is published for at least seventy-two hours; for goods that are not simple or not safe some users are also heard; in a trial in a pilot Pantry opinions are collected; the Storehouse Council gives a non-binding opinion. Reason: Const. 9.2 wants users and those who run the Storehouse in the validation.\nC63. Map of the World Pool (Code 15, Art. 8.1). It is drawn up by the AI of each Intermediate Circle for its own territory; maps are exchanged through open protocols; no system merges them into a single model. Reason: Const. 22.2.\nC64. Mandatory review of the AI (Code 7, Art. 5-ter.5). Three refusals by the same Circle on the same matter, by any body, without the twelve-month limit. Reason: Const. 19.6 is Core and does not provide for those limits."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C65. Electronic voting and signatures for the Objection (Code 7, new Art. 7-bis; Code 3, Arts. 12.2, 13-bis.1, 13-bis.4, 13-bis.6). Register of those entitled kept separate from the ballot box, open software, no log linking person and vote, counting by Community, never closed systems; the Objection may be signed by anyone entitled to vote, even without having voted, with 10% of voters and anonymous signatures; after confirmation by two thirds it cannot be raised again on the same part. Reason: Const. 34.2 and 34.4, with the author’s choices.\nC66. Personal and common AI Node (Const. 3.1, 3.4, 5.1 a, 8.2, 14.5; Code 7, Art. 5-bis.1). Common Goods are the Community and Circle AI Nodes; Circle 0 has a personal Node for each member; “AI-Logistics” becomes “Community and Circle AIs”; CdR and waste traceability use the Community Node; the personal Node is personal property (Const. 10.3). Reason: 3.1 and 5.1 a) contradicted 1.2 d) and 19.1. The amendments to 3.1 and 5.1 a), within the Core, are pure alignments of wording and therefore material corrections (Const. 37.5).\nC67. Annex B: phases and Legal Fund (Annex B, Arts. 5.7, 6.1, 8.6). Each element is activated in the phase in which Code 16 provides for it; when the Legal Fund runs out only the Annex’s initiatives stop, without counting as a missed threshold. Reason: the Annex mixed elements of different phases."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","C68. Perceived safety below 80% (Annex B, Art. 8.3 a and 8.5). Only the procedure of Annex A, Art. 4.6 is followed; if only the transition proposal had stopped, it restarts without another resolution. Reason: two procedures on the same condition.\nC69. Point of no return (Code 16, Art. 5.5). It refers to reversibility (Art. 2.2), to the rules on common goods (Art. 6) and to the external L3 opinion (Annex A, Art. 4.7). Reason: the reference led to an article about something else.\nC70. References of Code 16 and Annexes (Code 16, Arts. 1.2 i, 3.1 b, 10.3 b and new 1.8 and 1.9). The R0–R4 scale is that of Code 14; Code 6 applies in full from Phase 0; mandatory rules are those that no body may derogate from; each Annex, or its part for a phase, is approved by the Assembly of the scale to which it applies; references to Codes not yet active apply in so far as applicable, without prejudice to constitutional guarantees. Reason: wrong or vague references and an approval of the Annexes designed only for Circle 1."],[32,"21-october-2026--low-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c29c70","21 October 2026 — low-severity items of the consistency review (C29–C70)","","Reason: These are the corrections proposed by the consistency review for the low-severity items, with the author’s choices on C49 (choice of residence from fourteen), C37 (a Community not yet in a Basin does not activate the Protocol on its own), C66 (Const. 3.1 and 5.1 a as well, as a material correction) and C70 (Annexes approved by the Assembly of the scale to which they apply). The other amendments to the Constitution (Arts. 3.4, 7.3, 8.2, 14.5, 26.5, 29.3, 30.2, 31.3) do not touch the Inviolable Core and count as the text of the first adoption (Const. 38.1). With this entry all 76 items of the consistency review are resolved.\nVerification: consistency review, low-severity items.\nVersions: Constitution from V.32 to V.33, Code 1 from V1.8 to V1.9, C.1-Annex A from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 2 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 3 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 4 from V1.7 to V1.8, Code 5 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 6 from V1.7 to V1.8, Code 7 from V1.8 to V1.9, Code 8 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 9 from V3.2 to V3.3, Code 10 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 12 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 13 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 14 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 15 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 16 from V1.5 to V1.6, Annex B to Code 16 from V1.5 to V1.6, Annex C to Code 16 from V1.1 to V1.2."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","The twenty-one medium-severity items of the consistency review are corrected directly, by decision of the author. For each item: what changes and why.\nC8. Accompaniment Community (Code 4, Art. 30.3). It is an open Community, with no protected perimeter and no supervision by the Civic Protection Corps, distinct from the Containment Community; the person is obliged to remain there and has daily accompaniment, is not held by force and, if they leave, the Guarantee Tribunal decides. The maximum duration (five years for Grade 5, ten for Grade 6) is a firm limit. Reason: without a definition the measure could become High-Security Containment without reoffending, which Const. 1.3-bis prohibits.\nC9. Justice in Phase 0 (Code 4, Art. 37.5; Code 16, Art. 8.6). The panel drawn by lot outside the pilot Circle also applies when no Basin exists yet, also for the second Tribunal and for the substitute Guarantee Circle, with express derogations from the rules on the same Community; whoever carries out a review may not be whoever decided. Reason: the paragraph covered only an incomplete Basin.\nC10. Recordings of the Corps (Code 14, Art. 8-quater.3). The person on whom the Corps intervenes is also redacted; the full version is seen only by those who validate, by the competent justice bodies and by the person, for the part concerning them. Reason: the person is presumed not responsible (Const. 17.6; Code 4, Art. 14.3)."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C11. Binding opinions (Const. 6.6; Code 3, Arts. 7-ter.2, 7-quater.2 a, 8.1, 12-bis.9; Code 4, Art. 4.6 b; Code 9, Arts. 6-bis.3 c, 4 c, 5 d, 7.3; Code 10, Art. 4.4 d; Code 13, Arts. 3.2 c, 3.3, 3.5, 6.6; Code 14, Art. 3.6; Code 16, Art. 8.3). Article 8 of Code 3 becomes the closed list of binding negative opinions: Health, Network, Safety and, for the ecological limits that Const. 6.6 now names, Agroecological and Biodiversity. The welfare of animals that live or collaborate with people no longer goes through a “binding opinion” but through the authorisation that Const. 13.3 already gives to the Biodiversity Council. Reason: Code 3 forgot Safety and other Codes gave binding opinions that the Constitution did not provide for.\nC12. Hierarchy of priorities (Code 1, Arts. 2.8 a, 32.1-ter, 32.1-quater, 32.4; Code 6, Art. 7.2–7.3; Code 8, Art. 2.3; Code 11, Art. 2.4–2.5; Code 12, Arts. 2.4, 4.7). Article 32 of Code 1 applies to everyone; the scales of the sectoral Codes order only their own sector and level 1 is always the highest. The care of frail persons comes before perishable goods, but not before Basket food; spare parts come after everyone’s Basket, except for the urgent derogation of Art. 32.3. Reason: the sectoral Codes rewrote the hierarchy in different ways."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C13. “Levels 1 and 2 of the Basket” (Code 1, Art. 32.1-quinquies; Code 16, Art. 1.2 f; Code 16, Annex C, Art. 6.1; Code 11, Art. 1.4 d–e; C.1-Annex A, Art. 2.3 a). A single definition, in Code 1, for energy, food, care and the other goods of the Basket; the other texts refer to it. Reason: the definition was only in Code 16, was incomplete, and “level” also referred to the Circles.\nC14. Eighteen-year-olds who have not yet chosen (Code 5, Art. 6.2, 6.6 and new 6.6-bis; Code 4, Art. 29.3 e). Until the choice of Const. 26.4 the CBO remains voluntary and the refusal procedure may not be started; the Competences Council informs the person at least three months before they turn eighteen and the time limit does not run without the information. Reason: no Code implemented Const. 26.4.\nC15. Travel within the CBO (Code 1, Arts. 25.5 and 43.2). Travel imposed by the CBO counts as hours of CBO and gives no CdR; the supplement applies only to travel beyond the CBO. Reason: within the CBO, Const. 8.1 admits CdR only for unpleasant, arduous or night tasks.\nC16. Surpluses (Code 1, new Art. 15.1-bis; Code 15, Arts. 6.2 and 15.3). When the Balance is active, the federable surplus goes first to the Pool; direct donation, reuse and disposal outside apply to the rest. Reason: Codes 1 and 15 gave two different rules for the same goods."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C17. Pool and Balance (Code 15, Arts. 3.1–3.3, 9.2 c and new Art. 9-bis; Code 3, Art. 12-bis.2; Code 16, Arts. 3.2 g and 3.3 c). Each Circle 2 and 3 has a Storehouse Council drawn by lot that decides the allocations of the Pool within the annual criteria of the General Assembly, within 24 hours for vital needs; the Balance is no longer declared at “level 4”. Reason: no body decided above Circle 1.\nC18. The home of those who leave (Const. 3.3; Code 1, Art. 47.3 and new 47.3-bis). The dwelling is a Personal-Use Good for as long as the ownership of use lasts; on transfer, on leaving the Confederation or on termination of status the home is vacated, within the time limits already provided for, and those living in the same Circle 0 may stay. Reason: it was not said whether those who leave keep the home.\nC19. Housing allocation (Code 1, new Art. 47-ter). Annual plan proposed by the Storehouse Council and approved by the Assembly; allocation by need, never by hours of CBO, CdR or offices; public, anonymous waiting list; no one is left without shelter; appeal to the Guarantee Circle. Reason: the “housing planning” of Const. 3.7 had no procedure."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C20. Scarce water (Code 9, Arts. 2.5, 6-bis.2, 10.8; Code 8, Art. 4-bis.5; Code 13, Arts. 4-bis.6, 7.11). A single rule, in Code 9, Art. 10.8: first drinking water for people (minimum quota) and for health and care services; then, on an equal footing and divided in proportion to need, the minimum water for the survival of animals and pollinators and the minimum water for the fields of the essential Basket; other uses are cut first. The number of animals is planned on the water of dry years and, before an animal is left without water, the water or a transfer to another Community is requested through the Pool; until the Pool exists (Phase 0) the transfer is attempted directly with another Community or a kindred external body, by written agreement. Reason: Codes 9 and 13 and the clause “Code 1 prevails” gave opposite answers; the choice is the author’s.\nC21. STVs other than for goods (Code 2, Art. 1.3 and new Art. 3-bis; Code 7, Art. 6.5; Code 12, Art. 1.2 g; Code 14, Art. 1.2 b). STVs for procedures, services, automations, systems and safety follow the matrix of Code 3, with the cost over two years as the value; whoever drafts an STV may not approve it alone; the criticality of an automation is validated by two L3 persons, one of them independent. STV always means “Technical Validation Sheet”. Reason: the procedure was written only for goods."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C22. Cloud and closed software (Const. 19.4; Code 2, Arts. 5.2 b and new 12.5; Code 7, Arts. 3.9 and 5.5; Code 11, Art. 5.7; Code 16, new Art. 9-bis). The Constitution allows the Codes to admit, for a limited time and with a public Substitution Plan, systems whose source code is not available, excluding those that propose or decide on rights, voting and Commons; the Code of Transition governs, until closure, systems hosted outside and AI models that are not open source. A vehicle that depends on a mandatory cloud no longer has the twelve-month derogation and Management AIs use open-source models. Reason: Const. 19.4 did not admit the residual dependencies that Code 2 admits; the author chose to write this in the Constitution (option B).\nC23. Management AI (Code 7, Art. 5.1). It may perform all the support functions provided for by the Constitution and the Codes, always as a proposal; the list of prohibitions remains closed. Reason: the “exclusive” list excluded functions that other texts entrust to it."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C24. AIs of Circles 2 and 3 (Code 3, Art. 12-bis.6 b and new 12-bis.6-bis; Code 7, new Art. 4.7). A single scheme for the shared Council of Circles 2 and 3 (persons drawn by lot from the Councils of the Communities, only technical acts and urgent decisions to be ratified within thirty days, discretionary decisions to the General Assembly, revocation by the Community of origin); the shared Network Council manages the nodes and AIs of Circles 2 and 3 and each Community keeps the manual override. Reason: no body managed those AIs.\nC25. Logistics of Circles 2 and 3 (Code 11, new Art. 6-quater). A shared Logistics Council manages the vans, caravans and coastal transport of Circle 2 with a written rule for sharing the time of the means; for the railway of Circle 3, a protocol approved by its General Assembly. Reason: the shared means had no body.\nC26. After the closure of the transition (Code 4, new Art. 37.6; Code 16, Art. 10.2 a). Where the law of a State applies, the safeguards of the transition remain; Code 16 ceases to apply only for the Federation that has closed. Reason: on closure, safeguards tied to the law of the State fell away, while that law remains."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","C27. Birth of the Extended Circle (Code 3, new Art. 14-bis; Code 16, Art. 1.5 and 1.7). The minimum Federation becomes an Extended Circle when it closes its own transition; later, three or more Circles 2 may constitute one; the date is recorded and counts for the founding regime (Const. 38.2–38.3). Reason: no text said how an Extended Circle is born (option A of the draft).\nC28. Scale of the phases (Code 16, Arts. 1.2 a, 1.3, new 3.7, 4.5, 5.1, 10.1; Code 15, Art. 21.1). Phase 0 belongs to each pilot Circle, Phase 1 to each Basin, Phases 2–4 to each minimum Federation; whoever arrives later starts from Phase 0 and enters the phase of the group when it reaches its thresholds; each scale has its own Transition Council. Reason: it was not said at what scale the phases apply.\nCode 3, Art. 3.2, letter a). For low-risk allocative decisions the body of the matrix of Art. 7 decides: the Competence Circle if the value is low, the Quality Council with the Competence Circle if it is medium. Reason: a pure textual inconsistency with the matrix, found during the work (first part of item C61, low severity)."],[32,"20-october-2026--medium-severity-items-of-the-consistency-review-c8c28","20 October 2026 — medium-severity items of the consistency review (C8–C28)","","Reason: These are the corrections proposed by the consistency review for the medium-severity items, with the author’s choices on C20, C22 (option B), C27 (option A), on the precedence of care (it remains after the ordinary delivery of the Basket, except for the swap with perishable goods), with no addition on C8 and no separate expiry on C11. The amendments to the Constitution (Arts. 3.3, 6.6 and 19.4) do not touch the Inviolable Core and count as the text of the first adoption (Const. 38.1).\nVerification: consistency review, medium-severity items.\nVersions: Constitution from V.31 to V.32, Code 1 from V1.7 to V1.8, C.1-Annex A from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 2 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 3 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 4 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 5 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 6 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 7 from V1.7 to V1.8, Code 8 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 9 from V3.1 to V3.2, Code 10 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 11 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 12 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 13 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 14 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 15 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 16 from V1.4 to V1.5, Annex C to Code 16 from V1.0 to V1.1."],[32,"20-october-2026--constitution-new-art-179-code-14-art-1-para-1-and-new-art-8-sexies-code-4-art-25-para-1-art-26-para-3-new-art-26-bis-personal-defence","20 October 2026 — Constitution, new Art. 17.9; Code 14, Art. 1, para. 1, and new Art. 8-sexies; Code 4, Art. 25, para. 1; Art. 26, para. 3; new Art. 26-bis (personal defence)","","Personal defence. Every person may defend themselves, other persons and animals without weapons, by getting away, bringing to safety, interposing themselves and, if that is not enough, with only the force strictly necessary and proportionate to end the danger. This applies against an ongoing or imminent physical or sexual aggression, where the danger is concrete and immediate and not merely feared, and against ongoing ill-treatment that injures an animal or makes it suffer; disagreements on how an animal is cared for or trained do not justify force and go to the Biodiversity Council. Objects found on the spot may be used only where there is danger to life or of serious injury and only to stop the aggression. Attack, revenge, punishment, force after the danger has ceased, force on persons to protect property alone and force to correct, educate or hold back someone one lives with are not defence. A person may enter someone else’s Circle 0 without consent only to stop ongoing physical or sexual violence, notifying the Civic Protection Corps at once, leaving as soon as the danger has ceased and subject to review by the Guarantee Tribunal within twenty-four hours. After the fact, the person gives first aid and notifies the Corps or the Guarantee Circle; the Guarantee Tribunal makes the assessment. An error due to excusable fear is not an offence; excess is Grade 4, or Grade 2 if the harm is slight; for minors only restorative and educational measures apply. During the transition the law of the State applies, and those who have joined undertake to stay within the narrower limits of these rules.\nReason: The texts entrusted protection to the Civic Protection Corps alone and required an “unarmed and non-violent” defence, but did not say whether and how a person may defend themselves or others when the Corps is not there. The new Art. 17.9 is in Art. 17 and not in Art. 16, which is part of the Inviolable Core; Const. 5.1, letter a), also part of the Core, does not change: entry into a Circle 0 uses the exception it already provides “in accordance with Title VIII”, implemented by Code 4, Art. 26-bis.\nVersions: Constitution from V.30 to V.31, Code 4 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 14 from V1.3 to V1.4. The amendments to the Constitution count as the text of the first adoption (Const. 38.1)."],[32,"19-october-2026--constitution-arts-13-22-23-49-bis-and-71-new-art-73-bis-art-374-letter-a-code-4-art-13-para-6-art-25-paras-1-and-6-art-29-paras-1-and-6-and-new-paras-4-bis-and-6-bis-code-1-art-13-para-3-art-18-para-1-letter-a-art-44-para-6-items-c1-and-c1-bis","19 October 2026 — Constitution, Arts. 1.3, 2.2, 2.3, 4.9-bis and 7.1, new Art. 7.3-bis, Art. 37.4, letter a); Code 4, Art. 13, para. 6; Art. 25, paras. 1 and 6; Art. 29, paras. 1 and 6, and new paras. 4-bis and 6-bis; Code 1, Art. 13, para. 3; Art. 18, para. 1, letter a); Art. 44, para. 6 (items C1 and C1-bis)","","Refusal of the CBO. Before Grade 3 the Clarification Assembly hears the person, considers other tasks, proposes a mediation and informs the person of the Autonomous Coexistence Pact; only if, after at least fourteen days, all this has had no result may it suspend the CdR and access to non-essential resources, for a limited time and with an appeal to the Review Panel, without touching the Basket, housing, care or the vote. If the refusal continues without justification even after the Grade 3 Transformation Plan, the Guarantee Tribunal may declare, by six votes out of seven, the cessation of member status: the person receives only a portion of land with water, without a dwelling and without goods of the Community, keeps drinking water and life-saving emergency care and may return at any time by resuming the CBO. The children remain members with all rights and the Tribunal decides with whom they live. Illness, disability, age, pregnancy and conscientious objection are never refusal.\nChange to the Inviolable Core (C1-bis). Articles 1.3, 2.2, 2.3, 4.9-bis and 37.4, letter a), of the Constitution belong to the Inviolable Core or are bound up with it: 1.3 names the new Article 7.3-bis among the exceptions to the guarantee of rights; 2.2 and 2.3 say that the CBO is not the price of individual goods, but the contribution owed as a condition of membership; 4.9-bis says that in this case the land is given without a dwelling; 37.4 a) prohibits extending the exception. Drinking water, life-saving emergency care and the protection of minors remain for anyone. The Core cannot be revised (Constitution 37.4 and 38.4): this is not a revision, but a rewriting of the text by the author before any accession, possible because the Constitution is not yet in force for any Community (Constitution 38.1). After the first accession it could no longer be done. Why: the author chose to change the Core openly instead of resting the cessation on an indirect reference to Article 4.8, which would have left the new Article 7.3-bis in conflict with the words of 1.3, of 2.3 (“the CBO is not individual consideration for the rights”) and of 4.9-bis.\nReason: The Constitution allowed the CdR to be suspended before Grade 3, but Code 4 prohibited it and no text said who decides, how long the suspension lasts or how to appeal. The author also decided that persistent refusal after Grade 3 leads to the cessation of member status, with land and water only.\nVerification: consistency review, item C1."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-10-new-art-4-bis-art-4-para-1-new-letter-i-art-10-para-3-new-letter-f-code-4-new-art-32-bis-and-art-37-para-1-code-11-art-6-ter-para-4-item-c2","19 October 2026 — Code 10, new Art. 4-bis; Art. 4, para. 1, new letter i); Art. 10, para. 3, new letter f); Code 4, new Art. 32-bis and Art. 37, para. 1; Code 11, Art. 6-ter, para. 4 (item C2)","","Autonomous Coexistence Pact. The new Article 4-bis of Code 10 says who concludes the Pact, what it contains, how carrying capacity is documented and how to appeal. Every Circle 1 reserves for the Pact a share of land for at least one person for every hundred residents; if there is no room, Circle 2 and then Circle 3 look for it, and meanwhile the person is received even beyond fourteen days. The new Article 32-bis of Code 4 replaces, for those who live by means of the Pact, the measures reserved for members with the revision of the Pact, and sets out the non-violent procedure for removal from the common settlement provided for by Constitution 4.9-ter.\nReason: The Pact is a right of the Inviolable Core, but no Code set out its content, body, land and procedures, and Code 4 applied loss of status to those who are not members.\nVerification: consistency review, item C2."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-14-art-8-ter-para-3-code-12-art-7-bis-para-3-and-new-paras-3-bis-and-3-ter-code-4-art-27-para-6-item-c3","19 October 2026 — Code 14, Art. 8-ter, para. 3; Code 12, Art. 7-bis, para. 3, and new paras. 3-bis and 3-ter; Code 4, Art. 27, para. 6 (item C3)","","Circle 0 and the protection of minors. The Civic Protection Corps may enter a Circle 0 also to stop physical or sexual violence in progress, with validation by the Guarantee Tribunal within twenty-four hours, or with a reasoned authorisation of the Tribunal. A minor may be separated from their Circle 0 even without an immediate danger, by decision of the Guarantee Circle on a proposal from the Care Council, validated within twenty-four hours. The Tribunal takes the stable decision on whom the minor lives with within thirty days, which may be extended once only by a further thirty, after hearing the minor, and reviews it at least every six months.\nReason: The Corps could enter only where there was a danger to life, and after the separation was validated no text said who decides where the minor lives and for how long.\nVerification: consistency review, item C3."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-4-art-25-para-9-art-37-para-3-item-c4","19 October 2026 — Code 4, Art. 25, para. 9; Art. 37, para. 3 (item C4)","","Minors. A person who has not reached eighteen may never be placed in protective containment. If they are dangerous to others, the Guarantee Tribunal, by the final decision on a fact of Grade 4, 5 or 6 and by six votes out of seven, may order protected educational reception, open and without surveillance, for no more than twelve months, renewable once and reviewed at least every three months.\nReason: Code 4 excluded Articles 31, 32 and 33 for minors, but not the protective containment of Article 30, paragraph 3.\nVerification: consistency review, item C4."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-6-art-2-para-2-art-2-bis-new-paras-3-bis-3-ter-3-quater-and-3-quinquies-para-4-letter-d-and-para-7-art-11-para-2-letter-d-and-para-5-letter-d-code-13-art-7-para-10-code-14-art-8-ter-para-4-item-c5","19 October 2026 — Code 6, Art. 2, para. 2; Art. 2-bis, new paras. 3-bis, 3-ter, 3-quater and 3-quinquies, para. 4, letter d), and para. 7; Art. 11, para. 2, letter d), and para. 5, letter d); Code 13, Art. 7, para. 10; Code 14, Art. 8-ter, para. 4 (item C5)","","Epidemics. No treatment, test or vaccination may be performed on a person’s body without their consent. Those who refuse the protection indicated may be excluded only from the tasks and places at risk and receive another task; they keep the Basket, housing, care and the vote. Isolation and quarantine last no more than fourteen days, which may be extended once; if the person does not agree, the Guarantee Tribunal validates them within twenty-four hours. The list of permitted collective measures is closed.\nReason: Code 6 made an exception to consent for the measures of Article 2-bis without saying which measures were permitted. The author decided that no health treatment may be imposed.\nVerification: consistency review, item C5."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-6-art-8-para-6-letter-c-and-paras-7-and-10-code-12-art-9-para-1-letter-g-item-c6","19 October 2026 — Code 6, Art. 8, para. 6, letter c), and paras. 7 and 10; Code 12, Art. 9, para. 1, letter g) (item C6)","","Psychological crisis. Every coercive measure must be validated by the Guarantee Tribunal within twenty-four hours, otherwise it ceases; the Assembly no longer decides. It lasts no more than seventy-two hours at a time and fourteen days in all, is reassessed by the health persons at fixed intervals and may be revoked at any time on request. A new measure is possible only for a new episode, with other health persons and a Tribunal in a different composition, and never beyond twenty-eight days in ninety. The representative may not consent to a coercive measure.\nReason: The review lay with the Guarantee Circle, beyond twenty-four hours a resolution of the Assembly was required and there was no maximum duration.\nVerification: consistency review, item C6."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-6-new-art-2-ter-art-10-para-7-letter-a-code-12-art-7-bis-para-5-item-c7","19 October 2026 — Code 6, new Art. 2-ter; Art. 10, para. 7, letter a); Code 12, Art. 7-bis, para. 5 (item C7)","","Consent to care. From sixteen, people decide on their own care themselves; before that, those who hold responsibility for care decide, after hearing the minor, whose opinion weighs more with age. In the event of disagreement or conflict of interest the Guarantee Tribunal decides, with the opinion of an independent health person. Vaccinations of minors are never mandatory. For an adult who cannot decide for more than sixty days, the Tribunal appoints a stable representative, reviewed every year.\nReason: For the ordinary care of minors no text said who gives consent, and the temporary representative of Code 12 was renewed every thirty days without limit.\nVerification: consistency review, item C7."],[32,"19-october-2026--constitution-art-313-letter-c-code-4-art-27-para-3-art-37-new-para-5","19 October 2026 — Constitution, Art. 31.3, letter c); Code 4, Art. 27, para. 3; Art. 37, new para. 5","","Guarantee Tribunal. The Constitution assigns to it, besides offences of Grade 4, 5 and 6 and urgent review, “the other guarantee functions assigned by the Codes”, and Code 4 is aligned. In Phase 0, until the members can be drawn by lot in the Basin, the functions of the Tribunal, of the Review Panel and of the Transformation Council are performed by a panel drawn by lot from trained persons of other pilot Circles or of kindred entities or, failing that, from members unconnected with the facts, which may not order the most serious measures; if no panel is formed in time, the measure ceases.\nReason: The changes of items C1–C7 give the Tribunal new tasks (custody of minors, consent to care, stable representative, validation of health measures, cessation of status) that Constitution 31.3 c) did not name, and in Phase 0 the Tribunal could not be formed. Article 31.3 is not part of the Inviolable Core.\nVerification: consistency review, items C1–C7 and C9.\nVersions: Constitution from V.29 to V.30, Code 1 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 4 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 6 from V1.5 to V1.6, Code 10 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 11 from V1.3 to V1.4, Code 12 from V1.3 to V1.4, Code 13 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 14 from V1.2 to V1.3. The changes to the Constitution count as the text of the first adoption (Constitution 38.1)."],[32,"19-october-2026--c1-annex-a-art-4-paras-1-and-3-and-new-para-1-bis-code-1-art-32-new-para-1-bis-proposal-1","19 October 2026 — C.1-Annex A, Art. 4, paras. 1 and 3, and new para. 1-bis; Code 1, Art. 32, new para. 1-bis (proposal 1)","","Clothing and hygiene in the Material Basket. The accepted text is that of proposal 1, version 2.\nReason: Clothing and hygiene products are necessary for everyone, and no Basket named them.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 9)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-1-new-art-47-bis-proposal-2","19 October 2026 — Code 1, new Art. 47-bis (proposal 2)","","Standard of adequate dwelling. The accepted text is that of proposal 2, version 4.\nReason: The Constitution guarantees housing, but no text said what makes a dwelling adequate.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 10)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-7-art-3-new-para-9-code-5-art-2-new-para-4-bis-proposal-3","19 October 2026 — Code 7, Art. 3, new para. 9; Code 5, Art. 2, new para. 4-bis (proposal 3)","","Connectivity and learning materials. The accepted text is that of proposal 3, version 2.\nReason: Connectivity is a right, but Code 7 did not say with what it is exercised.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 11)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-8-art-4-para-4-proposal-4","19 October 2026 — Code 8, Art. 4, para. 4 (proposal 4)","","First-application value of the Energy Basket. The accepted text is that of proposal 4, version 2.\nReason: The Energy Basket listed the guaranteed services without any minimum value.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 12)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-1-art-43-new-para-1-bis-proposal-5","19 October 2026 — Code 1, Art. 43, new para. 1-bis (proposal 5)","","CdR for cultural activities offered to the Community. The accepted text is that of proposal 5, version 2.\nReason: What a person offers to everyone is a contribution; counting by hours and the annual limit keep it from becoming a ranking.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 13)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-10-art-1-paras-1-2-and-3-art-9-para-1-letter-a-proposal-6","19 October 2026 — Code 10, Art. 1, paras. 1, 2 and 3; Art. 9, para. 1, letter a) (proposal 6)","","Subsoil as a common good. The accepted text is that of proposal 6, version 2.\nReason: No text named the subsoil and deposits; now no rent can be drawn from them.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 14)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-10-new-art-8-bis-and-arts-3-and-7-code-15-art-12-new-para-5-bis-proposal-7","19 October 2026 — Code 10, new Art. 8-bis and Arts. 3 and 7; Code 15, Art. 12, new para. 5-bis (proposal 7)","","Raw materials: recovery and inventory. The accepted text is that of proposal 7, version 2.\nReason: Before asking whether to dig, one must know what can be recovered; the Article does not authorise new extraction. Code 10, Arts. 3 and 7 (inventory and access to the Land Register) aligned.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 15)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-7-art-1-para-2-new-letter-f-and-new-art-6-bis-code-1-art-8-para-3-proposal-8","19 October 2026 — Code 7, Art. 1, para. 2, new letter f), and new Art. 6-bis; Code 1, Art. 8, para. 3 (proposal 8)","","Robots and autonomous machines. The accepted text is that of proposal 8, version 2.\nReason: No text governed machines that act on their own among people. Code 1, Art. 8, para. 3 aligned.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 16)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-1-art-44-para-3-art-45-paras-2-and-3-proposal-9","19 October 2026 — Code 1, Art. 44, para. 3; Art. 45, paras. 2 and 3 (proposal 9)","","Decay of the CdR. The accepted text is that of proposal 9, version 2.\nReason: The text did not say how the value of CdR decreases; the gradual reduction respects both the twelve months and the twenty-fourth month.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 17)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-1-art-44-para-2-proposal-10","19 October 2026 — Code 1, Art. 44, para. 2 (proposal 10)","","CdR cap and supplements. The accepted text is that of proposal 10, version 2.\nReason: The text did not say whether the cap also applies to supplements, nor how the balance is measured.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 18)."],[32,"19-october-2026--code-3-art-7-para-3-and-new-paras-3-bis-and-3-ter-art-7-bis-para-1-art-21-para-3-proposal-11","19 October 2026 — Code 3, Art. 7, para. 3 and new paras. 3-bis and 3-ter; Art. 7-bis, para. 1; Art. 21, para. 3 (proposal 11)","","Economic thresholds in proportion to the size of the Community. The accepted text is that of proposal 11, version 2.\nReason: The same fixed thresholds weighed very differently in a Community of thirty people and in one of five hundred. Art. 21, para. 3 aligned.\nDiscussion: no comments received by 19 October 2026 (issue 19).\nVersions: Code 1 from V1.5 to V1.6, C.1-Annex A from V1.3 to V1.4, Code 3 from V1.4 to V1.5, Code 5 from V1.3 to V1.4, Code 7 from V1.6 to V1.7, Code 8 from V1.3 to V1.4, Code 10 from V1.3 to V1.4, Code 15 from V1.3 to V1.4. The Constitution remains at V.29. The proposals had been open since 6 October 2026 (proposal 2 since 19 October); when nobody answers, the register says so instead of writing “approved after discussion”."],[32,"5-october-2026--constitution-art-65-material-correction","5 October 2026 — Constitution, Art. 6.5 (material correction)","","“In Communities with fewer than one hundred and fifty persons” becomes “In Communities with fewer than one hundred and fifty persons entitled to vote”.\nReason: for the same threshold Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 12, counts those entitled to vote, while the Constitution counted persons. The measure of the Code has been kept, because the Councils are made up of persons drawn by lot from among those entitled to vote: it is their number that tells whether a Community can fill them. The version remains V.29."],[32,"how-the-rules-change","How the rules change","","From 19 October 2026 verified changes are applied directly and recorded on this page with the date, the text and the reason. The eleven proposals published between 5 and 19 October have been accepted and can still be consulted in the archive of proposals. Anyone can report a problem or propose a change: see How to contribute."],[32,"parameters-to-be-confirmed","Parameters to be confirmed","","These are numbers proposed in order to make the rules applicable. They may all be modified by ordinary procedure. Data and experience that confirm or refute them are welcome. The full register, with the origin of each value, its uncertainty and the test needed to confirm or correct it, is on the page Parameters to be verified. It is a calibration draft, not adopted, and changes no rule.\nParameter · Proposed value · Location\nSize of the Base Community · as a rule 30–500 persons · Const. 5.1\nQuorum of the Assembly · 1/3 of those entitled to vote; 1/2 for decisions with special guarantees (Art. 7-quater) · Code 3, Art. 12\nTechnical Councils · 3–5 persons, one year, staggered renewal, may not be drawn by lot again before 12 months · Const. 6.5; Code 3, Art. 12-bis\nGuarantee Circle · 3–7 persons, 90 days (maximum 180 in the year) · Code 4, Art. 4\nCap on the CdR · 240 CdR · Code 1, Art. 44\nDecay of the CdR · full value for 12 months, then one twelfth per month down to zero at 24 months · Code 1, Art. 44\nClarification Assembly / Transformation Council / Guarantee Tribunal · 7 / 5 / 7 persons; 6 votes out of 7 for the extreme measures · Code 4, Art. 27\nProtective containment · maximum 5 years (Grade 5) and 10 years (Grade 6), with review at least every six months · Code 4, Art. 30, para. 3\nPrecautionary measures · validation within 24 hours · Code 4, Art. 34\nWater Basket · at least 50 litres per person per day · Code 8, Art. 4-bis\nFounding regime · 2 Communities, 6 months, vote within 30 days, 2/3 of the persons and 2/3 of the Communities, ratification within 24 months · Const. 38\nReserve of the Common Fund · no more than 12 months of external requirement · Code 15, Art. 17\nThreshold of the small Community · fewer than 150 persons entitled to vote · Const. 6.5; Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 12\nArea Councils · three, each of 3–7 persons; pause between terms of office reduced to 6 months · Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 12\nAdministrative time · no more than 1/10 of the total CBO, on a quarterly basis · Const. 6.5; Code 3, Art. 12-bis, para. 13\nContribution on external income · 1/10 of net income on first application, never more than 1/5 · Code 15, Art. 19-bis\nDeclaration of external income · by brackets, confidential, kept for 12 months · Code 15, Art. 19-bis\nChoice of accession · within 12 months of reaching 18 years of age; silence counts as accession · Const. 26.4\nCritical functions · at least 3 trained persons · Code 15, Art. 12, para. 7\nReview of privileged access · quarterly, 3 persons drawn by lot · Code 7, Art. 8, para. 8\nAssembly in the first year · as a rule at least one per month · Annex C, Art. 4\nThe verification on concrete cases required by Codes 3 and 4 also remains to be carried out."]],"domande":[{"q":"What is the Circles Project?","a":"d-what-is-the-circles-project","k":["what is it","project","confederation","circles","proposal","summary","overview","in short","about"],"x":"A proposal for organising life in common starting from people's needs. It is written out in full, article by article: a Constitution and the Codes that put it into practice, with their technical annexes. It is not in force anywhere.","h":"<p>A proposal for organising life in common starting from people&#x27;s needs. It is written out in full, article by article: a Constitution and the Codes that put it into practice, with their technical annexes. It is not in force anywhere.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/index.html\">The register of texts</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-0.1\">Const. 0.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-0.2\">Const. 0.2</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-0.1","register/constitution.html#c-0.2"]},{"q":"Why this project?","a":"d-why-this-project","k":["why","reason","purpose","aim","problem","system","crisis","inequality","future","what for"],"x":"The system we live in produces a great deal, but does not turn that wealth proportionately into security, time and capacity for choice for people. Those who decide, those who benefit and those who pay are more and more often different people, and the present is financed by transferring costs onto the future. That is why correcting the effects is not enough: the conditions that produce them must change.","h":"<p>The system we live in produces a great deal, but does not turn that wealth proportionately into security, time and capacity for choice for people. Those who decide, those who benefit and those who pay are more and more often different people, and the present is financed by transferring costs onto the future. That is why correcting the effects is not enough: the conditions that produce them must change.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"path/3-the-system-against-the-individual.html\">The system against the individual</a> · <a href=\"path/2-analysis-of-the-present.html\">Analysis of the present</a></p>","r":[]},{"q":"Is it communism?","a":"d-is-it-communism","k":["communism","communist","socialism","anarchy","anarchist","ideology","left","right","politics","utopia","marxism","capitalism"],"x":"There is no State, no party and no central power. People join by choice and can leave whenever they wish; those who grow up in it choose at eighteen. What is personal stays personal, and those who contribute more are recognised. The project does not ask anyone to sign up to a label: it asks to be judged on its rules.","h":"<p>There is no State, no party and no central power. People join by choice and can leave whenever they wish; those who grow up in it choose at eighteen. What is personal stays personal, and those who contribute more are recognised. The project does not ask anyone to sign up to a label: it asks to be judged on its rules.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-0.2\">Const. 0.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-38.1\">Const. 38.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.8\">Const. 4.8</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-26.4\">Const. 26.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.3\">Const. 10.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-8.1\">Const. 8.1</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-0.2","register/constitution.html#c-38.1","register/constitution.html#c-4.8","register/constitution.html#c-26.4","register/constitution.html#c-10.3","register/constitution.html#c-8.1"]},{"q":"How is it built?","a":"d-how-is-it-built","k":["structure","organisation","organization","circles","nucleus","family","community","levels","federation","how it works"],"x":"Everyone lives in a Care Nucleus (Circle 0), which the Community does not enter except to protect someone. Nuclei form Base Communities, as a rule of 30 to 500 people, which decide in assembly: everyone votes for themselves. Communities federate into wider circles, which coordinate and do not command. Roles are assigned by lot among volunteers and are of limited duration.","h":"<p>Everyone lives in a Care Nucleus (Circle 0), which the Community does not enter except to protect someone. Nuclei form Base Communities, as a rule of 30 to 500 people, which decide in assembly: everyone votes for themselves. Communities federate into wider circles, which coordinate and do not command. Roles are assigned by lot among volunteers and are of limited duration.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-5.1\">Const. 5.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.2\">Const. 4.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.3\">Const. 4.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-6.5\">Const. 6.5</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.4\">Const. 4.4</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-5.1","register/constitution.html#c-4.2","register/constitution.html#c-4.3","register/constitution.html#c-6.5","register/constitution.html#c-4.4"]},{"q":"What does it guarantee?","a":"d-what-does-it-guarantee","k":["rights","guarantees","income","free","needs","basket","sufficiency","minimum","subsistence","welfare","housing","food"],"x":"To every member a home, food, water, energy, health care, education and connectivity. These are not payment for work: goods are taken from the Common Storehouses according to need, and no money circulates among members. What is personal stays personal.","h":"<p>To every member a home, food, water, energy, health care, education and connectivity. These are not payment for work: goods are taken from the Common Storehouses according to need, and no money circulates among members. What is personal stays personal.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-1.2\">Const. 1.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-1.3\">Const. 1.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.1\">Const. 3.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.4\">Const. 3.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5\">Const. 3.5</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5-bis\">Const. 3.5-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.3\">Const. 10.3</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-1.2","register/constitution.html#c-1.3","register/constitution.html#c-3.1","register/constitution.html#c-3.4","register/constitution.html#c-3.5","register/constitution.html#c-3.5-bis","register/constitution.html#c-10.3"]},{"q":"What does it ask?","a":"d-what-does-it-ask","k":["duties","obligations","give up","what must I do","conditions","rules","commitments","sacrifices"],"x":"Of every adult who is able, a rotating share of the work everyone needs: on average no more than fifteen hours a week. And giving up private ownership of land and of the means of production, accumulation, weapons and the exploitation of animals. Anyone who does not agree can leave whenever they wish.","h":"<p>Of every adult who is able, a rotating share of the work everyone needs: on average no more than fifteen hours a week. And giving up private ownership of land and of the means of production, accumulation, weapons and the exploitation of animals. Anyone who does not agree can leave whenever they wish.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-7.1\">Const. 7.1</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-3--legal-nature-and-maximum-limit-of-the-cbo\">Code 1, art. 3, para. 2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.1\">Const. 3.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.1\">Const. 10.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.4\">Const. 10.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-16.1\">Const. 16.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-13.1\">Const. 13.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.8\">Const. 4.8</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-7.1","register/code-01.html#art-3--legal-nature-and-maximum-limit-of-the-cbo","register/constitution.html#c-3.1","register/constitution.html#c-10.1","register/constitution.html#c-10.4","register/constitution.html#c-16.1","register/constitution.html#c-13.1","register/constitution.html#c-4.8"]},{"q":"Does it work? Has it been tried?","a":"d-does-it-work-has-it-been-tried","k":["does it work","tried","tested","experiments","examples","realistic","utopia","evidence","results","proven","exists"],"x":"Whether it works: no community has tried it yet, and many numbers are hypotheses still to be checked. That is why everything is public and the rules can be corrected.","h":"<p>Whether it works: no community has tried it yet, and many numbers are hypotheses still to be checked. That is why everything is public and the rules can be corrected.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/parameters.html\">Register of parameters to be verified</a> · <a href=\"contributing.html\">How to contribute</a></p>","r":[]},{"q":"Who decides? Is there a government or a leader?","a":"d-who-decides-is-there-a-government-or-a-leader","k":["government","leader","boss","president","who is in charge","power","assembly","politicians","elections","lot","sortition","direct democracy","parliament"],"x":"According to the Constitution there is no government and no leader. The assembly of each Base Community decides, and every person votes for themselves. The wider circles coordinate and do not command. Coordination roles are assigned by lot among volunteers, last a limited time and cannot be held twice in a row.","h":"<p>According to the Constitution there is no government and no leader. The assembly of each Base Community decides, and every person votes for themselves. The wider circles coordinate and do not command. Coordination roles are assigned by lot among volunteers, last a limited time and cannot be held twice in a row.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.1\">Const. 4.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.2\">Const. 4.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.3\">Const. 4.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-5.1\">Const. 5.1</a> · <a href=\"register/code-03.html#art-12--assembly-of-circle-1-composition-participation-and-quorum\">Code 3, art. 12, para. 1</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-4.1","register/constitution.html#c-4.2","register/constitution.html#c-4.3","register/constitution.html#c-5.1","register/code-03.html#art-12--assembly-of-circle-1-composition-participation-and-quorum"]},{"q":"Who can vote, and how?","a":"d-who-can-vote-and-how","k":["vote","voting","elections","majority","assembly","consensus","voting age","minors","online voting","quorum","referendum","proxy"],"x":"Every person from the age of fourteen votes, and the vote does not depend on the Recognition Contribution (CdR). It cannot be delegated, bought or exchanged. Consensus is sought first; if it is not reached after two meetings, a vote is taken, and for the ordinary decisions of the Community a simple majority of those taking part is enough. People take part in person or through the Network, and each vote is anonymous.","h":"<p>Every person from the age of fourteen votes, and the vote does not depend on the Recognition Contribution (CdR). It cannot be delegated, bought or exchanged. Consensus is sought first; if it is not reached after two meetings, a vote is taken, and for the ordinary decisions of the Community a simple majority of those taking part is enough. People take part in person or through the Network, and each vote is anonymous.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-36.1\">Const. 36.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-34.2\">Const. 34.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-35.1\">Const. 35.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-34.4\">Const. 34.4</a> · <a href=\"register/code-03.html#art-12--assembly-of-circle-1-composition-participation-and-quorum\">Code 3, art. 12</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-36.1","register/constitution.html#c-34.2","register/constitution.html#c-35.1","register/constitution.html#c-34.4","register/code-03.html#art-12--assembly-of-circle-1-composition-participation-and-quorum"]},{"q":"What stops a group from taking power?","a":"d-what-stops-a-group-from-taking-power","k":["power","dictatorship","leaders","clique","abuse","coup","parties","manipulation","charismatic leader","checks","corruption","cult"],"x":"The texts provide these obstacles. Coordination roles are assigned by lot among volunteers and last no more than ninety days in a row; standing as a candidate is forbidden. Parties and stable groups that accumulate mandates, resources or consensus are forbidden. The vote cannot be delegated or bought, and the Assembly can dismiss the members of the Councils at any time. Nobody has yet tested whether this is enough.","h":"<p>The texts provide these obstacles. Coordination roles are assigned by lot among volunteers and last no more than ninety days in a row; standing as a candidate is forbidden. Parties and stable groups that accumulate mandates, resources or consensus are forbidden. The vote cannot be delegated or bought, and the Assembly can dismiss the members of the Councils at any time. Nobody has yet tested whether this is enough.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.4\">Const. 4.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-36.1\">Const. 36.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-6.5\">Const. 6.5</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-4.4","register/constitution.html#c-36.1","register/constitution.html#c-6.5"]},{"q":"Can the Constitution be changed?","a":"d-can-the-constitution-be-changed","k":["change the rules","amend the constitution","revision","amendments","referendum","inviolable core","fixed rules","untouchable","reform","majority","dogma"],"x":"Yes, by a long procedure. Only a Community that has applied the rule for at least twenty-four months can propose a change; then come twelve months of trial in at least five Communities and a vote with two thirds of the Communities and two thirds of the voters. Shorter times apply in the founding phase. The Inviolable Core can never be changed: among other things the rights of the person, the commons, disarmament and the right to leave.","h":"<p>Yes, by a long procedure. Only a Community that has applied the rule for at least twenty-four months can propose a change; then come twelve months of trial in at least five Communities and a vote with two thirds of the Communities and two thirds of the voters. Shorter times apply in the founding phase. The Inviolable Core can never be changed: among other things the rights of the person, the commons, disarmament and the right to leave.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-37.1\">Const. 37.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-37.2\">Const. 37.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-37.3\">Const. 37.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-37.4\">Const. 37.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-38.2\">Const. 38.2</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-37.1","register/constitution.html#c-37.2","register/constitution.html#c-37.3","register/constitution.html#c-37.4","register/constitution.html#c-38.2"]},{"q":"Do I have to work? How many hours?","a":"d-do-i-have-to-work-how-many-hours","k":["compulsory work","working hours","how much work","shifts","obligation","CBO","base contribution","fifteen hours","working week","salary","job"],"x":"Yes: the texts ask every adult who is able for a Mandatory Base Contribution (CBO), a share of the work everyone needs. The written limit is an average of fifteen hours a week, calculated over the quarter, and there is no minimum. The texts themselves provide for checking this limit against real data. A home, food and care are not payment for this work, but someone who refuses it for a long time without a recognised reason can lose member status.","h":"<p>Yes: the texts ask every adult who is able for a Mandatory Base Contribution (CBO), a share of the work everyone needs. The written limit is an average of fifteen hours a week, calculated over the quarter, and there is no minimum. The texts themselves provide for checking this limit against real data. A home, food and care are not payment for this work, but someone who refuses it for a long time without a recognised reason can lose member status.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-7.1\">Const. 7.1</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-3--legal-nature-and-maximum-limit-of-the-cbo\">Code 1, art. 3, para. 2</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-6--quantitative-determination-and-apportionment-of-hours\">Code 1, art. 6</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-2--precedence-of-need-over-task\">Code 1, art. 2, para. 9</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-7.3-bis\">Const. 7.3-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-7.1","register/code-01.html#art-3--legal-nature-and-maximum-limit-of-the-cbo","register/code-01.html#art-6--quantitative-determination-and-apportionment-of-hours","register/code-01.html#art-2--precedence-of-need-over-task","register/constitution.html#c-7.3-bis"]},{"q":"What happens to someone who does not do their share of the work?","a":"d-what-happens-to-someone-who-does-not-do-their-share-of-the-w","k":["does not work","refusal","slackers","free riders","punishment","sanctions","not contributing","lazy","expulsion","conscientious objection","losing membership","cessation","land"],"x":"The texts provide for gradual steps. First an assembly chosen by lot hears the person, tries changing the tasks and mediation, and informs the person of the right to an Autonomous Coexistence Pact. If after at least fourteen days that is not enough, the Recognition Contribution (CdR) and non-essential goods can be suspended, but not housing, food, water, care and the vote. After sixty days of refusal without justification, a plan lasting up to twelve months is agreed with the person. If the refusal continues even after the plan, a tribunal chosen by lot can declare that the person is no longer a member: they receive only a piece of land with water, without a house, keep drinking water and emergency care and can come back at any time by resuming the contribution. The children remain members with all their rights. Illness, disability, age, pregnancy and conscientious objection are never refusal.","h":"<p>The texts provide for gradual steps. First an assembly chosen by lot hears the person, tries changing the tasks and mediation, and informs the person of the right to an Autonomous Coexistence Pact. If after at least fourteen days that is not enough, the Recognition Contribution (CdR) and non-essential goods can be suspended, but not housing, food, water, care and the vote. After sixty days of refusal without justification, a plan lasting up to twelve months is agreed with the person. If the refusal continues even after the plan, a tribunal chosen by lot can declare that the person is no longer a member: they receive only a piece of land with water, without a house, keep drinking water and emergency care and can come back at any time by resuming the contribution. The children remain members with all their rights. Illness, disability, age, pregnancy and conscientious objection are never refusal.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-7.3\">Const. 7.3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4\">Code 4, art. 29, para. 4-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4\">Code 4, art. 29, para. 2</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4\">Code 4, art. 29, para. 3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-7.3-bis\">Const. 7.3-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4\">Code 4, art. 29, para. 6-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-7.3","register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4","register/constitution.html#c-7.3-bis","register/code-04.html#art-29--measures-for-grades-1-to-4"]},{"q":"What do those who cannot work receive?","a":"d-what-do-those-who-cannot-work-receive","k":["children","elderly","sick","disabled","pension","disability","maternity","kids","illness","assistance","exemption","old age"],"x":"Housing, food, water, energy, care and education are guaranteed to every member without conditions. The texts exempt from the Base Contribution people over seventy-five, people with a severe disability and mothers up to twelve months after the birth. No compulsory work is assigned up to the age of fourteen. In case of illness the contribution is suspended, and the hours not worked do not become a debt.","h":"<p>Housing, food, water, energy, care and education are guaranteed to every member without conditions. The texts exempt from the Base Contribution people over seventy-five, people with a severe disability and mothers up to twelve months after the birth. No compulsory work is assigned up to the age of fourteen. In case of illness the contribution is suspended, and the hours not worked do not become a debt.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-1.3\">Const. 1.3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-11--exemptions-reductions-suspensions-and-solidarity\">Code 1, art. 11</a> · <a href=\"register/code-05.html#art-6--minors-and-learning\">Code 5, art. 6, para. 2</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-1.3","register/code-01.html#art-11--exemptions-reductions-suspensions-and-solidarity","register/code-05.html#art-6--minors-and-learning"]},{"q":"Is there money? How do people get food and goods?","a":"d-is-there-money-how-do-people-get-food-and-goods","k":["money","cash","currency","salary","pay","prices","shops","shopping","free food","storehouses","buy","bank"],"x":"The Constitution abolishes money among members. Food and necessary goods are taken from the Common Storehouses according to need and availability, without paying, and they do not depend on the work done. Goods that are not necessary and are scarce are obtained by booking or a waiting list. As long as an outside world exists, money is used only in dealings with the outside, and it is held by a Common Fund.","h":"<p>The Constitution abolishes money among members. Food and necessary goods are taken from the Common Storehouses according to need and availability, without paying, and they do not depend on the work done. Goods that are not necessary and are scarce are obtained by booking or a waiting list. As long as an outside world exists, money is used only in dealings with the outside, and it is held by a Common Fund.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5\">Const. 3.5</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.4\">Const. 3.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5-bis\">Const. 3.5-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-18--access-and-distribution\">Code 1, art. 18</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-3.5","register/constitution.html#c-3.4","register/constitution.html#c-3.5-bis","register/code-01.html#art-18--access-and-distribution"]},{"q":"What is the Recognition Contribution (CdR)? Is it a currency?","a":"d-what-is-the-recognition-contribution-cdr-is-it-a-currency","k":["CdR","credits","points","currency","reward","merit","earn more","incentives","gratitude","savings","wealth"],"x":"The Recognition Contribution (CdR) recognises work done beyond the Base Contribution: one validated hour is worth one CdR. For the texts it is not a currency: it cannot be passed on or inherited, it has a cap and it shrinks over time. It only gives priority over non-essential goods and services. It cannot buy or condition housing, food and care.","h":"<p>The Recognition Contribution (CdR) recognises work done beyond the Base Contribution: one validated hour is worth one CdR. For the texts it is not a currency: it cannot be passed on or inherited, it has a cap and it shrinks over time. It only gives priority over non-essential goods and services. It cannot buy or condition housing, food and care.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-8.1\">Const. 8.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-8.2\">Const. 8.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5\">Const. 3.5</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-43--award\">Code 1, art. 43</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-44--register-cap-and-decay\">Code 1, art. 44</a> · <a href=\"register/draft-cdr.html\">Public CdR table, draft</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-8.1","register/constitution.html#c-8.2","register/constitution.html#c-3.5","register/code-01.html#art-43--award","register/code-01.html#art-44--register-cap-and-decay"]},{"q":"What are UCR?","a":"d-what-are-ucr","k":["UCR","cost","price","value","unit of account","how much does it cost","accounting","hours of work","calculation","measure"],"x":"Reproduction Cost Units (UCR) are a unit of account: they measure the time, materials, energy and transport needed to make a good again or to provide a service. One UCR corresponds to one hour of ordinary Base Contribution. They are not a price or a means of exchange, and they are not assigned to people. The method of calculation is in an annex that is still only a draft.","h":"<p>Reproduction Cost Units (UCR) are a unit of account: they measure the time, materials, energy and transport needed to make a good again or to provide a service. One UCR corresponds to one hour of ordinary Base Contribution. They are not a price or a means of exchange, and they are not assigned to people. The method of calculation is in an annex that is still only a draft.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/code-03.html#art-9--ucr-reproduction-cost-and-methodology\">Code 3, art. 9</a> · <a href=\"register/draft-ucr.html\">UCR Annex, draft</a></p>","r":["register/code-03.html#art-9--ucr-reproduction-cost-and-methodology"]},{"q":"Can I keep my home and my belongings?","a":"d-can-i-keep-my-home-and-my-belongings","k":["house","home","property","my things","personal belongings","expropriation","dwelling","confiscation","mortgage","savings","car","private property"],"x":"Personal belongings remain yours: the Constitution protects them as personal property. The home you live in is held in use: it cannot be inherited and it cannot be taken from you without another adequate dwelling. You leave it if you change Community or leave the Confederation, after a transition period; those who live with you can stay. As long as an outside world exists, what you own outside the Confederation remains yours. Putting it in common is a voluntary choice.","h":"<p>Personal belongings remain yours: the Constitution protects them as personal property. The home you live in is held in use: it cannot be inherited and it cannot be taken from you without another adequate dwelling. You leave it if you change Community or leave the Confederation, after a transition period; those who live with you can stay. As long as an outside world exists, what you own outside the Confederation remains yours. Putting it in common is a voluntary choice.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.3\">Const. 10.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.7\">Const. 3.7</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5-ter\">Const. 3.5-ter</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use\">Code 1, art. 47, para. 3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use\">Code 1, art. 47, para. 3-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-47-ter--housing-planning-and-allocation\">Code 1, art. 47-ter</a> · <a href=\"register/code-15.html#art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons\">Code 15, art. 19-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-10.3","register/constitution.html#c-3.7","register/constitution.html#c-3.5-ter","register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use","register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use","register/code-01.html#art-47-ter--housing-planning-and-allocation","register/code-15.html#art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons"]},{"q":"Can I have my own activity, a workshop, a field?","a":"d-can-i-have-my-own-activity-a-workshop-a-field","k":["own business","enterprise","company","shop","workshop","field","garden","craftsman","self-employed","employees","sell"],"x":"Yes, in use and not in ownership. The Constitution recognises \"ownership of use\": a stable right to use and look after a common good, for example a workshop or a field, as long as you really use it. You cannot sell it or rent it out, nor use it to make others work for you or to draw an income from it. What you produce you can contribute to the Common Storehouse.","h":"<p>Yes, in use and not in ownership. The Constitution recognises &quot;ownership of use&quot;: a stable right to use and look after a common good, for example a workshop or a field, as long as you really use it. You cannot sell it or rent it out, nor use it to make others work for you or to draw an income from it. What you produce you can contribute to the Common Storehouse.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.2\">Const. 10.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-10.4\">Const. 10.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.2\">Const. 3.2</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use\">Code 1, art. 47</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-10.2","register/constitution.html#c-10.4","register/constitution.html#c-3.2","register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use"]},{"q":"What happens to inheritance when a person dies?","a":"d-what-happens-to-inheritance-when-a-person-dies","k":["inheritance","death","will","children","succession","leave to my children","parents' house","heirs","estate","memories","photos"],"x":"According to the Constitution, personal belongings, clothes and work tools go to the person's Care Nucleus or to whoever the person named while alive. The home is not inherited: it returns to the Community, which protects those who lived there together. The Recognition Contribution (CdR) is extinguished. Digital memories and works go to the Open Archive and enter the public domain, unless the person decided otherwise.","h":"<p>According to the Constitution, personal belongings, clothes and work tools go to the person&#x27;s Care Nucleus or to whoever the person named while alive. The home is not inherited: it returns to the Community, which protects those who lived there together. The Recognition Contribution (CdR) is extinguished. Digital memories and works go to the Open Archive and enter the public domain, unless the person decided otherwise.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.6\">Const. 3.6</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.7\">Const. 3.7</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.9\">Const. 3.9</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-44--register-cap-and-decay\">Code 1, art. 44, para. 5</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-3.6","register/constitution.html#c-3.7","register/constitution.html#c-3.9","register/code-01.html#art-44--register-cap-and-decay"]},{"q":"And the machines?","a":"d-and-the-machines","k":["robots","machines","automation","automate","unemployment","technology","factories"],"x":"They are commons. They take on repetitive, arduous or dangerous work first, and the time they free stays with the Community. AI calculates and proposes; people decide.","h":"<p>They are commons. They take on repetitive, arduous or dangerous work first, and the time they free stays with the Community. AI calculates and proposes; people decide.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.1\">Const. 3.1</a> · <a href=\"register/code-07.html#art-6--automation-of-the-cbo\">Code 7, art. 6</a> · <a href=\"register/code-07.html#art-2--principles\">Code 7, art. 2, para. 6</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-19.6\">Const. 19.6</a> · <a href=\"register/code-07.html#art-6-bis--robots-and-autonomous-machines\">Code 7, art. 6-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-3.1","register/code-07.html#art-6--automation-of-the-cbo","register/code-07.html#art-2--principles","register/constitution.html#c-19.6","register/code-07.html#art-6-bis--robots-and-autonomous-machines"]},{"q":"Can I leave? And can I come back?","a":"d-can-i-leave-and-can-i-come-back","k":["leave","go away","quit","return","come back","withdrawal","freedom to leave","cult","abandon","move","change community","exit"],"x":"Yes. Under the Constitution every person can leave the Confederation at any time: they keep their personal belongings, but leave the common home after a transition period and lose the vote and ordinary access to the Common Storehouses. They can ask to come back whenever they want, and return can be refused only in the very serious cases named by the Constitution. Someone who only changes Community is entitled to thirty days of transition and to accommodation in a Bridge House.","h":"<p>Yes. Under the Constitution every person can leave the Confederation at any time: they keep their personal belongings, but leave the common home after a transition period and lose the vote and ordinary access to the Common Storehouses. They can ask to come back whenever they want, and return can be refused only in the very serious cases named by the Constitution. Someone who only changes Community is entitled to thirty days of transition and to accommodation in a Bridge House.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.8\">Const. 4.8</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-5.2\">Const. 5.2</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use\">Code 1, art. 47, para. 3-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-4.8","register/constitution.html#c-5.2","register/code-01.html#art-47--personal-use-goods-and-ownership-of-use"]},{"q":"What about those who live in the territory but do not want to join?","a":"d-what-about-those-who-live-in-the-territory-but-do-not-want-t","k":["not joining","non-members","outsiders","neighbours","those who disagree","obligation to join","coexistence pact","residents","guests","foreigners","dissent"],"x":"Nobody is obliged to join. Those who do not join can stay under an Autonomous Coexistence Pact: they receive in use a portion of land and, if there is one, a house to live autonomously, without ordinary withdrawals from the Common Storehouses. They must respect the ecological limits, the rules on animals and the ban on weapons. The Pact cannot be refused to anyone who accepts its conditions: every Community sets aside land for at least one person for every hundred residents and, if there is no room, the neighbouring Communities look for it; while waiting the person is housed and receives what they need. Someone who keeps refusing the Pact as well can be moved out of the common settlement, without violence, onto an assigned portion of land.","h":"<p>Nobody is obliged to join. Those who do not join can stay under an Autonomous Coexistence Pact: they receive in use a portion of land and, if there is one, a house to live autonomously, without ordinary withdrawals from the Common Storehouses. They must respect the ecological limits, the rules on animals and the ban on weapons. The Pact cannot be refused to anyone who accepts its conditions: every Community sets aside land for at least one person for every hundred residents and, if there is no room, the neighbouring Communities look for it; while waiting the person is housed and receives what they need. Someone who keeps refusing the Pact as well can be moved out of the common settlement, without violence, onto an assigned portion of land.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-38.1\">Const. 38.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.9\">Const. 4.9</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.9-bis\">Const. 4.9-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.9-ter\">Const. 4.9-ter</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-1.3\">Const. 1.3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-10.html#art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact\">Code 10, art. 4-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-32-bis--persons-living-by-means-of-a-pact-and-removal-from-the-common-settlement\">Code 4, art. 32-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-38.1","register/constitution.html#c-4.9","register/constitution.html#c-4.9-bis","register/constitution.html#c-4.9-ter","register/constitution.html#c-1.3","register/code-10.html#art-4-bis--autonomous-coexistence-pact","register/code-04.html#art-32-bis--persons-living-by-means-of-a-pact-and-removal-from-the-common-settlement"]},{"q":"How does it start?","a":"d-how-does-it-start","k":["how to begin","transition","first steps","found a community","pilot circle","start","take part","how long","ecovillage","phases","where to start"],"x":"The texts provide for a start in phases. It begins with a pilot Circle of at least thirty people who join by choice, in compliance with local law. Then three or four neighbouring Circles come together, and no phase is passed before its thresholds have been checked. Nobody can be pushed to leave their outside job or to contribute property. The texts do not say how to obtain land and buildings.","h":"<p>The texts provide for a start in phases. It begins with a pilot Circle of at least thirty people who join by choice, in compliance with local law. Then three or four neighbouring Circles come together, and no phase is passed before its thresholds have been checked. Nobody can be pushed to leave their outside job or to contribute property. The texts do not say how to obtain land and buildings.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/code-16.html#art-3--phases-of-the-transition\">Code 16, art. 3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-16.html#art-2--principles-of-the-transition\">Code 16, art. 2</a> · <a href=\"register/code-16-annex-c.html#art-2--what-is-needed-to-start\">Annex 16-C, art. 2</a></p>","r":["register/code-16.html#art-3--phases-of-the-transition","register/code-16.html#art-2--principles-of-the-transition","register/code-16-annex-c.html#art-2--what-is-needed-to-start"]},{"q":"And relations with the State: taxes, purchases, trade?","a":"d-and-relations-with-the-state-taxes-purchases-trade","k":["taxes","State","laws","trade","sell","buy outside","outside salary","outside job","tax office","outside money","legal","pension"],"x":"As long as an outside world exists, the texts ask for local law and tax obligations to be respected. The Community's exchanges with the outside go through a Common Fund and serve to cover what is not yet produced, not to make a profit. Those who have an outside income keep it and pay a share to the Fund: in first application one tenth, never more than one fifth.","h":"<p>As long as an outside world exists, the texts ask for local law and tax obligations to be respected. The Community&#x27;s exchanges with the outside go through a Common Fund and serve to cover what is not yet produced, not to make a profit. Those who have an outside income keep it and pay a share to the Fund: in first application one tenth, never more than one fifth.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/code-15.html#art-15--scope-and-principles\">Code 15, art. 15</a> · <a href=\"register/code-15.html#art-17--common-fund-and-external-means-of-payment\">Code 15, art. 17</a> · <a href=\"register/code-15.html#art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons\">Code 15, art. 19-bis, para. 3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-3.5-ter\">Const. 3.5-ter</a></p>","r":["register/code-15.html#art-15--scope-and-principles","register/code-15.html#art-17--common-fund-and-external-means-of-payment","register/code-15.html#art-19-bis--external-income-and-external-assets-of-persons","register/constitution.html#c-3.5-ter"]},{"q":"How are conflicts and crimes handled? Is there prison?","a":"d-how-are-conflicts-and-crimes-handled-is-there-prison","k":["prison","jail","crimes","justice","court","judges","penalties","violence","theft","disputes","life sentence"],"x":"The texts aim to repair the harm and protect people, not to punish. The ordinary way is a restorative procedure; if that is not enough, people chosen by lot and in rotation decide. The word \"prison\" does not appear, but for serious violence there is containment, which is a form of confinement: reviewed every six months and lasting at most five or ten years. For those under eighteen containment is excluded: at most an open educational placement, for up to a year. During the transition these measures do not apply: for crimes the public authorities remain competent.","h":"<p>The texts aim to repair the harm and protect people, not to punish. The ordinary way is a restorative procedure; if that is not enough, people chosen by lot and in rotation decide. The word &quot;prison&quot; does not appear, but for serious violence there is containment, which is a form of confinement: reviewed every six months and lasting at most five or ten years. For those under eighteen containment is excluded: at most an open educational placement, for up to a year. During the transition these measures do not apply: for crimes the public authorities remain competent.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-30.1\">Const. 30.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-31.2\">Const. 31.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-1.3-bis\">Const. 1.3-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-30--grades-5-and-6-two-chances-rule\">Code 4, art. 30, para. 3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-04.html#art-25--common-principles-and-guarantees\">Code 4, art. 25, para. 9</a> · <a href=\"register/code-16-annex-c.html#art-7--conflicts\">Annex 16-C, art. 7, para. 2</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-30.1","register/constitution.html#c-31.2","register/constitution.html#c-1.3-bis","register/code-04.html#art-30--grades-5-and-6-two-chances-rule","register/code-04.html#art-25--common-principles-and-guarantees","register/code-16-annex-c.html#art-7--conflicts"]},{"q":"Are there police, an army, weapons?","a":"d-are-there-police-an-army-weapons","k":["police","army","weapons","security","defence","defense","war","guns","public order","military","self-defence","self-defense","personal defence","invasion"],"x":"The Constitution says there is no army and no permanent professional police. Protection is entrusted to an unarmed Civic Protection Corps, made up of volunteers chosen by lot, with shifts of ninety days at most. Weapons made to kill or seriously injure people are forbidden: they cannot be made or kept. The defence provided for is only civil and non-violent. Everyone may, however, defend themselves, other people and animals against an ongoing attack or ill-treatment, with only the force strictly necessary and without weapons. The texts do not say how it would withstand an armed attack.","h":"<p>The Constitution says there is no army and no permanent professional police. Protection is entrusted to an unarmed Civic Protection Corps, made up of volunteers chosen by lot, with shifts of ninety days at most. Weapons made to kill or seriously injure people are forbidden: they cannot be made or kept. The defence provided for is only civil and non-violent. Everyone may, however, defend themselves, other people and animals against an ongoing attack or ill-treatment, with only the force strictly necessary and without weapons. The texts do not say how it would withstand an armed attack.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-17.1\">Const. 17.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-17.2\">Const. 17.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-16.1\">Const. 16.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-16.4\">Const. 16.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-17.9\">Const. 17.9</a> · <a href=\"register/code-14.html#art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack\">Code 14, art. 8-sexies</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-17.1","register/constitution.html#c-17.2","register/constitution.html#c-16.1","register/constitution.html#c-16.4","register/constitution.html#c-17.9","register/code-14.html#art-8-sexies--personal-defence-and-help-for-persons-under-attack"]},{"q":"Is religion allowed?","a":"d-is-religion-allowed","k":["religion","faith","church","God","prayer","worship","atheism","secularism","mass","believers","spirituality","mosque"],"x":"Yes. The Constitution guarantees the freedom to believe, not to believe and to change belief at any time. The Confederation is secular: no faith is an official religion and nobody can be obliged to fund one through common work or the Storehouses. Every Community makes available a neutral common space for reflection, meditation and celebration.","h":"<p>Yes. The Constitution guarantees the freedom to believe, not to believe and to change belief at any time. The Confederation is secular: no faith is an official religion and nobody can be obliged to fund one through common work or the Storehouses. Every Community makes available a neutral common space for reflection, meditation and celebration.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-25.1\">Const. 25.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-25.2\">Const. 25.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-25.3\">Const. 25.3</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-25.1","register/constitution.html#c-25.2","register/constitution.html#c-25.3"]},{"q":"Why no meat and no exploitation of animals?","a":"d-why-no-meat-and-no-exploitation-of-animals","k":["meat","vegan","vegetarian","animals","farming","slaughter","fishing","hunting","milk","eggs","honey","diet"],"x":"Because the Constitution regards animals as sentient beings with interests of their own: not objects, not goods, not means of production. For this reason it abolishes the slaughter of animals and trade in them, and meat is not produced. The food guaranteed to everyone is plant-based. A few products gathered without harm, such as eggs, milk or honey, remain allowed to a marginal extent.","h":"<p>Because the Constitution regards animals as sentient beings with interests of their own: not objects, not goods, not means of production. For this reason it abolishes the slaughter of animals and trade in them, and meat is not produced. The food guaranteed to everyone is plant-based. A few products gathered without harm, such as eggs, milk or honey, remain allowed to a marginal extent.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-13.1\">Const. 13.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-13.5\">Const. 13.5</a> · <a href=\"register/code-09.html#art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators\">Code 9, art. 6-bis</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-13.1","register/constitution.html#c-13.5","register/code-09.html#art-6-bis--animals-and-pollinators"]},{"q":"What role does artificial intelligence have? Can it decide?","a":"d-what-role-does-artificial-intelligence-have-can-it-decide","k":["artificial intelligence","AI","algorithms","computer","who decides","control","technology","node","chatbot"],"x":"In the texts artificial intelligence (AI) has only advisory or technical tasks: it processes data and makes proposals. The Constitution says it does not vote, judge, monitor or report people. Every binding decision belongs to people, who can always override the machine's proposal. A central AI commanding several territories is forbidden.","h":"<p>In the texts artificial intelligence (AI) has only advisory or technical tasks: it processes data and makes proposals. The Constitution says it does not vote, judge, monitor or report people. Every binding decision belongs to people, who can always override the machine&#x27;s proposal. A central AI commanding several territories is forbidden.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-19.6\">Const. 19.6</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-19.2\">Const. 19.2</a> · <a href=\"register/code-07.html#art-2--principles\">Code 7, art. 2, para. 8</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-19.6","register/constitution.html#c-19.2","register/code-07.html#art-2--principles"]},{"q":"Is there surveillance? What happens to my data?","a":"d-is-there-surveillance-what-happens-to-my-data","k":["surveillance","privacy","personal data","cameras","control","facial recognition","tracking","spying","profiling","social score","delete my data"],"x":"The Constitution forbids using surveillance as a form of government or social control. Face or voice recognition and any biometric profiling are forbidden. Cameras in public spaces are allowed only if encrypted: the images are erased after seventy-two hours and can be read only after a serious event, with the validation of two neighbouring Communities. Every person can ask for their data to be removed.","h":"<p>The Constitution forbids using surveillance as a form of government or social control. Face or voice recognition and any biometric profiling are forbidden. Cameras in public spaces are allowed only if encrypted: the images are erased after seventy-two hours and can be read only after a serious event, with the validation of two neighbouring Communities. Every person can ask for their data to be removed.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-18.1\">Const. 18.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-18.2\">Const. 18.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-18.3\">Const. 18.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-18.4\">Const. 18.4</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-21.6\">Const. 21.6</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-18.1","register/constitution.html#c-18.2","register/constitution.html#c-18.3","register/constitution.html#c-18.4","register/constitution.html#c-21.6"]},{"q":"How do school and children's education work?","a":"d-how-do-school-and-children-s-education-work","k":["school","education","children","kids","teachers","marks","grades","diploma","university","exams","nursery"],"x":"The Constitution abolishes compulsory schooling that is the same for all, classes by age and marks. Children learn in the life of the Community, through practical activities, mentoring and play, accompanied by three reference persons who change every three months and are not from the family. By the age of eleven at least reading, writing and basic mathematics are guaranteed. There is no professional teacher.","h":"<p>The Constitution abolishes compulsory schooling that is the same for all, classes by age and marks. Children learn in the life of the Community, through practical activities, mentoring and play, accompanied by three reference persons who change every three months and are not from the family. By the age of eleven at least reading, writing and basic mathematics are guaranteed. There is no professional teacher.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-29.1\">Const. 29.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-29.3\">Const. 29.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-29.4\">Const. 29.4</a> · <a href=\"register/code-05.html#art-6--minors-and-learning\">Code 5, art. 6</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-29.1","register/constitution.html#c-29.3","register/constitution.html#c-29.4","register/code-05.html#art-6--minors-and-learning"]},{"q":"How does health care work?","a":"d-how-does-health-care-work","k":["health care","doctor","hospital","care","illness","medicines","health","emergency room","vaccines","mental health","epidemic","quarantine","consent"],"x":"For the texts health is a right, and it is neither a commodity nor a paid service. Three levels are provided for: a personal artificial intelligence with a preventive function, the carers of the Community and centres of high specialisation for wider territories. No treatment can be imposed without free and informed consent, not even a vaccine during an epidemic: those who refuse can be kept away only from places at risk, and isolation and quarantine last no more than fourteen days, which can be extended once. From sixteen, people decide on their own care themselves. In a psychological crisis a coercive measure must be validated by a tribunal within twenty-four hours and does not exceed fourteen days. Life-saving emergency care is guaranteed to anyone.","h":"<p>For the texts health is a right, and it is neither a commodity nor a paid service. Three levels are provided for: a personal artificial intelligence with a preventive function, the carers of the Community and centres of high specialisation for wider territories. No treatment can be imposed without free and informed consent, not even a vaccine during an epidemic: those who refuse can be kept away only from places at risk, and isolation and quarantine last no more than fourteen days, which can be extended once. From sixteen, people decide on their own care themselves. In a psychological crisis a coercive measure must be validated by a tribunal within twenty-four hours and does not exceed fourteen days. Life-saving emergency care is guaranteed to anyone.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-28.1\">Const. 28.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-28.2\">Const. 28.2</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-1.3\">Const. 1.3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-06.html#art-1--subject-matter-and-legal-nature\">Code 6, art. 1</a> · <a href=\"register/code-06.html#art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8\">Code 6, art. 2-bis</a> · <a href=\"register/code-06.html#art-2-ter--consent-of-minors-and-of-persons-who-cannot-decide\">Code 6, art. 2-ter</a> · <a href=\"register/code-06.html#art-8--mental-health-and-protocol-for-acute-crisis\">Code 6, art. 8</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-28.1","register/constitution.html#c-28.2","register/constitution.html#c-1.3","register/code-06.html#art-1--subject-matter-and-legal-nature","register/code-06.html#art-2-bis--immediate-collective-risk-and-coordination-with-article-8","register/code-06.html#art-2-ter--consent-of-minors-and-of-persons-who-cannot-decide","register/code-06.html#art-8--mental-health-and-protocol-for-acute-crisis"]},{"q":"How big is a Community, and how are Communities linked?","a":"d-how-big-is-a-community-and-how-are-communities-linked","k":["size","how many people","village","city","federation","circles","bioregion","town","network of communities","levels"],"x":"A Base Community normally has from thirty to five hundred people, so that everyone can decide together. At least three neighbouring Communities normally form an Intermediate Circle, which coordinates among other things transport, energy and the district hospital. Above it are the Bioregion, for railways and specialised centres, and the Global Circle, which is only a common protocol. The texts do not say how a large city would be organised.","h":"<p>A Base Community normally has from thirty to five hundred people, so that everyone can decide together. At least three neighbouring Communities normally form an Intermediate Circle, which coordinates among other things transport, energy and the district hospital. Above it are the Bioregion, for railways and specialised centres, and the Global Circle, which is only a common protocol. The texts do not say how a large city would be organised.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-5.1\">Const. 5.1</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-4.2\">Const. 4.2</a></p>","r":["register/constitution.html#c-5.1","register/constitution.html#c-4.2"]},{"q":"What happens if something runs short or there is an emergency?","a":"d-what-happens-if-something-runs-short-or-there-is-an-emergenc","k":["scarcity","famine","rationing","emergency","blackout","drought","catastrophe","pandemic","stocks","hunger","crisis","food shortage"],"x":"If a necessary good is scarce, the texts provide for temporary and reasoned rationing, approved by the Assembly. When there are not enough hands for everything, vital emergencies come first. For catastrophes or serious shortages a Solidarity Protocol can be activated, moving stocks and work towards the affected areas. An emergency does not suspend fundamental rights and does not last more than thirty days without a new vote of the Assembly.","h":"<p>If a necessary good is scarce, the texts provide for temporary and reasoned rationing, approved by the Assembly. When there are not enough hands for everything, vital emergencies come first. For catastrophes or serious shortages a Solidarity Protocol can be activated, moving stocks and work towards the affected areas. An emergency does not suspend fundamental rights and does not last more than thirty days without a new vote of the Assembly.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-19--conservative-thresholds-and-fair-rationing\">Code 1, art. 19, para. 3</a> · <a href=\"register/code-01.html#art-32--hierarchy-of-logistical-priorities\">Code 1, art. 32</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-36.3\">Const. 36.3</a> · <a href=\"register/constitution.html#c-36.4\">Const. 36.4</a></p>","r":["register/code-01.html#art-19--conservative-thresholds-and-fair-rationing","register/code-01.html#art-32--hierarchy-of-logistical-priorities","register/constitution.html#c-36.3","register/constitution.html#c-36.4"]},{"q":"How can I criticise, propose a change or contribute?","a":"d-how-can-i-criticise-propose-a-change-or-contribute","k":["contribute","criticise","criticize","propose","issue","take part","help","GitHub","email","contact","corrections","translate","feedback","write"],"x":"You can open an issue on GitHub or write to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com: one line is enough. Say which text and article, what the problem is and, if you have one, the change you propose. Today they are assessed by whoever maintains the repository, publicly and with reasons: a verified change is applied and recorded with its reason. Proposals that contradict the Inviolable Core are not accepted.","h":"<p>You can open an issue on GitHub or write to zerocirclesproject@gmail.com: one line is enough. Say which text and article, what the problem is and, if you have one, the change you propose. Today they are assessed by whoever maintains the repository, publicly and with reasons: a verified change is applied and recorded with its reason. Proposals that contradict the Inviolable Core are not accepted.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"contributing.html\">How to contribute</a> · <a href=\"register/proposals.html\">Archive of proposals</a></p>","r":[]},{"q":"Under which licence are the texts published? Can I reuse them?","a":"d-under-which-licence-are-the-texts-published-can-i-reuse-them","k":["licence","license","copyright","reuse","copy","Creative Commons","open source","republish","translate","cite","commercial use"],"x":"The rules (Constitution, Codes and Annexes) are published under the Creative Commons Attribution – ShareAlike 4.0 licence (CC BY-SA 4.0): they can be copied, modified and republished, citing the source and keeping the same licence. The four texts of the path are under CC BY-ND 4.0: they can be republished in full, but not modified. Translating them is allowed, on the conditions written in the licence.","h":"<p>The rules (Constitution, Codes and Annexes) are published under the Creative Commons Attribution – ShareAlike 4.0 licence (CC BY-SA 4.0): they can be copied, modified and republished, citing the source and keeping the same licence. The four texts of the path are under CC BY-ND 4.0: they can be republished in full, but not modified. Translating them is allowed, on the conditions written in the licence.</p><p class=\"dove\"><span>Where to read it:</span> <a href=\"https://github.com/ImTheZeroIt/progetto-dei-cerchi/blob/main/LICENSE.md\">Licence</a></p>","r":[]}]};
